95	587	*Note: This answer is provided as a general explanation and is not based on the specific lesson materials from your course database. Please check your course lessons for the precise terminology and details required by your syllabus.*\n\n***\n\nIn botany, the **stem primary structure** refers to the arrangement of tissues formed by the apical meristem (the growing tip of the plant). This primary growth increases the length of the plant. \n\nIn seed plants (specifically angiosperms and gymnosperms), a cross-section of a young, herbaceous stem reveals several distinct tissue layers arranged from the outside in. \n\nHere is a breakdown of the primary structure of a typical **dicotyledonous (dicot) stem**:\n\n### 1. Epidermis\n*   **Location:** The outermost layer of the stem.\n*   **Function:** Protection. It is typically a single layer of cells covered by a waxy **cuticle** that reduces water loss. It may contain stomata (for gas exchange) and trichomes (hairs).\n\n### 2. Cortex\n*   **Location:** The region directly beneath the epidermis, extending down to the vascular bundles.\n*   **Composition & Function:** \n    *   **Collenchyma:** Often found just beneath the epidermis, these cells have unevenly thickened walls and provide flexible structural support to growing stems.\n    *   **Parenchyma:** These are unspecialized cells used for packing, support, and storage of starches.\n    *   **Endodermis:** In some stems, the innermost layer of the cortex acts as a starch sheath (though it is less prominent in stems than in roots).\n\n### 3. Vascular Bundles\n*   **Location:** Arranged in a distinct **ring** around the central axis of the stem. This ring-like arrangement is a hallmark of dicot stems (in contrast, monocot stems have vascular bundles scattered throughout the ground tissue).\n*   **Components (from outside to inside):**\n    *   **Phloem:** Transports organic nutrients (sugars) made during photosynthesis. In primary growth, it consists of protophloem and metaphloem.\n    *   **Vascular Cambium:** A layer of meristematic tissue *between* the xylem and phloem. (Note: While present in primary structures, it is responsible for secondary growth—making the stem thicker later on).\n    *   **Xylem:** Transports water and dissolved minerals up from the roots. It consists of protoxylem (facing the center) and metaxylem (facing the outside).\n\n### 4. Pith (Medulla)\n*   **Location:** The central region of the stem, enclosed by the ring of vascular bundles.\n*   **Composition & Function:** Composed of large, thin-walled parenchyma cells. Its primary function is the storage and transport of nutrients radially across the stem.\n\n---\n\n### Key Difference: Dicot vs. Monocot Stems\nWhile the general layers (epidermis, cortex, vascular tissue) are similar in **monocot stems** (like corn or grass), there are two major differences in their primary structure:\n1.  **Vascular Bundle Arrangement:** Monocot stems do not have a ring of vascular bundles; instead, they are **scattered** throughout the ground tissue.\n2.  **Lack of Pith:** Because the bundles are scattered, there is no distinct central pith region.\n\n***\n\n**Recommendation:** Please review your specific Advanced Level Biology course lessons and diagrams for this topic, as your syllabus may require you to identify these tissues from specific microscope slides or diagrams and know the exact functional details emphasized by your instructor.	null	0.2	f	\N	2026-08-19 15:26:19.126361	\N	\N	\N	\N	f
