157	728	**Recombinant DNA technology uses restriction endonucleases as "molecular scissors" to cut DNA molecules at specific, predictable nucleotide sequences called recognition sites.** \n\nHere is how they function step-by-step in the process:\n\n* **Identifying and Binding:** Restriction endonucleases (restriction enzymes) scan and bind to specific short, palindromic target sequences within the DNA molecule.\n* **Cleavage:** They catalyze the hydrolysis of phosphodiester bonds on both strands of the DNA backbone within or near their recognition sites.\n* **Generating Ends:** Depending on the enzyme, this cut can produce:\n  * **Sticky ends (overhangs):** Staggered cuts that leave short, single-stranded complementary tails, which easily form hydrogen bonds with other DNA fragments cut by the same enzyme.\n  * **Blunt ends:** Straight cuts across both strands that leave no single-stranded overhangs.\n* **Ligation:** Once the gene of interest and a vector (such as a plasmid) are cut with the same restriction enzyme, their compatible ends allow them to be joined together permanently using DNA ligase to form a recombinant DNA molecule.	[{"course_id": 36, "lesson_id": 110, "material_id": 990, "title": "Lesson 8: Regulation of gene expression and biotechnology - Official Study Notes (PDF)", "material_type": "pdf", "lesson_title": "Lesson 8: Regulation of gene expression and biotechnology", "unit_name": "Unit 6: Genetics", "file_url": "/uploads/pdf/5341e06dcfd94e898d98d58551d68020.pdf", "relevance": 0.75}, {"course_id": 36, "lesson_id": 120, "material_id": 1000, "title": "Lesson 1: Applied agricultural biology and post-harvest technology - Official Study Notes (PDF)", "material_type": "pdf", "lesson_title": "Lesson 1: Applied agricultural biology and post-harvest technology", "unit_name": "Unit 10: Applied Biology", "file_url": "/uploads/pdf/dcb444943e5f4a3dbcad372dddbb816b.pdf", "relevance": 0.75}]	0.75	f	\N	2026-08-23 12:22:45.593202	\N	\N	\N	\N	f
