2969	210	20	generic_mcq	In a patient suffering from a specific endocrine disorder, laboratory tests reveal abnormally high levels of adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) in the blood, coupled with low levels of cortisol. Which of the following conditions best describes this physiological state?	\N	In primary hypocortisolism (Addison's disease), the adrenal cortex fails to secrete cortisol. The resulting low blood cortisol levels remove the normal negative feedback inhibition on the hypothalamus and anterior pituitary, leading to elevated secretion of ACTH.	1	apply	moderate	["A. Primary hypercortisolism (Cushing's disease due to adrenal adenoma)", "B. Secondary hypercortisolism due to anterior pituitary tumor", "C. Diabetes insipidus resulting from posterior pituitary dysfunction", "D. Hyperthyroidism caused by Graves' disease", "E. Primary hypocortisolism (Addison's disease) causing a loss of negative feedback on the anterior pituitary"]	E	\N	\N	null	null	null	null	2026-08-16 19:21:24.245829	\N	{"id": 2969, "points": 1.0, "exam_id": 210, "options": ["A. Primary hypercortisolism (Cushing's disease due to adrenal adenoma)", "B. Secondary hypercortisolism due to anterior pituitary tumor", "C. Diabetes insipidus resulting from posterior pituitary dysfunction", "D. Hyperthyroidism caused by Graves' disease", "E. Primary hypocortisolism (Addison's disease) causing a loss of negative feedback on the anterior pituitary"], "stem_text": "In a patient suffering from a specific endocrine disorder, laboratory tests reveal abnormally high levels of adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) in the blood, coupled with low levels of cortisol. Which of the following conditions best describes this physiological state?", "difficulty": "moderate", "diagram_url": null, "explanation": "In primary hypocortisolism (Addison's disease), the adrenal cortex fails to secrete cortisol. The resulting low blood cortisol levels remove the normal negative feedback inhibition on the hypothalamus and anterior p