2977	210	28	combination_grid	Which of the following statements concerning plant movements and tropisms are correct?\n\nA. Phototropism in stem tips is caused by the lateral redistribution of auxins towards the shaded side, resulting in greater cell elongation there.\nB. Thigmonastic movements in Mimosa pudica occur due to rapid loss of turgor pressure in pulvini motor cells.\nC. Gravitropism in roots is mediated by statoliths settling towards the lower side, inhibiting cell elongation on the lower surface so roots grow downwards.\nD. Photoperiodism in short-day plants is regulated by phytochrome pigments, where Pfr acts as an inhibitor of flowering.	\N	All four statements are accurate according to the A/L plant physiology syllabus. A is correct (auxin redistribution in phototropism). B is correct (turgor changes in pulvini of Mimosa pudica). C is correct (statoliths and gravitropism in roots). D is correct (phytochrome Pfr acts as an inhibitor of flowering in short-day plants).	1	understand	moderate	["A. A, B and C only", "B. B, C and D only", "C. All A, B, C and D are correct", "D. A, C and D only", "E. A, B and D only"]	C	\N	\N	[{"id": "A", "text": "Phototropism in stem tips is caused by the lateral redistribution of auxins towards the shaded side, resulting in greater cell elongation there.", "is_true": true}, {"id": "B", "text": "Thigmonastic movements in Mimosa pudica occur due to rapid loss of turgor pressure in pulvini motor cells.", "is_true": true}, {"id": "C", "text": "Gravitropism in roots is mediated by statoliths settling towards the lower side, inhibiting cell elongation on the lower surface so roots grow downwards.", "is_true": true}, {"id": "D", "text": "Photoperiodism in short-day plants is regulated by phytochrome pigments, where Pfr acts as an inhibitor of flowering.", "is_true": true}]	null	null	null	2026-08-16 19:21:24.245842	\N	{"id": 2977, "points": 1.0, "exam_id": 210, "options": ["A. A, B and C only", "B. B, C and D only", "C. All A, B, C an