3004	213	1	essay_rubric	Describe the generation and propagation of an action potential along a myelinated mammalian neuron and explain the mechanism of synaptic transmission across a chemical synapse.	\N	Students frequently confuse repolarization mechanisms with the action of the sodium-potassium pump. Ensure they distinguish between voltage-gated channel action potentials and active transport resting maintenance. Saltatory conduction and the role of Schwann cells must be clearly linked to Nodes of Ranvier.	40	analyze	medium	null	\N	\N	\N	null	null	null	{"structure_format": "single_complete", "structure_type": "single_complete", "stem_text": "Describe the generation and propagation of an action potential along a myelinated mammalian neuron and explain the mechanism of synaptic transmission across a chemical synapse.", "instruction": "", "marking_scheme": "Examiners should award 4.0 marks for each of the 10 core scientific points fully elaborated with appropriate terminology (Na+/K+ pump, threshold, saltatory conduction, Nodes of Ranvier, exocytosis, EPSP/IPSP, acetylcholinesterase). Partial marks may be awarded if concepts are partially described without correct ion references.", "examiner_notes": "Students frequently confuse repolarization mechanisms with the action of the sodium-potassium pump. Ensure they distinguish between voltage-gated channel action potentials and active transport resting maintenance. Saltatory conduction and the role of Schwann cells must be clearly linked to Nodes of Ranvier.", "answer_points": [{"id": "pt_1_1", "item_number": 1, "description": "Resting membrane potential is maintained at approximately -70 mV by the sodium-potassium pump (Na\\u207a/K\\u207a ATPase), which actively transports 3 Na\\u207a ions out and 2 K\\u207a ions in, alongside leaky K\\u207a channels.", "marks": 4, "accepted_alternatives": "Maintenance of negative resting potential via Na+/K+ pump and selective permeability."}, {"id": "pt_1_2", "item_number": 2, "description": "Depolarization phase: A threshold stimulus opens voltage-gated Na\\u207a channels, causing rapid influx of Na\\u207a ions down their electrochemical gradient, reversing the membrane potential to about +30 mV.", "marks": 4, "accepted_alternatives": "Inward movement of sodium ions through voltage-gated channels leading to spike generation."}, {"id": "pt_1_3", "item_number": 3, "description": "Repolarization phase: Voltage-gated Na\\u207a channels close and voltage-gated K\\u207a channels open, allowing rapid efflux of K\\u207a ions out of the axoplasm to restore the negative internal resting potential.", "marks": 4, "accepted_alternatives": "Outward potassium current restoring internal negativity."}, {"id": "pt_1_4", "item_number": 4, "description": "Hyperpolarization (undershoot): Delayed closing of voltage-gated K\\u207a channels causes an excess efflux of K\\u207a, making the membrane potential more negative than the resting state temporarily.", "marks": 4, "accepted_alternatives": "After-hyperpolarization due to slow closure of potassium gates."}, {"id": "pt_1_5", "item_number": 5, "description": "Saltatory conduction in myelinated axons: Myelin sheath acts as an electrical insulator; local current circuits jump from one Node of Ranvier to the next, drastically increasing the speed of impulse propagation.", "marks": 4, "accepted_alternatives": "Jumping of action potential across Nodes of Ranvier in myelinated nerve fibers."}, {"id": "pt_1_6", "item_number": 6, "description": "Arrival of the action potential at the presynaptic axon terminal depolarizes the presynaptic membrane, opening voltage-gated calcium channels and causing Ca\\u00b2\\u207a influx into the terminal.", "marks": 4, "accepted_alternatives": "Calcium influx triggering vesicular response at the terminal."}, {"id": "pt_1_7", "item_number": 7, "description": "Vesicular exocytosis: Influx of Ca\\u00b2\\u207a ions causes synaptic vesicles containing neurotransmitters (e.g., acetylcholine) to fuse with the presynaptic membrane, releasing neurotransmitter into the synaptic cleft.", "marks": 4, "accepted_alternatives": "Release of neurotransmitter molecules via exocytosis into synaptic gap."}, {"id": "pt_1_8", "item_number": 8, "description": "Neurotransmitter binds to specific ligand-gated ion channels on the postsynaptic membrane, inducing a conformational change that alters membrane permeability.", "marks": 4, "accepted_alternatives": "Binding of transmitter to postsynaptic receptors."}, {"id": "pt_1_9", "item_number": 9, "description": "Generation of graded potentials: Influx of Na\\u207a or Ca\\u00b2\\u207a produces an Excitatory Postsynaptic Potential (EPSP), while Cl\\u207b influx or K\\u207a efflux produces an Inhibitory Postsynaptic Potential (IPSP).", "marks": 4, "accepted_alternatives": "Formation of EPSP or IPSP at postsynaptic membrane."}, {"id": "pt_1_10", "item_number": 10, "description": "Termination of signal: Neurotransmitters in the synaptic cleft are rapidly degraded by specific enzymes (e.g., acetylcholinesterase) or reabsorbed by the presynaptic terminal via reuptake mechanisms.", "marks": 4, "accepted_alternatives": "Enzymatic breakdown or reuptake of neurotransmitters to prevent continuous stimulation."}], "criteria": [{"id": "pt_1_1", "item_number": 1, "description": "Resting membrane potential is maintained at approximately -70 mV by the sodium-potassium pump (Na\\u207a/K\\u207a ATPase), which actively transports 3 Na\\u207a ions out and 2 K\\u207a ions in, alongside leaky K\\u207a channels.", "marks": 4, "accepted_alternatives": "Maintenance of negative resting potential via Na+/K+ pump and selective permeability."}, {"id": "pt_1_2", "item_number": 2, "description": "Depolarization phase: A threshold stimulus opens voltage-gated Na\\u207a channels, causing rapid influx of Na\\u207a ions down their electrochemical gradient, reversing the membrane potential to about +30 mV.", "marks": 4, "accepted_alternatives": "Inward movement of sodium ions through voltage-gated channels leading to spike generation."}, {"id": "pt_1_3", "item_number": 3, "description": "Repolarization phase: Voltage-gated Na\\u207a channels close and voltage-gated K\\u207a channels open, allowing rapid efflux of K\\u207a ions out of the axoplasm to restore the negative internal resting potential.", "marks": 4, "accepted_alternatives": "Outward potassium current restoring internal negativity."}, {"id": "pt_1_4", "item_number": 4, "description": "Hyperpolarization (undershoot): Delayed closing of voltage-gated K\\u207a channels causes an excess efflux of K\\u207a, making the membrane potential more negative than the resting state temporarily.", "marks": 4, "accepted_alternatives": "After-hyperpolarization due to slow closure of potassium gates."}, {"id": "pt_1_5", "item_number": 5, "description": "Saltatory conduction in myelinated axons: Myelin sheath acts as an electrical insulator; local current circuits jump from one Node of Ranvier to the next, drastically increasing the speed of impulse propagation.", "marks": 4, "accepted_alternatives": "Jumping of action potential across Nodes of Ranvier in myelinated nerve fibers."}, {"id": "pt_1_6", "item_number": 6, "description": "Arrival of the action potential at the presynaptic axon terminal depolarizes the presynaptic membrane, opening voltage-gated calcium channels and causing Ca\\u00b2\\u207a influx into the terminal.", "marks": 4, "accepted_alternatives": "Calcium influx triggering vesicular response at the terminal."}, {"id": "pt_1_7", "item_number": 7, "description": "Vesicular exocytosis: Influx of Ca\\u00b2\\u207a ions causes synaptic vesicles containing neurotransmitters (e.g., acetylcholine) to fuse with the presynaptic membrane, releasing neurotransmitter into the synaptic cleft.", "marks": 4, "accepted_alternatives": "Release of neurotransmitter molecules via exocytosis into synaptic gap."}, {"id": "pt_1_8", "item_number": 8, "description": "Neurotransmitter binds to specific ligand-gated ion channels on the postsynaptic membrane, inducing a conformational change that alters membrane permeability.", "marks": 4, "accepted_alternatives": "Binding of transmitter to postsynaptic receptors."}, {"id": "pt_1_9", "item_number": 9, "description": "Generation of graded potentials: Influx of Na\\u207a or Ca\\u00b2\\u207a produces an Excitatory Postsynaptic Potential (EPSP), while Cl\\u207b influx or K\\u207a efflux produces an Inhibitory Postsynaptic Potential (IPSP).", "marks": 4, "accepted_alternatives": "Formation of EPSP or IPSP at postsynaptic membrane."}, {"id": "pt_1_10", "item_number": 10, "description": "Termination of signal: Neurotransmitters in the synaptic cleft are rapidly degraded by specific enzymes (e.g., acetylcholinesterase) or reabsorbed by the presynaptic terminal via reuptake mechanisms.", "marks": 4, "accepted_alternatives": "Enzymatic breakdown or reuptake of neurotransmitters to prevent continuous stimulation."}], "subparts": [{"id": "sub_1_1", "order": 1, "label": "(1)", "prompt": "(1) Explain the biological mechanisms and significance of the specified topic.", "marks": 40, "max_points": 40, "answer_points": [{"id": "pt_sub_1_1_1", "item_number": 1, "description": "Biological fact #1 for part (1)", "marks": 13.3, "accepted_alternatives": ""}, {"id": "pt_sub_1_1_2", "item_number": 2, "description": "Biological fact #2 for part (1)", "marks": 13.3, "accepted_alternatives": ""}, {"id": "pt_sub_1_1_3", "item_number": 3, "description": "Biological fact #3 for part (1)", "marks": 13.399999999999999, "accepted_alternatives": ""}], "marking_scheme": ""}]}	2026-08-16 19:52:11.70971	\N	{"id": 3004, "points": 40.0, "exam_id": 213, "options": null, "stem_text": "Describe the generation and propagation of an action potential along a myelinated mammalian neuron and explain the mechanism of synaptic transmission across a chemical synapse.", "difficulty": "medium", "diagram_url": null, "explanation": "Students frequently confuse re