sure of potassium gates."}, {"id": "pt_1_5", "marks": 4, "description": "Saltatory conduction in myelinated axons: Myelin sheath acts as an electrical insulator; local current circuits jump from one Node of Ranvier to the next, drastically increasing the speed of impulse propagation.", "item_number": 5, "accepted_alternatives": "Jumping of action potential across Nodes of Ranvier in myelinated nerve fibers."}, {"id": "pt_1_6", "marks": 4, "description": "Arrival of the action potential at the presynaptic axon terminal depolarizes the presynaptic membrane, opening voltage-gated calcium channels and causing Ca²⁺ influx into the terminal.", "item_number": 6, "accepted_alternatives": "Calcium influx triggering vesicular response at the terminal."}, {"id": "pt_1_7", "marks": 4, "description": "Vesicular exocytosis: Influx of Ca²⁺ ions causes synaptic vesicles containing neurotransmitters (e.g., acetylcholine) to fuse with the presynaptic membrane, releasing neurotransmitter into the synaptic cleft.", "item_number": 7, "accepted_alternatives": "Release of neurotransmitter molecules via exocytosis into synaptic gap."}, {"id": "pt_1_8", "marks": 4, "description": "Neurotransmitter binds to specific ligand-gated ion channels on the postsynaptic membrane, inducing a conformational change that alters membrane permeability.", "item_number": 8, "accepted_alternatives": "Binding of transmitter to postsynaptic receptors."}, {"id": "pt_1_9", "marks": 4, "description": "Generation of graded potentials: Influx of Na⁺ or Ca²⁺ produces an Excitatory Postsynaptic Potential (EPSP), while Cl⁻ influx or K⁺ efflux produces an Inhibitory Postsynaptic Potential (IPSP).", "item_number": 9, "accepted_alternatives": "Formation of EPSP or IPSP at postsynaptic membrane."}, {"id": "pt_1_10", "marks": 4, "description": "Termination of signal: Neurotransmitters in the synaptic cleft are rapidly degraded by specific enzymes (e.g., acetylcholinesterase) or reabsorbed by the presynaptic terminal via reuptake mechanisms.", "item_number": 10, "accepted_alternatives": "Enzymatic breakdown or reuptake of neurotransmitters to prevent continuous stimulation."}], "subparts": [{"id": "sub_1_1", "label": "(1)", "marks": 40, "order": 1, "prompt": "(1) Explain the biological mechanisms and significance of the specified topic.", "max_points": 40, "answer_points": [{"id": "pt_sub_1_1_1", "marks": 13.3, "description": "Biological fact #1 for part (1)", "item_number": 1, "accepted_alternatives": ""}, {"id": "pt_sub_1_1_2", "marks": 13.3, "description": "Biological fact #2 for part (1)", "item_number": 2, "accepted_alternatives": ""}, {"id": "pt_sub_1_1_3", "marks": 13.399999999999999, "description": "Biological fact #3 for part (1)", "item_number": 3, "accepted_alternatives": ""}], "marking_scheme": ""}], "stem_text": "Describe the generation and propagation of an action potential along a myelinated mammalian neuron and explain the mechanism of synaptic transmission across a chemical synapse.", "instruction": "", "answer_points": [{"id": "pt_1_1", "marks": 4, "description": "Resting membrane potential is maintained at approximately -70 mV by the sodium-potassium pump (Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase), which actively transports 3 Na⁺ ions out and 2 K⁺ ions in, alongside leaky K⁺ channels.", "item_number": 1, "accepted_alternatives": "Maintenance of negative resting potential via Na+/K+ pump and selective permeability."}, {"id": "pt_1_2", "marks": 4, "description": "Depolarization phase: A threshold stimulus opens voltage-gated Na⁺ channels, causing rapid influx of Na⁺ ions down their electrochemical gradient, reversing the membrane potential to about +30 mV.", "item_number": 2, "accepted_alternatives": "Inward movement of sodium ions through voltage-gated channels leading to spike generation."}, {"id": "pt_1_3", "marks": 4, "description": "Repolarization phase: Voltage-gated Na⁺ channels close and voltage-gated K⁺ channels open, allowing rapid efflux of K⁺ ions out of the axoplasm to restore the negative internal resting potential.", "item_number": 3, "accepted_alternatives": "Outward potassium current restoring internal negativity."}, {"id": "pt_1_4", "marks": 4, "description": "Hyperpolarization (undershoot): Delayed closing of voltage-gated K⁺ channels causes an excess efflux of K⁺, making the membrane potential more negative than the resting state temporarily.", "item_number": 4, "accepted_alternatives": "After-hyperpolarization due to slow closure of potassium gates."}, {"id": "pt_1_5", "marks": 4, "description": "Saltatory conduction in myelinated axons: Myelin sheath acts as an electrical insulator; local current circuits jump from one Node of Ranvier to the next, drastically increasing the speed of impulse propagation.", "item_number": 5, "accepted_alternatives": "Jumping of action potential across Nodes of Ranvier in myelinated nerve fibers."}, {"id": "pt_1_6", "marks": 4, "description": "Arrival of the action potential at the presynaptic axon terminal depolarizes the presynaptic membrane, opening voltage-gated calcium channels and causing Ca²⁺ influx into the terminal.", "item_number": 6, "accepted_alternatives": "Calcium influx triggering vesicular response at the terminal."}, {"id": "pt_1_7", "marks": 4, "description": "Vesicular exocytosis: Influx of Ca²⁺ ions causes synaptic vesicles containing neurotransmitters (e.g., acetylcholine) to fuse with the presynaptic membrane, releasing neurotransmitter into the synaptic cleft.", "item_number": 7, "accepted_alternatives": "Release of neurotransmitter molecules via exocytosis into synaptic gap."}, {"id": "pt_1_8", "marks": 4, "description": "Neurotransmitter binds to specific ligand-gated ion channels on the postsynaptic membrane, inducing a conformational change that alters membrane permeability.", "item_number": 8, "accepted_alternatives": "Binding of transmitter to postsynaptic receptors."}, {"id": "pt_1_9", "marks": 4, "description": "Generation of graded potentials: Influx of Na⁺ or Ca²⁺ produces an Excitatory Postsynaptic Potential (EPSP), while Cl⁻ influx or K⁺ efflux produces an Inhibitory Postsynaptic Potential (IPSP).", "item_number": 9, "accepted_alternatives": "Formation of EPSP or IPSP at postsynaptic membrane."}, {"id": "pt_1_10", "marks": 4, "description": "Termination of signal: Neurotransmitters in the synaptic cleft are rapidly degraded by specific enzymes (e.g., acetylcholinesterase) or reabsorbed by the presynaptic terminal via reuptake mechanisms.", "item_number": 10, "accepted_alternatives": "Enzymatic breakdown or reuptake of neurotransmitters to prevent continuous stimulation."}], "examiner_notes": "Students frequently confuse repolarization mechanisms with the action of the sodium-potassium pump. Ensure they distinguish between voltage-gated channel action potentials and active transport resting maintenance. Saltatory conduction and the role of Schwann cells must be clearly linked to Nodes of Ranvier.", "marking_scheme": "Examiners should award 4.0 marks for each of the 10 core scientific points fully elaborated with appropriate terminology (Na+/K+ pump, threshold, saltatory conduction, Nodes of Ranvier, exocytosis, EPSP/IPSP, acetylcholinesterase). Partial marks may be awarded if concepts are partially described without correct ion references.", "structure_type": "single_complete", "structure_format": "single_complete"}, "structured_subparts_json": null}	f	\N
3005	213	1	essay_rubric	Photosynthesis and cellular respiration are fundamental bioenergetic processes that sustain life on Earth through the transformation of energy and cycling of matter within ecosystems and cellular compartments.	\N	Examiners should look for precise biochemical terminology (e.g., photolysis, P680, P700, chemiosmosis, proton motive force, ATP synthase) and ensure students clearly delineate between chloroplast and mitochondrial membrane mechanisms.	40	analyze	medium	null	\N	\N	\N	null	null	null	{"structure_format": "multi_part", "structure_type": "multi_part", "stem_text": "Photosynthesis and cellular respiration are fundamental bioenergetic processes that sustain life on Earth through the transformation of energy and cycling of matter within ecosystems and cellular compartments.", "instruction": "", "marking_scheme": "Total marks = 40. Part (i) accounts for 10 marks testing non-cyclic photophosphorylation. Part (ii) accounts for 15 marks evaluating differences between cyclic and non-cyclic pathways. Part (iii) accounts for 15 marks detailing mitochondrial electron transport and oxidative phosphorylation.", "examiner_notes": "Examiners should look for precise biochemical terminology (e.g., photolysis, P680, P700, chemiosmosis, proton motive force, ATP synthase) and ensure students clearly delineate between chloroplast and mitochondrial membrane mechanisms.", "answer_points": [], "criteria": [], "subparts": [{"id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_gnxx0", "order": 1, "label": "(i)", "prompt": "(i) Explain the mechanism of non-cyclic photophosphorylation and state its primary biological products.", "marks": 10, "max_points": 10, "answer_points": [{"id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_gnxx0_1", "item_number": 1, "description": "Light energy is absorbed by Photosystem II (P680), exciting electrons to a higher energy state from where they are accepted by a primary electron acceptor.", "marks": 3.3, "accepted_alternatives": "Excitation of P680 chlorophyll molecules by photons and primary electron capture."}, {"id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_gnxx0_2", "item_number": 2, "description": "Photolysis of water occurs at the oxygen-evolving complex associated with PS II, releasing electrons to replace those lost by P680, along with protons (H+) and oxygen gas as a byproduct.", "marks": 3.3, "accepted_alternatives": "Splitting of water molecules to provide replacement electrons, protons, and oxygen."}, {"id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_gnxx0_3", "item_number": 3, "description": "Electrons pass down an electron transport chain to Photosystem I (P700), generating ATP via chemiosmosis, and subsequently reduce NADP+ to NADPH catalyzed by ferredoxin-NADP+ reductase.", "marks": 3.4000000000000004, "accepted_alternatives": "Electron transport from PS II to PS I producing ATP and final reduction of NADP+ to NADPH."}], "marking_scheme": "Award marks for clear sequential explanation of excitation, photolysis, electron transport chain, ATP synthesis via chemiosmosis, and NADPH formation. Total: 10 marks."}, {"id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_wtrbb", "order": 2, "label": "(ii)", "prompt": "(ii) Compare and contrast cyclic and non-cyclic photophosphorylation in higher plants.", "marks": 15, "max_points": 15, "answer_points": [{"id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_wtrbb_1", "item_number": 1, "description": "Comparison based on involvement of photosystems: Non-cyclic involves both Photosystem II and Photosystem I, whereas cyclic photophosphorylation involves only Photosystem I.", "marks": 5, "accepted_alternatives": "PS II and PS I utilized in non-cyclic; only PS I in cyclic."}, {"id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_wtrbb_2", "item_number": 2, "description": "Comparison based on photolysis of water and oxygen production: Water splitting and O\\u2082 evolution occur in non-cyclic photophosphorylation, but neither occurs in cyclic photophosphorylation.", "marks": 5, "accepted_alternatives": "Water photolysis and oxygen release present in non-cyclic, absent in cyclic."}, {"id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_wtrbb_3", "item_number": 3, "description": "Comparison based on end products: Non-cyclic produces ATP, NADPH, and O\\u2082; cyclic produces only ATP without forming NADPH or releasing oxygen.", "marks": 5, "accepted_alternatives": "Products: ATP + NADPH + O2 (non-cyclic) vs. ATP only (cyclic)."}], "marking_scheme": "Award marks based on accurate structural and functional comparisons covering photosystems involved, photolysis of water, and final products. Total: 15 marks."}, {"id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_9cdov", "order": 3, "label": "(iii)", "prompt": "(iii) Discuss the role of the mitochondrial electron transport chain and oxidative phosphorylation in cellular respiration.", "marks": 15, "max_points": 15, "answer_points": [{"id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_9cdov_1", "item_number": 1, "description": "NADH and FADH\\u2082 donate high-energy electrons to protein complexes embedded in the inner mitochondrial membrane, passing them sequentially down the electron transport chain to molecular oxygen as the final electron acceptor.", "marks": 5, "accepted_alternatives": "Electron donation by coenzymes through protein complexes to terminal oxygen acceptor."}, {"id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_9cdov_2", "item_number": 2, "description": "As electrons move through the complexes, energy released is used to pump protons (H+) from the mitochondrial matrix into the intermembrane space, establishing a steep electrochemical proton gradient.", "marks": 5, "accepted_alternatives": "Proton pumping across inner mitochondrial membrane creating proton motive force."}, {"id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_9cdov_3", "item_number": 3, "description": "Protons flow back into the matrix down their electrochemical gradient through ATP synthase complexes (chemiosmosis), driving the phosphorylation of ADP to yield the majority of cellular ATP.", "marks": 5, "accepted_alternatives": "Chemiosmotic ATP synthesis via ATP synthase driven by proton influx."}], "marking_scheme": "Award marks for detailed description of electron transport, proton gradient establishment, role of oxygen, and ATP synthase-mediated oxidative phosphorylation. Total: 15 marks."}]}	2026-08-16 19:52:11.755537	\N	{"id": 3005, "points": 40.0, "exam_id": 213, "options": null, "stem_text": "Photosynthesis and cellular respiration are fundamental bioenergetic processes that sustain life on Earth through the transformation of energy and cycling of matter within ecosystems and cellular compartments.", "difficulty": "medium", "diagram_url": null, "explanation": "Examiners should look for precise biochemical terminology (e.g., photolysis, P680, P700, chemiosmosis, proton motive force, ATP synthase) and ensure students clearly delineate between chloroplast and mitochondrial membrane mechanisms.", "reason_text": null, "grid_key_json": null, "template_type": "essay_rubric", "assertion_text": null, "correct_option": null, "cognitive_level": "analyze", "question_number": 1, "statements_json": null, "snapshot_created_at": "2026-08-16T19:52:20.685637", "essay_checklist_json": {"criteria": [], "subparts": [{"id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_gnxx0", "label": "(i)", "marks": 10, "order": 1, "prompt": "(i) Explain the mechanism of non-cyclic photophosphorylation and state its primary biological products.", "max_points": 10, "answer_points": [{"id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_gnxx0_1", "marks": 3.3, "description": "Light energy is absorbed by Photosystem II (P680), exciting electrons to a higher energy state from where they are accepted by a primary electron acceptor.", "item_number": 1, "accepted_alternatives": "Excitation of P680 chlorophyll molecules by photons and primary electron capture."}, {"id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_gnxx0_2", "marks": 3.3, "description": "Photolysis of water occurs at the oxygen-evolving complex associated with PS II, releasing electrons to replace those lost by P680, along with protons (H+) and oxygen gas as a byproduct.", "item_number": 2, "accepted_alternatives": "Splitting of water molecules to provide replacement electrons, protons, and oxygen."}, {"id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_gnxx0_3", "marks": 3.4000000000000004, "description": "Electrons pass down an electron transport chain to Photosystem I (P700), generating ATP via chemiosmosis, and subsequently reduce NADP+ to NADPH catalyzed by ferredoxin-NADP+ reductase.", "item_number": 3, "accepted_alternatives": "Electron transport from PS II to PS I producing ATP and final reduction of NADP+ to NADPH."}], "marking_scheme": "Award marks for clear sequential explanation of excitation, photolysis, electron transport chain, ATP synthesis via chemiosmosis, and NADPH formation. Total: 10 marks."}, {"id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_wtrbb", "label": "(ii)", "marks": 15, "order": 2, "prompt": "(ii) Compare and contrast cyclic and non-cyclic photophosphorylation in higher plants.", "max_points": 15, "answer_points": [{"id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_wtrbb_1", "marks": 5, "description": "Comparison based on involvement of photosystems: Non-cyclic involves both Photosystem II and Photosystem I, whereas cyclic photophosphorylation involves only Photosystem I.", "item_number": 1, "accepted_alternatives": "PS II and PS I utilized in non-cyclic; only PS I in cyclic."}, {"id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_wtrbb_2", "marks": 5, "description": "Comparison based on photolysis of water and oxygen production: Water splitting and O₂ evolution occur in non-cyclic photophosphorylation, but neither occurs in cyclic photophosphorylation.", "item_number": 2, "accepted_alternatives": "Water photolysis and oxygen release present in non-cyclic, absent in cyclic."}, {"id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_wtrbb_3", "marks": 5, "description": "Comparison based on end products: Non-cyclic produces ATP, NADPH, and O₂; cyclic produces only ATP without forming NADPH or releasing oxygen.", "item_number": 3, "accepted_alternatives": "Products: ATP + NADPH + O2 (non-cyclic) vs. ATP only (cyclic)."}], "marking_scheme": "Award marks based on accurate structural and functional comparisons covering photosystems involved, photolysis of water, and final products. Total: 15 marks."}, {"id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_9cdov", "label": "(iii)", "marks": 15, "order": 3, "prompt": "(iii) Discuss the role of the mitochondrial electron transport chain and oxidative phosphorylation in cellular respiration.", "max_points": 15, "answer_points": [{"id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_9cdov_1", "marks": 5, "description": "NADH and FADH₂ donate high-energy electrons to protein complexes embedded in the inner mitochondrial membrane, passing them sequentially down the electron transport chain to molecular oxygen as the final electron acceptor.", "item_number": 1, "accepted_alternatives": "Electron donation by coenzymes through protein complexes to terminal oxygen acceptor."}, {"id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_9cdov_2", "marks": 5, "description": "As electrons move through the complexes, energy released is used to pump protons (H+) from the mitochondrial matrix into the intermembrane space, establishing a steep electrochemical proton gradient.", "item_number": 2, "accepted_alternatives": "Proton pumping across inner mitochondrial membrane creating proton motive force."}, {"id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_9cdov_3", "marks": 5, "description": "Protons flow back into the matrix down their electrochemical gradient through ATP synthase complexes (chemiosmosis), driving the phosphorylation of ADP to yield the majority of cellular ATP.", "item_number": 3, "accepted_alternatives": "Chemiosmotic ATP synthesis via ATP synthase driven by proton influx."}], "marking_scheme": "Award marks for detailed description of electron transport, proton gradient establishment, role of oxygen, and ATP synthase-mediated oxidative phosphorylation. Total: 15 marks."}], "stem_text": "Photosynthesis and cellular respiration are fundamental bioenergetic processes that sustain life on Earth through the transformation of energy and cycling of matter within ecosystems and cellular compartments.", "instruction": "", "answer_points": [], "examiner_notes": "Examiners should look for precise biochemical terminology (e.g., photolysis, P680, P700, chemiosmosis, proton motive force, ATP synthase) and ensure students clearly delineate between chloroplast and mitochondrial membrane mechanisms.", "marking_scheme": "Total marks = 40. Part (i) accounts for 10 marks testing non-cyclic photophosphorylation. Part (ii) accounts for 15 marks evaluating differences between cyclic and non-cyclic pathways. Part (iii) accounts for 15 marks detailing mitochondrial electron transport and oxidative phosphorylation.", "structure_type": "multi_part", "structure_format": "multi_part"}, "structured_subparts_json": null}	f	\N
3006	213	1	essay_rubric	Write short notes on the following:	\N	Ensure candidates clearly state specific physiological and anatomical components (e.g., descending vs ascending limbs, periclinal divisions, P680 and P700 reaction centers).	40	understand	medium	null	\N	\N	\N	null	null	null	{"structure_format": "short_notes", "structure_type": "short_notes", "stem_text": "Write short notes on the following:", "instruction": "Write short notes on the following:", "marking_scheme": "Examiners evaluate concise scientific terminology, structural accuracy, and adherence to biological principles across all three short notes totaling 40.0 marks.", "examiner_notes": "Ensure candidates clearly state specific physiological and anatomical components (e.g., descending vs ascending limbs, periclinal divisions, P680 and P700 reaction centers).", "answer_points": [], "criteria": [], "subparts": [{"id": "sub_topic_1786909874856_602ka", "order": 1, "label": "(i)", "prompt": "(i) Counter-current multiplier mechanism in the loop of Henle", "marks": 10, "max_points": 10, "answer_points": [{"id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_602ka_1", "item_number": 1, "description": "Fluid flows in opposite directions in the ascending and descending limbs of the loop of Henle, creating a counter-current system that multiplies the osmotic gradient in the medullary interstitium.", "marks": 3.3, "accepted_alternatives": "Opposing flow in limbs creates multiplying osmotic gradient."}, {"id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_602ka_2", "item_number": 2, "description": "The descending limb is permeable to water but impermeable to sodium ions, leading to passive water reabsorption by osmosis into the hypertonic medullary interstitium, concentrating tubular fluid.", "marks": 3.3, "accepted_alternatives": "Descending limb permits water loss, concentrating filtrate."}, {"id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_602ka_3", "item_number": 3, "description": "The thick ascending limb is impermeable to water but actively transports sodium and chloride ions out into the interstitium, maintaining high medullary osmolality and producing hypotonic filtrate.", "marks": 3.4000000000000004, "accepted_alternatives": "Ascending limb actively pumps out NaCl, impermeable to water."}], "marking_scheme": "Award marks proportionally for mentioning counter-current flow (3.3 marks), descending limb water permeability (3.3 marks), and ascending limb active NaCl transport (3.4 marks)."}, {"id": "sub_topic_1786909874856_26s7v", "order": 2, "label": "(ii)", "prompt": "(ii) Secondary growth and vascular cambium activity in dicotyledonous stems", "marks": 10, "max_points": 10, "answer_points": [{"id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_26s7v_1", "item_number": 1, "description": "Secondary growth increases stem girth, resulting from the activity of lateral meristems, primarily the vascular cambium and cork cambium (phellogen).", "marks": 3.3, "accepted_alternatives": "Girth increase due to lateral meristems."}, {"id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_26s7v_2", "item_number": 2, "description": "The vascular cambium forms a continuous ring of meristematic cells derived from fascicular and interfascicular cambium, undergoing periclinal divisions.", "marks": 3.3, "accepted_alternatives": "Vascular cambium ring forms via fascicular and interfascicular cambium."}, {"id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_26s7v_3", "item_number": 3, "description": "Cell divisions cut off secondary xylem (wood) towards the inside and secondary phloem towards the outside, with secondary xylem production greatly exceeding secondary phloem.", "marks": 3.4000000000000004, "accepted_alternatives": "Cuts secondary xylem inside and secondary phloem outside."}], "marking_scheme": "Award marks for identifying lateral meristems (3.3 marks), vascular cambium ring formation (3.3 marks), and differential production of secondary xylem and phloem (3.4 marks)."}, {"id": "sub_topic_1786909874856_m5hg1", "order": 3, "label": "(iii)", "prompt": "(iii) Role of Photosystem I and Photosystem II in non-cyclic photophosphorylation", "marks": 20, "max_points": 20, "answer_points": [{"id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_m5hg1_1", "item_number": 1, "description": "Light energy absorbed by Photosystem II (P680) excites electrons, which are captured by a primary electron acceptor and passed down an electron transport chain to Photosystem I, releasing energy to synthesize ATP via photophosphorylation.", "marks": 6.7, "accepted_alternatives": "PSII absorbs light, excites electrons through ETC to PSI, generating ATP."}, {"id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_m5hg1_2", "item_number": 2, "description": "Photolysis of water at the oxygen-evolving complex of PSII replaces lost electrons by splitting water molecules into oxygen, protons, and electrons, while excited electrons in Photosystem I (P700) are boosted to a higher energy level and transferred to NADP+ reductase, reducing NADP+ to NADPH in the stroma.", "marks": 13.3, "accepted_alternatives": "Water photolysis replenishes PSII electrons; PSI reduces NADP+ to NADPH."}], "marking_scheme": "Award marks for describing PSII excitation and ATP generation via ETC (6.7 marks), and water photolysis coupled with PSI reduction of NADP+ to NADPH (13.3 marks)."}]}	2026-08-16 19:52:11.806762	\N	{"id": 3006, "points": 40.0, "exam_id": 213, "options": null, "stem_text": "Write short notes on the following:", "difficulty": "medium", "diagram_url": null, "explanation": "Ensure candidates clearly state specific physiological and anatomical components (e.g., descending vs ascending limbs, periclinal divisions, P680 and P700 reaction centers).", "reason_text": null, "grid_key_json": null, "template_type": "essay_rubric", "assertion_text": null, "correct_option": null, "cognitive_level": "understand", "question_number": 1, "statements_json": null, "snapshot_created_at": "2026-08-16T19:52:20.685647", "essay_checklist_json": {"criteria": [], "subparts": [{"id": "sub_topic_1786909874856_602ka", "label": "(i)", "marks": 10, "order": 1, "prompt": "(i) Counter-current multiplier mechanism in the loop of Henle", "max_points": 10, "answer_points": [{"id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_602ka_1", "marks": 3.3, "description": "Fluid flows in opposite directions in the ascending and descending limbs of the loop of Henle, creating a counter-current system that multiplies the osmotic gradient in the medullary interstitium.", "item_number": 1, "accepted_alternatives": "Opposing flow in limbs creates multiplying osmotic gradient."}, {"id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_602ka_2", "marks": 3.3, "description": "The descending limb is permeable to water but impermeable to sodium ions, leading to passive water reabsorption by osmosis into the hypertonic medullary interstitium, concentrating tubular fluid.", "item_number": 2, "accepted_alternatives": "Descending limb permits water loss, concentrating filtrate."}, {"id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_602ka_3", "marks": 3.4000000000000004, "description": "The thick ascending limb is impermeable to water but actively transports sodium and chloride ions out into the interstitium, maintaining high medullary osmolality and producing hypotonic filtrate.", "item_number": 3, "accepted_alternatives": "Ascending limb actively pumps out NaCl, impermeable to water."}], "marking_scheme": "Award marks proportionally for mentioning counter-current flow (3.3 marks), descending limb water permeability (3.3 marks), and ascending limb active NaCl transport (3.4 marks)."}, {"id": "sub_topic_1786909874856_26s7v", "label": "(ii)", "marks": 10, "order": 2, "prompt": "(ii) Secondary growth and vascular cambium activity in dicotyledonous stems", "max_points": 10, "answer_points": [{"id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_26s7v_1", "marks": 3.3, "description": "Secondary growth increases stem girth, resulting from the activity of lateral meristems, primarily the vascular cambium and cork cambium (phellogen).", "item_number": 1, "accepted_alternatives": "Girth increase due to lateral meristems."}, {"id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_26s7v_2", "marks": 3.3, "description": "The vascular cambium forms a continuous ring of meristematic cells derived from fascicular and interfascicular cambium, undergoing periclinal divisions.", "item_number": 2, "accepted_alternatives": "Vascular cambium ring forms via fascicular and interfascicular cambium."}, {"id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_26s7v_3", "marks": 3.4000000000000004, "description": "Cell divisions cut off secondary xylem (wood) towards the inside and secondary phloem towards the outside, with secondary xylem production greatly exceeding secondary phloem.", "item_number": 3, "accepted_alternatives": "Cuts secondary xylem inside and secondary phloem outside."}], "marking_scheme": "Award marks for identifying lateral meristems (3.3 marks), vascular cambium ring formation (3.3 marks), and differential production of secondary xylem and phloem (3.4 marks)."}, {"id": "sub_topic_1786909874856_m5hg1", "label": "(iii)", "marks": 20, "order": 3, "prompt": "(iii) Role of Photosystem I and Photosystem II in non-cyclic photophosphorylation", "max_points": 20, "answer_points": [{"id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_m5hg1_1", "marks": 6.7, "description": "Light energy absorbed by Photosystem II (P680) excites electrons, which are captured by a primary electron acceptor and passed down an electron transport chain to Photosystem I, releasing energy to synthesize ATP via photophosphorylation.", "item_number": 1, "accepted_alternatives": "PSII absorbs light, excites electrons through ETC to PSI, generating ATP."}, {"id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_m5hg1_2", "marks": 13.3, "description": "Photolysis of water at the oxygen-evolving complex of PSII replaces lost electrons by splitting water molecules into oxygen, protons, and electrons, while excited electrons in Photosystem I (P700) are boosted to a higher energy level and transferred to NADP+ reductase, reducing NADP+ to NADPH in the stroma.", "item_number": 2, "accepted_alternatives": "Water photolysis replenishes PSII electrons; PSI reduces NADP+ to NADPH."}], "marking_scheme": "Award marks for describing PSII excitation and ATP generation via ETC (6.7 marks), and water photolysis coupled with PSI reduction of NADP+ to NADPH (13.3 marks)."}], "stem_text": "Write short notes on the following:", "instruction": "Write short notes on the following:", "answer_points": [], "examiner_notes": "Ensure candidates clearly state specific physiological and anatomical components (e.g., descending vs ascending limbs, periclinal divisions, P680 and P700 reaction centers).", "marking_scheme": "Examiners evaluate concise scientific terminology, structural accuracy, and adherence to biological principles across all three short notes totaling 40.0 marks.", "structure_type": "short_notes", "structure_format": "short_notes"}, "structured_subparts_json": null}	f	\N
3000	212	1	structured_subparts	The human nephron and associated vascular structures maintain homeostasis through ultrafiltration, selective reabsorption, and secretion, operating via mechanisms such as the counter-current multiplier system in the loop of Henle.	\N	\N	40	recommended	balanced	null	A	\N	\N	null	null	[{"id": "node_1786909059509_hdzclp", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_direct_recall", "points": 10, "children": [{"id": "node_1786909059509_b6hlnc", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_direct_recall", "points": 2, "prompt": "Name the specialized epithelial cells that wrap around the glomerular capillaries, forming filtration slits in the renal corpuscle.", "model_answer": "Podocytes", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Correct identification of podocytes", "points": 2}], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_ri4yew", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_direct_recall", "points": 2, "prompt": "State the primary hormone secreted by the juxtaglomerular apparatus in response to a drop in systemic blood pressure.", "model_answer": "Renin", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Correct identification of renin", "points": 2}], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_jn1q1m", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_conceptual", "points": 6, "prompt": "Explain how the structural adaptations of the proximal convoluted tubule facilitate the reabsorption of glomerular filtrate components.", "model_answer": "The proximal convoluted tubule is lined with simple cuboidal epithelium possessing a prominent brush border of microvilli that vastly increases the surface area for reabsorption. It contains numerous mitochondria to provide ATP for active transport mechanisms, and tight junctions that permit paracellular movement of water and ions.", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Presence of microvilli (brush border) increasing surface area for absorption", "points": 3}, {"criterion": "Abundant mitochondria providing ATP for active transport of solutes like glucose and sodium ions", "points": 3}], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}], "prompt": "", "marking_points": [], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_9tpd6c", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_comparison", "points": 14, "children": [{"id": "node_1786909059509_0lbmpc", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_comparison", "points": 6, "prompt": "Compare the structural and permeability characteristics of the descending limb and the ascending limb of the loop of Henle.", "model_answer": "The descending limb is permeable to water but impermeable to solutes, whereas the thick ascending limb is impermeable to water but actively transports sodium and chloride ions out into the medullary interstitium.", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Accurate description of descending limb permeability properties", "points": 3}, {"criterion": "Accurate description of ascending limb permeability and transport properties", "points": 3}], "comparison_header_1": "Descending limb of loop of Henle", "comparison_header_2": "Ascending limb of loop of Henle", "comparison_pairs": [{"criterion": "Water Permeability", "value_1": "Highly permeable", "value_2": "Impermeable"}, {"criterion": "Solute Permeability / Transport", "value_1": "Impermeable to active solute transport", "value_2": "Actively transports Na\\u207a and Cl\\u207b out"}], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_yp4m3p", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_conceptual", "points": 4, "prompt": "Explain the role of the vasa recta in maintaining the medullary osmotic gradient without washing it away.", "model_answer": "The vasa recta act as counter-current exchangers. As blood flows down into the medulla, it gains solute and loses water; as it ascends toward the cortex, it loses solute and gains water, thereby preserving the hypertonic medullary gradient.", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Operation as a counter-current exchanger", "points": 2}, {"criterion": "Preservation of the medullary osmotic gradient without washout", "points": 2}], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_dj3eme", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_sequential", "points": 4, "prompt": "Outline the sequential events that occur in the collecting duct when the body is dehydrated.", "model_answer": "Osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus detect high blood osmolarity, stimulating the posterior pituitary to release ADH. ADH increases the insertion of aquaporin-2 water channels into the apical membrane of collecting duct cells. Water is reabsorbed from the lumen into the hypertonic medullary interstitium via osmosis, producing a small volume of concentrated urine.", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Release of ADH triggered by high blood osmolarity", "points": 2}, {"criterion": "Insertion of aquaporins and subsequent osmotic water reabsorption", "points": 2}], "sequence_items": ["Hypothalamic osmoreceptors detect elevated plasma osmolarity", "Posterior pituitary gland secretes antidiuretic hormone (ADH)", "Aquaporin channels are incorporated into collecting duct membranes", "Water is reabsorbed, resulting in concentrated urine production"], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}], "prompt": "", "marking_points": [], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_bu5co4", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_conceptual", "points": 16, "children": [{"id": "node_1786909059509_xrd9ta", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_matrix", "points": 8, "prompt": "Complete the following matrix matching renal segments with their primary physiological function.", "model_answer": "Proximal convoluted tubule reabsorbs bulk nutrients and ions; Distal convoluted tubule regulates potassium and pH via secretion.", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Correct match and description for Proximal convoluted tubule", "points": 4}, {"criterion": "Correct match and description for Distal convoluted tubule", "points": 4}], "matrix_data": {"col_headers": ["Nephron Segment", "Primary Physiological Function"], "rows": [{"item": "Proximal convoluted tubule", "expected": "Obligatory reabsorption of glucose, amino acids, and bulk water/ions"}, {"item": "Distal convoluted tubule", "expected": "Aldosterone-dependent sodium reabsorption and potassium/hydrogen ion secretion"}]}, "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_tqoddl", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_conceptual", "points": 8, "prompt": "Discuss how the kidneys compensate for prolonged metabolic acidosis in the human body.", "model_answer": "In metabolic acidosis, renal tubular cells increase the secretion of hydrogen ions (H+) into the filtrate and concurrently increase the reabsorption of bicarbonate ions (HCO3-) back into the blood. Additionally, renal tubule cells synthesize new bicarbonate via glutamine metabolism, excreting excess acid as ammonium (NH4+).", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Enhanced secretion of hydrogen ions into the tubular lumen", "points": 4}, {"criterion": "Reabsorption and generation of new bicarbonate ions to buffer blood pH", "points": 4}], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}], "prompt": "", "marking_points": [], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}}]	null	2026-08-16 19:38:10.246072	\N	{"id": 3000, "points": 40.0, "exam_id": 212, "options": null, "stem_text": "The human nephron and associated vascular structures maintain homeostasis through ultrafiltration, selective reabsorption, and secretion, operating via mechanisms such as the counter-current multiplier system in the loop of Henle.", "difficulty": "balanced", "diagram_url": null, "explanation": null, "reason_text": null, "grid_key_json": null, "template_type": "structured_subparts", "assertion_text": null, "correct_option"