changer"}, {"points": 2, "criterion": "Preservation of the medullary osmotic gradient without washout"}]}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_dj3eme", "label": "", "points": 4, "prompt": "Outline the sequential events that occur in the collecting duct when the body is dehydrated.", "children": [], "format_type": "structured_sequential", "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "model_answer": "Osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus detect high blood osmolarity, stimulating the posterior pituitary to release ADH. ADH increases the insertion of aquaporin-2 water channels into the apical membrane of collecting duct cells. Water is reabsorbed from the lumen into the hypertonic medullary interstitium via osmosis, producing a small volume of concentrated urine.", "marking_points": [{"points": 2, "criterion": "Release of ADH triggered by high blood osmolarity"}, {"points": 2, "criterion": "Insertion of aquaporins and subsequent osmotic water reabsorption"}], "sequence_items": ["Hypothalamic osmoreceptors detect elevated plasma osmolarity", "Posterior pituitary gland secretes antidiuretic hormone (ADH)", "Aquaporin channels are incorporated into collecting duct membranes", "Water is reabsorbed, resulting in concentrated urine production"]}], "format_type": "structured_comparison", "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "marking_points": []}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_bu5co4", "label": "", "points": 16, "prompt": "", "children": [{"id": "node_1786909059509_xrd9ta", "label": "", "points": 8, "prompt": "Complete the following matrix matching renal segments with their primary physiological function.", "children": [], "format_type": "structured_matrix", "matrix_data": {"rows": [{"item": "Proximal convoluted tubule", "expected": "Obligatory reabsorption of glucose, amino acids, and bulk water/ions"}, {"item": "Distal convoluted tubule", "expected": "Aldosterone-dependent sodium reabsorption and potassium/hydrogen ion secretion"}], "col_headers": ["Nephron Segment", "Primary Physiological Function"]}, "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "model_answer": "Proximal convoluted tubule reabsorbs bulk nutrients and ions; Distal convoluted tubule regulates potassium and pH via secretion.", "marking_points": [{"points": 4, "criterion": "Correct match and description for Proximal convoluted tubule"}, {"points": 4, "criterion": "Correct match and description for Distal convoluted tubule"}]}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_tqoddl", "label": "", "points": 8, "prompt": "Discuss how the kidneys compensate for prolonged metabolic acidosis in the human body.", "children": [], "format_type": "structured_conceptual", "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "model_answer": "In metabolic acidosis, renal tubular cells increase the secretion of hydrogen ions (H+) into the filtrate and concurrently increase the reabsorption of bicarbonate ions (HCO3-) back into the blood. Additionally, renal tubule cells synthesize new bicarbonate via glutamine metabolism, excreting excess acid as ammonium (NH4+).", "marking_points": [{"points": 4, "criterion": "Enhanced secretion of hydrogen ions into the tubular lumen"}, {"points": 4, "criterion": "Reabsorption and generation of new bicarbonate ions to buffer blood pH"}]}], "format_type": "structured_conceptual", "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "marking_points": []}]}	f	\N
3001	212	2	structured_subparts	The mammalian kidney plays a vital role in osmoregulation and excretion, maintaining homeostasis through complex processes occurring in the nephron, including the operation of the counter-current multiplier system and hormonal regulation.	\N	\N	40	recommended	balanced	null	A	\N	\N	null	null	[{"id": "node_1786909059509_ro9y4h", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_direct_recall", "points": 10, "children": [{"id": "node_1786909059509_8wqxp8", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_direct_recall", "points": 2, "prompt": "Name the two primary capillary networks associated with a mammalian nephron.", "model_answer": "Glomerulus and vasa recta (or peritubular capillaries).", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Glomerulus mentioned correctly", "points": 1}, {"criterion": "Vasa recta or peritubular capillaries mentioned correctly", "points": 1}], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_aqx0kr", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_direct_recall", "points": 2, "prompt": "State the hormone that primarily increases the permeability of the distal convoluted tubule and collecting duct to water.", "model_answer": "Antidiuretic hormone (ADH) / Vasopressin.", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Identifies Antidiuretic hormone or ADH", "points": 1}, {"criterion": "Correctly links it to vasopressin or water reabsorption function", "points": 1}], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_t9qq93", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_conceptual", "points": 6, "prompt": "Explain how the structural adaptations of the proximal convoluted tubule facilitate the reabsorption of glomerular filtrate components.", "model_answer": "The proximal convoluted tubule is lined by simple cuboidal epithelium with a prominent brush border of microvilli that greatly increases the surface area for absorption. It contains numerous mitochondria to provide ATP for active transport mechanisms, and its cells are tightly packed with carrier proteins and protein channels for reabsorbing glucose, amino acids, and ions.", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Presence of microvilli / brush border increasing surface area", "points": 3}, {"criterion": "Abundant mitochondria providing ATP for active transport of solutes", "points": 3}], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}], "prompt": "", "marking_points": [], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_gyqk04", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_comparison", "points": 14, "children": [{"id": "node_1786909059509_vvpswh", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_comparison", "points": 6, "prompt": "Compare the descending limb and the ascending limb of the loop of Henle in terms of their permeability to water and sodium ions.", "model_answer": "The descending limb is permeable to water but impermeable to sodium ions, whereas the thick ascending limb is impermeable to water but actively transports sodium and chloride ions out into the medullary interstitium.", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Accurate description of descending limb permeability characteristics", "points": 3}, {"criterion": "Accurate description of ascending limb permeability characteristics", "points": 3}], "comparison_header_1": "Descending limb of loop of Henle", "comparison_header_2": "Ascending limb of loop of Henle", "comparison_pairs": [{"criterion": "Water permeability", "value_1": "Highly permeable to water", "value_2": "Impermeable to water"}, {"criterion": "Sodium ion movement", "value_1": "Impermeable to sodium ions", "value_2": "Permeable / actively transports sodium ions out"}], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_suidt2", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_conceptual", "points": 4, "prompt": "Explain the significance of the counter-current multiplier system established by the loop of Henle.", "model_answer": "The counter-current multiplier system establishes and maintains a high osmotic gradient in the medullary interstitium (increasing from the cortex to the inner medulla), which allows the reabsorption of water from the collecting duct and production of concentrated urine.", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Establishes osmotic gradient in medullary interstitium", "points": 2}, {"criterion": "Enables production of concentrated urine / water conservation", "points": 2}], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_k85bta", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_sequential", "points": 4, "prompt": "Outline the sequential events that occur in the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) when blood pressure drops.", "model_answer": "A drop in blood pressure stimulates the juxtaglomerular apparatus to release renin. Renin converts angiotensinogen into angiotensin I. Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) converts angiotensin I into angiotensin II. Angiotensin II stimulates the adrenal cortex to secrete aldosterone, which increases sodium and water reabsorption in the distal tubules.", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Secretion of renin by juxtaglomerular apparatus and conversion of angiotensinogen", "points": 2}, {"criterion": "Formation of angiotensin II and subsequent release of aldosterone leading to ion/water retention", "points": 2}], "sequence_items": ["Drop in blood pressure triggers renin release from JGA", "Renin converts circulating angiotensinogen to angiotensin I", "ACE converts angiotensin I to active angiotensin II", "Angiotensin II stimulates aldosterone secretion from adrenal cortex"], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}], "prompt": "", "marking_points": [], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_p72pmy", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_conceptual", "points": 16, "children": [{"id": "node_1786909059509_fo1awo", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_matrix", "points": 8, "prompt": "Complete the following table regarding nitrogenous wastes and their characteristics across different vertebrate groups.", "model_answer": "Ammonia is highly toxic and requires large amounts of water for excretion, typical of bony fish. Urea is moderately toxic, requiring moderate water, produced by mammals and adult amphibians. Uric acid is least toxic and insoluble, requiring minimal water, produced by birds and reptiles.", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Correct identification of toxicity and water requirement profiles for ammonia and urea", "points": 4}, {"criterion": "Correct identification of uric acid properties and associated organisms", "points": 4}], "matrix_data": {"col_headers": ["Organism / Structure", "Characteristic / Function"], "rows": [{"item": "Ammonia excretion in aquatic bony fish", "expected": "Highly toxic; requires large volumes of water for immediate dilution and elimination across gills."}, {"item": "Uric acid excretion in birds and reptiles", "expected": "Least toxic; excreted as a paste or semi-solid precipitate to conserve water."}]}, "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_5rjyev", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_conceptual", "points": 8, "prompt": "Explain how homeostatic mechanisms regulate blood pH through renal tubular secretion and reabsorption when blood becomes too acidic.", "model_answer": "When blood pH drops (acidosis), the renal tubular cells secrete excess hydrogen ions (H+) into the filtrate and simultaneously reabsorb bicarbonate ions (HCO3-) back into the blood. Additionally, ammonia is secreted to buffer hydrogen ions as ammonium (NH4+), thereby raising systemic blood pH back to normal physiological range.", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Secretion of excess hydrogen ions into the renal tubule filtrate", "points": 4}, {"criterion": "Reabsorption of bicarbonate ions into the bloodstream and buffer action", "points": 4}], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}], "prompt": "", "marking_points": [], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}}]	null	2026-08-16 19:38:10.246077	\N	{"id": 3001, "points": 40.0, "exam_id": 212, "options": null, "stem_text": "The mammalian kidney plays a vital role in osmoregulation and excretion, maintaining homeostasis through complex processes occurring in the nephron, including the operation of the counter-current multiplier system and hormonal regulation.", "difficulty": "balanced", "diagram_url": null, "explanation": null, "reason_text": null, "grid_key_json": null, "template_type": "structured_subparts", "assertion_text": null, "correct_option": "A", "cognitive_level": "recommended", "question_number": 2, "statements_json": null, "snapshot_created_at": "2026-08-16T19:52:50.649773", "essay_checklist_json": null, "structured_subparts_json": [{"id": "node_1786909059509_ro9y4h", "label": "", "points": 10, "prompt": "", "children": [{"id": "node_1786909059509_8wqxp8", "label": "", "points": 2, "prompt": "Name the two primary capillary networks associated with a mammalian nephron.", "children": [], "format_type": "structured_direct_recall", "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "model_answer": "Glomerulus and vasa recta (or peritubular capillaries).", "marking_points": [{"points": 1, "criterion": "Glomerulus mentioned correctly"}, {"points": 1, "criterion": "Vasa recta or peritubular capillaries mentioned correctly"}]}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_aqx0kr", "label": "", "points": 2, "prompt": "State the hormone that primarily increases the permeability of the distal convoluted tubule and collecting duct to water.", "children": [], "format_type": "structured_direct_recall", "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "model_answer": "Antidiuretic hormone (ADH) / Vasopressin.", "marking_points": [{"points": 1, "criterion": "Identifies Antidiuretic hormone or ADH"}, {"points": 1, "criterion": "Correctly links it to vasopressin or water reabsorption function"}]}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_t9qq93", "label": "", "points": 6, "prompt": "Explain how the structural adaptations of the proximal convoluted tubule facilitate the reabsorption of glomerular filtrate components.", "children": [], "format_type": "structured_conceptual", "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "model_answer": "The proximal convoluted tubule is lined by simple cuboidal epithelium with a prominent brush border of microvilli that greatly increases the surface area for absorption. It contains numerous mitochondria to provide ATP for active transport mechanisms, and its cells are tightly packed with carrier proteins and protein channels for reabsorbing glucose, amino acids, and ions.", "marking_points": [{"points": 3, "criterion": "Presence of microvilli / brush border increasing surface area"}, {"points": 3, "criterion": "Abundant mitochondria providing ATP for active transport of solutes"}]}], "format_type": "structured_direct_recall", "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "marking_points": []}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_gyqk04", "label": "", "points": 14, "prompt": "", "children": [{"id": "node_1786909059509_vvpswh", "label": "", "points": 6, "prompt": "Compare the descending limb and the ascending limb of the loop of Henle in terms of their permeability to water and sodium ions.", "children": [], "format_type": "structured_comparison", "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "model_answer": "The descending limb is permeable to water but impermeable to sodium ions, whereas the thick ascending limb is impermeable to water but actively transports sodium and chloride ions out into the medullary interstitium.", "marking_points": [{"points": 3, "criterion": "Accurate description of descending limb permeability characteristics"}, {"points": 3, "criterion": "Accurate description of ascending limb permeability characteristics"}], "comparison_pairs": [{"value_1": "Highly permeable to water", "value_2": "Impermeable to water", "criterion": "Water permeability"}, {"value_1": "Impermeable to sodium ions", "value_2": "Permeable / actively transports sodium ions out", "criterion": "Sodium ion movement"}], "comparison_header_1": "Descending limb of loop of Henle", "comparison_header_2": "Ascending limb of loop of Henle"}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_suidt2", "label": "", "points": 4, "prompt": "Explain the significance of the counter-current multiplier system established by the loop of Henle.", "children": [], "format_type": "structured_conceptual", "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "model_answer": "The counter-current multiplier system establishes and maintains a high osmotic gradient in the medullary interstitium (increasing from the cortex to the inner medulla), which allows the reabsorption of water from the collecting duct and production of concentrated urine.", "marking_points": [{"points": 2, "criterion": "Establishes osmotic gradient in medullary interstitium"}, {"points": 2, "criterion": "Enables production of concentrated urine / water conservation"}]}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_k85bta", "label": "", "points": 4, "prompt": "Outline the sequential events that occur in the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) when blood pressure drops.", "children": [], "format_type": "structured_sequential", "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "model_answer": "A drop in blood pressure stimulates the juxtaglomerular apparatus to release renin. Renin converts angiotensinogen into angiotensin I. Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) converts angiotensin I into angiotensin II. Angiotensin II stimulates the adrenal cortex to secrete aldosterone, which increases sodium and water reabsorption in the distal tubules.", "marking_points": [{"points": 2, "criterion": "Secretion of renin by juxtaglomerular apparatus and conversion of angiotensinogen"}, {"points": 2, "criterion": "Formation of angiotensin II and subsequent release of aldosterone leading to ion/water retention"}], "sequence_items": ["Drop in blood pressure triggers renin release from JGA", "Renin converts circulating angiotensinogen to angiotensin I", "ACE converts angiotensin I to active angiotensin II", "Angiotensin II stimulates aldosterone secretion from adrenal cortex"]}], "format_type": "structured_comparison", "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "marking_points": []}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_p72pmy", "label": "", "points": 16, "prompt": "", "children": [{"id": "node_1786909059509_fo1awo", "label": "", "points": 8, "prompt": "Complete the following table regarding nitrogenous wastes and their characteristics across different vertebrate groups.", "children": [], "format_type": "structured_matrix", "matrix_data": {"rows": [{"item": "Ammonia excretion in aquatic bony fish", "expected": "Highly toxic; requires large volumes of water for immediate dilution and elimination across gills."}, {"item": "Uric acid excretion in birds and reptiles", "expected": "Least toxic; excreted as a paste or semi-solid precipitate to conserve water."}], "col_headers": ["Organism / Structure", "Characteristic / Function"]}, "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "model_answer": "Ammonia is highly toxic and requires large amounts of water for excretion, typical of bony fish. Urea is moderately toxic, requiring moderate water, produced by mammals and adult amphibians. Uric acid is least toxic and insoluble, requiring minimal water, produced by birds and reptiles.", "marking_points": [{"points": 4, "criterion": "Correct identification of toxicity and water requirement profiles for ammonia and urea"}, {"points": 4, "criterion": "Correct identification of uric acid properties and associated organisms"}]}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_5rjyev", "label": "", "points": 8, "prompt": "Explain how homeostatic mechanisms regulate blood pH through renal tubular secretion and reabsorption when blood becomes too acidic.", "children": [], "format_type": "structured_conceptual", "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "model_answer": "When blood pH drops (acidosis), the renal tubular cells secrete excess hydrogen ions (H+) into the filtrate and simultaneously reabsorb bicarbonate ions (HCO3-) back into the blood. Additionally, ammonia is secreted to buffer hydrogen ions as ammonium (NH4+), thereby raising systemic blood pH back to normal physiological range.", "marking_points": [{"points": 4, "criterion": "Secretion of excess hydrogen ions into the renal tubule filtrate"}, {"points": 4, "criterion": "Reabsorption of bicarbonate ions into the bloodstream and buffer action"}]}], "format_type": "structured_conceptual", "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "marking_points": []}]}	f	\N
3002	212	3	structured_subparts	Photosynthesis and cellular respiration are fundamental metabolic processes involving energy transformation in plants. The efficiency of these processes depends on specialized structures such as chloroplasts, photosystems, and mitochondrial electron transport chains.	\N	\N	40	recommended	balanced	null	A	\N	\N	null	null	[{"id": "node_1786909059509_9n6r9i", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_direct_recall", "points": 10, "children": [{"id": "node_1786909059509_hd871r", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_direct_recall", "points": 2, "prompt": "Name the primary photosynthetic pigment found in higher green plants that absorbs light energy primarily in the blue and red regions of the visible spectrum.", "model_answer": "Chlorophyll a", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Naming Chlorophyll a correctly", "points": 2}], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_djh8uo", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_direct_recall", "points": 2, "prompt": "State the specific site within the chloroplast where the light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis take place.", "model_answer": "Thylakoid membrane (or grana)", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Stating thylakoid membrane or grana correctly", "points": 2}], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_gtd468", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_conceptual", "points": 6, "prompt": "Briefly explain the role of water in the light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis.", "model_answer": "Water undergoes photolysis (light-driven splitting) replacing electrons lost by Photosystem II (P680). This process releases molecular oxygen as a by-product and provides protons (H+) that accumulate in the thylakoid lumen to establish a proton gradient for ATP synthesis.", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Mentioning photolysis of water and provision of electrons to Photosystem II", "points": 3}, {"criterion": "Mentioning release of oxygen and contribution of protons (H+) for ATP synthesis", "points": 3}], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}], "prompt": "", "marking_points": [], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_reshkt", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_comparison", "points": 14, "children": [{"id": "node_1786909059509_wtx0lo", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_comparison", "points": 6, "prompt": "Compare cyclic photophosphorylation and non-cyclic photophosphorylation based on the products formed and the involvement of Photosystem I and II.", "model_answer": "Non-cyclic photophosphorylation involves both Photosystem II and Photosystem I, producing ATP, NADPH, and O\\u2082. Cyclic photophosphorylation involves only Photosystem I and produces only ATP without NADPH or O\\u2082 evolution.", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Accurate comparison of photosystem involvement between the two processes", "points": 3}, {"criterion": "Accurate comparison of end products (ATP, NADPH, O\\u2082 vs ATP only)", "points": 3}], "comparison_header_1": "Cyclic Photophosphorylation", "comparison_header_2": "Non-Cyclic Photophosphorylation", "comparison_pairs": [{"criterion": "Photosystems involved", "value_1": "Only Photosystem I (P700)", "value_2": "Both Photosystem II and I"}, {"criterion": "Products formed", "value_1": "ATP only", "value_2": "ATP, NADPH, and O\\u2082"}], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_wpsu5p", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_conceptual", "points": 4, "prompt": "Explain why C4 plants have a higher photosynthetic efficiency than C3 plants under high temperatures and high light intensities.", "model_answer": "C4 plants possess a specialized leaf anatomy (Kranz anatomy) and initially fix CO\\u2082 using PEP carboxylase in mesophyll cells, which has no affinity for oxygen. This effectively minimizes photorespiration, unlike RuBisCO in C3 plants which rates high oxygenase activity at elevated temperatures.", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Mentioning PEP carboxylase and absence of oxygenase activity", "points": 2}, {"criterion": "Explaining the reduction/avoidance of photorespiration in C4 plants", "points": 2}], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_xsk27k", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_sequential", "points": 4, "prompt": "Outline the sequence of main events occurring during the light-independent reactions (Calvin cycle) of photosynthesis.", "model_answer": "1. Carbon dioxide is fixed onto ribulose bisphosphate (RuBP) by RuBisCO. 2. The unstable 6-carbon intermediate splits into two molecules of 3-phosphoglycerate (PGA). 3. PGA is reduced to glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate (G3P) using ATP and NADPH. 4. RuBP is regenerated from G3P utilizing ATP.", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Stating carbon fixation and formation of PGA correctly", "points": 2}, {"criterion": "Stating reduction to G3P and regeneration of RuBP correctly", "points": 2}], "sequence_items": ["Carbon dioxide fixation onto RuBP by RuBisCO", "Formation of 3-phosphoglycerate (PGA)", "Reduction of PGA to G3P using ATP and NADPH", "Regeneration of RuBP to sustain the cycle"], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}], "prompt": "", "marking_points": [], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_ql6z2l", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_conceptual", "points": 16, "children": [{"id": "node_1786909059509_la3ann", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_matrix", "points": 8, "prompt": "Complete the following table regarding major respiratory pathways and their intracellular locations in eukaryotic plant cells.", "model_answer": "Glycolysis occurs in the cytoplasm, and the Krebs cycle occurs in the mitochondrial matrix.", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Correctly identifying the location for glycolysis", "points": 4}, {"criterion": "Correctly identifying the location for the Krebs cycle", "points": 4}], "matrix_data": {"col_headers": ["Metabolic Pathway", "Intracellular Location in Eukaryotes"], "rows": [{"item": "Glycolysis", "expected": "Cytosol / Cytoplasm"}, {"item": "Krebs Cycle (TCA Cycle)", "expected": "Mitochondrial matrix"}]}, "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_gv0m6x", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_conceptual", "points": 8, "prompt": "Explain the mechanism of oxidative phosphorylation in the inner mitochondrial membrane, highlighting the role of ATP synthase.", "model_answer": "Electrons pass down the electron transport chain releasing energy, which is used to pump protons (H+) from the mitochondrial matrix into the intermembrane space, establishing an electrochemical gradient. Protons flow back into the matrix down their concentration gradient through ATP synthase, driving the phosphorylation of ADP to ATP.", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Explaining proton pumping and establishment of the electrochemical gradient", "points": 4}, {"criterion": "Explaining proton flow through ATP synthase and phosphorylation of ADP", "points": 4}], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}], "prompt": "", "marking_points": [], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}}]	null	2026-08-16 19:38:10.246079	\N	{"id": 3002, "points": 40.0, "exam_id": 212, "options": null, "stem_text": "Photosynthesis and cellular respiration are fundamental metabolic processes involving energy transformation in plants. The efficiency of these processes depends on specialized structures such as chloroplasts, photosystems, and mitochondrial electron transport chains.", "difficulty": "balanced", "diagram_url": null, "explanation": null, "reason_text": null, "grid_key_json": null, "template_type": "structured_subparts", "assertion_text": null, "correct_option": "A", "cognitive_level": "recommended", "question_number": 3, "statements_json": null, "snapshot_cre