 in C4 plants"}]}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_xsk27k", "label": "", "points": 4, "prompt": "Outline the sequence of main events occurring during the light-independent reactions (Calvin cycle) of photosynthesis.", "children": [], "format_type": "structured_sequential", "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "model_answer": "1. Carbon dioxide is fixed onto ribulose bisphosphate (RuBP) by RuBisCO. 2. The unstable 6-carbon intermediate splits into two molecules of 3-phosphoglycerate (PGA). 3. PGA is reduced to glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate (G3P) using ATP and NADPH. 4. RuBP is regenerated from G3P utilizing ATP.", "marking_points": [{"points": 2, "criterion": "Stating carbon fixation and formation of PGA correctly"}, {"points": 2, "criterion": "Stating reduction to G3P and regeneration of RuBP correctly"}], "sequence_items": ["Carbon dioxide fixation onto RuBP by RuBisCO", "Formation of 3-phosphoglycerate (PGA)", "Reduction of PGA to G3P using ATP and NADPH", "Regeneration of RuBP to sustain the cycle"]}], "format_type": "structured_comparison", "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "marking_points": []}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_ql6z2l", "label": "", "points": 16, "prompt": "", "children": [{"id": "node_1786909059509_la3ann", "label": "", "points": 8, "prompt": "Complete the following table regarding major respiratory pathways and their intracellular locations in eukaryotic plant cells.", "children": [], "format_type": "structured_matrix", "matrix_data": {"rows": [{"item": "Glycolysis", "expected": "Cytosol / Cytoplasm"}, {"item": "Krebs Cycle (TCA Cycle)", "expected": "Mitochondrial matrix"}], "col_headers": ["Metabolic Pathway", "Intracellular Location in Eukaryotes"]}, "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "model_answer": "Glycolysis occurs in the cytoplasm, and the Krebs cycle occurs in the mitochondrial matrix.", "marking_points": [{"points": 4, "criterion": "Correctly identifying the location for glycolysis"}, {"points": 4, "criterion": "Correctly identifying the location for the Krebs cycle"}]}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_gv0m6x", "label": "", "points": 8, "prompt": "Explain the mechanism of oxidative phosphorylation in the inner mitochondrial membrane, highlighting the role of ATP synthase.", "children": [], "format_type": "structured_conceptual", "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "model_answer": "Electrons pass down the electron transport chain releasing energy, which is used to pump protons (H+) from the mitochondrial matrix into the intermembrane space, establishing an electrochemical gradient. Protons flow back into the matrix down their concentration gradient through ATP synthase, driving the phosphorylation of ADP to ATP.", "marking_points": [{"points": 4, "criterion": "Explaining proton pumping and establishment of the electrochemical gradient"}, {"points": 4, "criterion": "Explaining proton flow through ATP synthase and phosphorylation of ADP"}]}], "format_type": "structured_conceptual", "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "marking_points": []}]}	f	\N
3003	212	4	structured_subparts	Photosynthesis and cellular respiration are fundamental metabolic processes in plants that involve specialized cellular compartments, electron transport chains, and enzyme-mediated carbon fixation pathways.	\N	\N	40	recommended	balanced	null	A	\N	\N	null	null	[{"id": "node_1786909059509_m8atg9", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_direct_recall", "points": 10, "children": [{"id": "node_1786909059509_n3jmy8", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_direct_recall", "points": 2, "prompt": "Name the primary enzyme responsible for carbon dioxide fixation in the Calvin cycle of C3 plants.", "model_answer": "Ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate carboxylase/oxygenase (RuBisCO).", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Naming Ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate carboxylase/oxygenase", "points": 1}, {"criterion": "Accepting standard abbreviation RuBisCO", "points": 1}], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_4hslio", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_direct_recall", "points": 2, "prompt": "State the specific sub-organellar site within the chloroplast where the light-dependent reactions take place.", "model_answer": "Thylakoid membrane.", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Identifying the thylakoid membrane correctly", "points": 2}], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_g4fi04", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_conceptual", "points": 6, "prompt": "Explain the biological significance of photolysis during the light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis.", "model_answer": "Photolysis involves the enzymatic splitting of water molecules using light energy. This process releases molecular oxygen as a by-product, provides protons (H+) to establish a proton gradient across the thylakoid membrane for ATP synthesis, and supplies replacement electrons to Photosystem II (P680).", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Mentions splitting of water yielding oxygen and protons", "points": 3}, {"criterion": "Explains the replenishment of electrons to Photosystem II", "points": 3}], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}], "prompt": "", "marking_points": [], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_gcw5ui", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_comparison", "points": 14, "children": [{"id": "node_1786909059509_v31pzh", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_comparison", "points": 6, "prompt": "Compare cyclic photophosphorylation and non-cyclic photophosphorylation based on the specified features.", "model_answer": "Cyclic photophosphorylation involves only Photosystem I and produces only ATP without forming NADPH or releasing oxygen. Non-cyclic photophosphorylation involves both Photosystem II and Photosystem I, producing ATP, NADPH, and oxygen through water photolysis.", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Accurate differentiation of photosystems involved", "points": 3}, {"criterion": "Accurate differentiation of end products (NADPH and Oxygen)", "points": 3}], "comparison_header_1": "Cyclic Photophosphorylation", "comparison_header_2": "Non-Cyclic Photophosphorylation", "comparison_pairs": [{"criterion": "Photosystems involved", "value_1": "Only Photosystem I (PSI)", "value_2": "Both Photosystem II and Photosystem I (PSII and PSI)"}, {"criterion": "Products formed", "value_1": "Only ATP", "value_2": "ATP, NADPH, and O\\u2082"}], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_p4emu7", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_conceptual", "points": 4, "prompt": "Briefly explain why cyclic photophosphorylation occurs alongside non-cyclic photophosphorylation in chloroplasts.", "model_answer": "Cyclic photophosphorylation occurs to supply the additional ATP required for the Calvin cycle and other metabolic processes, as non-cyclic photophosphorylation does not produce a high enough ATP to NADPH ratio.", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "States the requirement for extra ATP in the Calvin cycle", "points": 2}, {"criterion": "Mentions the unbalanced ATP to NADPH ratio produced by non-cyclic photophosphorylation", "points": 2}], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_a3fs8v", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_sequential", "points": 4, "prompt": "Outline the sequential steps of carbon assimilation in C4 plants starting from CO\\u2082 entry into the mesophyll cell.", "model_answer": "CO\\u2082 is fixed in mesophyll cells by phosphoenolpyruvate (PEP) carboxylase to form a 4-carbon compound (oxaloacetate), which is converted to malate and transported into bundle sheath cells where it is decarboxylated to release CO\\u2082 for the Calvin cycle.", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Correct initial fixation by PEP carboxylase in mesophyll cells", "points": 2}, {"criterion": "Transport and decarboxylation in bundle sheath cells", "points": 2}], "sequence_items": ["CO\\u2082 fixation by PEP carboxylase in mesophyll cells", "Formation of 4-carbon organic acid (oxaloacetate/malate)", "Transport of malate through plasmodesmata into bundle sheath cells", "Decarboxylation of malate to release CO\\u2082 into the Calvin cycle"], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}], "prompt": "", "marking_points": [], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_v4z1et", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_conceptual", "points": 16, "children": [{"id": "node_1786909059509_lxwix5", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_matrix", "points": 8, "prompt": "Complete the matrix below regarding respiratory pathways and their subcellular locations in eukaryotic cells.", "model_answer": "Glycolysis occurs in the cytosol, and the Krebs cycle occurs in the mitochondrial matrix.", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Correct identification of glycolysis location", "points": 4}, {"criterion": "Correct identification of Krebs cycle location", "points": 4}], "matrix_data": {"col_headers": ["Metabolic Pathway", "Subcellular Location"], "rows": [{"item": "Glycolysis", "expected": "Cytosol / Cytoplasm"}, {"item": "Krebs Cycle (Citric Acid Cycle)", "expected": "Mitochondrial matrix"}]}, "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}, {"id": "node_1786909059509_icgkqm", "label": "", "format_type": "structured_conceptual", "points": 8, "prompt": "Explain the role of oxygen as the final electron acceptor in oxidative phosphorylation and describe the consequence if oxygen is absent.", "model_answer": "Oxygen accepts electrons at the end of the mitochondrial electron transport chain and combines with protons to form water, maintaining the flow of electrons. In the absence of oxygen, the electron transport chain stops, stopping oxidative phosphorylation, which forces cells to rely on anaerobic respiration (such as fermentation) to regenerate NAD\\u207a.", "marking_points": [{"criterion": "Explains oxygen's role in accepting electrons and forming water to sustain the electron transport chain", "points": 4}, {"criterion": "Describes the cessation of ATP synthesis and shift to anaerobic pathways", "points": 4}], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "children": []}], "prompt": "", "marking_points": [], "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}}]	null	2026-08-16 19:38:10.246081	\N	{"id": 3003, "points": 40.0, "exam_id": 212, "options": null, "stem_text": "Photosynthesis and cellular respiration are fundamental metabolic processes in plants that involve specialized cellular compartments, electron transport chains, and enzyme-mediated carbon fixation pathways.", "difficulty": "balanced", "diagram_url": null, "explanation": null, "reason_text": null, "grid_key_json": null, "template_type": "structured_subparts", "assertion_text": null, "correct_option": "A", "cognitive_level": "recommended", "question_number": 4, "statements_json": null, "snapshot_created_at": "2026-08-16T19:52:50.649807", "essay_checklist_json": null, "structur