stem"}, {"points": 2, "criterion": "Completed the sequence up to the multicellular organism level"}], "sequence_items": ["Organelle level", "Cellular level", "Tissue level", "Organ and Organ System level"]}], "format_type": "structured_comparison", "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "marking_points": []}, {"id": "node_1787171248947_ih1v5a", "label": "", "points": 16, "prompt": "", "children": [{"id": "node_1787171248947_l0jfuw", "label": "", "points": 8, "prompt": "Complete the following table identifying specific eukaryotic organelles and their primary cellular functions.", "children": [], "format_type": "structured_matrix", "matrix_data": {"rows": [{"item": "Mitochondria", "expected": "Site of aerobic respiration and ATP production"}, {"item": "Nucleus", "expected": "Houses the genetic material and controls cellular activities"}, {"item": "Chloroplast", "expected": "Site of photosynthesis in photosynthetic eukaryotic cells"}, {"item": "Lysosome", "expected": "Intracellular digestion and breakdown of waste materials"}], "col_headers": ["Organelle / Structure", "Primary Cellular Function"]}, "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "model_answer": "Mitochondria function in aerobic respiration and ATP production. The nucleus contains the majority of the genetic material. Chloroplasts are responsible for photosynthesis in plants. Lysosomes contain digestive enzymes for intracellular breakdown.", "marking_points": [{"points": 4, "criterion": "Correctly matched functions for Mitochondria and Nucleus"}, {"points": 4, "criterion": "Correctly matched functions for Chloroplasts and Lysosomes"}]}, {"id": "node_1787171248947_220h2o", "label": "", "points": 8, "prompt": "Discuss why biological knowledge is considered dynamic and not permanently unchangeable, citing the role of technological advancements in microscopy and molecular biology.", "children": [], "format_type": "structured_conceptual", "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "model_answer": "Scientific knowledge evolves as new evidence is gathered. Advances in technology, such as improvements in high-resolution light and electron microscopy and DNA sequencing techniques, allow scientists to visualize cellular ultrastructures and analyze genetic relationships with unprecedented precision. This new empirical evidence frequently leads to the modification, refinement, or replacement of pre-existing biological classifications and theories.", "marking_points": [{"points": 4, "criterion": "Explained that scientific knowledge is open to modification based on new evidence"}, {"points": 4, "criterion": "Highlighted the specific contributions of microscopy, genetics, and DNA sequencing"}]}], "format_type": "structured_conceptual", "diagram_info": {"requires_image": false}, "marking_points": []}]}	f	\N
4343	1025	17	essay_rubric	Discuss the hierarchical nature and organizational patterns of the living world, emphasizing the structural and functional complexities ranging from the chemical and organelle levels to cellular, unicellular, and multicellular levels of biological organization.	\N	Ensure students clearly distinguish between the chemical, organelle, and cellular levels, and avoid confusing unicellular life adaptations with multicellular cellular differentiation.	40	analyze	medium	null	\N	\N	\N	null	null	null	{"structure_format": "single_complete", "structure_type": "single_complete", "stem_text": "Discuss the hierarchical nature and organizational patterns of the living world, emphasizing the structural and functional complexities ranging from the chemical and organelle levels to cellular, unicellular, and multicellular levels of biological organization.", "instruction": "", "marking_scheme": "Examiners should award marks systematically across the eight core structural components detailed in the answer points. Each complete, scientifically accurate explanation validated with appropriate biological terminology (e.g., prokaryotic vs. eukaryotic, unicellular vs. multicellular specialization, organelle functions) is worth 5.0 marks, totaling 40.0 marks. Deduct marks for generalized statements lacking specific examples or precise terminology from Unit 01.", "examiner_notes": "Ensure students clearly distinguish between the chemical, organelle, and cellular levels, and avoid confusing unicellular life adaptations with multicellular cellular differentiation.", "answer_points": [{"id": "pt_17_1", "item_number": 1, "description": "Definition of biology as the scientific study of life and living organisms, noting that it investigates organisms across a hierarchy ranging from chemical components, cells, and individuals to populations, communities, ecosystems, and the biosphere.", "marks": 5, "accepted_alternatives": "Study of life and living entities across various organizational levels from chemical to biosphere."}, {"id": "pt_17_2", "item_number": 2, "description": "Role of nucleic acids and other biomolecules at the chemical level in the storage and transmission of genetic information, serving as the foundational building blocks for all higher levels of biological organization.", "marks": 5, "accepted_alternatives": "Storage and transmission of genetic information by nucleic acids at the chemical level."}, {"id": "pt_17_3", "item_number": 3, "description": "The organelle level of organization, detailing how specialized structures within cells perform particular functions, such as mitochondria in aerobic respiration and ATP production, and the nucleus containing the primary genetic material in eukaryotic cells.", "marks": 5, "accepted_alternatives": "Specialized subcellular structures working together, including mitochondria, nucleus, and chloroplasts."}, {"id": "pt_17_4", "item_number": 4, "description": "The cellular level as the basic structural and functional unit of life, distinguishing between prokaryotic cellular organization (e.g., bacteria and archaea lacking a membrane-bound nucleus) and eukaryotic organization (e.g., animals, plants, fungi, and protists with membrane-bound nuclei and organelles).", "marks": 5, "accepted_alternatives": "Comparison of prokaryotic and eukaryotic cellular organizational patterns."}, {"id": "pt_17_5", "item_number": 5, "description": "Unicellular organization, explaining how a single cell constitutes a complete organism capable of performing all essential life functions independently, including nutrition, respiration, growth, excretion, reproduction, and response to stimuli (e.g., protozoans and many bacteria).", "marks": 5, "accepted_alternatives": "Single cell functioning as the complete organism performing all life processes."}, {"id": "pt_17_6", "item_number": 6, "description": "Multicellular organization, explaining how multiple cells aggregate and differentiate into specialized cell types such as red blood cells, neurons, muscle cells, epithelial cells, and bone cells to optimize overall physiological efficiency.", "marks": 5, "accepted_alternatives": "Specialization of diverse cell types in multicellular organisms."}, {"id": "pt_17_7", "item_number": 7, "description": "The interdisciplinary and evidence-based nature of biology, highlighting how empirical data, scientific observations, and advancements in techniques like microscopy and DNA sequencing continually shape and refine our understanding of biological organization.", "marks": 5, "accepted_alternatives": "Empirical and evidence-based nature of biological sciences with technological advancements."}, {"id": "pt_17_8", "item_number": 8, "description": "The scope of biology encompassing cellular life forms across domains (bacteria, archaea, protists, fungi, plants, animals) and acellular biological entities such as viruses, while appreciating that organelles and cells do not function independently but operate in coordinated networks.", "marks": 5, "accepted_alternatives": "Broad scope of biological diversity including cellular organisms and viruses with coordinated organelle functions."}], "criteria": [{"id": "pt_17_1", "item_number": 1, "description": "Definition of biology as the scientific study of life and living organisms, noting that it investigates organisms across a hierarchy ranging from chemical components, cells, and individuals to populations, communities, ecosystems, and the biosphere.", "marks": 5, "accepted_alternatives": "Study of life and living entities across various organizational levels from chemical to biosphere."}, {"id": "pt_17_2", "item_number": 2, "description": "Role of nucleic acids and other biomolecules at the chemical level in the storage and transmission of genetic information, serving as the foundational building blocks for all higher levels of biological organization.", "marks": 5, "accepted_alternatives": "Storage and transmission of genetic information b