4471	1025	22	multi_response_grid	In human nervous coordination,	\N	Statements A and B are correct descriptions of resting membrane potential maintenance and action potential propagation respectively. Statement C, D, and E contain inaccuracies regarding ion channel states or synaptic transmission mechanisms.	1	understand	difficult	["Option 1: Statements (A), (B), and (D) only are correct", "Option 2: Statements (A), (C), and (D) only are correct", "Option 3: Statements (A) and (B) only are correct", "Option 4: Statements (C) and (D) only are correct", "Option 5: Any other combination"]	3	\N	\N	[{"code": "A", "text": "The resting membrane potential of a neuron is maintained primarily by the sodium-potassium pump and potassium leak channels.", "is_true": true, "reason": "Sodium-potassium pump actively transports 3 Na\\u207a out and 2 K\\u207a in, while K\\u207a leak channels allow passive efflux."}, {"code": "B", "text": "During the depolarization phase of an action potential, voltage-gated Na\\u207a channels open, allowing Na\\u207a to rush into the cell.", "is_true": true, "reason": "Influx of sodium ions depolarizes the membrane, making the inside positive relative to the outside."}, {"code": "C", "text": "Saltatory conduction occurs in unmyelinated axons, increasing the velocity of impulse propagation.", "is_true": false, "reason": "Saltatory conduction occurs only in myelinated axons at the nodes of Ranvier."}, {"code": "D", "text": "Neurotransmitters are released from the postsynaptic membrane via exocytosis.", "is_true": false, "reason": "Neurotransmitters are released from the presynaptic membrane, not the postsynaptic membrane."}, {"code": "E", "text": "An inhibitory postsynaptic potential (IPSP) depolarizes the postsynaptic membrane.", "is_true": false, "reason": "IPSP hyperpolarizes the postsynaptic membrane, making it less likely to reach the threshold."}]	{"truth": {"A": true, "B": true, "C": false, "D": false, "E": false}, "true_statements": ["A", "B"], "