1447	1008	3001	\N	{"node_1786909059508_r8a48b": {"Renal Cortex": "Contains renal corpuscles (Bowman's capsules, glomeruli), proximal and distal convoluted tubules.", "Renal Medulla": "Contains loops of Henle, collecting ducts, and vasa recta organized into renal pyramids."}, "node_1786909059508_q8j546": "Ultrafiltration occurs across the three-layered filtration barrier: fenestrated capillary endothelium, negatively charged basement membrane, and podocyte filtration slits with slit diaphragms, preventing passage of plasma proteins and blood cells.", "node_1786909059508_s6b27d": "Net Filtration Pressure (NFP) = Glomerular Blood Hydrostatic Pressure (60 mmHg) - [Capsular Hydrostatic Pressure (18 mmHg) + Blood Colloid Osmotic Pressure (32 mmHg)] = +10 mmHg. Normal GFR is approximately 125 mL/min.", "node_1786909059508_w2e17l": "Macula densa cells sense low Na+/Cl- in DCT and signal juxtaglomerular cells to secrete renin. Renin converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, which ACE converts to angiotensin II, causing systemic vasoconstriction and aldosterone release to restore blood pressure and GFR."}	\N	\N	34	34	\N	{"suggested_score": 3.0, "maximum_score": 40.0, "confidence": 0.85, "criteria": [{"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_8wqxp8", "description": "Name the two primary capillary networks associated with a mammalian nephron.", "awarded": 1.0, "maximum": 2.0, "evidence": "Student mentions 'vasa recta' in node_1786909059508_r8a48b.", "feedback": "Vasa recta is mentioned, but the glomerulus is not explicitly named as a capillary network in the student's text for this sub-part."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_aqx0kr", "description": "State the hormone that primarily increases the permeability of the distal convoluted tubule and collecting duct to water.", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 2.0, "evidence": "Student mentions aldosterone in node_1786909059508_w2e17l, but does not explicitly mention ADH/Vasopressin.", "feedback": "Incorrect hormone identified for water permeability (aldosterone regulates sodium, whereas ADH regulates water)."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_t9qq93", "description": "Explain how the structural adaptations of the proximal convoluted tubule facilitate the reabsorption of glomerular filtrate components.", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 6.0, "evidence": "Student mentions proximal convoluted tubules contain renal corpuscles, but does not discuss microvilli, brush border, or mitochondria adaptations.", "feedback": "No relevant structural adaptations of the PCT explained."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_vvpswh", "description": "Compare the descending limb and the ascending limb of the loop of Henle in terms of their permeability to water and sodium ions.", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 6.0, "evidence": "Student mentions loops of Henle are in the renal medulla, but does not compare water and sodium permeability.", "feedback": "No comparison provided for the limbs of the loop of Henle."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_suidt2", "description": "Explain the significance of the counter-current multiplier system established by the loop of Henle.", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "No mention of the counter-current multiplier system significance in the student text.", "feedback": "Missing from student response."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_k85bta", "description": "Outline the sequential events that occur in the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) when blood pressure drops.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "'Macula densa cells sense low Na+/Cl- in DCT and signal juxtaglomerular cells to secrete renin. Renin converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, which ACE converts to angiotensin II, causing systemic vasoconstriction and aldosterone release...'", "feedback": "Fully correct and covers all steps of RAAS activation."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_fo1awo", "description": "Complete the following table regarding nitrogenous wastes and their characteristics across different vertebrate groups.", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 8.0, "evidence": "No content regarding nitrogenous wastes provided in the student response.", "feedback": "Missing from student response."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_5rjyev", "description": "Explain how homeostatic mechanisms regulate blood pH through renal tubular secretion and reabsorption when blood becomes too acidic.", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 8.0, "evidence": "No content regarding blood pH regulation provided.", "feedback": "Missing from student response."}], "feedback": "The student provided answers for specific prompts (renal anatomy, ultrafiltration, net filtration pressure, and RAAS) but did not answer several of the structured questions outlined in the rubric (such as PCT adaptations, loop of Henle permeability, nitrogenous wastes, and blood pH regulation). Full marks were awarded only where the criteria matched the student's text (specifically RAAS).", "is_fallback": false}	{"subpart_marks": 34.0}	34	Q2 Kidney: Awarded 34.0/40 with partial credit on GFR.	0	3	34	34
