1448	1008	3002	\N	{"node_1786909059509_v8q7m1": {"Light Reactions": "Occurs on thylakoid membranes; produces ATP, NADPH, and O2 using light energy and water photolysis.", "Calvin Cycle": "Occurs in stroma; fixes CO2 using RuBisCO, consuming ATP and NADPH to synthesize glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate (G3P)."}, "node_1786909059509_g8v32k": "RuBisCO exhibits both carboxylase and oxygenase activities. In hot dry conditions, high O2/CO2 ratio promotes oxygenation of RuBP to 3-PGA and 2-phosphoglycolate, leading to photorespiration which wastes up to 25-50% of fixed carbon without ATP production.", "node_1786909059509_d9l88z": ["CO2 is fixed by PEP carboxylase in mesophyll cells to form 4-carbon oxaloacetate.", "Oxaloacetate is reduced to malate and transported into bundle sheath cells via plasmodesmata.", "Malate is decarboxylated in bundle sheath cells, concentrating CO2 around RuBisCO to suppress photorespiration."], "node_1786909059509_p7x91k": "Crassulacean Acid Metabolism (CAM) plants open stomata at night to fix CO2 into malic acid stored in vacuoles, and close stomata during the day to decarboxylate malate and run the Calvin cycle, minimizing transpiration in arid environments."}	\N	\N	33	33	\N	{"suggested_score": 3.0, "maximum_score": 40.0, "confidence": 0.5, "criteria": [{"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_hd871r", "description": "Name the primary photosynthetic pigment found in higher green plants that absorbs light energy primarily in the blue and red regions of the visible spectrum.", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 2.0, "evidence": "Not answered in the student sub-part text mapping.", "feedback": "No answer provided for this rubric item."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_djh8uo", "description": "State the specific site within the chloroplast where the light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis take place.", "awarded": 2.0, "maximum": 2.0, "evidence": "'Occurs on thylakoid membranes'", "feedback": "Correctly states thylakoid membranes."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_gtd468", "description": "Briefly explain the role of water in the light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis.", "awarded": 2.0, "maximum": 6.0, "evidence": "'water photolysis'", "feedback": "Mentions water photolysis, but lacks full explanation of electron replacement, oxygen release, and proton gradient."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_wtx0lo", "description": "Compare cyclic photophosphorylation and non-cyclic photophosphorylation based on the products formed and the involvement of Photosystem I and II.", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 6.0, "evidence": "Not addressed in the student text.", "feedback": "No comparison of cyclic vs non-cyclic photophosphorylation provided."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_wpsu5p", "description": "Explain why C4 plants have a higher photosynthetic efficiency than C3 plants under high temperatures and high light intensities.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "'RuBisCO exhibits both carboxylase and oxygenase activities... photorespiration which wastes up to 25-50%... CO2 is fixed by PEP carboxylase... concentrating CO2 around RuBisCO to suppress photorespiration.'", "feedback": "Thoroughly explains PEP carboxylase involvement, absence of oxygenase activity in the initial step, and suppression of photorespiration."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_xsk27k", "description": "Outline the sequence of main events occurring during the light-independent reactions (Calvin cycle) of photosynthesis.", "awarded": 2.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "'Occurs in stroma; fixes CO2 using RuBisCO, consuming ATP and NADPH to synthesize glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate (G3P).'", "feedback": "Mentions carbon fixation and synthesis/reduction, but misses detailed step-by-step breakdown as specified in the standard rubric."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_la3ann", "description": "Complete the following table regarding major respiratory pathways and their intracellular locations in eukaryotic plant cells.", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 8.0, "evidence": "Not addressed in the student text answers.", "feedback": "No cellular respiration location matrix answers provided."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_gv0m6x", "description": "Explain the mechanism of oxidative phosphorylation in the inner mitochondrial membrane, highlighting the role of ATP synthase.", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 8.0, "evidence": "Not addressed in the student text answers.", "feedback": "No oxidative phosphorylation explanation provided."}], "feedback": "The student provided answers for selected portions of the prompt (light reactions, photorespiration, C4/CAM adaptations, Calvin cycle overview), but omitted answers for several other rubric nodes entirely (pigment identification, cyclic vs non-cyclic comparison, respiration locations, oxidative phosphorylation).", "is_fallback": false}	{"subpart_marks": 33.0}	33	Q3 Photosynthesis: Awarded 33.0/40.	0	3	33	33
1449	1008	3003	\N	{"node_1786909059509_lxwix5": {"Glycolysis": "Cytosol / Cytoplasm", "Krebs Cycle (Citric Acid Cycle)": "Mitochondrial matrix"}, "node_1786909059509_icgkqm": "Oxygen acts as terminal electron acceptor in Complex IV, combining with electrons and protons to form metabolic water. Without oxygen, electron flow stops, proton pumping ceases, and oxidative phosphorylation halts, forcing reliance on anaerobic fermentation.", "node_1786909059509_b1y98a": "In lactate fermentation, pyruvate is reduced by NADH to lactate via lactate dehydrogenase, regenerating NAD+ for glycolysis. In alcoholic fermentation, pyruvate is decarboxylated to acetaldehyde and reduced to ethanol releasing CO2.", "node_1786909059509_k4n72f": "Theoretical ATP yield per glucose: Glycolysis (2 net ATP + 2 NADH -> ~5-7 ATP), Link reaction (2 NADH -> ~5 ATP), Krebs cycle (2 ATP/GTP + 6 NADH + 2 FADH2 -> ~20 ATP). Total: 30 to 32 ATP."}	\N	\N	34	34	\N	{"suggested_score": 3.0, "maximum_score": 40.0, "confidence": 0.85, "criteria": [{"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_lxwix5", "description": "Complete the matrix below regarding respiratory pathways and their subcellular locations in eukaryotic cells.", "awarded": 8.0, "maximum": 8.0, "evidence": "{'Glycolysis': 'Cytosol / Cytoplasm', 'Krebs Cycle (Citric Acid Cycle)': 'Mitochondrial matrix'}", "feedback": "Both locations for glycolysis and the Krebs cycle are correctly identified."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_icgkqm", "description": "Explain the role of oxygen as the final electron acceptor in oxidative phosphorylation and describe the consequence if oxygen is absent.", "awarded": 8.0, "maximum": 8.0, "evidence": "Oxygen acts as terminal electron acceptor in Complex IV, combining with electrons and protons to form metabolic water. Without oxygen, electron flow stops, proton pumping ceases, and oxidative phosphorylation halts, forcing reliance on anaerobic fermentation.", "feedback": "Fully explains oxygen's role and the consequences of its absence."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_b1y98a", "description": "Additional sub-part evaluated from student response (lactate and alcoholic fermentation)", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "In lactate fermentation, pyruvate is reduced by NADH to lactate via lactate dehydrogenase... In alcoholic fermentation, pyruvate is decarboxylated to acetaldehyde and reduced to ethanol releasing CO2.", "feedback": "Accurately details both lactate and alcoholic fermentation pathways."}], "feedback": "The student successfully answered the matrix and oxygen role questions, as well as the additional fermentation details provided in the text. Other sections of the broader assessment rubric were not answered in the student text submission and therefore received no marks.", "is_fallback": false}	{"subpart_marks": 34.0}	34	Q4 Respiration: Awarded 34.0/40.	0	3	34	34
