1332	1002	3000	\N	{"node_1786909059508_o4p02n": {"Glomerulus": "Ultrafiltration of blood plasma under high hydrostatic pressure into Bowman's capsule.", "Proximal Convoluted Tubule": "Obligatory reabsorption of 100% glucose, amino acids, and ~70% of water and sodium ions.", "Loop of Henle": "Generation of hypertonic osmotic gradient in the medullary interstitium via counter-current multiplication.", "Distal Convoluted Tubule": "Facultative reabsorption of water under ADH and sodium reabsorption stimulated by aldosterone."}, "node_1786909059508_m2k1sp": ["Descending limb is water-permeable leading to passive water efflux into hypertonic interstitium.", "Filtrate reaches maximum concentration at hairpin bend.", "Thick ascending limb actively transports Na+ and Cl- out into medullary interstitium while impermeable to water.", "Continuous opposing flow establishes a multiplying vertical osmotic gradient from cortex (300 mOsm) to inner medulla (1200 mOsm)."], "node_1786909059508_5b3q1z": "When blood osmolarity increases, hypothalamic osmoreceptors trigger posterior pituitary to secrete ADH. ADH binds V2 receptors on collecting duct principal cells, causing aquaporin-2 vesicle exocytosis into the apical membrane. Water reabsorption increases, producing concentrated hypertonic urine and restoring plasma osmolarity.", "node_1786909059508_073q6c": "In chronic kidney disease of unknown etiology (CKDu), tubulointerstitial damage reduces the medullary osmotic gradient. Inulin or creatinine clearance tests measure GFR: Clearance = (U_cr * V) / P_cr. Progressive drop below 60 mL/min/1.73m\\u00b2 indicates renal failure requiring hemodialysis or transplantation."}	\N	\N	38	38	\N	{"suggested_score": 3.0, "maximum_score": 40.0, "confidence": 0.85, "criteria": [{"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_b6hlnc", "description": "Name the specialized epithelial cells that wrap around the glomerular capillaries, forming filtration slits in the renal corpuscle.", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 2.0, "evidence": "Not answered directly in the student text for this specific prompt.", "feedback": "The student did not mention podocytes by name in relation to this prompt."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_ri4yew", "description": "State the primary hormone secreted by Anu/JGA.", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 2.0, "evidence": "Not present in student text.", "feedback": "Student text omits mentioning renin secretion by the JGA in response to blood pressure drop."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_jn1q1m", "description": "Explain how the structural adaptations of the proximal convoluted tubule facilitate the reabsorption.", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 6.0, "evidence": "Student mentions 'Obligatory reabsorption of 100% glucose...' under node_1786909059505_o4p02n, but does not explain structural adaptations like microvilli and mitochondria here.", "feedback": "Lacks description of brush border microvilli and abundant mitochondria."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_0lbmpc", "description": "Compare the structural and permeability characteristics of the descending limb and the ascending limb of the loop of Henle.", "awarded": 6.0, "maximum": 6.0, "evidence": "Descending limb is water-permeable leading to passive water efflux; Thick ascending limb actively transports Na+ and Cl- out into medullary interstitium while impermeable to water.", "feedback": "Fully correct descriptions of both descending and ascending limb properties."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_yp4m3p", "description": "Explain the role of the vasa recta in maintaining the medullary osmotic gradient without washing it away.", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "Not addressed in the student's submission.", "feedback": "No mention of the vasa recta or counter-current exchange."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_dj3eme", "description": "Outline the sequential events that occur in the collecting duct when the body is dehydrated.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "When blood osmolarity increases, hypothalamic osmoreceptors trigger posterior pituitary to secrete ADH. ADH binds V2 receptors... causing aquaporin-2 vesicle exocytosis... Water reabsorption increases, producing concentrated hypertonic urine.", "feedback": "Accurately outlines ADH release and aquaporin insertion leading to water reabsorption."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_xrd9ta", "description": "Complete the following matrix matching renal segments with their primary physiological function.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 8.0, "evidence": "Proximal Convoluted Tubule: 'Obligatory reabsorption of 100% glucose, amino acids, and ~70% of water and sodium ions.' Distal Convoluted Tubule: 'Facultative reabsorption of water under ADH and sodium reabsorption stimulated by aldosterone.'", "feedback": "Good match for PCT. DCT partially aligns with aldosterone-dependent reabsorption, though potassium/hydrogen ion secretion details are absent."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_tqoddl", "description": "Discuss how the kidneys compensate for prolonged metabolic acidosis in the human body.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 8.0, "evidence": "Student briefly touches upon clearance and tubulointerstitial damage in chronic kidney disease, but does not discuss metabolic acidosis compensation mechanisms directly.", "feedback": "Incomplete addressing of metabolic acidosis compensation; student discussed CKD instead."}], "feedback": "The student showed strong knowledge regarding the loop of Henle permeability, collecting duct ADH/aquaporin response, and proximal tubule bulk reabsorption. However, several sections were left unanswered or substituted with unrelated information (such as CKD instead of metabolic acidosis).", "is_fallback": false}	{"subpart_marks": 38.0}	38	Teacher verified: strong grasp of anatomical concepts.	0	3	38	38
