1391	1005	3002	\N	{"node_1786909059509_v8q7m1": {"Light Reactions": "Occurs on thylakoid membranes; produces ATP, NADPH, and O2 using light energy and water photolysis.", "Calvin Cycle": "Occurs in stroma; fixes CO2 using RuBisCO, consuming ATP and NADPH to synthesize glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate (G3P)."}, "node_1786909059509_g8v32k": "RuBisCO exhibits both carboxylase and oxygenase activities. In hot dry conditions, high O2/CO2 ratio promotes oxygenation of RuBP to 3-PGA and 2-phosphoglycolate, leading to photorespiration which wastes up to 25-50% of fixed carbon without ATP production.", "node_1786909059509_d9l88z": ["CO2 is fixed by PEP carboxylase in mesophyll cells to form 4-carbon oxaloacetate.", "Oxaloacetate is reduced to malate and transported into bundle sheath cells via plasmodesmata.", "Malate is decarboxylated in bundle sheath cells, concentrating CO2 around RuBisCO to suppress photorespiration."], "node_1786909059509_p7x91k": "Crassulacean Acid Metabolism (CAM) plants open stomata at night to fix CO2 into malic acid stored in vacuoles, and close stomata during the day to decarboxylate malate and run the Calvin cycle, minimizing transpiration in arid environments."}	\N	\N	34	34	\N	{"suggested_score": 3.0, "maximum_score": 40.0, "confidence": 0.85, "criteria": [{"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_hd871r", "description": "Name the primary photosynthetic pigment found in higher green plants that absorbs light energy primarily in the blue and red regions of the visible spectrum.", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 2.0, "evidence": "Student text answers provided do not include this prompt.", "feedback": "No answer provided for this sub-part."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_djh8uo", "description": "State the specific site within the chloroplast where the light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis take place.", "awarded": 2.0, "maximum": 2.0, "evidence": "'Occurs on thylakoid membranes'", "feedback": "Correctly states the thylakoid membrane."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_gtd468", "description": "Briefly explain the role of water in the light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis.", "awarded": 2.0, "maximum": 6.0, "evidence": "'using light energy and water photolysis... produces ATP, NADPH, and O2'", "feedback": "Mentions photolysis and oxygen release, but lacks explicit detail on replacing electrons for Photosystem II and contributing protons for the ATP gradient."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_wtx0lo", "description": "Compare cyclic photophosphorylation and non-cyclic photophosphorylation based on the products formed and the involvement of Photosystem I and II.", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 6.0, "evidence": "Student text answers do not contain a direct comparison of cyclic vs. non-cyclic photophosphorylation.", "feedback": "No answer provided for this specific sub-part."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_wpsu5p", "description": "Explain why C4 plants have a higher photosynthetic efficiency than C3 plants under high temperatures and high light intensities.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "'CO2 is fixed by PEP carboxylase in mesophyll cells... Malate is decarboxylated in bundle sheath cells, concentrating CO2 around RuBisCO to suppress photorespiration.'", "feedback": "Accurately explains the role of PEP carboxylase and the suppression/avoidance of photorespiration."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_xsk27k", "description": "Outline the sequence of main events occurring during the light-independent reactions (Calvin cycle) of photosynthesis.", "awarded": 2.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "'Occurs in stroma; fixes CO2 using RuBisCO, consuming ATP and NADPH to synthesize glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate (G3P).'", "feedback": "Mentions carbon fixation and synthesis/reduction to G3P, but lacks full sequencing detail including PGA formation and RuBP regeneration."}, {"criterion_id": "node_1786909059509_la3ann", "description": "Complete the following table regarding major respiratory pathways