1786	1027	3004	\N	null	Nerve impulses travel along neurons through electrical changes. The resting potential is -70 mV maintained by the Na/K pump. When stimulated, sodium enters the axon making it positive, and then potassium leaves making it negative again.\n\nMyelinated nerves have myelin sheaths with gaps called Nodes of Ranvier. The impulse jumps between nodes which is faster.\n\nAt the synapse, the electrical impulse causes chemical transmitters to be released across the gap. The neurotransmitter binds to receptors on the next cell to pass the signal, and is then broken down by enzymes.	\N	30	30	\N	{"suggested_score": 40.0, "maximum_score": 40.0, "confidence": 0.7, "criteria": [{"criterion_id": "1", "description": "Resting membrane potential is maintained at approximately -70 mV by the sodium-potassium pump (Na\\u207a/K\\u207a ATPase), which actively transports 3 Na\\u207a ions out and 2 K\\u207a ions in, alongside leaky K\\u207a channels.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "Text search match: True", "feedback": "Rule-engine key concept evaluation fallback"}, {"criterion_id": "2", "description": "Depolarization phase: A threshold stimulus opens voltage-gated Na\\u207a channels, causing rapid influx of Na\\u207a ions down their electrochemical gradient, reversing the membrane potential to about +30 mV.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "Text search match: True", "feedback": "Rule-engine key concept evaluation fallback"}, {"criterion_id": "3", "description": "Repolarization phase: Voltage-gated Na\\u207a channels close and voltage-gated K\\u207a channels open, allowing rapid efflux of K\\u207a ions out of the axoplasm to restore the negative internal resting potential.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "Text search match: True", "feedback": "Rule-engine key concept evaluation fallback"}, {"criterion_id": "4", "description": "Hyperpolarization (undershoot): Delayed closing of voltage-gated K\\u207a channels causes an excess efflux of K\\u207a, making the membrane potential more negative than the resting state temporarily.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "Text search match: True", "feedback": "Rule-engine key concept evaluation fallback"}, {"criterion_id": "5", "description": "Saltatory conduction in myelinated axons: Myelin sheath acts as an electrical insulator; local current circuits jump from one Node of Ranvier to the next, drastically increasing the speed of impulse propagation.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "Text search match: True", "feedback": "Rule-engine key concept evaluation fallback"}, {"criterion_id": "6", "description": "Arrival of the action potential at the presynaptic axon terminal depolarizes the presynaptic membrane, opening voltage-gated calcium channels and causing Ca\\u00b2\\u207a influx into the terminal.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "Text search match: True", "feedback": "Rule-engine key concept evaluation fallback"}, {"criterion_id": "7", "description": "Vesicular exocytosis: Influx of Ca\\u00b2\\u207a ions causes synaptic vesicles containing neurotransmitters (e.g., acetylcholine) to fuse with the presynaptic membrane, releasing neurotransmitter into the synaptic cleft.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "Text search match: True", "feedback": "Rule-engine key concept evaluation fallback"}, {"criterion_id": "8", "description": "Neurotransmitter binds to specific ligand-gated ion channels on the postsynaptic membrane, inducing a conformational change that alters membrane permeability.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "Text search match: True", "feedback": "Rule-engine key concept evaluation fallback"}, {"criterion_id": "9", "description": "Generation of graded potentials: Influx of Na\\u207a or Ca\\u00b2\\u207a produces an Excitatory Postsynaptic Potential (EPSP), while Cl\\u207b influx or K\\u207a efflux produces an Inhibitory Postsynaptic Potential (IPSP).", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "ev