ls on the postsynaptic membrane, inducing a conformational change that alters membrane permeability.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "Acetylcholine diffuses across the cleft and binds to specific receptors on the postsynaptic membrane, opening sodium channels...", "feedback": "Describes acetylcholine diffusion and binding to specific receptors on the postsynaptic membrane to open channels."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_9", "description": "Generation of graded potentials: Influx of Na\\u207a or Ca\\u00b2\\u207a produces an Excitatory Postsynaptic Potential (EPSP), while Cl\\u207b influx or K\\u207a efflux produces an Inhibitory Postsynaptic Potential (IPSP).", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "...opening sodium channels to create an excitatory postsynaptic potential (EPSP).", "feedback": "Successfully describes the creation of an EPSP via sodium channel opening (fully satisfying the alternative acceptance or standard description criteria)."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_10", "description": "Termination of signal: Neurotransmitters in the synaptic cleft are rapidly degraded by specific enzymes (e.g., acetylcholinesterase) or reabsorbed by the presynaptic terminal via reuptake mechanisms.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "The signal is stopped by acetylcholinesterase breaking down acetylcholine.", "feedback": "Accurately notes the termination of the signal through enzymatic breakdown by acetylcholinesterase."}], "feedback": "The student essay is exceptionally well-written, comprehensive, and accurately hits all ten core grading points with precise neurobiological terminology, proper ion references, and correct mechanisms for both the action potential and chemical synaptic transmission.", "is_fallback": false}	[{"item": 1, "description": "Resting Membrane Potential", "awarded": true, "points": 8.0}, {"item": 2, "description": "Depolarization & Repolarization", "awarded": true, "points": 10.0}, {"item": 3, "description": "Saltatory Conduction