1394	1006	3005	\N	null	### Bioenergetics: Photophosphorylation and Mitochondrial Oxidative Phosphorylation\n\n#### 1. Non-Cyclic Photophosphorylation\nNon-cyclic photophosphorylation occurs on the thylakoid membranes of chloroplasts and involves both Photosystem II (P680) and Photosystem I (P700) working in series (Z-scheme). Photons absorbed by antenna complexes in PS II excite P680 reaction center electrons, which are transferred to a primary electron acceptor (pheophytin). The electron deficit in P680 is replenished by the photolysis of water at the oxygen-evolving complex:\n2 H₂O → 4 H⁺ + 4 e⁻ + O₂.\n\nElectrons flow down an electron transport chain consisting of plastoquinone (PQ), cytochrome b₆f complex, and plastocyanin (PC) to PS I. Cytochrome b₆f pumps protons into the thylakoid lumen, generating a proton motive force. Protons diffuse down their electrochemical gradient into the stroma through ATP synthase, synthesizing ATP via photophosphorylation. Simultaneously, photons absorbed by PS I excite P700 electrons, which travel via ferredoxin to ferredoxin-NADP⁺ reductase (FNR), reducing NADP⁺ to NADPH. Thus, non-cyclic photophosphorylation produces ATP, NADPH, and O₂.\n\n#### 2. Comparison: Cyclic vs Non-Cyclic Photophosphorylation\n- **Photosystems involved**: Non-cyclic utilizes both PS II and PS I; cyclic utilizes only PS I.\n- **Photolysis of water & oxygen evolution**: Water is split and O₂ is evolved in non-cyclic; neither occurs in cyclic.\n- **Terminal electron acceptor**: NADP⁺ receives electrons in non-cyclic; in cyclic, electrons return from ferredoxin back to the cytochrome b₆f complex.\n- **End products**: Non-cyclic yields ATP, NADPH, and O₂; cyclic yields only ATP (used to meet the excess ATP demand of the Calvin cycle and C4/CAM pathways).\n\n#### 3. Mitochondrial Electron Transport and Oxidative Phosphorylation\nIn the inner mitochondrial membrane, high-energy electrons donated by NADH (at Complex I) and FADH₂ (at Complex II) pass sequentially through ubiquinone (CoQ), Complex III (cytochrome bc₁), cytochrome c, and Complex IV (cytochrome c oxidase) to molecular oxygen, the terminal electron acceptor, forming H₂O.\nFree energy released during electron transfers drives proton pumping from the matrix across the inner membrane into the intermembrane space at Complexes I, III, and IV. The resulting proton motive force drives protons back into the matrix through the F₀F₁ ATP synthase complex (chemiosmosis), catalyzing the phosphorylation of ADP + Pi to ATP, generating the bulk of cellular energy.	\N	36	36	\N	{"suggested_score": 40.0, "maximum_score": 40.0, "confidence": 0.98, "criteria": [{"criterion_id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_gnxx0_1", "description": "Light energy is absorbed by Photosystem II (P680), exciting electrons to a higher energy state from where they are accepted by a primary electron acceptor.", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "Photons absorbed by antenna complexes in PS II excite P680 reaction center electrons, which are transferred to a primary electron acceptor (pheophytin).", "feedback": "Accurately described light excitation of P680 reaction center electrons in PS II and their transfer to a primary electron acceptor."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_gnxx0_2", "description": "Photolysis of water occurs at the oxygen-evolving complex associated with PS II, releasing electrons to replace those lost by P680, along with protons (H+) and oxygen gas as a byproduct.", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "The electron deficit in P680 is replenished by the photolysis of water at the oxygen-evolving complex: 2 H\\u2082O \\u2192 4 H\\u207a + 4 e\\u207b + O\\u2082.", "feedback": "Clearly explained photolysis of water, oxygen evolution, and replenishment of electrons for P680."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_gnxx0_3", "description": "Electrons pass down an electron transport chain to Photosystem I (P700), generating ATP via chemiosmosis, and subsequently reduce NADP+ to NADPH catalyzed by ferredoxin-NADP+ reductase.", "awarded": 3.4, "maximum": 3.4, "evidence": "Electrons flow down an electron transport chain consisting of plastoquinone (PQ), cytochrome b\\u2086f complex, and plastocyanin (PC) to PS I... synthesizing ATP via photophosphorylation. Simultaneously, photons absorbed by PS I excite P700 electrons, which travel via ferredoxin to ferredoxin-NADP\\u207a reductase (FNR), reducing NADP\\u207a to NADPH.", "feedback": "Thoroughly detailed electron transport through intermediates, chemiosmotic ATP synthesis, and reduction of NADP+ to NADPH by FNR."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_wtrbb_1", "description": "Comparison based on involvement of photosystems: Non-cyclic involves both Photosystem II and Photosystem I, whereas cyclic photophosphorylation involves only Photosystem I.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "Non-cyclic utilizes both PS II and PS I; cyclic utilizes only PS I.", "feedback": "Correctly differentiated the photosystems involved in non-cyclic versus cyclic photophosphorylation."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_wtrbb_2", "description": "Comparison based on photolysis of water and oxygen production: Water splitting and O\\u2082 evolution occur in non-cyclic photophosphorylation, but neither occurs in cyclic photophosphorylation.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "Water is split and O\\u2082 is evolved in non-cyclic; neither occurs in cyclic.", "feedback": "Correctly identified that photolysis of water and oxygen evolution occur only in non-cyclic photophosphorylation."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_wtrbb_3", "description": "Comparison based on end products: Non-cyclic produces ATP, NADPH, and O\\u2082; cyclic produces only ATP without forming NADPH or releasing oxygen.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "Non-cyclic yields ATP, NADPH, and O\\u2082; cyclic yields only ATP (used to meet the excess ATP demand of the Calvin cycle and C4/CAM pathways).", "feedback": "Accurately contrasted the products generated by both pathways."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_9cdov_1", "description": "NADH and FADH\\u2082 donate high-energy electrons to protein complexes embedded in the inner mitochondrial membrane, passing them sequentially down the electron transport chain to molecular oxygen as the final electron acceptor.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "high-energy electrons donated by NADH (at Complex I) and FADH\\u2082 (at Complex II) pass sequentially through ubiquinone (CoQ), Complex III (cytochrome bc\\u2081), cytochrome c, and Complex IV (cytochrome c oxidase) to molecular oxygen, the terminal electron acceptor, forming H\\u2082O.", "feedback": "Excellent detailed description of electron donation from coenzymes and sequential transport to oxygen."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_9cdov_2", "description": "As electrons move through the complexes, energy released is used to pump protons (H+) from the mitochondrial matrix into the intermembrane space, establishing a steep electrochemical proton gradient.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "Free energy released during electron transfers drives proton pumping from the matrix across the inner membrane into the intermembrane space at Complexes I, III, and IV. The resulting proton motive force...", "feedback": "Clear explanation of proton translocation across the inner mitochondrial membrane and proton motive force generation."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_9cdov_3", "description": "Protons flow back into the matrix down their electrochemical gradient through ATP synthase complexes (chemiosmosis), driving the phosphorylation of ADP to yield the majority of cellular ATP.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "drives protons back into the matrix through the F\\u2080F\\u2081 ATP synthase complex (chemiosmosis), catalyzing the phosphorylation of ADP + Pi to ATP, generating the bulk of cellular energy.", "feedback": "Clearly explained ATP synthesis driven by chemiosmosis and proton return through ATP synthase."}], "feedback": "The response is comprehensive, highly accurate, and directly satisfies all parts of the rubric with precise biochemical terminology.", "is_fallback": false}	[{"item": 1, "description": "Non-Cyclic Photophosphorylation", "awarded": true, "points": 15.0}, {"item": 2, "description": "Cyclic vs Non-Cyclic Comparison", "awarded": true, "points": 10.0}, {"item": 3, "description": "Mitochondrial Chemiosmosis", "awarded": true, "points": 15.0}]	36	Essay 2 Bioenergetics: Teacher awarded 36.0/40.	0	40	36	36
