1450	1009	3004	\N	null	### Generation and Propagation of Nerve Impulses and Synaptic Transmission\n\nThe resting membrane potential of a neuron is typically -70 mV, maintained by the sodium-potassium pump (pumping 3 Na⁺ out for every 2 K⁺ in using ATP) and leaky potassium channels.\n\nWhen a stimulus reaches the threshold potential, voltage-gated sodium channels open rapidly, allowing Na⁺ ions to enter the axon. This causes depolarization, reversing the membrane potential to +30 mV. Then sodium channels close and voltage-gated potassium channels open, allowing K⁺ ions to rush out of the cell. This repolarizes the membrane. A temporary hyperpolarization occurs due to slow potassium channel closure before resting potential returns.\n\nIn myelinated neurons, Schwann cells form a myelin sheath acting as an electrical insulator. Voltage-gated channels are concentrated at the Nodes of Ranvier. The action potential jumps from one node to the next in saltatory conduction, which greatly increases impulse speed and saves cellular energy.\n\nWhen the action potential arrives at the synaptic terminal, it opens voltage-gated calcium channels. Ca²⁺ ions enter the terminal, stimulating synaptic vesicles to fuse with the presynaptic membrane and release acetylcholine into the synaptic cleft by exocytosis. Acetylcholine diffuses across the cleft and binds to specific receptors on the postsynaptic membrane, opening sodium channels to create an excitatory postsynaptic potential (EPSP). The signal is stopped by acetylcholinesterase breaking down acetylcholine.	\N	33	33	\N	{"suggested_score": 40.0, "maximum_score": 40.0, "confidence": 0.95, "criteria": [{"criterion_id": "pt_1_1", "description": "Resting membrane potential is maintained at approximately -70 mV by the sodium-potassium pump (Na\\u207a/K\\u207a ATPase), which actively transports 3 Na\\u207a ions out and 2 K\\u207a ions in, alongside leaky K\\u207a channels.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "The resting membrane potential of a neuron is typically -70 mV, maintained by the sodium-potassium pump (pumping 3 Na\\u207a out for every 2 K\\u207a in using ATP) and leaky potassium channels.", "feedback": "Fully covered with accurate ion counts and terminology."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_2", "description": "Depolarization phase: A threshold stimulus opens voltage-gated Na\\u207a channels, causing rapid influx of Na\\u207a ions down their electrochemical gradient, reversing the membrane potential to about +30 mV.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "When a stimulus reaches the threshold potential, voltage-gated sodium channels open rapidly, allowing Na\\u207a ions to enter the axon. This causes depolarization, reversing the membrane potential to +30 mV.", "feedback": "Clearly describes threshold, voltage-gated sodium channels, influx, and reversal to +30 mV."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_3", "description": "Repolarization phase: Voltage-gated Na\\u207a channels close and voltage-gated K\\u207a channels open, allowing rapid efflux of K\\u207a ions out of the axoplasm to restore the negative internal resting potential.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "Then sodium channels close and voltage-gated potassium channels open, allowing K\\u207a ions to rush out of the cell. This repolarizes the membrane.", "feedback": "Accurately describes channel state changes and potassium efflux for repolarization."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_4", "description": "Hyperpolarization (undershoot): Delayed closing of voltage-gated K\\u207a channels causes an excess efflux of K\\u207a, making the membrane potential more negative than the resting state temporarily.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "A temporary hyperpolarization occurs due to slow potassium channel closure before resting potential returns.", "feedback": "Explicitly mentions temporary hyperpolarization due to slow potassium channel closure."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_5", "description": "Saltatory conduction in myelinated axons: Myelin sheath acts as an electrical insulator; local current circuits jump from one Node of Ranvier to the next, drastically increasing the speed of impulse propagation.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "In myelinated neurons, Schwann cells form a myelin sheath acting as an electrical insulator. Voltage-gated channels are concentrated at the Nodes of Ranvier. The action potential jumps from one node to the next in saltatory conduction, which greatly increases impulse speed and saves cellular energy.", "feedback": "Thoroughly covers myelin insulation, Nodes of Ranvier, and saltatory conduction."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_6", "description": "Arrival of the action potential at the presynaptic axon terminal depolarizes the presynaptic membrane, opening voltage-gated calcium channels and causing Ca\\u00b2\\u207a influx into the terminal.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "When the action potential arrives at the synaptic terminal, it opens voltage-gated calcium channels. Ca\\u00b2\\u207a ions enter the terminal...", "feedback": "Accurately describes action potential arrival, voltage-gated calcium channels, and calcium influx."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_7", "description": "Vesicular exocytosis: Influx of Ca\\u00b2\\u207a ions causes synaptic vesicles containing neurotransmitters (e.g., acetylcholine) to fuse with the presynaptic membrane, releasing neurotransmitter into the synaptic cleft.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "...stimulating synaptic vesicles to fuse with the presynaptic membrane and release acetylcholine into the synaptic cleft by exocytosis.", "feedback": "Correctly identifies vesicular fusion, exocytosis, and acetylcholine release into the cleft."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_8", "description": "Neurotransmitter binds to specific ligand-gated ion channels on the postsynaptic membrane, inducing a conformational change that alters membrane permeability.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "Acetylcholine diffuses across the cleft and binds to specific receptors on the postsynaptic membrane, opening sodium channels...", "feedback": "Describes neurotransmitter binding to postsynaptic receptors and opening of channels."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_9", "description": "Generation of graded potentials: Influx of Na\\u207a or Ca\\u00b2\\u207a produces an Excitatory Postsynaptic Potential (EPSP), while Cl\\u207b influx or K\\u207a efflux produces an Inhibitory Postsynaptic Potential (IPSP).", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "...opening sodium channels to create an excitatory postsynaptic potential (EPSP).", "feedback": "Covers EPSP creation via sodium channel opening as required by the marking scheme."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_10", "description": "Termination of signal: Neurotransmitters in the synaptic cleft are rapidly degraded by specific enzymes (e.g., acetylcholinesterase) or reabsorbed by the presynaptic terminal via reuptake mechanisms.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "The signal is stopped by acetylcholinesterase breaking down acetylcholine.", "feedback": "Accurately identifies signal termination via acetylcholinesterase enzymatic breakdown."}], "feedback": "The student has provided an exceptionally comprehensive, accurate, and well-structured response that addresses all ten marking criteria with precise biological terminology and concepts.", "is_fallback": false}	[{"item": 1, "description": "Resting Membrane Potential", "awarded": true, "points": 8.0}, {"item": 2, "description": "Depolarization & Repolarization", "awarded": true, "points": 10.0}, {"item": 3, "description": "Saltatory Conduction", "awarded": true, "points": 10.0}, {"item": 4, "description": "Chemical Synapse Transmission", "awarded": true, "points": 12.0}]	33	Essay 1 Action Potential: Teacher awarded 33.0/40.	0	40	33	33
1508	1012	3005	\N	null	Photosynthesis and cellular respiration are the central bioenergetic processes in living systems.\n\nIn non-cyclic photophosphorylation, light is absorbed by PS II (P680) exciting electrons. Water is split by photolysis into oxygen, protons, and replacement electrons. The electrons move through an electron transport chain (cytochrome complex) to PS I (P700), pumping protons to make ATP via ATP synthase. At PS I, electrons are re-excited and reduce NADP⁺ to NADPH.\n\nComparing cyclic and non-cyclic photophosphorylation:\n- Non-cyclic uses both PS I and PS II, whereas cyclic uses only PS I.\n- Non-cyclic splits water and produces O₂, while cyclic does neither.\n- Non-cyclic produces ATP, NADPH, and O₂, whereas cyclic produces only ATP.\n\nIn mitochondria, oxidative phosphorylation occurs on the cristae. NADH and FADH₂ donate electrons to electron transport complexes. As electrons flow to oxygen (terminal acceptor making water), protons are pumped into the intermembrane space. Protons return through ATP synthase, producing ATP by chemiosmosis.	\N	29	29	\N	{"suggested_score": 40.0, "maximum_score": 40.0, "confidence": 0.98, "criteria": [{"criterion_id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_gnxx0_1", "description": "Light energy is absorbed by Photosystem II (P680), exciting electrons to a higher energy state from where they are accepted by a primary electron acceptor.", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "In non-cyclic photophosphorylation, light is absorbed by PS II (P680) exciting electrons.", "feedback": "Correctly states light absorption by PS II (P680) and excitation of electrons."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_gnxx0_2", "description": "Photolysis of water occurs at the oxygen-evolving complex associated with PS II, releasing electrons to replace those lost by P680, along with protons (H+) and oxygen gas as a byproduct.", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "Water is split by photolysis into oxygen, protons, and replacement electrons.", "feedback": "Accurately describes photolysis of water yielding oxygen, protons, and replacement electrons."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_gnxx0_3", "description": "Electrons pass down an electron transport chain to Photosystem I (P700), generating ATP via chemiosmosis, and subsequently reduce NADP+ to NADPH catalyzed by ferredoxin-NADP+ reductase.", "awarded": 3.4, "maximum": 3.4, "evidence": "The electrons move through an electron transport chain (cytochrome complex) to PS I (P700), pumping protons to make ATP via ATP synthase. At PS I, electrons are re-excited and reduce NADP\\u207a to NADPH.", "feedback": "Clearly outlines electron transport to PS I, ATP generation via proton pumping/ATP synthase, and reduction of NADP+ to NADPH."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_wtrbb_1", "description": "Comparison based on involvement of photosystems: Non-cyclic involves both Photosystem II and Photosystem I, whereas cyclic photophosphorylation involves only Photosystem I.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "Non-cyclic uses both PS I and PS II, whereas cyclic uses only PS I.", "feedback": "Correctly differentiates the photosystems utilized in cyclic versus non-cyclic photophosphorylation."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_wtrbb_2", "description": "Comparison based on photolysis of water and oxygen production: Water splitting and O\\u2082 evolution occur in non-cyclic photophosphorylation, but neither occurs in cyclic photophosphorylation.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "Non-cyclic splits water and produces O\\u2082, while cyclic does neither.", "feedback": "Correctly notes that photolysis and oxygen evolution occur only in non-cyclic photophosphorylation."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_wtrbb_3", "description": "Comparison based on end products: Non-cyclic produces ATP, NADPH, and O\\u2082; cyclic produces only ATP without forming NADPH or releasing oxygen.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "Non-cyclic produces ATP, NADPH, and O\\u2082, whereas cyclic produces only ATP.", "feedback": "Accurately compares the end products of both pathways."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_9cdov_1", "description": "NADH and FADH\\u2082 donate high-energy electrons to protein complexes embedded in the inner mitochondrial membrane, passing them sequentially down the electron transport chain to molecular oxygen as the final electron acceptor.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "NADH and FADH\\u2082 donate electrons to electron transport complexes. As electrons flow to oxygen (terminal acceptor making water)...", "feedback": "Correctly identifies electron donors and oxygen as the terminal electron acceptor."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_9cdov_2", "description": "As electrons move through the complexes, energy released is used to pump protons (H+) from the mitochondrial matrix into the intermembrane space, establishing a steep electrochemical proton gradient.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "protons are pumped into the intermembrane space.", "feedback": "Accurately identifies proton pumping across the inner mitochondrial membrane into the intermembrane space."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_roman_1786909874856_9cdov_3", "description": "Protons flow back into the matrix down their electrochemical gradient through ATP synthase complexes (chemiosmosis), driving the phosphorylation of ADP to yield the majority of cellular ATP.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "Protons return through ATP synthase, producing ATP by chemiosmosis.", "feedback": "Correctly describes the flow of protons through ATP synthase and ATP synthesis via chemiosmosis."}], "feedback": "The response is exceptionally clear, precise, and comprehensively addresses all aspects of the three subparts according to the rubric criteria.", "is_fallback": false}	[{"item": 1, "description": "Non-Cyclic Photophosphorylation", "aw