1507	1012	3004	\N	null	An action potential is generated when a neuron is stimulated above its threshold. Sodium channels open and sodium ions enter, causing depolarization of the membrane to positive values. Then potassium channels open and potassium leaves, causing repolarization. The sodium-potassium pump maintains the resting potential at -70 mV.\n\nIn myelinated axons, myelin sheaths insulate the nerve fiber except at the Nodes of Ranvier. The nerve impulse jumps from node to node, called saltatory conduction, making transmission faster.\n\nAt a synapse, when the impulse reaches the end of the axon, calcium ions enter the presynaptic knob. This causes vesicles to release neurotransmitter (like acetylcholine) into the synaptic gap. The chemical binds to receptors on the next neuron, causing sodium to enter and exciting the postsynaptic cell. Enzymes like acetylcholinesterase break down the transmitter to stop continuous firing.	\N	30	30	\N	{"suggested_score": 28.0, "maximum_score": 40.0, "confidence": 0.95, "criteria": [{"criterion_id": "pt_1_1", "description": "Resting membrane potential is maintained at approximately -70 mV by the sodium-potassium pump (Na\\u207a/K\\u207a ATPase), which actively transports 3 Na\\u207a ions out and 2 K\\u207a ions in, alongside leaky K\\u207a channels.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "The sodium-potassium pump maintains the resting potential at -70 mV.", "feedback": "Mentions the pump and the resting potential value."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_2", "description": "Depolarization phase: A threshold stimulus opens voltage-gated Na\\u207a channels, causing rapid influx of Na\\u207a ions down their electrochemical gradient, reversing the membrane potential to about +30 mV.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "An action potential is generated when a neuron is stimulated above its threshold. Sodium channels open and sodium ions enter, causing depolarization of the membrane to positive values.", "feedback": "Clearly covers threshold stimulation, sodium channel opening, and depolarization."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_3", "description": "Repolarization phase: Voltage-gated Na\\u207a channels close and voltage-gated K\\u207a channels open, allowing rapid efflux of K\\u207a ions out of the axoplasm to restore the negative internal resting potential.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "Then potassium channels open and potassium leaves, causing repolarization.", "feedback": "Correctly identifies potassium channel opening and efflux for repolarization."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_4", "description": "Hyperpolarization (undershoot): Delayed closing of voltage-gated K\\u207a channels causes an excess efflux of K\\u207a, making the membrane potential more negative than the resting state temporarily.", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "None found in text.", "feedback": "Student response does not mention hyperpolarization or the undershoot phase."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_5", "description": "Saltatory conduction in myelinated axons: Myelin sheath acts as an electrical insulator; local current circuits jump from one Node of Ranvier to the next, drastically increasing the speed of impulse propagation.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "In myelinated axons, myelin sheaths insulate the nerve fiber except at the Nodes of Ranvier. The nerve impulse jumps from node to node, called saltatory conduction, making transmission faster.", "feedback": "Fully describes saltatory conduction, myelination insulation, and the Nodes of Ranvier."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_6", "description": "Arrival of the action potential at the presynaptic axon terminal depolarizes the presynaptic membrane, opening voltage-gated calcium channels and causing Ca\\u00b2\\u207a influx into the terminal.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "when the impulse reaches the end of the axon, calcium ions enter the presynaptic knob.", "feedback": "Notes the arrival of the impulse and calcium entry into the presynaptic knob."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_7", "description": "Vesicular exocytosis: Influx of Ca\\u00b2\\u207a ions causes synaptic vesicles containing neurotransmitters (e.g., acetylcholine) to fuse with the presynaptic membrane, releasing neurotransmitter into the synaptic cleft.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "This causes vesicles to release neurotransmitter (like acetylcholine) into the synaptic gap.", "feedback": "Mentions vesicle release of neurotransmitter like acetylcholine."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_8", "description": "Neurotransmitter binds to specific ligand-gated ion channels on the postsynaptic membrane, inducing a conformational change that alters membrane permeability.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "The chemical binds to receptors on the next neuron...", "feedback": "Describes binding of the chemical to receptors on the next neuron."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_9", "description": "Generation of graded potentials: Influx of Na\\u207a or Ca\\u00b2\\u207a produces an Excitatory Postsynaptic Potential (EPSP), while Cl\\u207b influx or K\\u207a efflux produces an Inhibitory Postsynaptic Potential (IPSP).", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "...causing sodium to enter and exciting the postsynaptic cell.", "feedback": "Mentions excitation and sodium entry, but does not explicitly detail the formation or terminology of graded potentials like EPSP/IPSP."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_10", "description": "Termination of signal: Neurotransmitters in the synaptic cleft are rapidly degraded by specific enzymes (e.g., acetylcholinesterase) or reabsorbed by the presynaptic terminal via reuptake mechanisms.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "Enzymes like acetylcholinesterase break down the transmitter to stop continuous firing.", "feedback": "Explicitly mentions acetylcholinesterase breaking down the transmitter."}], "feedback": "The student covers most of the core physiological steps accurately, missing hyperpolarization and explicit mention of EPSP/IPSP terminology while successfully addressing sodium/potassium dynamics, saltatory conduction, calcium influx, vesicular release, receptor binding, and enzymatic degradation.", "is_fallback": false}	[{"item": 1, "description": "Resting Membrane Potential", "awarded": true, "points": 8.0}, {"item": 2, "description": "Depolarization & Repolarization", "awarded": true, "points": 10.0}, {"item": 3, "description": "Saltatory Conduction", "awarded": true, "points": 10.0}, {"item": 4, "description": "Chemical Synapse Transmission", "awarded": true, "points": 12.0}]	30	Essay 1 Action Potential: Teacher awarded 30.0/40.	0	28	30	30
1509	1012	3006	\N	null	### Short Notes\n\n#### (i) Counter-Current Multiplier in Loop of Henle\nThe loop of Henle has two limbs with opposite fluid flow. The descending limb is permeable to water, allowing water to leave by osmosis and concentrating the urine. The ascending limb is impermeable to water and actively pumps out sodium and chloride into the kidney medulla. This creates a high osmotic gradient from the cortex to the inner medulla, enabling water conservation in collecting ducts.\n\n#### (ii) Secondary Growth in Dicot Stems\nSecondary growth causes thickening of dicot stems. It is driven by the vascular cambium, which forms a continuous ring from fascicular and interfascicular cambium. Through periclinal cell divisions, it produces secondary xylem towards the inside (forming wood) and secondary phloem towards the outside. More xylem is produced than phloem, increasing stem girth and producing annual rings.\n\n#### (iii) Role of PS I and PS II\nPhotosystem II (P680) absorbs light energy and excites electrons down the electron transport chain to generate ATP. It splits water by photolysis to release oxygen and replacement electrons. Photosystem I (P700) absorbs light at 700 nm to boost electrons to ferredoxin, reducing NADP⁺ to NADPH for sugar synthesis.	\N	28	28	\N	{"suggested_score": 40.0, "maximum_score": 40.0, "confidence": 1.0, "criteria": [{"criterion_id": "sub_topic_1786909874856_602ka_1", "description": "Fluid flows in opposite directions in the ascending and descending limbs of the loop of Henle, creating a counter-current system that multiplies the osmotic gradient in the medullary interstitium.", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "The loop of Henle has two limbs with opposite fluid flow.", "feedback": "Correctly states opposite fluid flow creating an osmotic gradient."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_topic_1786909874856_602ka_2", "description": "The descending limb is permeable to water but impermeable to sodium ions, leading to passive water reabsorption by osmosis into the hypertonic medullary interstitium, concentrating tubular fluid.", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "The descending limb is permeable to water, allowing water to leave by osmosis and concentrating the urine.", "feedback": "Accurately describes descending limb water permeability and concentration of urine."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_topic_1786909874856_602ka_3", "description": "The thick ascending limb is impermeable to water but actively transports sodium and chloride ions out into the interstitium, maintaining high medullary osmolality and producing hypotonic filtrate.", "awarded": 3.4, "maximum": 3.4, "evidence": "The ascending limb is impermeable to water and actively pumps out sodium and chloride into the kidney medulla.", "feedback": "Fully covers ascending limb characteristics and active pumping of sodium and chloride."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_topic_1786909874856_26s7v_1", "description": "Secondary growth increases stem girth, resulting from the activity of lateral meristems, primarily the vascular cambium and cork cambium (phellogen).", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "Secondary growth causes thickening of dicot stems. It is driven by the vascular cambium...", "feedback": "Identifies thickening and the driving vascular cambium."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_topic_1786909874856_26s7v_2", "description": "The vascular cambium forms a continuous ring of meristematic cells derived from fascicular and interfascicular cambium, undergoing periclinal divisions.", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "...which forms a continuous ring from fascicular and interfascicular cambium. Through periclinal cell divisions...", "feedback": "Mentions continuous ring from fascicular/interfascicular cambium and periclinal divisions."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_topic_1786909874856_26s7v_3", "description": "Cell divisions cut off secondary xylem (wood) towards the inside and secondary phloem towards the outside, with secondary xylem production greatly exceeding secondary phloem.", "awarded": 3.4, "maximum": 3.4, "evidence": "it produces secondary xylem towards the inside (forming wood) and secondary phloem towards the outside. More xylem is produced than phloem...", "feedback": "Accurately details secondary xylem and phloem production and differential amounts."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_topic_1786909874856_m5hg1_1", "description": "Light energy absorbed by Photosystem II (P680) excites electrons, which are captured by a primary electron acceptor and passed down an electron transport chain to Photosystem I, releasing energy to synthesize ATP via photophosphorylation.", "awarded": 6.7, "maximum": 6.7, "evidence": "Photosystem II (P680) absorbs light energy and excites electrons down the electron transport chain to generate ATP.", "feedback": "Correctly details PSII excitation and ATP generation via electron transport chain."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_topic_1786909874856_m5hg1_2", "description": "Photolysis of water at the oxygen-evolving complex of PSII replaces lost electrons by splitting water molecules into oxygen, protons, and electrons, while excited electrons in Photosystem I (P700) are boosted to a higher energy level and transferred to NADP+ reductase, reducing NADP+ to NADPH in the stroma.", "awarded": 13.3, "maximum": 13.3, "evidence": "It splits water by photolysis to release oxygen and replacement electrons. Photosystem I (P700) absorbs light at 700 nm to boost electrons to ferredoxin, reducing NADP\\u207a to NADPH for sugar synthesis.", "feedback": "Thoroughly addresses water photolysis and PSI reduction of NADP+ to NADPH."}], "feedback": "The student has provided an exceptionally accurate, concise, and scientifically rigorous set of short notes covering all required points across the three sections.", "is_fallback": false}	[{"item": 1, "description": "Loop of Henle Counter-Current", "awarded": true, "points": 13.3}, {"item": 2, "description": "Dicot Stem Secondary Growth", "awarded": true