1678	1021	3005	\N	null	Photosynthesis and cellular respiration are the central bioenergetic processes in living systems.\n\nIn non-cyclic photophosphorylation, light is absorbed by PS II (P680) exciting electrons. Water is split by photolysis into oxygen, protons, and replacement electrons. The electrons move through an electron transport chain (cytochrome complex) to PS I (P700), pumping protons to make ATP via ATP synthase. At PS I, electrons are re-excited and reduce NADP⁺ to NADPH.\n\nComparing cyclic and non-cyclic photophosphorylation:\n- Non-cyclic uses both PS I and PS II, whereas cyclic uses only PS I.\n- Non-cyclic splits water and produces O₂, while cyclic does neither.\n- Non-cyclic produces ATP, NADPH, and O₂, whereas cyclic produces only ATP.\n\nIn mitochondria, oxidative phosphorylation occurs on the cristae. NADH and FADH₂ donate electrons to electron transport complexes. As electrons flow to oxygen (terminal acceptor making water), protons are pumped into the intermembrane space. Protons return through ATP synthase, producing ATP by chemiosmosis.	\N	16	16	\N	{"suggested_score": 40.0, "maximum_score": 40.0, "confidence": 0.98, "criteria": [{"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_gnxx0_1", "description": "Light energy is absorbed by Photosystem II (P680), exciting electrons to a higher energy state from where they are accepted by a primary electron acceptor.", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "light is absorbed by PS II (P680) exciting electrons.", "feedback": "Student correctly identifies light absorption by PS II (P680) and electron excitation."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_gnxx0_2", "description": "Photolysis of water occurs at the oxygen-evolving complex associated with PS II, releasing electrons to replace those lost by P680, along with protons (H+) and oxygen gas as a byproduct.", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "Water is split by photolysis into oxygen, protons, and replacement electrons.", "feedback": "Photolysis of water is clearly detailed along with all required byproducts."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_gnxx0_3", "description": "Electrons pass down an electron transport chain to Photosystem I (P700), generating ATP via chemiosmosis, and subsequently reduce NADP+ to NADPH catalyzed by ferredoxin-NADP+ reductase.", "awarded": 3.4, "maximum": 3.4, "evidence": "The electrons move through an electron transport chain (cytochrome complex) to PS I (P700), pumping protons to make ATP via ATP synthase. At PS I, electrons are re-excited and reduce NADP\\u207a to NADPH.", "feedback": "Covers electron transport from PS II to PS I, ATP synthesis, and final reduction to NADPH."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_wtrbb_1", "description": "Comparison based on involvement of photosystems: Non-cyclic involves both Photosystem II and Photosystem I, whereas cyclic photophosphorylation involves only Photosystem I.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "Non-cyclic uses both PS I and PS II, whereas cyclic uses only PS I.", "feedback": "Accurately contrasts photosystem involvement between the two pathways."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_wtrbb_2", "description": "Comparison based on photolysis of water and oxygen production: Water splitting and O\\u2082 evolution occur in non-cyclic photophosphorylation, but neither occurs in cyclic photophosphorylation.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "Non-cyclic splits water and produces O\\u2082, while cyclic does neither.", "feedback": "Directly compares water splitting and oxygen release."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_wtrbb_3", "description": "Comparison based on end products: Non-cyclic produces ATP, NADPH, and O\\u2082; cyclic produces only ATP without forming NADPH or releasing oxygen.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "Non-cyclic produces ATP, NADPH, and O\\u2082, whereas cyclic produces only ATP.", "feedback": "Clearly contrasts the final products of both pathways."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_9cdov_1", "description": "NADH and FADH\\u2082 donate high-energy electrons to protein complexes embedded in the inner mitochondrial membrane, passing them sequentially down the electron transport chain to molecular oxygen as the final electron acceptor.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "NADH and FADH\\u2082 donate electrons to electron transport complexes... flow to oxygen (terminal acceptor making water)", "feedback": "Details electron donation from coenzymes and oxygen acting as the terminal electron acceptor."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_9cdov_2", "description": "As electrons move through the complexes, energy released is used to pump protons (H+) from the mitochondrial matrix into the intermembrane space, establishing a steep electrochemical proton gradient.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "As electrons flow to oxygen... protons are pumped into the intermembrane space.", "feedback": "Explains proton pumping into the intermembrane space driven by electron flow."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_9cdov_3", "description": "Protons flow back into the matrix down their electrochemical gradient through ATP synthase complexes (chemiosmosis), driving the phosphorylation of ADP to yield the majority of cellular ATP.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "Protons return through ATP synthase, producing ATP by chemiosmosis.", "feedback": "Correctly describes chemiosmotic ATP synthesis via ATP synthase."}], "feedback": "The student has provided an exceptionally accurate, concise, and well-structured response that covers all requirements across parts (i), (ii), and (iii). Precise biochemical terminology (e.g., photolysis, P680, P700, chemiosmosis, ATP synthase) is appropriately used throughout.", "is_fallback": false}	[{"item": 1, "description": "Non-Cyclic Photophosphorylation", "awarded": false, "points": 15.0}, {"item": 2, "description": "Cyclic vs Non-Cyclic C