1564	1015	3004	\N	null	An action potential is generated when a neuron is stimulated above its threshold. Sodium channels open and sodium ions enter, causing depolarization of the membrane to positive values. Then potassium channels open and potassium leaves, causing repolarization. The sodium-potassium pump maintains the resting potential at -70 mV.\n\nIn myelinated axons, myelin sheaths insulate the nerve fiber except at the Nodes of Ranvier. The nerve impulse jumps from node to node, called saltatory conduction, making transmission faster.\n\nAt a synapse, when the impulse reaches the end of the axon, calcium ions enter the presynaptic knob. This causes vesicles to release neurotransmitter (like acetylcholine) into the synaptic gap. The chemical binds to receptors on the next neuron, causing sodium to enter and exciting the postsynaptic cell. Enzymes like acetylcholinesterase break down the transmitter to stop continuous firing.	\N	26	26	\N	{"suggested_score": 28.0, "maximum_score": 40.0, "confidence": 0.95, "criteria": [{"criterion_id": "pt_1_1", "description": "Resting membrane potential is maintained at approximately -70 mV by the sodium-potassium pump (Na\\u207a/K\\u207a ATPase), which actively transports 3 Na\\u207a ions out and 2 K\\u207a ions in, alongside leaky K\\u207a channels.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "The sodium-potassium pump maintains the resting potential at -70 mV.", "feedback": "Student correctly identifies the resting potential value and the sodium-potassium pump."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_2", "description": "Depolarization phase: A threshold stimulus opens voltage-gated Na\\u207a channels, causing rapid influx of Na\\u207a ions down their electrochemical gradient, reversing the membrane potential to about +30 mV.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "An action potential is generated when a neuron is stimulated above its threshold. Sodium channels open and sodium ions enter, causing depolarization of the membrane to positive values.", "feedback": "Covers threshold stimulation, opening of sodium channels, sodium entry, and depolarization to positive values."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_3", "description": "Repolarization phase: Voltage-gated Na\\u207a channels close and voltage-gated K\\u207a channels open, allowing rapid efflux of K\\u207a ions out of the axoplasm to restore the negative internal resting potential.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "Then potassium channels open and potassium leaves, causing repolarization.", "feedback": "Correctly identifies potassium channels opening and potassium leaving to cause repolarization."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_4", "description": "Hyperpolarization (undershoot): Delayed closing of voltage-gated K\\u207a channels causes an excess efflux of K\\u207a, making the membrane potential more negative than the resting state temporarily.", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "None present in the essay.", "feedback": "The student does not mention hyperpolarization or the undershoot phase."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_5", "description": "Saltatory conduction in myelinated axons: Myelin sheath acts as an electrical insulator; local current circuits jump from one Node of Ranvier to the next, drastically increasing the speed of impulse propagation.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "In myelinated axons, myelin sheaths insulate the nerve fiber except at the Nodes of Ranvier. The nerve impulse jumps from node to node, called saltatory conduction, making transmission faster.", "feedback": "Accurately describes myelin insulation, Nodes of Ranvier, saltatory conduction, and increased speed."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_6", "description": "Arrival of the action potential at the presynaptic axon terminal depolarizes the presynaptic membrane, opening voltage-gated calcium channels and causing Ca\\u00b2\\u207a influx into the terminal.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "when the impulse reaches the end of the axon, calcium 