1566	1015	3006	\N	null	### Short Notes\n\n#### (i) Counter-Current Multiplier in Loop of Henle\nThe loop of Henle has two limbs with opposite fluid flow. The descending limb is permeable to water, allowing water to leave by osmosis and concentrating the urine. The ascending limb is impermeable to water and actively pumps out sodium and chloride into the kidney medulla. This creates a high osmotic gradient from the cortex to the inner medulla, enabling water conservation in collecting ducts.\n\n#### (ii) Secondary Growth in Dicot Stems\nSecondary growth causes thickening of dicot stems. It is driven by the vascular cambium, which forms a continuous ring from fascicular and interfascicular cambium. Through periclinal cell divisions, it produces secondary xylem towards the inside (forming wood) and secondary phloem towards the outside. More xylem is produced than phloem, increasing stem girth and producing annual rings.\n\n#### (iii) Role of PS I and PS II\nPhotosystem II (P680) absorbs light energy and excites electrons down the electron transport chain to generate ATP. It splits water by photolysis to release oxygen and replacement electrons. Photosystem I (P700) absorbs light at 700 nm to boost electrons to ferredoxin, reducing NADP⁺ to NADPH for sugar synthesis.	\N	25	25	\N	{"suggested_score": 40.0, "maximum_score": 40.0, "confidence": 0.95, "criteria": [{"criterion_id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_602ka_1", "description": "Fluid flows in opposite directions in the ascending and descending limbs of the loop of Henle, creating a counter-current system that multiplies the osmotic gradient in the medullary interstitium.", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "The loop of Henle has two limbs with opposite fluid flow.", "feedback": "Correctly identifies opposite fluid flow in the two limbs creating an osmotic gradient."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_602ka_2", "description": "The descending limb is permeable to water but impermeable to sodium ions, leading to passive water reabsorption by osmosis into the hypertonic medullary interstitium, concentrating tubular fluid.", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "The descending limb is permeable to water, allowing water to leave by osmosis and concentrating the urine.", "feedback": "Accurately details water permeability and concentration of urine in the descending limb."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_602ka_3", "description": "The thick ascending limb is impermeable to water but actively transports sodium and chloride ions out into the interstitium, maintaining high medullary osmolality and producing hypotonic filtrate.", "awarded": 3.4, "maximum": 3.4, "evidence": "The ascending limb is impermeable to water and actively pumps out sodium and chloride into the kidney medulla.", "feedback": "Correctly states the impermeability to water and active pumping of sodium and chloride."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_26s7v_1", "description": "Secondary growth increases stem girth, resulting from the activity of lateral meristems, primarily the vascular cambium and cork cambium (phellogen).", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "Secondary growth causes thickening of dicot stems. It is driven by the vascular cambium...", "feedback": "Clearly mentions the role of vascular cambium in increasing stem thickness/girth."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_26s7v_2", "description": "The vascular cambium forms a continuous ring of meristematic cells derived from fascicular and interfascicular cambium, undergoing periclinal divisions.", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "...which forms a continuous ring from fascicular and interfascicular cambium. Through periclinal cell divisions...", "feedback": "Directly references the continuous ring formed from fascicular and interfascicular cambium via periclinal divisions."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_26s7v_3", "description": "Cell divisions cut off secondary xylem (wood) towards the inside and secondary phloem towards the outside, with secondary xylem production greatly exceeding secondary phloem.", "awarded": 3.4, "maximum": 3.4, "evidence": "it produces secondary xylem towards the inside (forming wood) and secondary phloem towards the outside. More xylem is produced than phloem...", "feedback": "Accurately describes the inner secondary xylem and outer secondary phloem production, noting that more xylem is produced."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_m5hg1_1", "description": "Light energy absorbed by Photosystem II (P680) excites electrons, which are captured by a primary electron acceptor and passed down an electron transport chain to Photosystem I, releasing energy to synthesize ATP via photophosphorylation.", "awarded": 6.7, "maximum": 6.7, "evidence": "Photosystem II (P680) absorbs light energy and excites electrons down the electron transport chain to generate ATP.", "feedback": "Mentions PSII (P680) absorbing light, exciting electrons through the electron transport chain, and generating ATP."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_m5hg1_2", "description": "Photolysis of water at the oxygen-evolving complex of PSII replaces lost electrons by splitting water molecules into oxygen, protons, and electrons, while excited electrons in Photosystem I (P700) are boosted to a higher energy level and transferred to NADP+ reductase, reducing NADP+ to NADPH in the stroma.", "awarded": 13.3, "maximum": 13.3, "evidence": "It splits water by photolysis to release oxygen and replacement electrons. Photosystem I (P700) absorbs light at 700 nm to boost electrons to ferredoxin, reducing NADP\\u207a to NADPH for sugar synthesis.", "feedback": "Covers photolysis of water for replacement electrons and oxygen release, as well as PSI (P700) reducing NADP+ to NADPH."}], "feedback": "The student has provided clear, concise, and scientifically accurate short notes covering all required biological principles, terminology, and anatomical/physiological components outlined in the grading rubric.", "is_fallback": false}	[{"item": 1, "description": "Loop of Henle Counter-Current", "awarded": true, "points": 13.3}, {"item": 2, "description": "Dicot Stem Secondary Growth", "awarded": true, "points": 13.3}, {"item": 3, "description": "PS I & PS II Light Reactions", "awarded": true, "points": 13.4}]	25	Essay 3 Short Notes: Teacher awarded 25.0/40.	0	40	25	25
1679	1021	3006	\N	null	### Short Notes\n\n#### (i) Counter-Current Multiplier in Loop of Henle\nThe loop of Henle has two limbs with opposite fluid flow. The descending limb is permeable to water, allowing water to leave by osmosis and concentrating the urine. The ascending limb is impermeable to water and actively pumps out sodium and chloride into the kidney medulla. This creates a high osmotic gradient from the cortex to the inner medulla, enabling water conservation in collecting ducts.\n\n#### (ii) Secondary Growth in Dicot Stems\nSecondary growth causes thickening of dicot stems. It is driven by the vascular cambium, which forms a continuous ring from fascicular and interfascicular cambium. Through periclinal cell divisions, it produces secondary xylem towards the inside (forming wood) and secondary phloem towards the outside. More xylem is produced than phloem, increasing stem girth and producing annual rings.\n\n#### (iii) Role of PS I and PS II\nPhotosystem II (P680) absorbs light energy and excites electrons down the electron transport chain to generate ATP. It splits water by photolysis to release oxygen and replacement electrons. Photosystem I (P700) absorbs light at 700 nm to boost electrons to ferredoxin, reducing NADP⁺ to NADPH for sugar synthesis.	\N	20	20	\N	{"suggested_score": 40.0, "maximum_score": 40.0, "confidence": 0.95, "criteria": [{"criterion_id": "sub_topic_1786909874856_602ka_1", "description": "Fluid flows in opposite directions in the ascending and descending limbs of the loop of Henle, creating a counter-current system that multiplies the osmotic gradient in the medullary interstitium.", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "The loop of Henle has two limbs with opposite fluid flow.", "feedback": "Student correctly identifies the opposite fluid flow in the two limbs."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_topic_1786909874856_602ka_2", "description": "The descending limb is permeable to water but impermeable to sodium ions, leading to passive wa