1839	1030	3005	\N	null	Photosynthesis uses light to make food in chloroplasts, and respiration breaks down food to make ATP in mitochondria.\n\nIn light reactions, light strikes chlorophyll in PS II and PS I. Water is broken down releasing oxygen. Electrons travel down a chain to make ATP and NADPH for the Calvin cycle. Cyclic photosynthesis only uses PS I and makes ATP without making NADPH or oxygen.\n\nIn mitochondria, electron transport chain uses NADH to pump protons across the inner membrane. Oxygen accepts the electrons to form water. ATP synthase makes ATP as protons flow back.	\N	31.6	31.6	\N	{"suggested_score": 30.0, "maximum_score": 40.0, "confidence": 0.85, "criteria": [{"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_gnxx0_1", "description": "Light energy is absorbed by Photosystem II (P680), exciting electrons to a higher energy state from where they are accepted by a primary electron acceptor.", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "In light reactions, light strikes chlorophyll in PS II and PS I.", "feedback": "Student mentions light striking PS II and PS I."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_gnxx0_2", "description": "Photolysis of water occurs at the oxygen-evolving complex associated with PS II, releasing electrons to replace those lost by P680, along with protons (H+) and oxygen gas as a byproduct.", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "Water is broken down releasing oxygen.", "feedback": "Correctly identifies the breakdown of water releasing oxygen."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_gnxx0_3", "description": "Electrons pass down an electron transport chain to Photosystem I (P700), generating ATP via chemiosmosis, and subsequently reduce NADP+ to NADPH catalyzed by ferredoxin-NADP+ reductase.", "awarded": 3.4, "maximum": 3.4, "evidence": "Electrons travel down a chain to make ATP and NADPH for the Calvin cycle.", "feedback": "Covers electron transport leading to ATP and NADPH production."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_wtrbb_1", "description": "Comparison based on involvement of photosystems: Non-cyclic involves both Photosystem II and Photosystem I, whereas cyclic photophosphorylation involves only Photosystem I.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "Cyclic photosynthesis only uses PS I", "feedback": "Correctly states cyclic uses only PS I."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_wtrbb_2", "description": "Comparison based on photolysis of water and oxygen production: Water splitting and O\\u2082 evolution occur in non-cyclic photophosphorylation, but neither occurs in cyclic photophosphorylation.", "awarded": 2.5, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "Cyclic photosynthesis only uses PS I and makes ATP without making NADPH or oxygen.", "feedback": "Implies lack of oxygen production in cyclic, but lacks detail on water splitting comparison."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_wtrbb_3", "description": "Comparison based on end products: Non-cyclic produces ATP, NADPH, and O\\u2082; cyclic produces only ATP without forming NADPH or releasing oxygen.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "makes ATP without making NADPH or oxygen.", "feedback": "Accurately contrasts the end products of cyclic photophosphorylation."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_9cdov_1", "description": "NADH and FADH\\u2082 donate high-energy electrons to protein complexes embedded in the inner mitochondrial membrane, passing them sequentially down the electron transport chain to molecular oxygen as the final electron acceptor.", "awarded": 2.5, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "In mitochondria, electron transport chain uses NADH to pump protons... Oxygen accepts the electrons to form water.", "feedback": "Mentions NADH and oxygen accepting electrons, but misses FADH\\u2082 details."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_9cdov_2", "description": "As electrons move through the complexes, energy released is used to pump protons (H+) from the mitochondrial matrix into the intermembrane space, establishing a steep electrochemical proton gradient.", "awarded": 2.5, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "electron transport chain uses NADH to pump protons across the inner membrane.", "feedback": "Mentions proton pumping across the inner membrane."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_9cdov_3", "description": "Protons flow back into the matrix down their electrochemical gradient through ATP synthase complexes (chemiosmosis), driving the phosphorylation of ADP to yield the majority of cellular ATP.", "awarded": 2.5, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "ATP synthase makes ATP as protons flow back.", "feedback": "Briefly describes ATP synthase and proton flow, but lacks depth on chemiosmosis and ADP phosphorylation."}], "feedback": "The student provides a concise and generally accurate summary of photosynthesis and cellular respiration, successfully hitting most core points regarding non-cyclic photophosphorylation, cyclic differences, and mitochondrial electron transport. However, explanations are brief and lack some specific biochemical details required for full marks in certain criteria.", "is_fallback": false}	[{"item": 1, "description": "Non-Cyclic Photophosphorylation", "awarded": true, "points": 15, "points_earned": 15}, {"item": 2, "description": "Cyclic vs Non-Cyclic Comparison", "awarded": true, "points": 10, "points_earned": 10}, {"item": 3, "description": "Mitochondrial Chemiosmosis", "awarded": false, "points": 15}, {"awarded": true, "points_earned": 5}, {"awarded": true, "points_earned": 5}]	31.6	Essay 2 Bioenergetics: Teacher awarded 12.0/40.	0	30	31.6	31.6
