1621	1018	3004	\N	null	An action potential is generated when a neuron is stimulated above its threshold. Sodium channels open and sodium ions enter, causing depolarization of the membrane to positive values. Then potassium channels open and potassium leaves, causing repolarization. The sodium-potassium pump maintains the resting potential at -70 mV.\n\nIn myelinated axons, myelin sheaths insulate the nerve fiber except at the Nodes of Ranvier. The nerve impulse jumps from node to node, called saltatory conduction, making transmission faster.\n\nAt a synapse, when the impulse reaches the end of the axon, calcium ions enter the presynaptic knob. This causes vesicles to release neurotransmitter (like acetylcholine) into the synaptic gap. The chemical binds to receptors on the next neuron, causing sodium to enter and exciting the postsynaptic cell. Enzymes like acetylcholinesterase break down the transmitter to stop continuous firing.	\N	22	22	\N	{"suggested_score": 28.0, "maximum_score": 40.0, "confidence": 0.95, "criteria": [{"criterion_id": "pt_1_1", "description": "Resting membrane potential is maintained at approximately -70 mV by the sodium-potassium pump (Na\\u207a/K\\u207a ATPase), which actively transports 3 Na\\u207a ions out and 2 K\\u207a ions in, alongside leaky K\\u207a channels.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "The sodium-potassium pump maintains the resting potential at -70 mV.", "feedback": "Student correctly identifies the resting potential value and mentions the sodium-potassium pump."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_2", "description": "Depolarization phase: A threshold stimulus opens voltage-gated Na\\u207a channels, causing rapid influx of Na\\u207a ions down their electrochemical gradient, reversing the membrane potential to about +30 mV.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "An action potential is generated when a neuron is stimulated above its threshold. Sodium channels open and sodium ions enter, causing depolarization of the membrane to positive values.", "feedback": "Covers threshold stimulus, opening of sodium channels, sodium influx, and depolarization to positive values."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_3", "description": "Repolarization phase: Voltage-gated Na\\u207a channels close and voltage-gated K\\u207a channels open, allowing rapid efflux of K\\u207a ions out of the axoplasm to restore the negative internal resting potential.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "Then potassium channels open and potassium leaves, causing repolarization.", "feedback": "Clearly describes the repolarization phase via potassium channels opening and potassium leaving."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_4", "description": "Hyperpolarization (undershoot): Delayed closing of voltage-gated K\\u207a channels causes an excess efflux of K\\u207a, making the membrane potential more negative than the resting state temporarily.", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "None present in text.", "feedback": "Student essay does not mention hyperpolarization or after-hyperpolarization."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_5", "description": "Saltatory conduction in myelinated axons: Myelin sheath acts as an electrical insulator; local current circuits jump from one Node of Ranvier to the next, drastically increasing the speed of impulse propagation.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "In myelinated axons, myelin sheaths insulate the nerve fiber except at the Nodes of Ranvier. The nerve impulse jumps from node to node, called saltatory conduction, making transmission faster.", "feedback": "Accurately describes insulation, Nodes of Ranvier, saltatory conduction, and increased speed."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_6", "description": "Arrival of the action potential at the presynaptic axon terminal depolarizes the presynaptic membrane, opening voltage-gated calcium channels and causing Ca\\u00b2\\u207a influx into the terminal.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "when the impulse reaches the end of the axon, calcium ions enter the presynaptic knob.", "feedback": "Mentions impulse arrival and calcium ion entry into the presynaptic knob."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_7", "description": "Vesicular exocytosis: Influx of Ca\\u00b2\\u207a ions causes synaptic vesicles containing neurotransmitters (e.g., acetylcholine) to fuse with the presynaptic membrane, releasing neurotransmitter into the synaptic cleft.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "This causes vesicles to release neurotransmitter (like acetylcholine) into the synaptic gap.", "feedback": "Describes vesicular release of neurotransmitter (acetylcholine) into the synaptic gap upon calcium entry."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_8", "description": "Neurotransmitter binds to specific ligand-gated ion channels on the postsynaptic membrane, inducing a conformational change that alters membrane permeability.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "The chemical binds to receptors on the next neuron...", "feedback": "Mentions neurotransmitter binding to receptors on the postsynaptic neuron."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_9", "description": "Generation of graded potentials: Influx of Na\\u207a or Ca\\u00b2\\u207a produces an Excitatory Postsynaptic Potential (EPSP), while Cl\\u207b influx or K\\u207a efflux produces an Inhibitory Postsynaptic Potential (IPSP).", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "...causing sodium to enter and exciting the postsynaptic cell.", "feedback": "Mentions sodium entering and excitation, but does not explicitly detail graded potentials, EPSPs, or IPSPs using standard terminology."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_10", "description": "Termination of signal: Neurotransmitters in the synaptic cleft are rapidly degraded by specific enzymes (e.g., acetylcholinesterase) or reabsorbed by the presynaptic terminal via reuptake mechanisms.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "Enzymes like acetylcholinesterase break down the transmitter to stop continuous firing.", "feedback": "Correctly identifies enzym