1623	1018	3006	\N	null	### Short Notes\n\n#### (i) Counter-Current Multiplier in Loop of Henle\nThe loop of Henle has two limbs with opposite fluid flow. The descending limb is permeable to water, allowing water to leave by osmosis and concentrating the urine. The ascending limb is impermeable to water and actively pumps out sodium and chloride into the kidney medulla. This creates a high osmotic gradient from the cortex to the inner medulla, enabling water conservation in collecting ducts.\n\n#### (ii) Secondary Growth in Dicot Stems\nSecondary growth causes thickening of dicot stems. It is driven by the vascular cambium, which forms a continuous ring from fascicular and interfascicular cambium. Through periclinal cell divisions, it produces secondary xylem towards the inside (forming wood) and secondary phloem towards the outside. More xylem is produced than phloem, increasing stem girth and producing annual rings.\n\n#### (iii) Role of PS I and PS II\nPhotosystem II (P680) absorbs light energy and excites electrons down the electron transport chain to generate ATP. It splits water by photolysis to release oxygen and replacement electrons. Photosystem I (P700) absorbs light at 700 nm to boost electrons to ferredoxin, reducing NADP⁺ to NADPH for sugar synthesis.	\N	21	21	\N	{"suggested_score": 40.0, "maximum_score": 40.0, "confidence": 0.95, "criteria": [{"criterion_id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_602ka_1", "description": "Fluid flows in opposite directions in the ascending and descending limbs of the loop of Henle, creating a counter-current system that multiplies the osmotic gradient in the medullary interstitium.", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "The loop of Henle has two limbs with opposite fluid flow.", "feedback": "Correctly states opposite fluid flow and counter-current mechanism."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_602ka_2", "description": "The descending limb is permeable to water but impermeable to sodium ions, leading to passive water reabsorption by osmosis into the hypertonic medullary interstitium, concentrating tubular fluid.", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "The descending limb is permeable to water, allowing water to leave by osmosis and concentrating the urine.", "feedback": "Accurately describes descending limb water permeability and concentration of fluid."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_602ka_3", "description": "The thick ascending limb is impermeable to water but actively transports sodium and chloride ions out into the interstitium, maintaining high medullary osmolality and producing hypotonic filtrate.", "awarded": 3.4, "maximum": 3.4, "evidence": "The ascending limb is impermeable to water and actively pumps out sodium and chloride into the kidney medulla.", "feedback": "Fully captures the ascending limb properties and active transport of ions."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_26s7v_1", "description": "Secondary growth increases stem girth, resulting from the activity of lateral meristems, primarily the vascular cambium and cork cambium (phellogen).", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "Secondary growth causes thickening of dicot stems. It is driven by the vascular cambium...", "feedback": "Identifies vascular cambium and stem thickening (girth increase)."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_26s7v_2", "description": "The vascular cambium forms a continuous ring of meristematic cells derived from fascicular and interfascicular cambium, undergoing periclinal divisions.", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "which forms a continuous ring from fascicular and interfascicular cambium. Through periclinal cell divisions...", "feedback": "Explicitly mentions continuous ring formation from fascicular/interfascicular cambium and periclinal divisions."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_26s7v_3", "description": "Cell divisions cut off secondary xylem (wood) towards the inside and secondary phloem towards the outside, with secondary xylem production greatly exceeding secondary phloem.", "awarded": 3.4, "maximum": 3.4, "evidence": "it produces secondary xylem towards the inside (forming wood) and secondary phloem towards the outside. More xylem is produced than phloem...", "feedback": "Accurately covers internal xylem and external phloem production with differential rates."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_m5hg1_1", "description": "Light energy absorbed by Photosystem II (P680) excites electrons, which are captured by a primary electron acceptor and passed down an electron transport chain to Photosystem I, releasing energy to synthesize ATP via photophosphorylation.", "awarded": 6.7, "maximum": 6.7, "evidence": "Photosystem II (P680) absorbs light energy and excites electrons down the electron transport chain to generate ATP.", "feedback": "Correctly outlines PSII excitation and ATP generation through the electron transport chain."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_sub_topic_1786909874856_m5hg1_2", "description": "Photolysis of water at the oxygen-evolving complex of PSII replaces lost electrons by splitting water molecules into oxygen, protons, and electrons, while excited electrons in Photosystem I (P700) are boosted to a higher energy level and transferred to NADP+ reductase, reducing NADP+ to NADPH in the stroma.", "awarded": 13.3, "maximum": 13.3, "evidence": "It splits water by photolysis to release oxygen and replacement electrons. Photosystem I (P700) absorbs light at 700 nm to boost electrons to ferredoxin, reducing NADP\\u207a to NADPH for sugar synthesis.", "feedback": "Accurately details water photolysis and PSI reduction of NADP+ to NADPH."}], "feedback": "The student has provided exceptionally clear, concise, and scientifically accurate short notes covering all required points across the three sections with precise terminology.", "is_fallback": false}	[{"item": 1, "description": "Loop of Henle Counter-Current", "awarded": true, "points": 13.3}, {"item