1840	1030	3006	\N	null	### Short Notes\n\n#### (i) Loop of Henle\nThe loop of Henle has descending and ascending limbs. Water moves out in the descending limb and salt is pumped out in the ascending limb, creating a salt gradient in the kidney medulla.\n\n#### (ii) Secondary Growth\nVascular cambium produces secondary xylem on the inside and secondary phloem on the outside. This increases the diameter of dicot stems and forms wood.\n\n#### (iii) PS I and PS II\nPS II absorbs light and splits water to make oxygen and ATP. PS I absorbs light and makes NADPH for photosynthesis.	\N	40	40	\N	{"suggested_score": 23.3, "maximum_score": 40.0, "confidence": 0.95, "criteria": [{"criterion_id": "sub_topic_1786909874856_602ka_1", "description": "Fluid flows in opposite directions in the ascending and descending limbs of the loop of Henle, creating a counter-current system that multiplies the osmotic gradient in the medullary interstitium.", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "The loop of Henle has descending and ascending limbs. Water moves out in the descending limb and salt is pumped out in the ascending limb, creating a salt gradient in the kidney medulla.", "feedback": "Student mentions descending and ascending limbs and salt gradient, but fails to explicitly state the counter-current/opposite flow mechanism."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_topic_1786909874856_602ka_2", "description": "The descending limb is permeable to water but impermeable to sodium ions, leading to passive water reabsorption by osmosis into the hypertonic medullary interstitium, concentrating tubular fluid.", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "Water moves out in the descending limb", "feedback": "Correctly identifies water moving out of the descending limb."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_topic_1786909874856_602ka_3", "description": "The thick ascending limb is impermeable to water but actively transports sodium and chloride ions out into the interstitium, maintaining high medullary osmolality and 