1677	1021	3004	\N	null	Nerve impulses travel along neurons through electrical changes. The resting potential is -70 mV maintained by the Na/K pump. When stimulated, sodium enters the axon making it positive, and then potassium leaves making it negative again.\n\nMyelinated nerves have myelin sheaths with gaps called Nodes of Ranvier. The impulse jumps between nodes which is faster.\n\nAt the synapse, the electrical impulse causes chemical transmitters to be released across the gap. The neurotransmitter binds to receptors on the next cell to pass the signal, and is then broken down by enzymes.	\N	26	26	\N	{"suggested_score": 16.0, "maximum_score": 40.0, "confidence": 0.95, "criteria": [{"criterion_id": "pt_1_1", "description": "Resting membrane potential is maintained at approximately -70 mV by the sodium-potassium pump (Na\\u207a/K\\u207a ATPase), which actively transports 3 Na\\u207a ions out and 2 K\\u207a ions in, alongside leaky K\\u207a channels.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "The resting potential is -70 mV maintained by the Na/K pump.", "feedback": "Student correctly identifies the resting potential value and mentions the Na/K pump."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_2", "description": "Depolarization phase: A threshold stimulus opens voltage-gated Na\\u207a channels, causing rapid influx of Na\\u207a ions down their electrochemical gradient, reversing the membrane potential to about +30 mV.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "When stimulated, sodium enters the axon making it positive", "feedback": "Student correctly identifies sodium entering the axon during stimulation."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_3", "description": "Repolarization phase: Voltage-gated Na\\u207a channels close and voltage-gated K\\u207a channels open, allowing rapid efflux of K\\u207a ions out of the axoplasm to restore the negative internal resting potential.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "and then potassium leaves making it negative again.", "feedback": "Student notes that potassium leaves to restore negativity."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_4", "description": "Hyperpolarization (undershoot): Delayed closing of voltage-gated K\\u207a channels causes an excess efflux of K\\u207a, making the membrane potential more negative than the resting state temporarily.", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "", "feedback": "No mention of hyperpolarization or undershoot."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_5", "description": "Saltatory conduction in myelinated axons: Myelin sheath acts as an electrical insulator; local current circuits jump from one Node of Ranvier to the next, drastically increasing the speed of impulse propagation.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "Myelinated nerves have myelin sheaths with gaps called Nodes of Ranvier. The impulse jumps between nodes which is faster.", "feedback": "Student correctly describes saltatory conduction via Nodes of Ranvier."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_6", "description": "Arrival of the action potential at the presynaptic axon terminal depolarizes the presynaptic membrane, opening voltage-gated calcium channels and causing Ca\\u00b2\\u207a influx into the terminal.", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "", "feedback": "No mention of calcium channels or calcium influx."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_7", "description": "Vesicular exocytosis: Influx of Ca\\u00b2\\u207a ions causes synaptic vesicles containing neurotransmitters (e.g., acetylcholine) to fuse with the presynaptic membrane, releasing neurotransmitter into the synaptic cleft.", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "At the synapse, the electrical impulse causes chemical transmitters to be released across the gap.", "feedback": "Mentions release of transmitters, but lacks specific details of vesicular exocytosis and calcium involvement."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_8", "description": "Neurotransmitter binds to specific ligand-gated ion channels on the postsynaptic membrane, inducing a conformational change that alters membrane permeability.", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "The neurotransmitter binds to receptors on the next cell to pass the signal", "feedback": "Mentions binding to receptors, but does not detail ligand-gated ion channels or permeability changes."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_9", "description": "Generation of graded potentials: Influx of Na\\u207a or Ca\\u00b2\\u207a produces an Excitatory Postsynaptic Potential (EPSP), while Cl\\u207b influx or K\\u207a efflux produces an Inhibitory Postsynaptic Potential (IPSP).", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "", "feedback": "No mention of EPSP/IPSP or graded potentials."}, {"criterion_id": "pt_1_10", "description": "Termination of signal: Neurotransmitters in the synaptic cleft are rapidly degraded by specific enzymes (e.g., acetylcholinesterase) or reabsorbed by the presynaptic terminal via reuptake mechanisms.", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 4.0, "evidence": "and is then broken down by enzymes.", "feedback": "Mentions breakdown by enzymes, but lacks specific terminology (e.g., acetylcholinesterase) or reuptake mechanisms."}], "feedback": "The student essay correctly addresses basic elements of the resting potential, action potential ion movements, and saltatory conduction, as well as basic synaptic transmission and enzymatic breakdown. However, it lacks depth and specific scientific terminology (such as hyperpolarization, calcium channels, exocytosis mechanisms, ligand-gated channels, EPSP/IPSP, and acetylcholinesterase) required for full marks across all 10 criteria.", "is_fallback": false}	[{"item": 1, "description": "Resting Membrane Potential", "awarded": true, "points": 8.0}, {"item": 2, "description": "Depolarization & Repolarization", "awarded": true, "points": 10.0}, {"item": 3, "description": "Saltatory Conduction", "awarded": true, "points": 10.0}, {"item": 4, "description": "Chemical Synapse Transmission", "awarded": false, "points": 12.0}]	26	Essay 1 Action Potential: Teacher awarded 26.0/4