1733	1024	3005	\N	null	Photosynthesis and cellular respiration are the central bioenergetic processes in living systems.\n\nIn non-cyclic photophosphorylation, light is absorbed by PS II (P680) exciting electrons. Water is split by photolysis into oxygen, protons, and replacement electrons. The electrons move through an electron transport chain (cytochrome complex) to PS I (P700), pumping protons to make ATP via ATP synthase. At PS I, electrons are re-excited and reduce NADP⁺ to NADPH.\n\nComparing cyclic and non-cyclic photophosphorylation:\n- Non-cyclic uses both PS I and PS II, whereas cyclic uses only PS I.\n- Non-cyclic splits water and produces O₂, while cyclic does neither.\n- Non-cyclic produces ATP, NADPH, and O₂, whereas cyclic produces only ATP.\n\nIn mitochondria, oxidative phosphorylation occurs on the cristae. NADH and FADH₂ donate electrons to electron transport complexes. As electrons flow to oxygen (terminal acceptor making water), protons are pumped into the intermembrane space. Protons return through ATP synthase, producing ATP by chemiosmosis.	\N	20	20	\N	{"suggested_score": 40.0, "maximum_score": 40.0, "confidence": 0.95, "criteria": [{"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_gnxx0_1", "description": "Light energy is absorbed by Photosystem II (P680), exciting electrons to a higher energy state from where they are accepted by a primary electron acceptor.", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "light is absorbed by PS II (P680) exciting electrons.", "feedback": "Student correctly identifies light absorption by PS II (P680) and excitation of electrons."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_gnxx0_2", "description": "Photolysis of water occurs at the oxygen-evolving complex associated with PS II, releasing electrons to replace those lost by P680, along with protons (H+) and oxygen gas as a byproduct.", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "Water is split by photolysis into oxygen, protons, and replacement electrons.", "feedback": "Student accurately states the photolysis of water into oxygen, protons, and replacement electrons."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_gnxx0_3", "description": "Electrons pass down an electron transport chain to Photosystem I (P700), generating ATP via chemiosmosis, and subsequently reduce NADP+ to NADPH catalyzed by ferredoxin-NADP+ reductase.", "awarded": 3.4, "maximum": 3.4, "evidence": "The electrons move through an electron transport chain (cytochrome complex) to PS I (P700), pumping protons to make ATP via ATP synthase. At PS I, electrons are re-excited and reduce NADP\\u207a to NADPH.", "feedback": "Student details electron transport to PS I, ATP synthesis via ATP synthase, and the reduction of NADP+ to NADPH."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_wtrbb_1", "description": "Comparison based on involvement of photosystems: Non-cyclic involves both Photosystem II and Photosystem I, whereas cyclic photophosphorylation involves only Photosystem I.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "Non-cyclic uses both PS I and PS II, whereas cyclic uses only PS I.", "feedback": "Direct and accurate comparison of photosystems involved."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_wtrbb_2", "description": "Comparison based on photolysis of water and oxygen production: Water splitting and O\\u2082 evolution occur in non-cyclic photophosphorylation, but neither occurs in cyclic photophosphorylation.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "Non-cyclic splits water and produces O\\u2082, while cyclic does neither.", "feedback": "Clear contrast regarding photolysis and oxygen production."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_wtrbb_3", "description": "Comparison based on end products: Non-cyclic produces ATP, NADPH, and O\\u2082; cyclic produces only ATP without forming NADPH or releasing oxygen.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "Non-cyclic produces ATP, NADPH, and O\\u2082, whereas cyclic produces only ATP.", "feedback": "Accurate differentiation of the final products between the two pathways."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_9cdov_1", "description": "NADH and FADH\\u2082 donate high-energy electrons to protein complexes embedded in the inner mitochondrial membrane, passing them sequentially down the electron transport chain to molecular oxygen as the final electron acceptor.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "NADH and FADH\\u2082 donate electrons to electron transport complexes... flow to oxygen (terminal acceptor making water)", "feedback": "Student outlines electron donation by NADH/FADH2 and oxygen as the terminal electron acceptor."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_9cdov_2", "description": "As electrons move through the complexes, energy released is used to pump protons (H+) from the mitochondrial matrix into the intermembrane space, establishing a steep electrochemical proton gradient.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "protons are pumped into the intermembrane space.", "feedback": "Mentions proton pumping into the intermembrane space as electrons flow through the complexes."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_9cdov_3", "description": "Protons flow back into the matrix down their electrochemical gradient through ATP synthase complexes (chemiosmosis), driving the phosphorylation of ADP to yield the majority of cellular ATP.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "Protons return through ATP synthase, producing ATP by chemiosmosis.", "feedback": "Explicitly details chemiosmosis and ATP production via ATP synthase."}], "feedback": "The student provides a concise, accurate, and well-structured response that covers all required marking points across parts (i), (ii), and (iii) using correct biochemical terminology.", "is_fallback": false}	[{"item": 1, "description": "Non-Cyclic Photophosphorylation", "awarded": true, "points": 15.0}, {"item": 2, "description": "Cyclic vs Non-Cyclic Comparison", "awarded": true, "points": 10.0}, {"