1787	1027	3005	\N	null	Photosynthesis uses light to make food in chloroplasts, and respiration breaks down food to make ATP in mitochondria.\n\nIn light reactions, light strikes chlorophyll in PS II and PS I. Water is broken down releasing oxygen. Electrons travel down a chain to make ATP and NADPH for the Calvin cycle. Cyclic photosynthesis only uses PS I and makes ATP without making NADPH or oxygen.\n\nIn mitochondria, electron transport chain uses NADH to pump protons across the inner membrane. Oxygen accepts the electrons to form water. ATP synthase makes ATP as protons flow back.	\N	25	25	\N	{"suggested_score": 33.5, "maximum_score": 40.0, "confidence": 0.95, "criteria": [{"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_gnxx0_1", "description": "Light energy is absorbed by Photosystem II (P680), exciting electrons to a higher energy state from where they are accepted by a primary electron acceptor.", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "light strikes chlorophyll in PS II and PS I", "feedback": "Mentions PS II and light absorption, sufficient for basic credit."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_gnxx0_2", "description": "Photolysis of water occurs at the oxygen-evolving complex associated with PS II, releasing electrons to replace those lost by P680, along with protons (H+) and oxygen gas as a byproduct.", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "Water is broken down releasing oxygen.", "feedback": "States water is broken down releasing oxygen."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_gnxx0_3", "description": "Electrons pass down an electron transport chain to Photosystem I (P700), generating ATP via chemiosmosis, and subsequently reduce NADP+ to NADPH catalyzed by ferredoxin-NADP+ reductase.", "awarded": 3.4, "maximum": 3.4, "evidence": "Electrons travel down a chain to make ATP and NADPH for the Calvin cycle.", "feedback": "Matches required endpoints."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_wtrbb_1", "description": "Comparison based on involvement of photosystems: Non-cyclic involves both Photosystem II and Photosystem I, whereas cyclic photophosphorylation involves only Photosystem I.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "Cyclic photosynthesis only uses PS I and makes ATP", "feedback": "Clearly contrasts cyclic using only PS I versus non-cyclic using both."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_wtrbb_2", "description": "Comparison based on photolysis of water and oxygen production: Water splitting and O\\u2082 evolution occur in non-cyclic photophosphorylation, but neither occurs in cyclic photophosphorylation.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "without making NADPH or oxygen.", "feedback": "Implies lack of oxygen production in cyclic pathway relative to non-cyclic."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_wtrbb_3", "description": "Comparison based on end products: Non-cyclic produces ATP, NADPH, and O\\u2082; cyclic produces only ATP without forming NADPH or releasing oxygen.", "awarded": 5.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "makes ATP and NADPH... Cyclic photosynthesis only uses PS I and makes ATP without making NADPH or oxygen.", "feedback": "Explicitly compares products between the two pathways."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_9cdov_1", "description": "NADH and FADH\\u2082 donate high-energy electrons to protein complexes embedded in the inner mitochondrial membrane, passing them sequentially down the electron transport chain to molecular oxygen as the final electron acceptor.", "awarded": 3.5, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "electron transport chain uses NADH to pump protons... Oxygen accepts the electrons to form water.", "feedback": "Mentions NADH and oxygen acceptance, but omits FADH2 and explicit mention of inner mitochondrial membrane protein complexes."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_9cdov_2", "description": "As electrons move through the complexes, energy released is used to pump protons (H+) from the mitochondrial matrix into the intermembrane space, establishing a steep electrochemical proton gradient.", "awarded": 4.0, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "electron transport chain uses NADH to pump protons across the inner membrane.", "feedback": "Mentions proton pumping across the inner membrane, though lacks full detail on matrix to intermembrane space and gradient terminology."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_roman_1786909874856_9cdov_3", "description": "Protons flow back into the matrix down their electrochemical gradient through ATP synthase complexes (chemiosmosis), driving the phosphorylation of ADP to yield the majority of cellular ATP.", "awarded": 4.5, "maximum": 5.0, "evidence": "ATP synthase makes ATP as protons flow back.", "feedback": "Captures ATP synthase and proton flow back, though brief on chemiosmosis specifics."}], "feedback": "The student provides a concise and accurate essay that addresses all parts of the prompt with good scientific terminology. Most marking criteria are fully met, with minor omissions in the depth of mitochondrial electron transport complex details.", "is_fallback": false}	[{"item": 1, "description": "Non-Cyclic Photophosphorylation", "awarded": true, "points": 15, "points_earned": 15}, {"item": 2, "description": "Cyclic vs Non-Cyclic Comparison", "awarded": true, "points": 10}, {"item": 3, "description": "Mitochondrial Chemiosmosis", "awarded": false, "points": 15}]	25	Essay 2 Bioenergetics: Teacher awarded 15.0/40.	0	33.5	25	25
