1788	1027	3006	\N	null	### Short Notes\n\n#### (i) Loop of Henle\nThe loop of Henle has descending and ascending limbs. Water moves out in the descending limb and salt is pumped out in the ascending limb, creating a salt gradient in the kidney medulla.\n\n#### (ii) Secondary Growth\nVascular cambium produces secondary xylem on the inside and secondary phloem on the outside. This increases the diameter of dicot stems and forms wood.\n\n#### (iii) PS I and PS II\nPS II absorbs light and splits water to make oxygen and ATP. PS I absorbs light and makes NADPH for photosynthesis.	\N	46.7	46.7	\N	{"suggested_score": 23.3, "maximum_score": 40.0, "confidence": 0.9, "criteria": [{"criterion_id": "sub_topic_1786909874856_602ka_1", "description": "Fluid flows in opposite directions in the ascending and descending limbs of the loop of Henle, creating a counter-current system that multiplies the osmotic gradient in the medullary interstitium.", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "The loop of Henle has descending and ascending limbs. Water moves out in the descending limb and salt is pumped out in the ascending limb...", "feedback": "Student mentions limbs and water/salt movement, but does not explicitly state the counter-current/opposite flow mechanism creating the multiplying osmotic gradient."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_topic_1786909874856_602ka_2", "description": "The descending limb is permeable to water but impermeable to sodium ions, leading to passive water reabsorption by osmosis into the hypertonic medullary interstitium, concentrating tubular fluid.", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "Water moves out in the descending limb", "feedback": "Correctly identifies that water moves out of the descending limb."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_topic_1786909874856_602ka_3", "description": "The thick ascending limb is impermeable to water but actively transports sodium and chloride ions out into the interstitium, maintaining high medullary osmolality and producing hypotonic filtrate.", "awarded": 3.4, "maximum": 3.4, "evidence": "salt is pumped out in the ascending limb", "feedback": "Correctly identifies salt being pumped out in the ascending limb."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_topic_1786909874856_26s7v_1", "description": "Secondary growth increases stem girth, resulting from the activity of lateral meristems, primarily the vascular cambium and cork cambium (phellogen).", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "This increases the diameter of dicot stems...", "feedback": "Mentions increase in diameter (girth) of dicot stems."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_topic_1786909874856_26s7v_2", "description": "The vascular cambium forms a continuous ring of meristematic cells derived from fascicular and interfascicular cambium, undergoing periclinal divisions.", "awarded": 0.0, "maximum": 3.3, "evidence": "Vascular cambium produces secondary xylem on the inside and secondary phloem on the outside.", "feedback": "Does not mention the continuous ring formation or fascicular/interfascicular cambium/periclinal divisions."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_topic_1786909874856_26s7v_3", "description": "Cell divisions cut off secondary xylem (wood) towards the inside and secondary phloem towards the outside, with secondary xylem production greatly exceeding secondary phloem.", "awarded": 3.4, "maximum": 3.4, "evidence": "Vascular cambium produces secondary xylem on the inside and secondary phloem on the outside. ... forms wood.", "feedback": "Correctly states production of secondary xylem inside and secondary phloem outside, and mentions wood."}, {"criterion_id": "sub_topic_1786909874856_m5hg1_1", "description": "Light energy absorbed by Photosystem II (P680) excites electrons, which are captured by a primary electron acceptor and passed down an electron transport chain to Photosystem I, releasing energy to synthesize ATP via photophosphorylation.", "awarded": 3.3, "maximum": 6.7, "evidence": "PS II absorbs light... and ATP. PS I absorbs light and makes NADPH", "f