973	Lesson 1: Animal tissues and dietary nutrition - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 1: Animal tissues and dietary nutrition	PDF	uploads/pdf/5db31833dbda4032b50acf91867614d5.pdf	\N	Lesson 1 — Animal Tissues and Dietary Nutrition \nIntroduction \nAnimals are multicellular organisms whose bodies are organised into specialised cells, tissues, \norgans and organ systems. \nSpecialised cells perform particular functions and work together to maintain the survival of the \norganism. Groups of structurally and functionally similar cells form tissues, while different \ntissues combine to form organs. \nAnimals also require nutrients from their diets to provide energy, build and repair tissues, \nregulate physiological processes and maintain normal growth and development. \n \n1. Levels of Organisation in Animals \nThe body of a multicellular animal can be organised into several levels. \nCell → Tissue → Organ → Organ system → Organism \nEach level contains increasingly complex interactions between specialised structures. \n \n2. Cell Specialisation \nAnimal cells become specialised for particular functions. \nExamples include \n• \nRed blood cells for oxygen transport  \n• \nNeurons for communication  \n• \nMuscle cells for contraction  \n• \nEpithelial cells for protection and absorption  \n• \nSperm cells for reproduction  \nSpecialisation allows different parts of the body to perform different functions efficiently. \n \n3. Animal Tissues \nA tissue is a group of specialised cells and associated extracellular material that performs a \nparticular function. \nThe four primary animal tissue types are\n\n1. Epithelial tissue  \n2. Connective tissue  \n3. Muscle tissue  \n4. Nervous tissue  \n \n4. Epithelial Tissue \nEpithelial tissue covers external surfaces and lines internal surfaces and cavities. \nMajor functions include \n• \nProtection  \n• \nAbsorption  \n• \nSecretion  \n• \nExcretion  \n• \nDiffusion  \n• \nSensory reception  \nEpithelial cells are usually closely packed with relatively little extracellular material. \n \n5. Characteristics of Epithelial Tissue \nEpithelial tissues generally have \n• \nClosely packed cells  \n• \nVery little extracellular matrix  \n• \nA free or exposed surface  \n• \nA basement membrane  \n• \nStrong cell-to-cell junctions  \n• \nNo direct blood vessels within the epithelial layer  \nNutrients and oxygen reach epithelial cells by diffusion from underlying tissues. \n \n6. Simple Epithelium\n\nSimple epithelium consists of a single layer of cells. \nIt is suitable for functions such as \n• \nDiffusion  \n• \nFiltration  \n• \nAbsorption  \n• \nSecretion  \nExamples include the epithelium of blood vessels and some regions of the respiratory system. \n \n7. Stratified Epithelium \nStratified epithelium consists of multiple layers of cells. \nIts main function is protection against \n• \nMechanical damage  \n• \nChemical damage  \n• \nPathogens  \nThe outer layers can be replaced by cell division in deeper layers. \n \n8. Squamous Epithelium \nSquamous epithelial cells are thin and flattened. \nTheir shape makes them suitable for rapid diffusion and filtration. \nExamples include \n• \nAlveolar lining  \n• \nEndothelium of blood vessels  \n \n9. Cuboidal Epithelium \nCuboidal epithelial cells are approximately cube-shaped. \nThey are commonly involved in \n• \nSecretion\n\n• \nAbsorption  \nThey occur in structures such as certain kidney tubules and glands. \n \n10. Columnar Epithelium \nColumnar epithelial cells are taller than they are wide. \nThey commonly function in \n• \nAbsorption  \n• \nSecretion  \nThey occur extensively in the digestive tract. \n \n11. Ciliated Epithelium \nSome epithelial cells possess cilia on their apical surfaces. \nCilia are microscopic structures that beat rhythmically. \nThey help move substances along epithelial surfaces. \nFor example, cilia in parts of the respiratory tract help move mucus and trapped particles toward \nthe pharynx. \n \n12. Glandular Epithelium \nGlandular epithelial cells specialise in secretion. \nGlands can release substances such as \n• \nEnzymes  \n• \nMucus  \n• \nHormones  \n• \nSweat  \nGlands can be broadly classified as endocrine or exocrine. \n \n13. Endocrine Glands\n\nEndocrine glands release hormones directly into the blood or surrounding tissue fluid. \nExamples include \n• \nPituitary gland  \n• \nThyroid gland  \n• \nAdrenal glands  \n• \nPancreatic endocrine cells  \nTheir secretions act on target cells elsewhere in the body. \n \n14. Exocrine Glands \nExocrine glands release their secretions through ducts. \nExamples include glands that produce \n• \nSweat  \n• \nSaliva  \n• \nDigestive enzymes  \n• \nMucus  \n \n15. Connective Tissue \nConnective tissue supports, connects and protects other tissues. \nUnlike epithelial tissue, connective tissue generally contains substantial extracellular matrix. \nThe matrix consists of \n• \nGround substance  \n• \nProtein fibres  \n \n16. Functions of Connective Tissue \nConnective tissue can \n• \nSupport organs  \n• \nConnect tissues\n\n• \nStore energy  \n• \nTransport substances  \n• \nProtect against physical damage  \n• \nProvide structural strength  \n• \nParticipate in immune defence  \n \n17. Types of Connective Tissue \nImportant connective tissues include \n• \nAreolar tissue  \n• \nAdipose tissue  \n• \nDense connective tissue  \n• \nCartilage  \n• \nBone  \n• \nBlood  \nAlthough these tissues differ greatly in structure, they share the general characteristic of having \ncells embedded within extracellular material. \n \n18. Areolar Connective Tissue \nAreolar tissue contains loosely arranged fibres and cells. \nIt functions in \n• \nSupporting tissues  \n• \nBinding structures  \n• \nHolding tissue fluid  \n• \nAllowing movement between adjacent tissues  \nIt is commonly found beneath epithelial layers. \n \n19. Adipose Tissue\n\nAdipose tissue consists mainly of adipocytes. \nIts functions include \n• \nEnergy storage  \n• \nThermal insulation  \n• \nCushioning of organs  \n• \nEndocrine signalling  \nStored energy is primarily in the form of triglycerides. \n \n20. Dense Connective Tissue \nDense connective tissue contains abundant collagen fibres. \nIt provides high tensile strength. \nExamples include \nTendons \nConnect muscles to bones. \nLigaments \nConnect bones to other bones. \n \n21. Cartilage \nCartilage is a strong but flexible connective tissue. \nIt contains cells called chondrocytes embedded within a firm extracellular matrix. \nFunctions include \n• \nSupport  \n• \nShock absorption  \n• \nReducing friction  \n• \nProviding flexible structural support  \n \n22. Bone\n\nBone is a rigid connective tissue. \nIts matrix contains collagen fibres and mineral salts, particularly calcium phosphate. \nFunctions include \n• \nStructural support  \n• \nProtection  \n• \nMovement  \n• \nMineral storage  \n• \nBlood cell formation within bone marrow  \n \n23. Blood \nBlood is a specialised connective tissue. \nIts extracellular matrix is the liquid called plasma. \nBlood transports \n• \nOxygen  \n• \nCarbon dioxide  \n• \nNutrients  \n• \nHormones  \n• \nMetabolic wastes  \n• \nHeat  \n \n24. Muscle Tissue \nMuscle tissue is specialised for contraction. \nIts contraction produces force and movement. \nThere are three major types \n• \nSkeletal muscle  \n• \nCardiac muscle  \n• \nSmooth muscle\n\n25. Skeletal Muscle \nSkeletal muscle is generally attached to bones. \nIts major functions include \n• \nBody movement  \n• \nMaintenance of posture  \n• \nHeat production  \nSkeletal muscle is usually under voluntary control. \nIts cells are long and contain multiple nuclei. \n \n26. Cardiac Muscle \nCardiac muscle forms the walls of the heart. \nIt is specialised for continuous rhythmic contraction. \nCardiac muscle is involuntary. \nIts cells are connected by specialised structures called intercalated discs, which help coordinate \ncontraction. \n \n27. Smooth Muscle \nSmooth muscle is found in the walls of structures such as \n• \nIntestines  \n• \nBlood vessels  \n• \nUrinary bladder  \n• \nUterus  \nIt produces involuntary movements such as movement of food through the digestive tract. \nSmooth muscle cells are generally spindle-shaped and lack the striations characteristic of skeletal \nand cardiac muscle. \n \n28. Nervous Tissue\n\nNervous tissue is specialised for \n• \nReceiving information  \n• \nProcessing information  \n• \nTransmitting signals  \n• \nCoordinating responses  \nIt forms the nervous system. \nThe main cell types are \n• \nNeurons  \n• \nGlial cells  \n \n29. Neurons \nNeurons are specialised cells that transmit electrical and chemical signals. \nA typical neuron contains \n• \nCell body  \n• \nDendrites  \n• \nAxon  \nDendrites generally receive signals, while the axon transmits signals away from the cell body. \n \n30. Glial Cells \nGlial cells support neurons. \nTheir functions include \n• \nStructural support  \n• \nNutritional support  \n• \nProtection  \n• \nInsulation  \n• \nMaintenance of the neural environment  \nThey are essential for normal nervous system function.\n\n31. Tissue Cooperation \nThe four major tissue types work together. \nFor example, the heart contains \n• \nCardiac muscle  \n• \nConnective tissue  \n• \nNervous tissue  \n• \nEpithelial tissue  \nThese tissues cooperate to allow the heart to function as an organ. \n \n32. Organs \nAn organ consists of multiple tissue types organised to perform specific functions. \nExamples include \n• \nHeart  \n• \nStomach  \n• \nKidney  \n• \nLung  \n• \nLiver  \n• \nBrain  \nEach organ contains tissues arranged in a coordinated structure. \n \n33. Organ Systems \nAn organ system consists of organs working together to perform major physiological functions. \nExamples include \n• \nDigestive system  \n• \nRespiratory system  \n• \nCirculatory system\n\n• \nNervous system  \n• \nEndocrine system  \n• \nReproductive system  \n \n34. Dietary Nutrition \nAnimals require nutrients from food. \nNutrients can be classified into \n• \nMacronutrients  \n• \nMicronutrients  \n• \nWater  \nMacronutrients include \n• \nCarbohydrates  \n• \nProteins  \n• \nLipids  \nMicronutrients include \n• \nVitamins  \n• \nMinerals  \n \n35. Carbohydrates \nCarbohydrates are important sources of energy. \nThey include \n• \nMonosaccharides  \n• \nDisaccharides  \n• \nPolysaccharides  \nGlucose is a major carbohydrate used in cellular respiration. \n \n36. Functions of Carbohydrates\n\nCarbohydrates provide \n• \nEnergy  \n• \nEnergy storage  \n• \nStructural materials  \nExcess glucose can be stored as glycogen in animals, particularly in the liver and skeletal \nmuscles. \n \n37. Proteins \nProteins are polymers of amino acids. \nThey are essential for \n• \nGrowth  \n• \nTissue repair  \n• \nEnzymes  \n• \nHormones  \n• \nAntibodies  \n• \nTransport proteins  \n• \nStructural components  \nProtein provides approximately 4 kcal of energy per gram when metabolised. \n \n38. Essential Amino Acids \nHumans require several amino acids that cannot be synthesised in sufficient quantities. \nThese are called essential amino acids. \nThey must be obtained from the diet. \nThe exact dietary requirement can vary according to age and physiological condition. \n \n39. Lipids \nLipids include\n\n• \nTriglycerides  \n• \nPhospholipids  \n• \nSteroids  \nThey are important for \n• \nEnergy storage  \n• \nCell membranes  \n• \nInsulation  \n• \nProtection  \n• \nHormone production  \nLipids provide approximately 9 kcal of energy per gram. \n \n40. Dietary Fibre \nDietary fibre consists largely of plant-derived carbohydrates that are not completely digested by \nhuman digestive enzymes. \nIt contributes to \n• \nNormal bowel movement  \n• \nStool formation  \n• \nDigestive health  \nAdequate fibre intake is an important component of a balanced diet. \n \n41. Vitamins \nVitamins are organic compounds required in relatively small quantities. \nThey are essential for normal metabolic processes. \nThey are classified as \nWater-soluble vitamins \n• \nVitamin B group  \n• \nVitamin C\n\nFat-soluble vitamins \n• \nVitamin A  \n• \nVitamin D  \n• \nVitamin E  \n• \nVitamin K  \n \n42. Vitamin A \nVitamin A is important for \n• \nVision  \n• \nEpithelial tissue maintenance  \n• \nImmune function  \n• \nNormal growth  \nDeficiency can cause impaired vision, particularly under low-light conditions. \nSevere deficiency can lead to xerophthalmia. \n \n43. Vitamin D \nVitamin D is important for \n• \nCalcium absorption  \n• \nPhosphate regulation  \n• \nBone mineralisation  \nDeficiency in children can cause rickets. \nIn adults, severe deficiency can contribute to osteomalacia. \n \n44. Vitamin E \nVitamin E functions primarily as an antioxidant. \nIt helps protect cellular components from oxidative damage.\n\nSevere deficiency is uncommon in healthy individuals but can affect neurological and muscular \nfunction. \n \n45. Vitamin K \nVitamin K is important for normal blood clotting. \nIt is required for the synthesis or activation of several clotting factors. \nDeficiency can increase the risk of abnormal bleeding. \n \n46. Vitamin B Group \nB vitamins participate in numerous metabolic reactions. \nExamples include \n• \nThiamine  \n• \nRiboflavin  \n• \nNiacin  \n• \nPyridoxine  \n• \nFolate  \n• \nCobalamin  \nThey are particularly important in energy metabolism and the synthesis of cellular components. \n \n47. Vitamin C \nVitamin C is important for \n• \nCollagen synthesis  \n• \nWound healing  \n• \nAntioxidant protection  \n• \nNormal connective tissue function  \nSevere deficiency causes scurvy. \nSymptoms can include\n\n• \nBleeding gums  \n• \nPoor wound healing  \n• \nWeakness  \n• \nEasy bruising  \n \n48. Minerals \nMinerals are inorganic elements required for normal physiological functions. \nImportant dietary minerals include \n• \nCalcium  \n• \nPhosphorus  \n• \nIron  \n• \nSodium  \n• \nPotassium  \n• \nIodine  \n• \nMagnesium  \n• \nZinc  \nTheir requirements vary depending on the element and physiological condition. \n \n49. Calcium \nCalcium is required for \n• \nBones and teeth  \n• \nMuscle contraction  \n• \nBlood clotting  \n• \nNerve transmission  \n• \nCell signalling  \nInsufficient calcium intake can contribute to reduced bone mineralisation.\n\n50. Phosphorus \nPhosphorus is important for \n• \nBones and teeth  \n• \nATP  \n• \nNucleic acids  \n• \nPhospholipids  \n• \nEnergy metabolism  \nIt commonly occurs in association with calcium in bone tissue. \n \n51. Iron \nIron is essential for \n• \nHaemoglobin  \n• \nMyoglobin  \n• \nElectron transport proteins  \n• \nCellular respiration  \nIron deficiency can lead to iron-deficiency anaemia. \nCommon symptoms include fatigue and reduced exercise tolerance. \n \n52. Iodine \nIodine is required for the production of thyroid hormones. \nThese hormones regulate aspects of \n• \nMetabolic activity  \n• \nGrowth  \n• \nDevelopment  \nIodine deficiency can cause enlargement of the thyroid gland, known as goitre. \n \n53. Sodium\n\nSodium contributes to \n• \nOsmotic balance  \n• \nNerve impulse transmission  \n• \nMuscle function  \n• \nRegulation of extracellular fluid  \nThe body carefully regulates sodium concentration. \n \n54. Potassium \nPotassium is important for \n• \nIntracellular fluid balance  \n• \nNerve impulses  \n• \nMuscle contraction  \n• \nCellular function  \nNormal potassium concentration is particularly important for cardiac and neuromuscular activity. \n \n55. Magnesium \nMagnesium participates in \n• \nEnzyme activity  \n• \nMuscle function  \n• \nNerve function  \n• \nEnergy metabolism  \n• \nBone structure  \nIt is required for the proper functioning of many enzymes. \n \n56. Zinc \nZinc is important for \n• \nEnzyme activity\n\n• \nDNA synthesis  \n• \nProtein synthesis  \n• \nImmune function  \n• \nGrowth and development  \nDeficiency can impair growth and wound healing. \n \n57. Water \nWater is the most abundant component of the human body. \nIt is essential for \n• \nSolvent functions  \n• \nChemical reactions  \n• \nTransport  \n• \nTemperature regulation  \n• \nRemoval of metabolic wastes  \n• \nMaintenance of blood volume  \n \n58. Functions of Water \nWater acts as \n• \nA solvent  \n• \nA transport medium  \n• \nA reactant in metabolic reactions  \n• \nA temperature regulator  \n• \nA component of body fluids  \nAdequate water is essential for maintaining homeostasis. \n \n59. Balanced Diet \nA balanced diet provides appropriate amounts of\n\n• \nCarbohydrates  \n• \nProteins  \n• \nLipids  \n• \nVitamins  \n• \nMinerals  \n• \nWater  \n• \nDietary fibre  \nThe required amounts vary according to \n• \nAge  \n• \nSex  \n• \nBody size  \n• \nPhysical activity  \n• \nPhysiological state  \n \n60. Energy Requirements \nEnergy requirements depend on factors such as \n• \nBasal metabolic rate  \n• \nPhysical activity  \n• \nGrowth  \n• \nThermoregulation  \n• \nPregnancy  \n• \nLactation  \nEnergy intake should generally correspond to energy expenditure over time to maintain body \nmass. \n \n61. Basal Metabolic Rate \nBasal metabolic rate, or BMR, is the energy expenditure required to maintain essential \nphysiological functions under defined resting conditions.\n\nIt supports processes such as \n• \nBreathing  \n• \nCirculation  \n• \nMaintenance of body temperature  \n• \nCellular metabolism  \n \n62. Malnutrition \nMalnutrition occurs when nutrient intake is inadequate, excessive or improperly balanced. \nIt can involve \n• \nUndernutrition  \n• \nMicronutrient deficiencies  \n• \nOvernutrition  \nBoth insufficient and excessive nutrient intake can negatively affect health. \n \n63. Protein-Energy Malnutrition \nSevere deficiency of energy and protein can cause protein-energy malnutrition. \nTwo classic forms are \nMarasmus \nAssociated mainly with severe energy deficiency. \nKwashiorkor \nAssociated particularly with severe protein deficiency in the context of inadequate overall \nnutrition. \n \n64. Obesity \nObesity occurs when excessive body fat accumulates to a level that increases health risks. \nIt is associated with factors including \n• \nExcess energy intake\n\n• \nLow physical activity  \n• \nGenetic factors  \n• \nEnvironmental factors  \n• \nMetabolic factors  \nIt can increase the risk of several chronic diseases. \n \n65. Importance of a Balanced Nutrient Intake \nA balanced diet supports \n• \nGrowth  \n• \nTissue repair  \n• \nEnergy production  \n• \nImmune function  \n• \nHormone production  \n• \nNervous system function  \n• \nBone development  \n• \nMaintenance of homeostasis  \nNo single food provides every nutrient in the required amounts. \n \n66. Tissue and Nutrition Relationship \nNutrition and tissue function are closely connected. \nFor example \nProtein \nSupports tissue growth and repair. \nCalcium and phosphorus \nSupport bone structure. \nIron \nSupports oxygen transport.\n\nVitamin C \nSupports collagen synthesis. \nVitamin D \nSupports calcium absorption and bone mineralisation. \nTherefore, nutrient deficiencies can directly affect tissue structure and function. \n \n67. Key Terms \nTissue \nA group of specialised cells and associated extracellular material performing specific functions. \nEpithelium \nTissue covering surfaces and lining body cavities. \nConnective tissue \nTissue that supports, connects, protects or transports. \nMuscle tissue \nContractile tissue responsible for producing force and movement. \nNervous tissue \nTissue specialised for communication and coordination. \nMacronutrient \nA nutrient required in relatively large quantities. \nMicronutrient \nA nutrient required in relatively small quantities. \nVitamin \nAn organic micronutrient required for normal physiological functions. \nMineral \nAn inorganic nutrient required for physiological processes. \nBalanced diet \nA diet providing appropriate quantities of essential nutrients and energy.\n\nMalnutrition \nA condition resulting from inadequate, excessive or imbalanced nutrient intake. \n \n68. Final Summary \nAnimal bodies are organised from specialised cells into tissues, organs and organ systems. \nThe four primary animal tissues are \nEpithelial tissue \nProvides protection, absorption and secretion. \nConnective tissue \nProvides support, connection, protection, storage and transport. \nMuscle tissue \nProduces force and movement. \nNervous tissue \nReceives, processes and transmits information. \nAnimals require a balanced supply of macronutrients, micronutrients, water and dietary fibre. \nThe major macronutrients are carbohydrates, proteins and lipids. \nVitamins and minerals are required in smaller quantities but are essential for normal \nphysiological processes. \nWater is essential for transport, chemical reactions, temperature regulation and maintenance of \nhomeostasis. \nAdequate nutrition is therefore fundamental to the development, maintenance and functioning of \nanimal tissues and organ systems.	COMPLETED	93	2026-08-19 18:11:31.890278	2026-08-19 18:12:51.88288	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
949	Lesson 1: Nature, scope, and importance of biology - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 1: Nature, scope, and importance of biology	PDF	uploads/pdf/3ec069800a1744b7b996d8098a70c2d5.pdf	\N	Unit 1 - Introduction to Biology \nLesson 1: Nature, Scope, and Importance of Biology \n1. Lesson Overview \nBiology is the scientific study of life and living organisms. It examines how organisms are \nstructured, how they function, how they reproduce, how they interact with one another and with \ntheir environment, and how life has changed through evolutionary processes. \nBiology is a broad and interconnected science. It ranges from studying molecules inside a cell to \nstudying interactions between organisms within ecosystems and the entire biosphere. \nUnderstanding the nature of biology provides the foundation for studying all other areas of the \nsubject, including cell biology, genetics, physiology, evolution, ecology, microbiology, and \nbiotechnology. \n \n2. Learning Objectives \nBy the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n• \nDefine biology and explain its major areas of study.  \n• \nIdentify the fundamental characteristics of living organisms.  \n• \nExplain why viruses occupy a special position in discussions about life.  \n• \nDescribe the importance of biology to humans and society.  \n• \nExplain how biology is connected with other scientific disciplines.  \n• \nDescribe the major steps of the scientific method.  \n• \nDistinguish between observations, hypotheses, theories, and scientific laws.  \n• \nExplain the importance of experimentation and evidence in biological science.  \n \n3. What is Biology? \nThe word biology is derived from two Greek words: \n• \nBios — life  \n• \nLogos — study or knowledge  \nTherefore, biology can be defined as: \nThe scientific study of life and living organisms.\n\nBiology investigates organisms at different levels, from their chemical components and cells to \nindividuals, populations, communities, ecosystems, and the biosphere. \nBiology is not limited to studying animals and plants. It includes all forms of cellular life, \nincluding: \n• \nBacteria  \n• \nArchaea  \n• \nProtists  \n• \nFungi  \n• \nPlants  \n• \nAnimals  \nIt also investigates acellular biological entities such as viruses, although viruses are not generally \nregarded as independently living organisms. \n \n4. The Nature of Biology \nBiology is a natural science because it studies phenomena occurring in the natural world. \nIt is also an empirical science, meaning that biological knowledge is developed and evaluated \nusing observations, measurements, experiments, and other forms of evidence. \nBiology has several important characteristics. \n4.1 Biology is evidence-based \nBiological explanations must be supported by evidence. \nFor example, scientists do not conclude that a particular microorganism causes a disease simply \nbecause it is found in diseased individuals. Evidence must be obtained through appropriate \ninvestigation and experimentation. \n4.2 Biology is constantly developing \nScientific knowledge is not considered permanently unchangeable. \nNew evidence can modify or replace existing explanations. \nFor example, advances in microscopy, molecular biology, genetics, and DNA sequencing have \nsignificantly changed our understanding of relationships between organisms. \n4.3 Biology is interdisciplinary\n\nBiology overlaps with many other scientific disciplines. \nFor example: \nDiscipline \nRelationship with Biology \nChemistry \nChemical reactions occurring in organisms \nPhysics \nEnergy, forces, movement and biological structures \nMathematics \nPopulation growth, genetics and statistical analysis \nComputer science Bioinformatics and analysis of biological data \nGeography \nDistribution of organisms and ecosystems \nGeology \nFossils, Earth's history and evolution \nModern biology therefore requires knowledge from several scientific fields. \n \n5. Characteristics of Living Organisms \nAlthough organisms differ enormously in their structure and lifestyle, living organisms share \nseveral fundamental characteristics. \nThese characteristics are important because they help us distinguish living systems from non-\nliving matter. \n5.1 Cellular organisation \nThe cell is the fundamental structural and functional unit of life. \nAll organisms are composed of one or more cells. \nOrganisms may be: \nUnicellular \nAn organism consists of a single cell. \nExamples: \n• \nMany bacteria  \n• \nAmoeba  \n• \nSome fungi  \nMulticellular\n\nAn organism consists of many cells that may become specialised for different functions. \nExamples: \n• \nHumans  \n• \nTrees  \n• \nMost animals  \nIn multicellular organisms, cells can form tissues, tissues can form organs, and organs can form \norgan systems. \n \n5.2 Metabolism \nMetabolism is the sum of all chemical reactions occurring within a living organism. \nMetabolic reactions allow organisms to: \n• \nObtain energy  \n• \nBuild cellular components  \n• \nBreak down substances  \n• \nGrow  \n• \nRepair damaged structures  \n• \nMaintain cellular activities  \nMetabolism can broadly be divided into: \nAnabolism \nAnabolic reactions build complex molecules from simpler molecules. \nExample: \nAmino acids → Proteins \nThese reactions generally require energy. \nCatabolism \nCatabolic reactions break complex molecules into simpler molecules. \nExample: \nGlucose → Carbon dioxide + Water + Energy\n\nCellular respiration is an important example of catabolism. \n \n5.3 Nutrition \nOrganisms require materials and energy to maintain their life processes. \nNutrients provide substances needed for: \n• \nGrowth  \n• \nRepair  \n• \nEnergy production  \n• \nSynthesis of cellular components  \n• \nRegulation of physiological processes  \nOrganisms obtain nutrients in different ways. \nAutotrophic nutrition \nOrganisms produce organic compounds from inorganic substances. \nFor example, green plants produce glucose through photosynthesis. \nHeterotrophic nutrition \nOrganisms obtain organic substances from other organisms or organic material. \nAnimals, fungi and many microorganisms are heterotrophs. \n \n5.4 Respiration \nLiving organisms release and utilise energy through metabolic processes. \nCellular respiration involves the controlled breakdown of energy-rich substances and the \nproduction of usable cellular energy, particularly ATP. \nAerobic respiration generally uses oxygen and produces carbon dioxide and water as major end \nproducts. \nSome organisms can obtain energy without oxygen through anaerobic processes. \n \n5.5 Growth\n\nGrowth is a permanent increase in the size, dry mass, cell number, or structural complexity of an \norganism. \nIn multicellular organisms, growth commonly occurs through: \n1. Cell division  \n2. Cell enlargement  \n3. Production of additional cellular material  \nGrowth in organisms therefore involves changes at the cellular and molecular levels. \n \n5.6 Movement \nLiving organisms exhibit movement. \nMovement does not necessarily mean that the entire organism moves from one location to \nanother. \nFor example: \n• \nAnimals move their bodies from place to place.  \n• \nPlants grow towards light.  \n• \nRoots grow towards water and minerals.  \n• \nCells may move within tissues.  \n• \nCytoplasm can undergo movement within cells.  \nTherefore, movement includes both locomotion and internal or directional movement. \n \n5.7 Irritability and response to stimuli \nLiving organisms can detect and respond to changes in their internal or external environment. \nA change that produces a response is called a stimulus. \nExamples include: \n• \nLight  \n• \nTemperature  \n• \nSound  \n• \nChemicals\n\n• \nTouch  \n• \nWater availability  \n• \nGravity  \nExamples of responses: \n• \nPlants growing towards light  \n• \nWithdrawal of a hand from a hot object  \n• \nPupils constricting in bright light  \n• \nRoots growing downward in response to gravity  \nThe ability to detect and respond to stimuli allows organisms to survive in changing \nenvironments. \n \n5.8 Reproduction \nReproduction is the biological process through which organisms produce new individuals. \nThere are two major types. \nAsexual reproduction \nAsexual reproduction involves a single parent and generally does not involve the fusion of \ngametes. \nExamples include: \n• \nBinary fission in bacteria  \n• \nBudding in yeast  \n• \nVegetative propagation in plants  \nThe offspring are generally genetically very similar to the parent. \nSexual reproduction \nSexual reproduction generally involves the formation and fusion of gametes. \nThe fusion of male and female gametes produces a zygote. \nSexual reproduction produces genetic variation among offspring and is therefore important in \nevolution.\n\n5.9 Homeostasis \nHomeostasis is the maintenance of a relatively stable internal environment despite changes in \nexternal or internal conditions. \nExamples include regulation of: \n• \nBody temperature  \n• \nBlood glucose concentration  \n• \nWater balance  \n• \nBlood pH  \n• \nIon concentrations  \nFor example, humans maintain their internal body temperature within a relatively narrow range \ndespite changes in environmental temperature. \nHomeostasis is usually maintained through regulatory mechanisms involving receptors, control \ncentres and effectors. \n \n5.10 Adaptation \nOrganisms possess characteristics that enable them to survive and reproduce successfully in their \nenvironments. \nAn adaptation is a heritable characteristic that increases an organism's ability to survive and \nreproduce in a particular environment. \nExamples: \n• \nThick fur in animals living in cold environments  \n• \nReduced leaves and water-storage tissues in many desert plants  \n• \nStreamlined bodies in aquatic animals  \nAdaptations develop over generations through evolutionary processes such as natural selection. \n \n5.11 Heredity \nLiving organisms transmit biological information from parents to offspring. \nThis transmission is known as heredity. \nThe hereditary information is primarily stored in DNA.\n\nGenes are segments of DNA that contain information involved in determining biological \ncharacteristics and regulating cellular processes. \n \n5.12 Evolution \nPopulations of organisms change over generations. \nThis long-term change is known as evolution. \nEvolution explains both: \n• \nThe diversity of organisms  \n• \nThe similarities shared by different organisms  \nNatural selection is one of the major mechanisms responsible for evolutionary change. \nEvolution also provides an important unifying principle for modern biology. \n \n6. Levels of Biological Study \nBiology can be studied at different scales. \nA simplified sequence is: \nMolecules → Organelles → Cells → Tissues → Organs → Organ systems → Organisms → \nPopulations → Communities → Ecosystems → Biosphere \nDifferent biological disciplines focus on different levels. \nFor example: \n• \nMolecular biology — molecules and molecular processes  \n• \nCell biology — cells and organelles  \n• \nHistology — tissues  \n• \nAnatomy — structures of organisms  \n• \nPhysiology — functions of organisms and their parts  \n• \nGenetics — heredity and variation  \n• \nEcology — organisms and their environment  \n• \nEvolutionary biology — changes in populations over generations  \nThese levels are interconnected rather than completely separate.\n\n7. Major Branches of Biology \nBiology contains numerous specialised fields. \nBotany \nStudy of plants. \nZoology \nStudy of animals. \nMicrobiology \nStudy of microorganisms. \nCytology \nStudy of cells. \nHistology \nStudy of tissues. \nAnatomy \nStudy of the structure of organisms. \nPhysiology \nStudy of the functions of organisms and their structures. \nGenetics \nStudy of heredity and variation. \nEcology \nStudy of interactions between organisms and their environment. \nTaxonomy \nIdentification, naming and classification of organisms. \nEvolutionary biology \nStudy of evolutionary changes and relationships between organisms. \nMolecular biology \nStudy of biological processes at the molecular level.\n\nBiotechnology \nApplication of biological systems, organisms or biological molecules to develop useful products \nand processes. \n \n8. Importance of Biology \nBiology has a major influence on human life and modern society. \n8.1 Medicine and healthcare \nBiological knowledge is essential for understanding: \n• \nDiseases  \n• \nPathogens  \n• \nHuman anatomy  \n• \nHuman physiology  \n• \nImmune responses  \n• \nGenetics  \n• \nDrug action  \nBiological research contributes to the development of: \n• \nVaccines  \n• \nAntibiotics  \n• \nDiagnostic methods  \n• \nNew medicines  \n• \nTreatments for genetic disorders  \n \n8.2 Agriculture \nBiology contributes to agricultural productivity through the study of: \n• \nPlant physiology  \n• \nGenetics  \n• \nPlant diseases\n\n• \nSoil microorganisms  \n• \nPest management  \n• \nAnimal breeding  \n• \nCrop improvement  \nModern agricultural biotechnology can also be used to develop crops with desirable \ncharacteristics. \n \n8.3 Environmental management \nBiological knowledge is essential for understanding: \n• \nEcosystems  \n• \nBiodiversity  \n• \nPollution  \n• \nClimate change  \n• \nConservation  \n• \nPopulation changes  \n• \nHuman impacts on natural systems  \nThis knowledge helps develop strategies for sustainable use of natural resources. \n \n8.4 Food production \nBiology is applied in: \n• \nFermentation  \n• \nFood preservation  \n• \nFood microbiology  \n• \nLivestock production  \n• \nCrop production  \n• \nAquaculture  \nMicroorganisms such as yeast and bacteria are particularly important in the production of various \nfermented foods.\n\n8.5 Biotechnology and industry \nBiological organisms and biological molecules can be used to produce useful products. \nExamples include: \n• \nEnzymes  \n• \nAntibiotics  \n• \nHormones  \n• \nVaccines  \n• \nBiofuels  \n• \nFermented products  \nGenetic engineering has expanded the ability to modify organisms and produce biological \nproducts. \n \n8.6 Forensic science \nBiology is used in forensic investigations through techniques such as: \n• \nDNA profiling  \n• \nBlood analysis  \n• \nBiological sample identification  \n• \nGenetic analysis  \nDNA profiling can be used to establish biological relationships and assist in identifying \nindividuals. \n \n8.7 Conservation \nBiology provides the scientific basis for protecting: \n• \nEndangered species  \n• \nHabitats  \n• \nGenetic diversity  \n• \nEcosystems\n\nConservation biology is particularly important because human activities can cause habitat \ndestruction, pollution, overexploitation and biological invasions. \n \n9. Biology and Society \nBiological discoveries can have significant effects on society. \nHowever, biological technologies must be used responsibly. \nExamples of issues requiring ethical consideration include: \n• \nGenetic modification  \n• \nCloning  \n• \nStem-cell research  \n• \nGenetic testing  \n• \nHuman genome editing  \n• \nUse of animals in research  \n• \nGenetic privacy  \n• \nConservation of biological resources  \nTherefore, scientific knowledge alone is not sufficient. Scientists and society must also consider \nthe ethical, environmental, social and economic consequences of biological applications. \n \n10. The Scientific Method \nThe scientific method is a systematic approach used to investigate natural phenomena and \ndevelop explanations supported by evidence. \nThere is no single rigid procedure that every scientific investigation must follow, but scientific \ninvestigations commonly involve the following stages. \nStep 1 — Observation \nScientists observe a phenomenon or identify a problem. \nObservations may be: \nQualitative \nDescriptive observations that do not involve numerical measurements.\n\nExample: \nThe leaves of a plant become pale. \nQuantitative \nObservations involving numerical measurements. \nExample: \nThe plant grew 4 cm during seven days. \n \nStep 2 — Question or problem \nAn observation leads to a scientific question. \nExample: \nDoes light intensity affect the rate of photosynthesis? \nA good scientific question should be specific and capable of investigation. \n \nStep 3 — Hypothesis \nA hypothesis is a testable proposed explanation or prediction about a phenomenon. \nExample: \nIncreasing light intensity increases the rate of photosynthesis, provided that other limiting factors \nremain suitable. \nA scientific hypothesis must be capable of being tested. \n \nStep 4 — Prediction \nA prediction describes what should happen if the hypothesis is correct. \nFor example: \nIf light intensity is increased, the number of oxygen bubbles produced by an aquatic plant per \nminute should increase, within an appropriate range. \n \nStep 5 — Experiment or investigation \nAn experiment is designed to test the hypothesis.\n\nA well-designed experiment should control relevant variables and collect reliable data. \nThree important types of variables are: \nIndependent variable \nThe factor deliberately changed by the researcher. \nExample: \nLight intensity \nDependent variable \nThe factor measured in response to the independent variable. \nExample: \nRate of oxygen production \nControlled variables \nFactors kept constant so that they do not interfere with the investigation. \nExamples: \n• \nTemperature  \n• \nPlant species  \n• \nAmount of water  \n• \nCarbon dioxide concentration  \n \n11. Control Groups \nA control provides a standard for comparison. \nFor example, when testing the effect of a particular substance on plant growth, one group may be \ngrown without the substance while another group receives it. \nThe group without the experimental treatment serves as the control. \nControls help scientists determine whether observed changes are actually associated with the \nexperimental variable. \n \n12. Collection and Analysis of Data \nScientists collect data during an investigation.\n\nData may be: \n• \nNumerical  \n• \nDescriptive  \n• \nTables  \n• \nGraphs  \n• \nImages  \n• \nStatistical measurements  \nRepeated measurements are often used to increase reliability. \nScientists analyse the results to determine whether the evidence supports or contradicts the \nhypothesis. \nImportantly: \nA hypothesis is not proven to be permanently true simply because an experiment supports \nit. \nAdditional evidence and repeated investigations are required. \n \n13. Scientific Theory \nA scientific theory is a well-supported explanation of a natural phenomenon that is supported by \na large body of evidence. \nExamples include: \n• \nCell theory  \n• \nEvolutionary theory  \n• \nGerm theory of disease  \nA scientific theory is much more than a simple guess. \nIn everyday language, the word "theory" can mean a speculation or idea. In science, however, a \ntheory represents an explanation that has received substantial support from evidence. \n \n14. Scientific Law \nA scientific law describes a consistent pattern or relationship observed in nature.\n\nA law generally describes what happens, whereas a theory provides an explanation for why or \nhow it happens. \nScientific laws do not necessarily explain the underlying mechanism. \n \n15. Hypothesis, Theory and Law: Key Difference \nTerm \nMeaning \nObservation Information obtained through observation or measurement \nHypothesis \nTestable proposed explanation or prediction \nTheory \nBroad explanation strongly supported by evidence \nScientific law Description of a consistent natural relationship \nA common misconception is: \nHypothesis → Theory → Law \nThis is incorrect. \nA scientific hypothesis does not automatically become a theory and then a law. \nThey serve different purposes. \n \n16. Characteristics of a Good Scientific Hypothesis \nA good hypothesis should be: \n1. Testable  \n2. Falsifiable  \n3. Based on existing observations or knowledge  \n4. Clearly stated  \n5. Capable of making predictions  \nFalsifiable \nA hypothesis is falsifiable if it is possible to obtain evidence that could show the hypothesis to be \nincorrect. \nFor example:\n\n"Increasing temperature increases enzyme activity up to an optimum temperature." \nThis can be tested experimentally. \n \n17. Reliability and Validity \nThese two concepts are important in biological investigations. \nReliability \nReliability refers to the consistency of results. \nAn experiment is more reliable when repeated measurements produce similar results. \nReliability can be improved through: \n• \nRepetition  \n• \nLarger sample sizes  \n• \nCareful measurement  \n• \nStandardised procedures  \nValidity \nValidity refers to whether an investigation actually measures what it is intended to measure. \nFor example, if an experiment claims to investigate the effect of light intensity on photosynthesis \nbut temperature varies significantly between treatments, the investigation may have reduced \nvalidity because temperature can also affect photosynthesis. \n \n18. Scientific Evidence \nScientific conclusions should be based on evidence rather than personal beliefs or assumptions. \nEvidence may come from: \n• \nDirect observations  \n• \nControlled experiments  \n• \nField studies  \n• \nMicroscopic observations  \n• \nMolecular analyses  \n• \nStatistical analyses\n\n• \nFossil evidence  \n• \nComparative studies  \nStrong scientific conclusions are supported by multiple lines of evidence where possible. \n \n19. Important A/L Concepts to Understand \nLife is organised \nLiving systems are organised into hierarchical levels. \nLife requires energy \nOrganisms require energy to maintain their structures and perform biological processes. \nLife responds to the environment \nOrganisms detect changes and respond to stimuli. \nLife maintains internal stability \nOrganisms regulate their internal conditions through homeostatic mechanisms. \nLife reproduces \nOrganisms produce new individuals through asexual or sexual reproduction. \nLife evolves \nPopulations change over generations, producing biological diversity. \nLife is based on cellular organisation \nThe cell is the basic structural and functional unit of living organisms. \n \n20. Common Misconceptions \nMisconception 1: "All living things move from place to place." \nIncorrect. \nMovement includes internal movement and directional growth. Plants can exhibit movement \nwithout locomotion. \nMisconception 2: "Plants do not respire." \nIncorrect.\n\nPlants perform cellular respiration continuously. Photosynthesis and respiration are different \nprocesses. \nMisconception 3: "Growth is only an increase in size." \nGrowth also involves increases in cell number, cell size and biological material. \nMisconception 4: "A theory is just a guess." \nIncorrect. \nA scientific theory is a strongly supported explanation based on substantial evidence. \nMisconception 5: "A hypothesis that is supported becomes a scientific law." \nIncorrect. \nHypotheses, theories and laws have different scientific meanings and functions. \nMisconception 6: "All organisms are multicellular." \nIncorrect. \nMany organisms are unicellular. \n \n21. High-Yield Definitions \nTerm \nDefinition \nBiology \nScientific study of life and living organisms \nMetabolism \nSum of chemical reactions occurring within an organism \nAnabolism \nMetabolic reactions that build complex molecules from simpler molecules \nCatabolism \nMetabolic reactions that break complex molecules into simpler molecules \nHomeostasis \nMaintenance of a relatively stable internal environment \nStimulus \nA detectable change that can produce a response \nReproduction \nBiological process by which organisms produce offspring \nHeredity \nTransmission of biological characteristics from parents to offspring \nAdaptation \nHeritable characteristic that improves survival and reproductive success in a \nparticular environment\n\nTerm \nDefinition \nHypothesis \nTestable proposed explanation or prediction \nTheory \nWell-supported scientific explanation \nScientific law \nDescription of a consistent natural relationship \nIndependent \nvariable \nVariable deliberately changed by the researcher \nDependent \nvariable \nVariable measured in response to the independent variable \nControl variable \nVariable deliberately kept constant \n \n22. Examination-Focused Questions \nShort-answer questions \n1. Define biology.  \n2. State five characteristics of living organisms.  \n3. Define metabolism.  \n4. Distinguish between anabolism and catabolism.  \n5. What is homeostasis?  \n6. What is meant by a stimulus?  \n7. Distinguish between autotrophic and heterotrophic nutrition.  \n8. Define adaptation.  \n9. What is a scientific hypothesis?  \n10. Distinguish between a hypothesis and a scientific theory.  \nStructured questions \nQuestion 1 \nA student investigates the effect of temperature on the activity of an enzyme. \na. Identify the independent variable. \nb. Identify the dependent variable.\n\nc. State two variables that should be controlled. \nd. Explain why repeated measurements should be taken. \ne. Explain why a hypothesis must be testable. \n \nQuestion 2 \nExplain how homeostasis contributes to the survival of an organism. Give two examples of \nconditions maintained through homeostasis. \n \nQuestion 3 \nExplain why biology is considered an interdisciplinary science. Give three examples of other \nscientific disciplines that contribute to biological research. \n \n23. Final Revision Summary \nBiology is the scientific study of life. Living organisms show a number of fundamental \ncharacteristics, including cellular organisation, metabolism, nutrition, respiration, growth, \nmovement, response to stimuli, reproduction, homeostasis, adaptation, heredity and \nevolution. \nBiology can be studied at multiple levels, ranging from molecules and cells to organisms, \npopulations, communities, ecosystems and the biosphere. \nThe subject has numerous specialised branches, including botany, zoology, microbiology, \ncytology, anatomy, physiology, genetics, ecology, taxonomy, molecular biology and \nbiotechnology. \nBiology is important to medicine, agriculture, environmental management, food production, \nbiotechnology, forensic science and conservation. \nScientific knowledge is developed through systematic investigation. Important concepts include \nobservation, hypothesis, prediction, experimentation, variables, controls, data analysis and \nevidence. \nFinally, remember the distinction: \nHypothesis = testable proposed explanation \nTheory = strongly supported explanation \nLaw = consistent description of a natural relationship\n\nThese distinctions, together with the characteristics of life and the scientific method, form the \nconceptual foundation for the rest of A/L Biology.	COMPLETED	69	2026-08-19 18:09:10.582995	2026-08-19 18:12:51.8828	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
995	Lesson 1: Ecosystem components, energy flow, and biogeochemical cycles - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 1: Ecosystem components, energy flow, and biogeochemical cycles	PDF	uploads/pdf/3e858a83b06d47b2bba176a4f80e0d92.pdf	\N	Lesson 1 — Ecosystem Components, Energy Flow, and Biogeochemical Cycles \nIntroduction \nAn ecosystem is a functional unit of nature in which living organisms interact with one another \nand with the non-living components of their environment. \nEcosystems range from small systems such as a pond to large systems such as forests, oceans \nand the global biosphere. \nThe functioning of ecosystems depends on interactions among organisms, the flow of energy and \nthe cycling of chemical elements. \n \n1. Ecology \nEcology is the scientific study of interactions between organisms and their environment. \nIt examines relationships among \n• \nOrganisms  \n• \nPopulations  \n• \nCommunities  \n• \nEcosystems  \n• \nBiomes  \n• \nThe biosphere  \n \n2. Environment \nThe environment consists of all external conditions that influence an organism. \nIt includes both living and non-living components. \n \n3. Biotic Components \nBiotic components are the living components of an ecosystem. \nThey include \n• \nPlants  \n• \nAnimals\n\n• \nFungi  \n• \nBacteria  \n• \nArchaea  \n• \nProtists  \n• \nOther microorganisms  \n \n4. Abiotic Components \nAbiotic components are the non-living physical and chemical factors of an ecosystem. \nImportant abiotic factors include \n• \nLight  \n• \nTemperature  \n• \nWater  \n• \nSoil  \n• \nAir  \n• \nHumidity  \n• \npH  \n• \nSalinity  \n• \nMineral nutrients  \n \n5. Levels of Ecological Organisation \nEcological organisation can be represented as \nOrganism → Population → Community → Ecosystem → Biome → Biosphere \nEach level represents increasing complexity. \n \n6. Organism \nAn organism is an individual living entity. \nExamples include\n\n• \nA mango tree  \n• \nA frog  \n• \nAn elephant  \n• \nA bacterium  \n \n7. Population \nA population is a group of individuals of the same species living in a particular area and capable \nof interacting and reproducing. \nExamples include \n• \nA population of elephants in a national park  \n• \nA population of rice plants in a paddy field  \n \n8. Community \nA community consists of populations of different species living and interacting in the same area. \nFor example, a forest community may contain \n• \nTrees  \n• \nBirds  \n• \nMammals  \n• \nInsects  \n• \nFungi  \n• \nBacteria  \n \n9. Ecosystem \nAn ecosystem consists of a biological community together with its abiotic environment. \nThe organisms interact with one another and with physical and chemical factors. \n \n10. Biome\n\nA biome is a large geographical region characterised by particular climate conditions and \ndominant forms of vegetation and animal life. \nExamples include \n• \nTropical rainforest  \n• \nDesert  \n• \nGrassland  \n• \nTundra  \n• \nTemperate forest  \n \n11. Biosphere \nThe biosphere is the global zone of life. \nIt includes all ecosystems on Earth and extends through portions of the atmosphere, hydrosphere \nand lithosphere where living organisms occur. \n \n12. Ecosystem Structure \nAn ecosystem contains two major components \n• \nBiotic components  \n• \nAbiotic components  \nThe biotic component can further be divided according to how organisms obtain energy and \nnutrients. \n \n13. Producers \nProducers are organisms that manufacture organic compounds from inorganic substances using \nan external energy source. \nMost ecosystems depend on photosynthetic organisms. \nExamples include \n• \nGreen plants  \n• \nAlgae\n\n• \nCyanobacteria  \n \n14. Photosynthetic Producers \nPhotosynthetic organisms use light energy to convert carbon dioxide and water into organic \ncompounds. \nThe simplified equation is \nCarbon dioxide + Water + Light energy → Glucose + Oxygen \nThis process is called photosynthesis. \n \n15. Chemosynthetic Producers \nSome microorganisms obtain energy by oxidising inorganic substances rather than using \nsunlight. \nThis process is associated with chemosynthesis or chemoautotrophic metabolism. \nExamples include microorganisms that obtain energy from compounds containing \n• \nAmmonia  \n• \nNitrite  \n• \nHydrogen sulfide  \n• \nIron  \n \n16. Consumers \nConsumers are organisms that obtain organic nutrients by feeding on other organisms. \nThey cannot manufacture sufficient organic food from inorganic substances. \nConsumers are also called heterotrophs. \n \n17. Primary Consumers \nPrimary consumers feed directly on producers. \nThey are generally herbivores. \nExamples include\n\n• \nDeer  \n• \nCattle  \n• \nGrasshoppers  \n• \nCaterpillars  \n \n18. Secondary Consumers \nSecondary consumers feed primarily on primary consumers. \nThey are often carnivores or omnivores. \nFor example, a frog feeding on a grasshopper acts as a secondary consumer. \n \n19. Tertiary Consumers \nTertiary consumers feed on organisms at lower consumer levels. \nThey may occupy high trophic positions within food chains. \nExamples can include \n• \nLarge predatory fish  \n• \nEagles  \n• \nLeopards  \n \n20. Omnivores \nOmnivores consume both plant and animal material. \nDepending on what they consume, an omnivore can occupy different trophic levels. \nHumans are an example of an omnivorous species. \n \n21. Decomposers \nDecomposers break down dead organic material and waste products. \nImportant decomposers include \n• \nBacteria\n\n• \nFungi  \nThey release simpler substances and contribute to nutrient recycling. \n \n22. Detritivores \nDetritivores consume dead organic material called detritus. \nExamples include \n• \nEarthworms  \n• \nWoodlice  \n• \nCertain insects  \nDetritivores fragment organic material, increasing the surface area available for microbial \ndecomposition. \n \n23. Decomposers and Detritivores \nDecomposers and detritivores have different roles. \nDetritivores physically consume and fragment dead organic material. \nDecomposers chemically break down organic compounds into simpler substances. \nBoth contribute to nutrient cycling. \n \n24. Trophic Levels \nA trophic level represents the position an organism occupies in a feeding relationship. \nThe basic trophic structure is \nProducers → Primary consumers → Secondary consumers → Tertiary consumers \nEnergy decreases as it moves through successive trophic levels. \n \n25. Food Chain \nA food chain is a sequence showing the transfer of food and energy from one organism to \nanother. \nExample\n\nGrass → Grasshopper → Frog → Snake → Eagle \nEach arrow represents the direction of energy transfer. \n \n26. Food Web \nA food web consists of interconnected food chains within an ecosystem. \nFood webs are generally more realistic representations of ecosystems because most organisms \nconsume and are consumed by multiple species. \n \n27. Grazing Food Chain \nA grazing food chain begins with a living producer. \nExample \nGrass → Grasshopper → Frog → Snake \nEnergy enters the chain through photosynthesis. \n \n28. Detritus Food Chain \nA detritus food chain begins with dead organic material. \nExample \nDead leaves → Earthworm → Bird → Hawk \nThis pathway is particularly important in forest and soil ecosystems. \n \n29. Trophic Interactions \nOrganisms within ecosystems interact through relationships such as \n• \nPredation  \n• \nHerbivory  \n• \nCompetition  \n• \nMutualism  \n• \nCommensalism\n\n• \nParasitism  \nThese interactions influence population sizes and community structure. \n \n30. Predation \nPredation occurs when one organism kills and consumes another organism. \nThe organism that captures the prey is the predator. \nThe organism being consumed is the prey. \n \n31. Herbivory \nHerbivory occurs when animals feed on plants or algae. \nHerbivores obtain energy and nutrients from producers. \n \n32. Competition \nCompetition occurs when organisms require the same limited resource. \nResources may include \n• \nFood  \n• \nWater  \n• \nLight  \n• \nSpace  \n• \nNutrients  \n• \nMates  \nCompetition can occur between individuals of the same species or between different species. \n \n33. Mutualism \nMutualism is an interaction in which both participating species benefit. \nExamples include \n• \nMycorrhizal associations\n\n• \nRhizobium and legume associations  \n• \nPollination relationships  \n \n34. Commensalism \nCommensalism is an interaction in which one species benefits while the other is neither \nsignificantly helped nor harmed. \n \n35. Parasitism \nParasitism occurs when one organism benefits while the host is harmed. \nThe parasite obtains resources from the host. \nExamples include \n• \nTapeworms in humans  \n• \nTicks feeding on mammals  \n• \nCertain parasitic plants  \n \n36. Energy in Ecosystems \nEnergy is required for all biological processes. \nThe major energy source for most ecosystems is sunlight. \nEnergy enters ecosystems through producers and then passes through trophic levels. \n \n37. Energy Flow \nEnergy flow through an ecosystem is unidirectional. \nA simplified pathway is \nSun → Producers → Consumers → Decomposers → Environment \nEnergy is not recycled in the same way as chemical nutrients. \n \n38. Solar Energy\n\nThe Sun provides the primary energy input for most ecosystems. \nPhotosynthetic organisms capture a portion of incoming solar radiation and convert it into \nchemical energy. \n \n39. Gross Primary Productivity \nGross primary productivity, or GPP, is the total amount of chemical energy produced by \nphotosynthetic organisms through photosynthesis over a given period. \n \n40. Plant Respiration \nProducers use some of the organic compounds they produce for cellular respiration. \nEnergy released through respiration supports \n• \nGrowth  \n• \nMaintenance  \n• \nActive transport  \n• \nReproduction  \n• \nOther cellular activities  \n \n41. Net Primary Productivity \nNet primary productivity, or NPP, is the amount of energy remaining after producers have used \nsome of the energy they captured for respiration. \nThe relationship is \nNPP = GPP − Respiration by producers \nNPP represents the energy available for growth and for consumption by organisms at higher \ntrophic levels. \n \n42. Secondary Productivity \nSecondary productivity is the production of new biomass by consumers. \nIt depends on the amount of food energy obtained and assimilated by consumers.\n\n43. Energy Loss Between Trophic Levels \nEnergy is lost as it moves through trophic levels. \nLosses occur through \n• \nCellular respiration  \n• \nHeat production  \n• \nMovement  \n• \nExcretion  \n• \nUndigested material  \n• \nMaintenance activities  \nTherefore, only a fraction of energy at one trophic level becomes biomass available to the next \nlevel. \n \n44. Ecological Efficiency \nEcological efficiency describes the proportion of energy transferred from one trophic level to the \nnext. \nThe efficiency varies among ecosystems and organisms. \nA commonly used approximation is that roughly 10 percent of energy is transferred between \ntrophic levels, although actual values can vary considerably. \n \n45. Energy Pyramid \nAn energy pyramid represents the amount of energy available at successive trophic levels. \nThe producer level contains the greatest amount of available energy. \nEnergy decreases toward higher trophic levels. \n \n46. Biomass Pyramid \nA biomass pyramid represents the total mass of living biological material at different trophic \nlevels.\n\nIn many terrestrial ecosystems, biomass decreases at higher trophic levels. \nHowever, some aquatic ecosystems can have an inverted biomass pyramid because \nphytoplankton have rapid turnover. \n \n47. Pyramid of Numbers \nA pyramid of numbers represents the number of organisms at each trophic level. \nIt may be upright or inverted depending on the ecosystem. \nFor example, one large tree can support many herbivorous insects, resulting in an inverted \npattern. \n \n48. Biogeochemical Cycles \nChemical elements required by organisms continuously move between living organisms and the \nphysical environment. \nThese pathways are called biogeochemical cycles. \nImportant cycles include \n• \nWater cycle  \n• \nCarbon cycle  \n• \nNitrogen cycle  \n• \nPhosphorus cycle  \n• \nSulfur cycle  \n \n49. Water Cycle \nThe water cycle describes the movement of water through the atmosphere, land, organisms and \nbodies of water. \nMajor processes include \n• \nEvaporation  \n• \nTranspiration  \n• \nCondensation\n\n• \nPrecipitation  \n• \nInfiltration  \n• \nRunoff  \n• \nGroundwater flow  \n \n50. Evaporation \nEvaporation is the conversion of liquid water into water vapour. \nSolar energy drives much of the evaporation from \n• \nOceans  \n• \nLakes  \n• \nRivers  \n• \nSoil surfaces  \n \n51. Transpiration \nTranspiration is the loss of water vapour from plants, primarily through stomata in leaves. \nIt contributes significantly to the movement of water from terrestrial ecosystems into the \natmosphere. \n \n52. Condensation \nCondensation occurs when water vapour cools and changes into liquid water droplets. \nIt contributes to cloud formation. \n \n53. Precipitation \nPrecipitation occurs when water falls from the atmosphere to Earth's surface. \nForms include \n• \nRain  \n• \nSnow\n\n• \nSleet  \n• \nHail  \n \n54. Runoff \nRunoff is water that flows across the surface of land into streams, rivers, lakes and oceans. \nIt can transport dissolved substances and soil particles. \n \n55. Carbon Cycle \nCarbon is an essential component of biological molecules. \nThe carbon cycle involves movement of carbon between \n• \nAtmosphere  \n• \nOrganisms  \n• \nSoil  \n• \nOceans  \n• \nRocks  \n• \nFossil fuels  \n \n56. Carbon Dioxide Fixation \nPlants, algae and cyanobacteria remove carbon dioxide from the atmosphere or water during \nphotosynthesis. \nThe carbon becomes incorporated into organic molecules. \n \n57. Cellular Respiration \nDuring cellular respiration, organisms break down organic molecules and release carbon dioxide. \nThis returns carbon to the atmosphere or surrounding environment. \n \n58. Decomposition and Carbon\n\nDecomposers break down dead organisms and organic waste. \nCarbon-containing compounds are released through microbial respiration and other \ndecomposition processes. \n \n59. Combustion \nBurning organic material and fossil fuels releases carbon dioxide into the atmosphere. \nHuman activities have greatly increased the rate of fossil-fuel combustion. \n \n60. Ocean Carbon \nOceans absorb and release carbon dioxide. \nCarbon can exist in seawater in several chemical forms, including dissolved carbon dioxide, \nbicarbonate and carbonate. \nMarine organisms also incorporate carbon into their biomass and shells. \n \n61. Nitrogen Cycle \nNitrogen is essential for \n• \nAmino acids  \n• \nProteins  \n• \nNucleic acids  \n• \nATP  \n• \nChlorophyll  \nAlthough atmospheric nitrogen is abundant, most organisms cannot directly use nitrogen gas. \n \n62. Nitrogen Fixation \nNitrogen fixation converts atmospheric nitrogen into biologically usable nitrogen compounds. \nIt can occur through \n• \nBiological processes  \n• \nLightning\n\n• \nIndustrial processes  \n \n63. Nitrification \nNitrification is the microbial oxidation of reduced nitrogen compounds. \nIt generally occurs in two major stages \nAmmonia → Nitrite → Nitrate \nDifferent groups of microorganisms carry out these reactions. \n \n64. Assimilation \nAssimilation occurs when plants absorb inorganic nitrogen compounds and incorporate nitrogen \ninto organic molecules. \nAnimals obtain nitrogen primarily by consuming plants or other animals. \n \n65. Ammonification \nAmmonification occurs during decomposition when organic nitrogen compounds are converted \ninto ammonia or ammonium. \nMicroorganisms play an important role in this process. \n \n66. Denitrification \nDenitrification is the microbial conversion of nitrate into gaseous nitrogen compounds under \nlow-oxygen conditions. \nIt returns nitrogen to the atmosphere. \n \n67. Phosphorus Cycle \nPhosphorus is essential for \n• \nATP  \n• \nDNA  \n• \nRNA\n\n• \nPhospholipids  \n• \nBones and teeth  \nUnlike carbon and nitrogen, phosphorus has no major atmospheric gaseous phase in its natural \ncycle. \n \n68. Weathering \nPhosphate-containing rocks undergo weathering and release phosphate ions into soil and water. \nPlants can absorb these phosphate compounds. \n \n69. Phosphorus Assimilation \nPlants absorb phosphate from soil and incorporate phosphorus into biological molecules. \nAnimals obtain phosphorus by consuming plants or other organisms. \n \n70. Decomposition in the Phosphorus Cycle \nWhen organisms die or produce waste, decomposers break down organic phosphorus \ncompounds. \nPhosphorus is returned to soil and water in inorganic forms. \n \n71. Sedimentation \nPhosphorus can eventually enter aquatic sediments. \nOver geological time, sediments can form phosphate-containing rocks. \n \n72. Sulfur Cycle \nSulfur is an important component of certain amino acids and proteins. \nSulfur moves among \n• \nAtmosphere  \n• \nSoil  \n• \nWater\n\n• \nRocks  \n• \nOrganisms  \n \n73. Sources of Sulfur \nSulfur can enter ecosystems through \n• \nWeathering of rocks  \n• \nVolcanic activity  \n• \nDecomposition  \n• \nOcean processes  \n• \nHuman activities  \n \n74. Microorganisms in Biogeochemical Cycles \nMicroorganisms are essential to many nutrient cycles. \nThey participate in processes such as \n• \nNitrogen fixation  \n• \nNitrification  \n• \nDenitrification  \n• \nDecomposition  \n• \nCarbon transformation  \n• \nSulfur transformation  \nWithout microbial activity, nutrient recycling would be severely disrupted. \n \n75. Nutrient Cycling \nUnlike energy, chemical nutrients are continually recycled within ecosystems. \nThe general pathway is \nEnvironment → Producers → Consumers → Decomposers → Environment \nThis allows essential elements to be reused.\n\n76. Energy Flow Compared with Nutrient Cycling \nEnergy flow \nNutrient cycling \nPrimarily enters as sunlight \nElements originate from environmental reservoirs \nMoves through trophic levels \nElements move repeatedly between organisms and \nenvironment \nEnergy is eventually dissipated as \nheat \nChemical elements are recycled \nEssentially one-way \nCyclic \n \n77. Limiting Nutrients \nA limiting nutrient is a nutrient whose availability restricts biological growth. \nCommon limiting nutrients include \n• \nNitrogen  \n• \nPhosphorus  \nThe limiting nutrient can vary depending on the ecosystem. \n \n78. Ecological Productivity \nEcological productivity refers to the rate at which biomass or energy is produced within an \necosystem. \nPrimary productivity is associated with producers. \nSecondary productivity is associated with consumers. \n \n79. Factors Affecting Primary Productivity \nPrimary productivity can be influenced by \n• \nLight intensity  \n• \nTemperature\n\n• \nWater availability  \n• \nCarbon dioxide concentration  \n• \nNutrient availability  \n• \nLeaf area  \n• \nPhotosynthetic efficiency  \n \n80. Aquatic Productivity \nIn aquatic ecosystems, productivity is influenced by factors such as \n• \nLight penetration  \n• \nNutrient availability  \n• \nTemperature  \n• \nWater movement  \n• \nPhytoplankton abundance  \n \n81. Eutrophication \nEutrophication occurs when excessive nutrients enter an aquatic ecosystem. \nNitrogen and phosphorus enrichment can cause rapid growth of algae and aquatic plants. \n \n82. Algal Blooms \nExcessive nutrient availability can produce algal blooms. \nWhen large amounts of algae die, decomposing microorganisms consume oxygen. \nThis can lead to oxygen depletion. \n \n83. Dead Zones \nSevere oxygen depletion in aquatic environments can produce areas called dead zones. \nLow oxygen levels can cause fish and other aquatic organisms to die or leave the affected region.\n\n84. Human Influence on Biogeochemical Cycles \nHuman activities can alter natural nutrient cycles through \n• \nFossil-fuel combustion  \n• \nDeforestation  \n• \nAgriculture  \n• \nFertiliser use  \n• \nIndustrial activity  \n• \nMining  \n• \nWaste disposal  \nThese changes can affect climate, water quality and ecosystem stability. \n \n85. Ecosystem Stability \nEcosystem stability depends on interactions between organisms and environmental processes. \nImportant factors include \n• \nBiodiversity  \n• \nNutrient availability  \n• \nEnergy input  \n• \nPopulation interactions  \n• \nEnvironmental conditions  \n• \nDisturbance regimes  \n \n86. Key Terms \nEcology \nScientific study of interactions between organisms and their environment. \nEcosystem \nA community of organisms interacting with its physical and chemical environment. \nProducer\n\nOrganism that produces organic compounds from inorganic substances using an external energy \nsource. \nConsumer \nOrganism that obtains organic nutrients by feeding on other organisms. \nDecomposer \nOrganism that chemically breaks down dead organic matter and waste. \nFood chain \nSequence showing the transfer of energy and matter through feeding relationships. \nFood web \nNetwork of interconnected food chains. \nTrophic level \nFeeding position occupied by an organism in an ecosystem. \nPrimary productivity \nRate at which producers generate new organic matter. \nGPP \nTotal energy captured by producers through photosynthesis. \nNPP \nEnergy remaining after producer respiration. \nBiogeochemical cycle \nMovement and recycling of chemical elements between organisms and the physical environment. \nNitrogen fixation \nConversion of atmospheric nitrogen into biologically useful nitrogen compounds. \nNitrification \nMicrobial conversion of ammonia or ammonium into nitrite and nitrate. \nDenitrification \nMicrobial conversion of nitrate into gaseous nitrogen compounds. \nEutrophication\n\nNutrient enrichment of an aquatic ecosystem that can cause excessive biological growth and \noxygen depletion. \n \n87. Final Summary \nAn ecosystem is composed of living organisms and the physical and chemical environment with \nwhich they interact. Producers capture energy, consumers transfer that energy through feeding \nrelationships and decomposers return nutrients to the environment. \nFood chains describe linear feeding relationships, while food webs represent the complex \ninterconnected feeding relationships found in real ecosystems. Organisms occupy different \ntrophic levels, and energy decreases as it moves through these levels because much of it is lost \nthrough respiration and other metabolic processes. \nPrimary productivity describes the production of organic matter by producers. Gross primary \nproductivity represents the total energy captured, while net primary productivity represents the \nenergy remaining after producer respiration. \nUnlike energy, chemical elements are continuously recycled. The water, carbon, nitrogen, \nphosphorus and sulfur cycles move essential substances between organisms and their \nenvironment. Microorganisms are particularly important in nutrient cycling. \nHuman activities can significantly alter these natural cycles. Excess fertiliser use, fossil-fuel \ncombustion, deforestation and waste discharge can contribute to problems such as \neutrophication, climate change and deterioration of ecosystem health. \nUnderstanding energy flow and biogeochemical cycling is fundamental to understanding how \necosystems function and how human activities influence the natural environment.	COMPLETED	115	2026-08-19 18:12:04.580421	2026-08-19 18:12:51.883017	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
996	Lesson 2: Global biomes and Sri Lankan natural ecosystems - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 2: Global biomes and Sri Lankan natural ecosystems	PDF	uploads/pdf/bf6561ec7aa94be1b1ffb0232af794da.pdf	\N	Lesson 2 — Global Biomes and Sri Lankan Natural Ecosystems \nIntroduction \nEarth contains a wide variety of ecosystems shaped by climate, geography, soil, water \navailability and biological interactions. Large regions with characteristic climate conditions and \ndominant vegetation are known as biomes. \nSri Lanka, despite its relatively small geographical area, contains a remarkable diversity of \nterrestrial, freshwater and coastal ecosystems. This diversity is largely associated with \ndifferences in rainfall, elevation, temperature, geology and soil conditions. \n \n1. Biome \nA biome is a large ecological region characterised mainly by its climate and dominant \nvegetation. \nA biome contains many different ecosystems that share broadly similar environmental \nconditions. \nMajor terrestrial biomes include \n• \nTropical rainforests  \n• \nTropical seasonal forests  \n• \nSavannas and grasslands  \n• \nDeserts  \n• \nTemperate forests  \n• \nBoreal forests  \n• \nTundra  \n \n2. Climate and Biomes \nClimate is one of the most important factors determining the distribution of biomes. \nImportant climatic factors include \n• \nTemperature  \n• \nPrecipitation  \n• \nSeasonal variation\n\n• \nLength of growing season  \nTemperature and rainfall strongly influence the types of plants capable of surviving in a region. \n \n3. Tropical Rainforest \nTropical rainforests occur mainly near the equator where temperatures remain high throughout \nthe year and rainfall is abundant. \nCharacteristics include \n• \nHigh annual rainfall  \n• \nHigh temperature  \n• \nHigh humidity  \n• \nHigh species diversity  \n• \nDense vegetation  \n• \nComplex vertical structure  \n \n4. Structure of Tropical Rainforests \nTropical rainforests often contain several vegetation layers. \nThese include \n• \nEmergent layer  \n• \nCanopy  \n• \nUnderstory  \n• \nForest floor  \nThe canopy receives high levels of sunlight, while the forest floor receives much less light. \n \n5. Tropical Rainforest Biodiversity \nTropical rainforests contain exceptionally high biodiversity. \nThey support \n• \nTrees\n\n• \nClimbers  \n• \nEpiphytes  \n• \nMammals  \n• \nBirds  \n• \nReptiles  \n• \nAmphibians  \n• \nInsects  \n• \nFungi  \n• \nMicroorganisms  \nMany species have highly specialised ecological relationships. \n \n6. Tropical Seasonal Forest \nTropical seasonal forests occur in regions where rainfall is strongly seasonal. \nTrees may experience periods of reduced water availability. \nSome plants respond by \n• \nLosing leaves  \n• \nReducing transpiration  \n• \nEntering periods of reduced growth  \n \n7. Savanna \nA savanna is a tropical or subtropical ecosystem dominated by grasses with scattered trees and \nshrubs. \nImportant characteristics include \n• \nSeasonal rainfall  \n• \nPeriodic drought  \n• \nGrass-dominated vegetation  \n• \nFrequent fire in many regions\n\nFire can influence vegetation structure and nutrient cycling. \n \n8. Grassland \nGrasslands are ecosystems in which grasses are the dominant vegetation. \nThey generally occur where conditions are insufficient for extensive forest development. \nGrasslands can support \n• \nGrazing mammals  \n• \nHerbivorous insects  \n• \nPredators  \n• \nSoil microorganisms  \n \n9. Desert \nDeserts receive very little precipitation. \nThey can occur in both hot and cold regions. \nCharacteristics include \n• \nLow rainfall  \n• \nHigh evaporation  \n• \nLimited vegetation  \n• \nLarge temperature fluctuations in many deserts  \n \n10. Desert Adaptations \nDesert organisms possess adaptations that reduce water loss or improve water acquisition. \nPlant adaptations can include \n• \nReduced leaves  \n• \nThick cuticles  \n• \nExtensive root systems  \n• \nWater storage tissues\n\n• \nStomatal regulation  \nAnimals may be nocturnal to avoid extreme daytime temperatures. \n \n11. Temperate Forest \nTemperate forests occur in regions with moderate temperatures and seasonal variation. \nThey can include \n• \nDeciduous forests  \n• \nTemperate rainforests  \n• \nMixed forests  \nDeciduous trees commonly shed their leaves during unfavourable seasons. \n \n12. Temperate Grassland \nTemperate grasslands generally have \n• \nModerate rainfall  \n• \nSeasonal temperatures  \n• \nGrass-dominated vegetation  \n• \nRelatively fertile soils  \nLarge areas have been converted to agricultural land. \n \n13. Boreal Forest \nThe boreal forest, also called the taiga, occurs mainly at high northern latitudes. \nIt is dominated by coniferous trees such as \n• \nSpruce  \n• \nFir  \n• \nPine  \nWinters are long and cold, while growing seasons are relatively short.\n\n14. Tundra \nTundra occurs mainly at high latitudes and high elevations. \nCharacteristics include \n• \nVery low temperatures  \n• \nShort growing season  \n• \nLow-growing vegetation  \n• \nPermafrost in many regions  \nTypical vegetation includes \n• \nMosses  \n• \nLichens  \n• \nGrasses  \n• \nSmall shrubs  \n \n15. Aquatic Ecosystems \nAquatic ecosystems are broadly divided into \n• \nFreshwater ecosystems  \n• \nMarine ecosystems  \nFreshwater ecosystems include rivers, streams, lakes, wetlands and ponds. \nMarine ecosystems include oceans, coral reefs, estuaries and coastal habitats. \n \n16. Freshwater Ecosystems \nFreshwater contains relatively low concentrations of dissolved salts. \nMajor freshwater ecosystems include \n• \nRivers  \n• \nStreams  \n• \nLakes  \n• \nPonds\n\n• \nMarshes  \n• \nSwamps  \n \n17. Lotic Ecosystems \nLotic ecosystems contain flowing water. \nExamples include \n• \nRivers  \n• \nStreams  \nWater movement strongly influences oxygen concentration, sediment distribution and organism \nadaptations. \n \n18. Lentic Ecosystems \nLentic ecosystems contain relatively still water. \nExamples include \n• \nLakes  \n• \nPonds  \n• \nReservoirs  \nThey commonly develop distinct ecological zones based on light, temperature and depth. \n \n19. Wetlands \nWetlands are ecosystems where water covers or saturates the soil for sufficient periods to \ninfluence soil and biological characteristics. \nExamples include \n• \nMarshes  \n• \nSwamps  \n• \nMangrove ecosystems  \n• \nFloodplains  \nWetlands provide important ecological services.\n\n20. Ecological Importance of Wetlands \nWetlands can \n• \nStore water  \n• \nReduce flooding  \n• \nFilter pollutants  \n• \nStore carbon  \n• \nProvide habitat  \n• \nSupport fisheries  \n• \nRecharge groundwater  \nThey are among the most important ecosystems for maintaining water quality and biodiversity. \n \n21. Marine Ecosystems \nMarine ecosystems contain saltwater. \nMajor marine environments include \n• \nOpen oceans  \n• \nCoastal waters  \n• \nCoral reefs  \n• \nEstuaries  \n• \nSeagrass ecosystems  \n \n22. Estuaries \nAn estuary is a coastal ecosystem where freshwater from rivers mixes with seawater. \nEstuaries are generally highly productive. \nThey provide nursery habitats for many fish and invertebrates. \n \n23. Coral Reefs\n\nCoral reefs are highly diverse marine ecosystems formed largely by reef-building corals. \nThey support numerous organisms including \n• \nFish  \n• \nMolluscs  \n• \nCrustaceans  \n• \nSponges  \n• \nAlgae  \n• \nOther invertebrates  \nCoral reefs are especially sensitive to changes in temperature and water quality. \n \n24. Mangrove Ecosystems \nMangroves are coastal ecosystems dominated by salt-tolerant trees and shrubs. \nThey occur in sheltered tropical and subtropical coastal environments. \nMangroves are particularly important in tropical coastal regions. \n \n25. Mangrove Adaptations \nMangrove plants possess adaptations for living in saline, waterlogged environments. \nThese can include \n• \nSalt exclusion  \n• \nSalt excretion  \n• \nSpecialised roots  \n• \nAerial roots  \n• \nViviparous propagules in some species  \n \n26. Ecological Importance of Mangroves \nMangroves \n• \nProtect coastlines\n\n• \nReduce erosion  \n• \nTrap sediments  \n• \nStore carbon  \n• \nProvide nursery habitats  \n• \nSupport fisheries  \n• \nProvide habitat for birds and other animals  \n \n27. Sri Lanka's Ecological Diversity \nSri Lanka contains a remarkable range of ecosystems despite its relatively small land area. \nMajor factors contributing to this diversity include \n• \nTropical climate  \n• \nMonsoonal rainfall  \n• \nElevation  \n• \nTopography  \n• \nGeological variation  \n• \nSoil diversity  \n• \nSeasonal differences in rainfall  \n \n28. Climatic Zones of Sri Lanka \nSri Lanka is traditionally divided into three major climatic zones. \nThese are \n• \nWet zone  \n• \nIntermediate zone  \n• \nDry zone  \nThe distribution of natural vegetation is strongly influenced by these climatic differences. \n \n29. Wet Zone\n\nThe wet zone receives relatively high rainfall throughout much of the year. \nIt supports vegetation such as \n• \nEvergreen forests  \n• \nLowland rainforests  \n• \nMoist forests  \nThe southwestern region contains some of Sri Lanka's most biologically diverse forests. \n \n30. Dry Zone \nThe dry zone experiences a pronounced dry season. \nNatural vegetation includes \n• \nDry mixed evergreen forests  \n• \nDry forests  \n• \nScrublands  \n• \nGrasslands  \nPlants in these ecosystems have adaptations to seasonal water scarcity. \n \n31. Intermediate Zone \nThe intermediate zone has climatic conditions between those of the wet and dry zones. \nIt contains vegetation influenced by both rainfall patterns. \nThe boundaries between climatic zones are not always sharply defined. \n \n32. Lowland Rainforests \nLowland rainforests occur mainly in the wet zone at lower elevations. \nThey are characterised by \n• \nHigh rainfall  \n• \nEvergreen vegetation  \n• \nDense canopy\n\n• \nHigh humidity  \n• \nHigh biodiversity  \n• \nHigh endemism  \n \n33. Sinharaja Forest \nThe Sinharaja Forest Reserve is one of Sri Lanka's most important rainforest ecosystems. \nIt contains a high diversity of endemic plants and animals. \nIts conservation importance is internationally recognised. \n \n34. Endemism in Sri Lanka \nEndemism refers to the occurrence of a species naturally restricted to a particular geographical \nregion. \nSri Lanka has high levels of endemism, particularly among \n• \nAmphibians  \n• \nReptiles  \n• \nFreshwater fish  \n• \nPlants  \n• \nInvertebrates  \nThis makes habitat conservation particularly important. \n \n35. Montane Forests \nMontane forests occur at higher elevations. \nThey experience \n• \nLower temperatures  \n• \nHigh humidity  \n• \nFrequent cloud cover  \n• \nStrong elevation-related environmental gradients\n\nVegetation is generally shorter and structurally different from lowland rainforest. \n \n36. Cloud Forests \nCloud forests are high-elevation forests frequently influenced by persistent cloud and mist. \nThe high atmospheric moisture supports organisms such as \n• \nMosses  \n• \nLiverworts  \n• \nFerns  \n• \nEpiphytes  \n \n37. Grasslands of Sri Lanka \nSri Lanka contains several types of natural and semi-natural grasslands. \nHighland grasslands occur in the central highlands. \nThey form distinctive landscapes and provide habitats for specialised species. \n \n38. Dry Mixed Evergreen Forests \nDry mixed evergreen forests are characteristic of much of Sri Lanka's dry zone. \nThey contain a mixture of evergreen and seasonally responsive plant species. \nThese forests provide important habitats for large mammals. \n \n39. Riverine Ecosystems \nRiverine ecosystems occur along rivers and streams. \nThey contain vegetation adapted to periodic flooding and water availability. \nThey provide \n• \nWildlife corridors  \n• \nAquatic habitats  \n• \nWater resources\n\n• \nNutrient transport  \n \n40. Freshwater Ecosystems of Sri Lanka \nSri Lanka contains many freshwater ecosystems including \n• \nRivers  \n• \nStreams  \n• \nLakes  \n• \nReservoirs  \n• \nPonds  \n• \nWetlands  \nThese ecosystems support diverse freshwater organisms. \n \n41. Sri Lankan Freshwater Biodiversity \nSri Lanka has significant freshwater biodiversity. \nImportant groups include \n• \nFreshwater fish  \n• \nAmphibians  \n• \nAquatic plants  \n• \nInvertebrates  \n• \nFreshwater crustaceans  \nMany freshwater species have restricted distributions and are vulnerable to habitat modification. \n \n42. Coastal Ecosystems of Sri Lanka \nSri Lanka has extensive coastal ecosystems. \nImportant coastal ecosystems include \n• \nMangroves  \n• \nCoral reefs\n\n• \nSeagrass beds  \n• \nEstuaries  \n• \nLagoons  \n• \nSalt marshes  \n• \nSandy beaches  \n• \nRocky shores  \n \n43. Lagoons \nA lagoon is a shallow coastal water body separated from the sea partially or completely by \nbarriers such as sandbars. \nSri Lankan lagoons support \n• \nFish  \n• \nCrustaceans  \n• \nMolluscs  \n• \nBirds  \n• \nAquatic plants  \nThey also support important human livelihoods. \n \n44. Seagrass Ecosystems \nSeagrasses are flowering plants adapted to living submerged in marine environments. \nSeagrass beds provide \n• \nNursery habitats  \n• \nFood for marine organisms  \n• \nSediment stabilisation  \n• \nCarbon storage  \n• \nHabitat for many invertebrates and fish\n\n45. Coral Reefs of Sri Lanka \nSri Lanka contains several coral reef ecosystems. \nThey are particularly associated with shallow tropical marine environments. \nCoral reefs support high biodiversity and provide coastal protection. \n \n46. Threats to Coral Reefs \nMajor threats include \n• \nRising sea temperatures  \n• \nCoral bleaching  \n• \nSedimentation  \n• \nPollution  \n• \nOverfishing  \n• \nDestructive fishing  \n• \nCoastal development  \n \n47. Coral Bleaching \nCoral bleaching occurs when corals lose much of their symbiotic photosynthetic algae, \ncommonly called zooxanthellae. \nThe coral becomes pale or white. \nProlonged or severe bleaching can cause coral death. \n \n48. Sri Lankan Mangroves \nSri Lanka contains mangrove ecosystems around sheltered coastal environments, lagoons and \nestuaries. \nMangroves support \n• \nFish  \n• \nCrabs  \n• \nMolluscs\n\n• \nBirds  \n• \nReptiles  \n• \nOther organisms  \nThey are particularly important for coastal protection. \n \n49. Ecosystem Services \nEcosystem services are benefits that humans obtain from ecosystems. \nThey can be grouped into four major categories \n• \nProvisioning services  \n• \nRegulating services  \n• \nCultural services  \n• \nSupporting services  \n \n50. Provisioning Services \nProvisioning services provide material resources. \nExamples include \n• \nFood  \n• \nFreshwater  \n• \nTimber  \n• \nMedicinal resources  \n• \nFisheries  \n \n51. Regulating Services \nRegulating services control natural environmental processes. \nExamples include \n• \nClimate regulation  \n• \nFlood regulation\n\n• \nWater purification  \n• \nPollination  \n• \nCoastal protection  \n• \nCarbon storage  \n \n52. Cultural Services \nCultural services provide non-material benefits. \nExamples include \n• \nRecreation  \n• \nTourism  \n• \nEducation  \n• \nSpiritual values  \n• \nAesthetic appreciation  \n \n53. Supporting Services \nSupporting services maintain ecosystem functioning. \nExamples include \n• \nNutrient cycling  \n• \nSoil formation  \n• \nPrimary production  \n• \nHabitat formation  \n \n54. Ecological Connectivity \nEcological connectivity refers to the degree to which landscapes allow organisms and ecological \nprocesses to move between habitats. \nConnectivity is important for \n• \nMigration\n\n• \nGene flow  \n• \nDispersal  \n• \nRecolonisation  \n• \nMaintenance of viable populations  \n \n55. Habitat Fragmentation \nHabitat fragmentation occurs when a large continuous habitat is divided into smaller isolated \npatches. \nIt can reduce habitat connectivity and increase the vulnerability of populations. \n \n56. Sri Lankan Wildlife \nSri Lanka supports diverse wildlife including \n• \nAsian elephants  \n• \nLeopards  \n• \nSloth bears  \n• \nPrimates  \n• \nDeer  \n• \nWild boar  \n• \nNumerous bird species  \n• \nReptiles  \n• \nAmphibians  \nMany species are closely associated with particular habitats. \n \n57. Adaptations to Sri Lankan Ecosystems \nOrganisms living in Sri Lankan ecosystems show adaptations to \n• \nSeasonal rainfall  \n• \nHigh temperatures\n\n• \nWater availability  \n• \nSalinity  \n• \nElevation  \n• \nFlooding  \n• \nDrought  \nThese adaptations allow species to occupy specific ecological niches. \n \n58. Ecological Niche \nAn ecological niche describes the role of a species within an ecosystem and the conditions under \nwhich it survives and reproduces. \nIt includes relationships with \n• \nResources  \n• \nOther organisms  \n• \nEnvironmental factors  \n \n59. Biodiversity Across Environmental Gradients \nBiodiversity can change along gradients such as \n• \nElevation  \n• \nRainfall  \n• \nTemperature  \n• \nSoil conditions  \n• \nSalinity  \nSri Lanka's diverse environmental gradients contribute to its high biological diversity. \n \n60. Importance of Sri Lankan Natural Ecosystems \nSri Lankan ecosystems provide \n• \nBiodiversity conservation\n\n• \nFood resources  \n• \nFreshwater  \n• \nClimate regulation  \n• \nSoil protection  \n• \nFlood control  \n• \nCoastal protection  \n• \nTourism opportunities  \n• \nCultural benefits  \n• \nGenetic resources  \n \n61. Major Global Biomes Compared \nBiome \nClimate \nDominant vegetation \nMajor characteristics \nTropical rainforest Hot and wet \nTall evergreen trees \nExtremely high \nbiodiversity \nTropical seasonal \nforest \nWarm with seasonal \nrainfall \nTrees and shrubs \nSeasonal water \nlimitation \nSavanna \nWarm with seasonal \nrainfall \nGrasses and scattered \ntrees \nFire and grazing \nimportant \nDesert \nVery dry \nSparse drought-adapted \nplants \nSevere water limitation \nTemperate forest \nModerate with seasons Deciduous and evergreen \ntrees \nStrong seasonal changes \nTemperate \ngrassland \nModerate, often \nseasonal \nGrasses \nFertile soils and grazing \nBoreal forest \nCold \nConifers \nLong winters \nTundra \nVery cold \nMosses, lichens and small \nplants \nShort growing season\n\n62. Major Sri Lankan Ecosystems \nEcosystem \nMain characteristics \nLowland rainforest \nHigh rainfall, evergreen vegetation and high endemism \nDry mixed evergreen forest Seasonal drought and dry-zone vegetation \nMontane forest \nHigh elevation, cool and humid conditions \nHighland grassland \nCool highland environment dominated by grasses \nRivers and streams \nFlowing freshwater ecosystems \nWetlands \nWater-saturated habitats with high ecological importance \nMangroves \nSalt-tolerant coastal vegetation \nCoral reefs \nHighly diverse tropical marine ecosystems \nSeagrass beds \nMarine flowering-plant ecosystems \nLagoons and estuaries \nTransitional freshwater and marine environments \n \n63. Key Terms \nBiome \nLarge ecological region characterised by climate and dominant vegetation. \nEcosystem \nCommunity of organisms interacting with the abiotic environment. \nWetland \nEcosystem where water saturation strongly influences soil and biological conditions. \nEstuary \nCoastal ecosystem where freshwater and seawater mix. \nMangrove \nCoastal ecosystem dominated by salt-tolerant woody plants. \nCoral reef\n\nMarine ecosystem formed largely through calcium carbonate structures produced by reef-\nbuilding organisms. \nEndemism \nRestriction of a species to a particular geographical region. \nEcosystem services \nBenefits that humans obtain from ecosystems. \nEcological niche \nThe ecological role and environmental requirements of a species. \nHabitat fragmentation \nDivision of continuous habitat into smaller and often more isolated patches. \nCoral bleaching \nLoss of symbiotic photosynthetic organisms from corals, causing them to become pale or white. \n \n64. Final Summary \nBiomes are large ecological regions shaped primarily by climate and characterised by distinctive \nvegetation and associated organisms. Major global biomes include tropical rainforests, seasonal \nforests, savannas, deserts, temperate forests, grasslands, boreal forests and tundra. \nAquatic ecosystems include freshwater and marine environments. Rivers, lakes, wetlands, \nestuaries, mangroves, seagrass beds and coral reefs support diverse communities and provide \nimportant ecological services. \nSri Lanka contains exceptional ecological diversity because of its tropical climate, monsoonal \nrainfall, elevation, topography, geology and variation in environmental conditions. Its major \nnatural ecosystems include lowland rainforests, dry forests, montane forests, highland grasslands, \nfreshwater ecosystems, wetlands, mangroves, lagoons, seagrass beds and coral reefs. \nSri Lankan ecosystems are particularly important because the country has high species richness \nand significant endemism. Forests, wetlands and coastal ecosystems also provide essential \necosystem services such as water regulation, carbon storage, soil protection, fisheries and coastal \nprotection. \nUnderstanding global biomes and Sri Lankan ecosystems provides the ecological foundation \nneeded to understand biodiversity distribution, environmental change and conservation.	COMPLETED	116	2026-08-19 18:12:06.544769	2026-08-19 18:12:51.883018	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
997	Lesson 3: Human impacts, pollution, and environmental sustainability - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 3: Human impacts, pollution, and environmental sustainability	PDF	uploads/pdf/f01689cbd4904a6ea5302b3e9f110180.pdf	\N	Lesson 3 — Human Impacts, Pollution, and Environmental Sustainability \nIntroduction \nHuman societies depend on natural ecosystems for food, water, energy, raw materials and other \nessential resources. However, population growth, urbanisation, industrialisation, agriculture and \nincreased consumption have significantly altered natural ecosystems. \nHuman activities can change atmospheric composition, degrade habitats, pollute air and water, \nalter nutrient cycles and reduce biodiversity. Environmental sustainability aims to meet present \nneeds while maintaining the capacity of natural systems to support future generations. \n \n1. Human Impact on the Environment \nHuman activities influence ecosystems at local, regional and global scales. \nMajor environmental impacts include \n• \nHabitat destruction  \n• \nDeforestation  \n• \nPollution  \n• \nClimate change  \n• \nOverexploitation of resources  \n• \nIntroduction of invasive species  \n• \nSoil degradation  \n• \nEutrophication  \n• \nLoss of biodiversity  \n \n2. Population Growth \nIncreasing human population can increase demand for \n• \nFood  \n• \nWater  \n• \nEnergy  \n• \nHousing\n\n• \nTransportation  \n• \nLand  \n• \nNatural resources  \nThis can place increasing pressure on ecosystems. \n \n3. Urbanisation \nUrbanisation is the expansion of human settlements and the increasing proportion of people \nliving in urban areas. \nUrbanisation can result in \n• \nHabitat loss  \n• \nSoil sealing  \n• \nIncreased waste production  \n• \nIncreased energy consumption  \n• \nAir pollution  \n• \nWater pollution  \n• \nChanges in local climate  \n \n4. Industrialisation \nIndustrial activities provide materials, energy and employment but can also generate \n• \nAir pollutants  \n• \nWastewater  \n• \nSolid waste  \n• \nGreenhouse gases  \n• \nToxic substances  \nPoorly managed industrial activities can have long-term environmental effects. \n \n5. Deforestation\n\nDeforestation is the permanent or long-term removal of forest vegetation and conversion of \nforest land to other uses. \nMajor causes include \n• \nAgriculture  \n• \nUrban expansion  \n• \nInfrastructure development  \n• \nLogging  \n• \nMining  \n• \nFuelwood collection  \n \n6. Effects of Deforestation \nDeforestation can cause \n• \nHabitat loss  \n• \nBiodiversity reduction  \n• \nSoil erosion  \n• \nReduced carbon storage  \n• \nChanges in water cycles  \n• \nIncreased greenhouse gas emissions  \n• \nHabitat fragmentation  \n \n7. Habitat Destruction \nHabitat destruction occurs when the natural environment required by organisms is altered or \nremoved. \nIt is one of the major causes of biodiversity loss. \nSpecies may decline when suitable habitat becomes insufficient for survival and reproduction. \n \n8. Overexploitation\n\nOverexploitation occurs when biological resources are harvested faster than populations can \nnaturally recover. \nExamples include \n• \nOverfishing  \n• \nExcessive hunting  \n• \nIllegal wildlife collection  \n• \nUnsustainable logging  \nOverexploitation can cause population declines and even extinction. \n \n9. Pollution \nPollution is the introduction of substances or forms of energy into the environment at levels that \ncause harmful effects. \nPollution can affect \n• \nAir  \n• \nWater  \n• \nSoil  \n• \nLiving organisms  \n• \nEcosystem processes  \n \n10. Pollutants \nA pollutant is a substance or form of energy that causes adverse environmental effects when \npresent at harmful levels. \nPollutants may be \n• \nChemical  \n• \nBiological  \n• \nPhysical  \n \n11. Primary Pollutants\n\nPrimary pollutants are released directly into the environment from a source. \nExamples include \n• \nCarbon monoxide  \n• \nSulfur dioxide  \n• \nNitrogen oxides  \n• \nParticulate matter  \n \n12. Secondary Pollutants \nSecondary pollutants form through chemical reactions involving substances already present in \nthe environment. \nSome atmospheric pollutants react in sunlight to form new compounds. \n \n13. Air Pollution \nAir pollution occurs when harmful substances accumulate in the atmosphere at concentrations \ncapable of affecting organisms or environmental processes. \nImportant air pollutants include \n• \nParticulate matter  \n• \nCarbon monoxide  \n• \nSulfur dioxide  \n• \nNitrogen oxides  \n• \nOzone  \n• \nVolatile organic compounds  \n \n14. Particulate Matter \nParticulate matter consists of tiny solid particles or liquid droplets suspended in the \natmosphere. \nFine particles can penetrate deeply into the respiratory system. \nMajor sources include\n\n• \nVehicle emissions  \n• \nIndustrial combustion  \n• \nBiomass burning  \n• \nConstruction activities  \n• \nDust  \n \n15. Carbon Monoxide \nCarbon monoxide is a colourless and odourless gas produced mainly through incomplete \ncombustion. \nIt can interfere with oxygen transport in the blood by binding strongly to haemoglobin. \n \n16. Sulfur Dioxide \nSulfur dioxide is released mainly through combustion of sulfur-containing fuels and certain \nindustrial processes. \nIt can contribute to \n• \nRespiratory irritation  \n• \nAcid deposition  \n• \nDamage to vegetation  \n \n17. Nitrogen Oxides \nNitrogen oxides are produced largely through high-temperature combustion processes. \nThey contribute to \n• \nPhotochemical smog  \n• \nAcid deposition  \n• \nFormation of secondary particulate matter  \n \n18. Photochemical Smog\n\nPhotochemical smog forms when pollutants such as nitrogen oxides and volatile organic \ncompounds react in the presence of sunlight. \nA major secondary pollutant produced during these reactions is ground-level ozone. \n \n19. Ground-Level Ozone \nGround-level ozone is a secondary pollutant. \nUnlike the protective ozone layer in the stratosphere, ozone near the Earth's surface can damage \n• \nRespiratory tissues  \n• \nPlant tissues  \n• \nMaterials  \n \n20. Acid Deposition \nAcid deposition occurs when acidic substances formed in the atmosphere reach Earth's surface \nthrough rain, snow, fog or dry particles. \nSulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides are important contributors. \nEffects can include \n• \nSoil acidification  \n• \nFreshwater acidification  \n• \nDamage to vegetation  \n• \nChanges in nutrient availability  \n \n21. Water Pollution \nWater pollution occurs when harmful substances enter aquatic environments and adversely \naffect water quality or aquatic organisms. \nSources include \n• \nDomestic sewage  \n• \nIndustrial wastewater  \n• \nAgricultural runoff\n\n• \nOil pollution  \n• \nMining  \n• \nPlastics  \n• \nHeavy metals  \n \n22. Point Sources \nA point source is a pollution source that can be identified as a specific discharge location. \nExamples include \n• \nIndustrial discharge pipes  \n• \nSewage outlets  \n• \nWastewater treatment plant outlets  \n \n23. Non-Point Sources \nA non-point source comes from widespread or diffuse areas rather than one identifiable \ndischarge point. \nExamples include \n• \nAgricultural runoff  \n• \nUrban stormwater  \n• \nSoil erosion  \n \n24. Sewage Pollution \nUntreated sewage can introduce \n• \nPathogenic microorganisms  \n• \nOrganic matter  \n• \nNitrogen  \n• \nPhosphorus  \n• \nSuspended solids\n\ninto water bodies. \nThis can affect water quality and human health. \n \n25. Organic Pollution \nLarge quantities of biodegradable organic matter can stimulate microbial activity in water. \nMicroorganisms consume oxygen while decomposing this material. \nThis can reduce dissolved oxygen concentrations. \n \n26. Biological Oxygen Demand \nBiological oxygen demand, or BOD, represents the amount of dissolved oxygen required by \nmicroorganisms to decompose biodegradable organic matter. \nHigh BOD generally indicates a high concentration of biodegradable organic material. \n \n27. Chemical Oxygen Demand \nChemical oxygen demand, or COD, measures the oxygen equivalent required to chemically \noxidise substances in a water sample. \nCOD is generally higher than BOD because it includes substances that may not be readily \nbiodegradable. \n \n28. Eutrophication \nEutrophication is the enrichment of an aquatic ecosystem with nutrients, particularly nitrogen \nand phosphorus. \nExcessive nutrient input can stimulate rapid growth of algae and aquatic plants. \n \n29. Cultural Eutrophication \nWhen human activities accelerate nutrient enrichment beyond natural rates, the process is called \ncultural eutrophication. \nMajor sources include \n• \nAgricultural fertilisers\n\n• \nSewage  \n• \nIndustrial wastewater  \n• \nAnimal waste  \n \n30. Process of Eutrophication \nA simplified sequence is \nNutrient enrichment \n↓ \nExcessive algal growth \n↓ \nIncreased organic matter \n↓ \nMicrobial decomposition \n↓ \nOxygen depletion \n↓ \nStress or death of aquatic organisms \n \n31. Algal Blooms \nAn algal bloom is rapid and excessive growth of algae or cyanobacteria. \nSome blooms can produce toxins that harm \n• \nFish  \n• \nBirds  \n• \nAquatic animals  \n• \nHumans  \n \n32. Hypoxia\n\nHypoxia refers to abnormally low dissolved oxygen concentrations in water. \nSevere hypoxia can make aquatic environments unsuitable for many organisms. \n \n33. Biomagnification \nBiomagnification is the increase in concentration of certain persistent pollutants at progressively \nhigher trophic levels of a food chain. \nPersistent substances are especially likely to biomagnify. \n \n34. Bioaccumulation \nBioaccumulation occurs when an organism absorbs a substance faster than it can eliminate it. \nThe concentration of the substance therefore increases within the organism over time. \n \n35. Biomagnification Compared with Bioaccumulation \nBioaccumulation \nBiomagnification \nOccurs within an individual organism Occurs across trophic levels \nConcentration increases over time \nConcentration increases toward higher trophic levels \nDepends on uptake and elimination \nDepends strongly on feeding relationships \n \n36. Persistent Organic Pollutants \nSome organic pollutants resist degradation and remain in ecosystems for long periods. \nCharacteristics can include \n• \nPersistence  \n• \nFat solubility  \n• \nLong environmental residence times  \n• \nPotential for bioaccumulation  \n• \nPotential for biomagnification\n\n37. Heavy Metal Pollution \nHeavy metals can enter ecosystems through \n• \nMining  \n• \nIndustrial activities  \n• \nWaste disposal  \n• \nAgricultural chemicals  \n• \nFossil-fuel combustion  \nExamples include \n• \nMercury  \n• \nLead  \n• \nCadmium  \nSome heavy metals can accumulate in organisms and cause toxic effects. \n \n38. Mercury Biomagnification \nMercury can enter aquatic food webs and become concentrated in organisms at higher trophic \nlevels. \nPredatory fish may therefore contain higher mercury concentrations than organisms lower in the \nfood web. \n \n39. Soil Pollution \nSoil can become contaminated by \n• \nPesticides  \n• \nFertilisers  \n• \nIndustrial chemicals  \n• \nHeavy metals  \n• \nPetroleum products  \n• \nImproperly disposed waste  \nSoil pollution can affect microorganisms, plants, animals and humans.\n\n40. Pesticides \nPesticides are substances used to control organisms considered harmful to agriculture, public \nhealth or other activities. \nExamples include \n• \nInsecticides  \n• \nHerbicides  \n• \nFungicides  \n• \nRodenticides  \nExcessive or inappropriate use can harm non-target organisms. \n \n41. Fertiliser Pollution \nFertilisers provide nutrients required for plant growth. \nExcessive application can cause nutrient runoff into water bodies and contribute to \neutrophication. \n \n42. Plastic Pollution \nPlastic waste is persistent and can accumulate in terrestrial and aquatic environments. \nPlastic pollution can cause \n• \nPhysical entanglement  \n• \nIngestion by animals  \n• \nHabitat degradation  \n• \nMicroplastic contamination  \n \n43. Microplastics \nMicroplastics are very small plastic particles, generally less than 5 mm in size. \nThey can originate from \n• \nBreakdown of larger plastics\n\n• \nIndustrial materials  \n• \nSynthetic fibres  \n• \nCertain consumer products  \nMicroplastics can enter food webs and aquatic ecosystems. \n \n44. Thermal Pollution \nThermal pollution occurs when human activities cause undesirable changes in the temperature \nof natural water bodies. \nA common source is discharge of heated water from industrial or power-generation facilities. \nIncreased water temperature can \n• \nReduce dissolved oxygen  \n• \nAlter metabolic rates  \n• \nChange species composition  \n• \nStress aquatic organisms  \n \n45. Noise Pollution \nNoise pollution is excessive or harmful unwanted sound. \nSources include \n• \nTraffic  \n• \nConstruction  \n• \nIndustry  \n• \nAircraft  \n• \nUrban activities  \nIt can affect humans and wildlife by interfering with communication, behaviour and \nreproduction. \n \n46. Light Pollution \nArtificial light at night can alter natural biological rhythms and behaviour.\n\nIt can affect \n• \nNocturnal animals  \n• \nMigratory birds  \n• \nInsects  \n• \nMarine organisms  \n \n47. Climate Change \nClimate change refers to long-term changes in Earth's climate patterns. \nHuman activities have significantly increased atmospheric concentrations of greenhouse gases. \n \n48. Greenhouse Effect \nThe greenhouse effect is the natural process by which certain atmospheric gases absorb and re-\nemit outgoing infrared radiation. \nThis process keeps Earth warm enough to support life. \nThe problem arises when human activities increase greenhouse gas concentrations and \nstrengthen the natural greenhouse effect. \n \n49. Greenhouse Gases \nImportant greenhouse gases include \n• \nCarbon dioxide  \n• \nMethane  \n• \nNitrous oxide  \n• \nWater vapour  \n• \nOzone  \nHuman activities have particularly increased concentrations of carbon dioxide, methane and \nnitrous oxide. \n \n50. Sources of Greenhouse Gas Emissions\n\nMajor sources include \n• \nFossil-fuel combustion  \n• \nDeforestation  \n• \nAgriculture  \n• \nIndustrial processes  \n• \nWaste management  \n• \nLand-use changes  \n \n51. Global Warming \nGlobal warming refers specifically to the long-term increase in Earth's average surface \ntemperature. \nIt is one major component of broader climate change. \n \n52. Effects of Climate Change \nClimate change can contribute to \n• \nRising temperatures  \n• \nChanging rainfall patterns  \n• \nMore frequent or intense heat extremes  \n• \nMelting glaciers and ice sheets  \n• \nSea-level rise  \n• \nOcean warming  \n• \nChanges in species distributions  \n• \nCoral bleaching  \n• \nIncreased ecosystem stress  \n \n53. Ocean Acidification \nThe oceans absorb a substantial proportion of atmospheric carbon dioxide.\n\nWhen carbon dioxide dissolves in seawater, chemical reactions increase hydrogen ion \nconcentration and reduce seawater pH. \nThis process is known as ocean acidification. \n \n54. Effects of Ocean Acidification \nReduced ocean pH can make it more difficult for some marine organisms to build and maintain \ncalcium carbonate structures. \nPotentially affected organisms include \n• \nCorals  \n• \nMolluscs  \n• \nCertain planktonic organisms  \n \n55. Climate Change and Coral Reefs \nIncreasing ocean temperatures can cause coral bleaching. \nRepeated or prolonged bleaching events can reduce coral survival and damage reef ecosystems. \nClimate change therefore represents a major threat to tropical coral reefs. \n \n56. Waste \nWaste is material discarded because it is no longer considered useful by its current owner. \nWaste can include \n• \nOrganic waste  \n• \nPlastics  \n• \nPaper  \n• \nGlass  \n• \nMetals  \n• \nElectronic waste  \n• \nHazardous waste\n\n57. Solid Waste Management \nEffective waste management aims to minimise environmental impacts. \nThe preferred approach is generally represented by the waste hierarchy. \nReduce → Reuse → Recycle → Recover → Dispose \nPrevention and reduction are generally preferable to disposal. \n \n58. Reduce \nReduction means decreasing the amount of waste generated. \nExamples include \n• \nAvoiding unnecessary packaging  \n• \nReducing single-use products  \n• \nUsing resources efficiently  \n \n59. Reuse \nReuse involves using an item again rather than discarding it. \nThis reduces demand for new materials and decreases waste generation. \n \n60. Recycling \nRecycling involves processing waste materials into materials that can be used again. \nCommon recyclable materials include \n• \nPaper  \n• \nGlass  \n• \nMetals  \n• \nCertain plastics  \n \n61. Composting\n\nComposting is the controlled biological decomposition of organic waste under mainly aerobic \nconditions. \nIt converts organic material into a stable, nutrient-rich soil amendment. \nSuitable materials include \n• \nFood scraps  \n• \nLeaves  \n• \nGrass  \n• \nAgricultural residues  \n \n62. Benefits of Composting \nComposting can \n• \nReduce organic waste  \n• \nRecycle nutrients  \n• \nImprove soil structure  \n• \nIncrease soil organic matter  \n• \nReduce landfill waste  \n \n63. Biogas Production \nOrganic waste can be converted into biogas through anaerobic digestion. \nAnaerobic microorganisms break down organic material in the absence of oxygen. \nThe major combustible component of biogas is methane. \n \n64. Stages of Anaerobic Digestion \nAnaerobic digestion involves several microbial stages. \nThese include \n1. Hydrolysis  \n2. Acidogenesis\n\n3. Acetogenesis  \n4. Methanogenesis  \nDifferent microbial groups participate in different stages. \n \n65. Hydrolysis \nDuring hydrolysis, complex organic polymers are broken down into smaller molecules. \nExamples include \n• \nCarbohydrates → Simple sugars  \n• \nProteins → Amino acids  \n• \nLipids → Fatty acids and other components  \n \n66. Acidogenesis \nDuring acidogenesis, microorganisms convert soluble products of hydrolysis into compounds \nsuch as \n• \nOrganic acids  \n• \nAlcohols  \n• \nHydrogen  \n• \nCarbon dioxide  \n \n67. Acetogenesis \nDuring acetogenesis, microorganisms convert products from acidogenesis into compounds such \nas \n• \nAcetate  \n• \nHydrogen  \n• \nCarbon dioxide  \nThese products can be used by methanogens. \n \n68. Methanogenesis\n\nMethanogenesis is the final major stage of anaerobic digestion. \nMethanogenic archaea produce methane from substrates such as acetate, hydrogen and carbon \ndioxide. \n \n69. Biogas Composition \nBiogas consists primarily of \n• \nMethane  \n• \nCarbon dioxide  \nIt can also contain smaller amounts of \n• \nHydrogen sulfide  \n• \nWater vapour  \n• \nOther gases  \nThe methane content makes biogas useful as an energy source. \n \n70. Applications of Biogas \nBiogas can be used for \n• \nCooking  \n• \nHeating  \n• \nElectricity generation  \n• \nCombined heat and power production  \nThe remaining digestate can potentially be used as a nutrient-rich soil amendment after \nappropriate management. \n \n71. Sustainable Development \nSustainable development involves meeting present human needs while maintaining the \nenvironmental and resource conditions required by future generations. \nIt requires consideration of \n• \nEnvironmental protection\n\n• \nEconomic development  \n• \nSocial well-being  \n \n72. Sustainability \nEnvironmental sustainability means maintaining ecological processes, biodiversity and natural \nresources over the long term. \nSustainable practices aim to keep resource use within ecological limits. \n \n73. Renewable Resources \nRenewable resources can naturally regenerate over relatively short timescales when properly \nmanaged. \nExamples include \n• \nSolar energy  \n• \nWind energy  \n• \nHydropower  \n• \nBiomass  \n \n74. Non-Renewable Resources \nNon-renewable resources exist in finite quantities or regenerate extremely slowly on human \ntimescales. \nExamples include \n• \nCoal  \n• \nPetroleum  \n• \nNatural gas  \n• \nMany mineral ores  \n \n75. Sustainable Resource Use \nSustainable resource management aims to\n\n• \nReduce waste  \n• \nImprove efficiency  \n• \nProtect ecosystems  \n• \nMaintain biodiversity  \n• \nPrevent overexploitation  \n• \nSupport long-term resource availability  \n \n76. Renewable Energy \nRenewable energy sources can reduce dependence on fossil fuels. \nImportant renewable energy sources include \n• \nSolar  \n• \nWind  \n• \nHydropower  \n• \nGeothermal  \n• \nSustainable biomass  \n \n77. Conservation of Energy \nEnergy conservation means reducing unnecessary energy use and improving energy efficiency. \nExamples include \n• \nEfficient appliances  \n• \nPublic transportation  \n• \nEnergy-efficient buildings  \n• \nReduced industrial energy waste  \n \n78. Carbon Sequestration \nCarbon sequestration is the capture and long-term storage of carbon. \nNatural carbon sinks include\n\n• \nForests  \n• \nSoils  \n• \nWetlands  \n• \nOceans  \nProtecting and restoring ecosystems can increase carbon storage. \n \n79. Reforestation \nReforestation is the re-establishment of forest vegetation in areas where forests have previously \nbeen removed. \nIt can \n• \nStore carbon  \n• \nReduce soil erosion  \n• \nRestore habitat  \n• \nImprove water regulation  \n• \nSupport biodiversity  \n \n80. Afforestation \nAfforestation is the establishment of forests in areas that have not recently been forested. \nIts ecological effects depend strongly on the original ecosystem and the species planted. \n \n81. Environmental Impact Assessment \nAn Environmental Impact Assessment, or EIA, is a systematic process used to evaluate the \npotential environmental consequences of a proposed project before major decisions are made. \nIt can consider impacts on \n• \nBiodiversity  \n• \nWater  \n• \nSoil  \n• \nAir\n\n• \nCommunities  \n• \nNatural resources  \n \n82. Sustainable Agriculture \nSustainable agriculture aims to maintain agricultural productivity while reducing environmental \ndamage. \nApproaches include \n• \nCrop rotation  \n• \nIntegrated pest management  \n• \nEfficient irrigation  \n• \nOrganic matter management  \n• \nReduced soil erosion  \n• \nResponsible fertiliser use  \n \n83. Integrated Pest Management \nIntegrated pest management, or IPM, combines multiple methods to control agricultural pests \nwhile minimising environmental harm. \nMethods can include \n• \nBiological control  \n• \nCultural practices  \n• \nMechanical control  \n• \nMonitoring  \n• \nSelective pesticide use  \n \n84. Environmental Restoration \nEcological restoration involves assisting the recovery of degraded, damaged or destroyed \necosystems. \nRestoration can involve\n\n• \nReplanting native vegetation  \n• \nRemoving invasive species  \n• \nRestoring wetlands  \n• \nReconnecting habitats  \n• \nImproving water quality  \n \n85. Sustainable Waste Management \nSustainable waste management prioritises \n• \nWaste prevention  \n• \nReduction  \n• \nReuse  \n• \nRecycling  \n• \nBiological treatment  \n• \nEnergy recovery  \n• \nSafe disposal of unavoidable waste  \nThe objective is to minimise environmental and health impacts throughout the waste lifecycle. \n \n86. Sri Lankan Environmental Concerns \nSri Lanka faces environmental challenges including \n• \nDeforestation  \n• \nHabitat fragmentation  \n• \nHuman-wildlife conflict  \n• \nWater pollution  \n• \nPlastic pollution  \n• \nCoastal degradation  \n• \nSoil erosion  \n• \nBiodiversity loss\n\n• \nWaste-management problems  \n• \nClimate-related impacts  \nAddressing these problems requires scientific management, effective policies and public \nparticipation. \n \n87. Importance of Environmental Education \nEnvironmental education helps individuals understand \n• \nEcosystem processes  \n• \nResource limitations  \n• \nPollution  \n• \nBiodiversity  \n• \nClimate change  \n• \nSustainable practices  \nKnowledge can support responsible environmental decision-making. \n \n88. Key Terms \nPollution \nIntroduction of harmful substances or energy into the environment. \nPollutant \nSubstance or form of energy capable of causing environmental harm. \nEutrophication \nNutrient enrichment of aquatic ecosystems that can lead to excessive biological growth and \noxygen depletion. \nBiomagnification \nIncrease in concentration of certain persistent substances at higher trophic levels. \nBioaccumulation \nAccumulation of a substance within an individual organism. \nClimate change\n\nLong-term alteration of Earth's climate patterns. \nGlobal warming \nLong-term increase in Earth's average surface temperature. \nGreenhouse effect \nWarming caused by atmospheric gases absorbing and re-emitting infrared radiation. \nOcean acidification \nReduction in ocean pH associated primarily with increased absorption of atmospheric carbon \ndioxide. \nComposting \nControlled aerobic decomposition of organic waste. \nAnaerobic digestion \nMicrobial decomposition of organic matter in the absence of oxygen. \nBiogas \nGas produced through anaerobic digestion, consisting mainly of methane and carbon dioxide. \nSustainability \nLong-term management of resources and ecosystems without compromising future needs. \nEIA \nEnvironmental Impact Assessment used to evaluate potential environmental effects of proposed \nprojects. \n \n89. Final Summary \nHuman activities have transformed ecosystems through population growth, urbanisation, \nindustrialisation, agriculture, resource extraction and land-use change. These activities can result \nin habitat destruction, pollution, overexploitation and biodiversity loss. \nPollution can affect air, water and soil. Major forms include air pollution, water pollution, soil \ncontamination, plastic pollution, thermal pollution and noise pollution. Nutrient pollution can \ncause eutrophication, while persistent pollutants can accumulate in organisms and biomagnify \nthrough food webs. \nHuman activities have also increased atmospheric concentrations of greenhouse gases, \ncontributing to global warming and broader climate change. Climate change affects terrestrial,\n\nfreshwater and marine ecosystems and contributes to problems such as coral bleaching and sea-\nlevel rise. \nWaste management is an important component of environmental protection. Reduction, reuse, \nrecycling, composting and anaerobic digestion can reduce the amount of waste entering the \nenvironment. Anaerobic digestion also produces biogas, which can be used as a renewable \nenergy source. \nEnvironmental sustainability requires responsible resource use, conservation of biodiversity, \npollution prevention, renewable energy development, sustainable agriculture and ecosystem \nrestoration. Long-term environmental protection depends on integrating scientific knowledge \nwith effective management and responsible human behaviour.	COMPLETED	117	2026-08-19 18:12:09.247251	2026-08-19 18:12:51.88302	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
998	Lesson 1: Biodiversity levels, values, and threats - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 1: Biodiversity levels, values, and threats	PDF	uploads/pdf/02dade1237454ed6ad71172aeba6e68c.pdf	\N	Lesson 1 — Biodiversity Levels, Values, and Threats \nIntroduction \nBiodiversity refers to the variety and variability of living organisms and the ecological systems \nin which they occur. It includes variation within species, diversity among species and diversity \namong ecosystems. \nBiodiversity is essential for ecosystem functioning, human well-being, economic development \nand the stability of natural systems. However, human activities are causing biodiversity to \ndecline at an unprecedented rate in many parts of the world. \n \n1. Meaning of Biodiversity \nBiodiversity is the variety of life at different levels of biological organisation. \nIt can be considered at three major levels \n• \nGenetic diversity  \n• \nSpecies diversity  \n• \nEcosystem diversity  \nThese levels are interconnected. \n \n2. Genetic Diversity \nGenetic diversity refers to variation in genetic characteristics among individuals and \npopulations of the same species. \nExamples include variation in \n• \nBody size  \n• \nColour  \n• \nDisease resistance  \n• \nGrowth rate  \n• \nTolerance to environmental conditions  \nGenetic diversity provides the raw material for evolutionary adaptation. \n \n3. Importance of Genetic Diversity\n\nGenetic diversity helps populations \n• \nAdapt to environmental changes  \n• \nResist diseases  \n• \nSurvive changing climatic conditions  \n• \nMaintain reproductive fitness  \n• \nRespond to new ecological pressures  \nPopulations with low genetic diversity may be more vulnerable to environmental change and \ndisease. \n \n4. Species Diversity \nSpecies diversity refers to the variety of species within a particular region or ecosystem. \nIt includes two major components \n• \nSpecies richness  \n• \nSpecies evenness  \n \n5. Species Richness \nSpecies richness is the number of different species present in a particular area. \nFor example, an ecosystem containing 50 species has greater species richness than an ecosystem \ncontaining 20 species. \nSpecies richness does not consider how individuals are distributed among the species. \n \n6. Species Evenness \nSpecies evenness describes how equally individuals are distributed among the different species \nin a community. \nA community in which most species have similar population sizes has high evenness. \nA community dominated by one species has lower evenness. \n \n7. Ecosystem Diversity\n\nEcosystem diversity refers to the variety of ecosystems, habitats and ecological communities \nwithin a geographical area. \nExamples include \n• \nForests  \n• \nGrasslands  \n• \nWetlands  \n• \nRivers  \n• \nCoral reefs  \n• \nMangroves  \n• \nEstuaries  \n \n8. Biodiversity at Different Scales \nBiodiversity can be studied at different spatial scales. \nAlpha diversity refers to diversity within a particular habitat or ecosystem. \nBeta diversity refers to differences in species composition between habitats. \nGamma diversity refers to overall diversity across a larger geographical region. \n \n9. Species Richness and Ecosystem Stability \nGreater biodiversity can contribute to ecosystem resilience because different species may \nperform different ecological roles. \nIf one species declines, another species may sometimes partially compensate for its ecological \nfunction. \n \n10. Biodiversity and Ecosystem Function \nBiodiversity contributes to processes such as \n• \nPrimary production  \n• \nNutrient cycling  \n• \nDecomposition\n\n• \nPollination  \n• \nSeed dispersal  \n• \nSoil formation  \n• \nBiological control  \nLoss of species can therefore alter ecosystem processes. \n \n11. Biodiversity Hotspots \nA biodiversity hotspot is a region recognised for exceptionally high levels of endemic species \nand significant habitat loss. \nTo qualify as a global biodiversity hotspot, a region must meet established criteria relating to \nendemic vascular plants and loss of its original vegetation. \n \n12. Sri Lanka as a Biodiversity-Rich Region \nSri Lanka has exceptionally high biodiversity relative to its geographical area. \nImportant characteristics include \n• \nHigh species richness  \n• \nHigh endemism  \n• \nDiverse ecosystems  \n• \nSignificant genetic resources  \n• \nStrong variation across elevation and rainfall gradients  \n \n13. Endemic Species \nAn endemic species is a species naturally restricted to a particular geographical area. \nSri Lanka has many endemic species, particularly among \n• \nAmphibians  \n• \nReptiles  \n• \nFreshwater fish  \n• \nPlants\n\n• \nInvertebrates  \n \n14. Why Endemic Species Are Vulnerable \nSpecies with restricted geographical distributions are particularly vulnerable to habitat \ndestruction. \nIf a species occurs only within a small area, destruction or degradation of that area can affect a \nlarge proportion of its entire population. \n \n15. Biodiversity Values \nBiodiversity has many forms of value. \nMajor categories include \n• \nDirect use value  \n• \nIndirect use value  \n• \nOption value  \n• \nExistence value  \n• \nBequest value  \n \n16. Direct Use Value \nDirect use value refers to benefits obtained from directly using biological resources. \nExamples include \n• \nFood  \n• \nTimber  \n• \nMedicinal plants  \n• \nFibres  \n• \nFish  \n• \nOrnamental organisms  \n \n17. Indirect Use Value\n\nIndirect use value comes from ecosystem processes that benefit humans without requiring direct \nharvesting. \nExamples include \n• \nClimate regulation  \n• \nPollination  \n• \nFlood regulation  \n• \nSoil formation  \n• \nWater purification  \n• \nCarbon storage  \n \n18. Option Value \nOption value refers to the potential future benefits of conserving biodiversity. \nA species that has no known current economic use may contain genes or biochemical compounds \nthat become valuable in the future. \n \n19. Existence Value \nExistence value is the value people place on knowing that a species or ecosystem exists, even if \nthey do not directly use it. \nConservation can therefore be supported because organisms have value simply through their \ncontinued existence. \n \n20. Bequest Value \nBequest value refers to the value of preserving biodiversity so that future generations can benefit \nfrom it. \nIt reflects the responsibility to maintain natural resources for future generations. \n \n21. Ecological Value of Biodiversity \nBiodiversity supports ecosystem functioning. \nDifferent organisms perform different ecological roles as\n\n• \nProducers  \n• \nConsumers  \n• \nDecomposers  \n• \nPollinators  \n• \nPredators  \n• \nParasites  \n• \nNutrient recyclers  \n \n22. Economic Value \nBiodiversity contributes to economies through \n• \nAgriculture  \n• \nFisheries  \n• \nForestry  \n• \nPharmaceuticals  \n• \nBiotechnology  \n• \nTourism  \n• \nEcotourism  \nLoss of biodiversity can therefore produce significant economic consequences. \n \n23. Agricultural Importance \nAgriculture depends on biodiversity for \n• \nCrop genetic resources  \n• \nPollination  \n• \nSoil organisms  \n• \nNatural pest control  \n• \nNutrient cycling  \nGenetic diversity within crops and livestock can improve resilience to environmental stresses.\n\n24. Medicinal Importance \nMany medicinal compounds originate from organisms. \nPlants, microorganisms and animals can produce biologically active compounds useful in \nmedicine. \nMaintaining biodiversity preserves potential sources of future medicines. \n \n25. Cultural Value \nBiodiversity can have important cultural and educational significance. \nNatural ecosystems can contribute to \n• \nRecreation  \n• \nTourism  \n• \nEducation  \n• \nArt  \n• \nLiterature  \n• \nCultural traditions  \n \n26. Major Threats to Biodiversity \nThe major threats to biodiversity include \n• \nHabitat loss  \n• \nHabitat fragmentation  \n• \nOverexploitation  \n• \nInvasive alien species  \n• \nPollution  \n• \nClimate change  \n• \nDisease  \n• \nSmall population effects\n\nThese threats often interact with one another. \n \n27. Habitat Loss \nHabitat loss occurs when natural habitat is destroyed or converted to another land use. \nMajor causes include \n• \nAgriculture  \n• \nUrban development  \n• \nRoads  \n• \nMining  \n• \nIndustrial development  \n• \nDeforestation  \nHabitat loss is one of the most significant threats to biodiversity. \n \n28. Habitat Degradation \nHabitat degradation occurs when habitat remains present but its quality is reduced. \nCauses can include \n• \nPollution  \n• \nInvasive species  \n• \nOvergrazing  \n• \nAltered fire regimes  \n• \nExcessive resource extraction  \n• \nHuman disturbance  \nA degraded habitat may no longer provide suitable conditions for sensitive species. \n \n29. Habitat Fragmentation \nHabitat fragmentation occurs when continuous habitat is divided into smaller isolated patches. \nFragmentation can\n\n• \nReduce habitat area  \n• \nIsolate populations  \n• \nReduce gene flow  \n• \nIncrease edge effects  \n• \nRestrict movement  \n• \nIncrease extinction risk  \n \n30. Edge Effects \nAn edge effect occurs when environmental conditions at the boundary of a habitat differ from \nthose in its interior. \nEdges may experience \n• \nIncreased temperature  \n• \nIncreased wind  \n• \nGreater light availability  \n• \nLower humidity  \n• \nGreater human disturbance  \nSpecies requiring interior habitat can therefore be negatively affected. \n \n31. Habitat Corridors \nA habitat corridor is a strip or connection of suitable habitat linking otherwise separated habitat \npatches. \nCorridors can facilitate \n• \nAnimal movement  \n• \nGene flow  \n• \nMigration  \n• \nDispersal  \n• \nRecolonisation\n\n32. Overexploitation \nOverexploitation occurs when organisms or biological resources are harvested at unsustainable \nrates. \nExamples include \n• \nOverfishing  \n• \nExcessive hunting  \n• \nIllegal wildlife trade  \n• \nUnsustainable logging  \n• \nCollection of rare organisms  \n \n33. Wildlife Trade \nIllegal wildlife trade can involve \n• \nLive animals  \n• \nAnimal parts  \n• \nPlants  \n• \nTimber  \n• \nTraditional medicinal materials  \n• \nOrnamental species  \nIt can reduce wild populations and increase extinction risk. \n \n34. Invasive Alien Species \nAn alien species is a species introduced outside its natural geographical range. \nAn invasive alien species is an introduced species that establishes, spreads and causes \necological, economic or social harm. \n \n35. Characteristics of Invasive Species \nSuccessful invasive species often possess characteristics such as \n• \nRapid reproduction\n\n• \nBroad environmental tolerance  \n• \nEfficient dispersal  \n• \nLack of natural enemies  \n• \nAbility to compete effectively for resources  \n \n36. Effects of Invasive Alien Species \nThey can \n• \nCompete with native species  \n• \nPredate on native organisms  \n• \nIntroduce diseases  \n• \nAlter habitats  \n• \nChange nutrient cycles  \n• \nReduce native biodiversity  \n \n37. Invasive Alien Species in Sri Lanka \nSri Lanka faces threats from introduced organisms affecting terrestrial, freshwater and \nagricultural ecosystems. \nExamples of invasive plants include \n• \nLantana  \n• \nWater hyacinth  \n• \nGiant mimosa  \nTheir impacts can include competition with native plants and alteration of ecosystem structure. \n \n38. Pollution as a Biodiversity Threat \nPollution can directly kill organisms or alter environmental conditions. \nExamples include \n• \nPesticide pollution\n\n• \nHeavy metals  \n• \nPlastic pollution  \n• \nOil pollution  \n• \nSewage  \n• \nExcess nutrients  \n• \nAir pollution  \nSensitive species may disappear from polluted habitats. \n \n39. Climate Change as a Biodiversity Threat \nClimate change can alter \n• \nTemperature  \n• \nRainfall  \n• \nSeasonal patterns  \n• \nSea level  \n• \nOcean chemistry  \n• \nFrequency of extreme events  \nSpecies may need to shift their geographical ranges or adapt to changing conditions. \n \n40. Range Shifts \nAs environmental conditions change, species may move toward areas with more suitable \nconditions. \nFor terrestrial organisms, suitable habitats may shift \n• \nTowards higher elevations  \n• \nTowards higher latitudes  \n• \nAlong rainfall gradients  \nSpecies unable to move or adapt may face increased extinction risk.\n\n41. Phenological Changes \nPhenology refers to the timing of biological events. \nClimate change can alter the timing of \n• \nFlowering  \n• \nFruiting  \n• \nMigration  \n• \nBreeding  \n• \nInsect emergence  \nChanges in the timing of interacting species can disrupt ecological relationships. \n \n42. Ocean Warming and Biodiversity \nIncreasing ocean temperatures can affect marine organisms through \n• \nRange shifts  \n• \nCoral bleaching  \n• \nChanges in reproduction  \n• \nChanges in food availability  \n• \nIncreased physiological stress  \n \n43. Small Population Size \nSmall populations are particularly vulnerable to environmental changes and random events. \nThey may experience \n• \nInbreeding  \n• \nLoss of genetic diversity  \n• \nReduced reproductive success  \n• \nDemographic fluctuations  \n \n44. Genetic Bottleneck\n\nA genetic bottleneck occurs when a population undergoes a drastic reduction in size. \nThe surviving population may contain only a small proportion of the original genetic variation. \n \n45. Inbreeding \nInbreeding occurs when closely related individuals reproduce. \nIt can increase the probability that harmful recessive alleles occur in homozygous form. \nThis may reduce population fitness. \n \n46. Genetic Drift \nGenetic drift is the random change in allele frequencies caused by chance. \nIts effects are particularly strong in small populations. \nGenetic drift can reduce genetic diversity and increase genetic differentiation between \npopulations. \n \n47. Extinction \nExtinction occurs when no individuals of a species remain alive anywhere on Earth. \nExtinction is permanent on human timescales. \n \n48. Local Extinction \nLocal extinction, or extirpation, occurs when a species disappears from a particular geographical \narea but survives elsewhere. \nIt is different from global extinction. \n \n49. Background Extinction \nSpecies naturally become extinct over geological time. \nThe normal rate of extinction occurring without major external disturbances is known as the \nbackground extinction rate.\n\n50. Mass Extinction \nA mass extinction is a period during which extinction rates rise dramatically and a large \nproportion of Earth's species disappear over a relatively short geological period. \nEarth's history includes several major mass extinction events. \n \n51. Sixth Mass Extinction \nThe term sixth mass extinction is used by scientists and conservation researchers to describe the \nunusually high contemporary extinction and population-decline rates associated largely with \nhuman activities. \nMajor contributing factors include \n• \nHabitat destruction  \n• \nOverexploitation  \n• \nInvasive species  \n• \nPollution  \n• \nClimate change  \n \n52. Extinction Debt \nExtinction debt refers to future species extinctions that occur after habitat degradation or \nfragmentation has already taken place. \nSome populations may survive temporarily even though their long-term survival is unlikely. \n \n53. Ecological Resilience \nEcological resilience is the capacity of an ecosystem to withstand disturbance and recover while \nmaintaining its essential structure and functions. \nHigher biodiversity can sometimes increase resilience by providing functional redundancy and \nalternative ecological pathways. \n \n54. Functional Diversity\n\nFunctional diversity refers to the variety of biological traits and ecological roles present within \na community. \nExamples include differences in \n• \nFeeding strategies  \n• \nBody size  \n• \nGrowth forms  \n• \nReproductive strategies  \n• \nNutrient-use patterns  \n \n55. Keystone Species \nA keystone species has a disproportionately large effect on ecosystem structure relative to its \nabundance. \nRemoval of a keystone species can produce major changes throughout an ecosystem. \n \n56. Indicator Species \nAn indicator species is a species whose presence, absence or abundance provides information \nabout environmental conditions. \nCertain organisms are particularly useful for detecting \n• \nWater pollution  \n• \nHabitat degradation  \n• \nEnvironmental changes  \n \n57. Flagship Species \nA flagship species is a species selected to represent conservation efforts and help attract public \nsupport. \nFlagship species are often large, charismatic or culturally important organisms. \n \n58. Umbrella Species\n\nAn umbrella species is a species whose conservation requires protection of a large or diverse \nhabitat, thereby indirectly protecting many other species sharing that habitat. \n \n59. Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services \nBiodiversity supports ecosystem services such as \n• \nPollination  \n• \nWater purification  \n• \nSoil formation  \n• \nClimate regulation  \n• \nPest control  \n• \nNutrient cycling  \n• \nCarbon storage  \nTherefore, biodiversity loss can also reduce ecosystem services. \n \n60. Biodiversity Loss and Food Security \nBiodiversity contributes to food security through \n• \nCrop genetic diversity  \n• \nLivestock genetic diversity  \n• \nPollinators  \n• \nSoil organisms  \n• \nNatural pest control  \n• \nWild food resources  \nLoss of biodiversity can increase vulnerability of food systems. \n \n61. Biodiversity and Human Health \nBiodiversity contributes to human health through \n• \nMedicinal resources\n\n• \nNutritious food  \n• \nClean water  \n• \nPollution regulation  \n• \nMental and recreational benefits  \nHealthy ecosystems can also reduce certain environmental health risks. \n \n62. Biodiversity and Climate Regulation \nForests, wetlands, grasslands, mangroves and oceans store carbon and influence climate \nprocesses. \nProtecting these ecosystems can therefore contribute to climate regulation. \n \n63. Biodiversity Loss in Sri Lanka \nImportant factors contributing to biodiversity loss in Sri Lanka include \n• \nHabitat destruction  \n• \nHabitat fragmentation  \n• \nInvasive alien species  \n• \nPollution  \n• \nOverexploitation  \n• \nHuman-wildlife conflict  \n• \nClimate change  \n• \nUnsustainable development  \n \n64. Human-Wildlife Conflict \nHuman-wildlife conflict occurs when the needs or activities of humans and wild animals \nnegatively affect one another. \nExamples include \n• \nCrop damage  \n• \nLivestock predation\n\n• \nProperty damage  \n• \nHuman injury  \n• \nWildlife mortality  \nEffective conservation requires approaches that consider both biodiversity and human \nlivelihoods. \n \n65. Habitat Fragmentation and Sri Lankan Wildlife \nRoads, settlements, agriculture and other forms of development can divide natural habitats. \nFragmentation can restrict animal movement and increase interactions between wildlife and \nhuman communities. \n \n66. Importance of Conservation \nBiodiversity conservation aims to maintain \n• \nGenetic diversity  \n• \nSpecies diversity  \n• \nEcosystem diversity  \n• \nEcological processes  \n• \nEcosystem services  \nConservation is necessary to reduce extinction risk and maintain ecological stability. \n \n67. Conservation Priorities \nEffective biodiversity conservation generally requires \n• \nProtection of important habitats  \n• \nRestoration of degraded ecosystems  \n• \nSustainable resource use  \n• \nControl of invasive species  \n• \nReduction of pollution  \n• \nPrevention of illegal exploitation\n\n• \nMaintenance of ecological connectivity  \n \n68. Key Terms \nBiodiversity \nThe variety and variability of life at genetic, species and ecosystem levels. \nGenetic diversity \nVariation in genetic characteristics within and between populations. \nSpecies richness \nNumber of species present in an area. \nSpecies evenness \nDegree to which individuals are distributed equally among species. \nEcosystem diversity \nVariety of ecosystems and habitats within a region. \nEndemic species \nSpecies naturally restricted to a particular geographical region. \nHabitat fragmentation \nDivision of continuous habitat into smaller isolated patches. \nInvasive alien species \nIntroduced species that establish and cause ecological, economic or social harm. \nBioaccumulation \nAccumulation of a substance within an individual organism. \nBiomagnification \nIncrease in concentration of certain substances at higher trophic levels. \nGenetic bottleneck \nReduction in genetic diversity resulting from a major decrease in population size. \nGenetic drift \nRandom change in allele frequencies due to chance.\n\nExtinction \nPermanent disappearance of a species from Earth. \nEcological resilience \nCapacity of an ecosystem to withstand disturbance and recover. \nKeystone species \nSpecies with a disproportionately large influence on ecosystem structure. \nIndicator species \nSpecies whose condition provides information about environmental conditions. \nFlagship species \nSpecies used to represent and promote conservation efforts. \nUmbrella species \nSpecies whose protection indirectly protects other species sharing its habitat. \n \n69. Final Summary \nBiodiversity exists at genetic, species and ecosystem levels. Genetic diversity allows populations \nto adapt, species diversity contributes to ecological interactions and ecosystem diversity provides \na range of habitats and ecological processes. \nBiodiversity has ecological, economic, cultural, scientific and ethical value. Humans depend on \nbiodiversity for food, medicine, pollination, water purification, climate regulation, agriculture \nand many other ecosystem services. \nThe major threats to biodiversity include habitat loss, habitat fragmentation, overexploitation, \ninvasive alien species, pollution and climate change. These pressures can reduce population \nsizes, decrease genetic diversity, disrupt ecological interactions and increase extinction risk. \nSri Lanka is particularly important for biodiversity conservation because it contains many \nendemic species and diverse ecosystems within a relatively small geographical area. Protecting \nforests, wetlands, freshwater ecosystems and coastal habitats is therefore essential for \nmaintaining the country's biological heritage. \nEffective biodiversity conservation requires protection and restoration of habitats, sustainable use \nof natural resources, control of invasive species, reduction of pollution, maintenance of \necological connectivity and consideration of both ecological and human needs.	COMPLETED	118	2026-08-19 18:12:11.92077	2026-08-19 18:12:51.883022	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
999	Lesson 2: Conservation strategies and international conventions - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 2: Conservation strategies and international conventions	PDF	uploads/pdf/14c1af9a1e904ebaa0f7feee28c4e42b.pdf	\N	Lesson 2 — Conservation Strategies and International Conventions \nIntroduction \nBiodiversity conservation involves protecting species, genetic resources, habitats and ecological \nprocesses from degradation and extinction. Conservation requires both the protection of natural \necosystems and the management of biological resources. \nTwo major approaches to biodiversity conservation are in-situ conservation and ex-situ \nconservation. International agreements and organisations also provide frameworks for \nprotecting biodiversity across national boundaries. \n \n1. Biodiversity Conservation \nBiodiversity conservation is the protection, sustainable management and restoration of \nbiological diversity. \nIt aims to maintain \n• \nGenetic diversity  \n• \nSpecies diversity  \n• \nEcosystem diversity  \n• \nEcological processes  \n• \nEcosystem services  \n \n2. Main Conservation Approaches \nThe two major approaches are \n• \nIn-situ conservation  \n• \nEx-situ conservation  \nIn-situ conservation protects organisms within their natural habitats, while ex-situ conservation \nprotects them outside their natural habitats. \n \n3. In-Situ Conservation \nIn-situ conservation means conserving species within their natural habitats and maintaining the \necological processes associated with those habitats.\n\nIt is generally the preferred approach for maintaining complete ecosystems. \n \n4. Advantages of In-Situ Conservation \nIn-situ conservation \n• \nProtects entire ecosystems  \n• \nMaintains natural ecological interactions  \n• \nAllows natural evolution  \n• \nMaintains populations in their natural environments  \n• \nProtects multiple species simultaneously  \n• \nMaintains ecological processes  \n \n5. Protected Areas \nA protected area is a clearly defined geographical space managed to achieve long-term \nconservation of nature and associated ecosystem services. \nProtected areas are an important tool for in-situ conservation. \n \n6. National Parks \nNational parks are protected areas established primarily for the conservation of ecosystems, \nwildlife and natural landscapes. \nHuman activities are generally regulated to minimise ecological disturbance. \nNational parks can also support \n• \nScientific research  \n• \nEnvironmental education  \n• \nRecreation  \n• \nTourism  \n \n7. Wildlife Sanctuaries \nA wildlife sanctuary is an area established to protect wildlife and their habitats.\n\nHuman activities may be permitted under specific regulations depending on the objectives and \nlegal framework of the sanctuary. \n \n8. Strict Nature Reserves \nStrict nature reserves provide a high level of protection for biodiversity and natural processes. \nHuman access and activities are generally highly restricted. \nThey are primarily intended for conservation and scientific purposes. \n \n9. Forest Reserves \nForest reserves are areas managed to conserve forest ecosystems and their biological resources. \nThey can protect \n• \nNative vegetation  \n• \nWildlife  \n• \nWatersheds  \n• \nSoil  \n• \nGenetic resources  \n \n10. Biosphere Reserves \nA biosphere reserve is a designated area intended to reconcile biodiversity conservation with \nsustainable human use. \nBiosphere reserves commonly include \n• \nCore areas  \n• \nBuffer zones  \n• \nTransition areas  \n \n11. Core Area \nThe core area receives the highest level of protection. \nIts main purpose is to conserve biodiversity and maintain natural ecological processes.\n\n12. Buffer Zone \nThe buffer zone surrounds or adjoins the core area. \nActivities may be permitted if they are compatible with conservation objectives. \nExamples include \n• \nResearch  \n• \nEnvironmental education  \n• \nSustainable resource use  \n \n13. Transition Area \nThe transition area is where sustainable human activities and development are promoted. \nThe objective is to integrate conservation with the needs of local communities. \n \n14. Habitat Corridors \nHabitat corridors connect isolated habitat patches. \nThey help organisms \n• \nMove between habitats  \n• \nFind food  \n• \nFind mates  \n• \nDisperse  \n• \nMaintain gene flow  \nCorridors can reduce some negative effects of habitat fragmentation. \n \n15. Ecological Networks \nAn ecological network consists of connected protected areas and other suitable habitats that \ncollectively support biodiversity and ecological processes. \nNetworks can improve landscape-level conservation.\n\n16. Restoration Ecology \nEcological restoration involves assisting the recovery of degraded ecosystems. \nRestoration may involve \n• \nReplanting native species  \n• \nRemoving invasive species  \n• \nRestoring wetlands  \n• \nReconnecting fragmented habitats  \n• \nImproving water quality  \n \n17. Species Recovery Programmes \nSpecies recovery programmes are designed to increase the population size and long-term \nsurvival probability of threatened species. \nActions may include \n• \nHabitat protection  \n• \nPopulation monitoring  \n• \nCaptive breeding  \n• \nReintroduction  \n• \nDisease management  \n• \nReduction of human threats  \n \n18. Sustainable Use \nConservation does not always require complete prohibition of resource use. \nSustainable use means using biological resources at a rate that does not cause long-term \ndepletion or ecological damage. \nExamples include \n• \nSustainable fisheries  \n• \nControlled harvesting\n\n• \nSustainable forestry  \n• \nRegulated collection of biological resources  \n \n19. Community-Based Conservation \nLocal communities can play an important role in biodiversity conservation. \nCommunity participation can help \n• \nReduce illegal resource extraction  \n• \nProtect habitats  \n• \nManage natural resources  \n• \nImprove conservation compliance  \n• \nSupport local livelihoods  \nSuccessful conservation often requires consideration of local economic and social needs. \n \n20. Ex-Situ Conservation \nEx-situ conservation means conserving species outside their natural habitats. \nIt is particularly useful when wild populations are critically small or when natural habitats are \nseverely threatened. \n \n21. Advantages of Ex-Situ Conservation \nEx-situ conservation can \n• \nProtect organisms from immediate threats  \n• \nFacilitate controlled breeding  \n• \nPreserve genetic material  \n• \nSupport research  \n• \nProvide individuals for reintroduction  \n• \nMaintain populations when natural habitats are unsuitable\n\n22. Botanical Gardens \nBotanical gardens maintain collections of living plants for \n• \nConservation  \n• \nResearch  \n• \nEducation  \n• \nPropagation  \nRare and threatened plant species can sometimes be maintained under controlled conditions. \n \n23. Zoological Gardens \nZoos maintain living populations of animals under managed conditions. \nModern zoological institutions may contribute to \n• \nConservation breeding  \n• \nResearch  \n• \nEducation  \n• \nPublic awareness  \n• \nSpecies recovery  \n \n24. Seed Banks \nA seed bank stores seeds under controlled conditions for long-term conservation. \nSeed storage can preserve genetic diversity of plant species. \nAdvantages include \n• \nRelatively low storage cost  \n• \nLong-term preservation  \n• \nProtection from environmental disturbances  \n• \nAvailability of material for research and restoration  \n \n25. Gene Banks\n\nA gene bank stores genetic material for future use. \nMaterials can include \n• \nSeeds  \n• \nSperm  \n• \nEggs  \n• \nEmbryos  \n• \nTissue  \n• \nDNA  \nGene banks are particularly important for conserving genetic diversity. \n \n26. Cryopreservation \nCryopreservation involves preserving biological material at extremely low temperatures, \nusually using liquid nitrogen. \nAt these temperatures, cellular metabolic activity is greatly reduced. \nIt can be used to preserve \n• \nSperm  \n• \nEggs  \n• \nEmbryos  \n• \nPlant tissues  \n• \nMicroorganisms  \n• \nGenetic material  \n \n27. Captive Breeding \nCaptive breeding involves breeding organisms under controlled conditions outside their natural \nhabitats. \nIt can increase population size and maintain genetic material of threatened species. \n \n28. Reintroduction\n\nReintroduction is the deliberate release of a species into an area where it previously occurred \nbut has disappeared. \nSuccessful reintroduction requires \n• \nSuitable habitat  \n• \nAdequate food  \n• \nAppropriate population size  \n• \nThreat reduction  \n• \nMonitoring  \n \n29. Translocation \nTranslocation is the deliberate movement of organisms from one location to another. \nIt can be used to \n• \nEstablish new populations  \n• \nReinforce existing populations  \n• \nReduce risks from local threats  \n \n30. In-Situ and Ex-Situ Conservation Compared \nIn-situ conservation \nEx-situ conservation \nConservation within natural habitat \nConservation outside natural habitat \nProtects ecosystems \nUsually focuses on particular species or genetic \nmaterial \nMaintains natural ecological \ninteractions \nProvides controlled environmental conditions \nAllows natural adaptation \nAllows controlled breeding and management \nProtected areas are major examples \nZoos, botanical gardens and gene banks are examples \n \n31. Complementary Conservation\n\nIn-situ and ex-situ conservation should not be considered completely separate strategies. \nThey can complement one another. \nFor example, a threatened species may be protected in its natural habitat while a captive \npopulation is maintained as an additional genetic reserve. \n \n32. International Conservation \nBiodiversity does not follow political boundaries. \nMigratory species, marine organisms, wildlife trade and environmental problems can involve \nmultiple countries. \nInternational cooperation is therefore essential. \n \n33. International Conventions \nAn international convention is a formal agreement between countries addressing a particular \nissue. \nImportant biodiversity-related conventions include \n• \nCITES  \n• \nConvention on Biological Diversity  \n• \nRamsar Convention  \n• \nWorld Heritage Convention  \n \n34. CITES \nCITES stands for the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild \nFauna and Flora. \nIt regulates international trade in selected wild animal and plant species and their products. \nIts objective is to ensure that international trade does not threaten the survival of species. \n \n35. Purpose of CITES \nCITES controls international trade through a system of species listings and permits.\n\nIt covers \n• \nAnimals  \n• \nPlants  \n• \nAnimal products  \n• \nPlant products  \ndepending on the species and applicable listing. \n \n36. CITES Appendices \nCITES species are organised into three appendices. \nAppendix I \nIncludes species threatened with extinction. International commercial trade is generally \nprohibited except under exceptional circumstances. \nAppendix II \nIncludes species that may become threatened unless trade is regulated. \nAppendix III \nIncludes species protected in at least one country that requests cooperation from other countries \nto control international trade. \n \n37. Importance of CITES \nCITES helps prevent overexploitation caused by international wildlife trade. \nIt can protect species from commercial pressure involving \n• \nLive animals  \n• \nSkins  \n• \nIvory  \n• \nTimber  \n• \nMedicinal materials  \n• \nOrnamental plants\n\n38. Convention on Biological Diversity \nThe Convention on Biological Diversity, or CBD, is an international agreement focused on \nbiodiversity conservation and sustainable use. \nIt has three central objectives \n1. Conservation of biological diversity  \n2. Sustainable use of biodiversity  \n3. Fair and equitable sharing of benefits arising from the utilisation of genetic resources  \n \n39. Conservation under the CBD \nThe CBD promotes conservation of \n• \nEcosystems  \n• \nSpecies  \n• \nGenetic resources  \nIt encourages countries to develop national strategies and action plans for biodiversity \nconservation. \n \n40. Sustainable Use under the CBD \nThe CBD recognises that biological resources can be used by humans. \nHowever, use should be managed so that biodiversity is maintained for the long term. \n \n41. Access and Benefit Sharing \nThe CBD promotes fair and equitable sharing of benefits arising from the utilisation of genetic \nresources. \nThis principle is particularly important when genetic resources or associated traditional \nknowledge contribute to commercial or scientific developments. \n \n42. Cartagena Protocol \nThe Cartagena Protocol on Biosafety is a supplementary agreement to the CBD.\n\nIt addresses the safe handling, transport and use of living modified organisms that may have \nadverse effects on biodiversity. \n \n43. Nagoya Protocol \nThe Nagoya Protocol addresses access to genetic resources and the fair and equitable sharing of \nbenefits arising from their utilisation. \nIt is closely associated with the CBD's access and benefit-sharing objectives. \n \n44. Ramsar Convention \nThe Ramsar Convention is an international treaty focused on the conservation and wise use of \nwetlands. \nIts official name refers to wetlands of international importance. \n \n45. Ramsar Sites \nA Ramsar Site is a wetland designated as being of international importance under the Ramsar \nConvention. \nDesignation recognises the ecological significance of a wetland and promotes its conservation \nand wise use. \n \n46. Importance of Wetlands \nWetlands provide \n• \nWater storage  \n• \nFlood regulation  \n• \nWater purification  \n• \nFish habitats  \n• \nBird habitats  \n• \nCarbon storage  \n• \nGroundwater recharge  \n• \nLivelihood resources\n\n47. Ramsar Sites in Sri Lanka \nSri Lanka has several internationally recognised Ramsar wetlands. \nExamples include \n• \nBundala  \n• \nKumana  \n• \nVankalai Sanctuary  \n• \nAnawilundawa  \n• \nMaduganga  \n• \nWilpattu-associated wetland areas  \nThese sites support important wetland biodiversity. \n \n48. Ramsar Convention and Wise Use \nThe concept of wise use refers to maintaining the ecological character of wetlands while \nallowing sustainable use that is compatible with conservation. \nIt does not necessarily require complete exclusion of human activity. \n \n49. World Heritage Convention \nThe World Heritage Convention provides an international framework for identifying and \nprotecting cultural and natural heritage of outstanding universal value. \nNatural heritage sites can include areas with exceptional ecological or biological significance. \n \n50. UNESCO Biosphere Reserves \nThe UNESCO Man and the Biosphere Programme promotes sustainable relationships \nbetween people and their environments. \nBiosphere reserves demonstrate approaches that integrate \n• \nBiodiversity conservation  \n• \nSustainable development\n\n• \nResearch  \n• \nEducation  \n \n51. Biodiversity Hotspots and Conservation \nBiodiversity hotspots are important conservation priorities because they combine high levels of \nendemic biodiversity with substantial habitat loss. \nProtecting remaining natural habitats in these regions can prevent many species from becoming \nextinct. \n \n52. Protected Area Management \nSimply declaring an area protected does not guarantee successful conservation. \nEffective management requires \n• \nAdequate funding  \n• \nLaw enforcement  \n• \nScientific monitoring  \n• \nCommunity participation  \n• \nHabitat management  \n• \nThreat reduction  \n \n53. Biodiversity Monitoring \nBiodiversity monitoring involves systematically collecting information about species, \npopulations and ecosystems over time. \nMonitoring can identify \n• \nPopulation declines  \n• \nHabitat changes  \n• \nInvasive species  \n• \nDisease outbreaks  \n• \nConservation success\n\n54. Population Monitoring \nPopulation monitoring can measure \n• \nPopulation size  \n• \nPopulation density  \n• \nBirth rates  \n• \nDeath rates  \n• \nReproductive success  \n• \nDistribution  \nThis information helps determine whether conservation measures are effective. \n \n55. Species Recovery and Genetic Diversity \nConservation programmes should maintain sufficient genetic diversity. \nSmall populations can lose genetic variation through \n• \nGenetic drift  \n• \nInbreeding  \n• \nPopulation bottlenecks  \nMaintaining genetically diverse populations increases long-term adaptive potential. \n \n56. Conservation Genetics \nConservation genetics applies genetic principles to biodiversity conservation. \nIt can be used to \n• \nMeasure genetic diversity  \n• \nIdentify populations  \n• \nDetect inbreeding  \n• \nDetermine relationships between populations  \n• \nGuide captive breeding\n\n• \nSupport reintroduction programmes  \n \n57. IUCN Red List \nThe IUCN Red List of Threatened Species assesses the extinction risk of species. \nMajor categories include \n• \nLeast Concern  \n• \nNear Threatened  \n• \nVulnerable  \n• \nEndangered  \n• \nCritically Endangered  \n• \nExtinct in the Wild  \n• \nExtinct  \n \n58. Threatened Categories \nThe categories Vulnerable, Endangered and Critically Endangered are collectively used to \ndescribe species facing elevated levels of extinction risk. \nThe assessment is based on factors such as \n• \nPopulation decline  \n• \nPopulation size  \n• \nGeographic range  \n• \nDegree of fragmentation  \n \n59. Conservation Prioritisation \nLimited conservation resources require prioritisation. \nPriority may be given to \n• \nCritically endangered species  \n• \nEndemic species\n\n• \nKeystone species  \n• \nHighly threatened ecosystems  \n• \nAreas containing many threatened species  \n• \nEcosystems providing important services  \n \n60. Ex-Situ Limitations \nEx-situ conservation has limitations. \nThese include \n• \nHigh financial cost  \n• \nLimited genetic diversity  \n• \nArtificial selection  \n• \nBehavioural changes  \n• \nLoss of natural ecological interactions  \n• \nDifficulty returning organisms to the wild  \nTherefore, ex-situ conservation should generally complement rather than replace habitat \nconservation. \n \n61. In-Situ Limitations \nIn-situ conservation can also face challenges. \nThese include \n• \nIllegal exploitation  \n• \nHabitat fragmentation  \n• \nInvasive species  \n• \nHuman-wildlife conflict  \n• \nClimate change  \n• \nInsufficient management resources\n\n62. Conservation and Sustainable Development \nModern conservation increasingly recognises the need to balance biodiversity protection with \nhuman development. \nSustainable conservation can involve \n• \nSustainable agriculture  \n• \nEcotourism  \n• \nCommunity forestry  \n• \nSustainable fisheries  \n• \nEnvironmental education  \n• \nAlternative livelihoods  \n \n63. Ecotourism \nEcotourism is responsible travel to natural areas that aims to minimise environmental impacts \nand support conservation and local communities. \nWhen properly managed, ecotourism can provide economic incentives for protecting natural \necosystems. \n \n64. Community Participation \nLocal communities are important stakeholders in conservation. \nParticipation can improve \n• \nResource management  \n• \nCompliance  \n• \nLocal livelihoods  \n• \nConservation awareness  \n• \nLong-term protection  \n \n65. Conservation Education \nEducation helps people understand\n\n• \nThe importance of biodiversity  \n• \nCauses of biodiversity loss  \n• \nConservation methods  \n• \nSustainable resource use  \n• \nLegal responsibilities  \nPublic awareness can strengthen conservation programmes. \n \n66. Key Conservation Principles \nEffective conservation should \n• \nProtect habitats  \n• \nMaintain viable populations  \n• \nPreserve genetic diversity  \n• \nMaintain ecological connectivity  \n• \nReduce threats  \n• \nPromote sustainable resource use  \n• \nInvolve local communities  \n• \nUse scientific evidence  \n• \nMonitor outcomes  \n• \nAdapt management when conditions change  \n \n67. Key Terms \nIn-situ conservation \nConservation of organisms within their natural habitats. \nEx-situ conservation \nConservation of organisms outside their natural habitats. \nProtected area \nA geographically defined area managed for long-term conservation.\n\nHabitat corridor \nA connection between habitat patches that facilitates movement of organisms. \nCaptive breeding \nBreeding organisms under controlled conditions outside their natural habitats. \nReintroduction \nRelease of a species into an area where it previously occurred but has disappeared. \nCITES \nInternational convention regulating international trade in specified wild animals and plants. \nCBD \nConvention focused on biodiversity conservation, sustainable use and benefit sharing. \nRamsar Convention \nInternational treaty concerning the conservation and wise use of wetlands. \nRamsar Site \nWetland recognised as internationally important under the Ramsar Convention. \nBiosphere reserve \nArea designed to integrate biodiversity conservation with sustainable human activities. \nCryopreservation \nPreservation of biological material at extremely low temperatures. \nGene bank \nFacility or system for long-term preservation of genetic material. \nEcological restoration \nProcess of assisting the recovery of degraded ecosystems. \nIUCN Red List \nGlobal assessment system for evaluating species extinction risk. \n \n68. Final Summary\n\nBiodiversity conservation can be achieved through both in-situ and ex-situ strategies. In-situ \nconservation protects organisms within their natural habitats and maintains ecological \ninteractions, while ex-situ conservation protects species or genetic material outside natural \nhabitats through methods such as zoos, botanical gardens, seed banks, gene banks and \ncryopreservation. \nProtected areas, habitat corridors, ecosystem restoration, sustainable resource use and \ncommunity participation are important components of modern conservation. Conservation \nprogrammes must also consider genetic diversity and long-term population viability. \nInternational cooperation is essential because biodiversity and environmental threats cross \nnational boundaries. CITES regulates international trade in threatened wildlife and plant species, \nwhile the Convention on Biological Diversity promotes biodiversity conservation, sustainable \nuse and fair benefit sharing. The Ramsar Convention focuses on the conservation and wise use of \nwetlands of international importance. \nEffective biodiversity conservation requires scientific monitoring, appropriate legislation, \ninternational cooperation and participation from local communities. The ultimate goal is to \nmaintain genetic diversity, viable species populations, functioning ecosystems and the ecological \nservices upon which human societies depend.	COMPLETED	119	2026-08-19 18:12:13.723817	2026-08-19 18:12:51.883023	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
1000	Lesson 1: Applied agricultural biology and post-harvest technology - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 1: Applied agricultural biology and post-harvest technology	PDF	uploads/pdf/dcb444943e5f4a3dbcad372dddbb816b.pdf	\N	Lesson 1 — Applied Agricultural Biology and Post-Harvest Technology \nIntroduction \nAgriculture is the application of biological, chemical and technological knowledge to the \nproduction of food, fibre, animal products and other useful resources. Modern agricultural \nbiology applies principles of genetics, plant physiology, microbiology, biotechnology and animal \nscience to improve productivity and quality. \nAgricultural production does not end with harvesting. A significant proportion of agricultural \nproducts can be lost after harvest because of microbial activity, respiration, water loss, insects, \nmechanical damage and unsuitable storage conditions. Post-harvest technology therefore plays \nan important role in maintaining the quantity and quality of agricultural products. \n \n1. Applied Agricultural Biology \nApplied agricultural biology is the application of biological principles and technologies to \nimprove agricultural production, animal husbandry, crop quality and resource management. \nMajor areas include \n• \nPlant breeding  \n• \nPlant tissue culture  \n• \nAnimal breeding  \n• \nArtificial insemination  \n• \nEmbryo transfer  \n• \nPest management  \n• \nDisease control  \n• \nPost-harvest technology  \n \n2. Objectives of Agricultural Biotechnology \nModern agricultural biotechnology aims to \n• \nIncrease crop yield  \n• \nImprove crop quality  \n• \nProduce disease-resistant varieties\n\n• \nImprove tolerance to environmental stresses  \n• \nImprove livestock productivity  \n• \nPreserve valuable genetic resources  \n• \nReduce production losses  \n• \nImprove food security  \n \n3. Plant Breeding \nPlant breeding is the deliberate manipulation of plant reproduction and selection to produce \nplants with desirable characteristics. \nDesired characteristics may include \n• \nHigh yield  \n• \nDisease resistance  \n• \nPest resistance  \n• \nDrought tolerance  \n• \nImproved nutritional value  \n• \nImproved storage properties  \n• \nBetter quality  \n \n4. Conventional Plant Breeding \nConventional plant breeding generally involves selecting plants with desirable characteristics and \ncrossing them to combine useful traits. \nA simplified process is \nSelection of parents → Cross-pollination → Production of offspring → Selection → Testing \n→ Development of improved variety \n \n5. Hybridisation \nHybridisation is the crossing of genetically different individuals to produce offspring with \ndesirable combinations of characteristics.\n\nThe selected parents may possess different advantageous traits. \nFor example, one parent may have high yield while another has disease resistance. \n \n6. Selection \nSelection involves identifying and retaining individuals with desirable characteristics for further \nreproduction. \nSelection may be based on \n• \nPhenotypic characteristics  \n• \nGenetic information  \n• \nMolecular markers  \n• \nPerformance under environmental conditions  \n \n7. Plant Tissue Culture \nPlant tissue culture is the growth of plant cells, tissues or organs under sterile conditions on a \nnutrient medium. \nIt is an important technique in modern plant biotechnology. \n \n8. Principle of Plant Tissue Culture \nPlant tissue culture is based on the concept of cellular totipotency. \nTotipotency is the ability of a plant cell to potentially develop into a complete plant under \nsuitable conditions. \nThis ability allows a small piece of plant tissue to be used to produce new plants. \n \n9. Explant \nAn explant is a small piece of plant tissue removed from a plant and used to initiate a tissue \nculture. \nExamples include \n• \nShoot tips\n\n• \nMeristems  \n• \nLeaves  \n• \nStem sections  \n• \nRoot tissues  \n \n10. Culture Medium \nPlant tissues are grown on a nutrient medium containing substances required for growth. \nA culture medium may contain \n• \nWater  \n• \nMineral nutrients  \n• \nSugars  \n• \nVitamins  \n• \nAmino acids or organic supplements  \n• \nPlant growth regulators  \n \n11. Sterile Conditions \nPlant tissue culture must be performed under sterile conditions to prevent contamination by \n• \nBacteria  \n• \nFungi  \n• \nOther microorganisms  \nContamination can prevent successful growth of the cultured tissue. \n \n12. Micropropagation \nMicropropagation is the rapid production of genetically similar plants using tissue culture \ntechniques. \nA simplified sequence is \nExplant → Multiplication → Root formation → Plantlet development → Acclimatisation → \nField planting\n\n13. Multiplication \nDuring the multiplication stage, plant tissues are stimulated to produce numerous shoots. \nPlant growth regulators are used to control cell division and organ development. \n \n14. Root Formation \nDeveloping shoots are transferred to conditions that promote root formation. \nOnce a functional root system develops, the plantlet can be prepared for transfer outside the \nculture environment. \n \n15. Acclimatisation \nPlantlets produced under controlled tissue culture conditions are sensitive to environmental \nchanges. \nAcclimatisation gradually adapts plantlets to external conditions such as \n• \nLower humidity  \n• \nGreater light intensity  \n• \nNatural air movement  \n• \nSoil-based growth conditions  \n \n16. Advantages of Micropropagation \nMicropropagation allows \n• \nRapid multiplication  \n• \nProduction of large numbers of plants  \n• \nProduction of relatively uniform plants  \n• \nYear-round propagation  \n• \nPropagation of plants that are difficult to reproduce conventionally  \n \n17. Disease-Free Plant Production\n\nMeristem culture can be used to produce plants with reduced levels of certain systemic \npathogens. \nThis is particularly useful for vegetatively propagated crops. \n \n18. Meristem Culture \nA meristem contains actively dividing, relatively undifferentiated plant cells. \nMeristem tissues are commonly used in tissue culture because they can regenerate into complete \nplants. \n \n19. Somatic Embryogenesis \nSomatic embryogenesis is the development of embryo-like structures from somatic cells rather \nthan from fertilisation. \nThese structures can develop into complete plants under suitable conditions. \n \n20. Synthetic Seeds \nSome plant tissues or somatic embryos can be enclosed in protective materials to form structures \nknown as synthetic seeds or artificial seeds. \nThey can potentially be used for propagation and storage of selected plant material. \n \n21. Applications of Plant Tissue Culture \nPlant tissue culture is used for \n• \nRapid multiplication  \n• \nProduction of disease-free planting material  \n• \nConservation of rare plants  \n• \nGermplasm preservation  \n• \nProduction of uniform plants  \n• \nPropagation of commercially important crops\n\n22. Germplasm \nGermplasm refers to the genetic material of an organism that can be maintained and used for \nbreeding or conservation. \nPlant germplasm may be preserved through \n• \nSeed storage  \n• \nField collections  \n• \nTissue culture  \n• \nCryopreservation  \n \n23. Animal Breeding \nAnimal breeding involves selecting and mating animals to improve desirable characteristics in \ntheir offspring. \nImportant traits include \n• \nGrowth rate  \n• \nMilk production  \n• \nMeat quality  \n• \nReproductive performance  \n• \nDisease resistance  \n• \nFeed efficiency  \n \n24. Selective Breeding in Livestock \nSelective breeding involves choosing animals with desirable genetic characteristics as parents of \nthe next generation. \nOver successive generations, favourable characteristics can become more common in the \npopulation. \n \n25. Artificial Insemination \nArtificial insemination, or AI, is the introduction of collected semen into the reproductive tract \nof a female using artificial methods rather than natural mating.\n\nIt is widely used in livestock improvement. \n \n26. Procedure of Artificial Insemination \nA simplified process involves \n1. Selection of a suitable male  \n2. Collection of semen  \n3. Evaluation of semen quality  \n4. Processing and storage  \n5. Detection of the female's appropriate reproductive stage  \n6. Introduction of semen into the female reproductive tract  \n \n27. Advantages of Artificial Insemination \nArtificial insemination can \n• \nIncrease use of genetically superior males  \n• \nImprove breeding efficiency  \n• \nReduce the need to maintain many breeding males  \n• \nAllow semen storage  \n• \nFacilitate selective breeding  \n• \nReduce some risks associated with natural mating  \n \n28. Semen Storage \nSemen can be preserved for later use. \nCryopreservation allows sperm cells to be stored at extremely low temperatures. \nThis makes it possible to use genetic material from valuable males over long periods. \n \n29. Embryo Transfer\n\nEmbryo transfer, or ET, is a reproductive technology in which an embryo produced by a \ngenetically valuable female is transferred into the reproductive tract of another female capable of \ncarrying the pregnancy. \nThe donor and recipient animals therefore have different reproductive roles. \n \n30. Principle of Embryo Transfer \nA simplified sequence is \nSelection of donor female → Hormonal treatment → Mating or artificial insemination → \nEmbryo development → Embryo collection → Selection → Transfer to recipient female \n \n31. Donor Female \nThe donor female is a genetically valuable female from which embryos are obtained. \nThe donor is selected based on desirable characteristics. \n \n32. Recipient Female \nThe recipient female carries the transferred embryo through pregnancy. \nThe recipient does not necessarily contribute genetically to the offspring. \nHer role is primarily to provide the reproductive environment required for development. \n \n33. Superovulation \nBefore embryo collection, the donor female may receive hormonal treatment to stimulate the \ndevelopment and ovulation of multiple follicles. \nThis process is known as superovulation. \nIt increases the potential number of embryos produced during a reproductive cycle. \n \n34. Embryo Collection \nAfter fertilisation and early embryonic development, embryos can be collected from the donor's \nreproductive tract using appropriate reproductive techniques. \nThe embryos are then evaluated.\n\n35. Embryo Transfer \nSelected embryos are transferred into synchronised recipient females. \nSynchronization is important because the reproductive environment of the recipient must be \nappropriate for the developmental stage of the embryo. \n \n36. Advantages of Embryo Transfer \nEmbryo transfer can \n• \nIncrease the number of offspring obtained from valuable females  \n• \nAccelerate genetic improvement  \n• \nAllow wider distribution of superior genetics  \n• \nSupport conservation of valuable breeds  \n• \nReduce the generation interval in some breeding programmes  \n \n37. Artificial Insemination Compared with Embryo Transfer \nArtificial insemination \nEmbryo transfer \nTransfers sperm \nTransfers embryo \nUses semen from a selected male \nUses embryos from a selected female \nFemale produces the embryo \nDonor female produces the embryo \nRelatively simpler technique \nMore complex reproductive technology \nMainly increases use of superior male \ngenetics \nCan multiply the reproductive contribution of superior \nfemales \n \n38. Livestock Genetic Improvement \nAI and ET can be combined to accelerate genetic improvement. \nFor example\n\nSuperior male + superior female → Artificial insemination → Embryo production → \nEmbryo transfer → Multiple offspring \nThis allows desirable genetic combinations to be reproduced more efficiently. \n \n39. Agricultural Disease Management \nDiseases can significantly reduce agricultural productivity. \nDisease management involves \n• \nDisease-resistant varieties  \n• \nHealthy planting material  \n• \nVaccination where appropriate  \n• \nBiosecurity  \n• \nSanitation  \n• \nBiological control  \n• \nAppropriate chemical control  \n \n40. Biological Control \nBiological control involves using living organisms or their natural products to control pests or \npathogens. \nExamples include the use of \n• \nPredators  \n• \nParasitoids  \n• \nPathogenic microorganisms  \nIt can reduce reliance on chemical pesticides. \n \n41. Integrated Pest Management \nIntegrated Pest Management, or IPM, combines multiple pest-control approaches. \nIt may involve \n• \nMonitoring pest populations\n\n• \nBiological control  \n• \nCultural practices  \n• \nMechanical control  \n• \nResistant varieties  \n• \nSelective pesticide use  \nThe objective is effective pest management while reducing environmental damage. \n \n42. Post-Harvest Biology \nHarvesting does not immediately stop biological activity in agricultural products. \nFruits and vegetables remain metabolically active after harvest. \nImportant processes include \n• \nRespiration  \n• \nTranspiration  \n• \nRipening  \n• \nSenescence  \n• \nMicrobial growth  \n \n43. Post-Harvest Loss \nPost-harvest loss refers to the reduction in quantity or quality of agricultural products between \nharvesting and consumption. \nLosses may occur during \n• \nHandling  \n• \nTransportation  \n• \nStorage  \n• \nProcessing  \n• \nMarketing\n\n44. Causes of Post-Harvest Loss \nMajor causes include \n• \nMechanical damage  \n• \nWater loss  \n• \nRespiration  \n• \nMicrobial spoilage  \n• \nInsect infestation  \n• \nImproper temperature  \n• \nImproper humidity  \n• \nPoor packaging  \n \n45. Respiration After Harvest \nHarvested plant tissues continue to respire. \nDuring respiration, stored organic compounds are broken down to produce energy. \nA simplified representation is \nGlucose + Oxygen → Carbon dioxide + Water + Energy \nHigh respiration rates generally accelerate deterioration. \n \n46. Factors Affecting Respiration \nRespiration is affected by \n• \nTemperature  \n• \nOxygen concentration  \n• \nCarbon dioxide concentration  \n• \nProduct maturity  \n• \nTissue damage  \nReducing respiration can help extend storage life.\n\n47. Transpiration \nTranspiration is the loss of water vapour from plant tissues. \nAfter harvest, excessive water loss can cause \n• \nWeight loss  \n• \nWilting  \n• \nShrivelling  \n• \nReduced quality  \n \n48. Temperature Control \nTemperature is one of the most important factors affecting post-harvest storage. \nLowering temperature appropriately can reduce \n• \nRespiration  \n• \nMicrobial growth  \n• \nMetabolic activity  \nHowever, excessively low temperatures can cause chilling injury in sensitive crops. \n \n49. Cold Storage \nCold storage involves maintaining agricultural products at controlled low temperatures to slow \ndeterioration. \nIt is widely used for \n• \nFruits  \n• \nVegetables  \n• \nDairy products  \n• \nMeat  \n• \nFish  \nThe appropriate temperature depends on the product.\n\n50. Relative Humidity \nRelative humidity affects water loss from stored agricultural products. \nLow humidity can cause excessive water loss. \nExcessively high humidity can encourage condensation and microbial growth. \nTherefore, appropriate humidity must be maintained for each product. \n \n51. Controlled Atmosphere Storage \nControlled atmosphere storage involves regulating the concentrations of gases surrounding \nstored produce. \nTypically, oxygen is reduced and carbon dioxide is adjusted to appropriate levels. \nThis can slow respiration and delay deterioration. \n \n52. Modified Atmosphere Packaging \nModified atmosphere packaging, or MAP, involves changing the gaseous environment inside a \npackage. \nThe atmosphere changes through the interaction between the product's respiration and the \npackage's gas permeability. \nIt can extend the shelf life of some fresh products. \n \n53. Ethylene \nEthylene is a gaseous plant hormone involved in processes including \n• \nFruit ripening  \n• \nSenescence  \n• \nAbscission  \nManaging ethylene levels can be important during storage and transportation. \n \n54. Climacteric Fruits\n\nClimacteric fruits show a characteristic increase in respiration associated with ripening and are \ngenerally responsive to ethylene. \nExamples include \n• \nBanana  \n• \nMango  \n• \nTomato  \n• \nApple  \n \n55. Non-Climacteric Fruits \nNon-climacteric fruits do not show the same characteristic climacteric rise in respiration during \nripening. \nExamples include \n• \nCitrus fruits  \n• \nGrapes  \n• \nStrawberries  \n \n56. Harvest Maturity \nHarvesting at the appropriate stage of maturity is essential for maintaining quality. \nHarvest maturity affects \n• \nStorage life  \n• \nFlavour  \n• \nTexture  \n• \nColour  \n• \nNutritional quality  \n \n57. Mechanical Damage \nMechanical damage can occur through \n• \nDropping\n\n• \nCompression  \n• \nImpact  \n• \nVibration  \n• \nImproper handling  \nDamaged tissues are more susceptible to water loss and microbial infection. \n \n58. Sorting and Grading \nSorting separates agricultural products according to quality or condition. \nGrading classifies products according to established characteristics such as \n• \nSize  \n• \nColour  \n• \nShape  \n• \nMaturity  \n• \nQuality  \nThese processes improve uniformity and market value. \n \n59. Packaging \nAppropriate packaging protects agricultural products against \n• \nMechanical damage  \n• \nContamination  \n• \nExcessive water loss  \n• \nEnvironmental conditions  \nPackaging materials must be selected according to the product and storage conditions. \n \n60. Drying \nDrying reduces the water content of agricultural products. \nReducing available water can inhibit microbial growth and slow many deterioration processes.\n\nExamples include drying \n• \nGrains  \n• \nFruits  \n• \nVegetables  \n• \nSpices  \n \n61. Water Activity \nWater activity describes the availability of water for chemical reactions and microbial growth. \nReducing water activity can significantly improve the storage stability of many foods. \nDrying and dehydration are important methods for reducing water activity. \n \n62. Salting \nSalting preserves food by increasing the concentration of dissolved salts. \nHigh salt concentrations can reduce water availability to microorganisms and inhibit their \ngrowth. \n \n63. Sugar Preservation \nHigh concentrations of sugar can reduce water availability. \nThis principle is used in products such as \n• \nJams  \n• \nJellies  \n• \nPreserves  \n \n64. Fermentation \nFermentation uses microorganisms to convert organic substrates into useful products. \nDepending on the microorganisms and conditions, fermentation can produce \n• \nOrganic acids\n\n• \nAlcohols  \n• \nGases  \nFermentation can preserve foods while also changing their flavour and texture. \n \n65. Pasteurisation \nPasteurisation involves controlled heating of food or beverages to reduce harmful or spoilage \nmicroorganisms without causing the degree of heating associated with sterilisation. \nIt is widely used for products such as milk. \n \n66. Sterilisation \nSterilisation aims to eliminate all viable microorganisms, including resistant forms such as \nbacterial spores, using validated physical or chemical methods. \nIt requires more severe treatment than pasteurisation. \n \n67. Refrigeration \nRefrigeration slows microbial growth and biochemical reactions. \nIt is commonly used for \n• \nFruits  \n• \nVegetables  \n• \nDairy products  \n• \nMeat  \n• \nFish  \nRefrigeration does not generally eliminate microorganisms; it mainly slows their growth. \n \n68. Freezing \nFreezing reduces temperature sufficiently to convert much of the water in food into ice. \nThis greatly slows microbial growth and chemical reactions. \nFreezing does not necessarily kill all microorganisms.\n\n69. Irradiation \nFood irradiation exposes food to controlled ionising radiation. \nIt can be used to \n• \nReduce microbial contamination  \n• \nControl insects  \n• \nDelay sprouting  \n• \nExtend shelf life  \nThe process does not make food radioactive when appropriately applied. \n \n70. Biological Preservation \nBiological preservation uses beneficial microorganisms or their products to inhibit undesirable \nmicroorganisms. \nExamples include fermentation by selected microorganisms that produce acids or other inhibitory \nsubstances. \n \n71. Importance of Post-Harvest Technology \nEffective post-harvest management \n• \nReduces food losses  \n• \nMaintains quality  \n• \nExtends shelf life  \n• \nImproves food safety  \n• \nIncreases economic value  \n• \nImproves food availability  \nReducing post-harvest losses can increase food availability without necessarily increasing \nagricultural land use. \n \n72. Agricultural Biotechnology and Food Security\n\nAgricultural biotechnology can contribute to food security through \n• \nIncreased productivity  \n• \nImproved crop resilience  \n• \nDisease resistance  \n• \nBetter livestock breeding  \n• \nImproved propagation  \n• \nReduced post-harvest losses  \nHowever, technologies must be evaluated for environmental, economic and ethical \nconsiderations. \n \n73. Key Terms \nPlant breeding \nDeliberate selection and crossing of plants to produce desirable characteristics. \nPlant tissue culture \nGrowth of plant cells, tissues or organs under sterile controlled conditions. \nTotipotency \nAbility of a plant cell to potentially regenerate into a complete plant. \nMicropropagation \nRapid multiplication of plants using tissue culture. \nGermplasm \nGenetic material maintained for conservation, research or breeding. \nArtificial insemination \nArtificial introduction of semen into the female reproductive tract. \nEmbryo transfer \nTransfer of an embryo from a donor female to a recipient female. \nSuperovulation \nHormonal stimulation of multiple ovulations in a female.\n\nPost-harvest loss \nLoss of quantity or quality of agricultural products after harvesting. \nRespiration \nMetabolic process that releases energy from organic compounds. \nTranspiration \nLoss of water vapour from plant tissues. \nControlled atmosphere storage \nStorage under regulated concentrations of atmospheric gases. \nModified atmosphere packaging \nPackaging that creates a modified gaseous environment around a product. \nWater activity \nMeasure of the availability of water for microbial growth and chemical reactions. \nPasteurisation \nControlled heat treatment designed to reduce harmful or spoilage microorganisms. \nCryopreservation \nPreservation of biological material at extremely low temperatures. \n \n74. Final Summary \nApplied agricultural biology uses biological knowledge and technology to improve crop and \nlivestock production. Plant breeding, tissue culture and micropropagation allow desirable plant \ncharacteristics to be selected, multiplied and conserved. Plant tissue culture relies on cellular \ntotipotency and provides important applications in rapid propagation and conservation of \nvaluable germplasm. \nAnimal breeding can be accelerated using reproductive technologies such as artificial \ninsemination and embryo transfer. Artificial insemination allows semen from genetically superior \nmales to be used efficiently, while embryo transfer increases the reproductive contribution of \ngenetically valuable females. \nPost-harvest technology is essential because harvested agricultural products remain biologically \nactive. Respiration, transpiration, ripening, microbial growth and mechanical damage can reduce\n\nquality and cause substantial losses. Appropriate temperature, humidity, atmosphere, packaging \nand preservation methods can slow deterioration and extend shelf life. \nModern agricultural biology therefore contributes not only to increasing agricultural productivity \nbut also to conserving genetic resources, improving food quality, reducing post-harvest losses \nand supporting long-term food security.	COMPLETED	120	2026-08-19 18:12:16.449273	2026-08-19 18:12:51.883025	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
1001	Lesson 2: Modern medicine, molecular diagnostics, and forensic biology - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 2: Modern medicine, molecular diagnostics, and forensic biology	PDF	uploads/pdf/a5e8f3b4ddf94551a5107c717912daa9.pdf	\N	Lesson 2 — Modern Medicine, Molecular Diagnostics, and Forensic Biology \nIntroduction \nModern biology has transformed medicine through advances in molecular biology, genetics, \nimmunology and biotechnology. Techniques such as polymerase chain reaction, enzyme-linked \nimmunosorbent assay, DNA profiling and monoclonal antibody production allow biological \nprocesses to be investigated and diseases to be diagnosed with increasing accuracy. \nForensic biology applies biological principles to legal investigations. DNA analysis is \nparticularly important because genetic variation between individuals can be used to identify \nbiological samples and establish relationships. \n \n1. Modern Medicine \nModern medicine increasingly uses biological and molecular information to diagnose, prevent \nand treat disease. \nImportant applications include \n• \nMolecular diagnostics  \n• \nGenetic testing  \n• \nDNA profiling  \n• \nImmunological diagnosis  \n• \nMonoclonal antibodies  \n• \nStem cell technology  \n• \nMolecular treatment strategies  \n \n2. Molecular Diagnostics \nMolecular diagnostics involves detecting specific biological molecules associated with a \ndisease or biological condition. \nThe molecules examined may include \n• \nDNA  \n• \nRNA  \n• \nProteins\n\n• \nAntibodies  \n• \nAntigens  \nMolecular diagnostics can identify infections and genetic conditions with high specificity. \n \n3. Advantages of Molecular Diagnostics \nMolecular diagnostic methods can provide \n• \nHigh sensitivity  \n• \nHigh specificity  \n• \nRapid detection  \n• \nEarly diagnosis  \n• \nIdentification of genetic abnormalities  \n• \nDetection of microorganisms that are difficult to culture  \n \n4. Polymerase Chain Reaction \nPolymerase Chain Reaction, or PCR, is a technique used to amplify a specific DNA sequence. \nPCR can produce millions of copies of a selected DNA region from a very small starting \nquantity. \n \n5. Basic Components of PCR \nA typical PCR reaction requires \n• \nTemplate DNA  \n• \nPrimers  \n• \nDNA polymerase  \n• \nDeoxyribonucleotide triphosphates  \n• \nSuitable buffer  \n• \nMagnesium ions  \nA heat-stable DNA polymerase is used because the reaction involves repeated heating.\n\n6. PCR Cycle \nEach PCR cycle consists of three major stages \n1. Denaturation  \n2. Annealing  \n3. Extension  \nRepeated cycles result in exponential amplification of the target DNA sequence. \n \n7. Denaturation \nDuring denaturation, double-stranded DNA is heated so that hydrogen bonds between \ncomplementary bases are disrupted. \nThe two DNA strands separate. \n \n8. Annealing \nDuring annealing, the temperature is lowered so that primers bind to complementary sequences \non the template DNA. \nThe primers determine which region will be amplified. \n \n9. Extension \nDuring extension, DNA polymerase adds nucleotides to the primers. \nThe new DNA strands are synthesised in the 5′ to 3′ direction. \n \n10. Exponential Amplification \nIdeally, the amount of target DNA approximately doubles during each PCR cycle. \nAfter repeated cycles, a very large number of copies of the target sequence can be produced. \n \n11. Reverse Transcription PCR \nRNA cannot normally serve directly as the template for conventional DNA amplification.\n\nIn reverse transcription PCR, RNA is first converted into complementary DNA using the \nenzyme reverse transcriptase. \nThe resulting complementary DNA can then be amplified using PCR. \n \n12. Real-Time PCR \nReal-time PCR, also called quantitative PCR or qPCR, monitors DNA amplification during the \nreaction. \nFluorescent signals are used to measure the accumulation of amplified DNA. \nIt can be used to estimate the amount of target nucleic acid present in a sample. \n \n13. Medical Applications of PCR \nPCR can be used for \n• \nDetection of infectious microorganisms  \n• \nIdentification of genetic mutations  \n• \nCancer-related molecular testing  \n• \nDetection of inherited disorders  \n• \nMonitoring certain diseases  \n• \nResearch and drug development  \n \n14. PCR in Infectious Disease Diagnosis \nIf a pathogen contains a characteristic DNA sequence, PCR can detect that sequence in a clinical \nsample. \nFor RNA viruses, reverse transcription is generally required before amplification. \nThis allows molecular detection even when the pathogen is difficult to culture. \n \n15. Enzyme-Linked Immunosorbent Assay \nEnzyme-Linked Immunosorbent Assay, or ELISA, is an immunological technique used to \ndetect and measure specific antigens or antibodies.\n\nIt is based on the specific interaction between an antigen and an antibody. \n \n16. Principle of ELISA \nELISA generally involves \nAntigen-antibody binding → Enzyme-linked detection → Addition of substrate → Colour \ndevelopment \nThe intensity of the resulting signal can be related to the amount of target substance present. \n \n17. Antigens \nAn antigen is a substance that can be specifically recognised by components of the immune \nsystem, particularly antibodies or antigen receptors. \nExamples include molecules present on microorganisms. \n \n18. Antibodies \nAntibodies are proteins produced by B lymphocytes and their differentiated plasma cells. \nThey bind specifically to particular antigenic structures. \nThis specificity makes antibodies useful in diagnostic tests. \n \n19. ELISA Applications \nELISA can be used for \n• \nDetection of antibodies  \n• \nDetection of antigens  \n• \nDiagnosis of infectious diseases  \n• \nDetection of hormones  \n• \nDetection of certain proteins  \n• \nMedical and research applications  \n \n20. Direct ELISA\n\nIn direct ELISA, an enzyme-linked antibody binds directly to the target antigen. \nThe enzyme produces a detectable signal after addition of a suitable substrate. \n \n21. Indirect ELISA \nIn indirect ELISA, an unlabelled primary antibody binds to the target antigen. \nAn enzyme-linked secondary antibody then binds to the primary antibody. \nThis method is commonly used for detecting antibodies in patient samples. \n \n22. Sandwich ELISA \nIn sandwich ELISA, the target antigen is captured between antibodies. \nA simplified arrangement is \nCapture antibody → Antigen → Detection antibody \nThis method can provide high specificity for detecting particular antigens. \n \n23. Competitive ELISA \nIn competitive ELISA, the target molecule competes with a labelled or related molecule for \nbinding. \nThe resulting signal is related inversely to the concentration of the target in many assay designs. \n \n24. Monoclonal Antibodies \nMonoclonal antibodies are identical antibodies produced from a single B-cell clone and \nrecognise the same specific antigenic target. \nThey are important tools in medicine, research and biotechnology. \n \n25. Production of Monoclonal Antibodies \nA classical method involves hybridoma technology. \nThe general process is\n\nAntigen exposure → B lymphocyte production → Isolation of antibody-producing cells → \nFusion with myeloma cells → Hybridoma formation → Selection → Cloning → Antibody \nproduction \n \n26. B Lymphocytes \nB lymphocytes produce antibodies following appropriate activation. \nHowever, normal antibody-producing B cells generally have limited lifespans in culture. \nThis creates a challenge for continuous antibody production. \n \n27. Myeloma Cells \nMyeloma cells are tumour-derived cells capable of continuous division. \nThey can provide the immortality required for long-term antibody production when fused with \nsuitable B cells. \n \n28. Hybridoma \nA hybridoma is a cell produced by fusion between an antibody-producing B lymphocyte and an \nimmortal myeloma cell. \nIt combines \n• \nAntibody production capability  \n• \nLong-term proliferative capacity  \n \n29. Hybridoma Selection \nFollowing cell fusion, a selective medium is used to identify hybrid cells. \nThe desired hybridomas are then screened to determine which produce the required antibody. \n \n30. Clonal Selection \nA hybridoma producing the desired antibody is isolated and multiplied. \nBecause the resulting cells originate from a single cell, they produce antibodies with the same \nspecificity.\n\n31. Applications of Monoclonal Antibodies \nMonoclonal antibodies are used in \n• \nDisease diagnosis  \n• \nLaboratory testing  \n• \nCancer treatment  \n• \nAutoimmune disease treatment  \n• \nResearch  \n• \nDetection of specific molecules  \n \n32. Therapeutic Monoclonal Antibodies \nSome monoclonal antibodies are designed to bind specific molecules involved in disease \nprocesses. \nThey may \n• \nBlock signalling molecules  \n• \nTarget specific cell-surface proteins  \n• \nActivate immune responses  \n• \nDeliver therapeutic substances  \n \n33. Stem Cells \nStem cells are cells capable of self-renewal and differentiation into specialised cell types. \nTheir developmental potential varies depending on the type of stem cell. \n \n34. Main Properties of Stem Cells \nStem cells have two important characteristics \nSelf-renewal \nThe ability to produce more stem cells through cell division.\n\nDifferentiation \nThe ability to develop into specialised cell types. \n \n35. Embryonic Stem Cells \nEmbryonic stem cells are derived from the inner cell mass of an early-stage embryo. \nThey are pluripotent, meaning they can differentiate into many types of body cells. \n \n36. Adult Stem Cells \nAdult stem cells occur in various tissues. \nThey generally have more limited differentiation potential than embryonic stem cells. \nExamples include \n• \nHaematopoietic stem cells  \n• \nMesenchymal stem cells  \n \n37. Haematopoietic Stem Cells \nHaematopoietic stem cells produce the different types of blood cells. \nThey are particularly important in bone marrow transplantation. \n \n38. Induced Pluripotent Stem Cells \nInduced pluripotent stem cells, or iPSCs, are mature differentiated cells that have been \ngenetically reprogrammed to acquire pluripotent characteristics. \nThey provide an important research tool for studying development and disease. \n \n39. Applications of Stem Cells \nStem cell technology has applications in \n• \nRegenerative medicine  \n• \nTissue research\n\n• \nDisease modelling  \n• \nDrug testing  \n• \nBlood-cell replacement  \n• \nDevelopmental biology  \n \n40. Challenges of Stem Cell Technology \nChallenges include \n• \nControlling differentiation  \n• \nPreventing unwanted cell growth  \n• \nImmune compatibility  \n• \nGenetic stability  \n• \nEthical considerations  \n• \nSafe clinical application  \n \n41. Forensic Biology \nForensic biology applies biological techniques to legal investigations. \nBiological evidence may include \n• \nBlood  \n• \nSaliva  \n• \nSemen  \n• \nHair  \n• \nSkin cells  \n• \nTissue  \n• \nBone  \n• \nOther biological materials  \n \n42. DNA as Forensic Evidence\n\nDNA is particularly valuable in forensic investigations because individuals generally have \ndifferent DNA sequences. \nExceptions include genetically identical individuals such as monozygotic twins, who have \nextremely similar genomic DNA profiles. \n \n43. DNA Profiling \nDNA profiling is the analysis of selected highly variable regions of DNA to produce a genetic \nprofile that can be compared between biological samples. \nModern forensic profiling commonly analyses Short Tandem Repeats. \n \n44. Short Tandem Repeats \nShort Tandem Repeats, or STRs, are short DNA sequences repeated consecutively at particular \nlocations in the genome. \nThe number of repeats can vary between individuals. \nThis variation makes STRs useful for identification. \n \n45. STR Profile \nAn STR profile consists of the allele information obtained from multiple STR loci. \nThe probability that two unrelated individuals have the same complete profile can be extremely \nlow when sufficient loci are analysed. \n \n46. DNA Profiling Process \nA simplified forensic DNA workflow is \nSample collection → DNA extraction → DNA quantification → PCR amplification → STR \nanalysis → Profile generation → Comparison \n \n47. DNA Extraction \nDNA must first be isolated from the biological sample. \nThe process separates DNA from\n\n• \nProteins  \n• \nLipids  \n• \nCellular debris  \n• \nOther substances  \n \n48. DNA Quantification \nDNA concentration and quality may be measured before amplification. \nThis helps determine whether sufficient DNA is available for successful analysis. \n \n49. STR Amplification \nSelected STR regions are amplified using PCR. \nModern forensic systems commonly amplify multiple STR loci simultaneously using multiplex \nPCR. \n \n50. STR Detection \nAmplified STR fragments can be separated according to size using techniques such as capillary \nelectrophoresis. \nThe resulting data are analysed to determine the alleles present at each locus. \n \n51. DNA Profile Comparison \nA DNA profile from an evidence sample can be compared with \n• \nA suspect's reference sample  \n• \nA victim's reference sample  \n• \nA missing person's biological relatives  \n• \nA database profile where legally permitted  \n \n52. Forensic DNA Matching \nA match between DNA profiles does not simply mean that a person has been proven guilty.\n\nThe strength of the evidence depends on factors including \n• \nNumber of loci analysed  \n• \nProfile quality  \n• \nPopulation genetics  \n• \nProbability of a coincidental match  \n• \nQuality of evidence handling  \n• \nChain of custody  \n \n53. DNA Evidence and Probability \nForensic DNA results are interpreted statistically. \nA strong match may be associated with a very low probability that an unrelated individual would \npossess the same profile. \nThe statistical interpretation must be based on appropriate population data and validated \nmethods. \n \n54. Paternity Testing \nDNA profiling can also be used to determine biological relationships. \nA child inherits genetic material from both biological parents. \nTherefore, STR profiles can be compared among \n• \nChild  \n• \nMother  \n• \nAlleged father  \nto assess biological paternity. \n \n55. Forensic Identification of Remains \nDNA analysis can assist in identifying human remains when other identification methods are \ndifficult. \nComparisons may be made with\n\n• \nPersonal reference samples  \n• \nBiological relatives  \n• \nPreviously collected medical samples  \n \n56. DNA Databases \nDNA databases contain genetic profiles collected and stored according to applicable legal \nframeworks. \nThey can assist investigations by comparing crime-scene profiles with existing profiles. \nThe use of DNA databases requires appropriate legal, ethical and privacy safeguards. \n \n57. Contamination \nForensic DNA samples are vulnerable to contamination. \nContamination occurs when DNA from an unwanted source becomes mixed with the evidence. \nStrict procedures are therefore required during \n• \nCollection  \n• \nTransportation  \n• \nStorage  \n• \nLaboratory analysis  \n \n58. Chain of Custody \nChain of custody is the documented history of the handling and transfer of evidence. \nIt establishes \n• \nWho collected the evidence  \n• \nWhen it was collected  \n• \nHow it was stored  \n• \nWho handled it  \n• \nWhen it was transferred\n\nMaintaining chain of custody supports the reliability and legal admissibility of evidence. \n \n59. DNA Fingerprinting and DNA Profiling \nThe terms DNA fingerprinting and DNA profiling are often used interchangeably in general \ndiscussions. \nHistorically, DNA fingerprinting referred to earlier methods involving highly variable DNA \nregions and restriction fragment analysis. \nModern forensic identification primarily relies on STR-based DNA profiling. \n \n60. PCR in Forensic Biology \nPCR is particularly valuable in forensic science because only a small amount of DNA may be \navailable. \nIt allows selected DNA regions to be amplified from samples such as \n• \nBlood  \n• \nSaliva  \n• \nHair roots  \n• \nSkin cells  \n• \nTissue  \n \n61. PCR and Degraded Samples \nForensic biological material may be old or degraded. \nShort DNA targets can sometimes still be amplified even when larger DNA molecules have \nbroken down. \nThis contributes to the usefulness of STR analysis. \n \n62. Molecular Diagnostics and Forensic Biology Compared\n\nMolecular diagnostics \nForensic biology \nPrimarily concerned with health and \ndisease \nPrimarily concerned with legal investigations \nDetects disease-related molecules \nIdentifies or compares biological evidence \nUses PCR, ELISA and other methods \nUses DNA extraction, PCR and STR analysis \nSamples are usually clinical \nSamples may come from crime scenes or \nindividuals \n \n63. Genetic Testing \nGenetic testing examines DNA or chromosomes to identify genetic variations associated with \ninherited conditions or other biological characteristics. \nApplications include \n• \nDiagnosis  \n• \nCarrier testing  \n• \nPrenatal testing  \n• \nRisk assessment  \n• \nFamily relationship analysis  \n \n64. Prenatal Genetic Testing \nPrenatal genetic testing can identify certain genetic or chromosomal abnormalities before birth. \nMethods differ in their purpose and level of invasiveness. \nInterpretation requires appropriate medical and genetic counselling. \n \n65. Personalised Medicine \nPersonalised medicine uses information about an individual's genetic, environmental and \nlifestyle characteristics to guide prevention or treatment. \nGenetic information can sometimes influence \n• \nDrug selection\n\n• \nDrug dosage  \n• \nDisease risk assessment  \n• \nTreatment response  \n \n66. Pharmacogenomics \nPharmacogenomics studies how genetic differences influence an individual's response to \nmedicines. \nGenetic variation can affect \n• \nDrug metabolism  \n• \nDrug effectiveness  \n• \nRisk of adverse reactions  \n \n67. Molecular Cancer Diagnosis \nMolecular techniques can detect genetic and molecular changes associated with cancer. \nThese methods can help with \n• \nClassification of tumours  \n• \nPrognosis  \n• \nTreatment selection  \n• \nMonitoring disease  \n \n68. Biomarkers \nA biomarker is a measurable biological characteristic that can provide information about a \nphysiological or pathological condition. \nExamples include \n• \nProteins  \n• \nDNA mutations  \n• \nRNA molecules  \n• \nHormone levels\n\n69. Importance of Early Diagnosis \nEarly detection can allow disease to be identified before severe symptoms or complications \ndevelop. \nMolecular diagnostics can contribute to early diagnosis by detecting specific molecular markers. \n \n70. Ethical Considerations \nModern biological technologies raise important ethical questions. \nThese include \n• \nGenetic privacy  \n• \nInformed consent  \n• \nOwnership of genetic information  \n• \nDiscrimination  \n• \nUse of DNA databases  \n• \nEmbryo research  \n• \nStem cell research  \n• \nAccess to advanced treatments  \n \n71. Genetic Privacy \nGenetic information can reveal information about an individual and potentially their relatives. \nTherefore, genetic information should be handled with appropriate confidentiality and legal \nprotection. \n \n72. Informed Consent \nInformed consent means that an individual voluntarily agrees to a procedure after receiving \nsufficient information about its purpose, benefits, risks and implications. \nIt is particularly important in genetic testing and biological research.\n\n73. Key Terms \nPCR \nTechnique used to amplify specific DNA sequences. \nDenaturation \nSeparation of DNA strands during PCR. \nAnnealing \nBinding of primers to complementary DNA sequences. \nExtension \nDNA synthesis by DNA polymerase during PCR. \nELISA \nImmunological assay used to detect antigens or antibodies. \nMonoclonal antibody \nIdentical antibody molecules produced from a single B-cell clone. \nHybridoma \nCell formed by fusion of an antibody-producing B cell and an immortal myeloma cell. \nStem cell \nCell capable of self-renewal and differentiation. \nDNA profiling \nAnalysis of variable DNA regions to produce a genetic profile. \nSTR \nShort Tandem Repeat, a repeated DNA sequence used extensively in forensic profiling. \nForensic biology \nApplication of biological science to legal investigations. \nBiomarker \nMeasurable biological characteristic associated with a physiological or pathological state. \nPharmacogenomics \nStudy of how genetic variation affects responses to medicines.\n\nChain of custody \nDocumented history of the handling and transfer of evidence. \n \n74. Final Summary \nModern medicine increasingly relies on molecular biology and biotechnology. PCR allows \nspecific DNA sequences to be amplified, making it useful in disease diagnosis, genetic testing, \nresearch and forensic analysis. ELISA uses antigen-antibody interactions to detect specific \nbiological molecules and has applications in diagnosis and research. \nMonoclonal antibodies are produced using techniques such as hybridoma technology and are \nwidely used in diagnosis, research and treatment. Stem cells provide important opportunities for \nregenerative medicine, disease modelling and tissue research, although their clinical application \npresents biological, technical and ethical challenges. \nForensic biology uses biological evidence to support legal investigations. STR-based DNA \nprofiling is particularly important because variable repeat regions of the genome can produce \nhighly discriminating genetic profiles. PCR allows these regions to be amplified even from small \nbiological samples. \nThe development of these technologies has significantly increased the ability of scientists and \nmedical professionals to diagnose disease, investigate biological relationships, identify \nindividuals and develop targeted treatments. At the same time, responsible use requires attention \nto scientific validity, informed consent, privacy, ethics and appropriate legal safeguards.	COMPLETED	121	2026-08-19 18:12:18.708938	2026-08-19 18:12:51.883026	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
1002	Lesson 3: Biosafety, biopiracy, and ethical issues in biotechnology - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 3: Biosafety, biopiracy, and ethical issues in biotechnology	PDF	uploads/pdf/143e578c0c1d482a979141292dbf440d.pdf	\N	Lesson 3 — Biosafety, Biopiracy, and Ethical Issues in Biotechnology \nIntroduction \nModern biotechnology allows biological organisms, cells and genetic material to be used for \nmedicine, agriculture, industry and environmental management. Although biotechnology \nprovides many benefits, its application can also create biological, environmental, social and \nethical risks. \nBiosafety focuses on preventing or reducing risks associated with biological technologies. \nBiopiracy concerns the unauthorised use or commercial exploitation of biological resources and \nassociated traditional knowledge. Ethical considerations address whether biotechnology is being \ndeveloped and used responsibly and fairly. \n \n1. Biotechnology \nBiotechnology is the application of biological organisms, cells, cellular components or \nbiological processes to develop useful products and processes. \nApplications include \n• \nAgriculture  \n• \nMedicine  \n• \nFood production  \n• \nIndustrial production  \n• \nEnvironmental management  \n• \nGenetic engineering  \n• \nPharmaceutical production  \n \n2. Benefits of Biotechnology \nBiotechnology can contribute to \n• \nIncreased agricultural productivity  \n• \nImproved crop characteristics  \n• \nDisease diagnosis  \n• \nProduction of medicines\n\n• \nVaccine development  \n• \nIndustrial enzyme production  \n• \nEnvironmental remediation  \n• \nConservation of genetic resources  \n \n3. Biosafety \nBiosafety refers to measures designed to prevent or minimise risks arising from biological \nagents, organisms and biotechnology applications. \nThe objective is to protect \n• \nHuman health  \n• \nAnimal health  \n• \nPlant health  \n• \nBiodiversity  \n• \nThe environment  \n \n4. Biosecurity \nBiosecurity concerns measures used to prevent the intentional or accidental misuse, release or \nspread of biological agents. \nAlthough biosafety and biosecurity are related, they address different aspects of biological risk. \n \n5. Biological Risk \nBiological technologies can involve potential risks such as \n• \nAccidental release of organisms  \n• \nSpread of pathogens  \n• \nUnintended ecological effects  \n• \nGene transfer  \n• \nContamination  \n• \nHuman exposure\n\nRisk assessment is therefore an important part of biotechnology management. \n \n6. Risk Assessment \nRisk assessment is the systematic evaluation of potential hazards and the likelihood and \nconsequences of harmful outcomes. \nA simplified approach involves \nHazard identification → Risk assessment → Risk management → Monitoring \n \n7. Hazard Identification \nA hazard is something with the potential to cause harm. \nIn biotechnology, hazards may involve \n• \nPathogenic microorganisms  \n• \nGenetically modified organisms  \n• \nToxic substances  \n• \nAllergens  \n• \nEnvironmental effects  \n \n8. Risk Management \nRisk management involves implementing measures to reduce identified risks. \nMethods may include \n• \nContainment  \n• \nPersonal protective equipment  \n• \nSterilisation  \n• \nControlled laboratory procedures  \n• \nRestricted access  \n• \nWaste treatment  \n• \nEnvironmental monitoring\n\n9. Containment \nContainment refers to measures that prevent biological materials from escaping into unintended \nenvironments. \nContainment may involve \n• \nPhysical barriers  \n• \nSpecial laboratory facilities  \n• \nClosed equipment  \n• \nControlled access  \n• \nProper waste disposal  \n \n10. Laboratory Biosafety \nLaboratories handling biological materials must follow appropriate safety procedures. \nImportant practices include \n• \nCorrect use of protective equipment  \n• \nSafe handling of biological samples  \n• \nProper disinfection  \n• \nSafe disposal of biological waste  \n• \nAppropriate storage  \n• \nAccurate labelling  \n• \nEmergency procedures  \n \n11. Biosafety Levels \nLaboratories can be classified according to the level of biological containment required. \nThe commonly recognised levels are \n• \nBiosafety Level 1  \n• \nBiosafety Level 2\n\n• \nBiosafety Level 3  \n• \nBiosafety Level 4  \nHigher levels involve progressively stricter containment requirements. \n \n12. Biosafety Level 1 \nBiosafety Level 1 is generally suitable for biological agents that present minimal potential hazard \nto healthy adults under normal laboratory conditions. \nBasic microbiological practices and appropriate laboratory procedures are required. \n \n13. Biosafety Level 2 \nBiosafety Level 2 is used for organisms that can cause disease but generally pose moderate risks \nunder controlled conditions. \nAdditional precautions are required compared with Level 1. \n \n14. Biosafety Level 3 \nBiosafety Level 3 is used for agents that can cause serious disease, particularly through \ninhalation. \nSpecialised containment facilities and strict procedures are required. \n \n15. Biosafety Level 4 \nBiosafety Level 4 provides the highest level of laboratory containment. \nIt is used for certain highly dangerous agents associated with severe disease and limited \ntreatment or prevention options. \n \n16. Genetically Modified Organisms \nA genetically modified organism, or GMO, is an organism whose genetic material has been \naltered using biotechnology. \nGenetic modification can introduce, remove or modify particular genetic characteristics.\n\n17. Examples of Genetically Modified Crops \nGenetic modification has been used to develop crops with characteristics such as \n• \nInsect resistance  \n• \nHerbicide tolerance  \n• \nImproved nutritional properties  \n• \nDisease resistance  \n• \nEnvironmental stress tolerance  \n \n18. Benefits of GM Crops \nPotential benefits include \n• \nReduced crop losses  \n• \nIncreased productivity  \n• \nReduced use of certain pesticides  \n• \nImproved nutritional characteristics  \n• \nImproved tolerance to environmental conditions  \nThe benefits depend on the particular crop and genetic modification. \n \n19. Potential Environmental Concerns \nPossible environmental concerns include \n• \nGene flow to related plants  \n• \nEffects on non-target organisms  \n• \nDevelopment of resistant pests  \n• \nChanges in ecological interactions  \n• \nLoss of genetic diversity  \nThese risks must be assessed on a case-by-case basis. \n \n20. Gene Flow\n\nGene flow is the movement of genes between populations. \nIn genetically modified crops, genes may potentially move to related plants through processes \nsuch as pollen transfer. \nThe ecological significance depends on the crop, introduced trait and surrounding environment. \n \n21. Living Modified Organisms \nA Living Modified Organism, or LMO, is a living organism possessing a novel combination of \ngenetic material obtained through modern biotechnology. \nThe term is particularly important in international biosafety regulations. \n \n22. Cartagena Protocol on Biosafety \nThe Cartagena Protocol on Biosafety is an international agreement associated with the \nConvention on Biological Diversity. \nIt focuses on the safe handling, transport and use of LMOs that may have adverse effects on \nbiodiversity, while taking risks to human health into account. \n \n23. Advance Informed Agreement \nThe Cartagena Protocol includes procedures for certain transboundary movements of LMOs. \nThe Advance Informed Agreement procedure allows countries to receive information before \ncertain intentional introductions of LMOs into their environment. \n \n24. Precautionary Principle \nThe precautionary principle supports taking preventive action when there is a possibility of \nserious environmental harm, even when scientific certainty about the risk is incomplete. \nIt is particularly relevant when evaluating novel biological technologies. \n \n25. Biosafety and Sri Lanka \nSri Lanka has developed regulatory approaches concerning biotechnology and biosafety.\n\nThese aim to ensure that biotechnology applications are assessed and managed with \nconsideration of \n• \nHuman health  \n• \nEnvironmental safety  \n• \nBiodiversity  \n• \nSustainable development  \n \n26. Biopiracy \nBiopiracy refers broadly to the unauthorised or unfair appropriation or commercial exploitation \nof biological resources and associated traditional knowledge, particularly without appropriate \nrecognition or benefit sharing. \nIt can involve \n• \nPlants  \n• \nMicroorganisms  \n• \nGenetic resources  \n• \nTraditional medicinal knowledge  \n• \nIndigenous knowledge  \n \n27. Traditional Knowledge \nTraditional knowledge refers to knowledge, practices and innovations developed and \nmaintained by communities over generations. \nExamples can include knowledge about \n• \nMedicinal plants  \n• \nFood plants  \n• \nAgricultural practices  \n• \nNatural dyes  \n• \nTraditional treatments\n\n28. Bioprospecting \nBioprospecting involves systematic searching for biological resources and genetic or \nbiochemical properties that may have useful applications. \nIt can support pharmaceutical, agricultural and industrial research. \n \n29. Bioprospecting and Biopiracy \nBioprospecting itself is not necessarily unethical or illegal. \nThe distinction depends on whether biological resources and associated knowledge are accessed \nand used according to applicable laws, permissions and benefit-sharing arrangements. \n \n30. Example of Biopiracy \nSuppose a company obtains knowledge about a medicinal plant traditionally used by a local \ncommunity. \nIf the company develops a commercial product based on that knowledge without appropriate \nrecognition, consent or benefit sharing, concerns about biopiracy may arise. \n \n31. Impacts of Biopiracy \nBiopiracy can result in \n• \nLoss of community rights  \n• \nUnfair economic benefits  \n• \nExploitation of traditional knowledge  \n• \nLoss of control over biological resources  \n• \nInappropriate patent claims  \n• \nReduced incentives for conservation  \n \n32. Access and Benefit Sharing \nAccess and benefit sharing, or ABS, is a framework for ensuring that benefits arising from the \nuse of genetic resources are shared fairly and equitably. \nBenefits may include\n\n• \nMonetary payments  \n• \nTechnology transfer  \n• \nResearch collaboration  \n• \nTraining  \n• \nCapacity building  \n• \nSharing of scientific information  \n \n33. Nagoya Protocol \nThe Nagoya Protocol is a supplementary agreement to the Convention on Biological Diversity. \nIt establishes an international framework concerning access to genetic resources and fair and \nequitable sharing of benefits arising from their utilisation. \n \n34. Prior Informed Consent \nPrior informed consent, or PIC, means obtaining appropriate consent before accessing certain \ngenetic resources or associated traditional knowledge, according to applicable national laws and \nrequirements. \nIt helps ensure that resource providers are informed about proposed uses. \n \n35. Mutually Agreed Terms \nMutually agreed terms, or MAT, are agreed conditions governing the use of genetic resources \nand the sharing of benefits. \nThese terms can define \n• \nPermitted uses  \n• \nBenefit-sharing arrangements  \n• \nResearch responsibilities  \n• \nIntellectual property arrangements  \n \n36. Intellectual Property\n\nIntellectual property, or IP, refers to legally protected creations and innovations. \nIn biotechnology, intellectual property may involve \n• \nPatents  \n• \nPlant variety protection  \n• \nCopyright  \n• \nTrade secrets  \n• \nTrademarks  \n \n37. Patents \nA patent provides legal protection for an invention for a specified period, subject to applicable \nlegal requirements. \nBiotechnology-related patents can involve certain \n• \nProcesses  \n• \nProducts  \n• \nGenetic technologies  \n• \nPharmaceutical inventions  \nThe exact scope of patentability depends on national and international law. \n \n38. Traditional Knowledge and Patents \nA major concern is the possibility that traditional knowledge could be incorporated into an \ninvention without appropriate recognition of the community that developed the knowledge. \nDocumentation and legal frameworks can help protect traditional knowledge from inappropriate \nexploitation. \n \n39. Ethical Issues in Biotechnology \nEthics concerns principles used to evaluate whether biotechnology applications are responsible, \nfair and acceptable. \nMajor ethical issues include\n\n• \nHuman genetic modification  \n• \nAnimal experimentation  \n• \nGM organisms  \n• \nStem cell research  \n• \nGenetic privacy  \n• \nCloning  \n• \nOwnership of genetic information  \n• \nAccess to biotechnology  \n \n40. Human Genetic Modification \nGenetic technologies can potentially alter human DNA. \nThere is an important distinction between \nSomatic gene modification \nChanges are made in body cells and are generally not inherited by future generations. \nGermline modification \nChanges are made in reproductive cells or early embryos and may be inherited by future \ngenerations. \n \n41. Somatic Gene Therapy \nSomatic gene therapy aims to treat disease by modifying genetic material in a patient's somatic \ncells. \nThe genetic change is generally limited to the treated individual. \n \n42. Germline Genetic Modification \nGermline modification affects cells that contribute to future generations. \nBecause the changes may be inherited, it raises particularly significant ethical and safety \nquestions.\n\n43. Animal Biotechnology \nBiotechnology can be used to improve livestock production and conduct scientific research. \nEthical considerations include \n• \nAnimal welfare  \n• \nPain and suffering  \n• \nHousing conditions  \n• \nGenetic modification  \n• \nExperimental procedures  \n• \nNecessity of the research  \n \n44. The Three Rs \nThe Three Rs provide a framework for more responsible use of animals in research. \nReplacement \nUse alternative methods instead of animals whenever possible. \nReduction \nUse the minimum number of animals necessary to obtain scientifically valid results. \nRefinement \nModify procedures to minimise pain, suffering and distress. \n \n45. Genetic Privacy \nGenetic information can reveal information about an individual and potentially their relatives. \nPotential concerns include \n• \nUnauthorised access  \n• \nDiscrimination  \n• \nMisuse of genetic information  \n• \nUnwanted disclosure  \n• \nData security\n\n46. Genetic Discrimination \nGenetic discrimination occurs when individuals are treated unfairly because of genetic \ninformation. \nExamples could involve inappropriate decisions related to \n• \nEmployment  \n• \nInsurance  \n• \nSocial opportunities  \nAppropriate legal protections can reduce such risks. \n \n47. Human Cloning \nCloning refers to producing genetically very similar copies of biological material. \nDifferent forms include \n• \nGene cloning  \n• \nReproductive cloning  \n• \nTherapeutic or research-oriented cloning  \nThese applications have different scientific and ethical implications. \n \n48. Gene Cloning \nGene cloning involves producing many copies of a particular DNA sequence. \nIt is widely used in \n• \nResearch  \n• \nBiotechnology  \n• \nMedicine  \n• \nProduction of recombinant proteins  \nGene cloning should not be confused with cloning an entire organism.\n\n49. Reproductive Cloning \nReproductive cloning aims to produce a new organism genetically very similar to another \norganism. \nIt raises significant questions concerning \n• \nAnimal welfare  \n• \nGenetic diversity  \n• \nSafety  \n• \nHuman applications  \n• \nSocial consequences  \n \n50. Stem Cell Ethics \nStem cell research can raise ethical questions depending on the source of the cells. \nParticular debate has surrounded the use of embryonic stem cells because obtaining them \ntraditionally involves destruction of the early embryo from which the cells are derived. \n \n51. GMO Labelling \nConsumers may have ethical and practical concerns about genetically modified foods. \nLabelling policies can provide consumers with information about the characteristics and \nproduction methods of food products, depending on national regulations. \n \n52. Food Safety and GMOs \nGM foods should be assessed for potential risks such as \n• \nToxicity  \n• \nAllergenicity  \n• \nNutritional changes  \n• \nUnintended effects  \nSafety assessment should be based on scientific evidence and the characteristics of the particular \nmodification.\n\n53. Environmental Ethics \nBiotechnology can affect ecosystems. \nEthical environmental considerations include \n• \nProtection of biodiversity  \n• \nPrevention of unnecessary environmental damage  \n• \nResponsible resource use  \n• \nProtection of future generations  \n \n54. Equity and Access \nAdvanced biotechnology may be expensive. \nEthical questions therefore include \n• \nWho can access the technology?  \n• \nWho receives the benefits?  \n• \nWho bears the risks?  \n• \nAre benefits distributed fairly?  \nTechnological progress should be considered alongside social equity. \n \n55. Dual-Use Concerns \nSome biological technologies can have both beneficial and harmful applications. \nA technology developed for legitimate research may potentially be misused. \nTherefore, responsible biotechnology requires appropriate oversight, security and ethical review. \n \n56. Environmental Release \nBefore releasing a genetically modified organism into the environment, potential ecological \neffects should be assessed. \nImportant considerations include\n\n• \nPersistence  \n• \nReproduction  \n• \nGene flow  \n• \nEffects on non-target organisms  \n• \nInteraction with native species  \n• \nLong-term ecological effects  \n \n57. Containment Strategies \nContainment can reduce the probability of unintended environmental release. \nStrategies may include \n• \nPhysical containment  \n• \nBiological containment  \n• \nControlled field trials  \n• \nMonitoring  \n• \nRestricted movement  \n• \nAppropriate waste management  \n \n58. Ethical Decision-Making in Biotechnology \nResponsible decisions should consider \n• \nScientific evidence  \n• \nPotential benefits  \n• \nPotential risks  \n• \nEthical principles  \n• \nEnvironmental effects  \n• \nSocial consequences  \n• \nLegal requirements  \n• \nPublic interests\n\n59. Role of Regulation \nRegulatory systems help ensure that biotechnology is developed and applied responsibly. \nRegulation can address \n• \nResearch activities  \n• \nGM organisms  \n• \nEnvironmental release  \n• \nBiological materials  \n• \nGenetic data  \n• \nFood safety  \n• \nIntellectual property  \n \n60. Importance of Public Participation \nPublic participation can improve biotechnology governance. \nPeople affected by biotechnology should have opportunities to \n• \nAccess relevant information  \n• \nUnderstand potential benefits and risks  \n• \nExpress concerns  \n• \nParticipate in appropriate decision-making processes  \n \n61. Conservation and Biotechnology \nBiotechnology can contribute to conservation through \n• \nGenetic analysis  \n• \nDNA barcoding  \n• \nCryopreservation  \n• \nTissue culture  \n• \nAssisted reproduction\n\n• \nGenetic monitoring  \nHowever, biotechnology should complement rather than replace habitat conservation. \n \n62. Biotechnology and Sustainable Development \nResponsible biotechnology can contribute to sustainable development by \n• \nImproving agricultural productivity  \n• \nReducing resource use  \n• \nProducing medicines  \n• \nTreating environmental pollution  \n• \nConserving biological resources  \nSustainability requires consideration of long-term environmental and social effects. \n \n63. Key Terms \nBiosafety \nMeasures used to prevent or minimise risks associated with biological materials and \nbiotechnology. \nBiosecurity \nMeasures designed to prevent accidental or intentional misuse or harmful release of biological \nagents. \nGMO \nAn organism whose genetic material has been altered using biotechnology. \nLMO \nA living organism possessing a novel combination of genetic material produced through modern \nbiotechnology. \nBiopiracy \nUnauthorised or unfair exploitation of biological resources or associated traditional knowledge. \nBioprospecting \nSystematic investigation of biological resources for potentially useful properties.\n\nAccess and benefit sharing \nFair and equitable sharing of benefits arising from the utilisation of genetic resources. \nPIC \nPrior informed consent obtained before certain access to genetic resources or traditional \nknowledge. \nMAT \nMutually agreed terms governing access and benefit sharing. \nIntellectual property \nLegal rights protecting creations, inventions and other forms of intellectual output. \nSomatic gene therapy \nModification of genetic material in somatic cells to treat disease. \nGermline modification \nGenetic modification affecting reproductive cells or early developmental cells that may transmit \nchanges to future generations. \nPrecautionary principle \nPrinciple supporting preventive action when serious harm is possible despite scientific \nuncertainty. \n \n64. Final Summary \nBiosafety is essential for ensuring that biological technologies are developed and used without \ncausing unacceptable harm to humans, animals, plants or ecosystems. Risk assessment, \ncontainment, laboratory safety and regulatory oversight are important components of responsible \nbiotechnology. \nGenetically modified organisms and living modified organisms can provide significant benefits, \nbut potential environmental and health risks must be assessed individually. International \nframeworks such as the Cartagena Protocol provide mechanisms for addressing biosafety \nconcerns associated with living modified organisms. \nBiopiracy highlights the importance of protecting biological resources and traditional \nknowledge. The principles of access and benefit sharing aim to ensure that benefits derived from \ngenetic resources are distributed fairly. Prior informed consent and mutually agreed terms can \nhelp establish appropriate conditions for access and utilisation.\n\nBiotechnology also raises ethical questions involving human genetic modification, animal \nwelfare, stem cells, cloning, genetic privacy, environmental protection and equitable access. \nResponsible biotechnology requires a balance between scientific progress, potential benefits, risk \nmanagement, ethical principles, legal requirements and the protection of biodiversity.	COMPLETED	122	2026-08-19 18:12:20.317371	2026-08-19 18:12:51.883028	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
