950	Lesson 2: The nature and organisational patterns of the living world - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 2: The nature and organisational patterns of the living world	PDF	uploads/pdf/c244e16f8e5744a4bcce81bea4625627.pdf	\N	Lesson 2: The Nature and Organisational Patterns of the Living World \n1. Lesson Overview \nLiving organisms exhibit an extraordinary range of forms, structures and functions. A bacterium \nconsists of a single cell, while a human contains trillions of specialised cells organised into \ntissues, organs and organ systems. At an even larger scale, individual organisms interact to form \npopulations, communities and ecosystems. \nDespite this enormous diversity, living systems are organised according to a hierarchical pattern. \nThe study of biological organisation helps us understand how smaller components combine to \nform increasingly complex biological systems and how each level has properties that may not be \npresent at lower levels. \nThe major organisational sequence is: \nAtom → Molecule → Macromolecule → Organelle → Cell → Tissue → Organ → Organ \nsystem → Organism → Population → Community → Ecosystem → Biosphere \nNot every biological system contains every level. For example, a unicellular organism does not \nhave tissues, organs or organ systems. \n \n2. Learning Objectives \nBy the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n• \nExplain the hierarchical organisation of the living world.  \n• \nDescribe the major levels of biological organisation.  \n• \nDistinguish between microscopic and macroscopic levels of organisation.  \n• \nExplain the relationship between structure and function.  \n• \nDescribe the concept of emergent properties.  \n• \nDistinguish between an organism, population, community and ecosystem.  \n• \nExplain the concept of a biosphere.  \n• \nUnderstand why biological organisation is important when studying living systems.  \n \n3. Biological Organisation \nBiological organisation refers to the arrangement of biological structures and systems into \nincreasingly complex levels.\n\nEach level is built from components at the level below it. \nFor example: \nCells → Tissues → Organs → Organ systems → Organism \nIn the human body: \nMuscle cells → Muscle tissue → Heart → Circulatory system → Human \nAt ecological levels: \nOrganisms → Population → Community → Ecosystem → Biosphere \nThis hierarchical organisation allows biological systems to perform increasingly complex \nfunctions. \n \n4. Chemical Level \nThe chemical level represents the smallest level of organisation commonly considered when \nstudying biology. \nIt includes: \n• \nAtoms  \n• \nMolecules  \n• \nMacromolecules  \n4.1 Atoms \nAn atom is the smallest unit of an element that retains the chemical properties of that element. \nBiological organisms are composed of many chemical elements. \nMajor elements found in living organisms include: \n• \nCarbon  \n• \nHydrogen  \n• \nOxygen  \n• \nNitrogen  \n• \nPhosphorus  \n• \nSulfur  \nThese elements combine to form molecules required for life.\n\n4.2 Molecules \nA molecule consists of two or more atoms chemically bonded together. \nExamples include: \n• \nWater — H₂O  \n• \nOxygen — O₂  \n• \nCarbon dioxide — CO₂  \nMolecules may be relatively small or extremely complex. \n \n5. Macromolecules \nSome biological molecules are very large and complex and are known as macromolecules. \nThe four major groups of biological macromolecules are: \n1. Carbohydrates  \n2. Lipids  \n3. Proteins  \n4. Nucleic acids  \nThese molecules perform essential biological functions. \nMacromolecule Major functions \nCarbohydrates Energy source, energy storage, structural roles \nLipids \nEnergy storage, membranes, hormones and insulation \nProteins \nEnzymes, structure, transport, movement and regulation \nNucleic acids \nStorage and transmission of genetic information \nThe detailed chemical structures and functions of these molecules will be studied in Unit 2. \n \n6. Organelle Level \nAn organelle is a specialised structure within a cell that performs a particular function.\n\nExamples include: \n• \nNucleus  \n• \nMitochondria  \n• \nRibosomes  \n• \nEndoplasmic reticulum  \n• \nGolgi apparatus  \n• \nChloroplasts  \n• \nLysosomes  \nFor example, the mitochondrion is involved in aerobic respiration and ATP production. \nThe nucleus contains most of the genetic material in eukaryotic cells. \nOrganelles work together rather than functioning independently. \n \n7. Cellular Level \nThe cell is the basic structural and functional unit of life. \nAll cellular organisms consist of one or more cells. \nThere are two fundamental cellular organisational patterns: \nProkaryotic cells \nExamples: \n• \nBacteria  \n• \nArchaea  \nThey generally lack a membrane-bound nucleus. \nEukaryotic cells \nExamples: \n• \nAnimals  \n• \nPlants  \n• \nFungi  \n• \nProtists\n\nThey contain a membrane-bound nucleus and various membrane-bound organelles. \n \n8. Unicellular and Multicellular Organisation \nUnicellular organisms \nA unicellular organism consists of a single cell. \nThat cell performs all essential life functions, including: \n• \nNutrition  \n• \nRespiration  \n• \nGrowth  \n• \nExcretion  \n• \nReproduction  \n• \nResponse to stimuli  \nExamples include many bacteria and protozoans. \nThe single cell therefore functions as the complete organism. \n \nMulticellular organisms \nMulticellular organisms contain many cells. \nDifferent cells become specialised for particular functions. \nFor example, the human body contains: \n• \nRed blood cells  \n• \nNeurons  \n• \nMuscle cells  \n• \nEpithelial cells  \n• \nBone cells  \nThis specialisation allows multicellular organisms to perform complex functions efficiently. \n \n9. Tissue Level\n\nA tissue is a group of similar cells, or cells with a common function, organised to perform a \nparticular function. \nExamples in animals include: \n• \nEpithelial tissue  \n• \nConnective tissue  \n• \nMuscle tissue  \n• \nNervous tissue  \nExamples in plants include: \n• \nDermal tissue  \n• \nGround tissue  \n• \nVascular tissue  \nThe detailed structure and functions of tissues will be studied later in the course. \n \n10. Organ Level \nAn organ consists of different tissues organised together to perform one or more specific \nfunctions. \nExample: Human heart \nThe heart contains several types of tissue, including: \n• \nMuscle tissue  \n• \nConnective tissue  \n• \nNervous tissue  \n• \nEpithelial tissue  \nThese tissues work together to allow the heart to pump blood. \nExample: Leaf \nA leaf contains several tissues organised to perform functions including: \n• \nPhotosynthesis  \n• \nGas exchange  \n• \nRegulation of water loss\n\nThus, an organ is more complex than a tissue because it involves multiple tissues working \ntogether. \n \n11. Organ System Level \nAn organ system consists of several organs that work together to perform major physiological \nfunctions. \nFor example, the human digestive system includes: \n• \nMouth  \n• \nOesophagus  \n• \nStomach  \n• \nSmall intestine  \n• \nLarge intestine  \n• \nAssociated organs such as the liver and pancreas  \nTogether, these structures contribute to digestion and nutrient absorption. \nOther human organ systems include: \n• \nRespiratory system  \n• \nCirculatory system  \n• \nNervous system  \n• \nEndocrine system  \n• \nExcretory system  \n• \nReproductive system  \n• \nSkeletal system  \n• \nMuscular system  \n• \nImmune/lymphatic system  \n \n12. Organism Level \nAn organism is an individual living entity capable of carrying out the characteristics associated \nwith life.\n\nExamples include: \n• \nA bacterium  \n• \nA mushroom  \n• \nA mango tree  \n• \nA fish  \n• \nA human  \nIn a multicellular organism, multiple organ systems work together to maintain the organism's \nlife. \nFor example: \nRespiratory system + circulatory system + digestive system + excretory system → \nmaintenance of the human body \nThis demonstrates that organ systems are interdependent. \n \n13. Population Level \nA population is a group of individuals of the same species living in a particular geographical \narea at a particular time and capable of interacting and reproducing. \nFor example: \nAll wild Asian elephants living within a particular region can be considered an elephant \npopulation. \nImportant characteristics of populations include: \n• \nPopulation size  \n• \nPopulation density  \n• \nBirth rate  \n• \nDeath rate  \n• \nImmigration  \n• \nEmigration  \n• \nAge structure  \nPopulation biology is important for understanding changes in species numbers and conservation.\n\n14. Species \nA species is generally defined as a group of organisms capable of naturally interbreeding to \nproduce fertile offspring. \nFor example, members of the human species can reproduce with one another and generally \nproduce fertile offspring. \nHowever, the biological species concept has limitations because it cannot be applied \nstraightforwardly to: \n• \nAsexual organisms  \n• \nFossil organisms  \n• \nSome organisms that hybridise  \n• \nOrganisms for which reproductive compatibility cannot be directly tested  \nTherefore, scientists may use additional characteristics when identifying species. \n \n15. Community Level \nA community consists of populations of different species living and interacting within a \nparticular area. \nFor example, a forest community may contain populations of: \n• \nTrees  \n• \nBirds  \n• \nMammals  \n• \nInsects  \n• \nFungi  \n• \nBacteria  \n• \nOther microorganisms  \nThe important point is that a community contains multiple species. \nPopulation vs Community\n\nPopulation \nCommunity \nIndividuals of the same species Populations of different species \nExample: population of deer \nDeer + plants + insects + fungi + microorganisms \n \n16. Ecosystem Level \nAn ecosystem consists of a biological community together with the non-living components of its \nenvironment and the interactions between them. \nThe living components are called biotic components. \nExamples: \n• \nPlants  \n• \nAnimals  \n• \nFungi  \n• \nBacteria  \nThe non-living components are called abiotic components. \nExamples: \n• \nLight  \n• \nTemperature  \n• \nWater  \n• \nSoil  \n• \npH  \n• \nMinerals  \n• \nAir  \nTherefore: \nEcosystem = Biotic components + Abiotic components + Their interactions \n \n17. Example of an Ecosystem \nConsider a freshwater pond.\n\nBiotic components \n• \nAlgae  \n• \nAquatic plants  \n• \nFish  \n• \nInsects  \n• \nBacteria  \n• \nFungi  \nAbiotic components \n• \nWater  \n• \nSunlight  \n• \nTemperature  \n• \nDissolved oxygen  \n• \nMinerals  \n• \npH  \nThe organisms interact with each other and with the physical environment. \nFor example: \nSunlight → Photosynthesis → Plant biomass → Food for consumers → Energy transfer \nNutrients are also continuously recycled between organisms and the environment. \n \n18. Biosphere \nThe biosphere is the global ecosystem consisting of all regions of Earth where life exists. \nIt includes portions of: \n• \nThe atmosphere  \n• \nHydrosphere  \n• \nLithosphere  \nwhere living organisms are found. \nThe biosphere represents the highest level of biological organisation.\n\nIt includes all: \n• \nOrganisms  \n• \nPopulations  \n• \nCommunities  \n• \nEcosystems  \non Earth. \n \n19. Complete Hierarchy of Biological Organisation \nA useful hierarchy is: \nAtoms \n↓ \nMolecules \n↓ \nMacromolecules \n↓ \nOrganelles \n↓ \nCells \n↓ \nTissues \n↓ \nOrgans \n↓ \nOrgan systems \n↓ \nOrganisms \n↓\n\nPopulations \n↓ \nCommunities \n↓ \nEcosystems \n↓ \nBiosphere \nEach level depends on interactions between components at lower levels. \n \n20. Structure and Function \nOne of the most important principles in biology is: \nStructure and function are closely related. \nThe structure of a biological component allows it to perform its particular function. \nExample: Red blood cell \nRed blood cells have a biconcave shape. \nThis structure: \n• \nProvides a relatively large surface-area-to-volume ratio.  \n• \nAllows efficient gas exchange.  \n• \nAllows the cell to pass through narrow blood vessels.  \nTherefore, its structure is closely related to its function. \nExample: Neuron \nNeurons have long extensions that allow them to transmit electrical signals over considerable \ndistances. \nAgain: \nStructure → Function \nThis principle appears repeatedly throughout A/L Biology.\n\n21. Emergent Properties \nAn important feature of biological organisation is the presence of emergent properties. \nAn emergent property is a characteristic that appears at a particular level of organisation because \nof interactions between components at lower levels. \nThe individual components may not possess the same property independently. \nFor example: \nIndividual heart muscle cells can contract, but the organised interaction of many specialised cells \nand tissues allows the entire heart to function as a pump. \nSimilarly: \nIndividual organisms → interactions → population characteristics \nand \nDifferent species + interactions + abiotic environment → ecosystem properties \nEmergent properties therefore arise from interactions among components. \n \n22. Reductionism and Systems Thinking \nTwo useful approaches can be used when studying biological systems. \nReductionism \nReductionism involves studying a complex system by examining its smaller components. \nFor example, to understand muscle contraction, scientists may study: \n• \nMuscle tissue  \n• \nMuscle cells  \n• \nMyofibrils  \n• \nActin  \n• \nMyosin  \n• \nATP  \nThis approach allows detailed investigation of mechanisms. \n \nSystems thinking\n\nSystems thinking considers how different components interact to produce the behaviour of the \nwhole system. \nFor example, understanding human blood glucose regulation requires considering interactions \nbetween: \n• \nPancreas  \n• \nInsulin  \n• \nGlucagon  \n• \nLiver  \n• \nMuscle  \n• \nAdipose tissue  \n• \nBlood glucose concentration  \nBoth approaches are important in biology. \n \n23. Interdependence in Biological Systems \nBiological components do not function independently. \nAt the cellular level: \nOrganelles interact to maintain cell function. \nAt the organismal level: \nOrgan systems interact to maintain homeostasis. \nAt the ecological level: \nOrganisms interact with other organisms and with their physical environment. \nFor example, in humans: \nRespiratory system → obtains oxygen \nCirculatory system → transports oxygen \nCells → use oxygen during aerobic respiration \nExcretory system → removes metabolic waste \nThe functioning of one system therefore affects other systems.\n\n24. Biological Organisation and Energy Flow \nEnergy is required at virtually every level of biological organisation. \nAt the ecosystem level, energy usually enters through sunlight. \nA simplified pathway is: \nSunlight → Producers → Consumers → Decomposers \nEnergy is transformed and transferred through biological systems. \nUnlike matter, energy is not continuously recycled within ecosystems in the same way nutrients \nare. Much of the energy eventually dissipates as heat. \nThis concept will be studied in detail under Environmental Biology. \n \n25. Biological Organisation and Matter \nMatter is continuously rearranged within biological systems. \nFor example: \nCarbon dioxide → Photosynthesis → Organic compounds → Food chains → Respiration \n→ Carbon dioxide \nElements such as carbon, nitrogen and phosphorus move between organisms and the \nenvironment through biogeochemical cycles. \nThus, biological organisation is closely connected with both energy flow and matter cycling. \n \n26. Important Comparisons \nOrganism vs Population \nOrganism \nPopulation \nOne individual \nGroup of individuals \nUsually one species \nSame species \nExample: one elephant Elephant population in an area \nPopulation vs Community\n\nPopulation \nCommunity \nOne species \nMultiple species \nExample: all elephants in an area Elephants + trees + birds + insects \nCommunity vs Ecosystem \nCommunity \nEcosystem \nLiving organisms/populations \nLiving + non-living \ncomponents \nBiotic component only \nBiotic + abiotic components \nDoes not include physical environment as a defining \ncomponent \nIncludes physical environment \nEcosystem vs Biosphere \nEcosystem \nBiosphere \nA particular ecological system All ecosystems on Earth \nLimited geographical area \nGlobal scale \nExample: pond ecosystem \nEntire Earth where life exists \n \n27. Key Terms \nTerm \nDefinition \nAtom \nSmallest unit of an element retaining its chemical properties \nMolecule \nTwo or more atoms chemically bonded together \nMacromolecule \nLarge biological molecule such as a protein or nucleic acid \nOrganelle \nSpecialised structure within a cell \nCell \nBasic structural and functional unit of life \nTissue \nGroup of cells organised to perform a particular function \nOrgan \nStructure consisting of different tissues working together\n\nTerm \nDefinition \nOrgan system \nGroup of organs working together \nOrganism \nIndividual living entity \nPopulation \nIndividuals of the same species living in a particular area \nCommunity \nPopulations of different species living and interacting in an area \nEcosystem \nCommunity and its physical environment together with their interactions \nBiosphere \nGlobal ecosystem containing all regions of Earth where life exists \nBiotic \nRelating to living components \nAbiotic \nRelating to non-living environmental components \nEmergent property Property arising from interactions among components of a system \n \n28. Common Examination Mistakes \nMistake 1: Defining a community as all organisms in an ecosystem \nA community consists of the living populations of different species. \nAn ecosystem additionally includes the abiotic environment and interactions between biotic and \nabiotic components. \n \nMistake 2: Confusing population and species \nA species is a biological classification, whereas a population refers to members of a species \nliving in a particular area at a particular time. \nOne species may contain multiple populations. \n \nMistake 3: Saying that cells are the smallest possible biological structures \nAtoms and molecules are smaller, but the cell is the basic structural and functional unit of \nlife. \n \nMistake 4: Assuming every organism has tissues and organs\n\nUnicellular organisms perform all life functions within a single cell. \nTissues, organs and organ systems are characteristic of more complex multicellular organisms. \n \nMistake 5: Forgetting interactions in the definition of an ecosystem \nAn ecosystem is not simply a collection of organisms and environmental factors. \nIt involves the interactions between the biotic and abiotic components. \n \n29. A/L Examination Questions \nQuestion 1 \nArrange the following in increasing order of biological organisation: \n• \nEcosystem  \n• \nOrgan  \n• \nCell  \n• \nPopulation  \n• \nTissue  \n• \nOrgan system  \n• \nOrganism  \n• \nCommunity  \nAnswer: \nCell → Tissue → Organ → Organ system → Organism → Population → Community → \nEcosystem \n \nQuestion 2 \nDistinguish between: \na. Population and community \nb. Community and ecosystem \nc. Ecosystem and biosphere\n\nQuestion 3 \nExplain the statement: \n"Structure determines function in biological systems." \nUse two appropriate biological examples. \n \nQuestion 4 \nExplain what is meant by an emergent property. Give one biological example. \n \nQuestion 5 \nA freshwater lake contains fish, aquatic plants, algae, bacteria, dissolved minerals, water, \nsunlight and different temperature zones. \nIdentify: \na. Two biotic components \nb. Two abiotic components \nc. The ecological level represented by the lake \nd. Explain why the lake is considered an ecosystem rather than a community. \n \n30. Final Revision \nThe living world is organised hierarchically, from simple chemical components to increasingly \ncomplex biological systems. \nThe major sequence is: \nAtom → Molecule → Macromolecule → Organelle → Cell → Tissue → Organ → Organ \nsystem → Organism → Population → Community → Ecosystem → Biosphere \nThe cell is the basic structural and functional unit of life. \nA tissue consists of organised cells performing a common function. \nAn organ consists of different tissues working together. \nAn organ system consists of organs working together. \nAn organism is an individual living entity.\n\nA population consists of members of the same species in a particular area. \nA community consists of populations of different species. \nAn ecosystem consists of a biological community, its abiotic environment and their interactions. \nThe biosphere represents the global level and encompasses all ecosystems where life exists. \nTwo fundamental concepts should be remembered: \nStructure is closely related to function. \nInteractions between components can produce emergent properties. \nUnderstanding this organisational hierarchy is essential because almost every later topic in A/L \nBiology can be connected to one or more of these levels.	COMPLETED	70	2026-08-19 18:10:58.983665	2026-08-19 18:12:51.882808	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
951	Lesson 1: Elemental composition and properties of water  - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 1: Elemental composition and properties of water 	PDF	uploads/pdf/15e76cd3883345329c4b33727fb0874b.pdf	\N	Lesson 1: Elemental Composition and Properties of Water \n1. Lesson Overview \nAll living organisms are composed of matter. At the most fundamental chemical level, this matter \nconsists of atoms of different elements that combine to form molecules and compounds. \nAlthough more than 100 chemical elements are known, only a relatively small number are \nimportant components of living organisms. Certain elements occur in large quantities, while \nothers are required in very small amounts. \nAmong all substances found in living organisms, water is particularly important. Water is the \nmajor component of most cells and provides the medium in which many biological reactions \noccur. \nThe unusual chemical properties of water arise primarily from the polarity of its molecules and \ntheir ability to form hydrogen bonds. These properties make water essential for life. \n \n2. Learning Objectives \nBy the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n• \nDefine an element, atom, molecule and compound.  \n• \nIdentify the major elements present in living organisms.  \n• \nDistinguish between major bio-elements and trace elements.  \n• \nExplain why carbon is particularly important in biological molecules.  \n• \nDescribe the structure and polarity of a water molecule.  \n• \nExplain hydrogen bonding between water molecules.  \n• \nRelate the properties of water to its biological importance.  \n• \nExplain water's roles as a solvent, transport medium, reactant and temperature regulator.  \n• \nExplain cohesion, adhesion, surface tension and capillary action.  \n• \nExplain the importance of water's high specific heat capacity and high latent heat of \nvaporisation.  \n• \nExplain why ice is less dense than liquid water.  \n \n3. Matter and Chemical Elements\n\nAll living organisms are made of matter. \nMatter is anything that has: \n• \nMass  \n• \nVolume  \nMatter is composed of elements. \nAn element is a pure substance consisting of atoms that have the same number of protons. \nExamples include: \n• \nCarbon  \n• \nHydrogen  \n• \nOxygen  \n• \nNitrogen  \n• \nPhosphorus  \n• \nSulfur  \n• \nCalcium  \n• \nIron  \nElements are represented by chemical symbols. \nFor example: \nElement \nSymbol \nCarbon \nC \nHydrogen H \nOxygen \nO \nNitrogen \nN \nPhosphorus P \nSulfur \nS \nCalcium \nCa\n\nElement \nSymbol \nPotassium K \nSodium \nNa \nMagnesium Mg \nIron \nFe \nChlorine \nCl \n \n4. Atoms \nAn atom is the smallest unit of an element that retains the chemical properties of that element. \nAn atom consists mainly of three types of subatomic particles: \n• \nProtons  \n• \nNeutrons  \n• \nElectrons  \nProtons \nProtons have a positive electrical charge. \nThey are located in the nucleus. \nNeutrons \nNeutrons have no electrical charge. \nThey are also located in the nucleus. \nElectrons \nElectrons have a negative electrical charge. \nThey occupy regions around the nucleus. \nThe number of protons determines the identity of an element. \nFor example: \n• \nHydrogen has 1 proton.  \n• \nCarbon has 6 protons.\n\n• \nOxygen has 8 protons.  \n• \nNitrogen has 7 protons.  \n \n5. Bio-elements \nElements that are essential components of living organisms are commonly referred to as bio-\nelements. \nThe most abundant elements in living organisms are: \nCarbon, hydrogen, oxygen and nitrogen. \nOther important elements include: \n• \nPhosphorus  \n• \nSulfur  \n• \nCalcium  \n• \nPotassium  \n• \nSodium  \n• \nMagnesium  \n• \nChlorine  \n• \nIron  \nThe exact concentration of each element varies between organisms and tissues. \n \n6. Major and Trace Elements \nElements required by organisms can broadly be classified according to the quantity required. \nMajor elements \nThese are required in relatively large quantities. \nImportant examples include: \n• \nCarbon  \n• \nHydrogen  \n• \nOxygen\n\n• \nNitrogen  \n• \nPhosphorus  \n• \nSulfur  \n• \nCalcium  \n• \nPotassium  \n• \nSodium  \n• \nMagnesium  \n• \nChlorine  \nThese elements participate in structures and processes such as: \n• \nProteins  \n• \nNucleic acids  \n• \nCell membranes  \n• \nATP  \n• \nBones  \n• \nNerve impulses  \n• \nMuscle contraction  \n• \nOsmotic regulation  \n \nTrace elements \nTrace elements are required in very small quantities but can still be essential for normal \nbiological function. \nExamples include: \n• \nIron  \n• \nCopper  \n• \nZinc  \n• \nManganese  \n• \nIodine\n\n• \nFluorine  \n• \nMolybdenum  \nAn element being required in a very small quantity does not mean that it is unimportant. \nFor example, iron is required in relatively small amounts but is essential for haemoglobin \nformation. \n \n7. Importance of Major Bio-elements \nCarbon \nCarbon is one of the most important elements in biology. \nCarbon atoms can form stable covalent bonds with: \n• \nCarbon  \n• \nHydrogen  \n• \nOxygen  \n• \nNitrogen  \n• \nSulfur  \n• \nPhosphorus  \nCarbon can also form chains, branches and rings. \nThis enormous structural versatility allows carbon to form the framework of many biological \nmolecules. \nExamples include: \n• \nCarbohydrates  \n• \nLipids  \n• \nProteins  \n• \nNucleic acids  \nTherefore, carbon is the fundamental structural element of organic molecules. \n \nHydrogen \nHydrogen is present in:\n\n• \nWater  \n• \nCarbohydrates  \n• \nLipids  \n• \nProteins  \n• \nNucleic acids  \nHydrogen ions also play important roles in: \n• \nAcid-base balance  \n• \nATP production  \n• \nPhotosynthesis  \n• \nCellular respiration  \n \nOxygen \nOxygen is a major component of: \n• \nWater  \n• \nCarbohydrates  \n• \nLipids  \n• \nProteins  \n• \nNucleic acids  \nMolecular oxygen is also used in aerobic respiration to facilitate efficient energy production. \n \nNitrogen \nNitrogen is essential for: \n• \nAmino acids  \n• \nProteins  \n• \nNucleic acids  \n• \nATP  \n• \nSome biological pigments\n\nNitrogen is therefore essential for growth and reproduction. \n \nPhosphorus \nPhosphorus is an important component of: \n• \nATP  \n• \nDNA  \n• \nRNA  \n• \nPhospholipids  \n• \nBones and teeth  \nPhosphate groups are particularly important in energy transfer and nucleic acid structure. \n \nSulfur \nSulfur occurs in certain amino acids, particularly: \n• \nCysteine  \n• \nMethionine  \nSulfur-containing bonds can contribute to protein structure. \n \n8. Chemical Bonds \nAtoms combine with one another through chemical bonds. \nThree important types of chemical interactions in biology are: \n1. Covalent bonds  \n2. Ionic bonds  \n3. Hydrogen bonds  \n \n8.1 Covalent bonds \nA covalent bond forms when atoms share electrons. \nFor example, hydrogen and oxygen form covalent bonds within a water molecule.\n\nIn H₂O: \n• \nTwo hydrogen atoms are covalently bonded to one oxygen atom.  \nCovalent bonds are generally strong bonds. \n \n8.2 Ionic interactions \nAn ionic bond or electrostatic attraction occurs between oppositely charged ions. \nFor example: \nNa⁺ + Cl⁻ → NaCl \nSodium loses an electron and becomes positively charged. \nChlorine gains an electron and becomes negatively charged. \nThe oppositely charged ions attract each other. \n \n8.3 Hydrogen bonds \nA hydrogen bond is a relatively weak attraction between a partially positive hydrogen atom and \nan electronegative atom such as oxygen or nitrogen. \nHydrogen bonds are much weaker than covalent bonds individually. \nHowever, large numbers of hydrogen bonds can have major biological effects. \nHydrogen bonding is especially important in: \n• \nWater  \n• \nDNA  \n• \nProtein structure  \n \n9. Structure of Water \nA water molecule has the molecular formula: \nH₂O \nIt contains: \n• \nTwo hydrogen atoms\n\n• \nOne oxygen atom  \nThe oxygen atom is covalently bonded to both hydrogen atoms. \nHowever, the electrons are not shared equally. \nOxygen has a greater attraction for electrons than hydrogen. \nTherefore, the oxygen atom develops a partial negative charge, while the hydrogen atoms \ndevelop partial positive charges. \nThe symbols can be represented as: \nHδ⁺ — Oδ⁻ — Hδ⁺ \nThe water molecule is therefore polar. \n \n10. Polarity of Water \nA molecule is polar when it has an uneven distribution of electrical charge. \nWater is polar because: \n1. Oxygen is more electronegative than hydrogen.  \n2. The O–H bonds are polar.  \n3. The molecule has a bent shape.  \n4. The bond dipoles do not cancel each other.  \nTherefore, water has: \n• \nA slightly negative oxygen region  \n• \nSlightly positive hydrogen regions  \nThis polarity is responsible for many of water's important properties. \n \n11. Hydrogen Bonding Between Water Molecules \nThe partially positive hydrogen atom of one water molecule is attracted to the partially negative \noxygen atom of another water molecule. \nThis attraction is a hydrogen bond. \nA simplified representation is: \nHδ⁺ — Oδ⁻ ··· Hδ⁺ — Oδ⁻\n\nThe solid line represents a covalent bond. \nThe dotted line represents a hydrogen bond. \nEach water molecule can form hydrogen bonds with neighbouring water molecules. \nThis extensive hydrogen bonding produces many of water's unusual properties. \n \n12. Water as a Solvent \nWater is often called a universal solvent, although this expression should not be interpreted \nliterally. \nBecause water is polar, it can dissolve many: \n• \nIonic substances  \n• \nPolar molecules  \nFor example: \nNaCl → Na⁺ + Cl⁻ \nWater molecules surround the ions and help separate them. \nThe oxygen side of water is attracted to positive ions, while the hydrogen side is attracted to \nnegative ions. \n \n13. Hydrophilic and Hydrophobic Substances \nHydrophilic substances \n"Hydrophilic" means water-loving. \nHydrophilic substances interact readily with water. \nExamples include: \n• \nMany ions  \n• \nSugars  \n• \nSome proteins  \nThey are often polar or charged. \n \nHydrophobic substances\n\n"Hydrophobic" means water-fearing. \nHydrophobic substances do not interact favourably with water. \nExamples include: \n• \nOils  \n• \nMany non-polar lipids  \nThis distinction is extremely important in cell biology. \nFor example, phospholipids contain both: \n• \nHydrophilic regions  \n• \nHydrophobic regions  \nThis property allows them to form cell membranes. \n \n14. Water as a Medium for Metabolic Reactions \nMany biological reactions occur in aqueous environments. \nWater provides the medium in which: \n• \nReactants dissolve  \n• \nMolecules move  \n• \nEnzymes interact with substrates  \n• \nMetabolic reactions occur  \nWater can also directly participate in chemical reactions. \nFor example, hydrolysis reactions use water to break chemical bonds. \n \n15. Water as a Reactant \nWater participates directly in several biological processes. \nHydrolysis \nComplex molecules can be broken down by the addition of water. \nFor example: \nDisaccharide + H₂O → Monosaccharide + Monosaccharide\n\nHydrolysis is important during digestion. \n \nPhotosynthesis \nWater is also a reactant in photosynthesis. \nA simplified equation is: \n6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ \nThe oxygen released during photosynthesis originates from water molecules split during the \nlight-dependent reactions. \n \n16. High Specific Heat Capacity \nWater has a high specific heat capacity. \nSpecific heat capacity is the amount of heat energy required to raise the temperature of a unit \nmass of a substance by one degree Celsius. \nWater requires a relatively large amount of energy to increase its temperature. \nThis is largely due to hydrogen bonding between water molecules. \nBefore the molecules can increase their kinetic energy substantially, some supplied energy is \nused to disrupt hydrogen bonds. \n \n17. Biological Importance of High Specific Heat Capacity \nWater's high specific heat capacity helps: \nMaintain stable body temperatures \nWater in organisms can absorb considerable heat without rapidly changing temperature. \nStabilise aquatic environments \nLarge bodies of water experience relatively gradual temperature changes. \nThis reduces extreme temperature fluctuations for aquatic organisms. \nModerate environmental temperatures \nOceans and other bodies of water absorb and store large amounts of heat, influencing local and \nglobal climates.\n\n18. High Latent Heat of Vaporisation \nWater requires a large amount of energy to change from liquid to gas. \nThis is because substantial energy is required to overcome hydrogen bonding between water \nmolecules. \nThe energy required to convert a liquid into a gas without changing its temperature is associated \nwith the latent heat of vaporisation. \n \n19. Biological Importance of Evaporation \nWhen water evaporates from a surface, the highest-energy molecules escape first. \nThe remaining liquid therefore has a lower average kinetic energy and becomes cooler. \nThis is the basis of evaporative cooling. \nExamples include: \nSweating in humans \nSweat evaporates from the skin and removes heat from the body. \nTranspiration in plants \nWater evaporates from leaf surfaces, contributing to cooling of plant tissues. \n \n20. Cohesion \nCohesion is the attraction between molecules of the same substance. \nWater molecules are cohesive because of hydrogen bonding. \nWater therefore tends to remain connected as a continuous column. \nThis property is particularly important in plants. \nIn the xylem: \nWater molecules cohere to one another → continuous water column → enables \ntransmission of tension generated by transpiration \nThis contributes to the movement of water from roots towards leaves.\n\n21. Adhesion \nAdhesion is the attraction between molecules of different substances. \nWater can adhere to surfaces containing polar or charged molecules. \nFor example, water can adhere to the walls of narrow xylem vessels. \nTogether, cohesion and adhesion contribute to the movement of water through narrow tubes. \n \n22. Capillary Action \nCapillary action is the movement of a liquid through a narrow tube or space due to a \ncombination of adhesion, cohesion and surface tension. \nIn plants: \n• \nWater adheres to xylem walls.  \n• \nWater molecules cohere with one another.  \n• \nThese forces contribute to upward movement of water in narrow vessels.  \nCapillary action alone does not explain the entire upward movement of water in tall plants. The \ntranspiration pull and cohesion-tension mechanism are particularly important. \n \n23. Surface Tension \nWater has relatively high surface tension because water molecules at the surface are strongly \nattracted to neighbouring molecules through hydrogen bonding. \nSurface molecules experience a net inward attraction. \nAs a result, the water surface behaves somewhat like a stretched elastic membrane. \nThis allows some small organisms to interact with or move across the water surface. \n \n24. Density of Ice and Liquid Water \nWater behaves unusually when it freezes. \nMost substances become denser as they solidify. \nWater is different.\n\nWhen water freezes, hydrogen bonds hold water molecules in a relatively open crystalline \nstructure. \nThis structure occupies more space than an equivalent amount of liquid water. \nTherefore: \nIce is less dense than liquid water. \nAs a result, ice floats on liquid water. \n \n25. Biological Importance of Floating Ice \nFloating ice forms an insulating layer on the surface of lakes and other bodies of water. \nThis reduces heat loss from the water underneath. \nConsequently, water below the ice can remain liquid, allowing aquatic organisms to survive \nduring cold periods. \nThis property has major ecological importance. \n \n26. Water and Temperature Regulation \nWater's thermal properties are extremely important to organisms. \nThe major properties include: \n• \nHigh specific heat capacity  \n• \nHigh latent heat of vaporisation  \n• \nHigh thermal stability  \nTogether, these properties help organisms resist rapid changes in temperature. \nWater therefore acts as an effective thermal buffer. \n \n27. Water as a Transport Medium \nWater is the main component of many biological fluids. \nExamples include: \n• \nBlood plasma  \n• \nTissue fluid\n\n• \nLymph  \n• \nPlant cell sap  \nWater allows dissolved substances to be transported throughout organisms. \nIn humans, blood plasma transports: \n• \nNutrients  \n• \nHormones  \n• \nIons  \n• \nMetabolic waste  \n• \nDissolved gases  \nIn plants, water is transported through the xylem and contributes to the transport of mineral ions. \n \n28. Water and Waste Removal \nWater is important for removing metabolic waste products. \nFor example, in humans: \n• \nNitrogenous waste is excreted in urine.  \n• \nCarbon dioxide is transported in blood and eliminated through the lungs.  \n• \nSweat can remove small quantities of dissolved substances.  \nWater therefore contributes to maintaining chemical balance within organisms. \n \n29. Water and Turgor in Plants \nWater entering plant cells generates turgor pressure. \nThe cell membrane and cell wall allow plant cells to maintain a rigid structure when sufficiently \nhydrated. \nTurgor is important for: \n• \nSupporting herbaceous plants  \n• \nMaintaining leaf shape  \n• \nOpening and closing stomata\n\n• \nCell enlargement  \nLoss of water causes cells to lose turgor, which can lead to wilting. \n \n30. Water and Reproduction \nWater is involved in reproduction in many organisms. \nFor example: \n• \nSperm cells require aqueous environments for movement.  \n• \nMany aquatic organisms release gametes into water.  \n• \nWater is important for seed germination.  \n• \nIn some plants, water is essential for reproductive processes.  \nThe exact role of water varies among organisms. \n \n31. Summary of Major Properties of Water \nProperty \nChemical basis \nBiological importance \nPolarity \nUnequal electron distribution \nDissolves ions and polar \nsubstances \nHydrogen bonding \nAttraction between partial charges \nProduces many unique \nproperties \nCohesion \nHydrogen bonding between water \nmolecules \nMaintains continuous water \ncolumns \nAdhesion \nAttraction to other polar surfaces \nSupports capillary movement \nHigh specific heat \ncapacity \nHydrogen bonding \nTemperature regulation \nHigh latent heat of \nvaporisation \nStrong hydrogen bonding \nEvaporative cooling \nSurface tension \nCohesion at water surface \nSupports surface-dwelling \norganisms\n\nProperty \nChemical basis \nBiological importance \nIce less dense than water \nOpen hydrogen-bonded structure in \nice \nIce floats and insulates water \nGood solvent \nPolarity \nTransport and metabolic \nreactions \nReactivity \nParticipates in chemical reactions \nHydrolysis and photosynthesis \n \n32. Water and Life: The Overall Relationship \nThe importance of water can be understood through several major functions: \nWater \n→ Solvent \n→ Reaction medium \n→ Reactant \n→ Transport medium \n→ Temperature regulator \n→ Structural support \n→ Cooling agent \n→ Habitat \n→ Component of cells \nThis is why water is indispensable to almost every known form of life. \n \n33. Important A/L Distinctions \nCohesion vs Adhesion \nCohesion: attraction between water molecules. \nAdhesion: attraction between water and another substance. \n \nSpecific Heat Capacity vs Latent Heat of Vaporisation \nSpecific heat capacity: energy required to raise temperature. \nLatent heat of vaporisation: energy required to convert liquid into gas without a temperature \nchange.\n\nHydrophilic vs Hydrophobic \nHydrophilic: interacts readily with water. \nHydrophobic: does not interact favourably with water. \n \nPolar vs Non-polar \nPolar molecules: have an uneven distribution of electrical charge. \nNon-polar molecules: have a relatively even distribution of charge. \n \n34. Common Examination Mistakes \nMistake 1: Saying hydrogen bonds occur within a water molecule \nThe O–H bonds within a water molecule are covalent bonds. \nHydrogen bonds occur between neighbouring water molecules. \n \nMistake 2: Saying water has high specific heat because it is heavy \nThis is incorrect. \nWater's high specific heat capacity is primarily related to the extensive hydrogen bonding \nbetween water molecules. \n \nMistake 3: Saying ice sinks \nIce floats because its density is lower than that of liquid water. \n \nMistake 4: Saying cohesion means attraction between water and xylem walls \nThat is adhesion. \nCohesion is attraction between water molecules. \n \nMistake 5: Saying water dissolves everything\n\nWater is an excellent solvent for many ionic and polar substances, but many non-polar \nsubstances, such as oils, are poorly soluble in water. \n \nMistake 6: Saying transpiration occurs because of capillary action alone \nCapillary action contributes to water movement, but long-distance water transport in plants is \nprimarily explained by the cohesion-tension mechanism and transpiration pull. \n \n35. A/L Examination Questions \nQuestion 1 \nExplain why water is considered a polar molecule. \nYour answer should include: \n• \nElectronegativity  \n• \nUnequal electron distribution  \n• \nPartial charges  \n• \nMolecular shape  \n \nQuestion 2 \nExplain how hydrogen bonding gives water a high specific heat capacity. \n \nQuestion 3 \nDistinguish between cohesion and adhesion and explain how both contribute to water movement \nin plants. \n \nQuestion 4 \nExplain why ice floats on liquid water and describe the biological significance of this property. \n \nQuestion 5 \nExplain how water acts as:\n\na. A solvent \nb. A transport medium \nc. A temperature regulator \nd. A reactant \ne. A cooling agent \n \nQuestion 6 \nA plant is exposed to high environmental temperatures. \nExplain how the properties of water can help the plant regulate its temperature. \n \n36. Final Revision \nThe chemical composition of living organisms is based on a relatively small number of elements. \nThe major biological elements include carbon, hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen, phosphorus and \nsulfur, together with several other essential elements. \nCarbon is particularly important because it can form stable bonds with many elements and with \nother carbon atoms, allowing the formation of complex biological molecules. \nWater is a polar molecule consisting of two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen \natom. Because of its polarity, neighbouring water molecules form hydrogen bonds. \nThese hydrogen bonds are responsible for many important properties of water. \nThe most important properties to remember are: \n1. Polarity — makes water an effective solvent for many ionic and polar substances.  \n2. Cohesion — attraction between water molecules.  \n3. Adhesion — attraction between water and other substances.  \n4. High specific heat capacity — helps resist rapid temperature changes.  \n5. High latent heat of vaporisation — allows effective evaporative cooling.  \n6. High surface tension — results from strong cohesion at the water surface.  \n7. Ice is less dense than liquid water — allows ice to float and provide insulation.  \n8. Chemical reactivity — water participates in processes such as hydrolysis and \nphotosynthesis.  \n9. Transport function — water provides the medium for movement of substances.\n\n10. Structural function — water contributes to cell turgor and organismal stability.  \nThe central concept of this lesson is that the unique chemical structure of water produces \nproperties that make water exceptionally suitable for supporting life.	COMPLETED	71	2026-08-19 18:11:02.971958	2026-08-19 18:12:51.882842	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
952	Lesson 2: Chemical nature and functions of carbohydrates - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 2: Chemical nature and functions of carbohydrates	PDF	uploads/pdf/5b8b65b05d0d4177a978efe06f3df964.pdf	\N	Lesson 2 — Chemical Nature and Functions of Carbohydrates \n1. Lesson Overview \nCarbohydrates are one of the four major classes of biological macromolecules. They are \ncomposed primarily of carbon, hydrogen and oxygen and are particularly important as sources \nand stores of energy. \nCarbohydrates also have important structural and recognition functions. They occur in organisms \nranging from bacteria to plants and animals. \nThe simplest carbohydrates are monosaccharides. These can combine through condensation \nreactions to form disaccharides and polysaccharides. \nA clear understanding of carbohydrate structure is important because the biological function of a \ncarbohydrate is closely related to its molecular structure and the type of bonds between its \nconstituent sugars. \n \n2. Learning Objectives \nBy the end of this lesson, you should be able to \n• \nDefine carbohydrates.  \n• \nDescribe the general chemical composition of carbohydrates.  \n• \nExplain the classification of carbohydrates.  \n• \nDistinguish between monosaccharides, disaccharides and polysaccharides.  \n• \nDescribe the structures and functions of important monosaccharides.  \n• \nExplain glycosidic bond formation.  \n• \nDistinguish between condensation and hydrolysis reactions.  \n• \nCompare starch, glycogen and cellulose.  \n• \nExplain the biological importance of dietary fibre.  \n• \nExplain the reducing properties of some sugars.  \n• \nRelate carbohydrate structure to biological function.  \n \n3. What Are Carbohydrates \nCarbohydrates are organic compounds composed mainly of\n\n• \nCarbon  \n• \nHydrogen  \n• \nOxygen  \nMany carbohydrates approximately follow the general empirical formula \n(CH₂O)n \nHowever, this formula is not applicable to every carbohydrate, so it should not be treated as a \nuniversal structural formula. \nExamples of carbohydrates include \n• \nGlucose  \n• \nFructose  \n• \nGalactose  \n• \nSucrose  \n• \nLactose  \n• \nMaltose  \n• \nStarch  \n• \nGlycogen  \n• \nCellulose  \n \n4. Major Functions of Carbohydrates \nCarbohydrates have several important biological functions. \nEnergy source \nGlucose is an important respiratory substrate. \nCells can oxidise glucose during cellular respiration to release energy and produce ATP. \nEnergy storage \nPlants store excess carbohydrate mainly as starch. \nAnimals store carbohydrate mainly as glycogen. \nStructural role\n\nCellulose provides structural strength to plant cell walls. \nOther carbohydrates also contribute to structural materials in various organisms. \nCell recognition \nCarbohydrate-containing molecules on cell surfaces can participate in cell recognition and \ncommunication. \nComponents of biological molecules \nCarbohydrate derivatives occur in important molecules such as nucleotides and nucleic acids. \n \n5. Classification of Carbohydrates \nCarbohydrates can be classified according to the number of sugar units they contain. \nThe major categories are \n1. Monosaccharides  \n2. Disaccharides  \n3. Oligosaccharides  \n4. Polysaccharides  \nFor A/L Biology, the most important categories are monosaccharides, disaccharides and \npolysaccharides. \n \n6. Monosaccharides \nA monosaccharide is the simplest form of carbohydrate and cannot be hydrolysed into a smaller \ncarbohydrate. \nImportant examples include \n• \nGlucose  \n• \nFructose  \n• \nGalactose  \n• \nRibose  \n• \nDeoxyribose  \nMonosaccharides are generally soluble in water and many have a sweet taste.\n\n7. Glucose \nGlucose is one of the most important carbohydrates in biology. \nIts molecular formula is \nC₆H₁₂O₆ \nGlucose is a six-carbon sugar and is classified as a hexose. \nIt is an important substrate for cellular respiration. \nGlucose is also the main building unit of several important polysaccharides. \nExamples include \n• \nStarch  \n• \nGlycogen  \n• \nCellulose  \nAlthough all three contain glucose units, their structures and functions are different because the \nglucose units are connected differently. \n \n8. Fructose \nFructose also has the molecular formula \nC₆H₁₂O₆ \nTherefore, glucose and fructose have the same molecular formula but different molecular \nstructures. \nThey are therefore structural isomers. \nFructose occurs naturally in many fruits and is also a component of sucrose. \n \n9. Galactose \nGalactose also has the molecular formula \nC₆H₁₂O₆ \nIt is structurally related to glucose and is an important component of lactose. \nLactose consists of\n\nGlucose + Galactose \n \n10. Ribose \nRibose is a five-carbon sugar. \nIt has the molecular formula \nC₅H₁₀O₅ \nRibose is an important component of \n• \nRNA  \n• \nATP  \n• \nSeveral other biologically important nucleotides  \nThe presence of ribose in ATP is particularly important because ATP acts as a major energy-\ntransfer molecule in cells. \n \n11. Deoxyribose \nDeoxyribose is a five-carbon sugar found in DNA. \nIts molecular formula is \nC₅H₁₀O₄ \nIt differs from ribose by having one less oxygen atom. \nThis difference contributes to the structural distinction between DNA and RNA. \n \n12. Classification Based on Carbon Number \nMonosaccharides can also be classified according to the number of carbon atoms they contain. \nNumber of carbon atoms Name Example \n3 \nTriose Glyceraldehyde \n4 \nTetrose Erythrose \n5 \nPentose Ribose\n\nNumber of carbon atoms Name Example \n6 \nHexose Glucose \nFor A/L Biology, pentoses and hexoses are particularly important. \n \n13. Open-Chain and Ring Structures \nMonosaccharides such as glucose can exist in different structural forms. \nIn aqueous solutions, glucose predominantly exists in a ring structure rather than its open-chain \nform. \nThis occurs because functional groups within the same molecule react to form a cyclic structure. \nThe ring structure is particularly important when discussing \n• \nAlpha glucose  \n• \nBeta glucose  \n• \nStarch  \n• \nGlycogen  \n• \nCellulose  \n \n14. Alpha and Beta Glucose \nGlucose can form two important cyclic configurations \n• \nAlpha glucose  \n• \nBeta glucose  \nThe difference lies in the orientation of the hydroxyl group attached to the relevant carbon atom \nin the cyclic form. \nThis seemingly small structural difference has major biological consequences. \nFor example \nAlpha glucose → starch and glycogen \nBeta glucose → cellulose \nTherefore, small differences in molecular structure can produce major differences in biological \nfunction.\n\n15. Isomers \nIsomers are compounds that have the same molecular formula but different arrangements of \natoms. \nFor example \nGlucose and fructose \nBoth have the formula \nC₆H₁₂O₆ \nbut their structures differ. \nGlucose and fructose are therefore structural isomers. \nIsomerism is important because molecular structure determines the chemical properties and \nbiological functions of molecules. \n \n16. Disaccharides \nA disaccharide consists of two monosaccharide units joined together. \nThe monosaccharides are connected by a glycosidic bond. \nImportant disaccharides include \n• \nSucrose  \n• \nMaltose  \n• \nLactose  \n \n17. Formation of a Disaccharide \nTwo monosaccharides can combine through a condensation reaction. \nDuring the reaction \n• \nThe two monosaccharides join.  \n• \nA glycosidic bond forms.  \n• \nOne molecule of water is released.  \nFor example\n\nGlucose + Glucose → Maltose + H₂O \nThis process is called condensation because a small molecule, water, is removed during the \nformation of a larger molecule. \n \n18. Glycosidic Bonds \nA glycosidic bond is a covalent bond that joins carbohydrate units. \nThe specific arrangement of the bond depends on the monosaccharides involved and their \nconfiguration. \nExamples include \n• \nα-1,4 glycosidic bonds  \n• \nα-1,6 glycosidic bonds  \n• \nβ-1,4 glycosidic bonds  \nThese different linkages contribute to the different structures and properties of polysaccharides. \n \n19. Sucrose \nSucrose is commonly known as table sugar. \nIt consists of \nGlucose + Fructose \nSucrose is the major transport sugar in many plants. \nPlants produce carbohydrates during photosynthesis and commonly transport them from source \ntissues to other parts of the plant in the form of sucrose through the phloem. \n \n20. Maltose \nMaltose consists of \nGlucose + Glucose \nIt is commonly formed during the partial breakdown of starch. \nFor example, during starch digestion, enzymes break starch into smaller carbohydrate units \nincluding maltose.\n\nMaltose can subsequently be hydrolysed into glucose molecules. \n \n21. Lactose \nLactose is the major sugar found in milk. \nIt consists of \nGlucose + Galactose \nThe enzyme lactase hydrolyses lactose into glucose and galactose. \nSome individuals produce insufficient lactase after infancy and may experience difficulty \ndigesting lactose. \nThis condition is known as lactose intolerance. \n \n22. Comparison of Important Disaccharides \nDisaccharide Components \nImportant occurrence \nSucrose \nGlucose + Fructose Plants, table sugar \nMaltose \nGlucose + Glucose Starch digestion \nLactose \nGlucose + Galactose Milk \nThis table is highly relevant for examination revision. \n \n23. Polysaccharides \nA polysaccharide is a carbohydrate consisting of many monosaccharide units joined by \nglycosidic bonds. \nImportant polysaccharides include \n• \nStarch  \n• \nGlycogen  \n• \nCellulose  \nAlthough all three are polysaccharides composed largely of glucose units, they have very \ndifferent properties.\n\nThe differences arise mainly from \n• \nType of glucose  \n• \nType of glycosidic bond  \n• \nDegree of branching  \n• \nThree-dimensional arrangement  \n \n24. Starch \nStarch is the major storage polysaccharide in plants. \nIt is formed from glucose units. \nStarch has two main components \n• \nAmylose  \n• \nAmylopectin  \n \n25. Amylose \nAmylose is a relatively unbranched polymer of α-glucose. \nThe glucose molecules are primarily joined by \nα-1,4 glycosidic bonds \nThe chain tends to coil into a helical structure. \nThis compact structure is useful for storing large quantities of glucose without requiring \nexcessive cellular space. \n \n26. Amylopectin \nAmylopectin is a branched polymer of α-glucose. \nIt contains \nα-1,4 glycosidic bonds \nalong the main chains and \nα-1,6 glycosidic bonds\n\nat branch points. \nThe branching allows enzymes to access multiple ends of the molecule. \nThis facilitates relatively rapid release of glucose when required. \n \n27. Biological Importance of Starch \nStarch is an effective storage molecule because \n• \nIt is compact.  \n• \nIt is insoluble or only sparingly soluble in water.  \n• \nIt does not significantly affect the water potential of cells when stored as granules.  \n• \nIt can be hydrolysed to release glucose when required.  \nPlants therefore store excess glucose as starch rather than maintaining very high concentrations \nof free glucose. \n \n28. Glycogen \nGlycogen is the main storage polysaccharide in animals. \nIt is also found in many fungi. \nLike starch, glycogen consists of α-glucose units. \nHowever, glycogen is more highly branched than amylopectin. \nIt contains \n• \nα-1,4 glycosidic bonds  \n• \nα-1,6 glycosidic bonds  \n \n29. Biological Importance of Glycogen \nGlycogen is stored primarily in \n• \nLiver  \n• \nSkeletal muscles  \nLiver glycogen\n\nLiver glycogen helps maintain blood glucose concentration between meals. \nMuscle glycogen \nMuscle glycogen provides a local source of glucose for respiration during muscle activity. \nThe extensive branching of glycogen provides many terminal ends from which glucose can be \nreleased rapidly. \n \n30. Cellulose \nCellulose is a structural polysaccharide found mainly in plant cell walls. \nIt is composed of β-glucose units. \nThe glucose units are joined primarily through \nβ-1,4 glycosidic bonds \n \n31. Structure of Cellulose \nEach β-glucose molecule is oriented differently from the next. \nThis produces long, straight chains. \nMultiple cellulose chains form hydrogen bonds with neighbouring chains. \nThese interactions produce strong structures called microfibrils. \nMicrofibrils contribute greatly to the strength of plant cell walls. \n \n32. Why Humans Cannot Digest Cellulose \nHumans lack the enzyme required to hydrolyse the β-1,4 glycosidic bonds of cellulose \nefficiently. \nTherefore, cellulose passes through the digestive system largely undigested. \nHowever, cellulose is still important in the human diet as dietary fibre. \n \n33. Dietary Fibre \nDietary fibre consists largely of plant material that is resistant to digestion in the human small \nintestine.\n\nCellulose is an important component. \nDietary fibre contributes to \n• \nNormal bowel movement  \n• \nStool formation  \n• \nHealthy digestive function  \nAdequate fibre intake is associated with a healthy diet. \n \n34. Comparison of Starch, Glycogen and Cellulose \nFeature \nStarch \nGlycogen \nCellulose \nMain role \nEnergy storage \nEnergy storage \nStructural \nMain organisms \nPlants \nAnimals and \nfungi \nPlants \nMonomer \nα-glucose \nα-glucose \nβ-glucose \nMain bonds \nα-1,4 and α-1,6 \nα-1,4 and α-1,6 \nβ-1,4 \nBranching \nAmylose unbranched, amylopectin \nbranched \nHighly branched Unbranched \nMain location \nPlant storage tissues \nLiver and \nmuscles \nPlant cell \nwalls \nDigestibility by \nhumans \nYes \nYes \nNo \nThis comparison is particularly important for structured and essay questions. \n \n35. Reducing Sugars \nSome carbohydrates are classified as reducing sugars because they can reduce certain chemical \nreagents. \nImportant reducing sugars include \n• \nGlucose  \n• \nFructose\n\n• \nGalactose  \n• \nMaltose  \n• \nLactose  \nSucrose is generally considered a non-reducing sugar. \n \n36. Testing for Reducing Sugars \nA commonly used laboratory test involves Benedict's reagent. \nWhen a reducing sugar is heated with Benedict's reagent, the solution can change from blue \ntowards green, yellow, orange or red depending on the approximate concentration. \nA positive result indicates the presence of a reducing substance. \nThe test is therefore qualitative or semi-quantitative, depending on how it is performed. \n \n37. Test for Starch \nStarch can be tested using iodine solution. \nA positive starch test produces a characteristic blue-black colour. \nTherefore \nIodine test → starch → blue-black \nThis is an important practical biology test. \n \n38. Carbohydrates and Cellular Respiration \nGlucose is an important substrate for cellular respiration. \nA simplified equation for aerobic respiration is \nGlucose + Oxygen → Carbon dioxide + Water + Energy \nThe energy released through the controlled oxidation of glucose is used to produce ATP. \nThe detailed stages of cellular respiration will be studied later in Unit 2. \n \n39. Carbohydrates in Cell Recognition\n\nCarbohydrates can be attached to proteins and lipids on the surfaces of cells. \nThese form structures such as \n• \nGlycoproteins  \n• \nGlycolipids  \nThey can participate in \n• \nCell recognition  \n• \nCell adhesion  \n• \nImmune responses  \n• \nCellular communication  \nTherefore, carbohydrates are not limited to energy-related functions. \n \n40. Structure Determines Function \nOne of the most important concepts in carbohydrate biology is that small structural differences \ncan produce major functional differences. \nConsider starch and cellulose. \nBoth are polymers of glucose. \nHowever \nStarch → α-glucose → storage \nCellulose → β-glucose → structural support \nThe different configurations produce different bond arrangements and molecular structures. \nThis is an important example of the relationship between molecular structure and biological \nfunction. \n \n41. Condensation and Hydrolysis \nThese two processes should not be confused. \nCondensation \nSmaller molecules combine to form a larger molecule while releasing water. \nExample\n\nGlucose + Glucose → Maltose + H₂O \nHydrolysis \nA larger molecule is broken down by the addition of water. \nExample \nMaltose + H₂O → Glucose + Glucose \nA useful way to remember this is \nCondensation builds \nHydrolysis breaks \n \n42. Important Carbohydrate Relationships \nMonosaccharide \nOne sugar unit. \nExample \nGlucose \nDisaccharide \nTwo sugar units. \nExample \nMaltose \nPolysaccharide \nMany sugar units. \nExample \nStarch \nThe general relationship is \nMonosaccharides → Disaccharides → Polysaccharides \nthrough condensation reactions. \nThe reverse process occurs through hydrolysis.\n\n43. High-Yield Comparison \nCategory \nMonosaccharide \nDisaccharide \nPolysaccharide \nNumber of sugar \nunits \nOne \nTwo \nMany \nHydrolysis \nCannot produce smaller \ncarbohydrates \nProduces \nmonosaccharides \nProduces smaller \ncarbohydrate units \nExamples \nGlucose, fructose \nSucrose, maltose, \nlactose \nStarch, glycogen, \ncellulose \nGeneral role \nEnergy and building blocks Energy and transport Storage or structural \n \n44. Common Examination Mistakes \nMistake 1 \nSaying starch is a single molecule. \nStarch is a mixture of two major types of glucose polymer \nAmylose and amylopectin \n \nMistake 2 \nSaying glycogen is found only in the liver. \nGlycogen is also stored extensively in skeletal muscles. \n \nMistake 3 \nSaying cellulose is made from α-glucose. \nCellulose is composed of β-glucose units. \n \nMistake 4 \nSaying all carbohydrates are sweet. \nMany polysaccharides such as cellulose and starch are not sweet in the same way as simple \nsugars.\n\nMistake 5 \nSaying glucose and fructose are different elements. \nThey are different compounds with the same molecular formula. \nThey are structural isomers. \n \nMistake 6 \nConfusing sucrose and lactose. \nSucrose = glucose + fructose \nLactose = glucose + galactose \n \nMistake 7 \nConfusing condensation with hydrolysis. \nCondensation releases water. \nHydrolysis uses water. \n \n45. A/L Examination Questions \nQuestion 1 \nDefine a carbohydrate and classify carbohydrates according to the number of monosaccharide \nunits. \n \nQuestion 2 \nExplain how a disaccharide is formed from two monosaccharides. \nYour answer should include \n• \nCondensation  \n• \nWater removal  \n• \nGlycosidic bond formation\n\nQuestion 3 \nCompare starch, glycogen and cellulose with reference to \n• \nMonomer  \n• \nType of glycosidic bond  \n• \nBranching  \n• \nBiological function  \n• \nLocation  \n \nQuestion 4 \nExplain why cellulose is suitable as a structural component of plant cell walls. \n \nQuestion 5 \nExplain why glycogen is suitable for energy storage in animals. \n \nQuestion 6 \nA student is given three solutions containing glucose, starch and water. \nDescribe suitable tests that could be used to distinguish the substances. \n \nQuestion 7 \nExplain the relationship between the structures of α-glucose and β-glucose and the different \nproperties of starch and cellulose. \n \n46. Final Revision \nCarbohydrates are important biological molecules composed mainly of carbon, hydrogen and \noxygen. \nThey can be classified into \nMonosaccharides → Disaccharides → Polysaccharides\n\nImportant monosaccharides include \n• \nGlucose  \n• \nFructose  \n• \nGalactose  \n• \nRibose  \n• \nDeoxyribose  \nImportant disaccharides include \n• \nSucrose = glucose + fructose  \n• \nMaltose = glucose + glucose  \n• \nLactose = glucose + galactose  \nImportant polysaccharides include \n• \nStarch for energy storage in plants  \n• \nGlycogen for energy storage in animals and fungi  \n• \nCellulose for structural support in plant cell walls  \nMonosaccharides join through condensation reactions, forming glycosidic bonds and releasing \nwater. \nThe reverse process, in which water is used to break a glycosidic bond, is hydrolysis. \nThe most important structural comparison is \nStarch and glycogen → α-glucose \nCellulose → β-glucose \nStarch is primarily a storage carbohydrate in plants, glycogen is a highly branched storage \ncarbohydrate in animals, and cellulose forms strong structural fibres in plant cell walls. \nThe central principle to remember is that the arrangement of carbohydrate molecules \ndetermines their properties and biological functions.	COMPLETED	72	2026-08-19 18:11:05.45639	2026-08-19 18:12:51.882845	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
953	Lesson 3: Chemical nature and functions of lipids - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 3: Chemical nature and functions of lipids	PDF	uploads/pdf/f42a685a796d48bcae33b88ebe502461.pdf	\N	Lesson 3 — Chemical Nature and Functions of Lipids \n1. Lesson Overview \nLipids are a diverse group of organic compounds that are generally insoluble in water but soluble \nin non-polar organic solvents. \nUnlike carbohydrates, proteins and nucleic acids, lipids are not generally considered true \npolymers because they are not constructed from repeating monomer units in a regular manner. \nLipids are essential components of living organisms. They function as long-term energy stores, \nstructural components of cell membranes, signalling molecules, insulation and protective \nsubstances. \nThe major lipid groups studied in A/L Biology include \n• \nTriglycerides  \n• \nPhospholipids  \n• \nSteroids  \n• \nWaxes  \nUnderstanding the relationship between lipid structure and function is particularly important for \nunderstanding energy storage and the structure of biological membranes. \n \n2. Learning Objectives \nBy the end of this lesson, you should be able to \n• \nDefine lipids.  \n• \nExplain the general chemical properties of lipids.  \n• \nDescribe the structure of fatty acids.  \n• \nDistinguish between saturated and unsaturated fatty acids.  \n• \nExplain the formation of triglycerides.  \n• \nExplain the functions of triglycerides.  \n• \nDescribe the structure and function of phospholipids.  \n• \nExplain how phospholipids form biological membranes.  \n• \nDescribe the general structure of steroids.  \n• \nExplain important biological functions of steroids.\n\n• \nCompare triglycerides, phospholipids and steroids.  \n• \nRelate lipid structure to lipid function.  \n \n3. What Are Lipids \nLipids are a group of organic compounds that have limited solubility in water and are generally \nsoluble in non-polar solvents. \nCommon characteristics of many lipids include \n• \nHigh carbon and hydrogen content  \n• \nRelatively low oxygen content compared with carbohydrates  \n• \nHydrophobic or amphipathic properties  \n• \nHigh energy content  \nExamples include \n• \nFats  \n• \nOils  \n• \nPhospholipids  \n• \nSteroids  \n• \nWaxes  \nLipids have a wide range of structures, so they cannot be defined simply as molecules with one \nparticular chemical structure. \n \n4. Major Functions of Lipids \nLipids perform several important functions. \nEnergy storage \nTriglycerides are major long-term energy stores. \nMembrane structure \nPhospholipids are major structural components of cell membranes. \nInsulation \nStored fats provide thermal insulation in many animals.\n\nProtection \nFat deposits can protect internal organs from physical impacts. \nWaterproofing \nWaxes can form water-resistant coatings on plants and animals. \nHormonal signalling \nSome steroid lipids function as hormones. \nCell signalling \nCertain lipid-derived molecules participate in cellular communication. \n \n5. Fatty Acids \nA fatty acid consists of a long hydrocarbon chain with a terminal carboxyl group. \nA simplified structure is \nCH₃ — (CH₂)n — COOH \nThe hydrocarbon portion is largely non-polar, while the carboxyl group is polar. \nFatty acids therefore have both chemical regions with different properties. \nThe length and degree of saturation of the hydrocarbon chain influence the physical properties of \nlipids. \n \n6. Saturated Fatty Acids \nA saturated fatty acid contains no carbon-carbon double bonds in its hydrocarbon chain. \nThe carbon atoms are therefore bonded to the maximum possible number of hydrogen atoms. \nThe chain is relatively straight. \nExamples include \n• \nPalmitic acid  \n• \nStearic acid  \nSaturated fatty acids are commonly found in animal fats, although they also occur in some plant \noils.\n\n7. Unsaturated Fatty Acids \nAn unsaturated fatty acid contains one or more carbon-carbon double bonds. \nThey can be divided into \nMonounsaturated fatty acids \nContain one carbon-carbon double bond. \nPolyunsaturated fatty acids \nContain two or more carbon-carbon double bonds. \nExamples of foods containing significant amounts of unsaturated fats include many plant oils, \nnuts and seeds. \n \n8. Cis and Trans Unsaturation \nMost naturally occurring unsaturated fatty acids contain cis double bonds. \nA cis double bond introduces a bend or kink into the hydrocarbon chain. \nThis prevents the fatty acid molecules from packing together as closely as saturated fatty acids. \nTrans unsaturated fatty acids have a more linear structure. \nTrans fats can behave more like saturated fats in their physical properties. \nFor A/L Biology, the key concept is that double bonds, particularly cis double bonds, influence \nthe packing and physical state of lipids. \n \n9. Saturated vs Unsaturated Fatty Acids \nFeature \nSaturated fatty acid \nUnsaturated fatty acid \nC=C double bonds \nNone \nOne or more \nChain shape \nRelatively straight \nOften kinked \nPacking \nMore closely packed \nLess closely packed \nTypical physical state Often solid at room temperature Often liquid at room temperature \nCommon examples \nAnimal fats \nMany plant oils \nThis is a general comparison rather than an absolute rule.\n\n10. Triglycerides \nA triglyceride, also called a triacylglycerol, consists of \nOne glycerol + Three fatty acids \nGlycerol is a three-carbon alcohol containing three hydroxyl groups. \nEach fatty acid can react with one hydroxyl group of glycerol. \nThis produces a triglyceride and three molecules of water. \n \n11. Formation of a Triglyceride \nThe formation of a triglyceride occurs through a series of condensation reactions. \nGlycerol + 3 fatty acids → Triglyceride + 3H₂O \nThe bonds formed between glycerol and fatty acids are called ester bonds. \nTherefore, triglycerides contain three ester bonds. \n \n12. Ester Bonds \nAn ester bond forms between \n• \nThe hydroxyl group of an alcohol  \n• \nThe carboxyl group of a fatty acid  \nIn triglycerides, the alcohol is glycerol. \nThe formation of ester bonds involves condensation and the release of water. \nThis relationship is important to remember \nGlycerol + fatty acids → ester bonds → triglyceride \n \n13. Hydrolysis of Triglycerides \nThe reverse process is hydrolysis. \nWater is used to break ester bonds. \nTriglyceride + 3H₂O → Glycerol + 3 fatty acids\n\nDuring digestion, lipases catalyse the hydrolysis of triglycerides into smaller molecules that can \nbe absorbed. \n \n14. Why Triglycerides Are Good Energy Stores \nTriglycerides are highly effective long-term energy stores for several reasons. \nHigh energy yield \nThey contain many carbon-hydrogen bonds and release substantial energy when oxidised. \nLow water association \nBecause triglycerides are hydrophobic, they can be stored without attracting large quantities of \nwater. \nCompact storage \nLarge quantities of energy can therefore be stored in a relatively small mass. \nThis makes triglycerides particularly suitable for long-term energy storage. \n \n15. Triglycerides in Animals \nAnimals commonly store triglycerides in adipose tissue. \nAdipose tissue can function as \n• \nEnergy storage  \n• \nThermal insulation  \n• \nMechanical protection  \nDuring periods of energy demand, stored triglycerides can be hydrolysed and their components \nused in metabolism. \n \n16. Triglycerides in Plants \nPlants also store lipids. \nOil-rich seeds contain significant quantities of triglycerides. \nExamples include seeds of \n• \nSunflower\n\n• \nCoconut  \n• \nSesame  \n• \nGroundnut  \nThese stored lipids provide energy for the developing embryo during germination. \n \n17. Fats and Oils \nThe terms fat and oil are often used to describe triglycerides based on their physical state. \nFats \nUsually solid or semi-solid at room temperature. \nThey tend to contain relatively higher proportions of saturated fatty acids. \nOils \nUsually liquid at room temperature. \nThey often contain higher proportions of unsaturated fatty acids. \nAgain, this is a general pattern rather than an absolute distinction. \n \n18. Why Unsaturated Lipids Tend to Be Liquid \nCis double bonds introduce bends into fatty acid chains. \nThese bends prevent molecules from packing tightly. \nLess efficient packing means weaker intermolecular interactions between neighbouring \nmolecules. \nTherefore, many unsaturated lipids have lower melting points and remain liquid at room \ntemperature. \nThis is particularly common in plant oils. \n \n19. Phospholipids \nPhospholipids are essential structural components of cell membranes. \nA typical phospholipid consists of \n• \nGlycerol\n\n• \nTwo fatty acids  \n• \nA phosphate-containing group  \nUnlike triglycerides, phospholipids contain two fatty acid tails rather than three. \n \n20. Amphipathic Nature of Phospholipids \nPhospholipids are amphipathic molecules. \nThis means they contain both \n• \nA hydrophilic region  \n• \nA hydrophobic region  \nThe phosphate-containing head is generally hydrophilic. \nThe fatty acid tails are hydrophobic. \nTherefore \nHydrophilic head \n• \n \nHydrophobic tails \nThis property is fundamental to biological membrane structure. \n \n21. Phospholipid Bilayer \nWhen phospholipids are placed in an aqueous environment, they spontaneously arrange \nthemselves so that their hydrophilic heads interact with water while their hydrophobic tails avoid \nwater. \nIn a cell membrane, this produces a phospholipid bilayer. \nA simplified arrangement is \nWater \nHydrophilic heads \nHydrophobic tails \nHydrophobic tails \nHydrophilic heads\n\nWater \nThis arrangement creates a hydrophobic interior and hydrophilic surfaces. \n \n22. Importance of the Phospholipid Bilayer \nThe phospholipid bilayer provides the basic structural framework of the cell membrane. \nIt contributes to \n• \nSeparation of the cell interior from the external environment  \n• \nCompartmentalisation within cells  \n• \nSelective movement of substances  \n• \nCell signalling  \n• \nMembrane flexibility  \nProteins embedded within the bilayer perform many specialised functions. \nThe complete membrane structure is described by the fluid mosaic model, which will be \nexamined in greater detail during cell biology. \n \n23. Triglyceride vs Phospholipid \nFeature \nTriglyceride \nPhospholipid \nGlycerol \nOne \nOne \nFatty acids \nThree \nTwo \nPhosphate group Absent \nPresent \nMain property \nHydrophobic \nAmphipathic \nMajor function Energy storage \nMembrane structure \nMajor location Adipose tissue, seeds Cell membranes \n \n24. Steroids \nSteroids are a different class of lipid.\n\nUnlike triglycerides and phospholipids, steroids do not contain a glycerol backbone with fatty \nacid chains. \nThey possess a characteristic structure consisting of four fused carbon rings. \nThis ring-based structure gives steroids different physical and biological properties from \ntriglycerides. \n \n25. Important Steroids \nImportant biological steroids include \n• \nCholesterol  \n• \nTestosterone  \n• \nOestrogen  \n• \nProgesterone  \n• \nCortisol  \n• \nAldosterone  \nMany steroid hormones are derived from cholesterol. \n \n26. Cholesterol \nCholesterol is an important lipid found in animal cells. \nIt is a component of cell membranes and is also a precursor for several biologically important \nmolecules. \nFunctions of cholesterol include \n• \nContributing to membrane structure  \n• \nActing as a precursor for steroid hormones  \n• \nActing as a precursor for bile acids  \n• \nContributing to vitamin D synthesis  \nCholesterol is therefore not simply a harmful substance. It is essential for normal biological \nfunction, although abnormal cholesterol levels can be associated with disease risk.\n\n27. Cholesterol and Cell Membranes \nCholesterol is positioned between phospholipids in animal cell membranes. \nIt helps regulate membrane properties. \nCholesterol can \n• \nReduce excessive membrane fluidity at higher temperatures  \n• \nPrevent membranes from becoming excessively rigid at lower temperatures  \nIt therefore contributes to membrane stability and fluidity. \n \n28. Steroid Hormones \nSome steroids function as hormones. \nExamples include \n• \nTestosterone  \n• \nOestrogen  \n• \nProgesterone  \n• \nCortisol  \n• \nAldosterone  \nSteroid hormones are generally lipid-soluble. \nBecause of this, they can cross the phospholipid bilayer of target cells. \nThey commonly bind to intracellular receptors and influence gene expression. \nThe endocrine system and steroid hormone signalling will be studied in greater detail later. \n \n29. Waxes \nWaxes are another group of lipids. \nThey are generally formed from \nLong-chain fatty acids + Long-chain alcohols \nWaxes are highly hydrophobic. \nThey are therefore useful as protective and waterproofing substances.\n\n30. Waxes in Plants \nPlants can produce waxy substances on the surfaces of leaves and other organs. \nThe waxy layer is part of the cuticle. \nIt helps \n• \nReduce water loss  \n• \nProtect against environmental conditions  \n• \nReduce the entry of some pathogens  \nThe waxy cuticle is particularly important for plants living in dry environments. \n \n31. Waxes in Animals \nWaxes also occur in animals. \nExamples include waxes produced by certain glands and insects. \nTheir hydrophobic properties can provide \n• \nWater resistance  \n• \nProtection  \n• \nLubrication  \n \n32. Lipids and Waterproofing \nThe hydrophobic nature of many lipids makes them effective barriers against water. \nThis is important in \n• \nPlant cuticles  \n• \nAnimal skin  \n• \nFeathers  \n• \nInsect surfaces  \nWithout effective waterproofing, organisms could lose excessive amounts of water or become \nexcessively exposed to environmental moisture.\n\n33. Lipids as Thermal Insulators \nFat stored beneath the skin can reduce heat loss. \nThis is particularly important in animals living in cold environments. \nSubcutaneous fat acts as an insulating layer because lipid tissue conducts heat relatively poorly \ncompared with many body fluids. \nTherefore \nLipid storage → reduced heat loss → thermal insulation \n \n34. Lipids and Mechanical Protection \nFat deposits can also protect internal organs. \nFor example, adipose tissue around certain organs can provide cushioning against mechanical \nimpacts. \nTherefore, lipid storage has both metabolic and structural significance. \n \n35. Lipids and Buoyancy \nIn some aquatic animals, lipid-rich tissues can contribute to buoyancy. \nBecause the density of many lipid materials is lower than that of water, lipid stores can help \nreduce overall body density. \nThis is an additional example of how lipid properties can contribute to biological adaptation. \n \n36. Essential Fatty Acids \nSome fatty acids cannot be synthesised in sufficient quantities by the human body and therefore \nmust be obtained from the diet. \nThese are known as essential fatty acids. \nThey are important for \n• \nCell membrane structure  \n• \nNormal growth and development\n\n• \nCellular signalling  \n• \nSynthesis of certain lipid-derived molecules  \nExamples include certain omega-3 and omega-6 polyunsaturated fatty acids. \n \n37. Lipids and Energy Storage \nLipids are particularly suitable for long-term energy storage. \nA simplified comparison is \nCarbohydrates → rapid energy availability and shorter-term storage \nLipids → high-density, long-term energy storage \nThis does not mean carbohydrates cannot be stored for long periods or that lipids are never used \nrapidly. It describes their major physiological roles. \n \n38. Why Lipids Store More Energy \nLipids contain a high proportion of reduced carbon and hydrogen. \nDuring oxidation, these atoms can undergo reactions that release substantial amounts of energy. \nLipids therefore provide more energy per gram than carbohydrates. \nThis makes them efficient energy stores. \n \n39. Lipid Digestion \nDietary triglycerides are not directly absorbed efficiently in their original form. \nDuring digestion \n1. Large lipid droplets are dispersed through emulsification.  \n2. Bile salts help increase the surface area available for enzyme action.  \n3. Lipases hydrolyse triglycerides.  \n4. The products are absorbed by intestinal cells.  \n5. They can subsequently be reassembled and transported.  \nThe details of lipid digestion will be studied in the human digestive system lesson.\n\n40. Emulsification \nEmulsification is the physical breakdown of large fat droplets into smaller droplets. \nBile salts assist this process. \nEmulsification does not chemically digest triglycerides. \nInstead, it increases the surface area available for lipase action. \nThis distinction is important. \nEmulsification → physical process \nLipase action → chemical digestion \n \n41. Comparison of Major Lipid Types \nLipid \nBasic structure \nMajor function \nTriglyceride Glycerol + 3 fatty acids \nEnergy storage \nPhospholipid Glycerol + 2 fatty acids + phosphate \ngroup \nCell membrane structure \nSteroid \nFour fused carbon rings \nHormones, membrane components and \nprecursors \nWax \nLong-chain fatty acid + long-chain \nalcohol \nWaterproofing and protection \n \n42. Structure and Function Relationships \nSeveral important examples demonstrate how lipid structure determines function. \nTriglyceride \nThree fatty acid chains provide a highly reduced, energy-rich molecule suitable for storage. \nPhospholipid \nHydrophilic head + hydrophobic tails allows spontaneous bilayer formation. \nSteroid\n\nFour fused rings provide a rigid structure suitable for specific signalling and membrane \nfunctions. \nWax \nLong hydrophobic chains make waxes effective waterproofing materials. \nTherefore, lipid function cannot be understood separately from molecular structure. \n \n43. Important Definitions \nTerm \nDefinition \nLipid \nDiverse group of organic compounds generally insoluble in water \nFatty acid \nMolecule containing a hydrocarbon chain and terminal carboxyl group \nSaturated fatty acid Fatty acid with no carbon-carbon double bonds \nUnsaturated fatty \nacid \nFatty acid with one or more carbon-carbon double bonds \nTriglyceride \nLipid formed from glycerol and three fatty acids \nEster bond \nBond formed between an alcohol and a carboxylic acid \nPhospholipid \nAmphipathic lipid containing glycerol, two fatty acids and a phosphate-\ncontaining group \nAmphipathic \nHaving both hydrophilic and hydrophobic regions \nSteroid \nLipid characterised by four fused carbon rings \nCholesterol \nSterol important in animal membranes and as a precursor for several \nbiological molecules \nWax \nHydrophobic lipid formed from long-chain fatty acid and long-chain \nalcohol \n \n44. Common Examination Mistakes \nMistake 1 \nSaying all lipids are triglycerides.\n\nIncorrect. \nTriglycerides are only one major category of lipid. \n \nMistake 2 \nSaying phospholipids contain three fatty acids. \nThey generally contain two fatty acids attached to glycerol. \n \nMistake 3 \nSaying phospholipids are completely hydrophobic. \nIncorrect. \nPhospholipids are amphipathic, containing hydrophilic and hydrophobic regions. \n \nMistake 4 \nSaying emulsification chemically breaks down fats. \nEmulsification physically breaks large droplets into smaller droplets. \nLipase performs the chemical hydrolysis of triglycerides. \n \nMistake 5 \nSaying cholesterol is only harmful. \nCholesterol is essential for normal cell membranes and serves as a precursor for steroid \nhormones and other molecules. \n \nMistake 6 \nSaying all unsaturated fats are healthy and all saturated fats are unhealthy. \nThis is an oversimplification. Nutritional effects depend on the specific fatty acids, food sources, \nquantities and overall dietary pattern. \n \n45. A/L Examination Questions\n\nQuestion 1 \nDescribe the formation of a triglyceride from glycerol and fatty acids. \nYour answer should include \n• \nCondensation reaction  \n• \nNumber of fatty acid molecules  \n• \nEster bonds  \n• \nWater molecules released  \n \nQuestion 2 \nExplain why triglycerides are suitable as long-term energy storage molecules. \n \nQuestion 3 \nDescribe the structure of a phospholipid and explain how its structure allows it to form a cell \nmembrane. \n \nQuestion 4 \nDistinguish between saturated and unsaturated fatty acids. \n \nQuestion 5 \nCompare triglycerides, phospholipids and steroids according to their structures and biological \nfunctions. \n \nQuestion 6 \nExplain how the structure of cholesterol allows it to influence the properties of animal cell \nmembranes. \n \nQuestion 7 \nExplain the difference between emulsification and enzymatic digestion of lipids.\n\n46. Final Revision \nLipids are a diverse group of mainly hydrophobic organic compounds with important structural, \nmetabolic and regulatory functions. \nThe major lipid types to remember are \nTriglycerides \n• \nGlycerol + 3 fatty acids  \n• \nFormed through condensation  \n• \nContain ester bonds  \n• \nMajor long-term energy stores  \n• \nProvide insulation and protection  \nPhospholipids \n• \nGlycerol + 2 fatty acids + phosphate-containing group  \n• \nAmphipathic  \n• \nForm the basic structure of cell membranes  \nSteroids \n• \nFour fused carbon rings  \n• \nInclude cholesterol and several steroid hormones  \n• \nImportant in membranes and chemical signalling  \nWaxes \n• \nLong-chain fatty acid + long-chain alcohol  \n• \nStrongly hydrophobic  \n• \nImportant for waterproofing and protection  \nFatty acids may be saturated or unsaturated. Unsaturated fatty acids contain one or more \ncarbon-carbon double bonds, which can introduce bends into the chains and influence the \nphysical properties of fats and oils. \nThe most important structural principle in this lesson is \nLipid structure determines lipid function.\n\nThe hydrophobic nature of triglycerides makes them suitable for energy storage, while the \namphipathic structure of phospholipids makes them ideal for constructing biological membranes.	COMPLETED	73	2026-08-19 18:11:07.170362	2026-08-19 18:12:51.882848	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
954	Lesson 4: Structure, levels, and functions of proteins - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 4: Structure, levels, and functions of proteins	PDF	uploads/pdf/cf72b012c53044e2be3da1aea0b83919.pdf	\N	Lesson 4 — Structure, Levels, and Functions of Proteins \n1. Lesson Overview \nProteins are one of the most important groups of biological macromolecules. They are present in \nevery living cell and participate in almost every major biological process. \nProteins are composed of amino acids joined together by peptide bonds. The sequence of \namino acids determines how a protein folds into its three-dimensional structure, and this \nstructure determines its biological function. \nProteins perform a remarkably wide range of functions, including \n• \nEnzymatic catalysis  \n• \nStructural support  \n• \nTransport  \n• \nMovement  \n• \nDefence  \n• \nCell signalling  \n• \nRegulation  \n• \nStorage  \nA protein's structure can be described at four levels \nPrimary → Secondary → Tertiary → Quaternary \nUnderstanding these structural levels is essential for understanding how proteins function. \n \n2. Learning Objectives \nBy the end of this lesson, you should be able to \n• \nDefine proteins.  \n• \nDescribe the general structure of an amino acid.  \n• \nExplain the classification of amino acids.  \n• \nExplain how peptide bonds form.  \n• \nDistinguish between dipeptides, polypeptides and proteins.  \n• \nDescribe the four levels of protein organisation.\n\n• \nExplain the bonds and interactions responsible for protein structure.  \n• \nRelate protein structure to protein function.  \n• \nDistinguish between fibrous and globular proteins.  \n• \nExplain protein denaturation.  \n• \nDescribe important biological functions of proteins.  \n• \nExplain the effects of temperature and pH on protein structure.  \n \n3. What Are Proteins \nProteins are biological macromolecules composed of one or more polypeptide chains. \nTheir basic building blocks are amino acids. \nProteins contain mainly \n• \nCarbon  \n• \nHydrogen  \n• \nOxygen  \n• \nNitrogen  \nSome proteins also contain significant amounts of sulfur. \nProteins differ enormously in size, structure and function. \n \n4. Amino Acids \nAn amino acid is the basic structural unit of a protein. \nA typical amino acid contains a central carbon atom attached to \n• \nAn amino group  \n• \nA carboxyl group  \n• \nA hydrogen atom  \n• \nA variable side chain called the R group  \nA simplified structure is \nNH₂ — CH(R) — COOH\n\nThe R group differs between amino acids. \nThis difference gives individual amino acids their particular chemical properties. \n \n5. General Structure of an Amino Acid \nThe central carbon is often called the α-carbon. \nIt is attached to \nAmino group \nCarboxyl group \nHydrogen \nR group \nThe R group is the variable component. \nFor example, the R group may be \n• \nNon-polar  \n• \nPolar  \n• \nPositively charged  \n• \nNegatively charged  \nThese differences influence how a protein folds. \n \n6. Amino Acid Side Chains \nThe chemical properties of the R group strongly influence protein structure. \nNon-polar R groups \nThese tend to avoid water. \nThey are often located inside globular proteins. \nPolar R groups \nThese can interact with water and other polar groups. \nCharged R groups \nThese can participate in ionic interactions.\n\nSulfur-containing R groups \nSome amino acids contain sulfur. \nCysteine is particularly important because two cysteine residues can form a disulfide bond. \n \n7. Essential and Non-essential Amino Acids \nAmino acids can also be classified according to whether humans can synthesise them in \nsufficient quantities. \nEssential amino acids \nThese cannot be synthesised adequately by the body and must be obtained from the diet. \nNon-essential amino acids \nThese can be synthesised by the body. \nThe term "non-essential" does not mean that the amino acid is biologically unimportant. \nIt means that it does not normally have to be obtained directly from the diet. \n \n8. Amino Acids Joining Together \nAmino acids can join through condensation reactions. \nThe carboxyl group of one amino acid reacts with the amino group of another amino acid. \nA molecule of water is released. \nA covalent bond called a peptide bond is formed. \nSimplified reaction \nAmino acid + Amino acid → Dipeptide + H₂O \n \n9. Peptide Bonds \nA peptide bond is the covalent bond formed between amino acids. \nIt occurs between \n• \nThe carboxyl group of one amino acid  \n• \nThe amino group of another amino acid\n\nThe peptide bond can be represented as \n—CO—NH— \nPeptide bonds are strong covalent bonds and form the backbone of polypeptide chains. \n \n10. Dipeptides \nA molecule consisting of two amino acids joined by one peptide bond is called a dipeptide. \nThe reaction is \nAmino acid + Amino acid → Dipeptide + H₂O \nThe formation of the peptide bond is therefore a condensation reaction. \n \n11. Polypeptides \nA polypeptide is a long chain of amino acids joined by peptide bonds. \nThe number and sequence of amino acids can vary enormously. \nA polypeptide may contain dozens, hundreds or even thousands of amino acid residues. \nA functional protein may consist of \n• \nOne polypeptide chain  \n• \nSeveral polypeptide chains  \n \n12. Proteins and Polypeptides \nThe terms protein and polypeptide are related but are not always identical. \nA polypeptide is an amino acid chain. \nA protein is a functional biological molecule consisting of one or more folded polypeptide \nchains. \nTherefore, the final three-dimensional structure is crucial. \n \n13. Primary Structure \nThe primary structure of a protein is the specific sequence of amino acids in its polypeptide \nchain.\n\nFor example \nAla — Gly — Val — Ser — Leu — Lys \nThe order of amino acids is extremely important. \nEven a single amino acid substitution can alter protein structure and function. \n \n14. Importance of Primary Structure \nThe primary structure determines how the polypeptide can fold. \nThe sequence determines the locations of different R groups. \nThese R groups subsequently interact with one another and with the surrounding environment. \nTherefore \nAmino acid sequence → folding → three-dimensional structure → function \nThis is one of the most important principles in protein biology. \n \n15. Secondary Structure \nThe secondary structure refers to local folding patterns in a polypeptide backbone. \nThe two major types are \n• \nα-helix  \n• \nβ-pleated sheet  \nThese structures are stabilised mainly by hydrogen bonds between atoms in the peptide \nbackbone. \n \n16. Alpha Helix \nAn α-helix is a coiled structure. \nThe polypeptide backbone forms a regular spiral. \nHydrogen bonds form between atoms in different parts of the peptide backbone and help stabilise \nthe helix. \nThe R groups project outward from the backbone.\n\n17. Beta-Pleated Sheet \nA β-pleated sheet consists of extended regions of a polypeptide chain arranged alongside one \nanother. \nHydrogen bonds between peptide backbone groups stabilise the structure. \nThe arrangement produces a folded or pleated appearance. \nβ-sheets may contribute significantly to the structure of many fibrous and globular proteins. \n \n18. Tertiary Structure \nThe tertiary structure is the overall three-dimensional shape of a single polypeptide chain. \nIt results from interactions between the R groups of amino acids. \nImportant interactions include \n• \nHydrogen bonds  \n• \nIonic interactions  \n• \nHydrophobic interactions  \n• \nDisulfide bonds  \nThese interactions cause the polypeptide to fold into a specific three-dimensional shape. \n \n19. Hydrophobic Interactions \nNon-polar R groups tend to avoid contact with water. \nIn many globular proteins, hydrophobic side chains become buried within the interior of the \nprotein. \nPolar and charged groups are more commonly exposed to the aqueous environment. \nThese interactions contribute significantly to protein folding. \n \n20. Ionic Interactions \nCharged R groups can attract one another. \nFor example \nPositive R group ↔ Negative R group\n\nThese electrostatic attractions can help stabilise tertiary structure. \nChanges in pH can alter the charges on some amino acid side chains and therefore disrupt these \ninteractions. \n \n21. Hydrogen Bonds in Proteins \nHydrogen bonds can form between suitable polar groups. \nThey contribute to \n• \nSecondary structure  \n• \nTertiary structure  \n• \nInteractions between protein subunits  \nAlthough an individual hydrogen bond is relatively weak, many hydrogen bonds together can \nmake an important contribution to protein stability. \n \n22. Disulfide Bonds \nSome amino acids contain sulfur. \nThe amino acid cysteine contains a sulfur-containing side chain. \nTwo cysteine residues can form a covalent disulfide bond. \nThis can be represented as \nCys — S — S — Cys \nDisulfide bonds can strongly stabilise the tertiary or quaternary structure of proteins. \n \n23. Quaternary Structure \nThe quaternary structure occurs when a protein consists of two or more polypeptide subunits \nthat associate to form a functional protein. \nEach polypeptide is sometimes called a subunit. \nThe subunits are held together by interactions such as \n• \nHydrogen bonds  \n• \nIonic interactions\n\n• \nHydrophobic interactions  \n• \nSometimes disulfide bonds  \nNot all proteins have quaternary structure. \n \n24. Example of Quaternary Structure \nHaemoglobin is a classic example. \nHuman haemoglobin consists of four polypeptide subunits. \nIt contains \n• \nTwo α subunits  \n• \nTwo β subunits  \nEach subunit contains a haem group capable of binding oxygen. \nThe interaction between the four subunits produces the functional haemoglobin molecule. \n \n25. Four Levels of Protein Structure \nLevel \nMain feature \nImportant interactions \nPrimary \nAmino acid sequence \nPeptide bonds \nSecondary α-helix and β-sheet \nHydrogen bonds \nTertiary \nOverall 3D shape of one \npolypeptide \nHydrogen, ionic, hydrophobic interactions and \ndisulfide bonds \nQuaternary Association of multiple \npolypeptides \nVarious interactions \nThis table should be memorised accurately. \n \n26. Relationship Between Protein Structure and Function \nProtein function depends heavily on three-dimensional structure. \nA protein's structure determines \n• \nBinding sites\n\n• \nActive sites  \n• \nMolecular interactions  \n• \nStability  \n• \nSpecificity  \nFor example, an enzyme must have an appropriate three-dimensional active site to bind its \nsubstrate. \nIf the structure changes significantly, the enzyme may lose its function. \n \n27. Fibrous Proteins \nFibrous proteins generally have elongated structures. \nThey are often relatively insoluble in water and provide structural support. \nExamples include \n• \nCollagen  \n• \nKeratin  \n \n28. Collagen \nCollagen is an important structural protein in animals. \nIt occurs in tissues such as \n• \nTendons  \n• \nLigaments  \n• \nSkin  \n• \nBone  \n• \nCartilage  \nCollagen fibres provide tensile strength. \nIts structural organisation makes it particularly suitable for resisting stretching forces. \n \n29. Keratin\n\nKeratin is another structural protein. \nIt occurs in structures such as \n• \nHair  \n• \nNails  \n• \nOuter layers of skin  \nKeratin provides strength and protection. \nDisulfide bonds contribute to the stability of keratin. \n \n30. Globular Proteins \nGlobular proteins generally have compact, roughly spherical structures. \nMany are soluble in water. \nExamples include \n• \nEnzymes  \n• \nHaemoglobin  \n• \nAntibodies  \n• \nSome hormones  \n• \nMany transport proteins  \nGlobular proteins are often involved in dynamic biological processes. \n \n31. Fibrous vs Globular Proteins \nFeature \nFibrous proteins \nGlobular proteins \nShape \nLong and elongated Compact \nSolubility Often low \nOften relatively high \nMajor role Structural \nFunctional and metabolic \nExamples Collagen, keratin \nEnzymes, haemoglobin, antibodies\n\n32. Enzymes \nMany enzymes are proteins. \nEnzymes are biological catalysts that increase the rate of chemical reactions without being \npermanently consumed. \nTheir catalytic activity depends on their specific three-dimensional structure. \nThe region where the substrate binds is called the active site. \nThe structure of the active site determines enzyme specificity. \nEnzymes will be studied in greater detail in the lesson on energy relationships and enzymes. \n \n33. Structural Proteins \nProteins can provide structural support. \nExamples include \nCollagen \nProvides strength in connective tissues. \nKeratin \nProvides strength and protection in hair, nails and skin. \nProteins can therefore form major components of tissues and organs. \n \n34. Transport Proteins \nProteins can transport substances. \nHaemoglobin \nTransports oxygen in blood. \nMembrane transport proteins \nMove ions and molecules across cell membranes. \nExamples include \n• \nChannels  \n• \nCarriers\n\n• \nPumps  \nTheir structures allow them to recognise and transport specific substances. \n \n35. Defence Proteins \nAntibodies are proteins produced by the immune system. \nThey recognise specific foreign molecules called antigens. \nAntibodies participate in adaptive immunity by helping the immune system identify and respond \nto specific targets. \n \n36. Contractile Proteins \nMuscle contraction depends on proteins. \nThe major proteins involved include \n• \nActin  \n• \nMyosin  \nTheir interaction produces the force required for muscle contraction. \nThis will be studied in detail in the animal support and movement lesson. \n \n37. Hormonal and Signalling Proteins \nSome hormones are proteins or peptides. \nExamples include \n• \nInsulin  \n• \nGrowth hormone  \nThese molecules interact with specific receptors and regulate physiological processes. \nSome receptors themselves are proteins embedded in cell membranes. \n \n38. Storage Proteins \nSome proteins function in storage.\n\nExamples include proteins that store nutrients for developing embryos. \nCertain proteins also bind and store ions. \nTherefore, protein functions extend beyond structural and enzymatic roles. \n \n39. Protein Denaturation \nDenaturation is the disruption of the natural three-dimensional structure of a protein, resulting \nin loss or alteration of biological activity. \nDenaturation can be caused by factors such as \n• \nHigh temperature  \n• \nExtreme pH  \n• \nCertain chemicals  \n• \nHigh concentrations of some salts  \n• \nOrganic solvents  \nDenaturation generally disrupts secondary, tertiary and/or quaternary structure. \nThe primary structure may remain intact if peptide bonds are not broken. \n \n40. Effect of High Temperature \nIncreasing temperature increases molecular movement. \nAt sufficiently high temperatures, interactions maintaining protein structure can be disrupted. \nAs the protein loses its specific three-dimensional shape, its function may decrease or disappear. \nFor enzymes, this loss of functional shape results in reduced catalytic activity. \n \n41. Effect of pH \nChanges in pH alter the concentration of hydrogen ions. \nThis can change the charges of amino acid side chains. \nAs a result \n• \nIonic interactions can be disrupted.\n\n• \nHydrogen bonding can be affected.  \n• \nProtein structure can change.  \nExtreme pH can therefore cause denaturation. \n \n42. Reversible and Irreversible Changes \nSome protein structural changes can be reversed if the conditions return to normal. \nThis is sometimes called renaturation. \nHowever, severe denaturation can be irreversible. \nFor example, heating egg white causes proteins to unfold and aggregate. The original structure \ngenerally cannot be restored simply by cooling. \n \n43. Protein Coagulation \nWhen denatured proteins aggregate and form an insoluble mass, the process is often described as \ncoagulation. \nA familiar example is the heating of egg white. \nHeat causes the proteins to denature and then aggregate, changing the egg white from a relatively \ntransparent liquid to an opaque solid. \n \n44. Protein Structure and Temperature \nProtein stability depends on the interactions maintaining its structure. \nAt low or moderate temperatures, these interactions generally remain stable. \nAs temperature rises beyond the protein's optimal range \nIncreased molecular movement → disruption of interactions → altered structure → \nreduced function \nThis relationship is particularly important for enzymes. \n \n45. Protein Structure and pH \nDifferent proteins have different optimal pH ranges.\n\nFor example, digestive enzymes operate under different conditions depending on where they \nfunction. \nChanges in pH can alter \n• \nIonic interactions  \n• \nProtein folding  \n• \nActive-site shape  \nTherefore, pH is an important factor controlling protein structure and function. \n \n46. Protein Synthesis \nCells construct proteins by linking amino acids according to information encoded in genes. \nThe general sequence is \nDNA → RNA → Polypeptide → Functional protein \nThe process involves \n• \nTranscription  \n• \nTranslation  \n• \nProtein folding  \nThese processes will be studied in detail in the genetics unit. \n \n47. Protein Folding \nAfter a polypeptide is synthesised, it folds into a specific three-dimensional structure. \nThe folding process is influenced by \n• \nAmino acid sequence  \n• \nHydrophobic interactions  \n• \nHydrogen bonds  \n• \nIonic interactions  \n• \nDisulfide bonds  \n• \nThe surrounding environment\n\nCorrect folding is essential for biological activity. \n \n48. Protein Misfolding \nIf a protein folds incorrectly, it may lose its normal function. \nMisfolded proteins can sometimes aggregate and interfere with cellular processes. \nProtein folding is therefore an important part of maintaining normal cell function. \n \n49. Key Structure-Function Examples \nHaemoglobin \nStructure → four subunits + haem groups \nFunction → oxygen transport \nCollagen \nStructure → strong fibrous organisation \nFunction → tensile strength \nKeratin \nStructure → strong protein fibres with disulfide bonds \nFunction → protection and structural strength \nEnzymes \nStructure → specific three-dimensional active site \nFunction → catalysis \nAntibodies \nStructure → specific binding regions \nFunction → recognition of antigens \n \n50. Important Distinctions \nPeptide bond vs hydrogen bond \nPeptide bond\n\nStrong covalent bond joining amino acids. \nHydrogen bond \nWeak non-covalent interaction contributing to protein folding. \n \nPrimary vs secondary structure \nPrimary \nAmino acid sequence. \nSecondary \nLocal α-helices and β-sheets. \n \nTertiary vs quaternary structure \nTertiary \nThree-dimensional structure of one polypeptide. \nQuaternary \nAssociation of multiple polypeptide subunits. \n \nDenaturation vs hydrolysis \nDenaturation \nChanges protein shape without necessarily breaking peptide bonds. \nHydrolysis \nUses water to break covalent bonds such as peptide bonds. \n \n51. Common Examination Mistakes \nMistake 1 \nSaying amino acids are joined by hydrogen bonds. \nThey are joined by peptide bonds.\n\nMistake 2 \nSaying secondary structure is caused by interactions between R groups. \nThe main stabilising interactions in secondary structure are hydrogen bonds between groups in \nthe polypeptide backbone. \n \nMistake 3 \nSaying all proteins have quaternary structure. \nOnly proteins consisting of multiple associated polypeptide subunits have quaternary structure. \n \nMistake 4 \nSaying denaturation breaks all peptide bonds. \nDenaturation primarily disrupts the higher levels of protein organisation. The primary structure \ncan remain intact. \n \nMistake 5 \nSaying enzymes are always carbohydrates. \nMost biological enzymes are proteins, although some RNA molecules can also have catalytic \nactivity. \n \nMistake 6 \nSaying protein function depends only on the amino acid sequence. \nThe amino acid sequence determines the folding potential, but the final three-dimensional \nstructure is directly related to the protein's function. \n \n52. A/L Examination Questions \nQuestion 1 \nDescribe the general structure of an amino acid.\n\nQuestion 2 \nExplain how a peptide bond is formed between two amino acids. \n \nQuestion 3 \nDescribe the four levels of protein organisation. \n \nQuestion 4 \nExplain how the primary structure of a protein influences its tertiary structure. \n \nQuestion 5 \nCompare fibrous and globular proteins and give suitable examples. \n \nQuestion 6 \nExplain how temperature and pH can affect protein structure. \n \nQuestion 7 \nExplain why denaturation can cause an enzyme to lose its biological activity. \n \nQuestion 8 \nExplain the structural features that make phospholipids different from proteins. \n \n53. Final Revision \nProteins are biological macromolecules made from amino acids. \nEach amino acid contains \n• \nAn amino group  \n• \nA carboxyl group  \n• \nA hydrogen atom\n\n• \nAn R group  \nAmino acids are joined by peptide bonds through condensation reactions. \nThe four levels of protein organisation are \nPrimary \nAmino acid sequence. \nSecondary \nα-helices and β-pleated sheets, stabilised mainly by hydrogen bonds. \nTertiary \nOverall three-dimensional structure of a single polypeptide, stabilised by interactions involving \nR groups. \nQuaternary \nAssociation of two or more polypeptide subunits. \nProteins can function as \n• \nEnzymes  \n• \nStructural components  \n• \nTransport molecules  \n• \nDefence molecules  \n• \nHormones  \n• \nReceptors  \n• \nContractile molecules  \n• \nStorage molecules  \nTwo major structural categories are \nFibrous proteins → structural roles \nGlobular proteins → functional and metabolic roles \nProtein function depends strongly on its three-dimensional structure. Factors such as \ntemperature and pH can disrupt this structure and cause denaturation. \nThe key concept of this lesson is\n\nAmino acid sequence → protein folding → three-dimensional structure → biological \nfunction	COMPLETED	74	2026-08-19 18:11:09.389611	2026-08-19 18:12:51.88285	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
955	Lesson 5: Structure of nucleic acids and other nucleotides - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 5: Structure of nucleic acids and other nucleotides	PDF	uploads/pdf/875c4c750a9645ba9c92844d5ddeefaa.pdf	\N	Lesson 5 — Structure of Nucleic Acids and Other Nucleotides \n1. Lesson Overview \nNucleic acids are essential biological molecules responsible for storing, transmitting and \nexpressing genetic information. \nThe two major nucleic acids are \n• \nDNA  \n• \nRNA  \nBoth are polymers made from smaller units called nucleotides. \nNucleotides also have important functions beyond nucleic acid formation. For example, ATP \nacts as a major energy-transfer molecule in cells, while several nucleotides and nucleotide \nderivatives function as components of coenzymes involved in metabolism. \nThe structure of DNA allows genetic information to be stored in a stable form, while the \nstructure of RNA allows it to participate in processes such as gene expression and protein \nsynthesis. \n \n2. Learning Objectives \nBy the end of this lesson, you should be able to \n• \nDefine nucleic acids and nucleotides.  \n• \nDescribe the structure of a nucleotide.  \n• \nDistinguish between nucleosides and nucleotides.  \n• \nExplain the structure of DNA.  \n• \nExplain complementary base pairing.  \n• \nDescribe the double-helical structure of DNA.  \n• \nCompare DNA and RNA.  \n• \nDescribe the major types of RNA.  \n• \nExplain the structure and function of ATP.  \n• \nExplain the importance of nucleotide derivatives and coenzymes.  \n• \nRelate nucleic acid structure to biological function.\n\n3. Nucleic Acids \nNucleic acids are biological macromolecules composed of nucleotide units. \nThe two major nucleic acids are \nDNA — Deoxyribonucleic acid \nRNA — Ribonucleic acid \nTheir major biological roles are associated with genetic information. \nDNA \nDNA primarily stores hereditary information. \nRNA \nRNA participates in the expression of genetic information and has several additional cellular \nfunctions. \n \n4. Nucleotides \nA nucleotide is the basic structural unit of nucleic acids. \nEach nucleotide consists of three components \n1. A pentose sugar  \n2. A phosphate group  \n3. A nitrogenous base  \nTherefore \nPentose sugar + phosphate + nitrogenous base → nucleotide \n \n5. Nucleosides \nA nucleoside contains \nPentose sugar + nitrogenous base \nIt does not contain a phosphate group. \nTherefore \nNucleoside + phosphate → nucleotide\n\nThis distinction is important in examinations. \n \n6. Pentose Sugars \nDNA and RNA contain different pentose sugars. \nDNA \nContains deoxyribose. \nRNA \nContains ribose. \nThe difference occurs at the 2′ carbon of the sugar. \nRibose has a hydroxyl group at the 2′ carbon. \nDeoxyribose has a hydrogen instead of that hydroxyl group. \nTherefore, deoxyribose contains one less oxygen atom than ribose. \n \n7. Nitrogenous Bases \nThere are five major nitrogenous bases associated with DNA and RNA. \nThey are \n• \nAdenine  \n• \nGuanine  \n• \nCytosine  \n• \nThymine  \n• \nUracil  \nDNA contains \nAdenine, Guanine, Cytosine and Thymine \nRNA contains \nAdenine, Guanine, Cytosine and Uracil \nTherefore \nDNA → A, G, C, T\n\nRNA → A, G, C, U \n \n8. Purines and Pyrimidines \nNitrogenous bases can be divided into two structural groups. \nPurines \nPurines have two fused rings. \nThe purines are \n• \nAdenine  \n• \nGuanine  \nPyrimidines \nPyrimidines have one ring. \nThe pyrimidines are \n• \nCytosine  \n• \nThymine  \n• \nUracil  \nA useful examination rule is \nPurines → A and G \nPyrimidines → C, T and U \n \n9. Formation of a Nucleotide \nA nitrogenous base attaches to the pentose sugar through a covalent bond. \nThe phosphate group is attached to the sugar. \nThe resulting molecule is a nucleotide. \nIn nucleic acids, nucleotides are connected through phosphodiester bonds. \n \n10. Phosphodiester Bonds \nA phosphodiester bond joins adjacent nucleotides in a nucleic acid strand.\n\nThe phosphate group links the sugar of one nucleotide to the sugar of the next nucleotide. \nThis produces the repeating \nSugar — Phosphate — Sugar — Phosphate \nbackbone. \nThe nitrogenous bases project from this backbone. \n \n11. DNA \nDNA stands for \nDeoxyribonucleic acid \nDNA is the main hereditary material in most organisms. \nIt stores information required for the development, functioning and reproduction of organisms. \nThe information is encoded in the sequence of nitrogenous bases. \n \n12. Structure of DNA \nDNA consists of two polynucleotide strands arranged as a double helix. \nThe two strands are \n• \nAntiparallel  \n• \nComplementary  \nThe sugar-phosphate backbones are located on the outside. \nThe nitrogenous bases face inward. \n \n13. Antiparallel Strands \nThe two DNA strands run in opposite directions. \nOne strand runs \n5′ → 3′ \nThe complementary strand runs \n3′ → 5′\n\nThis arrangement is called antiparallel. \nThe direction is determined by the orientation of the sugar-phosphate backbone. \n \n14. Complementary Base Pairing \nThe bases in DNA pair specifically. \nAdenine pairs with Thymine \nGuanine pairs with Cytosine \nTherefore \nA = T \nG ≡ C \nThe base pairs are held together by hydrogen bonds. \n \n15. Hydrogen Bonding in DNA \nAdenine and thymine form two hydrogen bonds. \nGuanine and cytosine form three hydrogen bonds. \nTherefore, G-C base pairs have stronger hydrogen bonding than A-T pairs. \nHowever, the overall stability of DNA depends on several factors, not simply the number of \nhydrogen bonds. \n \n16. Complementarity \nIf one DNA strand has the sequence \nA T G C C A \nthe complementary strand will be \nT A C G G T \nThis complementary relationship is essential for \n• \nDNA replication  \n• \nDNA repair\n\n• \nGene expression  \nIt allows one strand to serve as a template for producing another complementary strand. \n \n17. DNA Double Helix \nThe DNA double helix has several important structural characteristics. \n• \nTwo polynucleotide strands  \n• \nAntiparallel orientation  \n• \nComplementary base pairing  \n• \nSugar-phosphate backbone  \n• \nBases positioned internally  \n• \nHydrogen bonds between complementary bases  \nThe structure provides both stability and accessibility of genetic information. \n \n18. Why DNA Is Suitable for Information Storage \nDNA is particularly suitable for storing genetic information because \n• \nIt is chemically stable.  \n• \nIts double-stranded structure provides redundancy.  \n• \nComplementary base pairing allows accurate copying.  \n• \nThe sequence of bases can encode enormous amounts of information.  \n• \nDamaged information can sometimes be repaired using the complementary strand.  \n \n19. DNA Base Sequence \nGenetic information is determined by the sequence of bases. \nFor example \nA — T — G — C — A — C — T \nrepresents different information from \nA — T — C — G — A — C — T\n\nTherefore, the order of bases rather than the bases themselves determines the information \nencoded in DNA. \n \n20. RNA \nRNA stands for \nRibonucleic acid \nRNA is usually a single-stranded nucleic acid. \nIt contains \n• \nRibose  \n• \nPhosphate  \n• \nAdenine  \n• \nGuanine  \n• \nCytosine  \n• \nUracil  \nRNA plays major roles in gene expression and protein synthesis. \n \n21. Major Types of RNA \nThree major types of RNA are \n• \nMessenger RNA  \n• \nTransfer RNA  \n• \nRibosomal RNA  \nThey are commonly abbreviated as \nmRNA \ntRNA \nrRNA \n \n22. Messenger RNA\n\nmRNA carries genetic information from DNA to ribosomes. \nThe information is encoded in a sequence of nucleotides. \nDuring protein synthesis, the sequence of bases in mRNA determines the sequence of amino \nacids in the resulting polypeptide. \n \n23. Transfer RNA \ntRNA participates in the delivery of amino acids during protein synthesis. \nEach tRNA molecule contains an anticodon that can pair with a complementary codon on \nmRNA. \nThe tRNA also carries a specific amino acid. \nTherefore, tRNA acts as an adaptor between nucleotide information and amino acid sequence. \n \n24. Ribosomal RNA \nrRNA is a major structural and functional component of ribosomes. \nRibosomes are the cellular structures where proteins are synthesised. \nrRNA contributes to \n• \nRibosome structure  \n• \nPositioning of mRNA and tRNA  \n• \nCatalysis of peptide bond formation  \nTherefore, RNA is not merely an information carrier. \n \n25. Other Types of RNA \nCells contain many additional RNA molecules. \nExamples include \n• \nSmall nuclear RNA  \n• \nMicroRNA  \n• \nSmall interfering RNA  \nThese can participate in processes such as\n\n• \nRNA processing  \n• \nGene regulation  \n• \nControl of gene expression  \nThe diversity of RNA demonstrates that RNA has many functions beyond protein synthesis. \n \n26. DNA vs RNA \nFeature \nDNA \nRNA \nFull name \nDeoxyribonucleic acid \nRibonucleic acid \nSugar \nDeoxyribose \nRibose \nPyrimidine unique to \nmolecule \nThymine \nUracil \nTypical structure \nDouble-stranded \nUsually single-stranded \nMain role \nGenetic information \nstorage \nGene expression and other \nfunctions \nStability \nGenerally more stable \nGenerally less chemically stable \n \n27. Why DNA and RNA Have Different Sugars \nThe absence of the 2′ hydroxyl group in deoxyribose contributes to DNA's greater chemical \nstability. \nRNA contains a 2′ hydroxyl group. \nThis makes RNA more chemically reactive and contributes to its greater susceptibility to \nhydrolysis. \nThis difference is one reason DNA is particularly suitable for long-term genetic information \nstorage. \n \n28. RNA Secondary Structure \nAlthough RNA is generally single-stranded, it can fold back on itself. \nComplementary bases within the same RNA molecule can form hydrogen bonds.\n\nThis produces structures such as \n• \nHairpin loops  \n• \nStem-loop structures  \nTherefore, RNA can have complex three-dimensional structures despite usually being single-\nstranded. \n \n29. Nucleotides Beyond DNA and RNA \nNucleotides are not used only to construct nucleic acids. \nThey also function as \n• \nEnergy-transfer molecules  \n• \nCellular signalling molecules  \n• \nComponents of coenzymes  \n• \nMetabolic intermediates  \nThe most important example for this lesson is ATP. \n \n30. ATP \nATP stands for \nAdenosine triphosphate \nATP consists of \n• \nAdenine  \n• \nRibose  \n• \nThree phosphate groups  \nAdenine + ribose forms adenosine. \nAdenosine + three phosphate groups forms ATP. \n \n31. Structure of ATP \nATP contains three phosphate groups connected in sequence.\n\nA simplified representation is \nAdenine — Ribose — P — P — P \nThe phosphate groups contain bonds that can participate in energy-transfer reactions. \nATP is therefore an important molecule for coupling energy-releasing reactions with energy-\nrequiring cellular processes. \n \n32. ATP and Energy Transfer \nATP is often described as the cell's energy currency. \nA common reaction is \nATP + H₂O → ADP + Pi + energy \nwhere \nADP = Adenosine diphosphate \nPi = inorganic phosphate \nEnergy released during ATP hydrolysis can be coupled to processes that require energy. \nExamples include \n• \nActive transport  \n• \nMuscle contraction  \n• \nBiosynthesis  \n• \nCell movement  \n \n33. ATP Is Not a Long-Term Energy Store \nATP should not be considered the body's primary long-term energy storage molecule. \nInstead, ATP is primarily an immediate energy-transfer molecule. \nEnergy from nutrients such as glucose and fatty acids is used to generate ATP. \nATP can then provide energy for cellular processes. \nThe overall concept is \nNutrients → energy released → ATP formation → cellular work\n\n34. ATP and Phosphorylation \nATP can transfer a phosphate group to another molecule. \nThis process is called phosphorylation. \nPhosphorylation can change the activity or properties of molecules. \nIt is important in \n• \nMetabolic pathways  \n• \nEnzyme regulation  \n• \nSignal transduction  \n• \nActive transport  \nATP therefore functions as more than simply an energy-storage molecule. \n \n35. Nucleotides and Coenzymes \nSeveral important coenzymes contain nucleotide-derived components. \nExamples include \n• \nNAD⁺  \n• \nFAD  \n• \nCoenzyme A  \nThese molecules participate in metabolic reactions. \nThey can function in processes such as \n• \nElectron transfer  \n• \nHydrogen transfer  \n• \nAcyl group transfer  \n \n36. NAD⁺ \nNAD⁺ stands for \nNicotinamide adenine dinucleotide\n\nIt is an important coenzyme involved in oxidation-reduction reactions. \nNAD⁺ can accept hydrogen/electrons and become reduced to NADH. \nSimplified representation \nNAD⁺ + hydrogen/electrons → NADH \nNADH can subsequently donate electrons during cellular respiration. \n \n37. FAD \nFAD stands for \nFlavin adenine dinucleotide \nIt is another important electron-carrying coenzyme. \nFAD can accept hydrogen/electrons to form FADH₂. \nFAD and FADH₂ participate particularly in metabolic pathways associated with cellular \nrespiration. \n \n38. Coenzyme A \nCoenzyme A is an important coenzyme involved in the transfer of acyl groups. \nIt plays an important role in metabolic pathways such as \n• \nPyruvate oxidation  \n• \nKrebs cycle  \n• \nFatty acid metabolism  \nCoenzyme A contains a nucleotide-derived component as part of its structure. \n \n39. Nucleotides and Cell Signalling \nSome nucleotides and their derivatives act as signalling molecules. \nExamples include \n• \ncAMP  \n• \ncGMP\n\nThese molecules can function as second messengers inside cells. \nFor example, cAMP can transmit signals from a cell-surface receptor to intracellular targets. \n \n40. Nucleic Acids and Genetic Information \nDNA and RNA are connected to the central pathway of genetic information. \nA simplified representation is \nDNA → RNA → Protein \nThis is commonly called the central dogma of molecular biology. \nDNA stores genetic information. \nRNA carries and processes information. \nProteins perform many of the resulting cellular functions. \n \n41. DNA and RNA Compared With Proteins \nThe three major information-related biological molecules have different basic units. \nDNA and RNA \nBuilt from nucleotides. \nProteins \nBuilt from amino acids. \nThe sequence of nucleotides in DNA ultimately determines the sequence of amino acids in \nproteins through gene expression. \n \n42. Important Bonds to Remember \nSeveral different bonds are associated with nucleic acids. \nPhosphodiester bond \nJoins nucleotides along the nucleic acid backbone. \nHydrogen bond \nJoins complementary bases between DNA strands.\n\nN-glycosidic bond \nConnects a nitrogenous base to the pentose sugar. \nThese bonds should not be confused. \n \n43. Nucleotide Structure Summary \nA nucleotide consists of \nNitrogenous base + Pentose sugar + Phosphate group \nDNA nucleotide \nBase + deoxyribose + phosphate \nRNA nucleotide \nBase + ribose + phosphate \nATP \nAdenine + ribose + three phosphate groups \n \n44. DNA Replication and Complementarity \nThe complementary structure of DNA is particularly important during replication. \nIf the two DNA strands separate, each strand can act as a template for synthesis of a new \ncomplementary strand. \nFor example \nOriginal strand \nA — T — G — C \nComplementary strand \nT — A — C — G \nThe complementary nature of DNA therefore allows genetic information to be copied accurately. \nDNA replication will be studied in greater detail in the Genetics unit. \n \n45. DNA Stability\n\nSeveral factors contribute to DNA stability. \nDouble-stranded structure \nThe complementary strands provide structural stability. \nBase pairing \nHydrogen bonds hold complementary bases together. \nBase stacking \nInteractions between neighbouring bases also contribute substantially to the stability of the DNA \ndouble helix. \nDeoxyribose \nThe absence of the 2′ hydroxyl group makes DNA less chemically reactive than RNA. \n \n46. Why RNA Is Suitable for Diverse Functions \nRNA has several characteristics that make it biologically versatile. \n• \nIt can form complementary base pairs.  \n• \nIt can fold into complex three-dimensional structures.  \n• \nIt can interact with proteins and other molecules.  \n• \nSome RNA molecules have catalytic activity.  \n• \nRNA can carry genetic information.  \nTherefore, RNA can function as both an information-containing molecule and a functional \nmolecule. \n \n47. Ribozymes \nSome RNA molecules can catalyse chemical reactions. \nCatalytic RNA molecules are called ribozymes. \nThis demonstrates that biological catalysis is not limited to proteins. \nRibozymes are important for understanding the evolution and versatility of RNA. \n \n48. Important Comparison\n\nMolecule \nMain components \nMajor role \nDNA \nDeoxyribose, phosphate, A, T, G, C \nGenetic information storage \nRNA \nRibose, phosphate, A, U, G, C \nGene expression and other cellular \nfunctions \nATP \nAdenine, ribose, 3 phosphate groups \nEnergy transfer \nNAD⁺ \nNucleotide-derived components \nElectron transfer \nFAD \nNucleotide-derived components \nElectron transfer \nCoenzyme \nA \nNucleotide-derived component and other \ngroups \nAcyl group transfer \n \n49. Common Examination Mistakes \nMistake 1 \nSaying a nucleotide consists only of a nitrogenous base. \nA nucleotide contains \nSugar + phosphate + nitrogenous base \n \nMistake 2 \nConfusing nucleoside and nucleotide. \nNucleoside = sugar + base \nNucleotide = sugar + base + phosphate \n \nMistake 3 \nSaying RNA contains thymine. \nThe standard RNA bases are \nA, U, G and C \n \nMistake 4\n\nSaying DNA contains uracil. \nDNA contains thymine, not uracil. \n \nMistake 5 \nSaying ATP stores energy for long periods. \nATP is primarily an immediate energy-transfer molecule. \n \nMistake 6 \nSaying all RNA is single-stranded and cannot form base pairs. \nRNA is generally single-stranded, but complementary regions within an RNA molecule can base-\npair and form complex structures. \n \nMistake 7 \nConfusing peptide bonds and phosphodiester bonds. \nPeptide bonds → amino acids \nPhosphodiester bonds → nucleotides \n \n50. A/L Examination Questions \nQuestion 1 \nDescribe the structure of a nucleotide. \n \nQuestion 2 \nDistinguish between a nucleoside and a nucleotide. \n \nQuestion 3 \nCompare DNA and RNA with reference to \n• \nSugar\n\n• \nNitrogenous bases  \n• \nNumber of strands  \n• \nStability  \n• \nBiological function  \n \nQuestion 4 \nExplain complementary base pairing in DNA. \n \nQuestion 5 \nExplain why DNA is suitable for long-term storage of genetic information. \n \nQuestion 6 \nDescribe the structure of ATP and explain its role in cells. \n \nQuestion 7 \nExplain the difference between ATP as an energy-transfer molecule and triglycerides as energy-\nstorage molecules. \n \nQuestion 8 \nDescribe the functions of mRNA, tRNA and rRNA. \n \nQuestion 9 \nExplain the importance of NAD⁺ and FAD in cellular metabolism. \n \n51. Final Revision \nNucleic acids are biological macromolecules made from nucleotides. \nA nucleotide contains\n\nNitrogenous base + pentose sugar + phosphate group \nDNA contains \nDeoxyribose + A, T, G and C \nRNA contains \nRibose + A, U, G and C \nDNA normally consists of two antiparallel strands forming a double helix. Complementary base \npairing occurs through \nA ↔ T \nG ↔ C \nRNA is generally single-stranded and performs several functions in gene expression. \nThe three major forms of RNA are \nmRNA → carries genetic information \ntRNA → carries amino acids \nrRNA → forms an important part of ribosomes \nNucleotides also have functions outside nucleic acids. \nATP → immediate energy transfer \nNAD⁺ and FAD → electron transfer \nCoenzyme A → acyl group transfer \nThe most important concept of this lesson is \nThe structure of nucleic acids allows genetic information to be stored, copied and \nexpressed, while other nucleotides and nucleotide derivatives perform essential metabolic \nand cellular functions.	COMPLETED	75	2026-08-19 18:11:11.14226	2026-08-19 18:12:51.882852	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
956	Lesson 6: Microscopy and the cell theory - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 6: Microscopy and the cell theory	PDF	uploads/pdf/b01bc5ad1b604d7d9c341d82ef0979e0.pdf	\N	Lesson 6 — Microscopy and the Cell Theory \n1. Lesson Overview \nCells are the fundamental structural and functional units of living organisms. However, most \ncells are too small to be observed clearly with the unaided human eye. \nThe development of microscopy allowed scientists to observe cells, investigate their structures \nand establish the principles of modern cell biology. \nThis lesson focuses on \n• \nThe history and development of microscopy  \n• \nMagnification and resolution  \n• \nLight microscopy  \n• \nElectron microscopy  \n• \nPreparation of biological specimens  \n• \nDifferences between light and electron microscopes  \n• \nThe development of cell theory  \n• \nModern concepts of cell theory  \n \n2. Learning Objectives \nBy the end of this lesson, you should be able to \n• \nExplain why microscopes are necessary for studying cells.  \n• \nDefine magnification and resolution.  \n• \nDistinguish between magnification and resolution.  \n• \nDescribe the basic principles of light microscopy.  \n• \nExplain the major components of a light microscope.  \n• \nDescribe electron microscopy.  \n• \nDistinguish between TEM and SEM.  \n• \nCompare light and electron microscopes.  \n• \nExplain common methods used to prepare specimens.  \n• \nDescribe the historical development of cell theory.\n\n• \nState the major principles of modern cell theory.  \n• \nExplain the importance and limitations of microscopy.  \n \n3. Why Microscopy Is Important \nMost cells are microscopic. \nThe human eye has a limited ability to distinguish two objects that are very close together. \nMicroscopes overcome this limitation by producing enlarged images with improved ability to \ndistinguish fine details. \nMicroscopy has allowed scientists to investigate \n• \nCell structure  \n• \nOrganelles  \n• \nMicroorganisms  \n• \nTissues  \n• \nCell division  \n• \nCellular interactions  \n• \nDisease-causing organisms  \nWithout microscopy, much of modern cell biology would not be possible. \n \n4. Microscopy \nMicroscopy is the use of microscopes to observe objects or structures that cannot be adequately \nseen with the unaided eye. \nA microscope produces an image that allows small structures to be observed in greater detail. \nThe two fundamental concepts that must be understood are \nMagnification \nand \nResolution \n \n5. Magnification\n\nMagnification describes how many times larger an image appears compared with the actual \nobject. \nFor example \nIf an object is 10 μm long and its image is 100 μm long \nMagnification = 100 ÷ 10 = 10× \nTherefore, the image is magnified ten times. \n \n6. Magnification Formula \nThe basic relationship is \nMagnification = Image size ÷ Actual size \nTherefore \nImage size = Magnification × Actual size \nand \nActual size = Image size ÷ Magnification \nThese relationships are frequently used in microscopy examination questions. \n \n7. Units Used in Microscopy \nBiological structures are commonly measured using small units. \nMillimetre \n1 mm = 10⁻³ m \nMicrometre \n1 μm = 10⁻⁶ m \nNanometre \n1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m \nTherefore \n1 mm = 1,000 μm \nand\n\n1 μm = 1,000 nm \n \n8. Resolution \nResolution is the ability of a microscope to distinguish two points that are very close together as \nseparate points. \nA microscope with high resolution can reveal finer details. \nFor example, if two structures are extremely close together \nA low-resolution microscope may show them as one structure. \nA high-resolution microscope may show them as two separate structures. \n \n9. Magnification vs Resolution \nThese two concepts must not be confused. \nMagnification \nMakes an image appear larger. \nResolution \nDetermines how much fine detail can be distinguished. \nA microscope can produce a highly enlarged image without revealing additional detail. \nTherefore \nHigh magnification does not necessarily mean high resolution. \n \n10. Light Microscope \nA light microscope, also called an optical microscope, uses visible light to illuminate the \nspecimen. \nGlass lenses are used to magnify and focus the image. \nLight microscopes are widely used in biology laboratories because they can observe many living \nspecimens. \n \n11. Main Components of a Light Microscope\n\nImportant components include \n• \nLight source  \n• \nCondenser  \n• \nObjective lenses  \n• \nEyepiece lens  \n• \nStage  \n• \nCoarse adjustment  \n• \nFine adjustment  \n• \nDiaphragm  \nEach component contributes to producing a focused and useful image. \n \n12. Objective Lenses \nThe objective lens is positioned close to the specimen. \nCommon objective magnifications include \n• \n4×  \n• \n10×  \n• \n40×  \n• \n100×  \nThe objective lens provides the primary magnification. \nThe 100× objective is commonly used with immersion oil in many light microscopes. \n \n13. Eyepiece Lens \nThe eyepiece, or ocular lens, is the lens through which the observer views the specimen. \nIf the objective lens provides 40× magnification and the eyepiece provides 10× \nTotal magnification = 40 × 10 \nTotal magnification = 400×\n\n14. Total Magnification \nThe total magnification of a compound light microscope is \nTotal magnification = Objective magnification × Eyepiece magnification \nFor example \nObjective = 40× \nEyepiece = 10× \nTherefore \nTotal = 400× \nThis is an important calculation for practical and examination questions. \n \n15. Condenser \nThe condenser focuses light onto the specimen. \nA well-adjusted condenser helps illuminate the specimen effectively. \nThe amount of light entering the optical system can also be controlled using the diaphragm. \n \n16. Stage \nThe stage supports the specimen. \nA microscope slide containing the specimen is positioned on the stage. \nStage clips or mechanical holders keep the slide in position. \n \n17. Coarse and Fine Adjustment \nCoarse adjustment \nMoves the objective lens or stage relatively rapidly to achieve approximate focus. \nFine adjustment \nMakes small adjustments to obtain a sharp, detailed image. \nThe fine adjustment is particularly important when observing structures at high magnification.\n\n18. Staining \nMany biological structures are almost transparent when viewed using ordinary transmitted light. \nStaining can increase contrast and make structures easier to distinguish. \nDifferent stains have different affinities for cellular components. \nExamples include \n• \nIodine  \n• \nMethylene blue  \n• \nSafranin  \n• \nHaematoxylin  \nStaining is particularly useful for fixed specimens. \n \n19. Living and Fixed Specimens \nMicroscopy can involve different types of specimens. \nLiving specimens \nCan be observed while alive. \nAdvantages include observing \n• \nCell movement  \n• \nCytoplasmic streaming  \n• \nSome cell behaviours  \nFixed specimens \nThe cells are preserved and are no longer alive. \nAdvantages include \n• \nGreater structural preservation  \n• \nAbility to use many staining procedures  \n• \nLong-term preparation and storage  \n \n20. Wet Mount\n\nA wet mount is a simple method of preparing a specimen for light microscopy. \nA specimen is placed in a drop of liquid on a microscope slide and covered with a coverslip. \nThis method is useful for observing \n• \nLiving cells  \n• \nMicroorganisms  \n• \nThin biological samples  \n \n21. Electron Microscopy \nElectron microscopes use a beam of electrons rather than visible light. \nElectrons have much shorter wavelengths than visible light. \nThis allows electron microscopes to achieve much greater resolution than conventional light \nmicroscopes. \nThey can therefore reveal ultrastructural details of cells and organelles. \n \n22. Transmission Electron Microscope \nA Transmission Electron Microscope, or TEM, passes electrons through a very thin specimen. \nThe resulting image reveals internal structures. \nTEM is particularly useful for studying \n• \nInternal organelle structure  \n• \nMembrane systems  \n• \nRibosomes  \n• \nMitochondria  \n• \nChloroplast ultrastructure  \n \n23. Scanning Electron Microscope \nA Scanning Electron Microscope, or SEM, scans a beam of electrons across the surface of a \nspecimen. \nIt produces an image showing surface characteristics.\n\nSEM is particularly useful for studying \n• \nSurface texture  \n• \nExternal structures  \n• \nThree-dimensional surface appearance  \n \n24. TEM vs SEM \nFeature \nTEM \nSEM \nElectron path \nThrough specimen \nScans specimen surface \nMain view \nInternal structure \nSurface structure \nSpecimen \nVery thin sections \nSurface-prepared specimen \nImage appearance Two-dimensional projection Three-dimensional appearance \nMain application Ultrastructure \nSurface morphology \nThe apparent three-dimensional appearance of SEM images results from the way surface \ninformation is detected and displayed. \n \n25. Light Microscope vs Electron Microscope \nFeature \nLight microscope \nElectron microscope \nIllumination \nVisible light \nElectron beam \nLenses \nGlass/electromagnetic optical system \nElectromagnetic lenses \nResolution \nLower \nMuch higher \nMagnification \nLower \nMuch higher \nLiving \nspecimens \nCan be observed \nGenerally cannot \nImage \nCan be coloured naturally or through \nstains \nGrayscale in conventional \nimaging \nPreparation \nGenerally simpler \nMore extensive\n\nFeature \nLight microscope \nElectron microscope \nMain advantage Can observe living material \nVery high resolution \n \n26. Why Electron Microscopes Have Higher Resolution \nResolution is related to the wavelength used to form the image. \nElectrons can have much shorter effective wavelengths than visible light. \nA shorter wavelength allows finer structures to be distinguished. \nTherefore \nShorter wavelength → potentially higher resolution \nThis is a major reason electron microscopy can reveal cellular ultrastructure that cannot be \nresolved using conventional light microscopy. \n \n27. Limitations of Electron Microscopy \nElectron microscopy has important limitations. \nLiving specimens generally cannot be observed \nThe preparation process and vacuum conditions are incompatible with normal living cells. \nSpecimen preparation is complex \nSamples often require fixation, dehydration and other treatments. \nImages are conventionally grayscale \nAny colours used in electron micrographs are generally added afterward for presentation. \nEquipment is expensive \nElectron microscopes require specialised equipment and controlled conditions. \n \n28. Electron Microscope Specimen Preparation \nElectron microscopy generally requires extensive preparation. \nTypical steps may include \n1. Fixation\n\n2. Dehydration  \n3. Embedding  \n4. Sectioning or surface preparation  \n5. Staining or coating  \n6. Examination under vacuum  \nThe exact procedure depends on whether TEM or SEM is being used. \n \n29. Fixation \nFixation preserves cellular structures and prevents significant degradation. \nChemical fixatives can stabilise cellular components. \nThe purpose is to maintain the structure of the specimen as closely as possible during subsequent \npreparation. \n \n30. Dehydration \nWater is removed from the specimen during dehydration. \nThis is necessary because some later preparation stages require the removal of water. \nHowever, dehydration can also cause structural changes or shrinkage. \nTherefore, preparation itself can introduce artefacts. \n \n31. Sectioning \nFor TEM, specimens are often embedded in a suitable material and cut into extremely thin \nsections. \nThe sections must be thin enough for electrons to pass through them. \nSpecial instruments such as ultramicrotomes can be used to produce these sections. \n \n32. Staining in Electron Microscopy \nElectron microscopy does not use ordinary visible-light stains in the same way as light \nmicroscopy.\n\nMaterials containing heavy metals can be used to increase electron scattering. \nThis creates contrast between different regions of the specimen. \nThe resulting image allows fine cellular structures to be distinguished. \n \n33. Artefacts \nAn artefact is a structure or feature introduced or altered by specimen preparation or the \nimaging process rather than being a true feature of the living specimen. \nPossible causes include \n• \nFixation  \n• \nDehydration  \n• \nSectioning  \n• \nStaining  \n• \nFreezing  \n• \nMechanical damage  \nScientists must therefore interpret microscopic images carefully. \n \n34. Development of Cell Theory \nThe development of cell theory occurred gradually as microscopy improved. \nImportant scientists include \n• \nRobert Hooke  \n• \nAntonie van Leeuwenhoek  \n• \nMatthias Schleiden  \n• \nTheodor Schwann  \n• \nRudolf Virchow  \nTheir observations and ideas contributed to the modern understanding of cells. \n \n35. Robert Hooke\n\nIn 1665, Robert Hooke examined thin slices of cork using a microscope. \nHe observed many small compartments. \nHe called these structures cells because they reminded him of small rooms. \nThe structures he observed were actually the remains of dead plant cells. \nHooke introduced the term "cell" into biology. \n \n36. Antonie van Leeuwenhoek \nAntonie van Leeuwenhoek developed powerful simple microscopes and observed living \nmicroscopic organisms. \nHe observed \n• \nBacteria  \n• \nProtozoa  \n• \nSperm cells  \n• \nBlood cells  \nHe referred to many of the microscopic organisms he observed as "animalcules". \nHis observations provided early evidence of the diversity of microscopic life. \n \n37. Matthias Schleiden \nMatthias Schleiden was a German botanist. \nIn the nineteenth century, he concluded that plants are composed of cells. \nHis work contributed to the development of cell theory. \n \n38. Theodor Schwann \nTheodor Schwann extended the cellular concept to animals. \nHe concluded that animals are also composed of cells. \nTogether, Schleiden and Schwann established the central idea that cells are fundamental units of \nplants and animals.\n\n39. Rudolf Virchow \nRudolf Virchow later emphasised that new cells arise from pre-existing cells. \nThis idea became a fundamental component of modern cell theory. \nIt rejected the concept that cells routinely arise spontaneously from non-living material under \nnormal biological conditions. \n \n40. Classical Cell Theory \nThe classical cell theory can be summarised by three major principles. \nPrinciple 1 \nAll living organisms are composed of one or more cells. \nPrinciple 2 \nThe cell is the basic structural and functional unit of life. \nPrinciple 3 \nAll cells arise from pre-existing cells. \nThese principles form the foundation of modern cell biology. \n \n41. Modern Cell Theory \nModern cell theory has expanded beyond the original statements. \nAdditional principles include \n• \nCells contain hereditary information.  \n• \nGenetic information is passed from cell to cell during cell division.  \n• \nEnergy flow and metabolism occur within cells.  \n• \nCells have broadly similar fundamental chemical compositions.  \n• \nThe activities of an organism depend on the coordinated activities of its cells.  \n \n42. Cells as Functional Units \nThe cell is not simply a structural building block.\n\nIt is also the basic functional unit of life. \nProcesses such as \n• \nRespiration  \n• \nProtein synthesis  \n• \nTransport  \n• \nGrowth  \n• \nReproduction  \n• \nMetabolism  \noccur within cells. \nIn multicellular organisms, specialised cells perform specific functions. \n \n43. Unicellular Organisms \nSome organisms consist of a single cell. \nExamples include \n• \nMany bacteria  \n• \nAmoeba  \n• \nSome algae  \n• \nSome fungi  \nA single cell must perform all essential life processes. \nTherefore, a single cell can represent an entire living organism. \n \n44. Multicellular Organisms \nMulticellular organisms consist of many cells. \nDifferent cells become specialised for different functions. \nThe organisational pattern is generally \nCells → Tissues → Organs → Organ systems → Organism \nSpecialisation allows complex organisms to perform many functions efficiently.\n\n45. Cell Size and Surface Area \nCell size is influenced by the relationship between \nSurface area \nand \nVolume \nAs a cell becomes larger, its volume increases faster relative to its surface area. \nThis can reduce the efficiency of exchange between the cell and its environment. \nTherefore, many cells remain relatively small. \n \n46. Surface Area to Volume Ratio \nA high surface area-to-volume ratio allows a cell to exchange substances efficiently. \nSmall cells generally have a greater surface area relative to their volume. \nThis supports efficient \n• \nNutrient uptake  \n• \nGas exchange  \n• \nWaste removal  \n• \nHeat exchange  \nAs cell size increases, the surface area-to-volume ratio decreases. \n \n47. Resolution and Cell Biology \nDifferent microscopy techniques allow scientists to investigate structures at different scales. \nLight microscopy \nUseful for observing \n• \nWhole cells  \n• \nTissues  \n• \nSome organelles\n\n• \nLiving cells  \nElectron microscopy \nUseful for observing \n• \nMembranes  \n• \nRibosomes  \n• \nFine organelle structures  \n• \nCellular ultrastructure  \nThe appropriate microscope therefore depends on the biological question being investigated. \n \n48. Magnification Calculations \nExample \nA cell is actually 20 μm long. \nIts image is 80 mm long. \nFirst convert the units. \n80 mm = 80,000 μm \nThen \nMagnification = Image size ÷ Actual size \nMagnification = 80,000 ÷ 20 \nMagnification = 4,000× \nAlways convert measurements into the same units before calculating magnification. \n \n49. Actual Size Calculations \nSuppose a microscope image has a length of 50 mm and the magnification is 500×. \nConvert 50 mm to micrometres \n50 mm = 50,000 μm \nThen \nActual size = Image size ÷ Magnification\n\nActual size = 50,000 ÷ 500 \nActual size = 100 μm \n \n50. Common Examination Mistakes \nMistake 1 \nConfusing magnification with resolution. \nMagnification increases apparent size. \nResolution determines the ability to distinguish fine detail. \n \nMistake 2 \nSaying an electron microscope can normally observe living cells. \nElectron microscopy generally requires conditions and preparation that prevent normal \nobservation of living cells. \n \nMistake 3 \nConfusing TEM and SEM. \nTEM → internal structures \nSEM → surface structures \n \nMistake 4 \nSaying Robert Hooke observed living cells in cork. \nThe cork cells he observed were dead and consisted mainly of cell walls. \n \nMistake 5 \nForgetting unit conversion in magnification calculations. \nAll measurements must be expressed in the same units before applying the formula.\n\nMistake 6 \nSaying higher magnification always produces a better image. \nIncreasing magnification without sufficient resolution can simply produce a larger but unclear \nimage. \n \n51. A/L Examination Questions \nQuestion 1 \nDefine magnification and resolution. \n \nQuestion 2 \nDistinguish between magnification and resolution. \n \nQuestion 3 \nExplain the difference between TEM and SEM. \n \nQuestion 4 \nCompare light microscopy and electron microscopy. \n \nQuestion 5 \nExplain why electron microscopes have a higher resolving power than conventional light \nmicroscopes. \n \nQuestion 6 \nDescribe the contributions of Hooke, Leeuwenhoek, Schleiden, Schwann and Virchow to cell \nbiology. \n \nQuestion 7 \nState the major principles of cell theory.\n\nQuestion 8 \nA cell has an actual diameter of 25 μm. Its image has a diameter of 5 cm. Calculate the \nmagnification. \n \n52. Final Revision \nMicroscopy is essential for studying structures that cannot be adequately observed with the \nunaided eye. \nThe two most important concepts are \nMagnification → how much larger the image appears \nResolution → ability to distinguish two closely positioned structures as separate \nFor a compound light microscope \nTotal magnification = Objective × Eyepiece \nLight microscopes use visible light and can be used to observe some living specimens. \nElectron microscopes use electrons and provide much greater resolving power. \nTEM → internal ultrastructure \nSEM → surface structure \nThe development of microscopy contributed to the development of cell theory. \nImportant scientists include \nRobert Hooke → introduced the term cell \nAntonie van Leeuwenhoek → observed living microorganisms \nMatthias Schleiden → plants are composed of cells \nTheodor Schwann → animals are composed of cells \nRudolf Virchow → cells arise from pre-existing cells \nThe major principles of cell theory are \n1. All living organisms consist of one or more cells.  \n2. The cell is the basic structural and functional unit of life.  \n3. All cells arise from pre-existing cells.\n\nThe key concept of this lesson is \nMicroscopy provided the evidence necessary to reveal cellular structure, while cell theory \nestablished the cell as the fundamental unit of life.	COMPLETED	76	2026-08-19 18:11:12.967619	2026-08-19 18:12:51.882854	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
957	Lesson 7: Structure and functions of subcellular organelles - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 7: Structure and functions of subcellular organelles	PDF	uploads/pdf/7c8203246b194727a5ef45b358eca286.pdf	\N	Lesson 7 — Structure and Functions of Subcellular Organelles \nIntroduction \nCells are the basic structural and functional units of living organisms. Although all cells share \nfundamental characteristics, they differ considerably in their internal organisation. \nCells can broadly be divided into two major types \nProkaryotic cells \nEukaryotic cells \nProkaryotic cells are structurally simpler and lack a membrane-bound nucleus, whereas \neukaryotic cells contain a nucleus and several specialised membrane-bound organelles. \nThe specialised structures within cells are called organelles. Each organelle performs particular \nfunctions that contribute to the survival and functioning of the cell. \n \n1. Prokaryotic Cells \nProkaryotic cells are found in organisms belonging mainly to the domains Bacteria and \nArchaea. \nThey are generally smaller and structurally simpler than eukaryotic cells. \nA typical prokaryotic cell does not contain \n• \nA membrane-bound nucleus  \n• \nMitochondria  \n• \nChloroplasts  \n• \nEndoplasmic reticulum  \n• \nGolgi apparatus  \nInstead, its genetic material is located in a region called the nucleoid. \n \n2. Main Features of Prokaryotic Cells \nA typical prokaryotic cell contains \n• \nPlasma membrane  \n• \nCytoplasm\n\n• \nRibosomes  \n• \nNucleoid  \n• \nCell wall  \n• \nCircular DNA  \n• \nSometimes plasmids  \n• \nSometimes capsules  \n• \nSometimes flagella  \nThe exact structures vary between different bacterial and archaeal species. \n \n3. Nucleoid \nThe nucleoid is the region of a prokaryotic cell where its main chromosome is located. \nUnlike the nucleus of a eukaryotic cell, the nucleoid is not surrounded by a nuclear membrane. \nThe bacterial chromosome is usually a single circular DNA molecule. \nThe DNA contains genes that carry information required for cellular functions. \n \n4. Plasmids \nPlasmids are small, usually circular DNA molecules separate from the main bacterial \nchromosome. \nThey can replicate independently of the main chromosome. \nPlasmids may contain genes that provide particular advantages to bacteria. \nFor example, some plasmids carry genes associated with antibiotic resistance. \nPlasmids can sometimes be transferred between bacteria, contributing to the spread of genetic \ncharacteristics within bacterial populations. \n \n5. Prokaryotic Ribosomes \nProkaryotic cells contain ribosomes. \nRibosomes are responsible for protein synthesis.\n\nBacterial ribosomes are generally smaller than the ribosomes found in the cytoplasm of \neukaryotic cells. \nProkaryotic ribosomes are commonly described as 70S ribosomes, consisting of \n• \n50S large subunit  \n• \n30S small subunit  \n \n6. Prokaryotic Cell Wall \nMany prokaryotic cells possess a cell wall outside the plasma membrane. \nIn bacteria, the cell wall is primarily composed of peptidoglycan. \nThe cell wall \n• \nMaintains cell shape  \n• \nProvides mechanical support  \n• \nProtects against osmotic bursting  \nThe structure of bacterial cell walls differs between major bacterial groups. \n \n7. Capsule \nSome bacteria possess an additional outer layer called a capsule or slime layer. \nIt can help bacteria \n• \nAttach to surfaces  \n• \nAvoid dehydration  \n• \nResist some host defence mechanisms  \n• \nForm biofilms  \nNot all bacteria possess capsules. \n \n8. Flagella in Prokaryotes \nSome bacteria possess flagella that enable movement. \nA bacterial flagellum has a different structure from the eukaryotic flagellum.\n\nBacterial flagella rotate and can propel the cell through liquid environments. \nMovement toward favourable conditions is called positive chemotaxis, while movement away \nfrom harmful chemical conditions is called negative chemotaxis. \n \n9. Eukaryotic Cells \nEukaryotic cells are found in \n• \nAnimals  \n• \nPlants  \n• \nFungi  \n• \nProtists  \nThey are generally larger and structurally more complex than prokaryotic cells. \nTheir most important characteristic is the presence of a membrane-bound nucleus. \nThey also contain numerous specialised membrane-bound organelles. \n \n10. Major Features of Eukaryotic Cells \nEukaryotic cells contain \n• \nPlasma membrane  \n• \nCytoplasm  \n• \nNucleus  \n• \nRibosomes  \n• \nEndoplasmic reticulum  \n• \nGolgi apparatus  \n• \nMitochondria  \nPlant cells additionally contain structures such as \n• \nCell wall  \n• \nChloroplasts  \n• \nLarge central vacuole\n\n11. Plasma Membrane \nThe plasma membrane surrounds the cell and separates its internal environment from the \nexternal environment. \nIt is mainly composed of \n• \nPhospholipids  \n• \nProteins  \n• \nCholesterol in animal cells  \n• \nCarbohydrate-containing molecules  \nThe basic structure is a phospholipid bilayer. \n \n12. Functions of the Plasma Membrane \nThe plasma membrane \n• \nControls movement of substances into and out of the cell  \n• \nMaintains the internal environment  \n• \nAllows cell signalling  \n• \nContains receptors  \n• \nProvides sites for certain cellular processes  \n• \nHelps cells interact with their surroundings  \nThe membrane is selectively permeable, meaning that different substances cross it with different \ndegrees of ease. \n \n13. Cytoplasm \nThe cytoplasm consists of the cytosol and the structures suspended within it, excluding the \nnucleus in the conventional definition. \nThe cytosol contains \n• \nWater  \n• \nDissolved ions\n\n• \nProteins  \n• \nMetabolites  \n• \nOther molecules  \nMany metabolic reactions occur within the cytoplasm. \n \n14. Nucleus \nThe nucleus is a membrane-bound organelle that contains most of the genetic material of a \neukaryotic cell. \nIt is surrounded by the nuclear envelope. \nThe nucleus functions as an important centre for \n• \nStorage of DNA  \n• \nRegulation of gene expression  \n• \nDNA replication  \n• \nTranscription  \n \n15. Nuclear Envelope \nThe nuclear envelope consists of two membranes. \nIt separates the nucleoplasm from the cytoplasm. \nThe nuclear envelope contains nuclear pores. \nThese pores regulate the movement of substances between the nucleus and cytoplasm. \nFor example, RNA molecules can leave the nucleus through nuclear pores. \n \n16. Nucleolus \nThe nucleolus is a dense region within the nucleus. \nIts major functions include \n• \nSynthesis of ribosomal RNA  \n• \nAssembly of ribosomal subunits\n\nThe nucleolus is not surrounded by a membrane. \nA nucleus may contain one or more nucleoli. \n \n17. Chromatin \nThe DNA within the nucleus is associated with proteins and forms chromatin. \nThe main proteins associated with DNA are histones. \nChromatin can become more condensed during cell division to form visible chromosomes. \nChromatin organisation allows a large amount of DNA to fit inside the nucleus. \n \n18. Ribosomes \nRibosomes are structures responsible for protein synthesis. \nThey consist of \n• \nRibosomal RNA  \n• \nProteins  \nRibosomes are not surrounded by membranes. \nThey can be found \n• \nFree in the cytoplasm  \n• \nAttached to the rough endoplasmic reticulum  \n \n19. Free and Bound Ribosomes \nFree ribosomes \nFree ribosomes generally synthesise proteins that function within the cytosol or certain internal \norganelles. \nRibosomes attached to rough ER \nThese generally synthesise proteins destined for \n• \nSecretion  \n• \nCell membranes\n\n• \nEndomembrane compartments  \nThe location of the ribosome is therefore related to the destination of the protein being \nsynthesised. \n \n20. Endoplasmic Reticulum \nThe endoplasmic reticulum, or ER, is an extensive membrane system within eukaryotic cells. \nThere are two major forms \n• \nRough endoplasmic reticulum  \n• \nSmooth endoplasmic reticulum  \n \n21. Rough Endoplasmic Reticulum \nThe rough ER has ribosomes attached to its cytoplasmic surface. \nIts major functions include \n• \nSynthesis of proteins  \n• \nInitial modification of proteins  \n• \nFolding of newly synthesised proteins  \n• \nTransport of proteins to other parts of the endomembrane system  \nProteins synthesised on the rough ER may subsequently be transported to the Golgi apparatus. \n \n22. Smooth Endoplasmic Reticulum \nThe smooth ER lacks ribosomes on its surface. \nFunctions include \n• \nLipid synthesis  \n• \nSteroid synthesis in specialised cells  \n• \nDetoxification  \n• \nCalcium ion storage  \n• \nParticipation in carbohydrate metabolism\n\nSmooth ER is particularly abundant in cells specialised for lipid metabolism or detoxification. \n \n23. Golgi Apparatus \nThe Golgi apparatus consists of stacks of flattened membrane-bound sacs called cisternae. \nIt receives molecules from the endoplasmic reticulum and modifies, sorts and packages them. \nMajor functions include \n• \nProtein modification  \n• \nLipid modification  \n• \nSorting of cellular products  \n• \nPackaging into vesicles  \n• \nFormation of some lysosomes  \n \n24. Golgi Processing \nA simplified pathway is \nRough ER → Transport vesicle → Golgi apparatus → Secretory vesicle → Destination \nDuring this process, proteins can undergo modifications that influence their final function and \ndestination. \nThe Golgi apparatus therefore acts as an important processing and distribution centre. \n \n25. Lysosomes \nLysosomes are membrane-bound organelles containing hydrolytic enzymes. \nThese enzymes function best in the acidic environment inside lysosomes. \nLysosomes participate in \n• \nBreakdown of cellular waste  \n• \nDigestion of engulfed materials  \n• \nRecycling of cellular components  \n• \nDestruction of damaged organelles\n\nThe controlled activity of lysosomal enzymes helps prevent unnecessary damage to the \ncytoplasm. \n \n26. Phagocytosis and Lysosomes \nWhen a cell engulfs a large particle, the material can become enclosed within a vesicle. \nA lysosome may fuse with this vesicle. \nDigestive enzymes then break down the material. \nUseful molecules can be released and reused by the cell, while waste products can eventually be \nremoved. \n \n27. Peroxisomes \nPeroxisomes are small membrane-bound organelles involved in several oxidative reactions. \nThey contain enzymes that can produce and break down hydrogen peroxide. \nThe enzyme catalase converts hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen. \nPeroxisomes are important in \n• \nFatty acid metabolism  \n• \nDetoxification  \n• \nBreakdown of harmful substances  \n \n28. Mitochondria \nMitochondria are major sites of aerobic cellular respiration. \nThey generate much of the ATP used by eukaryotic cells. \nMitochondria have a distinctive structure consisting of \n• \nOuter membrane  \n• \nIntermembrane space  \n• \nInner membrane  \n• \nCristae  \n• \nMatrix\n\n29. Outer Mitochondrial Membrane \nThe outer membrane forms the external boundary of the mitochondrion. \nIt contains proteins that allow certain relatively small molecules to pass into the intermembrane \nspace. \n \n30. Inner Mitochondrial Membrane \nThe inner membrane is highly folded. \nThe folds are called cristae. \nThe inner membrane contains proteins involved in the electron transport chain and ATP \nproduction. \nThe folds increase the membrane surface area available for these processes. \n \n31. Mitochondrial Matrix \nThe matrix is the region enclosed by the inner membrane. \nIt contains \n• \nEnzymes  \n• \nMitochondrial DNA  \n• \nRibosomes  \n• \nMetabolic intermediates  \nImportant reactions of aerobic respiration, including the Krebs cycle, occur in the matrix. \n \n32. Mitochondrial DNA \nMitochondria contain their own DNA. \nThis DNA is generally circular and resembles prokaryotic DNA. \nMitochondria also contain their own ribosomes. \nThese characteristics are important evidence supporting the endosymbiotic theory.\n\n33. Chloroplasts \nChloroplasts are specialised organelles found in plants and many algae. \nThey are the major sites of photosynthesis. \nChloroplasts contain the pigment chlorophyll, which absorbs light energy. \n \n34. Chloroplast Structure \nA chloroplast contains \n• \nOuter membrane  \n• \nInner membrane  \n• \nStroma  \n• \nThylakoids  \n• \nGrana  \n• \nChloroplast DNA  \n• \nRibosomes  \n \n35. Thylakoids \nThylakoids are flattened membrane-bound sacs inside chloroplasts. \nThey contain \n• \nChlorophyll  \n• \nPhotosynthetic pigments  \n• \nElectron transport proteins  \n• \nATP-producing machinery  \nThe light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis occur in the thylakoid membranes. \n \n36. Grana \nStacks of thylakoids are called grana.\n\nThe stacked arrangement provides a large membrane surface area for the light-dependent \nreactions. \nIndividual grana are connected by membrane structures called stroma lamellae. \n \n37. Stroma \nThe stroma is the fluid-filled region surrounding the thylakoids. \nIt contains \n• \nEnzymes  \n• \nChloroplast DNA  \n• \nRibosomes  \n• \nMetabolic intermediates  \nThe Calvin cycle occurs in the stroma. \n \n38. Vacuoles \nVacuoles are membrane-bound compartments involved in storage and other cellular functions. \nPlant cells commonly possess a large central vacuole. \nThe membrane surrounding the central vacuole is called the tonoplast. \n \n39. Functions of the Central Vacuole \nThe central vacuole contributes to \n• \nStorage of water  \n• \nStorage of ions  \n• \nStorage of pigments  \n• \nStorage of waste products  \n• \nMaintenance of turgor pressure  \n• \nCell growth  \nWater entering the vacuole increases turgor pressure and helps maintain the rigidity of plant \ntissues.\n\n40. Plant Cell Wall \nPlant cells possess a rigid cell wall outside the plasma membrane. \nThe main structural component is cellulose. \nThe cell wall provides \n• \nMechanical support  \n• \nProtection  \n• \nMaintenance of cell shape  \n• \nResistance to excessive water uptake  \n \n41. Cellulose \nCellulose is a polysaccharide composed of glucose units. \nThe glucose molecules are joined by β-1,4 glycosidic bonds. \nThe straight chains can form hydrogen bonds with neighbouring chains. \nThis produces strong fibres that contribute to the mechanical strength of the cell wall. \n \n42. Plasmodesmata \nPlant cells can communicate with neighbouring cells through microscopic channels called \nplasmodesmata. \nThese channels pass through the cell walls. \nThey allow communication and movement of certain substances between neighbouring cells. \n \n43. Cytoskeleton \nThe cytoskeleton is an internal network of protein filaments. \nIt contributes to \n• \nCell shape  \n• \nInternal organisation\n\n• \nMovement  \n• \nTransport of organelles  \n• \nCell division  \nThe three major components are \n• \nMicrofilaments  \n• \nIntermediate filaments  \n• \nMicrotubules  \n \n44. Microfilaments \nMicrofilaments are mainly composed of actin. \nThey are important in \n• \nCell shape  \n• \nCell movement  \n• \nMuscle contraction  \n• \nCytoplasmic streaming  \n• \nCytokinesis  \nThey are the thinnest major cytoskeletal elements. \n \n45. Intermediate Filaments \nIntermediate filaments provide mechanical strength. \nThey help maintain \n• \nCell shape  \n• \nTissue integrity  \n• \nStructural stability  \nDifferent types of intermediate filaments occur in different cell types. \nKeratin is one example of an intermediate filament protein.\n\n46. Microtubules \nMicrotubules are hollow cylindrical structures composed primarily of tubulin. \nThey function in \n• \nIntracellular transport  \n• \nChromosome movement  \n• \nCell division  \n• \nCell shape  \n• \nCilia and flagella formation  \nThey are the largest of the three major cytoskeletal filament types. \n \n47. Centrosome \nThe centrosome is a major microtubule-organising centre in animal cells. \nIt contains a pair of centrioles surrounded by associated proteins. \nCentrosomes contribute to the organisation of the microtubule network and play an important \nrole during cell division. \n \n48. Cilia and Flagella \nCilia and eukaryotic flagella are structures involved in movement. \nThey contain microtubules arranged in a characteristic 9 + 2 pattern. \nThis consists of \n• \nNine outer microtubule doublets  \n• \nTwo central microtubules  \nTheir movement is generated by motor proteins such as dynein. \n \n49. Cilia \nCilia are generally shorter and more numerous than eukaryotic flagella. \nThey can move fluids across cell surfaces.\n\nFor example, cilia in the respiratory tract help move mucus and trapped particles. \n \n50. Eukaryotic Flagella \nEukaryotic flagella are generally longer and fewer in number than cilia. \nA major example is the human sperm cell, which uses a flagellum for movement. \nThe structure of eukaryotic flagella differs fundamentally from bacterial flagella. \n \n51. Endomembrane System \nSeveral eukaryotic organelles function together as the endomembrane system. \nImportant components include \n• \nNuclear envelope  \n• \nEndoplasmic reticulum  \n• \nGolgi apparatus  \n• \nLysosomes  \n• \nVesicles  \n• \nPlasma membrane  \nThese structures cooperate in the synthesis, modification, transport and processing of cellular \nmaterials. \n \n52. Protein Transport Through the Endomembrane System \nA simplified pathway for a secreted protein is \nDNA in nucleus \n↓ \nmRNA \n↓ \nRibosome on rough ER \n↓\n\nProtein enters ER \n↓ \nTransport vesicle \n↓ \nGolgi apparatus \n↓ \nSecretory vesicle \n↓ \nPlasma membrane \n↓ \nExocytosis \nThis is an important example of organelle cooperation. \n \n53. Plant Cells vs Animal Cells \nFeature \nPlant cell \nAnimal cell \nPlasma membrane \nPresent \nPresent \nNucleus \nPresent \nPresent \nMitochondria \nPresent \nPresent \nRibosomes \nPresent \nPresent \nCell wall \nPresent \nAbsent \nChloroplasts \nPresent in photosynthetic cells \nAbsent \nLarge central vacuole \nUsually present \nUsually \nabsent \nPlasmodesmata \nPresent \nAbsent \nCentrosome with \ncentrioles \nGenerally not organised in the same way as animal \ncells \nProminent\n\n54. Prokaryotic vs Eukaryotic Cells \nFeature \nProkaryotic \nEukaryotic \nNucleus \nAbsent \nPresent \nMembrane-bound organelles Absent \nPresent \nDNA \nUsually circular Usually linear chromosomes \nRibosomes \n70S \n80S in cytoplasm \nTypical size \nSmaller \nLarger \nCell division \nBinary fission \nMitosis or meiosis \nCell wall \nCommon \nPresent in plants and fungi, absent in animals \nExamples \nBacteria, Archaea Animals, plants, fungi, protists \n \n55. 70S and 80S Ribosomes \nRibosomes are classified according to their sedimentation coefficients. \nProkaryotic ribosomes \n70S \nComposed of \n50S + 30S \nEukaryotic cytoplasmic ribosomes \n80S \nComposed of \n60S + 40S \nThe S values are not simple arithmetic units, so they should not be added as though they \nrepresent ordinary numerical sizes. \n \n56. Organelles With Their Own DNA\n\nTwo important eukaryotic organelles contain their own genetic material \n• \nMitochondria  \n• \nChloroplasts  \nThey also contain ribosomes. \nThese characteristics support the idea that these organelles evolved from free-living prokaryotic \norganisms that entered into symbiotic relationships with ancestral eukaryotic cells. \n \n57. Endosymbiotic Theory \nThe endosymbiotic theory proposes that mitochondria and chloroplasts originated from free-\nliving prokaryotic organisms that were engulfed by ancestral cells. \nOver evolutionary time, the engulfed organisms developed a permanent symbiotic relationship \nwith the host cell. \nEvidence supporting the theory includes \n• \nCircular DNA  \n• \nRibosomes resembling those of bacteria  \n• \nDivision by a process resembling binary fission  \n• \nDouble membranes  \n• \nSimilarities in molecular characteristics  \n \n58. Organelles and Their Major Functions \nOrganelle \nMajor function \nNucleus \nStores DNA and regulates gene expression \nNucleolus \nProduces rRNA and assembles ribosomal subunits \nRibosome \nProtein synthesis \nRough ER \nProtein synthesis and processing \nSmooth ER \nLipid synthesis, detoxification and calcium storage \nGolgi apparatus Modification, sorting and packaging\n\nOrganelle \nMajor function \nLysosome \nIntracellular digestion and recycling \nPeroxisome \nOxidation and detoxification \nMitochondrion Aerobic respiration and ATP production \nChloroplast \nPhotosynthesis \nVacuole \nStorage and water balance \nCell wall \nSupport and protection \nCytoskeleton \nShape, movement and intracellular organisation \nCentrosome \nMicrotubule organisation \nCilium \nMovement of cell or fluid \nFlagellum \nCell movement \n \n59. Functional Cooperation Between Organelles \nOrganelles do not operate independently. \nFor example, protein production involves cooperation between \nNucleus \n↓ \nGenetic information is transcribed. \nRibosome \n↓ \nProtein is synthesised. \nRough ER \n↓ \nProtein enters the endomembrane system. \nGolgi apparatus \n↓\n\nProtein is modified and sorted. \nVesicles \n↓ \nProtein is transported. \nPlasma membrane \n↓ \nProtein may be secreted or inserted into the membrane. \nThis cooperation allows complex cellular functions to occur efficiently. \n \n60. Key Concept \nThe structure of an organelle is closely related to its function. \nExamples include \nMitochondrial cristae → increased membrane surface area for respiration \nChloroplast thylakoids → provide membrane surface for light-dependent reactions \nRough ER ribosomes → allow synthesis of proteins entering the endomembrane system \nGolgi cisternae → allow sequential modification and sorting \nLarge plant vacuole → storage and maintenance of turgor \nCell wall → mechanical support \nTherefore, understanding organelles requires understanding both their structure and function.	COMPLETED	77	2026-08-19 18:11:14.86836	2026-08-19 18:12:51.882855	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
958	Lesson 8: The cell cycle and cell division - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 8: The cell cycle and cell division	PDF	uploads/pdf/0185d6c381b64067ae8d8b3a8c11a4af.pdf	\N	Lesson 8 — The Cell Cycle and Cell Division \nIntroduction \nThe continuity of life depends on the ability of cells to grow, replicate their genetic material and \ndivide. Cell division allows organisms to grow, replace damaged cells, reproduce and maintain \ntissues. \nThe cell cycle is the ordered sequence of events through which a cell grows, duplicates its \ngenetic material and divides into daughter cells. \nThere are two major types of nuclear division in eukaryotic organisms \nMitosis \nMeiosis \nMitosis generally produces genetically similar daughter cells and is important for growth, repair \nand asexual reproduction. Meiosis produces haploid cells with genetically different combinations \nof chromosomes and is essential for sexual reproduction. \n \n1. The Cell Cycle \nThe cell cycle consists of a sequence of stages through which a cell passes during its lifetime. \nIt can be divided into \n• \nInterphase  \n• \nM phase  \nInterphase consists of \n• \nG₁ phase  \n• \nS phase  \n• \nG₂ phase  \nThe M phase includes nuclear division and cytoplasmic division. \n \n2. Interphase \nInterphase is the period between cell divisions. \nIt is not a resting stage. During interphase, the cell carries out normal metabolic activities, grows \nand prepares for division.\n\nInterphase consists of \nG₁ → S → G₂ \n \n3. G₁ Phase \nThe G₁ phase is the first major stage of interphase. \nDuring G₁ \n• \nThe cell grows.  \n• \nOrganelles increase in number.  \n• \nProteins are synthesised.  \n• \nNormal cellular activities continue.  \n• \nThe cell prepares for DNA replication.  \nSome cells can leave the active cell cycle and enter a specialised state called G₀. \n \n4. G₀ Phase \nCells in G₀ are metabolically active but are not actively progressing through the cell cycle. \nSome cells can re-enter the cell cycle when stimulated. \nOther highly specialised cells may remain in G₀ for long periods. \nThe entry into and exit from G₀ varies between cell types. \n \n5. S Phase \nThe S phase is the stage during which DNA replication occurs. \nEach chromosome is replicated to produce two genetically similar DNA molecules. \nThese replicated copies remain associated as sister chromatids. \nThe amount of DNA in the cell therefore increases during S phase. \n \n6. Chromosome and Chromatid \nThese terms are frequently confused.\n\nA chromosome is a DNA-containing structure associated with proteins. \nAfter DNA replication, each duplicated chromosome consists of two sister chromatids joined at \nthe centromere region. \nBefore replication, a chromosome contains one chromatid. \nAfter replication, one duplicated chromosome contains two sister chromatids. \n \n7. G₂ Phase \nThe G₂ phase occurs after DNA replication. \nDuring G₂ \n• \nThe cell continues to grow.  \n• \nProteins required for division are synthesised.  \n• \nOrganelles and cellular components are prepared for division.  \n• \nDNA is checked for damage.  \n• \nThe cell prepares for mitosis.  \n \n8. M Phase \nThe M phase consists of \n• \nKaryokinesis  \n• \nCytokinesis  \nKaryokinesis refers to division of the nucleus. \nCytokinesis refers to division of the cytoplasm. \nIn mitosis, karyokinesis produces two nuclei with equivalent chromosome sets. \n \n9. Mitosis \nMitosis is nuclear division in which duplicated chromosomes are separated so that each daughter \nnucleus receives an equivalent set of chromosomes. \nMitosis is important for \n• \nGrowth\n\n• \nTissue repair  \n• \nReplacement of cells  \n• \nAsexual reproduction in some organisms  \nMitosis maintains the chromosome number of the parent cell. \n \n10. Stages of Mitosis \nMitosis is conventionally divided into \n1. Prophase  \n2. Metaphase  \n3. Anaphase  \n4. Telophase  \nA useful sequence is \nProphase → Metaphase → Anaphase → Telophase \n \n11. Prophase \nDuring prophase \n• \nChromatin condenses into visible chromosomes.  \n• \nEach chromosome consists of two sister chromatids.  \n• \nThe nucleolus becomes less distinct.  \n• \nThe mitotic spindle begins to form.  \n• \nCentrosomes move toward opposite poles in animal cells.  \nThe spindle consists mainly of microtubules. \n \n12. Prometaphase \nSome descriptions include prometaphase as a separate stage between prophase and metaphase. \nDuring prometaphase \n• \nThe nuclear envelope breaks down.\n\n• \nSpindle microtubules interact with chromosomes.  \n• \nMicrotubules attach to chromosome structures called kinetochores.  \nThis allows chromosomes to be moved and positioned during subsequent stages. \n \n13. Metaphase \nDuring metaphase \n• \nChromosomes become highly condensed.  \n• \nChromosomes align approximately at the equator of the cell.  \n• \nSpindle fibres attach to kinetochores.  \n• \nSister chromatids are connected to spindle microtubules from opposite poles.  \nThe alignment at the equatorial region is called the metaphase plate. \n \n14. Anaphase \nDuring anaphase \n• \nSister chromatids separate.  \n• \nEach separated chromatid becomes an individual chromosome.  \n• \nSpindle microtubules move the chromosomes toward opposite poles.  \nOnce sister chromatids separate, they are considered separate chromosomes. \nThis ensures that each future daughter cell receives one copy of each chromosome. \n \n15. Telophase \nDuring telophase \n• \nChromosomes reach opposite poles.  \n• \nChromosomes begin to decondense.  \n• \nNuclear envelopes reform around the chromosome sets.  \n• \nNucleoli become visible again.  \n• \nThe spindle breaks down.\n\nTwo daughter nuclei are therefore formed. \n \n16. Cytokinesis \nCytokinesis is the division of the cytoplasm. \nThe mechanism differs between animal and plant cells. \n \n17. Cytokinesis in Animal Cells \nIn animal cells, the plasma membrane forms an inward indentation called a cleavage furrow. \nThe furrow deepens until the cytoplasm is separated into two daughter cells. \nA contractile ring containing actin and myosin contributes to this process. \n \n18. Cytokinesis in Plant Cells \nPlant cells cannot simply form a cleavage furrow because their rigid cell walls restrict this type \nof movement. \nInstead, vesicles accumulate at the centre of the cell. \nThese vesicles fuse to form a cell plate. \nThe cell plate develops into a new partition between the daughter cells. \n \n19. Result of Mitosis \nA typical mitotic division produces \nOne parent cell → Two daughter cells \nThe daughter cells generally have \n• \nThe same chromosome number as the parent cell  \n• \nGenetically equivalent chromosome sets, apart from mutations or other sources of \nvariation  \nFor a diploid cell \n2n → 2n + 2n\n\n20. Importance of Mitosis \nMitosis allows multicellular organisms to \n• \nIncrease in size  \n• \nReplace damaged cells  \n• \nReplace worn-out cells  \n• \nMaintain tissues  \n• \nHeal injuries  \nIt is also involved in asexual reproduction in many organisms. \n \n21. Cell Cycle Control \nThe cell cycle must be carefully regulated. \nUncontrolled cell division can lead to abnormal tissue growth and cancer. \nCells contain regulatory mechanisms called checkpoints. \nImportant checkpoints occur around \n• \nG₁  \n• \nG₂  \n• \nM phase  \nThese checkpoints help determine whether the cell is ready to proceed. \n \n22. G₁ Checkpoint \nThe G₁ checkpoint assesses whether conditions are suitable for DNA replication. \nThe cell can evaluate factors such as \n• \nCell size  \n• \nNutrient availability  \n• \nGrowth signals  \n• \nDNA damage  \nIf conditions are unsuitable, progression through the cell cycle may be delayed.\n\n23. G₂ Checkpoint \nThe G₂ checkpoint occurs after DNA replication. \nIt helps ensure that \n• \nDNA replication has been completed  \n• \nDNA damage has been detected and addressed  \n• \nThe cell is prepared for mitosis  \n \n24. M Checkpoint \nThe M checkpoint is associated with chromosome attachment to the spindle. \nIt helps ensure that chromosomes are appropriately attached before sister chromatids separate. \nThis reduces the likelihood of incorrect chromosome distribution. \n \n25. Meiosis \nMeiosis is a specialised form of cell division that produces haploid cells from a diploid cell. \nIt is important for sexual reproduction. \nMeiosis involves \nOne round of DNA replication \nfollowed by \nTwo rounds of nuclear division \nThese divisions are called \nMeiosis I \nand \nMeiosis II \n \n26. Purpose of Meiosis \nMeiosis performs two major functions.\n\nReduction of chromosome number \nIt reduces the chromosome number from diploid to haploid. \nGeneration of genetic variation \nIt creates genetically different cells through processes such as \n• \nCrossing over  \n• \nIndependent assortment  \n• \nRandom fertilisation contributes additional variation after meiosis  \n \n27. Meiosis I \nMeiosis I is called the reductional division. \nHomologous chromosomes separate from each other. \nThe chromosome number is therefore reduced. \nThe stages are \n1. Prophase I  \n2. Metaphase I  \n3. Anaphase I  \n4. Telophase I  \n \n28. Prophase I \nProphase I is the longest and most complex stage of meiosis. \nDuring Prophase I \n• \nChromosomes condense.  \n• \nHomologous chromosomes pair.  \n• \nCrossing over can occur.  \n• \nThe spindle develops.  \n• \nThe nuclear envelope breaks down.  \nProphase I is divided into five substages\n\n• \nLeptotene  \n• \nZygotene  \n• \nPachytene  \n• \nDiplotene  \n• \nDiakinesis  \n \n29. Leptotene \nDuring leptotene \n• \nChromosomes begin to condense.  \n• \nChromosomal threads become visible.  \nAlthough DNA has already replicated, the sister chromatids are closely associated and may not \nbe individually distinguishable at this stage. \n \n30. Zygotene \nDuring zygotene \nHomologous chromosomes begin to pair. \nThis pairing process is called synapsis. \nA protein structure called the synaptonemal complex helps stabilise the pairing of homologous \nchromosomes. \n \n31. Pachytene \nDuring pachytene \n• \nHomologous chromosomes are fully paired.  \n• \nCrossing over occurs between non-sister chromatids.  \nCrossing over involves the exchange of corresponding DNA segments between homologous \nchromosomes. \nThis contributes to genetic variation.\n\n32. Diplotene \nDuring diplotene \n• \nThe synaptonemal complex breaks down.  \n• \nHomologous chromosomes begin to separate.  \n• \nThe points where homologues remain connected become visible as chiasmata.  \nChiasmata represent sites associated with previous crossing-over events. \n \n33. Diakinesis \nDuring diakinesis \n• \nChromosomes become highly condensed.  \n• \nChiasmata become more clearly visible.  \n• \nThe spindle develops.  \n• \nThe nuclear envelope breaks down.  \nThe chromosomes are then prepared for metaphase I. \n \n34. Metaphase I \nDuring metaphase I \n• \nHomologous chromosome pairs align at the equator.  \n• \nEach homologous chromosome is attached to spindle fibres.  \n• \nThe orientation of each homologous pair is random.  \nThis random orientation contributes to independent assortment. \n \n35. Anaphase I \nDuring anaphase I \n• \nHomologous chromosomes separate.  \n• \nOne chromosome from each homologous pair moves toward each pole.  \n• \nSister chromatids remain together.\n\nThis is a major difference between meiosis I and mitosis. \nIn mitosis \nSister chromatids separate. \nIn meiosis I \nHomologous chromosomes separate. \n \n36. Telophase I \nDuring telophase I \n• \nChromosomes reach opposite poles.  \n• \nNuclear envelopes may reform.  \n• \nCytokinesis may occur.  \nThe resulting cells contain one chromosome from each homologous pair. \nHowever, each chromosome still consists of two sister chromatids. \n \n37. Interkinesis \nSome cells enter a short period called interkinesis between meiosis I and meiosis II. \nImportantly, DNA is not replicated again during interkinesis. \nThe cells proceed directly toward the second meiotic division. \n \n38. Meiosis II \nMeiosis II resembles mitosis in several respects. \nIt separates sister chromatids. \nThe stages are \n1. Prophase II  \n2. Metaphase II  \n3. Anaphase II  \n4. Telophase II\n\n39. Prophase II \nDuring prophase II \n• \nChromosomes condense.  \n• \nA spindle forms.  \n• \nThe nuclear envelope breaks down if it had reformed.  \nEach chromosome still consists of two sister chromatids. \n \n40. Metaphase II \nDuring metaphase II \n• \nChromosomes align at the equator.  \n• \nSpindle fibres attach to the sister chromatids.  \nThe chromosomes are positioned for separation. \n \n41. Anaphase II \nDuring anaphase II \n• \nCentromeres separate.  \n• \nSister chromatids move toward opposite poles.  \n• \nEach chromatid becomes an individual chromosome.  \nThis resembles chromosome separation during mitotic anaphase. \n \n42. Telophase II \nDuring telophase II \n• \nChromosomes reach opposite poles.  \n• \nNuclear envelopes reform.  \n• \nChromosomes decondense.  \n• \nCytokinesis occurs.\n\nThe result is the formation of haploid cells. \n \n43. Result of Meiosis \nOne diploid parent cell typically produces \nFour haploid cells \nThe cells are genetically different from one another. \nIn simplified chromosome notation \n2n → n + n + n + n \n \n44. Mitosis vs Meiosis \nFeature \nMitosis \nMeiosis \nNumber of divisions \nOne \nTwo \nDNA replication \nOne \nOne \nDaughter cells \nUsually 2 \nUsually 4 \nChromosome number \nMaintained \nReduced \nHomologous pairing \nAbsent \nPresent \nCrossing over \nNormally absent \nPresent \nGenetic similarity \nUsually similar \nGenetically different \nMain function \nGrowth, repair, cell replacement Sexual reproduction \nSeparation in first division Sister chromatids \nHomologous chromosomes \n \n45. Homologous Chromosomes \nA pair of homologous chromosomes consists of chromosomes that \n• \nHave similar structure  \n• \nCarry the same types of genes at corresponding loci  \n• \nOne is usually inherited from the mother\n\n• \nOne is usually inherited from the father  \nThey may contain different alleles of the same gene. \nHomologous chromosomes are not identical copies. \n \n46. Sister Chromatids \nSister chromatids are replicated copies of the same chromosome. \nThey are produced during DNA replication. \nThey generally contain highly similar DNA sequences. \nSister chromatids remain connected until they separate during \n• \nAnaphase of mitosis  \n• \nAnaphase II of meiosis  \n \n47. Crossing Over \nCrossing over is the exchange of DNA segments between non-sister chromatids of homologous \nchromosomes. \nIt occurs during prophase I of meiosis. \nCrossing over produces new combinations of alleles. \nThis contributes significantly to genetic variation among offspring. \n \n48. Independent Assortment \nDuring metaphase I, homologous chromosome pairs align independently of one another. \nThe orientation of one homologous pair does not determine the orientation of another pair. \nAs a result, different combinations of maternal and paternal chromosomes can enter the gametes. \nThis is known as independent assortment. \n \n49. Genetic Variation Produced by Meiosis \nMeiosis generates genetic variation through\n\nCrossing over \nCreates new combinations of alleles on chromosomes. \nIndependent assortment \nProduces different combinations of homologous chromosomes. \nTogether, these processes contribute to the genetic diversity of sexually reproducing populations. \n \n50. Chromosome Number \nHuman somatic cells are normally diploid. \n2n = 46 \nThey contain \n23 pairs of chromosomes \nHuman gametes are haploid. \nn = 23 \nTherefore \nSperm → 23 chromosomes \nEgg → 23 chromosomes \nDuring fertilisation \n23 + 23 → 46 \nThe diploid chromosome number is restored. \n \n51. Karyokinesis and Cytokinesis \nThese terms should be clearly distinguished. \nKaryokinesis \nDivision of the nucleus. \nCytokinesis \nDivision of the cytoplasm. \nA complete cell division generally requires both processes.\n\n52. Binary Fission \nProkaryotic cells generally reproduce through binary fission rather than mitosis or meiosis. \nDuring binary fission \n• \nDNA is replicated.  \n• \nThe chromosomes move apart.  \n• \nThe cell elongates.  \n• \nA division septum forms.  \n• \nTwo daughter cells are produced.  \nBinary fission is structurally different from eukaryotic mitosis. \n \n53. Significance of Cell Division \nCell division is essential for \n• \nGrowth  \n• \nDevelopment  \n• \nTissue maintenance  \n• \nRepair  \n• \nReplacement of damaged cells  \n• \nReproduction  \n• \nFormation of gametes  \nWithout controlled cell division, multicellular organisms could not develop or maintain their \ntissues. \n \n54. Cell Cycle and Cancer \nCancer is associated with abnormal regulation of cell proliferation. \nMutations can affect genes involved in \n• \nCell-cycle regulation\n\n• \nDNA repair  \n• \nCell death  \n• \nGrowth signalling  \nWhen normal regulatory mechanisms fail, cells may divide uncontrollably. \nThis can result in formation of a tumour. \n \n55. Apoptosis \nApoptosis is a regulated form of programmed cell death. \nIt is an important part of normal development and tissue maintenance. \nApoptosis allows the body to remove \n• \nUnnecessary cells  \n• \nDamaged cells  \n• \nPotentially dangerous cells  \nApoptosis is different from uncontrolled cell death caused by injury. \n \n56. Key Differences Between Meiosis I and Meiosis II \nMeiosis I \nHomologous chromosomes separate. \nChromosome number is reduced. \nMeiosis II \nSister chromatids separate. \nChromosome number is not further reduced. \nTherefore \nMeiosis I → reductional division \nMeiosis II → equational division \n \n57. Sequence of Cell Division\n\nMitosis \nProphase → Metaphase → Anaphase → Telophase → Cytokinesis \nMeiosis I \nProphase I → Metaphase I → Anaphase I → Telophase I \nMeiosis II \nProphase II → Metaphase II → Anaphase II → Telophase II \n \n58. Overall Concept \nThe cell cycle ensures that genetic material is accurately copied and distributed. \nIn mitosis \nDNA replication → chromosome separation → two genetically similar cells \nIn meiosis \nDNA replication → homologous chromosome separation → sister chromatid separation → \nfour genetically different haploid cells \nThe fundamental difference is that mitosis maintains chromosome number, while meiosis \nreduces chromosome number and generates genetic variation.	COMPLETED	78	2026-08-19 18:11:16.418388	2026-08-19 18:12:51.882857	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
959	Lesson 9: Energy relationships and enzymes - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 9: Energy relationships and enzymes	PDF	uploads/pdf/9ea453b667544ad6a1eded1b0476bb5e.pdf	\N	Lesson 9 — Energy Relationships and Enzymes \nIntroduction \nAll living cells require energy to maintain their organisation and perform essential biological \nprocesses. Energy is required for processes such as active transport, movement, growth, \nsynthesis of biological molecules and cellular communication. \nCells obtain energy through metabolic reactions. These reactions are organised into \ninterconnected pathways and are controlled by enzymes. \nEnzymes are biological catalysts that increase the rate of biochemical reactions without being \nconsumed in the reaction. \nUnderstanding energy relationships and enzyme activity is essential for understanding \nmetabolism, cellular respiration, photosynthesis and many other biological processes. \n \n1. Energy in Biological Systems \nEnergy is the capacity to do work. \nLiving organisms require energy to perform biological work. \nExamples include \n• \nSynthesis of proteins  \n• \nDNA replication  \n• \nActive transport  \n• \nMuscle contraction  \n• \nMovement of cells  \n• \nMaintenance of ion gradients  \n• \nGrowth and development  \nEnergy within biological systems can be transformed from one form into another. \n \n2. Forms of Energy \nTwo important forms of energy in biological systems are \nKinetic energy \nPotential energy\n\nKinetic energy \nKinetic energy is associated with movement. \nExamples include \n• \nMovement of molecules  \n• \nMovement of muscles  \n• \nMovement of organisms  \nPotential energy \nPotential energy is stored energy associated with position or chemical structure. \nExamples include \n• \nChemical energy stored in glucose  \n• \nEnergy stored in ATP  \n• \nElectrochemical gradients across membranes  \n \n3. Thermodynamics \nThermodynamics deals with energy transformations. \nTwo fundamental laws are particularly important in biology. \nFirst law of thermodynamics \nSecond law of thermodynamics \n \n4. First Law of Thermodynamics \nThe first law states that \nEnergy cannot be created or destroyed, but it can be transferred or transformed from one \nform to another. \nFor example, during cellular respiration \nChemical energy in glucose → chemical energy in ATP + heat \nThe energy is not destroyed. \nIt is transformed into different forms.\n\n5. Second Law of Thermodynamics \nThe second law states that every energy transfer or transformation increases the overall entropy \nof the universe. \nEntropy can be understood as a measure related to the dispersal of energy and the number of \npossible arrangements of a system. \nBiological organisms maintain internal organisation by continuously obtaining and using energy. \nThey do not violate the second law because energy transformations also result in heat dissipation \nand increased entropy in the surroundings. \n \n6. Free Energy \nThe energy available to perform useful work is called Gibbs free energy. \nIt is represented by \nG \nChanges in free energy are represented by \nΔG \nThe sign of ΔG indicates whether a reaction is energetically favourable in the specified \nconditions. \n \n7. Exergonic Reactions \nAn exergonic reaction releases free energy. \nFor an exergonic reaction \nΔG < 0 \nSuch reactions are thermodynamically favourable under the stated conditions. \nAn example is the overall breakdown of glucose during cellular respiration. \nHowever, an exergonic reaction does not necessarily occur rapidly. \nThe reaction may still require activation energy.\n\n8. Endergonic Reactions \nAn endergonic reaction requires an input of free energy. \nFor an endergonic reaction \nΔG > 0 \nExamples include many biosynthetic processes such as \n• \nProtein synthesis  \n• \nDNA synthesis  \n• \nFormation of complex carbohydrates  \nCells can drive endergonic reactions by coupling them to energy-releasing reactions. \n \n9. Equilibrium \nA chemical reaction can reach a state of equilibrium when the forward and reverse reactions \noccur at equal rates. \nAt equilibrium \nΔG = 0 \nThe concentrations of reactants and products remain relatively constant, although molecular \nreactions continue to occur. \nBiological systems are generally maintained away from equilibrium through continuous energy \nand matter exchange with their surroundings. \n \n10. Metabolism \nMetabolism is the total collection of chemical reactions occurring within an organism or cell. \nMetabolism consists of \nCatabolic reactions \nand \nAnabolic reactions \n \n11. Catabolism\n\nCatabolism involves the breakdown of complex molecules into simpler molecules. \nCatabolic pathways often release energy. \nExamples include \n• \nGlycolysis  \n• \nBreakdown of fatty acids  \n• \nCellular respiration  \nThe released energy can be captured in forms such as ATP and reduced electron carriers. \n \n12. Anabolism \nAnabolism involves the synthesis of complex molecules from simpler molecules. \nAnabolic processes generally require energy. \nExamples include \n• \nProtein synthesis  \n• \nDNA synthesis  \n• \nGlycogen synthesis  \n• \nLipid synthesis  \nEnergy from ATP and other sources can drive these reactions. \n \n13. Coupled Reactions \nCells frequently couple an energy-releasing reaction with an energy-requiring reaction. \nFor example \nATP hydrolysis → energy release \nThis energy can be coupled to \nEndergonic cellular process \nThis allows reactions that would otherwise be energetically unfavourable to proceed. \n \n14. ATP as an Energy Coupling Molecule\n\nATP is an important energy-transfer molecule. \nIts hydrolysis can be represented as \nATP + H₂O → ADP + Pi \nThe free-energy change associated with ATP hydrolysis can be used to drive cellular processes. \nATP therefore connects energy-releasing pathways with energy-requiring pathways. \n \n15. Activation Energy \nEven when a reaction is energetically favourable, it may not occur rapidly. \nA certain minimum amount of energy is required to initiate a chemical reaction. \nThis is called activation energy. \nActivation energy is represented as \nEa \n \n16. Activation Energy and Enzymes \nEnzymes increase the rate of chemical reactions by lowering the activation energy required to \nreach the transition state. \nThey do not change \n• \nThe overall free-energy change of the reaction  \n• \nThe equilibrium position of the reaction  \nThey simply provide an alternative reaction pathway with a lower activation-energy barrier. \n \n17. Enzymes \nAn enzyme is a biological catalyst. \nMost enzymes are proteins, although some RNA molecules can also function as catalysts. \nCatalysts increase reaction rates without being permanently consumed. \nEnzymes are highly important because most biochemical reactions would occur too slowly to \nsupport life under normal cellular conditions without them.\n\n18. Substrates \nThe molecules upon which enzymes act are called substrates. \nAn enzyme binds to its substrate and facilitates its conversion into product or products. \nThe simplified reaction is \nEnzyme + Substrate → Enzyme-substrate complex → Enzyme + Product \nThe enzyme can then participate in another reaction. \n \n19. Active Site \nThe active site is the specific region of an enzyme where substrate binding and catalysis occur. \nThe active site has chemical and structural properties that allow it to interact with particular \nsubstrates. \nThese properties include \n• \nShape  \n• \nCharge  \n• \nPolarity  \n• \nHydrogen-bonding capacity  \n• \nHydrophobic interactions  \n \n20. Enzyme Specificity \nMany enzymes show high specificity for their substrates. \nThis specificity arises because the chemical properties of the active site complement those of the \nsubstrate. \nFor example, an enzyme involved in breaking down a particular substrate may not effectively \nbind unrelated molecules. \nHowever, enzyme specificity is not always absolute. \nSome enzymes can act on groups of structurally related substrates. \n \n21. Lock and Key Model\n\nThe lock and key model proposes that the active site has a relatively rigid shape complementary \nto the substrate. \nThe substrate fits into the active site similarly to a key fitting into a lock. \nThis model helps explain enzyme specificity. \nHowever, it does not completely explain the flexibility of many enzymes. \n \n22. Induced Fit Model \nThe induced fit model provides a more flexible explanation. \nWhen the substrate approaches the enzyme, interactions between the substrate and enzyme can \ncause changes in the enzyme's conformation. \nThe active site adjusts to better interact with the substrate. \nThis can position reactive groups correctly and promote catalysis. \nThe induced fit model is widely used to explain enzyme-substrate interactions. \n \n23. Enzyme-Substrate Complex \nWhen a substrate binds to an enzyme, an enzyme-substrate complex forms. \nThe formation of this complex brings the substrate into an environment favourable for the \nreaction. \nThe enzyme may \n• \nPosition substrates correctly  \n• \nStrain particular bonds  \n• \nProvide suitable chemical groups  \n• \nStabilise the transition state  \n• \nCreate a favourable local environment  \nThese mechanisms help reduce the activation energy. \n \n24. Transition State\n\nThe transition state is a high-energy, unstable state that occurs during conversion of reactants \ninto products. \nEnzymes help stabilise the transition state. \nThis reduces the activation energy required for the reaction. \nThe enzyme therefore provides an alternative pathway with a lower energy barrier. \n \n25. Enzymes Do Not Change ΔG \nA common misconception is that enzymes make an energetically unfavourable reaction \nfavourable. \nThis is incorrect. \nEnzymes lower activation energy, but they do not change the overall free-energy difference \nbetween reactants and products. \nTherefore \nEnzyme → lowers activation energy \nEnzyme → does not change ΔG \n \n26. Cofactors \nSome enzymes require additional non-protein components called cofactors. \nCofactors may be \n• \nInorganic ions  \n• \nOrganic molecules  \nExamples of inorganic cofactors include \n• \nMg²⁺  \n• \nZn²⁺  \n• \nFe²⁺  \nCofactors can be necessary for proper enzyme structure or catalytic activity. \n \n27. Coenzymes\n\nOrganic cofactors are called coenzymes. \nCoenzymes often participate in the transfer of \n• \nElectrons  \n• \nHydrogen atoms  \n• \nChemical groups  \nExamples include \n• \nNAD⁺  \n• \nFAD  \n• \nCoenzyme A  \nMany coenzymes are derived from vitamins. \n \n28. Apoenzyme and Holoenzyme \nThe protein portion of an enzyme without its required cofactor is called an apoenzyme. \nThe complete, functional enzyme consisting of the apoenzyme and its required cofactor is called \na holoenzyme. \nTherefore \nApoenzyme + Cofactor → Holoenzyme \n \n29. Factors Affecting Enzyme Activity \nEnzyme activity is influenced by several factors. \nImportant factors include \n• \nTemperature  \n• \npH  \n• \nSubstrate concentration  \n• \nEnzyme concentration  \n• \nInhibitors  \n• \nCofactor availability\n\nChanges in these factors can alter the rate of enzyme-catalysed reactions. \n \n30. Effect of Temperature \nIncreasing temperature generally increases enzyme activity up to an optimum temperature. \nHigher temperature increases molecular movement and collision frequency. \nHowever, excessive temperature can disrupt the interactions maintaining the enzyme's three-\ndimensional structure. \nThis can cause denaturation. \n \n31. Enzyme Denaturation \nDenaturation is the disruption of an enzyme's functional three-dimensional structure. \nIt can result from factors such as \n• \nHigh temperature  \n• \nExtreme pH  \n• \nCertain chemicals  \nDenaturation can alter the active site and reduce or eliminate enzyme activity. \n \n32. Temperature and Enzyme Activity \nA typical temperature-response curve for an enzyme shows \nLow temperature → low activity \n↓ \nIncreasing temperature → increasing activity \n↓ \nOptimum temperature → maximum activity \n↓ \nExcessive temperature → rapid decrease in activity \nThe exact optimum temperature depends on the enzyme and its biological environment.\n\n33. Effect of pH \nEach enzyme has a range of pH values within which it functions effectively. \nChanges in pH can alter \n• \nIonic interactions  \n• \nHydrogen bonding  \n• \nShape of the active site  \n• \nCharge of amino acid side chains  \nExtreme pH can cause denaturation. \nDifferent enzymes have different optimum pH values. \n \n34. Examples of Enzyme pH Adaptation \nPepsin functions effectively in the acidic environment of the stomach. \nMany enzymes in the small intestine function best in a near-neutral to slightly alkaline \nenvironment. \nThis illustrates how enzyme activity is adapted to the environment in which an enzyme operates. \n \n35. Effect of Substrate Concentration \nWhen enzyme concentration remains constant, increasing substrate concentration generally \nincreases the reaction rate. \nAt low substrate concentration \nMore substrate → more enzyme-substrate complexes → faster reaction \nHowever, the rate eventually reaches a maximum. \n \n36. Enzyme Saturation \nAt sufficiently high substrate concentration, most active sites are occupied. \nThe enzymes become saturated with substrate.\n\nFurther increases in substrate concentration produce little or no additional increase in reaction \nrate. \nThe maximum rate is called Vmax. \n \n37. Michaelis-Menten Concept \nFor many enzyme-catalysed reactions, reaction rate can be described using the Michaelis-Menten \nmodel. \nThe relationship is commonly expressed as \nv = Vmax[S] / (Km + [S]) \nwhere \nv = reaction velocity \nVmax = maximum reaction velocity \n[S] = substrate concentration \nKm = Michaelis constant \n \n38. Km \nKm is the substrate concentration at which the reaction velocity is approximately half of Vmax \nin the standard Michaelis-Menten model. \nTherefore \nWhen [S] = Km \nv = Vmax/2 \nA lower Km often indicates higher apparent affinity between an enzyme and its substrate under \nthe model assumptions, although Km is not universally identical to a simple binding-affinity \nconstant. \n \n39. Effect of Enzyme Concentration \nIf substrate is available in sufficient quantity, increasing enzyme concentration generally \nincreases the maximum reaction rate. \nMore enzyme molecules mean more active sites are available.\n\nTherefore \nMore enzyme → greater potential reaction rate \nprovided that substrate and other conditions are not limiting. \n \n40. Enzyme Inhibition \nAn enzyme inhibitor is a substance that reduces enzyme activity. \nInhibitors can interfere with \n• \nSubstrate binding  \n• \nCatalysis  \n• \nEnzyme structure  \n• \nRegulation of metabolic pathways  \nTwo major reversible inhibition types are \nCompetitive inhibition \nNon-competitive inhibition \nOther forms of inhibition also exist. \n \n41. Competitive Inhibition \nA competitive inhibitor competes with the substrate for access to the active site. \nThe inhibitor and substrate may have structural similarities. \nIf the inhibitor occupies the active site, the substrate cannot bind there at that moment. \n \n42. Competitive Inhibition Characteristics \nCompetitive inhibition can generally be reduced by increasing substrate concentration. \nIn the simple Michaelis-Menten model \nVmax remains unchanged \nKm increases \nThe apparent affinity for substrate is therefore reduced.\n\nA sufficiently high substrate concentration can outcompete the inhibitor. \n \n43. Non-Competitive Inhibition \nIn simple non-competitive inhibition, the inhibitor binds at a site other than the active site. \nThis can alter enzyme conformation and reduce catalytic activity. \nIn the idealised pure non-competitive model \nVmax decreases \nKm remains unchanged \nIncreasing substrate concentration does not fully overcome the inhibition. \n \n44. Allosteric Regulation \nSome enzymes contain regulatory sites separate from the active site. \nThese are called allosteric sites. \nBinding of a regulatory molecule can change the enzyme's conformation and therefore alter its \nactivity. \nThis can result in \n• \nActivation  \n• \nInhibition  \nAllosteric regulation is important in metabolic control. \n \n45. Feedback Inhibition \nIn many metabolic pathways, the final product can inhibit an enzyme involved early in the \npathway. \nThis is called feedback inhibition. \nA simplified pathway is \nA → B → C → D \nIf D accumulates, it may inhibit the enzyme responsible for \nA → B\n\nThis prevents unnecessary production of D. \nFeedback inhibition helps maintain metabolic balance. \n \n46. Irreversible Inhibition \nSome inhibitors bind very strongly or covalently to enzymes. \nThe enzyme may become permanently inactive unless the cell produces a new enzyme molecule. \nThese are examples of irreversible inhibitors. \nSuch inhibition can have serious biological consequences when essential enzymes are affected. \n \n47. Enzyme Regulation \nCells regulate enzymes to ensure that metabolic pathways operate appropriately. \nRegulation can occur through \n• \nAllosteric control  \n• \nCompetitive inhibition  \n• \nCovalent modification  \n• \nChanges in enzyme concentration  \n• \nCompartmentalisation  \n• \nFeedback inhibition  \nThis prevents wasteful use of cellular resources. \n \n48. Enzymes and Metabolic Pathways \nMost metabolic reactions occur as sequences of enzyme-catalysed steps. \nFor example \nA → B → C → D → E \nEach step may be controlled by a different enzyme. \nThis organisation allows the cell to regulate the production and use of metabolic products.\n\n49. Intracellular Compartmentalisation \nEukaryotic cells contain membrane-bound organelles. \nThis allows different metabolic reactions to occur in specialised compartments. \nFor example \nGlycolysis → Cytosol \nKrebs cycle → Mitochondrial matrix \nElectron transport chain → Inner mitochondrial membrane \nLight-dependent photosynthesis → Thylakoid membrane \nThis compartmentalisation improves metabolic organisation. \n \n50. Enzymes and Activation Energy \nConsider a reaction without an enzyme. \nThe activation-energy barrier is relatively high. \nWith an enzyme \nActivation energy is reduced \nThe reaction can therefore occur much faster at the same temperature. \nThe enzyme does not provide energy to the reaction. \nInstead, it provides a lower-energy pathway. \n \n51. Enzyme Specificity and Active-Site Structure \nThe specificity of an enzyme depends on the chemical characteristics of its active site. \nThe active site may contain specific amino acid side chains that interact with the substrate. \nInteractions may include \n• \nHydrogen bonds  \n• \nIonic interactions  \n• \nHydrophobic interactions  \n• \nVan der Waals interactions\n\nThe arrangement of these interactions determines which substrates can bind effectively. \n \n52. Enzyme Activity and Cellular Conditions \nEnzymes function within the controlled environment of cells. \nCells regulate \n• \nTemperature  \n• \npH  \n• \nIon concentration  \n• \nSubstrate availability  \n• \nProduct concentration  \nMaintaining suitable conditions is essential because enzymes are sensitive to changes in their \nenvironment. \n \n53. Energy Relationships and Enzymes \nEnergy and enzymes are closely connected. \nEnergy-releasing metabolic reactions can provide energy for energy-requiring reactions. \nEnzymes control the rates of these reactions. \nTherefore \nMetabolism requires energy transformations \nand \nEnzymes regulate the rates of metabolic reactions \nTogether, these principles explain how cells maintain organised biochemical activity. \n \n54. Important Comparisons \nConcept \nMeaning \nExergonic reaction \nReleases free energy\n\nConcept \nMeaning \nEndergonic reaction \nRequires free energy \nCatabolism \nBreakdown of molecules \nAnabolism \nSynthesis of molecules \nActivation energy \nEnergy barrier required to initiate a reaction \nEnzyme \nBiological catalyst \nSubstrate \nMolecule acted upon by an enzyme \nActive site \nRegion where substrate binding and catalysis occur \nCofactor \nNon-protein component required by some enzymes \nCoenzyme \nOrganic cofactor \nDenaturation \nLoss of functional protein structure \nCompetitive inhibition \nInhibitor competes for active site \nNon-competitive inhibition Inhibitor reduces activity through binding away from the active site \n \n55. Important Relationships \nFree energy \nΔG < 0 → Exergonic \nΔG > 0 → Endergonic \nΔG = 0 → Equilibrium \nEnzyme action \nEnzyme → lowers activation energy \nEnzyme → increases reaction rate \nEnzyme → does not change overall ΔG \nCompetitive inhibition \nVmax → unchanged\n\nKm → increased \nPure non-competitive inhibition \nVmax → decreased \nKm → unchanged \n \n56. Essential Concepts to Remember \nAn enzyme is a biological catalyst. \nThe substrate binds to the enzyme's active site. \nThe enzyme-substrate complex forms. \nThe enzyme stabilises the transition state and lowers activation energy. \nThe reaction proceeds more rapidly. \nThe products are released. \nThe enzyme remains available for another reaction. \nTherefore \nE + S ⇌ ES → E + P \nwhere \nE = enzyme \nS = substrate \nES = enzyme-substrate complex \nP = product \n \n57. Final Summary \nCells constantly transform energy to maintain life. \nThe first law of thermodynamics states that energy is conserved. \nThe second law states that energy transformations increase overall entropy. \nMetabolic reactions can be classified as catabolic or anabolic. \nCatabolism → breakdown and often energy release\n\nAnabolism → synthesis and energy requirement \nExergonic reactions release free energy, while endergonic reactions require free energy. \nATP provides an important mechanism for coupling energy-releasing and energy-requiring \nprocesses. \nEnzymes accelerate biochemical reactions by lowering activation energy. \nThe activity of enzymes depends on factors such as \n• \nTemperature  \n• \npH  \n• \nSubstrate concentration  \n• \nEnzyme concentration  \n• \nCofactors  \n• \nInhibitors  \nEnzyme activity can be regulated through mechanisms including competitive inhibition, \nallosteric regulation and feedback inhibition. \nThe central concept of this lesson is \nCells control their metabolism through energy coupling and enzyme regulation, allowing \nbiochemical reactions to occur rapidly and in a coordinated manner under cellular \nconditions.	COMPLETED	79	2026-08-19 18:11:18.729871	2026-08-19 18:12:51.882858	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
960	Lesson 10: Photosynthesis and cellular respiration - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 10: Photosynthesis and cellular respiration	PDF	uploads/pdf/aba06ea8abd444948ba5007949d0a9ee.pdf	\N	Lesson 10 — Photosynthesis and Cellular Respiration \nIntroduction \nLiving organisms require a continuous supply of energy to carry out cellular activities. Two \nmajor metabolic processes responsible for the acquisition, transformation and utilisation of \nenergy are photosynthesis and cellular respiration. \nPhotosynthesis converts light energy into chemical energy stored in organic molecules. Cellular \nrespiration releases energy from organic molecules and captures much of it in ATP. \nThese processes are closely interconnected in ecosystems. \nPhotosynthesis stores energy in organic molecules. \nCellular respiration releases energy from organic molecules. \n \n1. Photosynthesis \nPhotosynthesis is the process by which photoautotrophic organisms use light energy to \nsynthesise organic compounds from carbon dioxide and water. \nIn plants, photosynthesis occurs mainly in the chloroplasts. \nThe overall simplified equation is \n6CO₂ + 6H₂O + light energy → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ \nThe glucose represented in this equation is a simplified representation of the organic products \nformed through photosynthetic carbon fixation. \n \n2. Importance of Photosynthesis \nPhotosynthesis is essential because it \n• \nConverts solar energy into chemical energy  \n• \nProduces organic compounds  \n• \nProvides energy-rich materials for food webs  \n• \nReleases oxygen into the atmosphere  \n• \nRemoves carbon dioxide from the atmosphere  \n• \nSupports almost all major terrestrial food chains\n\nThe chemical energy initially captured through photosynthesis ultimately supports many forms \nof life. \n \n3. Chloroplast Structure \nPhotosynthesis occurs in specialised organelles called chloroplasts. \nA chloroplast contains \n• \nOuter membrane  \n• \nInner membrane  \n• \nStroma  \n• \nThylakoids  \n• \nGrana  \n• \nStroma lamellae  \nThe two major stages of photosynthesis occur in different regions. \nLight-dependent reactions → Thylakoid membranes \nCalvin cycle → Stroma \n \n4. Photosynthetic Pigments \nPhotosynthetic pigments absorb light energy. \nThe major pigment in plants is chlorophyll. \nImportant chlorophylls include \n• \nChlorophyll a  \n• \nChlorophyll b  \nOther accessory pigments include carotenoids. \nDifferent pigments absorb different wavelengths of visible light. \n \n5. Absorption Spectrum \nAn absorption spectrum shows the wavelengths of light absorbed by a pigment.\n\nChlorophyll absorbs strongly in regions corresponding mainly to blue-violet and red light. \nGreen wavelengths are absorbed less effectively and are therefore reflected or transmitted to a \ngreater extent. \nThis contributes to the characteristic green appearance of plants. \n \n6. Action Spectrum \nAn action spectrum shows the effectiveness of different wavelengths of light in driving \nphotosynthesis. \nThe action spectrum of photosynthesis generally corresponds closely to the absorption properties \nof photosynthetic pigments. \nThis demonstrates that pigments are responsible for capturing the light energy used in \nphotosynthesis. \n \n7. Photosystems \nPhotosynthetic pigments are organised into structures called photosystems within the thylakoid \nmembranes. \nTwo major photosystems participate in oxygenic photosynthesis \nPhotosystem II \nPhotosystem I \nThey contain \n• \nAntenna pigments  \n• \nReaction-centre chlorophyll  \n• \nElectron carriers  \n \n8. Antenna Complex \nThe antenna complex contains pigment molecules that absorb photons. \nThe absorbed energy is transferred between pigment molecules until it reaches the reaction \ncentre.\n\nThis allows light energy captured over a relatively large area to be concentrated at the reaction \ncentre. \n \n9. Reaction Centre \nThe reaction centre contains a specialised chlorophyll molecule. \nWhen it receives sufficient excitation energy, an electron becomes excited and is transferred to a \nprimary electron acceptor. \nThis initiates a series of electron-transfer reactions. \n \n10. Light-Dependent Reactions \nThe light-dependent reactions occur in the thylakoid membranes. \nThey require light energy. \nMajor products include \n• \nATP  \n• \nNADPH  \n• \nOxygen  \nATP and NADPH are subsequently used during the Calvin cycle. \n \n11. Photolysis of Water \nDuring the light-dependent reactions, water is split in a process called photolysis. \nThe simplified reaction is \n2H₂O → 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ + O₂ \nThe electrons replace electrons lost from chlorophyll in Photosystem II. \nThe hydrogen ions contribute to the proton gradient. \nOxygen is released as a by-product. \n \n12. Photosystem II \nPhotosystem II is associated with the beginning of the linear electron flow pathway.\n\nLight energy excites electrons in the reaction centre. \nThe electrons are transferred to an electron transport chain. \nElectrons lost from Photosystem II are replaced by electrons obtained through water splitting. \n \n13. Electron Transport Chain in Photosynthesis \nThe excited electrons pass through a series of electron carriers. \nAs electrons move through the chain, energy is used to transport protons into the thylakoid \nlumen. \nThis produces a proton gradient across the thylakoid membrane. \nThe gradient stores potential energy. \n \n14. Chemiosmosis in Chloroplasts \nThe concentration of protons becomes higher inside the thylakoid lumen than in the stroma. \nProtons move back toward the stroma through an enzyme complex called ATP synthase. \nThe movement of protons provides energy for ATP synthesis. \nThis process is called chemiosmosis. \n \n15. Photophosphorylation \nThe production of ATP using light-driven electron transport is called photophosphorylation. \nThere are two major forms \n• \nNon-cyclic photophosphorylation  \n• \nCyclic photophosphorylation  \n \n16. Non-Cyclic Photophosphorylation \nNon-cyclic electron flow involves \nPhotosystem II → Electron transport chain → Photosystem I → NADPH \nIt results in the production of\n\n• \nATP  \n• \nNADPH  \n• \nOxygen  \nElectrons ultimately originate from water and are transferred to NADP⁺ to form NADPH. \n \n17. Formation of NADPH \nAt the end of linear electron flow, electrons are transferred to NADP⁺. \nNADP⁺ accepts electrons and a proton to form NADPH. \nNADPH acts as a reducing agent during the Calvin cycle. \n \n18. Photosystem I \nPhotosystem I absorbs light and excites electrons that have passed through the electron transport \nsystem. \nThe high-energy electrons can eventually reduce NADP⁺ to NADPH. \nPhotosystem I therefore plays an important role in the production of reducing power. \n \n19. Cyclic Photophosphorylation \nIn cyclic electron flow, electrons from Photosystem I return through electron carriers rather than \nreducing NADP⁺. \nThis process \n• \nProduces ATP  \n• \nDoes not produce NADPH  \n• \nDoes not split water  \n• \nDoes not release oxygen  \nCyclic electron flow can help provide additional ATP when the cell requires more ATP relative to \nNADPH. \n \n20. Comparison of Photophosphorylation\n\nFeature \nNon-cyclic Cyclic \nPhotosystem II \nInvolved \nNot involved \nPhotosystem I \nInvolved \nInvolved \nATP \nProduced Produced \nNADPH \nProduced Not produced \nOxygen \nProduced Not produced \nWater splitting \nOccurs \nDoes not occur \nElectron destination NADP⁺ \nReturns to electron transport pathway \n \n21. Calvin Cycle \nThe Calvin cycle occurs in the chloroplast stroma. \nIt uses \n• \nCO₂  \n• \nATP  \n• \nNADPH  \nto produce carbohydrate precursors. \nThe Calvin cycle does not directly require photons, but it depends on ATP and NADPH \ngenerated by the light-dependent reactions. \n \n22. Three Main Stages of the Calvin Cycle \nThe Calvin cycle can be divided into \n1. Carbon fixation  \n2. Reduction  \n3. Regeneration of RuBP  \n \n23. Carbon Fixation\n\nCarbon fixation incorporates atmospheric CO₂ into an organic molecule. \nThe enzyme RuBisCO catalyses the fixation of CO₂ to ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate, abbreviated \nas RuBP. \nThe unstable intermediate formed rapidly produces molecules of 3-phosphoglycerate, \ncommonly abbreviated as 3-PGA. \n \n24. Reduction Phase \nATP and NADPH generated during the light-dependent reactions are used during the reduction \nstage. \n3-PGA is converted through several steps into glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate, abbreviated as \nG3P. \nG3P is a three-carbon sugar. \nSome G3P can be used to form carbohydrates and other organic compounds. \n \n25. Regeneration of RuBP \nMost of the G3P produced by the cycle is used to regenerate RuBP. \nATP provides energy for this process. \nRuBP can then accept another CO₂ molecule. \nThe cycle therefore continues. \n \n26. Carbon Balance of the Calvin Cycle \nFor the net production of one G3P molecule \n3 CO₂ → 1 net G3P \nThe cycle requires \n9 ATP \nand \n6 NADPH \nfor the fixation and reduction of three CO₂ molecules under the standard accounting convention.\n\nFor one six-carbon sugar equivalent \n6 CO₂ → 1 glucose equivalent \nThe approximate requirement is \n18 ATP \nand \n12 NADPH \n \n27. Relationship Between the Two Stages of Photosynthesis \nThe light-dependent reactions produce \nATP + NADPH \nThe Calvin cycle uses \nATP + NADPH + CO₂ \nThe Calvin cycle returns \nADP + Pi + NADP⁺ \nThese molecules can then be reused in the light-dependent reactions. \nThe two stages are therefore functionally interconnected. \n \n28. Factors Affecting Photosynthesis \nPhotosynthetic rate is influenced by factors such as \n• \nLight intensity  \n• \nCarbon dioxide concentration  \n• \nTemperature  \n• \nWater availability  \n• \nChlorophyll content  \nWhen one factor becomes limiting, increasing another factor may have little effect. \n \n29. Light Intensity\n\nIncreasing light intensity generally increases the rate of photosynthesis up to a certain point. \nAt high light intensity, another factor may become limiting. \nThe photosynthetic rate may therefore reach a plateau. \n \n30. Carbon Dioxide Concentration \nCarbon dioxide is a substrate for the Calvin cycle. \nIncreasing CO₂ concentration can increase photosynthetic rate when CO₂ is limiting. \nEventually, another factor becomes limiting and the rate reaches a maximum under the given \nconditions. \n \n31. Temperature \nPhotosynthesis depends on enzyme-catalysed reactions. \nIncreasing temperature can increase reaction rates up to an optimum. \nBeyond the optimum, excessive temperature can \n• \nReduce enzyme activity  \n• \nIncrease water loss  \n• \nAffect membrane function  \n• \nCause damage to photosynthetic machinery  \n \n32. Cellular Respiration \nCellular respiration is a series of metabolic reactions through which cells extract energy from \norganic molecules. \nIn aerobic respiration, oxygen serves as the final electron acceptor in the electron transport chain. \nThe simplified overall equation is \nC₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + energy \nMuch of the released energy is captured in ATP. \n \n33. Main Stages of Aerobic Respiration\n\nAerobic cellular respiration can be considered in four major stages \n1. Glycolysis  \n2. Pyruvate oxidation  \n3. Krebs cycle  \n4. Electron transport and oxidative phosphorylation  \nGlycolysis occurs in the cytosol. \nThe remaining stages occur mainly within mitochondria in eukaryotic cells. \n \n34. Glycolysis \nGlycolysis is the breakdown of glucose into pyruvate. \nIt occurs in the cytosol. \nGlycolysis does not directly require oxygen. \nOne glucose molecule produces \n2 pyruvate \nThe pathway consists of multiple enzyme-catalysed reactions. \n \n35. Energy Investment Phase \nThe first part of glycolysis requires an investment of ATP. \nATP is used to phosphorylate glucose-derived intermediates. \nThis prepares the molecule for subsequent reactions. \nA total of \n2 ATP \nis invested per glucose molecule. \n \n36. Energy Payoff Phase \nThe later reactions of glycolysis generate \n• \nATP\n\n• \nNADH  \n• \nPyruvate  \nFor each glucose molecule, glycolysis produces \n4 ATP \nbut because 2 ATP were invested earlier, the net ATP gain is \n2 ATP \nIt also produces \n2 NADH \nand \n2 pyruvate \n \n37. Net Products of Glycolysis \nFor one glucose molecule \nGlucose → 2 pyruvate + 2 NADH + net 2 ATP \nGlycolysis therefore provides both a small amount of ATP and reduced electron carriers for \nsubsequent stages. \n \n38. Pyruvate Oxidation \nIn aerobic eukaryotic cells, pyruvate enters the mitochondrial matrix. \nEach pyruvate is converted into acetyl-CoA. \nThis process involves \n• \nRemoval of CO₂  \n• \nOxidation  \n• \nFormation of NADH  \n• \nAttachment of the remaining two-carbon group to coenzyme A  \nFor two pyruvate molecules \n2 pyruvate → 2 acetyl-CoA + 2 CO₂ + 2 NADH\n\n39. Krebs Cycle \nThe Krebs cycle, also called the citric acid cycle or tricarboxylic acid cycle, occurs in the \nmitochondrial matrix in eukaryotic cells. \nIts main function is to oxidise acetyl groups and transfer high-energy electrons to electron \ncarriers. \n \n40. Entry of Acetyl-CoA \nAcetyl-CoA contains a two-carbon acetyl group. \nIt combines with a four-carbon molecule called oxaloacetate. \nThis produces a six-carbon molecule called citrate. \nThe series of reactions that follows regenerates oxaloacetate. \n \n41. Products of One Krebs Cycle Turn \nFor each acetyl-CoA molecule, the cycle produces \n• \n3 NADH  \n• \n1 FADH₂  \n• \n1 ATP or GTP  \n• \n2 CO₂  \nBecause one glucose produces two acetyl-CoA molecules, the cycle operates twice per glucose. \nTherefore, per glucose \n6 NADH \n2 FADH₂ \n2 ATP or GTP \n4 CO₂ \nare produced by the Krebs cycle. \n \n42. Electron Carriers\n\nTwo important electron carriers produced during cellular respiration are \nNADH \nFADH₂ \nThey carry high-energy electrons to the electron transport chain. \nTheir electrons ultimately contribute to ATP production. \n \n43. Electron Transport Chain \nThe electron transport chain is located in the inner mitochondrial membrane. \nIt consists of a series of protein complexes and electron carriers. \nElectrons from NADH and FADH₂ pass through the chain. \nEnergy released during electron transfer is used to pump protons across the inner mitochondrial \nmembrane. \n \n44. Proton Gradient \nElectron transport creates a high concentration of protons in the intermembrane space. \nThis produces an electrochemical gradient. \nThe gradient represents stored potential energy. \nProtons then move back into the mitochondrial matrix through ATP synthase. \n \n45. Oxidative Phosphorylation \nThe production of ATP using energy from the electron transport chain and chemiosmosis is \ncalled oxidative phosphorylation. \nIt includes \nElectron transport \nand \nChemiosmosis \nMost ATP generated during aerobic respiration is produced through oxidative phosphorylation.\n\n46. ATP Synthase \nATP synthase is a membrane-associated enzyme complex. \nProtons flow through ATP synthase down their electrochemical gradient. \nThe energy released drives \nADP + Pi → ATP \nATP synthase therefore converts the energy stored in the proton gradient into chemical energy in \nATP. \n \n47. Oxygen as the Final Electron Acceptor \nAt the end of the electron transport chain, oxygen accepts electrons. \nOxygen combines with electrons and protons to form water. \nThe overall process can be simplified as \nO₂ + electrons + H⁺ → H₂O \nWithout an appropriate final electron acceptor, electron transport would eventually stop. \n \n48. Chemiosmosis in Cellular Respiration \nChemiosmosis refers to the use of an electrochemical proton gradient to drive ATP synthesis. \nIn mitochondria \nElectron transport → proton pumping → proton gradient → ATP synthase → ATP \nThis mechanism is fundamental to oxidative phosphorylation. \n \n49. ATP Yield \nThe exact ATP yield from one glucose molecule can vary depending on the organism, shuttle \nsystems and cellular conditions. \nA commonly used modern estimate for aerobic respiration in eukaryotic cells is approximately \n30–32 ATP per glucose molecule \nOlder textbooks may provide higher fixed values such as 36 or 38 ATP.\n\nThe important concept is that most ATP is generated during oxidative phosphorylation rather \nthan directly during glycolysis or the Krebs cycle. \n \n50. Substrate-Level Phosphorylation \nATP can be produced directly by transferring a phosphate group from a substrate to ADP. \nThis is called substrate-level phosphorylation. \nIt occurs during \n• \nGlycolysis  \n• \nKrebs cycle  \nIt differs from oxidative phosphorylation because it does not depend directly on the electron \ntransport chain and chemiosmosis. \n \n51. Aerobic and Anaerobic Respiration \nAerobic respiration uses oxygen as the terminal electron acceptor in the electron transport \nchain. \nAnaerobic processes occur without oxygen. \nSome organisms use alternative terminal electron acceptors during anaerobic respiration. \nIn many eukaryotic cells, when oxygen is unavailable, fermentation can allow glycolysis to \ncontinue by regenerating NAD⁺. \n \n52. Fermentation \nFermentation allows cells to regenerate NAD⁺ from NADH when oxidative phosphorylation \ncannot proceed normally due to the absence of oxygen. \nTwo common forms are \n• \nLactic acid fermentation  \n• \nAlcoholic fermentation  \n \n53. Lactic Acid Fermentation \nIn lactic acid fermentation\n\nPyruvate + NADH → Lactate + NAD⁺ \nThis allows glycolysis to continue producing ATP. \nThe net ATP yield remains \n2 ATP per glucose \nbecause ATP is generated only through glycolysis. \n \n54. Alcoholic Fermentation \nAlcoholic fermentation occurs in organisms such as yeast. \nPyruvate is converted into \nEthanol + CO₂ \nNADH is oxidised back to NAD⁺. \nThe regenerated NAD⁺ allows glycolysis to continue. \n \n55. Photosynthesis and Respiration \nPhotosynthesis and cellular respiration are closely connected. \nPhotosynthesis \nCO₂ + H₂O + light energy → organic compounds + O₂ \nAerobic respiration \nOrganic compounds + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O + ATP \nThe products of one process can serve as reactants for the other. \n \n56. Comparison of Photosynthesis and Cellular Respiration \nFeature \nPhotosynthesis \nCellular respiration \nMain function \nStores light energy \nReleases energy from organic \nmolecules \nMain organelle in \nplants \nChloroplast \nMitochondrion\n\nFeature \nPhotosynthesis \nCellular respiration \nMajor input \nCO₂, H₂O, light \nOrganic molecules, O₂ \nMajor products \nOrganic compounds, O₂ \nCO₂, H₂O, ATP \nLight required \nYes for light-dependent reactions \nNo \nElectron source \nWater \nOrganic molecules \nFinal electron \nacceptor \nNADP⁺ in linear photosynthetic \nelectron flow \nO₂ in aerobic respiration \n \n57. Energy Flow in Photosynthesis \nThe overall energy transformation is \nLight energy \n↓ \nExcited electrons \n↓ \nElectron transport \n↓ \nATP + NADPH \n↓ \nCarbon fixation \n↓ \nOrganic molecules \nChemical energy is therefore stored in carbon compounds. \n \n58. Energy Flow in Cellular Respiration \nThe overall sequence is \nOrganic molecules\n\n↓ \nGlycolysis \n↓ \nPyruvate oxidation \n↓ \nKrebs cycle \n↓ \nNADH + FADH₂ \n↓ \nElectron transport chain \n↓ \nProton gradient \n↓ \nATP synthesis \nChemical energy stored in organic molecules is progressively transferred into ATP and other \nforms, with some energy released as heat. \n \n59. Relationship Between ATP and NADPH \nATP and NADPH perform different but complementary roles in photosynthesis. \nATP \nProvides energy for cellular reactions. \nNADPH \nProvides reducing power, carrying high-energy electrons for biosynthetic reactions such as the \nreduction phase of the Calvin cycle. \n \n60. Relationship Between NADH and FADH₂ \nDuring cellular respiration \nNADH and FADH₂\n\ntransport high-energy electrons to the electron transport chain. \nTheir oxidation provides energy for proton pumping. \nThe resulting proton gradient drives ATP synthesis. \n \n61. Carbon Dioxide in Photosynthesis and Respiration \nCarbon dioxide has opposite roles in the two processes. \nDuring photosynthesis \nCO₂ → incorporated into organic molecules \nDuring aerobic respiration \nOrganic carbon → CO₂ \nThis contributes to the biological carbon cycle. \n \n62. Oxygen in Photosynthesis and Respiration \nOxygen is produced during oxygenic photosynthesis through the splitting of water. \nOxygen is consumed during aerobic cellular respiration as the final electron acceptor. \nTherefore, photosynthesis and aerobic respiration contribute to the cycling of oxygen between \norganisms and the atmosphere. \n \n63. Photosynthesis and Cellular Respiration in Plants \nPlants perform both processes. \nDuring daylight \nPhotosynthesis and respiration can both occur. \nPhotosynthesis captures light energy. \nRespiration continuously supplies ATP and metabolic intermediates required by cells. \nPhotosynthesis does not replace respiration. \n \n64. Overall Significance\n\nPhotosynthesis is the primary biological process responsible for converting external light energy \ninto chemical energy. \nCellular respiration allows cells to extract usable energy from organic molecules. \nTogether, these processes \n• \nMaintain energy flow through ecosystems  \n• \nSupport food webs  \n• \nContribute to atmospheric gas balance  \n• \nDrive cellular metabolism  \n• \nConnect carbon and energy cycles  \n \n65. Essential Pathway Summary \nPhotosynthesis \nLight → Photosystems → Electron transport → ATP + NADPH → Calvin cycle → \nCarbohydrates \nCellular respiration \nGlucose → Glycolysis → Pyruvate → Acetyl-CoA → Krebs cycle → NADH + FADH₂ → \nElectron transport → Chemiosmosis → ATP \n \n66. Key Concepts \nPhotosynthesis \nOccurs mainly in chloroplasts. \nLight-dependent reactions occur in thylakoid membranes. \nThe Calvin cycle occurs in the stroma. \nWater provides electrons and oxygen is released. \nATP and NADPH produced by light-dependent reactions support carbon fixation and reduction. \nCellular respiration \nGlycolysis occurs in the cytosol. \nPyruvate oxidation and the Krebs cycle occur in the mitochondrial matrix.\n\nThe electron transport chain is located in the inner mitochondrial membrane. \nOxygen is the final electron acceptor in aerobic respiration. \nMost ATP is produced through oxidative phosphorylation. \n \n67. Final Summary \nPhotosynthesis captures light energy and converts it into chemical energy. \nThe light-dependent reactions produce ATP and NADPH while releasing oxygen through water \nsplitting. \nThe Calvin cycle uses ATP and NADPH to incorporate carbon dioxide into organic compounds. \nCellular respiration extracts energy from organic molecules. \nGlycolysis converts glucose into pyruvate. \nPyruvate oxidation produces acetyl-CoA. \nThe Krebs cycle generates reduced electron carriers and a small amount of ATP. \nThe electron transport chain uses electrons from NADH and FADH₂ to establish a proton \ngradient. \nATP synthase uses this gradient to produce ATP. \nThe overall relationship can be summarised as \nPhotosynthesis → stores energy in organic molecules \nCellular respiration → releases energy from organic molecules \nTogether, these processes form a fundamental connection between energy transformation, carbon \ncycling and life.	COMPLETED	80	2026-08-19 18:11:20.462025	2026-08-19 18:12:51.88286	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
