975	Lesson 3: Circulatory systems in animals - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 3: Circulatory systems in animals	PDF	uploads/pdf/6bee278b4ee74d0e87cbeb2a63112a9d.pdf	\N	Lesson 3 — Circulatory Systems in Animals \nIntroduction \nMulticellular animals require efficient transport systems because most cells are too far from the \nexternal environment for direct exchange of substances. \nCirculatory systems transport oxygen, nutrients, hormones, metabolic wastes, heat and other \nsubstances throughout the body. \nAnimals have evolved different types of circulatory systems according to their body size, \nstructure and metabolic requirements. \n \n1. Functions of Circulatory Systems \nThe circulatory system performs several important functions \n• \nTransport of oxygen  \n• \nTransport of carbon dioxide  \n• \nDistribution of nutrients  \n• \nTransport of hormones  \n• \nRemoval of metabolic wastes  \n• \nRegulation of body temperature  \n• \nMaintenance of fluid balance  \n• \nDefence against pathogens  \n• \nDistribution of immune cells  \n• \nTransport of blood-clotting components  \n \n2. Components of a Circulatory System \nA typical circulatory system consists of \n• \nCirculatory fluid  \n• \nPump  \n• \nVessels  \nIn humans\n\nBlood is the circulatory fluid. \nHeart is the pump. \nBlood vessels form the transport network. \n \n3. Open Circulatory Systems \nIn an open circulatory system, circulatory fluid is not continuously confined to blood vessels. \nThe fluid is pumped into body spaces called hemocoels. \nOrgans are directly exposed to the circulating fluid. \nOpen circulatory systems occur in many \n• \nArthropods  \n• \nMolluscs  \n \n4. Closed Circulatory Systems \nIn a closed circulatory system, blood remains within vessels. \nExchange occurs between blood and tissues through the walls of small vessels. \nClosed systems occur in \n• \nAnnelids  \n• \nCephalopods  \n• \nVertebrates  \n \n5. Open and Closed Circulatory Systems \nFeature \nOpen system \nClosed system \nFluid confined to vessels No \nYes \nPressure \nGenerally lower \nGenerally higher \nFlow control \nLess precise \nMore precise \nTransport efficiency \nGenerally lower \nGenerally higher\n\nFeature \nOpen system \nClosed system \nExamples \nInsects, many molluscs Earthworms, vertebrates \n \n6. Advantages of Closed Circulation \nClosed circulatory systems allow \n• \nHigher blood pressure  \n• \nFaster transport  \n• \nMore precise distribution of blood  \n• \nEfficient oxygen delivery  \n• \nGreater control of blood flow to individual organs  \nThis is particularly advantageous for animals with high metabolic demands. \n \n7. Circulatory Systems in Vertebrates \nVertebrate circulatory systems are closed systems. \nThey generally contain \n• \nHeart  \n• \nArteries  \n• \nVeins  \n• \nCapillaries  \n• \nBlood  \nThe heart generates pressure that drives blood through the vessels. \n \n8. Single Circulation \nIn a single circulatory system, blood passes through the heart once during each complete circuit. \nA simplified pathway is \nHeart → Respiratory organ → Body → Heart \nFish possess a single circulatory system.\n\n9. Fish Circulation \nIn fish, the heart generally contains \n• \nOne atrium  \n• \nOne ventricle  \nThe pathway is \nHeart → Gills → Body tissues → Heart \nBlood becomes oxygenated in the gills before travelling to body tissues. \n \n10. Double Circulation \nIn a double circulatory system, blood passes through the heart twice during one complete circuit. \nIt consists of \nPulmonary circulation \nHeart ↔ Lungs \nSystemic circulation \nHeart ↔ Body tissues \nDouble circulation allows oxygenated and deoxygenated blood to be transported through \nseparate circuits. \n \n11. Advantages of Double Circulation \nDouble circulation allows \n• \nHigh pressure to the systemic circulation  \n• \nEfficient oxygen delivery  \n• \nMaintenance of separate pulmonary and systemic pressures  \n• \nSupport for high metabolic activity  \nIt is characteristic of mammals and birds and is also present in modified forms in other \nvertebrates.\n\n12. Human Circulatory System \nThe human circulatory system is a closed double circulatory system. \nIt consists of \n• \nHeart  \n• \nBlood vessels  \n• \nBlood  \nThe two major circuits are \nPulmonary circulation \nSystemic circulation \n \n13. Location of the Human Heart \nThe heart is located in the thoracic cavity between the lungs. \nIt lies slightly to the left of the body's midline. \nIt is enclosed within a protective sac called the pericardium. \n \n14. Pericardium \nThe pericardium surrounds the heart. \nIt \n• \nProtects the heart  \n• \nAnchors it within the thoracic cavity  \n• \nReduces friction during heart movements  \nA small amount of fluid between the membranes allows relatively friction-free movement. \n \n15. Chambers of the Heart \nThe human heart has four chambers \n• \nRight atrium  \n• \nRight ventricle\n\n• \nLeft atrium  \n• \nLeft ventricle  \nThe atria receive blood. \nThe ventricles pump blood out of the heart. \n \n16. Right Atrium \nThe right atrium receives deoxygenated blood from the systemic circulation. \nBlood enters mainly through \n• \nSuperior vena cava  \n• \nInferior vena cava  \nIt then passes into the right ventricle. \n \n17. Right Ventricle \nThe right ventricle pumps deoxygenated blood toward the lungs. \nBlood leaves through the pulmonary artery. \nThe pulmonary artery is unusual because it carries deoxygenated blood. \n \n18. Left Atrium \nThe left atrium receives oxygenated blood from the lungs. \nBlood enters through the pulmonary veins. \nIt then passes into the left ventricle. \n \n19. Left Ventricle \nThe left ventricle pumps oxygenated blood into the systemic circulation. \nBlood leaves through the aorta. \nThe left ventricular wall is thicker than the right ventricular wall because it must generate higher \npressure.\n\n20. Septum \nThe septum is a muscular wall separating the right and left sides of the heart. \nIt helps prevent mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood. \n \n21. Heart Valves \nValves ensure that blood flows in one direction. \nThe major valves are \n• \nTricuspid valve  \n• \nPulmonary semilunar valve  \n• \nMitral valve  \n• \nAortic semilunar valve  \n \n22. Atrioventricular Valves \nThe atrioventricular valves lie between atria and ventricles. \nTricuspid valve \nBetween right atrium and right ventricle. \nMitral valve \nBetween left atrium and left ventricle. \nThey prevent backflow into the atria during ventricular contraction. \n \n23. Semilunar Valves \nThe semilunar valves are located at the exits of the ventricles. \nPulmonary valve \nBetween right ventricle and pulmonary artery. \nAortic valve \nBetween left ventricle and aorta.\n\nThey prevent blood from flowing back into the ventricles after contraction. \n \n24. Chordae Tendineae \nThe atrioventricular valves are connected to chordae tendineae. \nThese attach to papillary muscles. \nDuring ventricular contraction, they help prevent the valves from inverting into the atria. \n \n25. Blood Flow Through the Heart \nThe pathway is \nBody \n↓ \nVenae cavae \n↓ \nRight atrium \n↓ \nTricuspid valve \n↓ \nRight ventricle \n↓ \nPulmonary valve \n↓ \nPulmonary artery \n↓ \nLungs \n↓ \nPulmonary veins \n↓\n\nLeft atrium \n↓ \nMitral valve \n↓ \nLeft ventricle \n↓ \nAortic valve \n↓ \nAorta \n↓ \nBody \n \n26. Coronary Circulation \nThe heart muscle requires its own blood supply. \nThe coronary arteries supply oxygenated blood to cardiac muscle. \nThe coronary veins drain deoxygenated blood. \nCoronary circulation is essential because the myocardium cannot obtain sufficient oxygen \ndirectly from the blood inside the heart chambers. \n \n27. Cardiac Muscle \nThe heart wall contains cardiac muscle tissue called the myocardium. \nCardiac muscle contracts involuntarily and rhythmically. \nSpecialised cardiac cells generate and conduct electrical signals that coordinate the heartbeat. \n \n28. Pacemaker \nThe natural pacemaker of the human heart is the sinoatrial node, or SA node. \nIt is located in the right atrium.\n\nThe SA node generates electrical impulses that initiate each normal heartbeat. \n \n29. Atrioventricular Node \nThe atrioventricular node, or AV node, receives the electrical impulse from the atria. \nIt introduces a brief delay before transmitting the signal to the ventricles. \nThis allows the ventricles time to fill. \n \n30. Cardiac Conduction Pathway \nThe normal sequence is \nSA node → Atria → AV node → AV bundle → Bundle branches → Purkinje fibres → \nVentricles \nThis produces coordinated contraction. \n \n31. Cardiac Cycle \nThe cardiac cycle is one complete sequence of heart contraction and relaxation. \nIt consists broadly of \n• \nAtrial systole  \n• \nVentricular systole  \n• \nDiastole  \n \n32. Systole \nSystole refers to contraction of cardiac chambers. \nDuring ventricular systole \n• \nVentricles contract  \n• \nAtrioventricular valves close  \n• \nSemilunar valves open  \n• \nBlood is ejected into arteries\n\n33. Diastole \nDiastole refers to relaxation of cardiac chambers. \nDuring ventricular diastole \n• \nVentricles relax  \n• \nSemilunar valves close  \n• \nVentricles fill with blood  \n \n34. Heart Sounds \nThe characteristic heart sounds are associated with valve closure. \nFirst heart sound \nMainly caused by closure of the atrioventricular valves. \nSecond heart sound \nMainly caused by closure of the semilunar valves. \n \n35. Heart Rate \nHeart rate is the number of heartbeats per minute. \nIt varies depending on factors such as \n• \nExercise  \n• \nAge  \n• \nEmotions  \n• \nTemperature  \n• \nHormonal activity  \n• \nAutonomic nervous system activity  \n \n36. Stroke Volume \nStroke volume is the volume of blood pumped by one ventricle during a single contraction.\n\nIt is influenced by \n• \nVentricular filling  \n• \nContractility  \n• \nArterial pressure  \n \n37. Cardiac Output \nCardiac output is the volume of blood pumped by one ventricle per minute. \nThe relationship is \nCardiac output = Heart rate × Stroke volume \nDuring exercise, cardiac output generally increases to meet increased tissue demands. \n \n38. Blood Vessels \nThe major blood vessels are \n• \nArteries  \n• \nArterioles  \n• \nCapillaries  \n• \nVenules  \n• \nVeins  \nThey form a continuous network throughout the body. \n \n39. Arteries \nArteries carry blood away from the heart. \nMost arteries carry oxygenated blood. \nThe main exception is the pulmonary artery. \nArteries generally have \n• \nThick walls  \n• \nStrong smooth muscle layers\n\n• \nAbundant elastic tissue  \n• \nRelatively narrow lumens compared with veins  \n \n40. Elastic Arteries \nLarge arteries close to the heart contain substantial elastic tissue. \nWhen the heart contracts, their walls stretch. \nDuring diastole, elastic recoil helps maintain blood flow. \nThe aorta is the largest example. \n \n41. Arterioles \nArterioles are smaller branches of arteries. \nThey contain smooth muscle that can change vessel diameter. \nTheir major functions include \n• \nRegulating blood flow  \n• \nControlling blood pressure  \n• \nDetermining distribution of blood to tissues  \n \n42. Vasoconstriction \nVasoconstriction is narrowing of a blood vessel. \nIt occurs when smooth muscle in the vessel wall contracts. \nEffects can include \n• \nIncreased vascular resistance  \n• \nReduced blood flow through that vessel  \n• \nIncreased blood pressure  \n \n43. Vasodilation \nVasodilation is widening of a blood vessel.\n\nIt occurs when vascular smooth muscle relaxes. \nEffects can include \n• \nReduced resistance  \n• \nIncreased blood flow  \n• \nIncreased delivery of oxygen and nutrients  \n \n44. Capillaries \nCapillaries are the smallest blood vessels. \nTheir walls generally consist of a single layer of endothelial cells. \nThis short diffusion distance makes them highly suitable for exchange. \n \n45. Functions of Capillaries \nCapillaries allow exchange of \n• \nOxygen  \n• \nCarbon dioxide  \n• \nNutrients  \n• \nWater  \n• \nHormones  \n• \nMetabolic wastes  \nbetween blood and tissue fluid. \n \n46. Capillary Beds \nArterioles branch into networks of capillaries called capillary beds. \nThe amount of blood entering a capillary bed can be regulated according to tissue requirements. \nPrecapillary sphincters and changes in arteriole diameter contribute to this regulation in many \ntissues.\n\n47. Veins \nVeins carry blood toward the heart. \nMost veins carry deoxygenated blood. \nThe main exception is the pulmonary veins. \nVeins generally have \n• \nThinner walls  \n• \nLarger lumens  \n• \nLess smooth muscle  \n• \nLess elastic tissue  \nthan corresponding arteries. \n \n48. Venules \nVenules collect blood from capillaries. \nThey merge to form larger veins. \nVenules also participate in the movement of white blood cells from the blood into tissues during \nimmune responses. \n \n49. Valves in Veins \nMany veins contain valves. \nThese prevent the backflow of blood. \nVenous valves are particularly important in the limbs, where blood must return to the heart \nagainst gravity. \n \n50. Venous Return \nBlood returns to the heart through several mechanisms. \nThese include \n• \nSkeletal muscle pump  \n• \nRespiratory pump\n\n• \nVenous valves  \n• \nSympathetic venoconstriction  \nThe skeletal muscle pump is particularly important in the legs. \n \n51. Blood Pressure \nBlood pressure is the force exerted by blood against the walls of blood vessels. \nIt is highest in the arteries close to the heart. \nBlood pressure gradually decreases as blood moves through the circulation. \n \n52. Systolic Pressure \nSystolic pressure is the arterial pressure during ventricular contraction. \nIt represents the higher value of a blood pressure measurement. \n \n53. Diastolic Pressure \nDiastolic pressure is the arterial pressure during ventricular relaxation. \nIt represents the lower value of a blood pressure measurement. \n \n54. Factors Affecting Blood Pressure \nBlood pressure is influenced by \n• \nCardiac output  \n• \nBlood volume  \n• \nBlood viscosity  \n• \nVessel diameter  \n• \nElasticity of arteries  \n• \nPeripheral resistance  \nChanges in these factors can alter blood pressure.\n\n55. Peripheral Resistance \nPeripheral resistance is the resistance that blood encounters as it flows through vessels. \nArterioles are particularly important in regulating peripheral resistance. \nSmall changes in arteriole diameter can produce significant changes in resistance. \n \n56. Capillary Exchange \nSubstances cross capillary walls through processes including \n• \nDiffusion  \n• \nFiltration  \n• \nReabsorption  \n• \nVesicular transport in some tissues  \nThe direction and rate of movement depend on concentration gradients and hydrostatic and \nosmotic pressures. \n \n57. Tissue Fluid \nSome fluid leaves capillaries and enters the spaces surrounding cells. \nThis extracellular fluid is called tissue fluid or interstitial fluid. \nIt provides the immediate environment for exchange between blood and cells. \n \n58. Formation of Tissue Fluid \nAt the arterial end of many capillaries, hydrostatic pressure promotes movement of water and \nsmall dissolved substances out of the blood. \nLarge plasma proteins and blood cells generally remain within the vessels. \n \n59. Return of Tissue Fluid \nNot all filtered fluid returns directly to blood capillaries. \nExcess tissue fluid enters lymphatic vessels. \nThe lymphatic system eventually returns this fluid to the bloodstream.\n\n60. Oxygen Transport \nMost oxygen is transported in blood bound to haemoglobin inside red blood cells. \nA small amount is dissolved directly in plasma. \nHaemoglobin allows blood to transport much more oxygen than plasma alone could carry. \n \n61. Haemoglobin \nHaemoglobin is a protein containing iron-containing haem groups. \nOxygen binds reversibly to haemoglobin. \nThe binding allows oxygen to be picked up in the lungs and released in tissues. \n \n62. Carbon Dioxide Transport \nCarbon dioxide is transported in three major forms \n• \nDissolved in plasma  \n• \nBound to haemoglobin  \n• \nMainly as bicarbonate ions  \nMost carbon dioxide is transported as bicarbonate. \n \n63. Red Blood Cells \nRed blood cells are specialised for gas transport. \nThey have \n• \nBiconcave shape  \n• \nNo nucleus when mature  \n• \nLarge surface-area-to-volume ratio  \n• \nHigh haemoglobin content  \nThese characteristics facilitate efficient oxygen transport.\n\n64. Blood \nBlood consists of \nPlasma \nand formed elements \n• \nRed blood cells  \n• \nWhite blood cells  \n• \nPlatelets  \n \n65. Plasma \nPlasma is the liquid component of blood. \nIt contains \n• \nWater  \n• \nPlasma proteins  \n• \nElectrolytes  \n• \nNutrients  \n• \nHormones  \n• \nDissolved gases  \n• \nMetabolic wastes  \nIt provides the medium for transport throughout the body. \n \n66. White Blood Cells \nWhite blood cells participate in immune defence. \nMajor types include \n• \nNeutrophils  \n• \nLymphocytes  \n• \nMonocytes  \n• \nEosinophils\n\n• \nBasophils  \nThey protect the body against pathogens and abnormal cells. \n \n67. Platelets \nPlatelets are small cell fragments involved in blood clotting. \nWhen a blood vessel is damaged, platelets contribute to formation of a platelet plug and \nactivation of the coagulation system. \n \n68. Blood Clotting \nBlood clotting prevents excessive blood loss after vessel injury. \nThe process involves \n• \nPlatelet activation  \n• \nCoagulation factors  \n• \nFormation of thrombin  \n• \nConversion of fibrinogen to fibrin  \n• \nFormation of a stable clot  \n \n69. Double Circulation in Humans \nHuman circulation can be summarised as \nRight heart → Lungs → Left heart → Body → Right heart \nThe right side pumps blood to the lungs. \nThe left side pumps blood to the rest of the body. \n \n70. Pulmonary Circulation \nPulmonary circulation carries blood between the heart and lungs. \nRight ventricle → Pulmonary artery → Lung capillaries → Pulmonary veins → Left \natrium \nGas exchange occurs in the pulmonary capillaries.\n\n71. Systemic Circulation \nSystemic circulation carries blood between the heart and the rest of the body. \nLeft ventricle → Aorta → Body tissues → Venae cavae → Right atrium \nIt supplies tissues with oxygen and nutrients and removes wastes. \n \n72. Hepatic Portal Circulation \nThe digestive organs have a specialised blood supply. \nAbsorbed nutrients from the digestive tract are transported through the hepatic portal vein to the \nliver before entering the general circulation. \nThis allows the liver to process and regulate absorbed nutrients. \n \n73. Renal Circulation \nThe kidneys receive a substantial proportion of cardiac output. \nBlood enters the kidneys through the renal arteries. \nThe kidneys filter blood and regulate \n• \nWater balance  \n• \nElectrolytes  \n• \nAcid-base balance  \n• \nNitrogenous wastes  \n• \nBlood pressure  \n \n74. Blood Vessel Comparison \nFeature \nArtery \nCapillary \nVein \nDirection \nAway from heart \nExchange network Toward heart \nWall \nThick \nVery thin \nRelatively thin\n\nFeature \nArtery \nCapillary \nVein \nMuscle \nProminent \nMinimal \nLess prominent \nElastic tissue Relatively abundant in large arteries Minimal \nLess \nLumen \nRelatively narrow \nVery narrow \nRelatively wide \nValves \nUsually absent \nAbsent \nCommon in many veins \nPressure \nHigh \nLow \nVery low \n \n75. Open and Closed Circulation Comparison \nFeature \nOpen circulation \nClosed circulation \nCirculatory fluid Bathes organs directly Remains within vessels \nPressure \nGenerally lower \nGenerally higher \nFlow regulation Less precise \nMore precise \nTransport speed Generally slower \nGenerally faster \nEnergy efficiency Generally lower \nGenerally higher \nExamples \nInsects, many molluscs Earthworms, vertebrates \n \n76. Major Circulatory Adaptations \nDifferent animals have different circulatory arrangements. \nFish \nTwo-chambered heart and single circulation. \nAmphibians \nThree-chambered heart and double circulation with incomplete separation. \nMost reptiles \nThree-chambered heart with varying degrees of ventricular separation. \nBirds and mammals\n\nFour-chambered heart and complete separation of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood. \n \n77. Four-Chambered Heart \nA four-chambered heart contains \n• \nTwo atria  \n• \nTwo ventricles  \nIt completely separates oxygenated and deoxygenated blood. \nThis supports high metabolic demands. \nBirds and mammals possess four-chambered hearts. \n \n78. Importance of Circulatory Separation \nComplete separation prevents oxygen-rich and oxygen-poor blood from mixing. \nThis allows highly oxygenated blood to reach tissues. \nIt supports high rates of cellular respiration and sustained activity. \n \n79. Cardiovascular Diseases \nImportant cardiovascular diseases include \n• \nHypertension  \n• \nCoronary artery disease  \n• \nAtherosclerosis  \n• \nHeart failure  \n• \nStroke  \nRisk factors can include \n• \nSmoking  \n• \nPhysical inactivity  \n• \nUnhealthy diet  \n• \nObesity\n\n• \nHigh blood pressure  \n• \nAbnormal blood lipid levels  \n \n80. Atherosclerosis \nAtherosclerosis involves the development of plaques within arterial walls. \nPlaques can narrow the arterial lumen and reduce blood flow. \nIf a plaque ruptures, a blood clot can form and potentially block blood flow. \n \n81. Coronary Artery Disease \nCoronary artery disease occurs when coronary arteries become narrowed or blocked. \nReduced blood supply to cardiac muscle can cause ischaemia. \nSevere or prolonged blockage can result in a myocardial infarction. \n \n82. Hypertension \nHypertension refers to persistently elevated blood pressure. \nLong-term hypertension can damage \n• \nBlood vessels  \n• \nHeart  \n• \nKidneys  \n• \nBrain  \n• \nEyes  \nIt is an important risk factor for cardiovascular disease. \n \n83. Stroke \nA stroke occurs when blood supply to part of the brain is interrupted or when a blood vessel in \nthe brain ruptures. \nMajor types include\n\n• \nIschaemic stroke  \n• \nHaemorrhagic stroke  \nBrain cells are highly dependent on continuous oxygen and glucose delivery. \n \n84. Exercise and Circulation \nDuring exercise \n• \nHeart rate increases  \n• \nStroke volume generally increases  \n• \nCardiac output increases  \n• \nBlood flow to active muscles increases  \n• \nOxygen delivery increases  \nThese changes help meet the increased metabolic demands of exercising tissues. \n \n85. Thermoregulation and Circulation \nBlood circulation contributes to temperature regulation. \nDuring heat stress, increased blood flow to the skin can facilitate heat loss. \nDuring cold conditions, reduced skin blood flow can reduce heat loss. \nThese responses are regulated by the nervous and endocrine systems. \n \n86. Key Terms \nCirculatory system \nA transport system that distributes substances throughout an animal. \nOpen circulatory system \nA system in which circulatory fluid is not continuously confined to vessels. \nClosed circulatory system \nA system in which blood remains within vessels. \nPulmonary circulation\n\nCirculation between the heart and lungs. \nSystemic circulation \nCirculation between the heart and the rest of the body. \nArtery \nBlood vessel carrying blood away from the heart. \nVein \nBlood vessel carrying blood toward the heart. \nCapillary \nSmall vessel specialised for exchange between blood and tissues. \nSystole \nContraction phase of the cardiac cycle. \nDiastole \nRelaxation phase of the cardiac cycle. \nCardiac output \nVolume of blood pumped by one ventricle per minute. \nStroke volume \nVolume of blood ejected by one ventricle during one contraction. \nBlood pressure \nForce exerted by circulating blood against vessel walls. \n \n87. Final Summary \nCirculatory systems allow animals to transport substances efficiently throughout their bodies. \nOpen circulatory systems expose organs directly to circulating fluid, whereas closed circulatory \nsystems keep blood within vessels and allow more precise regulation of blood flow. \nHumans possess a closed double circulatory system consisting of pulmonary and systemic \ncircuits. \nThe four chambers of the heart work together to maintain directional blood flow. Valves prevent \nbackflow, while specialised cardiac cells coordinate rhythmic contraction.\n\nArteries carry blood away from the heart, veins return blood toward the heart and capillaries \nprovide the primary site of exchange between blood and tissues. \nBlood transports oxygen, carbon dioxide, nutrients, hormones, wastes and immune components. \nHaemoglobin enables efficient oxygen transport, while plasma provides the liquid medium for \nmany dissolved substances. \nThe circulatory system also contributes to temperature regulation, immune defence, fluid balance \nand maintenance of homeostasis.	COMPLETED	95	2026-08-19 18:11:34.261682	2026-08-19 18:12:51.882883	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
976	Lesson 4: Lymphatic system and gas exchange - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 4: Lymphatic system and gas exchange	PDF	uploads/pdf/250c59c390fa438eb9b3a03786889c97.pdf	\N	Lesson 4 — Lymphatic System and Gas Exchange \nIntroduction \nAnimals require efficient systems for transporting substances between cells and the external \nenvironment. The lymphatic system maintains tissue-fluid balance and contributes to immune \ndefence, while respiratory systems enable the exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide. \nIn humans, the lymphatic and respiratory systems work closely with the circulatory system to \nmaintain homeostasis. \n \n1. Functions of the Lymphatic System \nThe lymphatic system performs three major functions \n• \nReturning excess tissue fluid to the bloodstream  \n• \nAbsorbing and transporting dietary lipids  \n• \nContributing to immune defence  \nIt consists of lymph, lymphatic vessels and lymphoid organs and tissues. \n \n2. Lymph \nLymph is the fluid contained within lymphatic vessels. \nIt originates from tissue fluid that enters lymphatic capillaries. \nLymph contains \n• \nWater  \n• \nElectrolytes  \n• \nProteins  \n• \nLymphocytes  \n• \nOther immune components  \n• \nAbsorbed dietary lipids in intestinal lymph  \n \n3. Formation of Tissue Fluid \nBlood enters capillaries under pressure.\n\nSome plasma fluid is filtered through capillary walls into the spaces surrounding cells. \nThis forms tissue fluid, which surrounds and bathes body cells. \nTissue fluid allows exchange between blood and cells. \n \n4. Need for the Lymphatic System \nNot all tissue fluid returns directly to blood capillaries. \nExcess fluid must be collected and returned to the circulation. \nWithout an effective lymphatic system, fluid can accumulate within tissues and cause oedema. \n \n5. Lymphatic Capillaries \nLymphatic capillaries are microscopic vessels located within tissues. \nThey have highly permeable walls that allow \n• \nExcess tissue fluid  \n• \nProteins  \n• \nCellular debris  \n• \nCertain cells  \nto enter. \nOnce tissue fluid enters these vessels, it is called lymph. \n \n6. Lymphatic Vessels \nLymphatic capillaries join to form larger lymphatic vessels. \nThese vessels transport lymph toward the heart. \nMany lymphatic vessels contain valves that prevent backward movement of lymph. \n \n7. Movement of Lymph \nThe lymphatic system does not have a central pump equivalent to the heart. \nLymph movement is assisted by\n\n• \nSkeletal muscle contractions  \n• \nRespiratory movements  \n• \nSmooth muscle contraction in lymphatic vessel walls  \n• \nValves  \nThese mechanisms maintain one-way lymph flow. \n \n8. Lymph Nodes \nLymph nodes are small structures located along lymphatic vessels. \nThey filter lymph and contain immune cells. \nLymph nodes contain populations of \n• \nLymphocytes  \n• \nMacrophages  \n• \nAntigen-presenting cells  \nThey provide sites where immune responses can be initiated. \n \n9. Lymphatic Organs \nImportant lymphatic organs and tissues include \n• \nLymph nodes  \n• \nSpleen  \n• \nThymus  \n• \nTonsils  \n• \nBone marrow  \nThey contribute to immune defence and the production, maturation or storage of immune cells. \n \n10. Spleen \nThe spleen is located in the upper left region of the abdomen. \nIt\n\n• \nFilters blood  \n• \nRemoves aged red blood cells  \n• \nStores certain blood cells  \n• \nParticipates in immune responses  \nUnlike lymph nodes, the spleen filters blood rather than lymph. \n \n11. Thymus \nThe thymus is particularly important during childhood. \nIt is a major site for the maturation of T lymphocytes. \nThe thymus gradually decreases in size and activity after puberty. \n \n12. Tonsils \nTonsils are lymphoid tissues located around the entrance to the respiratory and digestive tracts. \nThey help detect and respond to pathogens entering through the mouth and nose. \n \n13. Lymphatic Ducts \nLymph is eventually returned to the bloodstream through large lymphatic ducts. \nThe two major ducts are \n• \nThoracic duct  \n• \nRight lymphatic duct  \nThey empty lymph into large veins near the base of the neck. \n \n14. Thoracic Duct \nThe thoracic duct drains lymph from most of the body. \nIt ultimately empties into the venous circulation near the junction of the left internal jugular and \nleft subclavian veins.\n\n15. Right Lymphatic Duct \nThe right lymphatic duct drains lymph from the right upper region of the body. \nIt empties into the venous circulation near the junction of the right internal jugular and right \nsubclavian veins. \n \n16. Lymphatic System and Fat Absorption \nSpecialised lymphatic vessels called lacteals occur inside intestinal villi. \nThey absorb dietary lipids packaged into chylomicrons. \nThe pathway is \nSmall intestine → Lacteals → Lymphatic vessels → Thoracic duct → Bloodstream \nThis allows most absorbed dietary fats to enter the circulation through the lymphatic system. \n \n17. Oedema \nOedema is the abnormal accumulation of fluid in tissues. \nIt can occur when \n• \nLymphatic drainage is impaired  \n• \nVenous pressure increases  \n• \nPlasma protein concentration decreases  \n• \nCapillary permeability increases  \nPersistent lymphatic obstruction can produce significant swelling. \n \n18. Lymphatic System and Immunity \nThe lymphatic system provides locations where immune cells encounter foreign substances. \nLymph nodes filter lymph and facilitate interactions between \n• \nAntigens  \n• \nLymphocytes  \n• \nMacrophages\n\n• \nAntigen-presenting cells  \nThis contributes to adaptive immune responses. \n \n19. Gas Exchange \nGas exchange is the movement of respiratory gases between an organism and its environment. \nThe major gases involved in animals are \n• \nOxygen  \n• \nCarbon dioxide  \nOxygen is required for aerobic cellular respiration. \nCarbon dioxide is produced as a waste product of cellular respiration. \n \n20. Requirements of an Efficient Gas Exchange Surface \nAn efficient gas exchange surface generally has \n• \nLarge surface area  \n• \nThin barrier  \n• \nMoist surface  \n• \nGood ventilation  \n• \nGood blood supply  \n• \nMaintenance of concentration gradients  \nThese characteristics increase the rate of diffusion. \n \n21. Diffusion \nGas exchange occurs mainly through diffusion. \nDiffusion is the net movement of particles from a region of higher concentration or partial \npressure to a region of lower concentration or partial pressure. \nNo direct expenditure of cellular energy is required.\n\n22. Partial Pressure \nThe movement of respiratory gases depends strongly on differences in partial pressure. \nOxygen moves from areas with higher oxygen partial pressure toward areas with lower oxygen \npartial pressure. \nCarbon dioxide moves in the opposite direction. \n \n23. Respiratory Surfaces in Animals \nDifferent animals have evolved different respiratory structures. \nExamples include \n• \nBody surfaces  \n• \nGills  \n• \nTracheal systems  \n• \nLungs  \nThe structure of the respiratory organ is closely related to the animal's habitat and body \norganisation. \n \n24. Gas Exchange Through Body Surfaces \nSome small animals exchange gases directly across their body surface. \nExamples include \n• \nFlatworms  \n• \nSome annelids  \nThis is possible because their bodies are relatively thin, allowing gases to diffuse over short \ndistances. \n \n25. Cutaneous Respiration \nCutaneous respiration is gas exchange through the skin. \nEarthworms depend heavily on cutaneous gas exchange. \nThe skin must remain moist for efficient diffusion.\n\n26. Gills \nGills are specialised respiratory structures found mainly in aquatic animals. \nThey provide a large surface area for gas exchange with water. \nGills are found in \n• \nFish  \n• \nMany aquatic invertebrates  \n• \nSome amphibian life stages  \n \n27. Fish Gills \nFish gills consist of structures including \n• \nGill arches  \n• \nGill filaments  \n• \nLamellae  \nThe numerous lamellae provide a large surface area for gas exchange. \n \n28. Countercurrent Exchange \nFish gills use a countercurrent exchange system. \nBlood flows through the gill capillaries in the opposite direction to the movement of water over \nthe gill surface. \nThis maintains an oxygen concentration gradient along much of the exchange surface. \nAs a result, efficient oxygen uptake is possible. \n \n29. Advantages of Countercurrent Exchange \nCountercurrent exchange allows \n• \nEfficient oxygen extraction  \n• \nMaintenance of diffusion gradients\n\n• \nContinuous oxygen uptake along the gill surface  \nIt is more effective than a system in which water and blood flow in the same direction. \n \n30. Tracheal System \nInsects use a tracheal system for gas exchange. \nIt consists of \n• \nSpiracles  \n• \nTracheae  \n• \nTracheoles  \nAir travels through branching tubes directly toward tissues. \n \n31. Spiracles \nSpiracles are openings on the body surface of insects. \nThey allow air to enter and leave the tracheal system. \nMany insects can regulate spiracle opening to reduce water loss. \n \n32. Tracheae \nTracheae are larger air-conducting tubes. \nThey branch extensively throughout the body. \nThe branching system ultimately reaches smaller tracheoles near individual cells. \n \n33. Tracheoles \nTracheoles are extremely fine branches of the tracheal system. \nThey extend close to cells and provide a short diffusion distance. \nOxygen can therefore reach tissues without relying primarily on blood for transport. \n \n34. Advantages of the Insect Tracheal System\n\nThe tracheal system provides \n• \nDirect oxygen delivery to tissues  \n• \nShort diffusion distances  \n• \nEfficient gas exchange  \n• \nReduced dependence on circulatory transport for oxygen  \nThis system is particularly effective for small terrestrial arthropods. \n \n35. Vertebrate Lungs \nTerrestrial vertebrates generally use lungs for gas exchange. \nThe lungs provide internal respiratory surfaces protected from excessive water loss. \nMammalian lungs contain millions of microscopic air sacs called alveoli. \n \n36. Human Respiratory System \nThe major structures of the human respiratory system are \n• \nNose and nasal cavity  \n• \nPharynx  \n• \nLarynx  \n• \nTrachea  \n• \nBronchi  \n• \nBronchioles  \n• \nAlveoli  \n• \nLungs  \nThe respiratory system conducts air to the gas exchange surfaces. \n \n37. Nasal Cavity \nThe nasal cavity performs several functions. \nIt\n\n• \nFilters incoming air  \n• \nWarms air  \n• \nHumidifies air  \n• \nContains olfactory receptors  \nMucus traps particles and microorganisms. \n \n38. Pharynx \nThe pharynx serves as a passageway for air. \nIt connects the nasal cavity and mouth with the larynx. \nIt is also involved in the digestive pathway. \n \n39. Larynx \nThe larynx connects the pharynx to the trachea. \nIt \n• \nMaintains an open airway  \n• \nParticipates in sound production  \n• \nHelps protect the lower respiratory tract  \nThe vocal cords are located within the larynx. \n \n40. Trachea \nThe trachea is a tube that conducts air toward the bronchi. \nIts walls contain cartilage rings that help prevent collapse. \nThe inner lining contains mucus-producing cells and ciliated epithelial cells. \n \n41. Ciliated Epithelium \nCiliated cells help move mucus toward the pharynx. \nMucus traps\n\n• \nDust  \n• \nMicroorganisms  \n• \nOther particles  \nThe upward movement of mucus contributes to airway defence. \n \n42. Bronchi \nThe trachea divides into the right and left primary bronchi. \nEach bronchus enters one lung. \nThe bronchi branch repeatedly into smaller airways. \n \n43. Bronchioles \nBronchioles are smaller branches of the bronchial tree. \nThey contain smooth muscle that can regulate airway diameter. \nThe smallest bronchioles lead toward the respiratory regions containing alveoli. \n \n44. Alveoli \nAlveoli are microscopic air sacs where gas exchange occurs. \nThey have \n• \nVery thin walls  \n• \nLarge combined surface area  \n• \nMoist internal surfaces  \n• \nDense capillary networks  \nThese features facilitate rapid diffusion. \n \n45. Alveolar-Capillary Surface \nThe respiratory surface consists mainly of \nAlveolar epithelium → Basement membranes → Capillary endothelium\n\nThe thin barrier minimises the diffusion distance between air and blood. \n \n46. Type I Alveolar Cells \nType I alveolar cells are thin epithelial cells that form most of the alveolar surface. \nTheir thin structure facilitates gas diffusion. \n \n47. Type II Alveolar Cells \nType II alveolar cells produce pulmonary surfactant. \nSurfactant reduces surface tension within alveoli. \nThis helps prevent alveolar collapse, particularly during expiration. \n \n48. Pulmonary Surfactant \nPulmonary surfactant is a mixture containing lipids and proteins. \nIts major function is to reduce surface tension at the air-liquid interface of alveoli. \nThis \n• \nReduces the tendency of alveoli to collapse  \n• \nDecreases the work required for breathing  \n• \nHelps stabilise alveoli  \n \n49. Pleural Membranes \nThe lungs are surrounded by pleural membranes. \nThey consist of \n• \nVisceral pleura  \n• \nParietal pleura  \nA thin layer of pleural fluid lies between them. \nThis reduces friction and helps maintain the mechanical connection between lungs and thoracic \nwall.\n\n50. Ventilation \nVentilation is the movement of air into and out of the lungs. \nIt is commonly described as \n• \nInspiration  \n• \nExpiration  \nVentilation maintains the concentration gradients necessary for gas exchange. \n \n51. Inspiration \nDuring normal inspiration \n• \nDiaphragm contracts and flattens  \n• \nExternal intercostal muscles contract  \n• \nThoracic volume increases  \n• \nIntrapulmonary pressure decreases  \n• \nAir enters the lungs  \n \n52. Expiration \nDuring quiet expiration \n• \nDiaphragm relaxes  \n• \nExternal intercostal muscles relax  \n• \nThoracic volume decreases  \n• \nIntrapulmonary pressure increases  \n• \nAir leaves the lungs  \nForced expiration involves additional respiratory muscles. \n \n53. Diaphragm \nThe diaphragm is a dome-shaped skeletal muscle separating the thoracic and abdominal cavities.\n\nIts contraction increases thoracic volume. \nIts relaxation contributes to a decrease in thoracic volume. \nIt is the primary muscle of quiet breathing. \n \n54. Intrapulmonary Pressure \nIntrapulmonary pressure is the pressure of air inside the lungs. \nAir moves into the lungs when intrapulmonary pressure becomes lower than atmospheric \npressure. \nAir moves out when intrapulmonary pressure becomes higher than atmospheric pressure. \n \n55. Boyle's Law \nFor a given amount of gas at constant temperature, pressure and volume are inversely related. \nTherefore \nVolume increases → Pressure decreases \nVolume decreases → Pressure increases \nThis principle helps explain pulmonary ventilation. \n \n56. External Respiration \nExternal respiration is gas exchange between alveolar air and pulmonary blood. \nOxygen diffuses \nAlveoli → Blood \nCarbon dioxide diffuses \nBlood → Alveoli \n \n57. Internal Respiration \nInternal respiration is gas exchange between systemic blood and body tissues. \nOxygen diffuses\n\nBlood → Tissues \nCarbon dioxide diffuses \nTissues → Blood \n \n58. Cellular Respiration \nCellular respiration occurs within cells and uses oxygen to release energy from organic \nmolecules during aerobic metabolism. \nIt produces \n• \nATP  \n• \nCarbon dioxide  \n• \nWater  \nThe carbon dioxide produced by cells must be transported to the lungs for elimination. \n \n59. Oxygen Transport \nMost oxygen is transported by haemoglobin in red blood cells. \nThe overall pathway is \nAtmospheric oxygen → Alveoli → Blood → Haemoglobin → Tissues → Cells \n \n60. Carbon Dioxide Transport \nCarbon dioxide produced by cellular respiration enters the blood. \nIt is transported mainly as bicarbonate ions. \nThe overall pathway is \nCells → Blood → Lungs → Alveoli → Atmosphere \n \n61. Bohr Effect \nThe affinity of haemoglobin for oxygen decreases when carbon dioxide concentration increases \nand pH decreases. \nThis phenomenon is known as the Bohr effect.\n\nIt promotes oxygen release in metabolically active tissues where carbon dioxide production is \nhigh. \n \n62. Oxygen-Haemoglobin Dissociation \nHaemoglobin binds oxygen reversibly. \nIn the lungs, relatively high oxygen partial pressure favours oxygen loading. \nIn tissues, lower oxygen partial pressure and conditions associated with increased metabolism \nfavour oxygen unloading. \n \n63. Carbon Dioxide and Blood pH \nCarbon dioxide reacts with water to form carbonic acid. \nThis reaction can be represented as \nCO₂ + H₂O ⇌ H₂CO₃ ⇌ H⁺ + HCO₃⁻ \nThis relationship is important in maintaining acid-base balance. \n \n64. Role of Carbonic Anhydrase \nCarbonic anhydrase is an enzyme found in high concentrations within red blood cells. \nIt rapidly catalyses the conversion between carbon dioxide, water and carbonic acid. \nThis facilitates efficient carbon dioxide transport. \n \n65. Bicarbonate Transport \nMost carbon dioxide is transported in the blood as bicarbonate ions. \nInside red blood cells, carbon dioxide is converted into bicarbonate. \nBicarbonate then moves into plasma in exchange for chloride ions. \nThis is known as the chloride shift. \n \n66. Regulation of Breathing \nBreathing is controlled by respiratory centres in the brainstem.\n\nThe system responds particularly strongly to changes related to carbon dioxide and hydrogen ion \nconcentration. \nChemoreceptors provide information about the chemical state of the blood and surrounding \nfluids. \n \n67. Chemoreceptors \nChemoreceptors detect changes associated with \n• \nCarbon dioxide  \n• \nHydrogen ion concentration  \n• \nOxygen levels  \nCentral and peripheral chemoreceptors contribute to regulation of ventilation. \n \n68. Effect of Exercise on Breathing \nDuring exercise \n• \nOxygen consumption increases  \n• \nCarbon dioxide production increases  \n• \nVentilation increases  \n• \nHeart rate increases  \n• \nBlood flow to active tissues increases  \nThese responses help maintain oxygen delivery and carbon dioxide removal. \n \n69. Lymphatic and Respiratory System Relationship \nThe lymphatic and respiratory systems contribute to homeostasis in different but interconnected \nways. \nThe lymphatic system \n• \nReturns excess tissue fluid  \n• \nSupports immune defence  \n• \nTransports absorbed dietary lipids\n\nThe respiratory system \n• \nSupplies oxygen  \n• \nRemoves carbon dioxide  \n• \nContributes to acid-base regulation  \nBoth systems interact with the cardiovascular system. \n \n70. Respiratory Surface Comparison \nAnimal group Respiratory structure Main environment \nFlatworms \nBody surface \nAquatic or moist \nEarthworms \nMoist skin \nTerrestrial \nFish \nGills \nAquatic \nInsects \nTracheal system \nTerrestrial \nMammals \nLungs \nTerrestrial \nBirds \nLungs with air sacs \nTerrestrial \n \n71. Gills and Lungs Comparison \nFeature \nGills \nLungs \nMain environment Aquatic \nMainly terrestrial \nRespiratory medium Water \nAir \nExchange surface \nExternal \nInternal \nMajor example \nFish \nMammals \nSpecial adaptation \nCountercurrent exchange Extensive internal branching and alveoli \n \n72. Factors Affecting Gas Exchange \nGas exchange efficiency depends on\n\n• \nSurface area  \n• \nThickness of exchange membrane  \n• \nConcentration gradients  \n• \nPartial pressure gradients  \n• \nVentilation  \n• \nBlood flow  \n• \nSolubility of gases  \nA large surface area and short diffusion distance increase exchange efficiency. \n \n73. Respiratory Disorders \nExamples include \n• \nAsthma  \n• \nPneumonia  \n• \nChronic obstructive pulmonary disease  \n• \nEmphysema  \n• \nTuberculosis  \nThese conditions can interfere with ventilation or gas exchange. \n \n74. Asthma \nAsthma involves variable narrowing and inflammation of the airways. \nAirway narrowing can result from \n• \nSmooth muscle contraction  \n• \nInflammation  \n• \nIncreased mucus production  \nThis can make breathing difficult. \n \n75. Emphysema\n\nEmphysema involves destruction of alveolar walls and loss of elastic recoil. \nThis reduces the effective surface area available for gas exchange. \nIt is commonly associated with long-term exposure to tobacco smoke. \n \n76. Pneumonia \nPneumonia is an infection or inflammation of lung tissue. \nFluid and inflammatory material can accumulate in alveolar regions, interfering with normal gas \nexchange. \n \n77. Key Terms \nLymph \nFluid transported within lymphatic vessels. \nLymphatic vessel \nA vessel that transports lymph toward the bloodstream. \nLymph node \nA lymphoid structure that filters lymph and contains immune cells. \nGas exchange \nMovement of respiratory gases between an organism and its environment or tissues. \nDiffusion \nNet movement of particles down a concentration or partial-pressure gradient. \nVentilation \nMovement of air into and out of respiratory structures. \nAlveolus \nA microscopic air sac where gas exchange occurs in mammalian lungs. \nSurfactant \nA substance that reduces surface tension in alveoli. \nPulmonary circulation\n\nBlood circulation between the heart and lungs. \nExternal respiration \nGas exchange between alveoli and pulmonary blood. \nInternal respiration \nGas exchange between systemic blood and tissues. \nCutaneous respiration \nGas exchange through the skin. \nCountercurrent exchange \nExchange system in which two fluids flow in opposite directions, maintaining a concentration \ngradient. \n \n78. Final Summary \nThe lymphatic system maintains fluid balance by collecting excess tissue fluid and returning it to \nthe bloodstream. It also contributes to immune defence and transports dietary lipids through \nspecialised lymphatic vessels called lacteals. \nGas exchange allows animals to obtain oxygen and eliminate carbon dioxide. Different animals \nuse different respiratory structures according to their body organisation and environment. \nFish use gills with countercurrent exchange, insects use tracheal systems, and mammals use \nlungs containing extensive networks of alveoli. \nIn humans, ventilation moves air into and out of the lungs. Oxygen diffuses from alveoli into \nblood, while carbon dioxide diffuses from blood into alveoli. Haemoglobin transports most \noxygen, while most carbon dioxide is transported as bicarbonate. \nEfficient gas exchange depends on a large surface area, short diffusion distance, suitable \nmoisture, strong concentration gradients and adequate ventilation and blood flow. \nThe lymphatic, respiratory and cardiovascular systems work together to maintain the internal \nenvironment required for normal cellular activity.	COMPLETED	96	2026-08-19 18:11:35.131975	2026-08-19 18:12:51.882885	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
977	Lesson 5: Immunity and disease defence - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 5: Immunity and disease defence	PDF	uploads/pdf/b33c9c3359a64827af82e1481d7557ec.pdf	\N	Lesson 5 — Immunity and Disease Defence \nIntroduction \nAnimals are continuously exposed to microorganisms and other foreign substances that can \ncause disease. The immune system protects the body by recognising and eliminating harmful \norganisms and abnormal cells. \nHuman immunity can be broadly divided into innate immunity and adaptive immunity. \nInnate immunity provides rapid, non-specific protection, while adaptive immunity produces \nhighly specific responses and immunological memory. \n \n1. Pathogens \nA pathogen is an organism or infectious agent capable of causing disease. \nMajor groups include \n• \nBacteria  \n• \nViruses  \n• \nFungi  \n• \nProtozoans  \n• \nParasitic worms  \nPathogens can enter the body through the respiratory tract, digestive tract, skin, mucous \nmembranes and other routes. \n \n2. Disease \nA disease occurs when normal body functions are disturbed. \nInfectious diseases are caused by pathogens. \nNon-infectious diseases can result from factors such as \n• \nGenetic abnormalities  \n• \nNutritional deficiencies  \n• \nEnvironmental factors  \n• \nMetabolic disorders\n\n• \nAbnormal cell growth  \n \n3. Immune System \nThe immune system consists of cells, tissues, organs and molecules that protect the body. \nImportant components include \n• \nWhite blood cells  \n• \nAntibodies  \n• \nComplement proteins  \n• \nLymphatic vessels  \n• \nLymph nodes  \n• \nSpleen  \n• \nThymus  \n• \nBone marrow  \n \n4. Innate Immunity \nInnate immunity is the body's immediate, non-specific defence system. \nIt is present from birth and does not require previous exposure to a particular pathogen. \nMajor components include \n• \nPhysical barriers  \n• \nChemical barriers  \n• \nPhagocytic cells  \n• \nNatural killer cells  \n• \nComplement proteins  \n• \nInflammatory responses  \n• \nFever  \n \n5. Adaptive Immunity\n\nAdaptive immunity is a specific defence system that develops following exposure to particular \nantigens. \nIt involves mainly \n• \nB lymphocytes  \n• \nT lymphocytes  \n• \nAntibodies  \n• \nMemory cells  \nAdaptive immunity produces immunological memory. \n \n6. First Line of Defence \nThe first line of defence consists mainly of physical and chemical barriers. \nThese prevent pathogens from entering the internal environment. \nExamples include \n• \nSkin  \n• \nMucous membranes  \n• \nMucus  \n• \nCilia  \n• \nTears  \n• \nSaliva  \n• \nStomach acid  \n \n7. Skin \nThe skin provides an important physical barrier. \nIts outer layer contains tightly packed cells and keratin, making penetration difficult for many \nmicroorganisms. \nThe skin also produces substances that inhibit microbial growth. \n \n8. Mucous Membranes\n\nMucous membranes line many body passages. \nThey produce mucus that traps \n• \nMicroorganisms  \n• \nDust particles  \n• \nForeign materials  \nThis helps prevent pathogens from reaching deeper tissues. \n \n9. Cilia \nCiliated epithelial cells occur in parts of the respiratory tract. \nCilia move mucus toward the pharynx. \nTrapped microorganisms and particles can then be removed by coughing, swallowing or other \nmechanisms. \n \n10. Tears and Saliva \nTears and saliva contain antimicrobial substances. \nOne important enzyme is lysozyme, which can damage bacterial cell walls. \nThese secretions therefore contribute to innate defence. \n \n11. Stomach Acid \nThe stomach contains hydrochloric acid. \nThe low pH creates an environment that destroys or inhibits many microorganisms entering with \nfood and water. \nThis provides an important chemical barrier. \n \n12. Normal Microbiota \nThe human body contains communities of microorganisms known as the normal microbiota. \nMany of these microorganisms are harmless or beneficial. \nThey can help protect against pathogens by\n\n• \nCompeting for nutrients  \n• \nOccupying attachment sites  \n• \nProducing inhibitory substances  \n \n13. Second Line of Defence \nIf pathogens pass through external barriers, internal innate defences become important. \nThese include \n• \nPhagocytosis  \n• \nInflammation  \n• \nComplement  \n• \nNatural killer cells  \n• \nFever  \nThese mechanisms act rapidly and are not directed against only one specific pathogen. \n \n14. Phagocytosis \nPhagocytosis is the process by which specialised cells engulf and digest particles. \nImportant phagocytic cells include \n• \nNeutrophils  \n• \nMacrophages  \n• \nDendritic cells  \n \n15. Neutrophils \nNeutrophils are abundant white blood cells involved in rapid responses to infection. \nThey can \n• \nMigrate toward infected tissues  \n• \nEngulf microorganisms  \n• \nRelease antimicrobial substances\n\n• \nParticipate in inflammation  \n \n16. Macrophages \nMacrophages are large phagocytic cells. \nThey \n• \nEngulf pathogens  \n• \nRemove damaged cells  \n• \nRelease signalling molecules  \n• \nPresent antigens to lymphocytes  \nThey are important links between innate and adaptive immunity. \n \n17. Dendritic Cells \nDendritic cells are specialised antigen-presenting cells. \nThey capture antigens and can present them to T lymphocytes. \nThis makes them particularly important in initiating adaptive immune responses. \n \n18. Inflammation \nInflammation is a protective response to tissue injury or infection. \nTypical signs include \n• \nRedness  \n• \nHeat  \n• \nSwelling  \n• \nPain  \nInflammation helps recruit immune cells and molecules to damaged or infected tissues. \n \n19. Inflammatory Response \nDuring inflammation\n\n1. Damaged cells and immune cells release signalling molecules.  \n2. Blood vessels dilate.  \n3. Vessel permeability increases.  \n4. Plasma proteins and immune cells enter the affected tissue.  \n5. Phagocytes migrate toward the site.  \n6. Pathogens and damaged cells are removed.  \n7. Tissue repair begins.  \n \n20. Histamine \nHistamine is an important inflammatory mediator. \nIt can cause \n• \nVasodilation  \n• \nIncreased capillary permeability  \nThis allows immune components to reach affected tissues more efficiently. \n \n21. Fever \nA fever is an elevation of body temperature caused by a regulated change in the body's \ntemperature set point. \nFever can \n• \nEnhance certain immune responses  \n• \nReduce the growth rate of some pathogens  \n• \nIncrease metabolic activity  \nExcessively high body temperature, however, can be harmful. \n \n22. Natural Killer Cells \nNatural killer cells are lymphocytes involved in innate immunity. \nThey can recognise and destroy certain\n\n• \nVirus-infected cells  \n• \nAbnormal cells  \n• \nTumour cells  \nThey induce target-cell death without requiring the highly specific antigen recognition \ncharacteristic of conventional adaptive lymphocytes. \n \n23. Complement System \nThe complement system consists of proteins present mainly in blood plasma. \nComplement contributes to defence through \n• \nPathogen coating  \n• \nInflammation  \n• \nRecruitment of immune cells  \n• \nDirect destruction of certain pathogens  \n \n24. Opsonisation \nOpsonisation occurs when molecules such as complement proteins or antibodies coat a \npathogen. \nThis makes the pathogen easier for phagocytes to recognise and engulf. \n \n25. Membrane Attack Complex \nSome complement proteins assemble into a structure called the membrane attack complex. \nIt forms pores in susceptible target-cell membranes. \nThis can lead to cell lysis. \n \n26. Antigens \nAn antigen is a substance that can be specifically recognised by components of the adaptive \nimmune system. \nAntigens are commonly found on\n\n• \nPathogens  \n• \nForeign cells  \n• \nToxins  \n• \nAbnormal cells  \nSpecific regions of antigens recognised by immune receptors are called epitopes. \n \n27. Lymphocytes \nThe major lymphocytes involved in adaptive immunity are \n• \nB lymphocytes  \n• \nT lymphocytes  \nThey develop from stem cells in the bone marrow. \nB lymphocytes mature in the bone marrow. \nT lymphocytes mature in the thymus. \n \n28. B Lymphocytes \nB lymphocytes are responsible for humoral immunity. \nWhen activated, appropriate B cells can differentiate into \n• \nPlasma cells  \n• \nMemory B cells  \nPlasma cells produce antibodies. \n \n29. Plasma Cells \nPlasma cells are specialised B cells that produce and secrete large quantities of antibodies. \nAntibodies bind specifically to particular antigens. \n \n30. Memory B Cells \nMemory B cells remain in the body after an immune response.\n\nThey allow a faster and stronger response when the same antigen is encountered again. \nThis contributes to long-term immunity. \n \n31. T Lymphocytes \nT lymphocytes are responsible for important aspects of cell-mediated immunity. \nMajor functional groups include \n• \nHelper T cells  \n• \nCytotoxic T cells  \n• \nRegulatory T cells  \n• \nMemory T cells  \n \n32. Helper T Cells \nHelper T cells coordinate immune responses. \nThey release signalling molecules called cytokines that stimulate or regulate other immune cells. \nThey can promote \n• \nB-cell activation  \n• \nCytotoxic T-cell activity  \n• \nMacrophage activity  \n \n33. Cytotoxic T Cells \nCytotoxic T cells destroy infected or abnormal cells. \nThey recognise specific antigen-presenting molecules on target cells. \nThey can induce programmed cell death in the target cell. \n \n34. Regulatory T Cells \nRegulatory T cells help control immune responses. \nThey limit excessive immune activity and contribute to immune tolerance.\n\n35. Memory T Cells \nMemory T cells remain after an adaptive immune response. \nThey allow a faster response during subsequent exposure to the same antigen. \n \n36. Humoral Immunity \nHumoral immunity is the antibody-mediated component of adaptive immunity. \nThe general sequence is \nAntigen recognition → B-cell activation → Clonal expansion → Plasma cell formation → \nAntibody production \nMemory B cells are also generated. \n \n37. Cell-Mediated Immunity \nCell-mediated immunity involves T lymphocytes. \nThe general sequence is \nAntigen presentation → T-cell activation → Clonal expansion → Effector T cells → Target-\ncell destruction or immune regulation \nMemory T cells can also develop. \n \n38. Antibodies \nAntibodies are proteins called immunoglobulins. \nThey are produced by plasma cells. \nEach antibody has specific regions that bind particular antigens. \n \n39. Antibody Structure \nAn antibody generally consists of \n• \nTwo heavy chains  \n• \nTwo light chains\n\n• \nVariable regions  \n• \nConstant regions  \nThe variable regions form antigen-binding sites. \n \n40. Antibody Functions \nAntibodies can \n• \nNeutralise toxins  \n• \nNeutralise viruses  \n• \nAgglutinate pathogens  \n• \nOpsonise pathogens  \n• \nActivate complement  \n• \nPrevent pathogens from attaching to host cells  \n \n41. Neutralisation \nDuring neutralisation, antibodies bind to pathogens or toxins and prevent them from interacting \neffectively with host cells. \nThis reduces their harmful effects. \n \n42. Agglutination \nAgglutination occurs when antibodies bind multiple particles together. \nThis produces clusters that can be more easily removed by phagocytic cells. \n \n43. Clonal Selection \nAdaptive immunity is based on clonal selection. \nEach lymphocyte carries receptors with particular antigen specificity. \nWhen an antigen binds to a matching receptor, the corresponding lymphocyte can become \nactivated. \nIt then undergoes clonal expansion.\n\n44. Clonal Expansion \nClonal expansion is the rapid production of genetically similar lymphocytes from an activated \nlymphocyte. \nThe resulting cells can differentiate into effector and memory cells. \n \n45. Primary Immune Response \nThe primary immune response occurs when the body encounters an antigen for the first time. \nIt generally involves \n• \nInitial recognition  \n• \nLymphocyte activation  \n• \nClonal expansion  \n• \nAntibody production or cellular responses  \n• \nFormation of memory cells  \nThe response is relatively slow compared with subsequent responses. \n \n46. Secondary Immune Response \nThe secondary immune response occurs after subsequent exposure to the same antigen. \nMemory cells respond rapidly. \nThe response is generally \n• \nFaster  \n• \nStronger  \n• \nLonger-lasting  \nthan the primary response. \n \n47. Immunological Memory \nImmunological memory allows the immune system to respond more effectively to previously \nencountered pathogens.\n\nMemory cells can persist for long periods. \nThis is the biological basis of many successful vaccines. \n \n48. Active Immunity \nActive immunity develops when the person's own immune system produces the response. \nIt can occur through \n• \nNatural infection  \n• \nVaccination  \nActive immunity generally produces memory cells. \n \n49. Passive Immunity \nPassive immunity occurs when antibodies are obtained from another source. \nExamples include \n• \nMaternal antibodies transferred to an infant  \n• \nAdministration of antibody-containing preparations  \nPassive immunity acts rapidly but generally does not produce long-term immunological memory. \n \n50. Natural Active Immunity \nNatural active immunity develops after natural exposure to a pathogen. \nThe immune system responds to the pathogen and generates memory cells. \n \n51. Artificial Active Immunity \nArtificial active immunity is produced through vaccination. \nA vaccine exposes the immune system to an antigen or antigenic material without causing the \nfull disease associated with the pathogen. \nThis stimulates immune memory.\n\n52. Natural Passive Immunity \nMaternal antibodies can be transferred to an infant. \nExamples include \n• \nIgG crossing the placenta  \n• \nIgA supplied through breast milk  \nThis provides temporary protection. \n \n53. Artificial Passive Immunity \nArtificial passive immunity occurs when preformed antibodies are administered. \nThis provides immediate protection but usually does not create long-term immune memory. \n \n54. Vaccination \nVaccination is a method of inducing active adaptive immunity. \nA vaccine contains an antigenic component designed to stimulate an immune response. \nPossible vaccine platforms include \n• \nLive attenuated vaccines  \n• \nInactivated vaccines  \n• \nSubunit vaccines  \n• \nToxoid vaccines  \n• \nConjugate vaccines  \n• \nViral-vector vaccines  \n• \nNucleic-acid vaccines  \n \n55. Live Attenuated Vaccines \nThese contain weakened forms of a pathogen. \nThey can produce strong immune responses because the weakened organism can stimulate \nmultiple aspects of immunity.\n\n56. Inactivated Vaccines \nThese contain pathogens that have been rendered unable to replicate. \nThey can stimulate an immune response without causing infection by replication of the pathogen. \n \n57. Subunit Vaccines \nSubunit vaccines contain selected components of a pathogen. \nThese may include specific proteins or other antigenic molecules. \nThey do not contain the complete pathogen. \n \n58. Herd Immunity \nWhen a sufficiently large proportion of a population is immune to an infectious disease, \ntransmission can be reduced. \nThis indirect protection is commonly called herd immunity or population immunity. \nIts effectiveness depends on factors such as \n• \nPathogen transmissibility  \n• \nProportion of immune individuals  \n• \nDuration of immunity  \n• \nPopulation mixing  \n \n59. Antigen-Presenting Cells \nCertain immune cells process antigens and display antigen fragments using major \nhistocompatibility complex molecules. \nImportant antigen-presenting cells include \n• \nDendritic cells  \n• \nMacrophages  \n• \nB lymphocytes  \nThese cells help activate appropriate T lymphocytes.\n\n60. Major Histocompatibility Complex \nMHC molecules display peptide fragments on cell surfaces. \nTwo major classes are \nMHC class I \nFound on most nucleated cells and involved in presenting intracellular antigens to cytotoxic T \ncells. \nMHC class II \nFound mainly on professional antigen-presenting cells and involved in presenting extracellularly \nderived antigens to helper T cells. \n \n61. Immune Tolerance \nThe immune system normally distinguishes self from non-self. \nImmune tolerance prevents strong immune responses against the body's own tissues. \nFailure of tolerance can contribute to autoimmune disease. \n \n62. Autoimmune Diseases \nAn autoimmune disease occurs when the immune system attacks the body's own tissues. \nExamples include \n• \nType 1 diabetes mellitus  \n• \nRheumatoid arthritis  \n• \nSystemic lupus erythematosus  \nThe underlying mechanisms vary between diseases. \n \n63. Allergies \nAn allergy is an inappropriate or excessive immune response to a usually harmless substance \ncalled an allergen. \nCommon allergens include\n\n• \nPollen  \n• \nDust mites  \n• \nCertain foods  \n• \nAnimal allergens  \n• \nInsect venom  \n \n64. Hypersensitivity \nSome immune responses can cause tissue damage rather than providing beneficial protection. \nThese excessive responses are classified as hypersensitivity reactions. \nDifferent types involve different immune mechanisms. \n \n65. Immunodeficiency \nAn immunodeficiency occurs when part of the immune system is absent or functions \ninadequately. \nIt can be \n• \nPrimary  \n• \nSecondary  \nImmunodeficiency increases susceptibility to certain infections. \n \n66. HIV and AIDS \nHuman immunodeficiency virus, or HIV, targets cells involved in immune regulation, \nparticularly CD4 T lymphocytes. \nProgressive damage to the immune system can lead to acquired immunodeficiency syndrome, \nor AIDS. \nWithout effective treatment, susceptibility to opportunistic infections and certain cancers \nincreases. \n \n67. Antibiotics and Immunity\n\nAntibiotics are medicines used to treat susceptible bacterial infections. \nThey do not directly treat viral infections. \nAppropriate antibiotic use is important because unnecessary exposure contributes to the \ndevelopment and spread of antibiotic resistance. \n \n68. Immune Defence Against Bacteria \nBacterial infections can trigger \n• \nInnate immune responses  \n• \nPhagocytosis  \n• \nComplement activation  \n• \nAntibody production  \n• \nT-cell responses  \nThe exact response depends on the bacterial species and its location in the body. \n \n69. Immune Defence Against Viruses \nViruses replicate inside host cells. \nImportant antiviral mechanisms include \n• \nInterferons  \n• \nNatural killer cells  \n• \nCytotoxic T cells  \n• \nNeutralising antibodies  \nAntibodies can prevent viruses from entering cells, while cytotoxic T cells can destroy infected \ncells. \n \n70. Interferons \nInterferons are signalling proteins produced by cells in response to certain infections. \nThey can induce antiviral states in neighbouring cells and influence immune activity.\n\n71. Primary and Secondary Responses \nFeature \nPrimary response Secondary response \nExposure \nFirst exposure \nSubsequent exposure \nResponse speed \nSlower \nFaster \nMagnitude \nGenerally lower \nGenerally higher \nMemory cells \nProduced \nAlready present \nAntibody response Develops gradually Develops rapidly \n \n72. Innate and Adaptive Immunity \nFeature \nInnate immunity \nAdaptive immunity \nSpecificity \nBroad \nHighly specific \nResponse \nRapid \nInitially slower \nMemory \nLimited \nStrong immunological memory \nMajor cells \nPhagocytes, NK cells \nB and T lymphocytes \nRecognition \nGeneral molecular patterns Specific antigens \nPresent from birth Yes \nDevelops through exposure \n \n73. Humoral and Cell-Mediated Immunity \nFeature \nHumoral immunity \nCell-mediated immunity \nMain cells \nB lymphocytes \nT lymphocytes \nMajor effectors Antibodies \nEffector T cells \nMain targets \nExtracellular pathogens and toxins Infected or abnormal cells \nMemory \nMemory B cells \nMemory T cells \n \n74. Importance of the Immune System\n\nThe immune system protects the body by \n• \nPreventing pathogen entry  \n• \nDetecting foreign substances  \n• \nDestroying infectious organisms  \n• \nRemoving damaged cells  \n• \nControlling infections  \n• \nProducing immunological memory  \n• \nDetecting some abnormal cells  \n \n75. Key Terms \nPathogen \nAn infectious agent capable of causing disease. \nAntigen \nA substance specifically recognised by components of the adaptive immune system. \nInnate immunity \nRapid, non-specific defence present from birth. \nAdaptive immunity \nAntigen-specific immunity capable of producing immunological memory. \nAntibody \nAn immunoglobulin produced by plasma cells that binds specifically to an antigen. \nPhagocytosis \nEngulfment and digestion of particles by specialised cells. \nInflammation \nA protective response to tissue injury or infection. \nComplement \nA group of proteins that contributes to innate and adaptive immune defence. \nB lymphocyte\n\nA lymphocyte responsible primarily for antibody-mediated immunity. \nT lymphocyte \nA lymphocyte involved primarily in cell-mediated and immune-regulatory functions. \nVaccine \nA preparation designed to stimulate protective active immunity and immune memory. \nImmunological memory \nThe ability to respond more rapidly and strongly to a previously encountered antigen. \nActive immunity \nImmunity produced by the individual's own immune response. \nPassive immunity \nImmunity obtained through transfer of antibodies from another source. \n \n76. Final Summary \nThe immune system protects the body through coordinated innate and adaptive defence \nmechanisms. \nInnate immunity provides rapid, non-specific protection through barriers, phagocytes, \ninflammation, complement and natural killer cells. \nAdaptive immunity provides highly specific protection through B and T lymphocytes. B cells \nproduce antibodies and memory cells, while T cells coordinate immune responses and destroy \ninfected or abnormal cells. \nThe adaptive immune system uses clonal selection and expansion to produce antigen-specific \neffector cells. Memory cells allow a faster and stronger response during subsequent exposure. \nImmunity can be active or passive and can arise naturally or through medical intervention. \nVaccination produces active immunity and is based on the development of immunological \nmemory. \nThe immune system must also maintain tolerance to the body's own tissues. Failure of regulation \ncan result in autoimmune disease, allergies or other immune disorders. \nOverall, effective disease defence depends on the coordinated action of physical barriers, innate \nimmunity, adaptive immunity and immunological memory.	COMPLETED	97	2026-08-19 18:11:35.972713	2026-08-19 18:12:51.882886	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
978	Lesson 6: Osmoregulation and metabolic excretion - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 6: Osmoregulation and metabolic excretion	PDF	uploads/pdf/d279ad199add4025924de838de440fde.pdf	\N	Lesson 6 — Osmoregulation and Metabolic Excretion \nIntroduction \nAnimals must maintain a stable internal environment despite continuous changes in water \navailability, salt concentration and metabolic activity. \nTwo important processes involved in maintaining this balance are osmoregulation and \nexcretion. \nOsmoregulation controls the water and solute composition of body fluids, while excretion \nremoves metabolic waste products from the body. \n \n1. Osmoregulation \nOsmoregulation is the regulation of water and dissolved solutes in an organism's body fluids. \nIt maintains suitable concentrations of \n• \nWater  \n• \nMineral ions  \n• \nElectrolytes  \nwithin the internal environment. \n \n2. Importance of Osmoregulation \nCells require appropriate water and ion concentrations for normal functioning. \nOsmoregulation helps maintain \n• \nCell volume  \n• \nEnzyme activity  \n• \nMembrane function  \n• \nBlood volume  \n• \nBlood pressure  \n• \nAcid-base balance  \n• \nNormal nervous and muscular activity\n\n3. Osmosis \nOsmosis is the net movement of water across a selectively permeable membrane from a region \nof higher water potential toward a region of lower water potential. \nThe movement continues until the relevant water potential gradient is reduced or balanced by \nopposing forces. \n \n4. Osmotic Pressure \nOsmotic pressure is related to the tendency of a solution to draw water across a selectively \npermeable membrane. \nSolutions containing higher concentrations of dissolved solutes generally have greater osmotic \npressure. \n \n5. Osmoconformers \nSome aquatic animals allow their internal osmotic concentration to change relatively closely with \ntheir environment. \nSuch animals are called osmoconformers. \nMany marine invertebrates are osmoconformers. \n \n6. Osmoregulators \nAnimals that actively maintain their internal osmotic conditions despite environmental changes \nare called osmoregulators. \nExamples include \n• \nFreshwater fish  \n• \nMarine bony fish  \n• \nTerrestrial mammals  \n \n7. Osmoregulation in Freshwater Fish \nFreshwater fish live in an environment with lower solute concentration than their body fluids. \nTherefore\n\n• \nWater tends to enter the body by osmosis  \n• \nSolutes tend to diffuse outward  \nFreshwater fish must prevent excessive water gain and conserve salts. \n \n8. Freshwater Fish Adaptations \nFreshwater fish generally \n• \nProduce large volumes of dilute urine  \n• \nDo not usually drink large amounts of water  \n• \nActively absorb ions through the gills  \n• \nObtain additional ions from food  \nThese adaptations maintain internal osmotic balance. \n \n9. Osmoregulation in Marine Bony Fish \nMarine bony fish live in water with a higher solute concentration than their body fluids. \nTherefore \n• \nWater tends to leave the body  \n• \nSalts tend to enter the body  \nThey must conserve water and remove excess salts. \n \n10. Marine Bony Fish Adaptations \nMarine bony fish generally \n• \nDrink seawater  \n• \nProduce relatively small volumes of urine  \n• \nActively excrete excess salts through gill cells  \n• \nConserve water  \n \n11. Osmoregulation in Cartilaginous Fish\n\nSharks and related cartilaginous fish maintain relatively high concentrations of urea and other \nsolutes in their body fluids. \nThis reduces the osmotic gradient between their tissues and seawater. \nThey also regulate excess ions through specialised organs such as the rectal gland. \n \n12. Terrestrial Osmoregulation \nTerrestrial animals face a major challenge because water can be lost through \n• \nUrine  \n• \nFaeces  \n• \nRespiration  \n• \nSweating  \n• \nEvaporation from body surfaces  \nTherefore, terrestrial animals require mechanisms for conserving water. \n \n13. Nitrogenous Waste \nProtein and nucleic acid metabolism produces nitrogen-containing waste products. \nImportant nitrogenous wastes include \n• \nAmmonia  \n• \nUrea  \n• \nUric acid  \nThese substances differ in toxicity and water requirements for elimination. \n \n14. Ammonia \nAmmonia is highly toxic. \nIt requires large quantities of water for safe elimination. \nMany aquatic animals can excrete ammonia directly because water is readily available.\n\n15. Urea \nUrea is less toxic than ammonia. \nIt requires less water for elimination. \nUrea is the major nitrogenous waste product in \n• \nMammals  \n• \nAdult amphibians  \n• \nMany other terrestrial vertebrates  \n \n16. Uric Acid \nUric acid is relatively insoluble and has low toxicity. \nIt can be eliminated with very little water. \nIt is the major nitrogenous waste in \n• \nBirds  \n• \nReptiles  \n• \nMany insects  \n \n17. Nitrogenous Waste Comparison \nWaste \nToxicity \nWater requirement Major examples \nAmmonia High \nHigh \nMany aquatic animals \nUrea \nModerate to low Moderate \nMammals, adult amphibians \nUric acid Low \nVery low \nBirds, reptiles, insects \n \n18. Excretion \nExcretion is the removal of metabolic waste products from the body. \nExamples include \n• \nCarbon dioxide\n\n• \nNitrogenous wastes  \n• \nExcess water  \n• \nExcess ions  \nExcretion should be distinguished from egestion, which is the removal of undigested food from \nthe digestive tract. \n \n19. Major Excretory Organs in Humans \nThe main organs involved in waste removal include \n• \nKidneys  \n• \nLungs  \n• \nSkin  \n• \nLiver  \nThe kidneys are the principal organs responsible for regulating water, electrolytes and \nnitrogenous wastes. \n \n20. Functions of the Kidneys \nThe kidneys \n• \nRemove nitrogenous wastes  \n• \nRegulate water balance  \n• \nRegulate electrolyte concentrations  \n• \nMaintain acid-base balance  \n• \nHelp regulate blood pressure  \n• \nProduce endocrine signals  \n• \nContribute to red blood cell production regulation  \n \n21. Human Urinary System \nThe urinary system consists of \n• \nTwo kidneys\n\n• \nTwo ureters  \n• \nUrinary bladder  \n• \nUrethra  \nThe kidneys produce urine. \nThe ureters transport urine to the bladder. \nThe bladder stores urine. \nThe urethra carries urine outside the body. \n \n22. Structure of the Kidney \nThe kidney has three major regions \n• \nRenal cortex  \n• \nRenal medulla  \n• \nRenal pelvis  \nThe renal pelvis collects urine and directs it toward the ureter. \n \n23. Nephron \nThe nephron is the functional unit of the kidney. \nEach kidney contains a very large number of nephrons. \nA nephron performs processes including \n• \nFiltration  \n• \nReabsorption  \n• \nSecretion  \nThese processes contribute to urine formation. \n \n24. Main Parts of a Nephron \nA nephron includes \n• \nRenal corpuscle\n\n• \nProximal convoluted tubule  \n• \nLoop of Henle  \n• \nDistal convoluted tubule  \n• \nCollecting duct  \nThe renal corpuscle consists of the glomerulus and Bowman's capsule. \n \n25. Glomerulus \nThe glomerulus is a network of capillaries inside Bowman's capsule. \nBlood enters through the afferent arteriole. \nHigh hydrostatic pressure promotes filtration of water and small solutes from the blood into \nBowman's capsule. \n \n26. Bowman's Capsule \nBowman's capsule surrounds the glomerulus. \nThe fluid filtered from the blood enters the capsular space and then passes into the proximal \nconvoluted tubule. \n \n27. Glomerular Filtration \nGlomerular filtration is the movement of water and small dissolved substances from blood into \nthe nephron. \nNormally filtered substances include \n• \nWater  \n• \nGlucose  \n• \nAmino acids  \n• \nUrea  \n• \nIons  \nLarge plasma proteins and blood cells are normally retained in the blood.\n\n28. Proximal Convoluted Tubule \nThe proximal convoluted tubule is a major site of selective reabsorption. \nSubstances normally reabsorbed include \n• \nGlucose  \n• \nAmino acids  \n• \nSodium ions  \n• \nOther ions  \n• \nWater  \nThe exact extent of reabsorption is regulated according to physiological conditions. \n \n29. Microvilli in the Proximal Tubule \nCells lining the proximal convoluted tubule have numerous microvilli. \nThese increase the surface area available for reabsorption. \nThe cells also contain many mitochondria because active transport requires substantial ATP. \n \n30. Loop of Henle \nThe loop of Henle consists of \n• \nDescending limb  \n• \nAscending limb  \nIt plays an important role in generating the osmotic gradient of the renal medulla. \nThis gradient allows the kidney to produce concentrated urine when necessary. \n \n31. Descending Limb \nThe descending limb is relatively permeable to water. \nWater can leave the tubule by osmosis when the surrounding renal medulla has a lower water \npotential. \nSolute permeability differs from that of the ascending limb.\n\n32. Ascending Limb \nThe ascending limb is relatively impermeable to water. \nIt contributes to the movement of ions into the surrounding medulla. \nThis helps establish the osmotic gradient necessary for urine concentration. \n \n33. Countercurrent Multiplier \nThe opposing flow of fluid through the descending and ascending limbs contributes to the \ncountercurrent multiplication mechanism. \nThis creates and maintains an increasing osmotic gradient toward the inner renal medulla. \n \n34. Distal Convoluted Tubule \nThe distal convoluted tubule contributes to the regulation of \n• \nSodium ions  \n• \nPotassium ions  \n• \nHydrogen ions  \n• \nWater  \n• \nAcid-base balance  \nIts activity is influenced by hormones and the body's physiological requirements. \n \n35. Collecting Duct \nThe collecting duct receives fluid from several nephrons. \nIts permeability to water can be regulated by antidiuretic hormone, or ADH. \nWhen ADH levels are high, more water is reabsorbed and urine becomes more concentrated. \n \n36. Urine Formation \nUrine formation involves three major processes\n\n1. Glomerular filtration  \n2. Selective reabsorption  \n3. Tubular secretion  \nThese processes transform filtered fluid into urine. \n \n37. Selective Reabsorption \nSelective reabsorption returns useful substances from the nephron back into the blood. \nSubstances commonly reabsorbed include \n• \nWater  \n• \nGlucose  \n• \nAmino acids  \n• \nSodium ions  \n• \nOther electrolytes  \nThis prevents unnecessary loss of valuable substances. \n \n38. Tubular Secretion \nTubular secretion is the movement of substances from blood into the nephron tubule. \nSubstances secreted can include \n• \nHydrogen ions  \n• \nPotassium ions  \n• \nCertain drugs  \n• \nMetabolic wastes  \nTubular secretion contributes to waste removal and acid-base regulation. \n \n39. Peritubular Capillaries \nBlood leaving the glomerulus enters vessels associated with the nephron. \nThese include peritubular capillaries and, in juxtamedullary nephrons, the vasa recta.\n\nThey are important for reabsorption and secretion. \n \n40. Vasa Recta \nThe vasa recta are specialised blood vessels associated with long loops of Henle. \nThey help maintain the medullary osmotic gradient while supplying blood to the renal medulla. \n \n41. Antidiuretic Hormone \nADH, also called vasopressin, regulates water reabsorption in the kidneys. \nWhen blood osmolarity increases, ADH secretion generally increases. \nADH promotes water reabsorption in the collecting ducts. \n \n42. Effect of ADH \nHigh ADH concentration \n→ Increased water permeability of collecting ducts \n→ Increased water reabsorption \n→ Smaller volume of concentrated urine \nLow ADH concentration \n→ Reduced water reabsorption \n→ Larger volume of dilute urine \n \n43. Aldosterone \nAldosterone is a steroid hormone produced by the adrenal cortex. \nIt promotes \n• \nSodium ion reabsorption  \n• \nPotassium ion secretion  \nin parts of the distal nephron.\n\nBecause water follows sodium osmotically, aldosterone contributes indirectly to maintaining \nextracellular fluid volume. \n \n44. Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System \nThe renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, or RAAS, helps regulate blood pressure and fluid \nbalance. \nA simplified pathway is \nLow blood pressure or low renal perfusion \n↓ \nRenin release \n↓ \nAngiotensin II formation \n↓ \nVasoconstriction and aldosterone release \n↓ \nIncreased sodium and water retention \n↓ \nIncrease in blood volume and blood pressure \n \n45. Atrial Natriuretic Peptide \nAtrial natriuretic peptide, or ANP, is released by the heart when atrial walls are stretched. \nIt promotes sodium and water excretion and therefore contributes to reducing blood volume and \nblood pressure. \n \n46. Regulation of Blood pH \nThe kidneys contribute to acid-base balance by \n• \nSecreting hydrogen ions  \n• \nReabsorbing bicarbonate\n\n• \nProducing new bicarbonate  \n• \nRegulating urinary acid excretion  \nThis works together with respiratory mechanisms. \n \n47. Kidney and Water Balance \nThe kidneys continuously adjust the amount of water excreted. \nWhen the body is dehydrated \n• \nADH secretion increases  \n• \nWater reabsorption increases  \n• \nUrine volume decreases  \n• \nUrine becomes more concentrated  \nWhen the body has excess water \n• \nADH secretion decreases  \n• \nWater reabsorption decreases  \n• \nUrine volume increases  \n• \nUrine becomes more dilute  \n \n48. Urine Composition \nNormal urine mainly contains \n• \nWater  \n• \nUrea  \n• \nCreatinine  \n• \nUric acid  \n• \nIons  \n• \nOther dissolved waste products  \nNormally, significant amounts of glucose and protein are not present in urine.\n\n49. Why Glucose Is Normally Absent From Urine \nGlucose is freely filtered at the glomerulus. \nUnder normal conditions, almost all filtered glucose is reabsorbed in the proximal convoluted \ntubule. \nTherefore, glucose is normally absent or present only in negligible amounts in urine. \n \n50. Glucose in Urine \nThe presence of significant glucose in urine is called glycosuria. \nIt can occur when blood glucose levels become high enough to exceed the kidney's reabsorptive \ncapacity. \nDiabetes mellitus is a common cause. \n \n51. Kidney and Blood Pressure \nThe kidneys contribute to long-term blood pressure regulation. \nThey regulate \n• \nBlood volume  \n• \nSodium balance  \n• \nWater balance  \n• \nRenin release  \nThrough these mechanisms, the kidneys influence extracellular fluid volume and vascular \npressure. \n \n52. Excretion Through the Lungs \nThe lungs remove \n• \nCarbon dioxide  \n• \nWater vapour  \nCarbon dioxide is produced during cellular respiration. \nIts removal is essential for maintaining blood acid-base balance.\n\n53. Excretion Through the Skin \nThe skin can eliminate \n• \nWater  \n• \nSodium chloride  \n• \nSmall amounts of nitrogenous waste  \nSweat is mainly important for thermoregulation rather than nitrogenous waste removal. \n \n54. Liver and Excretion \nThe liver contributes to waste processing. \nIt \n• \nConverts ammonia into urea  \n• \nDetoxifies many substances  \n• \nProcesses haemoglobin breakdown products  \n• \nExcretes certain substances through bile  \nThe liver therefore works closely with the kidneys in waste management. \n \n55. Urea Formation \nAmino acid metabolism produces ammonia. \nBecause ammonia is highly toxic, the liver converts it into urea through the urea cycle. \nThe urea enters the bloodstream and is subsequently removed mainly by the kidneys. \n \n56. Nitrogen Balance \nThe body obtains nitrogen mainly through dietary proteins. \nNitrogen is required for the synthesis of \n• \nAmino acids  \n• \nProteins\n\n• \nNucleic acids  \n• \nOther nitrogen-containing compounds  \nExcess nitrogen is converted into excretory products. \n \n57. Excretory Structures in Animals \nDifferent animal groups possess different excretory structures. \nExamples include \n• \nContractile vacuoles  \n• \nProtonephridia  \n• \nMetanephridia  \n• \nMalpighian tubules  \n• \nKidneys  \n \n58. Contractile Vacuoles \nFreshwater unicellular organisms may use contractile vacuoles to remove excess water. \nWater enters the cell by osmosis. \nThe contractile vacuole collects and expels excess water. \n \n59. Protonephridia \nProtonephridia are excretory structures found in organisms such as flatworms. \nThey contain specialised cells called flame cells in many species. \nThey help regulate water balance and remove wastes. \n \n60. Metanephridia \nMetanephridia are excretory structures found in annelids and some other invertebrates. \nThey collect coelomic fluid and selectively reabsorb useful substances. \nThe remaining fluid is expelled as urine.\n\n61. Malpighian Tubules \nInsects use Malpighian tubules for excretion and osmoregulation. \nThey remove nitrogenous wastes from haemolymph and contribute to water and ion balance. \nMany insects excrete uric acid, conserving water. \n \n62. Comparative Excretory Systems \nAnimal group \nMain excretory structure Major nitrogenous waste \nFreshwater protozoans Contractile vacuole \nAmmonia \nFlatworms \nProtonephridia \nMainly ammonia \nAnnelids \nMetanephridia \nMainly ammonia or urea \nInsects \nMalpighian tubules \nUric acid \nMammals \nKidneys \nUrea \n \n63. CKDu in Sri Lanka \nChronic kidney disease of uncertain or unknown aetiology, commonly abbreviated CKDu, \nrefers to chronic kidney disease occurring in some populations where the usual major causes of \nchronic kidney disease do not fully explain the condition. \nIt has been an important public-health concern in parts of Sri Lanka, particularly in some \nagricultural communities. \n \n64. Features Associated With CKDu \nCKDu involves progressive impairment of kidney function. \nAs kidney function declines, the body's ability to regulate \n• \nWater  \n• \nElectrolytes  \n• \nAcid-base balance\n\n• \nNitrogenous wastes  \ncan become impaired. \n \n65. Possible Risk Factors for CKDu \nResearch into CKDu in Sri Lanka has investigated multiple possible contributing factors. \nThese have included \n• \nEnvironmental exposures  \n• \nOccupational factors  \n• \nAgricultural conditions  \n• \nHeat stress and dehydration  \n• \nPossible exposure to nephrotoxic substances  \n• \nDietary and lifestyle factors  \n• \nWater quality  \nThe precise causes and relative importance of these factors remain an area of scientific \ninvestigation. \n \n66. Kidney Failure \nSevere loss of kidney function can result in kidney failure. \nWaste products and excess fluid accumulate in the body. \nMajor consequences can include disturbances in \n• \nElectrolyte balance  \n• \nAcid-base balance  \n• \nFluid balance  \n• \nBlood pressure  \n \n67. Dialysis \nDialysis is a treatment that partially replaces kidney functions when the kidneys cannot \nadequately remove wastes and excess fluid.\n\nTwo major forms are \n• \nHaemodialysis  \n• \nPeritoneal dialysis  \n \n68. Haemodialysis \nDuring haemodialysis, blood is circulated through a specialised filter called a dialyser. \nWaste products and excess substances move across a semipermeable membrane into dialysis \nfluid. \nThe treated blood is returned to the patient. \n \n69. Peritoneal Dialysis \nPeritoneal dialysis uses the patient's peritoneum as a semipermeable exchange surface. \nDialysis fluid is introduced into the abdominal cavity. \nWaste products and excess substances move from blood into the dialysis fluid. \nThe fluid is subsequently drained. \n \n70. Kidney Transplantation \nA kidney transplant involves surgically placing a functioning kidney from a suitable donor into a \nrecipient with severe kidney failure. \nThe recipient requires medical management to reduce the risk of immune rejection. \n \n71. Osmoregulation and Homeostasis \nOsmoregulation is an important component of homeostasis. \nThe kidneys continuously adjust water and ion excretion according to the body's needs. \nThis maintains relatively stable conditions for cells despite changes in \n• \nWater intake  \n• \nSalt intake  \n• \nTemperature\n\n• \nPhysical activity  \n• \nMetabolic activity  \n \n72. Relationship Between Excretion and Osmoregulation \nExcretion and osmoregulation are closely related but are not identical. \nExcretion \nRemoves metabolic waste products. \nOsmoregulation \nMaintains appropriate water and solute concentrations. \nThe kidneys perform both functions simultaneously. \n \n73. Key Terms \nOsmoregulation \nRegulation of water and solute concentrations within body fluids. \nOsmosis \nMovement of water across a selectively permeable membrane down a water-potential gradient. \nExcretion \nRemoval of metabolic waste products from the body. \nAmmonia \nHighly toxic nitrogenous waste requiring substantial water for elimination. \nUrea \nLess toxic nitrogenous waste produced mainly in the liver from ammonia. \nUric acid \nLow-toxicity nitrogenous waste that can be eliminated with minimal water. \nNephron \nFunctional unit of the kidney. \nGlomerulus\n\nCapillary network where blood filtration begins. \nFiltration \nMovement of water and small solutes from blood into the nephron. \nReabsorption \nMovement of substances from the nephron back into the blood. \nTubular secretion \nMovement of substances from blood into the nephron. \nADH \nHormone that increases water reabsorption in the kidneys. \nAldosterone \nHormone that promotes sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion. \nCKDu \nChronic kidney disease of uncertain or unknown aetiology. \n \n74. Final Summary \nOsmoregulation maintains the water and solute composition of body fluids, while excretion \nremoves metabolic wastes. \nDifferent animals have evolved different mechanisms for managing water and nitrogenous \nwastes. Aquatic animals can often excrete toxic ammonia directly, whereas terrestrial animals \ncommonly convert ammonia into less toxic urea or uric acid. \nThe human kidneys are the primary organs responsible for osmoregulation and nitrogenous \nwaste excretion. Each nephron performs filtration, selective reabsorption and tubular secretion to \nproduce urine. \nThe loop of Henle, collecting ducts and associated blood vessels allow the kidneys to regulate \nurine concentration. Hormones such as ADH and aldosterone adjust water and electrolyte \nbalance according to the body's needs. \nThe kidneys also contribute to blood pressure regulation and acid-base balance, while the lungs, \nliver and skin contribute to other aspects of waste removal.\n\nIn Sri Lanka, CKDu represents an important area of environmental and public-health research. \nUnderstanding kidney function and the factors that can impair it is essential for understanding \nthis condition.	COMPLETED	98	2026-08-19 18:11:36.76872	2026-08-19 18:12:51.882888	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
979	Lesson 7: Nervous coordination and sensory receptors - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 7: Nervous coordination and sensory receptors	PDF	uploads/pdf/b218b50ac6eb4f63acaaa0d1cae3f34b.pdf	\N	Lesson 7 — Nervous Coordination and Sensory Receptors \nIntroduction \nAnimals must detect changes in their internal and external environments and respond \nappropriately. The nervous system provides rapid communication and coordination throughout \nthe body. \nThe nervous system receives information through sensory receptors, processes that information \nand produces appropriate responses through muscles and glands. \n \n1. Nervous Coordination \nNervous coordination is the control and integration of body activities through electrical and \nchemical signals transmitted by neurons. \nIt allows animals to \n• \nDetect environmental changes  \n• \nCoordinate movements  \n• \nMaintain homeostasis  \n• \nRespond rapidly to stimuli  \n• \nIntegrate information from different parts of the body  \n \n2. Components of the Nervous System \nThe human nervous system can be divided into \n• \nCentral nervous system  \n• \nPeripheral nervous system  \nThe central nervous system consists of the \n• \nBrain  \n• \nSpinal cord  \nThe peripheral nervous system consists mainly of nerves and ganglia connecting the central \nnervous system with the rest of the body. \n \n3. Central Nervous System\n\nThe central nervous system, or CNS, processes and integrates information. \nThe brain is responsible for complex functions such as \n• \nConscious perception  \n• \nMemory  \n• \nLearning  \n• \nReasoning  \n• \nCoordination of movement  \nThe spinal cord provides communication between the brain and the rest of the body and \nparticipates in reflex actions. \n \n4. Peripheral Nervous System \nThe peripheral nervous system, or PNS, connects the CNS with receptors, muscles and glands. \nIt can be functionally divided into \n• \nSensory pathways  \n• \nMotor pathways  \nMotor pathways can be further divided into \n• \nSomatic nervous system  \n• \nAutonomic nervous system  \n \n5. Neurons \nA neuron is a specialised cell that receives, processes and transmits information through \nelectrical and chemical signals. \nA typical neuron contains \n• \nCell body  \n• \nDendrites  \n• \nAxon  \n• \nAxon terminals\n\n6. Cell Body \nThe cell body, or soma, contains the nucleus and most of the organelles required for cellular \nmaintenance. \nIt integrates signals received by the neuron. \n \n7. Dendrites \nDendrites are specialised extensions that generally receive signals from \n• \nSensory receptors  \n• \nOther neurons  \nThey conduct signals toward the cell body. \n \n8. Axon \nThe axon is a long cellular extension that carries electrical signals away from the cell body. \nSome neurons have very long axons that extend considerable distances through the body. \n \n9. Axon Terminals \nAxon terminals occur at the ends of axonal branches. \nThey release chemical signalling molecules called neurotransmitters at synapses. \n \n10. Types of Neurons \nThree major functional types are \n• \nSensory neurons  \n• \nInterneurons  \n• \nMotor neurons  \n \n11. Sensory Neurons \nSensory neurons carry information from sensory receptors toward the central nervous system.\n\nThey provide information about \n• \nTouch  \n• \nTemperature  \n• \nPain  \n• \nLight  \n• \nSound  \n• \nChemical substances  \n• \nBody position  \n \n12. Interneurons \nInterneurons are found mainly within the central nervous system. \nThey connect neurons and integrate information. \nThey are particularly important in processing sensory information and coordinating responses. \n \n13. Motor Neurons \nMotor neurons carry signals from the central nervous system toward effectors. \nEffectors include \n• \nMuscles  \n• \nGlands  \n \n14. Neuroglia \nNeuroglia, or glial cells, support neurons. \nTheir functions include \n• \nStructural support  \n• \nNutrition  \n• \nProtection  \n• \nMaintaining the extracellular environment\n\n• \nFormation of myelin  \n• \nRepair and defence  \nGlial cells are essential for normal nervous-system function. \n \n15. Myelin Sheath \nSome axons are surrounded by a myelin sheath. \nMyelin acts as an electrical insulator and increases the speed at which action potentials travel \nalong the axon. \nIn the peripheral nervous system, myelin is produced mainly by Schwann cells. \nIn the central nervous system, myelin is produced by oligodendrocytes. \n \n16. Nodes of Ranvier \nThe myelin sheath is interrupted at small gaps called nodes of Ranvier. \nAction potentials can effectively jump between these nodes. \nThis type of propagation is called saltatory conduction. \n \n17. Resting Membrane Potential \nA neuron at rest has a difference in electrical potential across its plasma membrane. \nThe inside of the neuron is normally more negative relative to the outside. \nThis is called the resting membrane potential. \nIt is maintained by \n• \nIon concentration gradients  \n• \nSelective membrane permeability  \n• \nSodium-potassium pumps  \n \n18. Sodium-Potassium Pump \nThe sodium-potassium pump is an active transport protein.\n\nFor each cycle, it generally moves \n• \nThree Na⁺ ions out of the cell  \n• \nTwo K⁺ ions into the cell  \nThis contributes to maintaining ion concentration gradients across the membrane. \n \n19. Membrane Potential \nThe membrane potential results from differences in ion distribution and membrane permeability. \nImportant ions include \n• \nSodium ions  \n• \nPotassium ions  \n• \nChloride ions  \nChanges in membrane permeability can alter the membrane potential. \n \n20. Action Potential \nAn action potential is a rapid, temporary change in membrane potential that travels along the \naxon. \nIt allows neurons to transmit information over relatively long distances. \n \n21. Threshold \nAn action potential is normally generated when membrane depolarisation reaches a critical level \ncalled the threshold. \nOnce threshold is reached, an action potential occurs according to the all-or-none principle. \n \n22. Depolarisation \nDuring depolarisation \n• \nVoltage-gated sodium channels open  \n• \nNa⁺ enters the neuron  \n• \nThe membrane potential becomes less negative\n\n• \nThe membrane may temporarily become positive  \nThis is the rising phase of the action potential. \n \n23. Repolarisation \nAfter depolarisation \n• \nSodium channels become inactivated  \n• \nVoltage-gated potassium channels open  \n• \nK⁺ leaves the neuron  \n• \nThe membrane potential becomes more negative  \nThis restores the membrane toward its resting state. \n \n24. Hyperpolarisation \nPotassium channels may remain open briefly after the membrane has returned toward its resting \npotential. \nAdditional K⁺ movement out of the neuron can make the membrane potential temporarily more \nnegative than the resting level. \nThis phase is called hyperpolarisation. \n \n25. Refractory Period \nAfter an action potential, the neuron enters a period during which another action potential is \ndifficult or impossible to generate. \nThis is called the refractory period. \nIt helps \n• \nLimit the maximum frequency of action potentials  \n• \nEnsure one-way propagation  \n• \nAllow the membrane to recover  \n \n26. Propagation of an Action Potential\n\nAn action potential at one region of the axon causes adjacent regions to reach threshold. \nIn this way, the action potential travels along the axon. \nIn myelinated axons, propagation occurs through saltatory conduction between nodes of Ranvier. \n \n27. Synapse \nA synapse is a functional junction where communication occurs between \n• \nNeuron and neuron  \n• \nNeuron and muscle  \n• \nNeuron and gland  \nThe small gap between communicating cells is called the synaptic cleft. \n \n28. Chemical Synapse \nAt a chemical synapse, the presynaptic neuron releases neurotransmitters. \nThe general sequence is \nAction potential arrives at axon terminal \n↓ \nVoltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels open \n↓ \nCa²⁺ enters the axon terminal \n↓ \nSynaptic vesicles fuse with the membrane \n↓ \nNeurotransmitter is released \n↓ \nNeurotransmitter binds receptors on the postsynaptic cell \n↓ \nPostsynaptic response occurs\n\n29. Neurotransmitters \nNeurotransmitters are chemical messengers released by neurons. \nExamples include \n• \nAcetylcholine  \n• \nGlutamate  \n• \nGABA  \n• \nDopamine  \n• \nNoradrenaline  \n• \nSerotonin  \nDifferent neurotransmitters can have different effects depending on the receptors they activate. \n \n30. Synaptic Vesicles \nSynaptic vesicles are membrane-bound structures located within axon terminals. \nThey store neurotransmitters. \nWhen an action potential causes calcium ions to enter the terminal, vesicles can fuse with the \npresynaptic membrane and release neurotransmitters. \n \n31. Postsynaptic Receptors \nNeurotransmitters bind to specific receptors on the postsynaptic membrane. \nThis can produce \n• \nDepolarisation  \n• \nHyperpolarisation  \n• \nChanges in ion permeability  \n• \nActivation of intracellular signalling pathways  \n \n32. Excitatory and Inhibitory Signals\n\nA neurotransmitter can produce an excitatory or inhibitory effect depending on the receptor and \nion channels involved. \nExcitatory postsynaptic potentials increase the likelihood of an action potential. \nInhibitory postsynaptic potentials decrease the likelihood of an action potential. \n \n33. Synaptic Delay \nChemical synaptic transmission takes a short amount of time because it involves \n• \nCalcium entry  \n• \nVesicle fusion  \n• \nNeurotransmitter release  \n• \nDiffusion across the synaptic cleft  \n• \nReceptor activation  \nThis creates a small synaptic delay. \n \n34. One-Way Transmission \nChemical synapses generally transmit signals in one direction. \nThe presynaptic neuron releases neurotransmitter. \nThe postsynaptic cell contains the corresponding receptors. \nThis gives nervous pathways directional organisation. \n \n35. Removal of Neurotransmitters \nNeurotransmitters must be removed or inactivated after signalling. \nMechanisms include \n• \nEnzymatic breakdown  \n• \nReuptake into neurons  \n• \nUptake by glial cells  \n• \nDiffusion away from the synapse\n\n36. Reflex Action \nA reflex action is a rapid, automatic response to a stimulus. \nReflexes help protect the body and maintain homeostasis. \nExamples include \n• \nWithdrawal from a painful stimulus  \n• \nKnee-jerk reflex  \n• \nPupillary response to light  \n \n37. Reflex Arc \nA typical reflex arc includes \nReceptor \n↓ \nSensory neuron \n↓ \nInterneuron in CNS \n↓ \nMotor neuron \n↓ \nEffector \nThe response can occur rapidly without requiring conscious processing before the initial \nresponse. \n \n38. Receptors \nA sensory receptor is a specialised structure that detects a particular type of stimulus. \nReceptors convert stimulus energy into electrical signals. \nThis process is called sensory transduction.\n\n39. Types of Sensory Receptors \nSensory receptors can be classified according to the stimulus detected. \nReceptor \nStimulus \nMechanoreceptor Mechanical forces \nChemoreceptor \nChemical substances \nPhotoreceptor \nLight \nThermoreceptor Temperature \nNociceptor \nPotentially damaging stimuli \n \n40. Mechanoreceptors \nMechanoreceptors respond to mechanical deformation. \nThey detect stimuli such as \n• \nTouch  \n• \nPressure  \n• \nStretch  \n• \nVibration  \n• \nSound  \n• \nBody movement  \n \n41. Chemoreceptors \nChemoreceptors detect chemical substances. \nThey contribute to sensations such as \n• \nTaste  \n• \nSmell  \nThey also monitor internal variables such as blood carbon dioxide and pH.\n\n42. Photoreceptors \nPhotoreceptors detect light. \nIn humans, photoreceptors are located in the retina. \nThe two major types are \n• \nRods  \n• \nCones  \n \n43. Thermoreceptors \nThermoreceptors detect changes in temperature. \nThey are located in the skin and other tissues. \nThey provide information required for temperature regulation and perception. \n \n44. Nociceptors \nNociceptors respond to stimuli associated with actual or potential tissue damage. \nThey contribute to the sensation of pain. \n \n45. Human Eye \nThe eye is the major organ of vision. \nImportant structures include \n• \nCornea  \n• \nIris  \n• \nPupil  \n• \nLens  \n• \nCiliary muscles  \n• \nRetina  \n• \nOptic nerve\n\n• \nAqueous humour  \n• \nVitreous humour  \n \n46. Cornea \nThe cornea is the transparent outer covering at the front of the eye. \nIt provides significant refraction of incoming light. \n \n47. Iris \nThe iris is the coloured portion of the eye. \nIt contains muscles that regulate the size of the pupil. \n \n48. Pupil \nThe pupil is the opening through which light enters the eye. \nIts size changes according to light intensity. \n \n49. Lens \nThe lens is a transparent structure that focuses light onto the retina. \nIts curvature can change to allow focusing on objects at different distances. \nThis process is called accommodation. \n \n50. Accommodation \nWhen viewing a nearby object \n• \nCiliary muscles contract  \n• \nSuspensory ligaments become less tense  \n• \nLens becomes more rounded  \n• \nRefractive power increases  \nWhen viewing a distant object\n\n• \nCiliary muscles relax  \n• \nSuspensory ligaments become more tense  \n• \nLens becomes flatter  \n• \nRefractive power decreases  \n \n51. Retina \nThe retina contains photoreceptors and associated neurons. \nIt converts light information into neural signals. \nThe signals are processed within the retina and transmitted through the optic nerve. \n \n52. Rod Cells \nRod cells are highly sensitive to low levels of light. \nThey are important for \n• \nDim-light vision  \n• \nPeripheral vision  \n• \nDetection of movement  \nThey do not provide detailed colour vision. \n \n53. Cone Cells \nCone cells function best under relatively bright conditions. \nThey are responsible for \n• \nColour vision  \n• \nHigh visual acuity  \n• \nDetailed vision  \nDifferent cone types have different spectral sensitivities. \n \n54. Fovea\n\nThe fovea is a specialised region of the retina with a high density of cone cells. \nIt provides the greatest visual acuity. \n \n55. Blind Spot \nThe blind spot is the point where the optic nerve leaves the eye. \nThere are no photoreceptors at this location. \nTherefore, light falling directly on this region cannot normally produce visual information. \n \n56. Optic Nerve \nThe optic nerve carries electrical signals from the retina toward the brain. \nVisual information is ultimately processed in the visual cortex of the brain. \n \n57. Pupillary Reflex \nThe pupil adjusts its size in response to light intensity. \nIn bright light \n→ Circular muscles of the iris contract \n→ Pupil becomes smaller \n→ Less light enters the eye \nIn dim light \n→ Radial muscles contract \n→ Pupil becomes larger \n→ More light enters the eye \n \n58. Human Ear \nThe ear is responsible for \n• \nHearing  \n• \nBalance\n\nIt consists of \n• \nOuter ear  \n• \nMiddle ear  \n• \nInner ear  \n \n59. Outer Ear \nThe outer ear includes \n• \nPinna  \n• \nExternal auditory canal  \nThe pinna collects sound waves and directs them toward the auditory canal. \n \n60. Middle Ear \nThe middle ear contains three small bones \n• \nMalleus  \n• \nIncus  \n• \nStapes  \nThese bones transmit and amplify vibrations from the tympanic membrane toward the inner ear. \n \n61. Tympanic Membrane \nThe tympanic membrane, or eardrum, vibrates when sound waves reach it. \nThese vibrations are transferred to the ossicles. \n \n62. Eustachian Tube \nThe Eustachian tube connects the middle ear with the upper throat region. \nIt helps equalise air pressure across the tympanic membrane. \n \n63. Inner Ear\n\nThe inner ear contains structures involved in \n• \nHearing  \n• \nBalance  \nMajor structures include \n• \nCochlea  \n• \nVestibule  \n• \nSemicircular canals  \n \n64. Cochlea \nThe cochlea is a spiral-shaped structure involved in hearing. \nSound-induced vibrations create movements of fluid within the cochlea. \nThese movements stimulate sensory hair cells. \n \n65. Organ of Corti \nThe organ of Corti contains sensory hair cells responsible for detecting sound-related \nmechanical vibrations. \nMovement of the hair cells generates electrical signals that are transmitted through the auditory \npathway. \n \n66. Semicircular Canals \nThe semicircular canals detect rotational movements of the head. \nThey contain fluid and sensory structures that respond to movement of the fluid. \nThey contribute to maintaining balance. \n \n67. Vestibule \nThe vestibule contains sensory structures that detect \n• \nLinear acceleration  \n• \nHead position relative to gravity\n\nIt contributes to balance and spatial orientation. \n \n68. Hearing Mechanism \nThe basic pathway of hearing is \nSound waves \n↓ \nPinna \n↓ \nAuditory canal \n↓ \nTympanic membrane \n↓ \nOssicles \n↓ \nOval window \n↓ \nCochlear fluid movement \n↓ \nHair-cell stimulation \n↓ \nElectrical signals \n↓ \nAuditory nerve \n↓ \nBrain \n \n69. Balance\n\nThe vestibular system provides information about head movement and position. \nIt consists mainly of \n• \nSemicircular canals  \n• \nUtricle  \n• \nSaccule  \nThis information is integrated with visual and proprioceptive information to maintain posture and \nbalance. \n \n70. Autonomic Nervous System \nThe autonomic nervous system, or ANS, regulates many involuntary functions. \nIt controls activities involving \n• \nSmooth muscle  \n• \nCardiac muscle  \n• \nGlands  \nIt contributes to the regulation of internal organs. \n \n71. Sympathetic Division \nThe sympathetic division generally prepares the body for increased activity or stress. \nTypical effects include \n• \nIncreased heart rate  \n• \nIncreased cardiac output  \n• \nIncreased pupil diameter  \n• \nReduced digestive activity  \n• \nIncreased blood flow to skeletal muscles  \n \n72. Parasympathetic Division \nThe parasympathetic division generally supports rest, digestion and energy conservation.\n\nTypical effects include \n• \nReduced heart rate  \n• \nIncreased digestive activity  \n• \nIncreased intestinal movement  \n• \nIncreased glandular secretion  \n• \nConstriction of the pupil  \n \n73. Sympathetic and Parasympathetic Comparison \nFeature \nSympathetic \nParasympathetic \nGeneral role Activity and stress Rest and digestion \nHeart rate \nGenerally increases Generally decreases \nPupil \nDilates \nConstricts \nDigestion \nGenerally inhibited Generally stimulated \nEnergy use Mobilises resources Conserves resources \n \n74. Somatic Nervous System \nThe somatic nervous system is involved mainly in communication with \n• \nSkeletal muscles  \n• \nSkin  \n• \nSensory organs  \nIt contributes to voluntary movement and conscious sensory perception. \n \n75. Autonomic Versus Somatic Nervous System \nFeature \nSomatic \nAutonomic \nMain targets Skeletal muscles \nSmooth muscle, cardiac muscle, glands\n\nFeature \nSomatic \nAutonomic \nControl \nMainly voluntary \nMainly involuntary \nMain role \nBody movement and conscious sensation Internal organ regulation \n \n76. Sensory Adaptation \nSensory receptors can reduce their response when exposed continuously to an unchanged \nstimulus. \nThis phenomenon is called sensory adaptation. \nIt allows the nervous system to focus attention on changes in the environment. \n \n77. Homeostasis and the Nervous System \nThe nervous system contributes to homeostasis by rapidly detecting changes and coordinating \nresponses. \nExamples include regulation of \n• \nBody temperature  \n• \nBlood pressure  \n• \nBreathing  \n• \nHeart rate  \n• \nWater balance  \nThe nervous system works closely with the endocrine system to maintain internal stability. \n \n78. Key Terms \nNeuron \nA specialised cell that transmits nervous signals. \nAction potential \nA rapid electrical change that propagates along a neuron. \nSynapse\n\nA junction through which a neuron communicates with another cell. \nNeurotransmitter \nA chemical messenger released by neurons. \nReceptor \nA specialised structure that detects a particular stimulus. \nReflex \nA rapid, automatic response to a stimulus. \nMyelin sheath \nAn insulating layer surrounding some axons. \nSaltatory conduction \nPropagation of an action potential between nodes of Ranvier. \nCNS \nThe brain and spinal cord. \nPNS \nNerves and ganglia outside the CNS. \nPhotoreceptor \nA receptor that detects light. \nMechanoreceptor \nA receptor that detects mechanical stimuli. \nChemoreceptor \nA receptor that detects chemical stimuli. \nThermoreceptor \nA receptor that detects temperature changes. \nNociceptor \nA receptor involved in detecting potentially damaging stimuli. \n \n79. Final Summary\n\nThe nervous system provides rapid coordination by detecting stimuli, transmitting information \nand producing appropriate responses. \nNeurons transmit electrical signals through action potentials and communicate with other cells \nthrough synapses. Action potentials depend on changes in membrane permeability to ions, while \nchemical synapses use neurotransmitters to transmit information between cells. \nThe peripheral nervous system contains sensory and motor pathways. The motor system includes \nsomatic pathways controlling skeletal muscles and autonomic pathways regulating internal \norgans. The autonomic system is divided mainly into sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions. \nSensory receptors convert environmental stimuli into neural signals. Mechanoreceptors detect \nmechanical forces, chemoreceptors detect chemicals, photoreceptors detect light, \nthermoreceptors detect temperature and nociceptors detect potentially damaging stimuli. \nThe eye detects light using rods and cones within the retina. The ear detects sound through the \ncochlea and contributes to balance through the vestibular system. \nTogether, nervous coordination and sensory reception allow animals to rapidly detect \nenvironmental changes, coordinate movement, regulate internal conditions and interact \neffectively with their surroundings.	COMPLETED	99	2026-08-19 18:11:37.65669	2026-08-19 18:12:51.882889	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
980	Lesson 8: The endocrine system and chemical signaling - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 8: The endocrine system and chemical signaling	PDF	uploads/pdf/262932e54a6e4256b63ee0a308dd4544.pdf	\N	Lesson 8 — The Endocrine System and Chemical Signaling \nIntroduction \nAnimals require coordinated systems to regulate growth, metabolism, reproduction, development \nand internal balance. The endocrine system performs these functions through hormones, which \nare chemical messengers released by endocrine cells or glands. \nUnlike nervous signals, which are generally rapid and short-lived, hormonal signals often act \nmore slowly and can produce effects that persist for longer periods. \n \n1. Endocrine System \nThe endocrine system consists of glands and specialised cells that release hormones into the \nblood or surrounding tissue fluid. \nMajor endocrine organs include \n• \nHypothalamus  \n• \nPituitary gland  \n• \nThyroid gland  \n• \nParathyroid glands  \n• \nAdrenal glands  \n• \nPancreas  \n• \nOvaries  \n• \nTestes  \n• \nPineal gland  \n \n2. Hormones \nA hormone is a chemical messenger that is released by endocrine cells and transported to target \ncells where it produces a specific physiological response. \nHormones regulate processes such as \n• \nGrowth  \n• \nMetabolism  \n• \nReproduction\n\n• \nDevelopment  \n• \nWater balance  \n• \nBlood glucose regulation  \n• \nStress responses  \n \n3. Endocrine and Exocrine Glands \nEndocrine glands release their secretions without ducts, usually into the blood. \nExocrine glands release their secretions through ducts. \nExamples of endocrine glands include \n• \nPituitary gland  \n• \nThyroid gland  \n• \nAdrenal glands  \nExamples of exocrine glands include \n• \nSweat glands  \n• \nSalivary glands  \nSome organs, such as the pancreas, have both endocrine and exocrine functions. \n \n4. Target Cells \nHormones do not normally affect every cell in the body. \nA hormone acts mainly on cells containing the appropriate receptor. \nThese cells are called target cells. \nThe presence or absence of receptors determines whether a cell can respond to a particular \nhormone. \n \n5. Hormone Receptors \nHormone receptors are specialised proteins that recognise specific hormones. \nThey may be located\n\n• \nOn the plasma membrane  \n• \nWithin the cytoplasm  \n• \nInside the nucleus  \nThe location depends largely on the chemical properties of the hormone. \n \n6. Peptide and Protein Hormones \nPeptide and protein hormones are generally water-soluble. \nExamples include \n• \nInsulin  \n• \nGlucagon  \n• \nGrowth hormone  \n• \nAntidiuretic hormone  \nBecause they cannot easily pass through the lipid bilayer of the plasma membrane, they usually \nbind to receptors on the cell surface. \n \n7. Steroid Hormones \nSteroid hormones are derived from cholesterol and are generally lipid-soluble. \nExamples include \n• \nTestosterone  \n• \nOestrogen  \n• \nProgesterone  \n• \nCortisol  \n• \nAldosterone  \nThey can pass through the plasma membrane and commonly bind to intracellular receptors. \n \n8. Peptide Hormone Signalling \nA typical peptide hormone pathway is\n\nHormone \n↓ \nMembrane receptor \n↓ \nIntracellular signalling pathway \n↓ \nSecond messenger or protein kinase activity \n↓ \nCellular response \nThe hormone itself does not necessarily enter the target cell. \n \n9. Second Messengers \nA second messenger is an intracellular molecule that helps transmit a signal from a cell-surface \nreceptor into the cell. \nExamples include \n• \nCyclic AMP  \n• \nCalcium ions  \n• \nInositol trisphosphate  \nThey can amplify hormonal signals and activate intracellular proteins. \n \n10. Cyclic AMP \nCyclic AMP, or cAMP, is an important second messenger. \nA simplified pathway is \nHormone binds receptor \n↓ \nG protein activation \n↓\n\nAdenylyl cyclase activation \n↓ \nATP converted to cAMP \n↓ \nProtein kinase activation \n↓ \nCellular response \n \n11. Steroid Hormone Signalling \nSteroid hormones can cross the plasma membrane. \nThey bind to intracellular receptors. \nThe hormone-receptor complex can interact with DNA and regulate gene transcription. \nTherefore, steroid hormones often produce changes in protein synthesis. \n \n12. Comparison of Hormone Types \nFeature \nPeptide hormones \nSteroid hormones \nChemical nature \nAmino-acid based \nCholesterol-derived \nSolubility \nGenerally water-soluble \nGenerally lipid-soluble \nMembrane crossing Usually cannot cross freely \nCan cross \nReceptor \nUsually membrane receptor \nUsually intracellular receptor \nMain mechanism \nSecond messengers/signalling pathways Regulation of gene transcription \nExamples \nInsulin, ADH \nTestosterone, cortisol \n \n13. Hypothalamus \nThe hypothalamus is a region of the brain that links the nervous system with the endocrine \nsystem.\n\nIt regulates \n• \nBody temperature  \n• \nWater balance  \n• \nAppetite  \n• \nReproductive functions  \n• \nCircadian rhythms  \n• \nHormonal activity  \nIt controls the pituitary gland through releasing and inhibiting hormones. \n \n14. Pituitary Gland \nThe pituitary gland is located beneath the hypothalamus. \nIt consists of two major regions \n• \nAnterior pituitary  \n• \nPosterior pituitary  \nThe pituitary regulates several other endocrine organs. \nIt is therefore often described as a major control centre of the endocrine system. \n \n15. Anterior Pituitary \nThe anterior pituitary produces and releases several hormones, including \n• \nGrowth hormone  \n• \nThyroid-stimulating hormone  \n• \nAdrenocorticotropic hormone  \n• \nFollicle-stimulating hormone  \n• \nLuteinising hormone  \n• \nProlactin  \n \n16. Posterior Pituitary\n\nThe posterior pituitary stores and releases hormones produced by the hypothalamus. \nThe major hormones are \n• \nAntidiuretic hormone  \n• \nOxytocin  \n \n17. Growth Hormone \nGrowth hormone, or GH, promotes growth and influences metabolism. \nIt acts partly through stimulation of the production of insulin-like growth factors. \nGrowth hormone is particularly important during childhood and adolescence. \n \n18. Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone \nThyroid-stimulating hormone, or TSH, stimulates the thyroid gland to produce thyroid \nhormones. \nIt is part of the hypothalamus-pituitary-thyroid regulatory pathway. \n \n19. Adrenocorticotropic Hormone \nAdrenocorticotropic hormone, or ACTH, stimulates the adrenal cortex. \nIt particularly promotes the secretion of glucocorticoids such as cortisol. \n \n20. Follicle-Stimulating Hormone \nFollicle-stimulating hormone, or FSH, plays important roles in reproduction. \nIn females, it promotes ovarian follicle development. \nIn males, it contributes to sperm production through its effects on Sertoli cells. \n \n21. Luteinising Hormone \nLuteinising hormone, or LH, has different functions in males and females. \nIn females, an LH surge contributes to ovulation and formation of the corpus luteum.\n\nIn males, LH stimulates Leydig cells to produce testosterone. \n \n22. Prolactin \nProlactin promotes milk production in mammary glands. \nIts secretion is regulated by the hypothalamus and is strongly influenced by suckling. \n \n23. Antidiuretic Hormone \nAntidiuretic hormone, or ADH, regulates water balance. \nIt increases the water permeability of collecting ducts in the kidneys. \nThis increases water reabsorption and reduces water loss through urine. \n \n24. Oxytocin \nOxytocin contributes to \n• \nUterine contractions during childbirth  \n• \nMilk ejection during breastfeeding  \nIts release can involve positive feedback mechanisms. \n \n25. Thyroid Gland \nThe thyroid gland is located in the neck. \nIt produces thyroid hormones that influence \n• \nMetabolic rate  \n• \nGrowth  \n• \nDevelopment  \n• \nEnergy metabolism  \nThe major thyroid hormones are \n• \nThyroxine  \n• \nTriiodothyronine\n\n26. Thyroxine \nThyroxine, or T4, is produced by thyroid follicular cells. \nIt is converted in many tissues into the more biologically active T3. \nThyroid hormones increase metabolic activity in many tissues. \n \n27. Thyroid Hormone Regulation \nThe pathway is \nHypothalamus \n↓ \nTRH \n↓ \nAnterior pituitary \n↓ \nTSH \n↓ \nThyroid gland \n↓ \nT3 and T4 \nThyroid hormones then exert negative feedback on the hypothalamus and pituitary. \n \n28. Parathyroid Glands \nThe parathyroid glands are small endocrine glands located behind the thyroid gland. \nThey produce parathyroid hormone, or PTH. \nPTH helps increase blood calcium concentration. \n \n29. Parathyroid Hormone\n\nPTH increases blood calcium through actions involving \n• \nBone  \n• \nKidneys  \n• \nIndirect effects on the intestine through vitamin D activation  \nMaintaining calcium concentration is essential for \n• \nMuscle contraction  \n• \nNerve function  \n• \nBlood clotting  \n• \nBone metabolism  \n \n30. Adrenal Glands \nThe adrenal glands are located above the kidneys. \nEach adrenal gland consists of \n• \nAdrenal cortex  \n• \nAdrenal medulla  \nThese regions produce different hormones. \n \n31. Adrenal Cortex \nThe adrenal cortex produces steroid hormones including \n• \nCortisol  \n• \nAldosterone  \n• \nAdrenal androgens  \n \n32. Cortisol \nCortisol is a glucocorticoid involved in the response to stress. \nIt influences \n• \nCarbohydrate metabolism\n\n• \nProtein metabolism  \n• \nLipid metabolism  \n• \nImmune activity  \nCortisol secretion is regulated through the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis. \n \n33. Aldosterone \nAldosterone is a mineralocorticoid. \nIt acts mainly on the kidneys to \n• \nIncrease sodium reabsorption  \n• \nIncrease potassium secretion  \nIt contributes to maintaining extracellular fluid volume and blood pressure. \n \n34. Adrenal Medulla \nThe adrenal medulla produces the catecholamine hormones \n• \nAdrenaline  \n• \nNoradrenaline  \nThese hormones help prepare the body for rapid responses to stress. \n \n35. Adrenaline \nAdrenaline, also called epinephrine, contributes to the fight-or-flight response. \nIts effects can include \n• \nIncreased heart rate  \n• \nIncreased cardiac output  \n• \nIncreased blood glucose availability  \n• \nIncreased airflow through the lungs  \n• \nIncreased blood flow to skeletal muscles\n\n36. Pancreas \nThe pancreas has both endocrine and exocrine functions. \nThe endocrine component consists mainly of the islets of Langerhans. \nImportant pancreatic hormones include \n• \nInsulin  \n• \nGlucagon  \n \n37. Insulin \nInsulin lowers blood glucose concentration. \nIt promotes \n• \nGlucose uptake by certain tissues  \n• \nGlycogen formation  \n• \nFat synthesis  \n• \nProtein synthesis  \n \n38. Glucagon \nGlucagon increases blood glucose concentration. \nIt promotes \n• \nGlycogen breakdown  \n• \nGlucose production by the liver  \nInsulin and glucagon work together to maintain blood glucose homeostasis. \n \n39. Blood Glucose Regulation \nWhen blood glucose rises \nIncreased blood glucose \n↓ \nPancreatic beta cells release insulin\n\n↓ \nIncreased glucose uptake and storage \n↓ \nBlood glucose decreases \nWhen blood glucose falls \nDecreased blood glucose \n↓ \nPancreatic alpha cells release glucagon \n↓ \nIncreased glycogen breakdown and glucose production \n↓ \nBlood glucose increases \n \n40. Diabetes Mellitus \nDiabetes mellitus is a group of disorders characterised by chronically elevated blood glucose \nlevels. \nTwo major forms are \n• \nType 1 diabetes mellitus  \n• \nType 2 diabetes mellitus  \n \n41. Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus \nType 1 diabetes usually results from autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells. \nThis causes insufficient insulin production. \nPeople with type 1 diabetes require insulin replacement. \n \n42. Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus \nType 2 diabetes involves insulin resistance combined with progressive impairment of insulin \nsecretion.\n\nRisk is influenced by genetic, environmental and lifestyle factors. \n \n43. Ovaries \nThe ovaries are female reproductive organs with endocrine functions. \nThey produce hormones including \n• \nOestrogens  \n• \nProgesterone  \nThese hormones regulate reproductive development and reproductive cycles. \n \n44. Oestrogen \nOestrogens contribute to \n• \nDevelopment of female secondary sexual characteristics  \n• \nGrowth of reproductive tissues  \n• \nRegulation of the menstrual cycle  \n• \nMaintenance of reproductive function  \n \n45. Progesterone \nProgesterone contributes to \n• \nPreparation of the uterine lining  \n• \nMaintenance of the uterine lining during pregnancy  \n• \nRegulation of the reproductive cycle  \n \n46. Testes \nThe testes are male reproductive organs with endocrine functions. \nThey produce testosterone, mainly through Leydig cells. \n \n47. Testosterone\n\nTestosterone contributes to \n• \nDevelopment of male reproductive organs  \n• \nDevelopment of male secondary sexual characteristics  \n• \nSperm production  \n• \nMaintenance of reproductive function  \n \n48. Pineal Gland \nThe pineal gland produces melatonin. \nMelatonin contributes to the regulation of \n• \nSleep-wake cycles  \n• \nCircadian rhythms  \nIts secretion is influenced by environmental light conditions. \n \n49. Negative Feedback \nNegative feedback occurs when the response to a stimulus reduces the original stimulus. \nIt is one of the most important mechanisms maintaining homeostasis. \nExample \nIncreased thyroid hormones \n↓ \nReduced hypothalamic and pituitary stimulation \n↓ \nReduced thyroid stimulation \n↓ \nThyroid hormone production decreases \n \n50. Positive Feedback \nPositive feedback occurs when the response amplifies the original stimulus.\n\nAn important example is oxytocin during childbirth. \nUterine stretching \n↓ \nOxytocin release \n↓ \nStronger uterine contractions \n↓ \nIncreased cervical stretching \n↓ \nFurther oxytocin release \nThe cycle continues until childbirth occurs. \n \n51. Endocrine Control of Growth \nGrowth is regulated by interactions between \n• \nGrowth hormone  \n• \nThyroid hormones  \n• \nInsulin  \n• \nSex hormones  \n• \nGrowth factors  \nGrowth hormone stimulates the production of insulin-like growth factor 1, which contributes \nsignificantly to growth of bones and other tissues. \n \n52. Hormonal Regulation of Metabolism \nHormones regulate how nutrients are \n• \nAbsorbed  \n• \nStored  \n• \nMobilised\n\n• \nUsed for energy  \nImportant metabolic hormones include \n• \nInsulin  \n• \nGlucagon  \n• \nThyroid hormones  \n• \nCortisol  \n• \nAdrenaline  \n \n53. Hormonal Regulation of Water Balance \nWater balance is controlled through interactions involving \n• \nHypothalamus  \n• \nADH  \n• \nKidneys  \n• \nThirst mechanisms  \nAn increase in blood osmolarity stimulates mechanisms that increase water conservation and \nwater intake. \n \n54. Hypothalamic-Pituitary Axis \nThe hypothalamus controls the pituitary using releasing and inhibiting hormones. \nThe pituitary then regulates other endocrine glands. \nExamples include \n• \nHypothalamus-pituitary-thyroid axis  \n• \nHypothalamus-pituitary-adrenal axis  \n• \nHypothalamus-pituitary-gonadal axis  \n \n55. Hypothalamic-Pituitary-Thyroid Axis \nThe pathway is\n\nHypothalamus \n↓ \nTRH \n↓ \nAnterior pituitary \n↓ \nTSH \n↓ \nThyroid gland \n↓ \nT3 and T4 \n↓ \nIncreased metabolic activity \nThyroid hormones provide negative feedback to the hypothalamus and pituitary. \n \n56. Hypothalamic-Pituitary-Adrenal Axis \nThe pathway is \nHypothalamus \n↓ \nCRH \n↓ \nAnterior pituitary \n↓ \nACTH \n↓ \nAdrenal cortex \n↓\n\nCortisol \nCortisol contributes to metabolic and stress responses and provides negative feedback to the \nhypothalamus and pituitary. \n \n57. Hypothalamic-Pituitary-Gonadal Axis \nThe reproductive axis involves \nHypothalamus \n↓ \nGnRH \n↓ \nAnterior pituitary \n↓ \nFSH and LH \n↓ \nGonads \n↓ \nSex hormones and gamete production \nSex hormones provide feedback to the hypothalamus and pituitary. \n \n58. Endocrine and Nervous Coordination \nThe nervous and endocrine systems work together. \nThe nervous system generally provides \n• \nRapid communication  \n• \nShorter-lasting responses  \n• \nPrecise signalling  \nThe endocrine system generally provides \n• \nSlower responses\n\n• \nLonger-lasting effects  \n• \nWidespread regulation  \nThe hypothalamus provides an important connection between these systems. \n \n59. Hormonal Communication \nHormonal signalling generally follows this sequence \nStimulus \n↓ \nEndocrine cell or gland activation \n↓ \nHormone secretion \n↓ \nHormone transport \n↓ \nBinding to target-cell receptor \n↓ \nCellular response \n↓ \nTermination or reduction of signal \n \n60. Hormone Interactions \nHormones can interact in several ways. \nSynergistic effects \nTwo hormones produce a combined effect greater than either alone. \nAntagonistic effects \nTwo hormones produce opposing effects. \nPermissive effects\n\nOne hormone enables another hormone to produce its full effect. \n \n61. Hormonal Control and Homeostasis \nHormonal regulation contributes to maintaining stable internal conditions. \nImportant homeostatic functions include \n• \nBlood glucose regulation  \n• \nCalcium regulation  \n• \nWater balance  \n• \nBlood pressure regulation  \n• \nMetabolic regulation  \n• \nReproductive regulation  \n• \nTemperature-related responses  \n \n62. Key Terms \nHormone \nA chemical messenger that acts on specific target cells. \nEndocrine gland \nA gland that releases hormones without ducts. \nTarget cell \nA cell containing receptors capable of responding to a particular hormone. \nPeptide hormone \nA hormone composed of amino acids and generally acting through cell-surface receptors. \nSteroid hormone \nA lipid-soluble hormone derived from cholesterol. \nReceptor \nA protein that specifically recognises a signalling molecule. \nSecond messenger\n\nAn intracellular molecule that transmits signals from a cell-surface receptor. \nNegative feedback \nA regulatory mechanism in which the response reduces the original stimulus. \nPositive feedback \nA regulatory mechanism in which the response amplifies the original stimulus. \nInsulin \nA pancreatic hormone that generally decreases blood glucose concentration. \nGlucagon \nA pancreatic hormone that generally increases blood glucose concentration. \nADH \nA hormone that promotes water reabsorption by the kidneys. \nCortisol \nA glucocorticoid involved in metabolism and stress responses. \nAldosterone \nA hormone that promotes sodium retention and potassium secretion. \n \n63. Final Summary \nThe endocrine system coordinates many long-term physiological processes through hormones. \nHormones act only on cells containing appropriate receptors. Peptide and protein hormones \ngenerally bind to cell-surface receptors and activate intracellular signalling pathways, whereas \nsteroid hormones can enter cells and influence gene expression through intracellular receptors. \nThe hypothalamus and pituitary gland form a major control system for endocrine regulation. \nThey influence thyroid activity, adrenal function, growth and reproduction through several \nhormonal axes. \nThe thyroid regulates metabolic activity, the parathyroid glands regulate calcium balance, the \nadrenal glands participate in stress and electrolyte regulation, and the pancreas maintains blood \nglucose through insulin and glucagon. \nThe reproductive organs also function as endocrine organs, producing hormones essential for \nsexual development and reproduction.\n\nMost endocrine regulation depends on negative feedback, which maintains physiological \nvariables within suitable ranges. Positive feedback occurs in specialised situations such as \nchildbirth. \nTogether, the nervous and endocrine systems provide integrated control of the body, allowing \nanimals to respond to environmental changes while maintaining internal stability.	COMPLETED	100	2026-08-19 18:11:38.390659	2026-08-19 18:12:51.882891	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
981	Lesson 9: Animal reproduction and development - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 9: Animal reproduction and development	PDF	uploads/pdf/6cf6078b77794883820ee6804a0c79fc.pdf	\N	Lesson 9 — Animal Reproduction and Development \nIntroduction \nReproduction is the biological process through which organisms produce new individuals. In \nanimals, reproduction may occur sexually or asexually, although sexual reproduction is the \ndominant form in most complex animals. \nAnimal reproduction involves specialised reproductive organs, gamete formation, fertilisation, \nhormonal regulation and embryonic development. \n \n1. Types of Animal Reproduction \nAnimal reproduction can be divided into \n• \nAsexual reproduction  \n• \nSexual reproduction  \nAsexual reproduction involves a single parent and does not require the fusion of gametes. \nSexual reproduction involves the formation and fusion of male and female gametes. \n \n2. Asexual Reproduction \nAsexual reproduction produces offspring without the fusion of gametes. \nThe offspring are usually genetically very similar to the parent. \nExamples include \n• \nBudding  \n• \nFragmentation  \n• \nBinary fission  \n• \nParthenogenesis  \n \n3. Sexual Reproduction \nSexual reproduction involves the production of haploid gametes through meiosis and their \nsubsequent fusion during fertilisation. \nIt generates genetic variation among offspring.\n\nThe major stages include \n1. Gametogenesis  \n2. Fertilisation  \n3. Embryonic development  \n4. Growth and maturation  \n \n4. Male Reproductive System \nThe major structures of the human male reproductive system include \n• \nTestes  \n• \nEpididymis  \n• \nVas deferens  \n• \nSeminal vesicles  \n• \nProstate gland  \n• \nBulbourethral glands  \n• \nUrethra  \n• \nPenis  \n \n5. Testes \nThe testes are the male reproductive organs. \nThey have two major functions \n• \nProduction of sperm  \n• \nProduction of testosterone  \nThe testes are located in the scrotum, outside the main body cavity. \n \n6. Scrotum \nThe scrotum holds the testes outside the abdominal cavity.\n\nThis helps maintain the testes at a temperature slightly below core body temperature, which is \nimportant for normal sperm production. \n \n7. Seminiferous Tubules \nThe testes contain numerous seminiferous tubules. \nSpermatogenesis occurs within these tubules. \nThe developing sperm cells are supported by Sertoli cells. \n \n8. Sertoli Cells \nSertoli cells support developing sperm cells. \nTheir functions include \n• \nProviding nutrients to developing germ cells  \n• \nSupporting spermatogenesis  \n• \nForming the blood-testis barrier  \n• \nProducing regulatory substances  \n• \nSecreting inhibin  \n \n9. Leydig Cells \nLeydig cells are located between the seminiferous tubules. \nThey produce testosterone in response to luteinising hormone. \n \n10. Epididymis \nThe epididymis is a highly coiled duct attached to each testis. \nIt is the site where sperm \n• \nMature  \n• \nGain motility  \n• \nAre stored temporarily\n\n11. Vas Deferens \nThe vas deferens transports sperm from the epididymis toward the ejaculatory duct. \nDuring ejaculation, muscular contractions help propel sperm through the reproductive tract. \n \n12. Seminal Vesicles \nSeminal vesicles produce a nutrient-rich fluid containing substances including fructose. \nTheir secretions contribute significantly to semen and provide energy sources for sperm. \n \n13. Prostate Gland \nThe prostate gland contributes fluid to semen. \nIts secretion contains substances that support sperm function and contribute to the appropriate \nchemical environment of semen. \n \n14. Bulbourethral Glands \nBulbourethral glands produce a lubricating secretion that contributes to the fluid released before \nejaculation. \n \n15. Semen \nSemen consists of sperm together with secretions from the male reproductive glands. \nThese secretions provide \n• \nNutrients  \n• \nFluid for sperm transport  \n• \nSuitable chemical conditions  \n• \nSubstances supporting sperm survival and movement  \n \n16. Female Reproductive System \nThe major structures of the female reproductive system include\n\n• \nOvaries  \n• \nOviducts  \n• \nUterus  \n• \nCervix  \n• \nVagina  \n• \nExternal genital structures  \n \n17. Ovaries \nThe ovaries are the female gonads. \nThey \n• \nProduce oocytes  \n• \nProduce female reproductive hormones  \nThe major hormones include \n• \nOestrogens  \n• \nProgesterone  \n \n18. Oviducts \nThe oviducts, or fallopian tubes, extend from the region of the ovaries toward the uterus. \nThey transport the oocyte toward the uterus. \nFertilisation normally occurs within the oviduct. \n \n19. Uterus \nThe uterus is a muscular organ where implantation and development of the embryo occur. \nIts wall contains three major layers \n• \nEndometrium  \n• \nMyometrium  \n• \nPerimetrium\n\n20. Endometrium \nThe endometrium is the inner lining of the uterus. \nIt undergoes cyclical changes during the menstrual cycle. \nIt becomes suitable for implantation under the influence of ovarian hormones. \n \n21. Myometrium \nThe myometrium is the thick muscular layer of the uterus. \nIt contracts during menstruation and plays a major role in labour. \n \n22. Cervix \nThe cervix is the lower portion of the uterus that connects with the vagina. \nIt produces cervical mucus and forms a passage between the uterus and vagina. \n \n23. Vagina \nThe vagina is a muscular canal that \n• \nReceives the penis during sexual intercourse  \n• \nProvides a passage for menstrual flow  \n• \nForms the birth canal  \n \n24. Gametogenesis \nGametogenesis is the formation of gametes. \nThere are two major forms \n• \nSpermatogenesis  \n• \nOogenesis  \nBoth involve meiosis but differ significantly in their timing and products.\n\n25. Spermatogenesis \nSpermatogenesis is the production of sperm cells in the seminiferous tubules of the testes. \nThe process begins with diploid spermatogonia. \n \n26. Stages of Spermatogenesis \nA simplified sequence is \nSpermatogonium \n↓ \nPrimary spermatocyte \n↓ \nSecondary spermatocytes \n↓ \nSpermatids \n↓ \nSpermatozoa \nMeiosis reduces the chromosome number from diploid to haploid. \n \n27. Spermiogenesis \nSpermiogenesis is the process through which spermatids differentiate into mature sperm cells. \nMajor changes include \n• \nFormation of the flagellum  \n• \nDevelopment of the acrosome  \n• \nCondensation of the nucleus  \n• \nReduction of excess cytoplasm  \n• \nDevelopment of mitochondria in the midpiece  \n \n28. Structure of a Sperm Cell\n\nA mature sperm consists mainly of \n• \nHead  \n• \nMidpiece  \n• \nTail  \n \n29. Sperm Head \nThe sperm head contains the haploid nucleus. \nIt also contains the acrosome, a specialised structure containing enzymes involved in penetration \nof the egg coverings. \n \n30. Sperm Midpiece \nThe midpiece contains numerous mitochondria. \nThese provide ATP required for movement of the sperm flagellum. \n \n31. Sperm Tail \nThe tail is a flagellum responsible for sperm movement. \nIts beating propels the sperm through the female reproductive tract. \n \n32. Oogenesis \nOogenesis is the formation and maturation of female gametes. \nIt begins during fetal development and follows a different pattern from spermatogenesis. \n \n33. Oogonia and Primary Oocytes \nDuring fetal development, oogonia develop into primary oocytes. \nPrimary oocytes begin meiosis but become arrested during the first meiotic division. \nMany primary oocytes undergo degeneration during development.\n\n34. Secondary Oocyte \nDuring each ovarian cycle, usually one dominant follicle releases a secondary oocyte during \novulation. \nThe secondary oocyte begins the second meiotic division but typically remains arrested until \nfertilisation. \n \n35. Polar Bodies \nCytoplasmic division during oogenesis is unequal. \nMost of the cytoplasm remains in the developing oocyte, while smaller cells called polar bodies \nare produced. \nThis allows the functional egg cell to retain substantial cytoplasmic resources. \n \n36. Menstrual Cycle \nThe menstrual cycle involves coordinated changes in \n• \nOvaries  \n• \nUterus  \n• \nHormone concentrations  \nA typical cycle is approximately 28 days, although normal cycle length varies considerably \nbetween individuals. \n \n37. Main Phases of the Menstrual Cycle \nThe cycle can be described through \n• \nMenstrual phase  \n• \nFollicular phase  \n• \nOvulation  \n• \nLuteal phase  \nThe ovarian and uterine cycles occur simultaneously.\n\n38. Menstrual Phase \nMenstruation occurs when the endometrium is shed. \nThis usually occurs when ovarian hormone concentrations fall following degeneration of the \ncorpus luteum. \n \n39. Follicular Phase \nDuring the follicular phase, ovarian follicles develop. \nFSH stimulates follicular development. \nDeveloping follicles produce increasing amounts of oestrogen. \n \n40. Ovulation \nOvulation is the release of a secondary oocyte from a mature ovarian follicle. \nIt is triggered by a rapid increase in LH concentration known as the LH surge. \n \n41. Luteal Phase \nAfter ovulation, the ruptured follicle develops into the corpus luteum. \nThe corpus luteum produces \n• \nProgesterone  \n• \nOestrogen  \nThese hormones help maintain the uterine lining. \n \n42. Corpus Luteum \nThe corpus luteum is a temporary endocrine structure formed after ovulation. \nIf fertilisation does not result in pregnancy, it eventually degenerates. \nThis causes progesterone and oestrogen levels to decline, leading to menstruation. \n \n43. Hormonal Regulation of the Menstrual Cycle\n\nThe menstrual cycle is regulated by interactions among \n• \nHypothalamus  \n• \nPituitary gland  \n• \nOvaries  \n• \nUterus  \nThe major hormones are \n• \nGnRH  \n• \nFSH  \n• \nLH  \n• \nOestrogen  \n• \nProgesterone  \n \n44. GnRH \nGonadotropin-releasing hormone, or GnRH, is released by the hypothalamus. \nIt stimulates the anterior pituitary to release \n• \nFSH  \n• \nLH  \n \n45. FSH in Females \nFSH promotes the development of ovarian follicles. \nDeveloping follicles produce oestrogen. \nFSH also contributes to the selection and maturation of a dominant follicle. \n \n46. LH in Females \nLH contributes to \n• \nFinal follicular maturation  \n• \nOvulation\n\n• \nCorpus luteum formation  \nThe LH surge is the immediate hormonal trigger for ovulation. \n \n47. Oestrogen During the Cycle \nOestrogen is produced mainly by developing ovarian follicles during the follicular phase. \nIt contributes to the growth of the endometrium. \nAt sufficiently high concentrations, oestrogen switches from predominantly negative feedback to \npositive feedback, contributing to the LH surge. \n \n48. Progesterone During the Cycle \nProgesterone is produced mainly by the corpus luteum during the luteal phase. \nIt \n• \nMaintains the endometrium  \n• \nReduces uterine contractility  \n• \nProvides negative feedback to the hypothalamus and pituitary  \n \n49. Fertilisation \nFertilisation is the fusion of male and female gametes to form a diploid zygote. \nIn humans, fertilisation normally occurs in the ampulla of the oviduct. \n \n50. Sperm Capacitation \nBefore fertilisation, sperm undergo physiological changes within the female reproductive tract. \nThis process is called capacitation. \nIt enables sperm to acquire the ability to fertilise the oocyte. \n \n51. Acrosome Reaction \nThe sperm must interact with the coverings of the oocyte.\n\nThe acrosome reaction releases enzymes that assist sperm penetration through the zona \npellucida. \n \n52. Fusion of Gametes \nAfter a sperm successfully penetrates the oocyte coverings, the sperm and oocyte membranes \nfuse. \nThis triggers changes that prevent additional sperm from entering. \nThis prevents polyspermy. \n \n53. Polyspermy \nPolyspermy is the entry of more than one sperm into an egg. \nIt normally prevents successful development. \nThe oocyte therefore undergoes mechanisms that alter its surrounding structures after \nfertilisation. \n \n54. Formation of the Zygote \nThe male and female genetic material eventually combines. \nA diploid zygote is formed. \nThe zygote begins a series of mitotic divisions called cleavage. \n \n55. Cleavage \nCleavage consists of rapid mitotic divisions of the early embryo. \nThe overall size of the embryo does not initially increase substantially because the original \ncytoplasm is divided into progressively smaller cells. \n \n56. Morula \nThe embryo develops into a compact ball of cells called the morula. \nFurther development produces a blastocyst.\n\n57. Blastocyst \nThe human blastocyst contains \n• \nTrophoblast  \n• \nInner cell mass  \n• \nBlastocoel  \nThe inner cell mass gives rise to the embryo. \nThe trophoblast contributes significantly to the formation of the placenta. \n \n58. Implantation \nImplantation occurs when the developing blastocyst attaches to and becomes embedded in the \nendometrium. \nImplantation normally occurs approximately one week after fertilisation. \n \n59. Human Chorionic Gonadotropin \nAfter implantation, trophoblast cells produce human chorionic gonadotropin, or hCG. \nhCG maintains the corpus luteum during early pregnancy. \nThis allows progesterone production to continue. \n \n60. Pregnancy \nDuring pregnancy, hormonal changes maintain the uterine environment and support fetal \ndevelopment. \nImportant hormones include \n• \nProgesterone  \n• \nOestrogens  \n• \nhCG  \n• \nHuman placental lactogen  \n• \nRelaxin\n\n61. Placenta \nThe placenta develops at the interface between maternal and fetal tissues. \nIt performs important functions including \n• \nExchange of oxygen  \n• \nExchange of carbon dioxide  \n• \nTransfer of nutrients  \n• \nRemoval of fetal wastes  \n• \nHormone production  \n• \nImmune-related functions  \n \n62. Maternal and Fetal Blood \nMaternal and fetal blood normally do not directly mix. \nExchange occurs across the placental barrier. \nThis allows substances to move between the maternal and fetal circulations. \n \n63. Umbilical Cord \nThe umbilical cord connects the fetus to the placenta. \nIt contains \n• \nOne umbilical vein  \n• \nTwo umbilical arteries  \nThe umbilical vein carries oxygenated, nutrient-rich blood toward the fetus. \nThe umbilical arteries carry blood containing carbon dioxide and metabolic wastes away from \nthe fetus toward the placenta. \n \n64. Embryonic Development \nEarly embryonic development involves several major processes\n\n• \nCleavage  \n• \nGastrulation  \n• \nOrganogenesis  \n• \nGrowth  \n• \nDifferentiation  \n \n65. Gastrulation \nGastrulation is a major developmental process that reorganises the embryo and establishes the \nthree primary germ layers. \nThese are \n• \nEctoderm  \n• \nMesoderm  \n• \nEndoderm  \n \n66. Ectoderm \nThe ectoderm gives rise to structures including \n• \nNervous system  \n• \nEpidermis of the skin  \n• \nSome sensory structures  \n \n67. Mesoderm \nThe mesoderm gives rise to structures including \n• \nMuscles  \n• \nBones  \n• \nBlood  \n• \nConnective tissues  \n• \nKidneys\n\n• \nGonads  \n• \nCardiovascular tissues  \n \n68. Endoderm \nThe endoderm contributes to the formation of the epithelial linings of \n• \nDigestive tract  \n• \nRespiratory tract  \nIt also contributes to organs such as the liver and pancreas. \n \n69. Organogenesis \nOrganogenesis is the process through which organs develop from the embryonic germ layers. \nCells undergo \n• \nDifferentiation  \n• \nMigration  \n• \nGrowth  \n• \nSpecialisation  \nThese processes establish the structures and functions of organs. \n \n70. Cell Differentiation \nCell differentiation is the process through which unspecialised cells develop specialised \nstructures and functions. \nDifferent cells express different sets of genes, resulting in specialised cell types. \n \n71. Stem Cells \nStem cells are cells capable of self-renewal and differentiation into one or more specialised cell \ntypes. \nEmbryonic development involves extensive production and differentiation of stem and \nprogenitor cells.\n\n72. Fetal Development \nAfter the embryonic period, development enters the fetal period. \nDuring this stage \n• \nOrgans continue to mature  \n• \nBody size increases  \n• \nTissues become increasingly specialised  \n• \nPhysiological systems develop further  \n \n73. Birth \nNear the end of pregnancy, hormonal and mechanical changes initiate labour. \nImportant factors include \n• \nIncreased uterine sensitivity  \n• \nOxytocin  \n• \nProstaglandins  \n• \nCervical stretching  \nOxytocin contributes to the positive feedback mechanism that strengthens uterine contractions. \n \n74. Lactation \nLactation involves the production and secretion of milk by mammary glands. \nTwo important hormones are \n• \nProlactin  \n• \nOxytocin  \nProlactin promotes milk production. \nOxytocin promotes milk ejection. \n \n75. Spermatogenesis and Oogenesis Comparison\n\nFeature \nSpermatogenesis \nOogenesis \nLocation \nTestes \nOvaries \nBegins \nAt puberty \nBefore birth \nGametes \nproduced \nFour functional sperm from one primary \nspermatocyte \nOne functional ovum from one \nprimary oocyte \nCytoplasmic \ndivision \nApproximately equal \nUnequal \nProduction \npattern \nContinuous after puberty \nCyclic after puberty \nMeiotic \ncompletion \nCompleted during sperm formation \nMeiosis II completes following \nfertilisation \n \n76. Male and Female Hormonal Regulation \nMale reproductive function is regulated through the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis. \nGnRH \n↓ \nFSH and LH \n↓ \nTestes \n↓ \nSpermatogenesis and testosterone production \nFemale reproductive function follows the same basic hormonal axis but involves cyclical \nchanges in ovarian activity. \n \n77. Puberty \nPuberty is the developmental period during which reproductive organs mature and secondary \nsexual characteristics develop. \nIt involves increased activity of the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis.\n\nChanges include \n• \nDevelopment of reproductive organs  \n• \nGamete production  \n• \nIncreased sex hormone secretion  \n• \nDevelopment of secondary sexual characteristics  \n \n78. Menopause \nMenopause is the permanent cessation of menstrual cycles resulting from loss of ovarian \nfollicular activity. \nOestrogen and progesterone production decline substantially. \n \n79. Birth Control \nContraceptive methods prevent pregnancy through different mechanisms. \nExamples include \n• \nBarrier methods  \n• \nHormonal contraceptives  \n• \nIntrauterine devices  \n• \nSterilisation  \nHormonal contraceptives can prevent ovulation and alter reproductive tract conditions. \n \n80. Key Terms \nGametogenesis \nFormation of gametes. \nSpermatogenesis \nFormation of sperm. \nOogenesis \nFormation and maturation of female gametes.\n\nFertilisation \nFusion of male and female gametes. \nZygote \nDiploid cell formed by fertilisation. \nOvulation \nRelease of a secondary oocyte from an ovarian follicle. \nCorpus luteum \nTemporary endocrine structure formed from the ruptured follicle after ovulation. \nImplantation \nAttachment and embedding of the blastocyst in the uterine lining. \nBlastocyst \nEarly embryonic structure that implants in the endometrium. \nGastrulation \nDevelopmental process that establishes the three germ layers. \nOrganogenesis \nFormation of organs during embryonic development. \nPlacenta \nTemporary organ that mediates exchange between maternal and fetal circulations and produces \nhormones. \nSpermiogenesis \nDifferentiation of spermatids into spermatozoa. \n \n81. Final Summary \nAnimal reproduction involves specialised reproductive organs, gamete formation, fertilisation \nand development. \nIn males, sperm are produced continuously after puberty through spermatogenesis in the \nseminiferous tubules. In females, oogenesis begins before birth, and ovarian follicles undergo \ncyclical development after puberty.\n\nThe reproductive system is regulated through the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis. GnRH \nstimulates the pituitary to release FSH and LH, which regulate gonadal activity and reproductive \nhormone production. \nIn females, coordinated changes in FSH, LH, oestrogen and progesterone produce the menstrual \ncycle. The LH surge causes ovulation, while the corpus luteum produces progesterone during the \nluteal phase. \nFertilisation produces a diploid zygote. Cleavage produces the morula and blastocyst, followed \nby implantation in the uterine endometrium. Gastrulation establishes the ectoderm, mesoderm \nand endoderm, which subsequently contribute to the formation of different tissues and organs. \nThe placenta supports exchange between the mother and developing fetus and also functions as \nan endocrine organ. Hormonal mechanisms involving hCG, progesterone, oestrogens and \noxytocin are essential for maintaining pregnancy and coordinating childbirth.	COMPLETED	101	2026-08-19 18:11:39.26607	2026-08-19 18:12:51.882892	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
982	Lesson 10: Support and movement in animals - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 10: Support and movement in animals	PDF	uploads/pdf/576ee4ee30964fe183b9e024093cd7cf.pdf	\N	Lesson 10 — Support and Movement in Animals \nIntroduction \nAnimals require structural support to maintain body shape, protect internal organs and produce \ncontrolled movement. Different animal groups have evolved specialised support systems \naccording to their body structure and environment. \nIn humans, support and movement depend mainly on the skeletal system, joints and muscular \nsystem. Bones provide a rigid framework, while skeletal muscles generate forces that move \nbones around joints. \n \n1. Functions of the Skeletal System \nThe human skeleton performs several important functions. \n• \nProvides structural support  \n• \nProtects internal organs  \n• \nAllows movement  \n• \nProvides attachment sites for muscles  \n• \nStores minerals  \n• \nProduces blood cells  \n• \nStores energy in the form of lipids within yellow bone marrow  \n \n2. Types of Skeletal Systems \nAnimals possess three major types of skeletal organisation. \n• \nHydrostatic skeleton  \n• \nExoskeleton  \n• \nEndoskeleton  \n \n3. Hydrostatic Skeleton \nA hydrostatic skeleton consists of fluid contained within a body cavity or compartment \nsurrounded by muscles. \nMuscular contractions change the pressure and shape of the fluid-filled structure.\n\nExamples include \n• \nEarthworms  \n• \nCnidarians  \n• \nSome soft-bodied animals  \n \n4. Exoskeleton \nAn exoskeleton is a rigid supporting structure located outside the body. \nArthropods possess exoskeletons mainly composed of chitin. \nFunctions include \n• \nProtection  \n• \nSupport  \n• \nPrevention of water loss  \n• \nMuscle attachment  \nBecause the exoskeleton cannot expand continuously, arthropods must periodically shed it during \ngrowth. \nThis process is called ecdysis. \n \n5. Endoskeleton \nAn endoskeleton is a supporting structure located inside the body. \nVertebrates possess an endoskeleton composed mainly of \n• \nBone  \n• \nCartilage  \nThe endoskeleton grows with the animal and provides attachment sites for muscles. \n \n6. Human Skeleton \nThe adult human skeleton normally contains approximately 206 bones. \nIt can be divided into\n\n• \nAxial skeleton  \n• \nAppendicular skeleton  \n \n7. Axial Skeleton \nThe axial skeleton forms the central framework of the body. \nIt includes \n• \nSkull  \n• \nVertebral column  \n• \nRibs  \n• \nSternum  \nIts major functions include support and protection of the brain, spinal cord and organs of the \nthoracic cavity. \n \n8. Appendicular Skeleton \nThe appendicular skeleton includes the bones associated with the limbs and their supporting \nstructures. \nIt includes \n• \nPectoral girdles  \n• \nUpper limbs  \n• \nPelvic girdle  \n• \nLower limbs  \nIt is particularly important for movement. \n \n9. Bone \nBone is a specialised connective tissue. \nIt consists of cells embedded within a mineralised extracellular matrix. \nThe matrix contains substantial amounts of \n• \nCollagen\n\n• \nCalcium salts  \n• \nPhosphate compounds  \n \n10. Functions of Bone \nBone provides \n• \nMechanical support  \n• \nProtection  \n• \nMuscle attachment  \n• \nMineral storage  \n• \nBlood cell production  \n• \nEnergy storage  \n \n11. Bone Cells \nMajor bone cell types include \n• \nOsteoblasts  \n• \nOsteocytes  \n• \nOsteoclasts  \n \n12. Osteoblasts \nOsteoblasts are bone-forming cells. \nThey produce components of the bone matrix and contribute to bone formation. \n \n13. Osteocytes \nOsteocytes are mature bone cells located within spaces called lacunae. \nThey maintain the surrounding bone matrix and communicate with other bone cells through \ncellular extensions.\n\n14. Osteoclasts \nOsteoclasts are large cells responsible for bone resorption. \nThey break down bone tissue and release minerals into the surrounding fluid. \n \n15. Bone Remodelling \nBone is continuously remodelled throughout life. \nThis involves coordinated activity of osteoclasts and osteoblasts. \nBone remodelling helps \n• \nMaintain bone strength  \n• \nRepair microscopic damage  \n• \nRegulate mineral levels  \n• \nAdapt bones to mechanical stresses  \n \n16. Compact Bone \nCompact bone is dense bone tissue forming the outer regions of many bones. \nIt is particularly well developed in the shafts of long bones. \nCompact bone provides substantial mechanical strength. \n \n17. Osteon \nThe structural unit of compact bone is called an osteon, or Haversian system. \nAn osteon consists of \n• \nCentral canal  \n• \nConcentric lamellae  \n• \nLacunae  \n• \nCanaliculi  \n \n18. Central Canal\n\nThe central canal contains \n• \nBlood vessels  \n• \nNerves  \n• \nConnective tissue  \nIt runs through the centre of an osteon. \n \n19. Concentric Lamellae \nConcentric lamellae are layers of mineralised bone matrix arranged around the central canal. \nTheir organisation contributes to the strength of compact bone. \n \n20. Lacunae \nLacunae are small spaces within the bone matrix. \nOsteocytes occupy these spaces. \n \n21. Canaliculi \nCanaliculi are tiny channels connecting lacunae. \nThey allow communication between osteocytes and facilitate exchange of substances through the \nbone tissue. \n \n22. Spongy Bone \nSpongy bone, also called cancellous or trabecular bone, contains a network of thin structures \ncalled trabeculae. \nIt is lighter than compact bone. \nSpongy bone is commonly found at the ends of long bones and inside many flat bones. \n \n23. Bone Marrow \nBone marrow occurs within spaces of bones. \nTwo major types are\n\n• \nRed bone marrow  \n• \nYellow bone marrow  \nRed marrow is involved in blood cell production. \nYellow marrow contains substantial amounts of stored fat. \n \n24. Cartilage \nCartilage is a flexible connective tissue that provides support and reduces friction. \nIt contains cells called chondrocytes embedded in an extracellular matrix. \nCartilage generally lacks blood vessels, so nutrients reach its cells mainly by diffusion. \n \n25. Types of Cartilage \nThe three major types are \n• \nHyaline cartilage  \n• \nElastic cartilage  \n• \nFibrocartilage  \n \n26. Hyaline Cartilage \nHyaline cartilage provides support while allowing some flexibility. \nIt occurs in structures such as \n• \nArticular surfaces of joints  \n• \nTracheal rings  \n• \nNasal structures  \n• \nCostal cartilage  \n \n27. Elastic Cartilage \nElastic cartilage contains numerous elastic fibres. \nIt provides flexible support.\n\nExamples include \n• \nExternal ear  \n• \nEpiglottis  \n \n28. Fibrocartilage \nFibrocartilage is particularly strong and resistant to compression. \nIt occurs in structures such as \n• \nIntervertebral discs  \n• \nPubic symphysis  \n• \nSome joint structures  \n \n29. Joints \nA joint, or articulation, is a region where two or more bones meet. \nJoints provide varying degrees of \n• \nStability  \n• \nFlexibility  \n• \nMovement  \n \n30. Structural Classification of Joints \nStructurally, joints can be classified as \n• \nFibrous joints  \n• \nCartilaginous joints  \n• \nSynovial joints  \n \n31. Fibrous Joints \nFibrous joints are connected by dense connective tissue. \nThey generally allow little or no movement.\n\nAn example is the joint between bones of the skull. \n \n32. Cartilaginous Joints \nCartilaginous joints are connected by cartilage. \nThey allow limited movement. \nExamples include \n• \nIntervertebral joints  \n• \nPubic symphysis  \n \n33. Synovial Joints \nSynovial joints are freely movable joints. \nThey are characterised by \n• \nSynovial cavity  \n• \nArticular cartilage  \n• \nFibrous joint capsule  \n• \nSynovial membrane  \n• \nSynovial fluid  \n \n34. Articular Cartilage \nArticular cartilage covers the surfaces of bones within synovial joints. \nIt \n• \nReduces friction  \n• \nAbsorbs mechanical stress  \n• \nProtects bone surfaces  \n \n35. Synovial Fluid \nSynovial fluid is found within the joint cavity.\n\nIt \n• \nLubricates the joint  \n• \nReduces friction  \n• \nHelps nourish articular cartilage  \n• \nContributes to shock absorption  \n \n36. Joint Capsule \nThe joint capsule surrounds the synovial joint. \nIt helps \n• \nHold the bones together  \n• \nProtect the joint  \n• \nMaintain the synovial cavity  \n \n37. Ligaments \nLigaments are strong connective tissue structures that connect bone to bone. \nThey stabilise joints and limit excessive movement. \n \n38. Tendons \nTendons connect skeletal muscles to bones. \nWhen a muscle contracts, tension is transmitted through the tendon to the bone. \nThis produces movement around joints. \n \n39. Types of Synovial Joints \nMajor types include \n• \nBall-and-socket joints  \n• \nHinge joints  \n• \nPivot joints\n\n• \nCondyloid joints  \n• \nSaddle joints  \n• \nPlane joints  \n \n40. Ball-and-Socket Joint \nA ball-and-socket joint allows movement in several directions. \nExamples include \n• \nShoulder  \n• \nHip  \nMovements can include \n• \nFlexion  \n• \nExtension  \n• \nAbduction  \n• \nAdduction  \n• \nRotation  \n \n41. Hinge Joint \nA hinge joint mainly permits movement in one plane. \nExamples include \n• \nElbow  \n• \nKnee  \nThe main movements are \n• \nFlexion  \n• \nExtension  \n \n42. Pivot Joint \nA pivot joint allows rotation around an axis.\n\nAn example is the joint between the first and second cervical vertebrae. \nIt allows rotation of the head. \n \n43. Condyloid Joint \nA condyloid joint permits movement in two main planes. \nAn example is the wrist joint. \nIt allows movements such as \n• \nFlexion  \n• \nExtension  \n• \nAbduction  \n• \nAdduction  \n \n44. Saddle Joint \nA saddle joint allows movement in two planes. \nAn important example is the joint at the base of the thumb. \nIt allows the thumb to perform movements important for grasping. \n \n45. Plane Joint \nPlane joints allow bones to slide or glide against one another. \nExamples occur between some bones of the wrist and ankle. \n \n46. Skeletal Muscle \nSkeletal muscle is specialised muscle tissue responsible mainly for voluntary movement of the \nskeleton. \nSkeletal muscles also contribute to \n• \nMaintaining posture  \n• \nStabilising joints\n\n• \nProducing heat  \n \n47. Types of Muscle Tissue \nThere are three major types of muscle tissue. \n• \nSkeletal muscle  \n• \nCardiac muscle  \n• \nSmooth muscle  \n \n48. Skeletal Muscle \nSkeletal muscle is \n• \nStriated  \n• \nUsually voluntary  \n• \nMultinucleated  \n• \nAttached mainly to bones  \nIt produces body movement. \n \n49. Cardiac Muscle \nCardiac muscle is found in the heart. \nIt is \n• \nStriated  \n• \nInvoluntary  \n• \nUsually composed of branched cells  \n• \nConnected through specialised junctions  \nIt contracts rhythmically to pump blood. \n \n50. Smooth Muscle \nSmooth muscle occurs in the walls of structures such as\n\n• \nIntestines  \n• \nBlood vessels  \n• \nUrinary bladder  \n• \nUterus  \nIt is \n• \nNon-striated  \n• \nInvoluntary  \n• \nSpecialised for sustained contractions  \n \n51. Muscle Fibre \nA skeletal muscle is composed of many elongated muscle cells called muscle fibres. \nEach muscle fibre contains numerous myofibrils. \nMyofibrils contain the contractile proteins responsible for muscle contraction. \n \n52. Myofibrils \nMyofibrils are long cylindrical structures within skeletal muscle fibres. \nThey consist of repeating units called sarcomeres. \nSarcomeres are the fundamental contractile units of skeletal muscle. \n \n53. Sarcomere \nA sarcomere extends from one Z disc to the next. \nIts major protein structures include \n• \nActin  \n• \nMyosin  \n• \nTroponin  \n• \nTropomyosin\n\n54. Actin \nActin forms the major component of the thin filaments. \nActin contains binding sites for myosin heads. \nThese sites are normally covered by tropomyosin when the muscle is relaxed. \n \n55. Myosin \nMyosin forms the thick filaments. \nEach myosin molecule has heads capable of \n• \nBinding actin  \n• \nHydrolysing ATP  \n• \nProducing movement of the actin filament  \n \n56. Troponin \nTroponin is a regulatory protein associated with the thin filament. \nWhen calcium ions bind to troponin, the regulatory complex changes shape. \nThis allows tropomyosin to move away from the myosin-binding sites on actin. \n \n57. Tropomyosin \nTropomyosin lies along the actin filament. \nAt rest, it blocks the myosin-binding sites. \nCalcium-triggered changes involving troponin move tropomyosin and expose the binding sites. \n \n58. Sliding-Filament Theory \nThe sliding-filament theory explains muscle contraction. \nDuring contraction \n• \nActin filaments slide toward the centre of the sarcomere  \n• \nSarcomeres shorten\n\n• \nThe muscle fibre shortens  \nThe actin and myosin filaments themselves do not significantly shorten. \n \n59. Neuromuscular Junction \nA neuromuscular junction is the synapse between a motor neuron and a skeletal muscle fibre. \nThe neurotransmitter released is usually acetylcholine. \n \n60. Excitation of Muscle \nThe sequence begins when an action potential reaches the motor neuron terminal. \nAction potential \n↓ \nAcetylcholine release \n↓ \nAcetylcholine binds receptors on muscle membrane \n↓ \nMuscle action potential generated \n↓ \nSignal travels along the muscle fibre \n↓ \nCalcium released inside the muscle cell \n↓ \nMuscle contraction begins \n \n61. Sarcoplasmic Reticulum \nThe sarcoplasmic reticulum is a specialised form of endoplasmic reticulum in muscle cells. \nIt stores calcium ions.\n\nWhen a muscle action potential reaches the appropriate structures, calcium is released from the \nsarcoplasmic reticulum. \n \n62. Calcium and Contraction \nAt rest, calcium concentration in the muscle cytoplasm is low. \nWhen calcium is released \n↓ \nCalcium binds to troponin \n↓ \nTropomyosin moves \n↓ \nMyosin-binding sites on actin become exposed \n↓ \nMyosin binds actin \n↓ \nCross-bridge cycling occurs \n↓ \nThe sarcomere contracts \n \n63. Cross-Bridge Formation \nA myosin head binds to an exposed site on actin. \nThis forms an actin-myosin cross-bridge. \nThe myosin head then undergoes a conformational change that moves the actin filament. \n \n64. Power Stroke \nThe power stroke occurs when the myosin head pivots and pulls the actin filament toward the \ncentre of the sarcomere. \nADP and inorganic phosphate are released during this process.\n\n65. ATP and Muscle Contraction \nATP is essential for the cross-bridge cycle. \nATP binding to myosin causes the myosin head to detach from actin. \nATP hydrolysis then re-energises the myosin head. \nTherefore, ATP is required both for contraction cycling and for relaxation. \n \n66. Cross-Bridge Cycle \nThe simplified cycle is \nATP binds myosin \n↓ \nMyosin detaches from actin \n↓ \nATP is hydrolysed \n↓ \nMyosin head becomes energised \n↓ \nMyosin binds actin \n↓ \nPower stroke occurs \n↓ \nADP and phosphate are released \n↓ \nNew ATP binds \n↓ \nThe cycle repeats\n\n67. Role of ATP in Muscle \nATP is required for \n• \nDetachment of myosin from actin  \n• \nRe-cocking of myosin heads  \n• \nCalcium ion pumping into the sarcoplasmic reticulum  \nWithout ATP, normal muscle relaxation cannot occur. \n \n68. Muscle Relaxation \nWhen neural stimulation stops \n• \nCalcium release decreases  \n• \nCalcium is actively pumped back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum  \n• \nCytoplasmic calcium concentration decreases  \n• \nCalcium dissociates from troponin  \n• \nTropomyosin returns to its blocking position  \n• \nCross-bridge formation decreases  \n• \nThe muscle relaxes  \n \n69. Changes Within a Sarcomere \nDuring contraction \n• \nZ discs move closer together  \n• \nI band becomes shorter  \n• \nH zone becomes shorter or may disappear  \n• \nA band remains approximately constant  \nThe A band corresponds mainly to the length of the thick filament. \n \n70. Muscle Antagonism \nMany movements require muscles to work in opposing pairs.\n\nAn antagonistic muscle pair consists of muscles that produce opposite movements. \nAn example is \n• \nBiceps  \n• \nTriceps  \n \n71. Biceps and Triceps \nDuring elbow flexion \n• \nBiceps contracts  \n• \nTriceps relaxes  \nDuring elbow extension \n• \nTriceps contracts  \n• \nBiceps relaxes  \nThis arrangement allows controlled movement around the joint. \n \n72. Isotonic Contraction \nDuring an isotonic contraction, muscle length changes while tension is used to produce \nmovement. \nExamples include \n• \nLifting an object  \n• \nWalking  \n• \nRunning  \n \n73. Isometric Contraction \nDuring an isometric contraction, muscle tension increases but muscle length remains \napproximately constant. \nAn example is holding a heavy object stationary. \n \n74. Muscle Fatigue\n\nMuscle fatigue is a decline in the ability of a muscle to maintain force during sustained activity. \nIt can involve several factors including \n• \nReduced energy availability  \n• \nAccumulation of metabolites  \n• \nChanges in ion balance  \n• \nImpaired excitation-contraction processes  \n \n75. Energy Sources for Muscle \nMuscle cells require ATP. \nATP can be supplied through \n• \nExisting ATP stores  \n• \nPhosphocreatine  \n• \nAerobic respiration  \n• \nAnaerobic glycolysis  \nThe relative contribution depends on the intensity and duration of activity. \n \n76. Bone and Muscle Interaction \nBones and muscles work together as a mechanical system. \nThe general arrangement is \nMuscle contraction \n↓ \nTendon tension \n↓ \nForce applied to bone \n↓ \nMovement around a joint \nThe skeleton provides the rigid framework while muscles provide active force.\n\n77. Levers in the Human Body \nBones can act as levers. \nJoints act as pivot points. \nMuscles provide the force required to move the lever. \nThe arrangement of bones and muscles determines mechanical advantage and movement. \n \n78. Posture \nSkeletal muscles continuously make small adjustments to maintain posture. \nPostural muscles generate relatively sustained contractions to keep the body balanced against \ngravity. \n \n79. Calcium and Bone \nCalcium is essential for \n• \nBone mineralisation  \n• \nMuscle contraction  \n• \nNervous system function  \n• \nBlood clotting  \nThe body therefore maintains blood calcium within a relatively narrow range. \n \n80. Bone and Calcium Homeostasis \nWhen blood calcium decreases, PTH secretion increases. \nPTH promotes mechanisms that increase blood calcium. \nWhen blood calcium is sufficiently high, PTH secretion decreases. \nThis contributes to calcium homeostasis. \n \n81. Bone Growth\n\nLong bones increase in length at the epiphyseal growth plates during childhood and \nadolescence. \nCartilage cells divide and enlarge, and the cartilage is gradually replaced by bone. \nGrowth eventually stops when the growth plates close. \n \n82. Bone Repair \nWhen a bone is fractured, repair involves several stages. \nThese include \n• \nFormation of a blood clot  \n• \nFormation of a soft callus  \n• \nFormation of a hard callus  \n• \nBone remodelling  \nOsteoblasts and osteoclasts participate in the repair process. \n \n83. Osteoporosis \nOsteoporosis is a condition characterised by reduced bone mass and deterioration of bone \nstructure. \nThis increases susceptibility to fractures. \nRisk is influenced by factors including \n• \nAge  \n• \nHormonal changes  \n• \nNutrition  \n• \nPhysical activity  \n• \nGenetic factors  \n \n84. Importance of Mechanical Loading \nRegular mechanical loading stimulates bone maintenance and remodelling. \nWeight-bearing physical activity can therefore contribute to maintaining bone strength.\n\n85. Support and Movement in Different Animals \nDifferent animals use different structures for movement. \nAnimal group Main support system \nExample \nCnidarians \nHydrostatic support \nHydra \nAnnelids \nHydrostatic skeleton \nEarthworm \nArthropods \nExoskeleton \nInsect \nMolluscs \nShell and muscular structures Snail \nVertebrates \nEndoskeleton \nHuman \n \n86. Key Terms \nEndoskeleton \nAn internal supporting skeleton. \nExoskeleton \nAn external supporting structure. \nHydrostatic skeleton \nA support system based on fluid pressure and surrounding muscles. \nOsteoblast \nBone-forming cell. \nOsteocyte \nMature bone cell that maintains bone tissue. \nOsteoclast \nCell responsible for bone resorption. \nOsteon \nStructural unit of compact bone. \nJoint\n\nAn articulation between bones. \nLigament \nConnective tissue joining bone to bone. \nTendon \nConnective tissue joining muscle to bone. \nSarcomere \nFunctional contractile unit of skeletal muscle. \nActin \nProtein forming the main component of thin filaments. \nMyosin \nMotor protein forming thick filaments. \nSliding-filament theory \nModel explaining muscle contraction through the sliding of actin and myosin filaments. \nNeuromuscular junction \nSynapse between a motor neuron and skeletal muscle fibre. \n \n87. Final Summary \nAnimal support systems include hydrostatic skeletons, exoskeletons and endoskeletons. \nVertebrates possess an internal skeleton composed primarily of bone and cartilage. \nThe human skeleton provides support, protection, movement, mineral storage and blood cell \nproduction. Bone contains specialised cells including osteoblasts, osteocytes and osteoclasts, \nwhich participate in continuous bone formation and remodelling. \nJoints connect bones and determine the type and range of movement possible. Synovial joints \ncontain structures such as articular cartilage, synovial fluid, ligaments and joint capsules that \nallow controlled movement while maintaining stability. \nMovement is produced mainly through skeletal muscle. Muscle fibres contain myofibrils \ncomposed of repeating sarcomeres. The interaction between actin and myosin produces \ncontraction according to the sliding-filament theory. \nAn action potential at the neuromuscular junction leads to acetylcholine release, muscle \nexcitation and calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Calcium allows actin-myosin\n\ninteractions, while ATP powers the cross-bridge cycle and calcium reuptake required for \nrelaxation. \nMuscles frequently work in antagonistic pairs to control movement around joints. Together, the \nskeletal and muscular systems provide the mechanical framework required for posture, \nlocomotion and coordinated movement.	COMPLETED	102	2026-08-19 18:11:40.464224	2026-08-19 18:12:51.882894	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
983	Lesson 1: Mendelian inheritance and monohybrid crosses - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 1: Mendelian inheritance and monohybrid crosses	PDF	uploads/pdf/e44e04bf7c7d481d9fcd74f1ffe2ccf1.pdf	\N	Lesson 1 — Mendelian Inheritance and Monohybrid Crosses \nIntroduction \nGenetics is the branch of biology concerned with heredity and variation. Heredity refers to the \ntransmission of biological characteristics from parents to offspring, while variation describes \ndifferences between individuals of the same species. \nThe foundations of classical genetics were established through the experiments of Gregor \nMendel, who studied inheritance patterns in garden pea plants. \nMendel's experiments led to fundamental principles that explain how hereditary factors are \ntransmitted between generations. \n \n1. Heredity \nHeredity is the transmission of genetic information from parents to their offspring. \nGenetic information influences characteristics such as \n• \nEye colour  \n• \nBlood group  \n• \nPlant height  \n• \nSeed colour  \n• \nHair characteristics  \n• \nSusceptibility to certain genetic conditions  \n \n2. Variation \nVariation refers to differences in characteristics among individuals of a population. \nVariation may arise from \n• \nGenetic differences  \n• \nEnvironmental influences  \n• \nInteraction between genes and the environment  \nGenetic variation provides the basis for evolutionary change.\n\n3. Genetics \nGenetics is the scientific study of \n• \nGenes  \n• \nHeredity  \n• \nGenetic variation  \n• \nTransmission of characteristics  \nModern genetics extends from Mendelian inheritance to molecular genetics and genomics. \n \n4. Gregor Mendel \nGregor Mendel was an Austrian monk and scientist who conducted important inheritance \nexperiments using the garden pea, Pisum sativum. \nHis experiments established important principles of inheritance. \nMendel selected pea plants because they \n• \nHave clearly distinguishable characteristics  \n• \nCan self-pollinate  \n• \nCan also be cross-pollinated artificially  \n• \nHave relatively short generation times  \n• \nProduce many offspring  \n• \nHave several easily observable contrasting characteristics  \n \n5. Mendel's Experimental Approach \nMendel studied characteristics individually and systematically recorded the characteristics \nappearing in successive generations. \nHe began with plants that consistently produced the same characteristic when self-pollinated. \nThese plants were considered true-breeding. \n \n6. True-Breeding Organisms\n\nA true-breeding organism produces offspring with the same characteristic when crossed with \nanother organism of the same genetic type or when self-fertilised. \nFor a single gene, a true-breeding organism is generally homozygous. \nFor example \nTT \nrepresents a homozygous dominant genotype. \ntt \nrepresents a homozygous recessive genotype. \n \n7. Characteristics Studied by Mendel \nMendel studied several contrasting characteristics in pea plants. \nExamples include \n• \nTall plants and dwarf plants  \n• \nYellow seeds and green seeds  \n• \nRound seeds and wrinkled seeds  \n• \nPurple flowers and white flowers  \n• \nGreen pods and yellow pods  \nThese contrasting forms helped Mendel identify predictable inheritance patterns. \n \n8. Character \nA character is a heritable feature of an organism. \nFor example \nPlant height \nis a character. \nDifferent forms of that character are called traits. \n \n9. Trait\n\nA trait is a particular form of a character. \nFor plant height \n• \nTall  \n• \nDwarf  \ncan represent alternative traits. \n \n10. Gene \nA gene is a segment of DNA that contains information influencing a biological characteristic or \nfunctional product. \nGenes are located at specific positions on chromosomes called loci. \n \n11. Alleles \nDifferent forms of the same gene are called alleles. \nFor example, a gene influencing plant height may have two alleles represented as \nT \nand \nt \nThe alleles occupy corresponding loci on homologous chromosomes. \n \n12. Dominant Allele \nA dominant allele is expressed in the phenotype when present in either a homozygous or \nheterozygous condition, under a simple Mendelian model. \nIt is commonly represented using a capital letter. \nFor example \nT \nmay represent the allele for tallness. \n \n13. Recessive Allele\n\nA recessive allele is expressed in the phenotype under a simple Mendelian model only when the \ndominant allele is absent. \nIt is commonly represented using a lowercase letter. \nFor example \nt \nmay represent the allele for dwarfness. \n \n14. Genotype \nThe genotype is the genetic constitution of an organism with respect to one or more genes. \nExamples include \n• \nTT  \n• \nTt  \n• \ntt  \n \n15. Phenotype \nThe phenotype is the observable characteristic of an organism. \nFor example \n• \nTall plant  \n• \nDwarf plant  \nPhenotype results from genetic factors and, for many characteristics, environmental influences. \n \n16. Homozygous \nAn organism is homozygous when it possesses two identical alleles for a particular gene. \nExamples \nTT \nand \ntt\n\n17. Heterozygous \nAn organism is heterozygous when it possesses two different alleles for a particular gene. \nExample \nTt \n \n18. Homologous Chromosomes \nHomologous chromosomes are chromosome pairs that \n• \nHave similar structure  \n• \nCarry genes for the same characteristics at corresponding loci  \n• \nInclude one chromosome inherited from each parent  \nThe alleles at corresponding loci may be identical or different. \n \n19. Locus \nA locus is the specific position of a gene or DNA sequence on a chromosome. \nFor a pair of homologous chromosomes, corresponding genes occupy corresponding loci. \n \n20. Mendel's First Principle \nMendel's observations led to the principle commonly called the law of segregation. \nThe two alleles of a gene separate during gamete formation. \nTherefore, each gamete receives only one allele of a particular gene. \n \n21. Law of Segregation \nConsider a heterozygous individual \nTt \nDuring meiosis, the two alleles separate. \nThe resulting gametes contain either\n\nT \nor \nt \nEach gamete therefore carries only one allele from the pair. \n \n22. Why Segregation Occurs \nDuring meiosis, homologous chromosomes separate. \nSince the alleles of a gene are located at corresponding loci on homologous chromosomes, their \nseparation results in different gametes receiving different alleles. \n \n23. Gametes \nA gamete is a reproductive cell containing a haploid set of chromosomes. \nExamples include \n• \nSperm  \n• \nEgg cells  \nGametes produced through meiosis contain one allele of each gene. \n \n24. Fertilisation \nDuring fertilisation, two haploid gametes fuse to form a diploid zygote. \nOne allele is inherited from each parent. \nTherefore, the offspring receives a pair of alleles for each autosomal gene. \n \n25. Monohybrid Cross \nA monohybrid cross is a genetic cross involving one pair of contrasting traits or one gene under \nconsideration. \nFor example \nTT × tt \ncan be used to study inheritance of plant height.\n\n26. Parental Generation \nThe original parents used in a genetic cross are called the P generation. \nFor example \nTT × tt \nThe offspring produced are called the first filial generation. \n \n27. First Filial Generation \nThe offspring of the parental generation are called the F₁ generation. \nFor \nTT × tt \nthe gametes are \nT \nand \nt \nTherefore, all F₁ offspring are \nTt \n \n28. F₁ Phenotype \nIf T is dominant for tallness, the F₁ genotype \nTt \nproduces a tall phenotype. \nTherefore \nF₁ genotype \n100% Tt \nF₁ phenotype \n100% tall\n\n29. F₂ Generation \nWhen F₁ individuals are crossed with each other \nTt × Tt \nthe resulting offspring form the F₂ generation. \nEach parent produces two possible types of gametes \n• \nT  \n• \nt  \n \n30. Punnett Square \nA Punnett square is a diagram used to predict possible genetic combinations in offspring. \nFor the cross \nTt × Tt \nthe possible combinations are \n T t \nT TT Tt \nt Tt tt \n \n31. F₂ Genotypic Ratio \nThe Punnett square gives \n• \n1 TT  \n• \n2 Tt  \n• \n1 tt  \nTherefore, the genotypic ratio is \n1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt \n \n32. F₂ Phenotypic Ratio\n\nIf T is completely dominant over t \n• \nTT is tall  \n• \nTt is tall  \n• \ntt is dwarf  \nTherefore, the phenotypic ratio is \n3 tall : 1 dwarf \n \n33. Important Mendelian Ratio \nFor a typical monohybrid cross between two heterozygous individuals \nTt × Tt \nthe expected ratios are \nGenotypic ratio \n1 : 2 : 1 \nPhenotypic ratio \n3 : 1 \nThe 3:1 ratio applies when complete dominance exists and the gene behaves according to simple \nMendelian assumptions. \n \n34. Test Cross \nA test cross is used to determine the genotype of an individual showing a dominant phenotype. \nThe individual is crossed with a homozygous recessive individual. \nFor example \nT_ × tt \nThe unknown genotype could be \nTT \nor \nTt\n\n35. Test Cross with Homozygous Dominant \nIf the unknown individual is \nTT \nthen \nTT × tt \nproduces \n100% Tt \nAll offspring are tall. \n \n36. Test Cross with Heterozygous Individual \nIf the unknown individual is \nTt \nthen \nTt × tt \nproduces \n t t \nT Tt Tt \nt tt tt \nThe expected offspring are \n• \n50% Tt  \n• \n50% tt  \nPhenotypically \n1 tall : 1 dwarf \n \n37. Backcross\n\nA backcross is a cross between an offspring and one of its parents or an individual genetically \nsimilar to a parent. \nA test cross is a specific type of backcross when the individual is crossed with a homozygous \nrecessive organism. \n \n38. Probability in Genetics \nProbability is used to predict the likelihood of particular genetic outcomes. \nFor example, in \nTt × Tt \neach offspring has a probability of \n1/4 \nof being TT, \n1/2 \nof being Tt, \nand \n1/4 \nof being tt. \n \n39. Product Rule \nThe product rule is used when two independent events must both occur. \nThe probability of both events occurring is obtained by multiplying their individual probabilities. \nFor example \nIf the probability of event A is 1/2 and event B is 1/2 \nP(A and B) = 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4 \n \n40. Sum Rule \nThe sum rule is used when either of two mutually exclusive outcomes can occur. \nThe probabilities are added.\n\nFor example \nIn a Tt × Tt cross, an offspring can be heterozygous through \n• \nT from the mother and t from the father  \n• \nt from the mother and T from the father  \nTherefore \n1/4 + 1/4 = 1/2 \n \n41. Reciprocal Crosses \nA reciprocal cross reverses the sexes of the parental genotypes. \nFor example \nMale TT × Female tt \ncan be compared with \nMale tt × Female TT \nFor autosomal genes with simple inheritance, reciprocal crosses generally produce the same \nexpected offspring ratios. \n \n42. Mendelian Inheritance \nMendelian inheritance describes patterns in which alleles segregate and are transmitted \naccording to predictable principles. \nThe simplest model assumes \n• \nOne gene  \n• \nTwo alleles  \n• \nComplete dominance  \n• \nEqual probability of allele transmission  \n• \nNormal meiosis  \n• \nRandom fertilisation  \n \n43. Mendel's Law of Dominance\n\nIn a simple dominant-recessive system, the dominant allele determines the phenotype of a \nheterozygote. \nFor example \nTt \nhas the same phenotype as \nTT \nwhen T is completely dominant. \nThe recessive phenotype appears only in \ntt \n \n44. Limitations of Simple Mendelian Dominance \nNot all characteristics follow complete dominance. \nOther inheritance patterns include \n• \nIncomplete dominance  \n• \nCodominance  \n• \nMultiple alleles  \n• \nPolygenic inheritance  \n• \nSex-linked inheritance  \n• \nGene linkage  \nThese patterns are studied in later genetics lessons. \n \n45. Autosomal Inheritance \nAn autosomal gene is located on one of the non-sex chromosomes. \nAutosomal genes are generally inherited similarly in males and females. \n \n46. Sex Chromosomes \nSex chromosomes are chromosomes involved in sex determination.\n\nIn humans \n• \nFemales generally have XX  \n• \nMales generally have XY  \nThe remaining chromosomes are called autosomes. \n \n47. Carrier \nA carrier is an individual who possesses a recessive allele associated with a particular genetic \ncondition but does not express the recessive phenotype under a simple recessive model. \nFor example \nAa \ncan represent a carrier of a recessive allele a. \n \n48. Genetic Cross Notation \nCommon symbols include \nP \nParental generation \nF₁ \nFirst filial generation \nF₂ \nSecond filial generation \n× \nCross \n♂ \nMale \n♀ \nFemale \n→\n\nProduces \n \n49. Genotypic and Phenotypic Ratios \nA genetic ratio can describe either genotype or phenotype. \nFor example, in \nAa × Aa \nthe genotypes are \n1 AA : 2 Aa : 1 aa \nIf A is completely dominant, the phenotypes are \n3 dominant : 1 recessive \nThese two ratios should not be confused. \n \n50. Probability Versus Ratio \nA ratio describes the relative expected frequency of outcomes. \nA probability describes the likelihood of an outcome. \nFor example \n1 AA : 2 Aa : 1 aa \ncorresponds to \n• \n25% AA  \n• \n50% Aa  \n• \n25% aa  \n \n51. Independent Offspring Events \nThe genetic outcome of one offspring does not normally determine the genetic outcome of \nanother offspring. \nFor example, if two heterozygous parents have a child with a recessive genotype, this does not \nchange the probability that their next child will have the same genotype. \nEach fertilisation represents a separate probability event.\n\n52. Pedigree Analysis \nA pedigree is a diagram representing the inheritance of a characteristic through generations of a \nfamily. \nCommon symbols include \n• \nSquare for male  \n• \nCircle for female  \n• \nShaded symbol for an affected individual  \n• \nHorizontal line for a mating relationship  \n• \nVertical line for offspring  \nPedigrees can help determine possible inheritance patterns. \n \n53. Dominant Pedigree Pattern \nA dominant characteristic often appears in successive generations. \nAn affected individual generally has at least one affected parent, assuming complete penetrance \nand no new mutation. \nBoth males and females can be affected by an autosomal dominant characteristic. \n \n54. Recessive Pedigree Pattern \nA recessive characteristic can appear in offspring even when neither parent displays the \ncharacteristic. \nThis occurs when both parents are carriers. \nFor example \nAa × Aa \ncan produce \naa \noffspring.\n\n55. Genetic Variation and Meiosis \nMeiosis contributes to genetic variation through \n• \nSegregation of homologous chromosomes  \n• \nIndependent assortment  \n• \nCrossing over  \nThese processes generate genetically different gametes. \n \n56. Mendelian Genetics and Evolution \nMendelian inheritance explains how genetic variation is transmitted between generations. \nEvolution occurs when allele frequencies within populations change over generations. \nTherefore, understanding inheritance is fundamental to understanding evolutionary processes. \n \n57. Key Terms \nGenetics \nThe study of heredity and variation. \nHeredity \nTransmission of genetic information from parents to offspring. \nGene \nA segment of DNA associated with a biological function or characteristic. \nAllele \nAn alternative form of a gene. \nCharacter \nA heritable feature. \nTrait \nA particular form of a character. \nGenotype \nThe genetic constitution of an organism.\n\nPhenotype \nThe observable characteristics of an organism. \nHomozygous \nHaving two identical alleles at a locus. \nHeterozygous \nHaving two different alleles at a locus. \nLocus \nThe position of a gene on a chromosome. \nGamete \nA haploid reproductive cell. \nMonohybrid cross \nA cross involving one gene or pair of contrasting traits. \nTest cross \nA cross between an individual of unknown dominant genotype and a homozygous recessive \nindividual. \nCarrier \nAn individual carrying a recessive allele without expressing its associated recessive phenotype. \n \n58. Final Summary \nMendelian genetics provides the foundation for understanding the transmission of hereditary \ncharacteristics. \nGregor Mendel used pea plants to establish predictable patterns of inheritance. His work led to \nthe law of segregation, which states that the two alleles of a gene separate during gamete \nformation. \nGenes occur in alternative forms called alleles. An organism may be homozygous or \nheterozygous for a particular gene. Its genotype describes its genetic constitution, while its \nphenotype describes its observable characteristics. \nA monohybrid cross examines the inheritance of one gene. In a typical cross between two \nheterozygous individuals, such as Tt × Tt, the expected genotypic ratio is 1:2:1, while the \nphenotypic ratio is 3:1 when complete dominance occurs.\n\nPunnett squares, probability rules and test crosses are useful tools for predicting genetic \noutcomes. Pedigree analysis can also be used to investigate inheritance within families. \nMendelian principles form the foundation for understanding more complex inheritance patterns, \nincluding dihybrid inheritance, multiple alleles, polygenic inheritance, sex-linked inheritance and \ngene linkage.	COMPLETED	103	2026-08-19 18:11:41.570753	2026-08-19 18:12:51.882896	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
984	Lesson 2: Dihybrid crosses and law of independent assortment - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 2: Dihybrid crosses and law of independent assortment	PDF	uploads/pdf/405a067a32654a2099a3bfddcb19958f.pdf	\N	Lesson 2 — Dihybrid Crosses and Law of Independent Assortment \nIntroduction \nA dihybrid cross examines the inheritance of two different characteristics simultaneously. While \na monohybrid cross follows one gene, a dihybrid cross allows the inheritance of two genes to be \nstudied together. \nMendel used dihybrid crosses to establish the law of independent assortment, which describes \nhow alleles of different genes are distributed into gametes during meiosis. \nThis lesson also introduces probability methods and inheritance patterns that do not follow \ncomplete dominance. \n \n1. Dihybrid Cross \nA dihybrid cross is a genetic cross involving two different genes or two pairs of contrasting \ncharacteristics. \nFor example, in pea plants \n• \nSeed shape may be controlled by one gene  \n• \nSeed colour may be controlled by another gene  \nThe symbols can be represented as \nR for round seeds \nr for wrinkled seeds \nY for yellow seeds \ny for green seeds \n \n2. Two Gene Pairs \nAn individual can possess two pairs of alleles. \nFor example \nRrYy \ncontains \n• \nR and r for seed shape  \n• \nY and y for seed colour\n\nThe individual is heterozygous for both genes. \nSuch an individual is called a double heterozygote. \n \n3. Mendel's Dihybrid Experiment \nMendel crossed pea plants differing in two characteristics. \nA typical parental cross can be represented as \nRRYY × rryy \nThe first parent is homozygous dominant for both genes. \nThe second parent is homozygous recessive for both genes. \n \n4. Gametes of the Parental Generation \nThe parent \nRRYY \ncan produce only one type of gamete \nRY \nThe parent \nrryy \ncan produce only one type of gamete \nry \n \n5. F₁ Generation \nWhen \nRRYY × rryy \nis crossed, all F₁ offspring are \nRrYy \nTherefore, all F₁ individuals are double heterozygotes. \nIf R and Y are dominant, all F₁ offspring display\n\nRound yellow seeds \n \n6. Gametes Produced by F₁ Individuals \nAn F₁ individual has the genotype \nRrYy \nDuring meiosis, the alleles can be combined into four types of gametes \n• \nRY  \n• \nRy  \n• \nrY  \n• \nry  \nEach type is expected to occur with equal frequency when the genes assort independently. \n \n7. Law of Independent Assortment \nThe law of independent assortment states that alleles of different genes assort independently \ninto gametes when the genes are not linked. \nThis occurs because homologous chromosome pairs align independently during meiosis I. \n \n8. Chromosomal Basis of Independent Assortment \nDuring metaphase I of meiosis, homologous chromosome pairs align independently of one \nanother. \nFor two gene pairs located on different chromosomes, the orientation of one chromosome pair \ndoes not determine the orientation of another. \nThis produces different combinations of chromosomes in gametes. \n \n9. Dihybrid F₂ Cross \nThe F₁ individuals can be crossed \nRrYy × RrYy \nEach parent can produce\n\n• \nRY  \n• \nRy  \n• \nrY  \n• \nry  \nTherefore, there are 16 possible combinations in a Punnett square. \n \n10. Dihybrid Punnett Square \n \nRY \nRy \nrY \nry \nRY RRYY RRYy RrYY RrYy \nRy RRYy RRyy RrYy Rryy \nrY RrYY RrYy rrYY rrYy \nry RrYy Rryy rrYy rryy \n \n11. F₂ Phenotypic Ratio \nWhen complete dominance occurs and the genes assort independently, the F₂ generation \nproduces the characteristic \n9 : 3 : 3 : 1 \nphenotypic ratio. \nFor the example above \n9 round yellow \n3 round green \n3 wrinkled yellow \n1 wrinkled green \n \n12. Meaning of the 9:3:3:1 Ratio \nThe ratio represents the expected frequency of the four phenotype categories.\n\nPhenotype \nExpected proportion \nRound yellow \n9/16 \nRound green \n3/16 \nWrinkled yellow 3/16 \nWrinkled green 1/16 \nThis ratio assumes complete dominance and independent assortment. \n \n13. Relationship Between Monohybrid and Dihybrid Ratios \nThe dihybrid ratio can be derived from two monohybrid ratios. \nFor each individual gene \n3 dominant : 1 recessive \nCombining the two independent traits produces \n3 : 1 × 3 : 1 \nThis gives \n9 : 3 : 3 : 1 \n \n14. Product Rule in Dihybrid Crosses \nProbability calculations can often replace a large Punnett square. \nFor example, in \nRrYy × RrYy \nthe probability of a round phenotype is \n3/4 \nThe probability of yellow is also \n3/4 \nTherefore, the probability of round yellow is \n3/4 × 3/4 = 9/16\n\n15. Probability of Round Green \nThe probability of round is \n3/4 \nThe probability of green is \n1/4 \nTherefore \n3/4 × 1/4 = 3/16 \n \n16. Probability of Wrinkled Yellow \nThe probability of wrinkled is \n1/4 \nThe probability of yellow is \n3/4 \nTherefore \n1/4 × 3/4 = 3/16 \n \n17. Probability of Wrinkled Green \nThe probability of wrinkled is \n1/4 \nThe probability of green is \n1/4 \nTherefore \n1/4 × 1/4 = 1/16 \n \n18. Dihybrid Test Cross\n\nA dihybrid test cross involves crossing a double heterozygote with a double homozygous \nrecessive individual. \nFor example \nRrYy × rryy \nThe heterozygous parent produces \n• \nRY  \n• \nRy  \n• \nrY  \n• \nry  \nThe recessive parent produces only \nry \n \n19. Dihybrid Test Cross Ratio \nIf the genes assort independently, the expected phenotypic ratio is \n1 : 1 : 1 : 1 \nThe four phenotype classes occur in approximately equal proportions. \n \n20. Importance of Test Crosses \nA test cross can reveal whether an individual showing dominant phenotypes is homozygous or \nheterozygous. \nFor two genes, it can also provide evidence about whether the genes are assorting independently. \n \n21. Complete Dominance \nIn complete dominance, the heterozygous genotype has the same phenotype as the homozygous \ndominant genotype. \nFor example \nAA \nand\n\nAa \nproduce the same phenotype. \nThe recessive phenotype appears only in \naa \n \n22. Incomplete Dominance \nIncomplete dominance occurs when neither allele completely masks the other. \nThe heterozygote has a phenotype intermediate between the two homozygotes. \nFor example \nRR = red \nrr = white \nRr = pink \n \n23. Incomplete Dominance Cross \nConsider \nRr × Rr \nThe offspring are \n• \nRR  \n• \nRr  \n• \nRr  \n• \nrr  \nThe genotypic ratio is \n1 RR : 2 Rr : 1 rr \nThe phenotypic ratio is also \n1 red : 2 pink : 1 white \n \n24. Codominance\n\nCodominance occurs when both alleles are expressed in the heterozygote. \nNeither allele completely suppresses the other. \nThe heterozygote therefore displays characteristics associated with both alleles. \nThe ABO blood group system provides an important example. \n \n25. Comparison of Dominance Patterns \nInheritance pattern Heterozygous phenotype \nComplete dominance Same as dominant homozygote \nIncomplete dominance Intermediate phenotype \nCodominance \nBoth alleles expressed \n \n26. Genotypic Ratio and Phenotypic Ratio \nGenotypic ratios describe the relative frequency of genetic combinations. \nPhenotypic ratios describe the relative frequency of observable characteristics. \nThe two ratios may differ because different genotypes can produce the same phenotype under \ncomplete dominance. \n \n27. Independent Assortment and Gene Location \nIndependent assortment is most straightforward when genes are located on different \nchromosomes. \nGenes located far apart on the same chromosome can also behave approximately independently \nbecause crossing over can separate them. \nGenes located close together on the same chromosome tend to be inherited together. \n \n28. Linked Genes \nGenes located on the same chromosome are called linked genes. \nLinked genes do not always assort independently.\n\nThe closer two genes are on a chromosome, the more likely they are to be inherited together. \nGene linkage is studied in greater detail in a later genetics lesson. \n \n29. Crossing Over \nCrossing over occurs during prophase I of meiosis. \nHomologous chromosomes exchange corresponding DNA segments. \nThis produces new combinations of alleles. \n \n30. Recombination \nA recombinant chromosome contains a combination of alleles that differs from the original \nparental chromosome combinations. \nCrossing over therefore contributes to genetic variation. \n \n31. Independent Assortment and Genetic Variation \nIndependent assortment increases genetic variation by producing different combinations of \nmaternal and paternal chromosomes in gametes. \nTogether with crossing over and random fertilisation, it contributes significantly to genetic \ndiversity. \n \n32. Random Fertilisation \nEach gamete carries a particular combination of alleles. \nThe random fusion of male and female gametes creates additional combinations of genetic \ninformation in offspring. \nTherefore, sexual reproduction produces substantial genetic variation. \n \n33. Dihybrid Cross Using Probability \nA dihybrid cross does not always require a 16-cell Punnett square. \nFor example\n\nAaBb × AaBb \nThe probability of recessive phenotype for gene A is \n1/4 \nThe probability of recessive phenotype for gene B is \n1/4 \nTherefore, the probability of being recessive for both is \n1/4 × 1/4 = 1/16 \n \n34. Conditional Probability in Genetics \nSometimes the probability of an outcome must be calculated after another condition is known. \nFor example, if an offspring is known to display a dominant phenotype, the possible genotypes \nmay need to be reconsidered. \nConditional probability can therefore be useful in pedigree and genetic analysis. \n \n35. Addition Rule in Genetics \nWhen several mutually exclusive genetic outcomes can produce the same phenotype, their \nprobabilities can be added. \nFor example, the dominant phenotype from \nAa × Aa \ncan arise from \nAA \nor \nAa \nTherefore \nP(dominant) = P(AA) + P(Aa) \n= 1/4 + 1/2 \n= 3/4\n\n36. Multiplication Rule in Genetics \nWhen two independent conditions must occur simultaneously, their probabilities are multiplied. \nFor example \nP(A_ and B_) = P(A_) × P(B_) \nFor two independently assorting genes \n3/4 × 3/4 = 9/16 \n \n37. Branch Diagram \nA branch diagram can be used to calculate genetic probabilities without constructing a large \nPunnett square. \nFor each gene, determine the probability of each phenotype. \nThen multiply the relevant probabilities along the branches. \nThis is particularly useful for larger genetic crosses. \n \n38. Phenotypic Classes in a Dihybrid Cross \nFor \nAaBb × AaBb \nassuming complete dominance and independent assortment, the four phenotypic categories are \n• \nA_B_  \n• \nA_bb  \n• \naaB_  \n• \naabb  \nThe underscore indicates that either allele can occur at that position. \nFor example \nA_B_ \nrepresents \n• \nAABB\n\n• \nAABb  \n• \nAaBB  \n• \nAaBb  \n \n39. Homozygous and Heterozygous Conditions \nFor two genes, an individual can have several combinations of homozygous and heterozygous \nconditions. \nExamples include \nAABB \nDouble homozygous dominant \nAAbb \nHomozygous at both loci \nAaBb \nDouble heterozygous \nAabb \nHeterozygous for one gene and homozygous recessive for the other \n \n40. Gamete Formation in a Double Heterozygote \nConsider \nAaBb \nEach gamete receives one allele from each gene. \nPossible gametes are \n• \nAB  \n• \nAb  \n• \naB  \n• \nab  \nAssuming independent assortment, each type has an expected probability of\n\n1/4 \n \n41. Why Four Gametes Are Produced \nThe first gene contributes two possibilities \nA or a \nThe second gene contributes two possibilities \nB or b \nTherefore \n2 × 2 = 4 \npossible gamete combinations. \n \n42. General Gamete Number \nFor an organism heterozygous at n independently assorting loci, the maximum number of \ndifferent gamete types is \n2ⁿ \nFor example \nOne heterozygous gene \n2¹ = 2 \nTwo heterozygous genes \n2² = 4 \nThree heterozygous genes \n2³ = 8 \n \n43. Significance of Independent Assortment \nIndependent assortment explains why alleles from different genes can be combined into many \ndifferent gametes. \nIt contributes to \n• \nGenetic variation\n\n• \nNew allele combinations  \n• \nDifferences among siblings  \n• \nEvolutionary potential  \n \n44. Exceptions to Independent Assortment \nIndependent assortment does not apply in the same way to genes that are closely linked on the \nsame chromosome. \nOther factors can also modify expected Mendelian ratios. \nThese include \n• \nGene linkage  \n• \nEpistasis  \n• \nLethal alleles  \n• \nIncomplete dominance  \n• \nCodominance  \n• \nMultiple alleles  \n• \nPolygenic inheritance  \n \n45. Mendelian Ratios Are Expectations \nThe ratios such as \n3:1 \nand \n9:3:3:1 \nrepresent expected probabilities. \nActual offspring numbers may differ because fertilisation and inheritance are probabilistic \nevents. \nThe ratios become increasingly close to theoretical expectations as the number of offspring \nbecomes larger.\n\n46. Chi-Square Analysis \nThe chi-square test can be used to determine whether observed genetic results differ \nsignificantly from expected results. \nIt helps distinguish between \n• \nDifferences expected due to random chance  \n• \nDifferences suggesting that the genetic model may not adequately explain the \nobservations  \nThe basic expression is \nχ² = Σ (Observed − Expected)² / Expected \n \n47. Significance of Chi-Square Testing \nSuppose a genetic cross is expected to produce a particular ratio. \nThe actual offspring numbers may not exactly match the ratio. \nChi-square analysis determines whether the difference is sufficiently large to reject the proposed \ngenetic model. \n \n48. Complete Dominance Versus Incomplete Dominance \nConsider a heterozygous cross \nRr × Rr \nWith complete dominance \nPhenotypic ratio = 3:1 \nWith incomplete dominance \nPhenotypic ratio = 1:2:1 \nThe difference occurs because the heterozygote has a distinct phenotype under incomplete \ndominance. \n \n49. Codominance and Heterozygotes \nUnder codominance, both alleles are expressed in the heterozygote.\n\nTherefore, the heterozygote has a phenotype that clearly displays the effects of both alleles rather \nthan an intermediate phenotype. \n \n50. Genetic Terminology \nImportant terms include \nDihybrid \nAn organism or cross involving two genes or two characteristics. \nDouble heterozygote \nAn individual heterozygous at two loci. \nIndependent assortment \nIndependent distribution of alleles of different genes into gametes when the genes are \nindependently assorting. \nIncomplete dominance \nInheritance pattern where the heterozygote has an intermediate phenotype. \nCodominance \nInheritance pattern where both alleles are expressed in the heterozygote. \nLinked genes \nGenes located on the same chromosome. \nRecombination \nFormation of new combinations of alleles. \n \n51. Key Genetic Ratios \nCross or condition \nExpected ratio \nAa × Aa genotype \n1:2:1 \nAa × Aa phenotype with complete dominance \n3:1 \nAa × aa phenotype \n1:1 \nAaBb × AaBb phenotype with independent assortment 9:3:3:1\n\nCross or condition \nExpected ratio \nAaBb × aabb phenotype with independent assortment 1:1:1:1 \nRr × Rr with incomplete dominance \n1:2:1 \n \n52. Final Summary \nA dihybrid cross examines the inheritance of two genes simultaneously. In a typical cross such as \nRrYy × RrYy, each double heterozygous parent can produce four gamete types, assuming \nindependent assortment. \nMendel's law of independent assortment states that alleles of different genes assort independently \nduring gamete formation when the genes are not linked. Independent alignment of homologous \nchromosome pairs during meiosis I provides the chromosomal basis for this principle. \nWhen two independently assorting genes show complete dominance, a dihybrid cross between \ndouble heterozygotes produces the characteristic 9:3:3:1 phenotypic ratio. \nProbability rules provide an alternative to large Punnett squares. The product rule is used when \nindependent events must occur together, while the addition rule is used when alternative \noutcomes can produce the same result. \nNot all inheritance follows complete dominance. In incomplete dominance, the heterozygote \nhas an intermediate phenotype. In codominance, both alleles are expressed. \nIndependent assortment is particularly applicable to genes on different chromosomes or genes \nsufficiently far apart on the same chromosome. Closely located genes may be linked and \ntherefore inherited together more frequently than expected under independent assortment. \nThese principles form the foundation for understanding more complex patterns of inheritance \nand chromosome behaviour.	COMPLETED	104	2026-08-19 18:11:42.397646	2026-08-19 18:12:51.882897	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
985	Lesson 3: Multiple alleles and polygenic inheritance - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 3: Multiple alleles and polygenic inheritance	PDF	uploads/pdf/9248ce2b390c4565a57d291014f19a9d.pdf	\N	Lesson 3 — Multiple Alleles and Polygenic Inheritance \nIntroduction \nMany biological characteristics cannot be explained by a single gene with only two alleles. Some \ngenes exist in more than two alternative forms within a population, while some characteristics \nare influenced by several genes simultaneously. \nMultiple alleles describe a situation in which a gene has more than two possible alleles within a \npopulation. Polygenic inheritance occurs when a characteristic is influenced by two or more \ngenes. \nThis lesson also covers the ABO blood group system, Rhesus factor, Rhesus incompatibility, \nquantitative inheritance and DNA fingerprinting. \n \n1. Multiple Alleles \nA gene may exist in more than two alternative forms within a population. \nThese alternative forms are called multiple alleles. \nAlthough a population may contain several alleles for a gene, an individual diploid organism can \npossess only two alleles of that gene because it has two homologous chromosomes. \n \n2. Population and Individual \nFor a gene with three alleles \nA¹, A², A³ \nthe population may contain all three alleles. \nHowever, an individual can have only two of them. \nPossible genotypes include \n• \nA¹A¹  \n• \nA¹A²  \n• \nA¹A³  \n• \nA²A²  \n• \nA²A³  \n• \nA³A³\n\n3. ABO Blood Group System \nThe ABO blood group system is an important example of multiple alleles and codominance. \nThe ABO blood group is determined by three alleles \nIᴬ \nIᴮ \ni \nThese alleles produce four possible blood group phenotypes \n• \nA  \n• \nB  \n• \nAB  \n• \nO  \n \n4. Relationship Between ABO Alleles \nThe alleles Iᴬ and Iᴮ are codominant. \nBoth are dominant over i. \nTherefore \nIᴬ = Iᴮ > i \n \n5. Blood Group A \nBlood group A can result from two genotypes \nIᴬIᴬ \nor \nIᴬi \nThe red blood cells contain A antigens. \n \n6. Blood Group B\n\nBlood group B can result from \nIᴮIᴮ \nor \nIᴮi \nThe red blood cells contain B antigens. \n \n7. Blood Group AB \nBlood group AB has the genotype \nIᴬIᴮ \nBoth A and B alleles are expressed. \nTherefore, red blood cells possess both A and B antigens. \nThis demonstrates codominance. \n \n8. Blood Group O \nBlood group O has the genotype \nii \nNeither A nor B antigen is produced on the red blood cells. \n \n9. ABO Blood Group Table \nBlood group Possible genotype Red cell antigen \nA \nIᴬIᴬ or Iᴬi \nA \nB \nIᴮIᴮ or Iᴮi \nB \nAB \nIᴬIᴮ \nA and B \nO \nii \nNeither A nor B \n \n10. Antigens\n\nAn antigen is a molecule or molecular structure recognised by the immune system. \nIn the ABO system, A and B antigens are present on the surface of red blood cells according to \nthe person's genotype. \n \n11. Antibodies \nAn antibody is a protein produced by B lymphocytes that specifically recognises particular \nantigens. \nIndividuals normally possess antibodies against ABO antigens that are absent from their own red \nblood cells. \n \n12. ABO Antibodies \nBlood group Red cell antigens Plasma antibodies \nA \nA \nAnti-B \nB \nB \nAnti-A \nAB \nA and B \nNeither anti-A nor anti-B \nO \nNeither A nor B \nAnti-A and anti-B \n \n13. Blood Transfusion \nBlood transfusion requires compatibility between donor red blood cells and recipient plasma. \nIf incompatible red blood cells are introduced, antibodies can bind to the donor cells and cause \nagglutination and destruction of red blood cells. \n \n14. Agglutination \nAgglutination is the clumping of cells caused by antigen-antibody interactions. \nIn an incompatible blood transfusion, antibodies can bind to antigens on red blood cells and \ncause them to clump. \nThis can lead to serious transfusion reactions.\n\n15. ABO Compatibility \nFor red blood cell transfusion under the ABO system \nGroup O red cells \ndo not carry A or B antigens and can be used broadly in emergency settings when type-specific \nblood is unavailable, subject to appropriate transfusion protocols. \nGroup AB individuals \nhave both A and B antigens and do not normally have anti-A or anti-B antibodies in their plasma. \nHowever, transfusion compatibility involves more than the ABO system, including the Rhesus \nsystem and other blood group antigens. \n \n16. Rhesus Blood Group System \nThe Rhesus system is another important blood group system. \nThe most clinically significant antigen is the RhD antigen. \nIndividuals are commonly classified as \n• \nRh-positive  \n• \nRh-negative  \n \n17. Rh-Positive \nAn Rh-positive person possesses the RhD antigen on their red blood cells. \n \n18. Rh-Negative \nAn Rh-negative person does not possess the RhD antigen. \nUnlike the ABO system, Rh-negative individuals do not normally have anti-D antibodies without \nprior exposure. \nExposure can occur through \n• \nBlood transfusion  \n• \nPregnancy  \n• \nChildbirth\n\n• \nCertain medical procedures  \n \n19. Rhesus Incompatibility \nRhesus incompatibility can become important when an Rh-negative mother carries an Rh-\npositive fetus. \nFetal red blood cells may enter the maternal circulation, particularly during childbirth or certain \npregnancy events. \nThe mother may then become sensitised and produce anti-D antibodies. \n \n20. Sensitisation \nSensitisation occurs when an Rh-negative individual is exposed to RhD-positive red blood cells \nand develops an immune response against the RhD antigen. \nThe first exposure may lead to the formation of memory cells and anti-D antibodies. \n \n21. Haemolytic Disease of the Fetus and Newborn \nIf an Rh-negative mother has previously developed anti-D antibodies and later carries an Rh-\npositive fetus, maternal IgG anti-D antibodies can cross the placenta. \nThese antibodies can attack fetal red blood cells. \nThis condition is called haemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn. \n \n22. Prevention of Rhesus Disease \nRh-negative mothers at risk of sensitisation can receive anti-D immunoglobulin. \nThe administered antibodies bind to RhD-positive fetal red blood cells in the mother's circulation \nand help prevent the mother's immune system from becoming sensitised. \n \n23. Multiple Alleles and Dominance \nMultiple alleles do not necessarily have the same dominance relationship. \nFor the ABO system \nIᴬ and Iᴮ are codominant\n\nwhile \ni is recessive to both. \nTherefore, different allele combinations produce different phenotypes. \n \n24. Polygenic Inheritance \nPolygenic inheritance occurs when a characteristic is influenced by multiple genes. \nEach gene may contribute to the overall phenotype. \nSuch characteristics often show continuous variation rather than a small number of clearly \nseparated categories. \n \n25. Polygenes \nGenes contributing to a polygenic characteristic are sometimes called polygenes. \nEach gene may have a relatively small effect, and their combined effects determine the \nphenotype. \n \n26. Quantitative Traits \nA quantitative trait is a characteristic that can be measured numerically. \nExamples include \n• \nHeight  \n• \nBody mass  \n• \nSkin pigmentation  \n• \nGrain yield  \n• \nMilk production  \nThese traits often show continuous variation. \n \n27. Continuous Variation \nContinuous variation occurs when individuals display a range of intermediate phenotypes \nrather than a few distinct categories.\n\nFor example, human height varies across a broad range. \nMany genes and environmental factors can contribute to this variation. \n \n28. Discontinuous Variation \nDiscontinuous variation produces distinct categories. \nExamples include \n• \nABO blood groups  \n• \nSome inherited genetic disorders  \n• \nCertain qualitative characteristics  \nThe difference between continuous and discontinuous variation depends on how the \ncharacteristic is genetically and environmentally determined. \n \n29. Polygenic Inheritance Model \nConsider a simplified characteristic controlled by two genes \nA/a \nand \nB/b \nSuppose each dominant allele contributes one unit toward the phenotype. \nThen \nAABB \nhas four contributing alleles. \nAaBB \nhas three. \nAABb \nalso has three. \nAaBb \nhas two.\n\nAabb \nhas one. \naabb \nhas zero. \nThis produces several phenotypic classes. \n \n30. Additive Gene Action \nIn a simple additive model, each contributing allele makes a small additional contribution to the \nphenotype. \nThe combined effects of multiple genes produce a range of phenotypes. \nThis is called additive gene action. \n \n31. Number of Phenotypic Classes \nFor a simplified polygenic characteristic controlled by n independently assorting gene pairs with \nadditive effects, the theoretical number of phenotypic classes is \n2n + 1 \nFor example \nTwo gene pairs \n2(2) + 1 = 5 \nphenotypic classes. \nThree gene pairs \n2(3) + 1 = 7 \nphenotypic classes. \n \n32. Number of Genotypes \nFor n independently assorting loci, each with two alleles, the number of possible genotypes is \n3ⁿ \nFor two loci\n\n3² = 9 \npossible genotype combinations. \n \n33. Phenotypic Distribution \nPolygenic characteristics often produce a distribution where intermediate phenotypes are more \ncommon than extreme phenotypes. \nIn a large population, this can produce a pattern resembling a bell-shaped distribution. \nEnvironmental effects can further broaden the distribution. \n \n34. Environmental Influence \nPhenotype is not always determined solely by genotype. \nEnvironmental factors can influence quantitative characteristics. \nExamples include \n• \nNutrition  \n• \nTemperature  \n• \nLight  \n• \nWater availability  \n• \nExercise  \n• \nDisease  \nTherefore \nPhenotype = Genetic effects + Environmental effects + Gene-environment interactions \nThis is a simplified representation rather than a mathematical equation applicable to every trait. \n \n35. Heritability \nHeritability describes the proportion of variation in a characteristic within a particular \npopulation that can be statistically attributed to genetic differences. \nIt does not indicate how much of an individual's characteristic is caused by genes. \nHeritability is therefore a population-level concept.\n\n36. Genetic and Environmental Variation \nObserved variation in a population may result from \n• \nGenetic variation  \n• \nEnvironmental variation  \n• \nInteraction between genotype and environment  \nUnderstanding these components is important when studying quantitative traits. \n \n37. DNA Fingerprinting \nDNA fingerprinting, also called DNA profiling, is a technique used to distinguish individuals \nbased on variations in their DNA. \nModern DNA profiling commonly examines highly variable regions of the genome. \n \n38. Short Tandem Repeats \nShort tandem repeats, or STRs, are short DNA sequences repeated consecutively. \nThe number of repeats at particular loci varies among individuals. \nThese variations can be analysed to produce a DNA profile. \n \n39. DNA Profile \nA DNA profile is a pattern of genetic markers obtained from an individual's DNA. \nExcept for identical twins, individuals generally have different DNA profiles. \nThis makes DNA profiling useful in \n• \nForensic investigations  \n• \nPaternity testing  \n• \nIdentification of human remains  \n• \nMissing-person investigations\n\n40. DNA Profiling and Inheritance \nA child inherits one set of chromosomes from each biological parent. \nTherefore, genetic markers in the child's DNA profile should be explainable by inheritance from \nthe biological parents. \nThis principle allows DNA profiling to be used in parentage testing. \n \n41. DNA Sample Sources \nDNA for profiling can potentially be obtained from biological material containing nucleated \ncells. \nExamples include \n• \nBlood  \n• \nSaliva  \n• \nBuccal cells  \n• \nHair roots  \n• \nSemen  \n• \nTissue  \nThe suitability of a sample depends on the amount and quality of DNA available. \n \n42. STR Analysis \nA simplified STR profiling process involves \nSample collection \n↓ \nDNA extraction \n↓ \nPCR amplification of selected STR loci \n↓ \nSeparation and detection of amplified DNA fragments \n↓\n\nGeneration of DNA profile \n↓ \nComparison with reference profiles \n \n43. Polymerase Chain Reaction in DNA Profiling \nPCR allows selected DNA regions to be amplified from a small starting quantity of DNA. \nThis makes it possible to analyse genetic markers even when only a limited biological sample is \navailable. \n \n44. DNA Fragment Analysis \nAfter amplification, DNA fragments can be separated according to their size. \nThe resulting fragment pattern is analysed to determine the alleles present at selected STR loci. \n \n45. DNA Profiling in Forensic Biology \nDNA profiling can be used to compare biological material recovered from a crime scene with \nreference samples. \nA match between profiles can provide strong evidence that the samples originated from the same \nindividual, subject to appropriate statistical interpretation and proper sample handling. \n \n46. DNA Profiling and Paternity \nA child receives genetic material from both biological parents. \nTherefore, a child's STR profile can be compared with the profiles of an alleged mother and \nfather. \nGenetic markers inherited from the child must be consistent with parental inheritance. \n \n47. Genetic Probability in Paternity Testing \nA DNA profile match does not simply mean that two individuals have identical DNA. \nInstead, the probability of observing the matching genetic profile in an unrelated individual is \ncalculated.\n\nThe greater the number of informative STR markers analysed, the stronger the statistical \nevidence can become. \n \n48. Importance of Multiple STR Loci \nUsing multiple STR loci greatly reduces the probability that two unrelated individuals will have \nthe same profile. \nTherefore, forensic laboratories generally examine several loci rather than relying on a single \ngenetic marker. \n \n49. DNA Fingerprinting and Identical Twins \nIdentical twins originate from the same fertilised egg and generally possess extremely similar or \neffectively indistinguishable standard STR profiles. \nTherefore, conventional STR profiling usually cannot distinguish identical twins. \n \n50. Multiple Alleles Versus Polygenic Inheritance \nThese concepts should not be confused. \nMultiple alleles \nOne gene has more than two possible alleles within a population. \nExample \nABO blood groups \nPolygenic inheritance \nMultiple genes contribute to one characteristic. \nExamples \n• \nHuman height  \n• \nSkin pigmentation  \n• \nMany quantitative agricultural characteristics  \n \n51. Multiple Alleles and Codominance\n\nMultiple alleles and codominance can occur together. \nThe ABO blood group system demonstrates both. \nThere are three alleles \nIᴬ, Iᴮ, i \nand \nIᴬ and Iᴮ are codominant. \n \n52. Multiple Alleles and Population Genetics \nMultiple alleles increase the possible genetic variation within a population. \nAlthough an individual can possess only two alleles at a diploid autosomal locus, a population \nmay contain many allelic forms. \nThis contributes to genetic diversity. \n \n53. Importance of Genetic Diversity \nGenetic diversity allows populations to contain different genetic characteristics. \nIt contributes to \n• \nAdaptation  \n• \nNatural selection  \n• \nDisease resistance  \n• \nPopulation survival  \n• \nEvolutionary change  \n \n54. ABO Inheritance Example \nConsider a parent with blood group A and genotype \nIᴬi \nand a parent with blood group B and genotype \nIᴮi\n\nTheir possible gametes are \nFirst parent \nIᴬ or i \nSecond parent \nIᴮ or i \nPossible offspring are \n \nIᴮ i \nIᴬ IᴬIᴮ Iᴬi \ni Iᴮi ii \nPossible blood groups are \n• \nAB  \n• \nA  \n• \nB  \n• \nO  \nEach has an expected probability of 25% under these assumptions. \n \n55. Another ABO Cross \nConsider \nIᴬIᴬ × ii \nThe first parent produces only \nIᴬ \nThe second produces only \ni \nTherefore, all offspring are \nIᴬi \nAll offspring have blood group A.\n\n56. ABO and Recessive Inheritance \nBlood group O requires \nii \nTherefore, a child with blood group O must inherit an i allele from both biological parents. \nBoth parents must therefore be capable of transmitting i. \n \n57. ABO and Parentage \nABO blood groups can sometimes exclude possible biological parentage. \nHowever, ABO blood typing alone generally cannot establish parentage conclusively because \nmany unrelated individuals share the same blood group. \nDNA profiling provides much greater discriminatory power. \n \n58. Rhesus Inheritance \nThe RhD system is genetically more complex than a simple single-gene classroom model, but \nRh-positive and Rh-negative inheritance is often introduced using a simplified dominant-\nrecessive model. \nIn this simplified model \nD \nrepresents the Rh-positive allele. \nd \nrepresents the Rh-negative allele. \nRh-negative individuals are represented as \ndd \n \n59. Rh Cross Example \nConsider \nDd × dd \nPossible offspring are\n\n• \nDd  \n• \nDd  \n• \ndd  \n• \ndd  \nExpected phenotype \n1 Rh-positive : 1 Rh-negative \nunder the simplified model. \n \n60. Genetic Counselling \nKnowledge of inheritance patterns can help assess the likelihood of genetic characteristics or \nconditions occurring in offspring. \nGenetic counselling can involve \n• \nFamily history  \n• \nGenetic testing  \n• \nRisk assessment  \n• \nExplanation of inheritance patterns  \n• \nDiscussion of reproductive options  \n \n61. Key Terms \nMultiple alleles \nMore than two alternative alleles of a gene within a population. \nCodominance \nBoth alleles are expressed in a heterozygote. \nAntigen \nA molecule recognised by the immune system. \nAntibody \nA specific immune protein that binds an antigen.\n\nAgglutination \nClumping of cells caused by antigen-antibody interactions. \nRh-positive \nPresence of the RhD antigen. \nRh-negative \nAbsence of the RhD antigen. \nPolygenic inheritance \nInheritance in which multiple genes contribute to a characteristic. \nQuantitative trait \nA measurable characteristic that can show continuous variation. \nContinuous variation \nVariation showing a range of intermediate phenotypes. \nDNA profiling \nIdentification or comparison of individuals using patterns of genetic markers. \nSTR \nShort tandem repeat, a highly variable DNA marker used in genetic profiling. \nPCR \nPolymerase chain reaction, a technique used to amplify specific DNA sequences. \n \n62. Comparison of Major Inheritance Patterns \nFeature \nComplete \ndominance \nIncomplete \ndominance \nCodominance \nPolygenic \ninheritance \nGenes \ninvolved \nUsually one \nUsually one \nUsually one \nMultiple \nHeterozygote Dominant \nphenotype \nIntermediate \nphenotype \nBoth alleles \nexpressed \nDepends on \ncontributing alleles\n\nFeature \nComplete \ndominance \nIncomplete \ndominance \nCodominance \nPolygenic \ninheritance \nTypical \nvariation \nDiscontinuous \nDiscontinuous \nDiscontinuous \nOften continuous \nExample \nPea plant height \nFlower colour in \nsome plants \nABO blood group Human height \n \n63. Final Summary \nMultiple alleles occur when a gene has more than two possible alleles within a population. The \nABO blood group system is an important example, involving the alleles Iᴬ, Iᴮ and i. Iᴬ and Iᴮ are \ncodominant, while i is recessive to both. \nThe ABO system produces four blood groups, A, B, AB and O. Blood transfusion requires \ncompatibility because antibodies can recognise incompatible red blood cell antigens and cause \nagglutination. \nThe Rhesus system, particularly the RhD antigen, is another important blood group system. Rh \nincompatibility can occur when an Rh-negative mother becomes sensitised to RhD-positive fetal \nred blood cells. Anti-D immunoglobulin can prevent sensitisation in appropriate circumstances. \nPolygenic inheritance occurs when multiple genes contribute to a single characteristic. Such \ncharacteristics often show continuous variation and are frequently influenced by environmental \nfactors. Human height is a commonly used example. \nDNA profiling uses variable genetic markers, particularly STRs, to distinguish individuals. PCR \ncan amplify selected STR regions, allowing DNA profiles to be generated from relatively small \nbiological samples. DNA profiling has applications in forensic investigations, parentage testing \nand human identification. \nUnderstanding multiple alleles, polygenic inheritance and DNA profiling demonstrates how \ngenetic variation can arise from different forms of gene organisation and how this variation can \nbe studied in biological and practical applications.	COMPLETED	105	2026-08-19 18:11:43.362526	2026-08-19 18:12:51.882899	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
986	Lesson 4: Sex determination and sex-linked inheritance - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 4: Sex determination and sex-linked inheritance	PDF	uploads/pdf/9d9b41c8604941f784b71180872e3e36.pdf	\N	Lesson 4 — Sex Determination and Sex-Linked Inheritance \nIntroduction \nSex determination is the biological process by which an organism develops as a particular sex. \nDifferent organisms use different mechanisms to determine sex, including chromosome-based \nsystems, environmental factors and haplodiploidy. \nIn humans, sex is associated with the sex chromosomes X and Y. Genes located on these \nchromosomes can show distinctive inheritance patterns known as sex-linked inheritance. \nUnderstanding sex determination and sex-linked inheritance is important for explaining \nconditions such as haemophilia and red-green colour vision deficiency and for interpreting \ngenetic pedigrees. \n \n1. Sex Determination \nSex determination refers to the mechanisms that establish the sexual development of an \norganism. \nDifferent organisms use different systems. \nMajor mechanisms include \n• \nChromosomal sex determination  \n• \nHaplodiploid sex determination  \n• \nEnvironmental sex determination  \n \n2. Sex Chromosomes \nChromosomes involved in determining biological sex are called sex chromosomes. \nIn humans, these are \n• \nX chromosome  \n• \nY chromosome  \nThe remaining chromosomes are called autosomes. \n \n3. Human Chromosome Number \nHuman somatic cells normally contain\n\n46 chromosomes \nThese consist of \n• \n22 pairs of autosomes  \n• \n1 pair of sex chromosomes  \nTherefore, humans normally have 23 chromosome pairs. \n \n4. Female Chromosomal Constitution \nIn the typical human chromosomal system, females have \nXX \nBoth sex chromosomes are X chromosomes. \n \n5. Male Chromosomal Constitution \nIn the typical human chromosomal system, males have \nXY \nOne sex chromosome is X and the other is Y. \n \n6. Homogametic Sex \nThe sex that produces only one type of sex chromosome in its gametes is called the \nhomogametic sex. \nIn humans, females are homogametic. \nTheir eggs contain \nX \n \n7. Heterogametic Sex \nThe sex that produces two types of sex chromosomes in its gametes is called the heterogametic \nsex. \nIn humans, males are heterogametic. \nTheir sperm may contain\n\nX \nor \nY \n \n8. Human Sex Determination \nAn egg normally contributes an X chromosome. \nA sperm contributes either X or Y. \nTherefore \nX egg + X sperm = XX \nX egg + Y sperm = XY \nUnder the typical XX-XY system, the sperm determines whether the resulting zygote has an XX \nor XY chromosome complement. \n \n9. Probability of XX and XY \nFor a typical cross \nXX × XY \nthe expected chromosomal combinations are \n X Y \nX XX XY \nX XX XY \nExpected outcome \n50% XX \n50% XY \nThis is a simplified chromosomal model and does not account for all differences of sexual \ndevelopment. \n \n10. Sex Determination and Sexual Development\n\nChromosomal sex is not the only factor involved in sexual development. \nGenes, hormones, receptors and developmental pathways also contribute to the development of \nreproductive structures and secondary sexual characteristics. \n \n11. The Y Chromosome \nThe human Y chromosome contains a gene called SRY. \nSRY is an important trigger for the development of the embryonic gonad toward a testicular \npathway. \n \n12. SRY Gene \nThe SRY gene encodes a transcription factor involved in initiating testis development. \nIts activity contributes to a developmental pathway involving the formation of testes and \nsubsequent production of hormones that influence sexual differentiation. \n \n13. X Chromosome \nThe X chromosome contains many genes that have functions unrelated to sex determination. \nTherefore, X-linked genes can influence characteristics throughout the body. \n \n14. Sex-Linked Genes \nA sex-linked gene is a gene located on a sex chromosome. \nSex-linked inheritance can involve \n• \nX-linked genes  \n• \nY-linked genes  \nBecause X and Y chromosomes differ substantially in gene content, X-linked inheritance is much \nmore common than Y-linked inheritance. \n \n15. X-Linked Inheritance \nA gene located on the X chromosome is called an X-linked gene.\n\nMales have only one X chromosome. \nTherefore, an X-linked gene in a typical XY male is present in a single copy. \n \n16. Hemizygous Condition \nA male with one X chromosome is hemizygous for most X-linked genes. \nFor example, if a recessive allele is represented by \nXᵃ \na male can have \nXᵃY \nThere is no corresponding allele on the Y chromosome for many X-linked genes. \n \n17. X-Linked Dominant Inheritance \nAn X-linked dominant characteristic is expressed when a dominant allele is present on the X \nchromosome. \nBoth males and females can be affected. \nHowever, the inheritance pattern differs from autosomal dominant inheritance because fathers \ntransmit their X chromosome to daughters but not to sons. \n \n18. X-Linked Recessive Inheritance \nAn X-linked recessive characteristic is caused by a recessive allele located on the X \nchromosome. \nIt is more frequently expressed in males because males have only one X chromosome. \n \n19. X-Linked Recessive Male \nA male who inherits a recessive allele on his X chromosome generally expresses the associated \nphenotype. \nFor example \nXʰY\n\ncan represent a male affected by an X-linked recessive condition. \n \n20. X-Linked Recessive Female \nA female generally needs two copies of an X-linked recessive allele to express the condition. \nFor example \nXʰXʰ \nrepresents an affected female under a simple X-linked recessive model. \n \n21. Carrier Female \nA heterozygous female \nXᴴXʰ \ncan be a carrier of an X-linked recessive allele without expressing the associated condition under \na simple model. \nShe can transmit the allele to her offspring. \n \n22. X-Linked Recessive Inheritance Pattern \nConsider a carrier mother \nXᴴXʰ \nand an unaffected father \nXᴴY \nPossible offspring include \n \nXᴴ \nY \nXᴴ XᴴXᴴ XᴴY \nXʰ XᴴXʰ XʰY \nPossible outcomes \n• \nUnaffected female  \n• \nCarrier female\n\n• \nUnaffected male  \n• \nAffected male  \nEach has an expected probability of 25% under this simplified model. \n \n23. Transmission from Father to Son \nA father gives his \nY chromosome \nto his sons. \nTherefore, an X-linked allele carried by the father is not transmitted directly from father to son. \nThis is an important characteristic of X-linked inheritance. \n \n24. Transmission from Father to Daughter \nA father gives his \nX chromosome \nto all of his daughters. \nTherefore, every daughter of a male inherits his X-linked alleles. \nThis creates distinctive patterns in X-linked pedigrees. \n \n25. Transmission from Mother to Son \nA mother gives one of her X chromosomes to each child. \nTherefore, each son receives his only X chromosome from his mother. \nIf the mother carries an X-linked recessive allele, she can transmit it to her sons. \n \n26. Transmission from Mother to Daughter \nA daughter receives \n• \nOne X chromosome from her mother  \n• \nOne X chromosome from her father\n\nTherefore, both parents can contribute X-linked alleles to daughters. \n \n27. Haemophilia \nHaemophilia refers to inherited bleeding disorders involving deficiencies or abnormalities of \nspecific blood-clotting factors. \nSome common forms are inherited in an X-linked recessive manner. \nA simplified example uses \nXᴴ \nfor the normal allele \nand \nXʰ \nfor the recessive disease-associated allele. \n \n28. Haemophilia Inheritance \nA carrier female \nXᴴXʰ \nand an unaffected male \nXᴴY \ncan have \n• \nUnaffected daughters  \n• \nCarrier daughters  \n• \nUnaffected sons  \n• \nAffected sons  \nThe exact phenotype and clinical severity depend on the specific haemophilia type and genetic \nvariant. \n \n29. Red-Green Colour Vision Deficiency \nSome forms of red-green colour vision deficiency are inherited as X-linked recessive traits.\n\nAffected individuals have difficulty distinguishing certain colours, particularly within the red-\ngreen range. \nThe responsible genes encode proteins involved in colour detection by cone photoreceptors. \n \n30. Y-Linked Inheritance \nGenes located on the Y chromosome can show Y-linked inheritance. \nY-linked traits can be transmitted \nfrom father to son \nbecause fathers transmit their Y chromosome to sons. \n \n31. Y-Linked Characteristics \nA Y-linked allele in a father is generally transmitted to all biological sons who inherit that Y \nchromosome. \nIt is not transmitted to daughters because daughters do not normally receive a Y chromosome. \nTrue Y-linked traits are relatively uncommon because the Y chromosome contains far fewer \ngenes than the X chromosome. \n \n32. Sex-Influenced Traits \nA sex-influenced trait is controlled by autosomal genes but is expressed differently in males and \nfemales. \nThe phenotype may depend on the hormonal or physiological environment associated with sex. \nSex-influenced inheritance is therefore different from sex-linked inheritance. \n \n33. Sex-Limited Traits \nA sex-limited trait is a characteristic controlled by genes that is expressed only in one sex. \nBoth sexes may carry the relevant genes, but physiological differences restrict expression to one \nsex. \nExamples include certain reproductive characteristics.\n\n34. Sex-Linked Versus Sex-Influenced \nThese terms should not be confused. \nSex-linked \nThe gene is located on a sex chromosome. \nSex-influenced \nThe gene is generally located on an autosome, but its expression differs between sexes. \nSex-limited \nThe gene may be present in both sexes, but the phenotype is expressed in only one sex. \n \n35. Pedigree Analysis \nA pedigree is a diagram showing the inheritance of a characteristic across generations. \nIt can help determine whether a characteristic is likely to be \n• \nAutosomal dominant  \n• \nAutosomal recessive  \n• \nX-linked dominant  \n• \nX-linked recessive  \n• \nY-linked  \n \n36. Pedigree Symbols \nCommon symbols include \nSquare \nMale \nCircle \nFemale \nShaded symbol \nAffected individual \nUnshaded symbol\n\nUnaffected individual \nHorizontal line \nMating relationship \nVertical line \nParent-offspring relationship \n \n37. Identifying X-Linked Recessive Patterns \nTypical clues include \n• \nMore affected males than females  \n• \nAffected sons can have unaffected carrier mothers  \n• \nNo direct father-to-son transmission  \n• \nAn affected male can transmit the allele to all daughters  \n• \nThe characteristic may skip generations  \nThese are clues rather than absolute rules. \n \n38. Identifying X-Linked Dominant Patterns \nTypical clues include \n• \nBoth males and females can be affected  \n• \nAn affected father transmits the allele to all daughters  \n• \nAn affected father does not transmit it to sons  \n• \nAffected individuals often have an affected parent  \n \n39. Identifying Y-Linked Patterns \nA Y-linked characteristic generally shows \n• \nOnly males affected  \n• \nAffected fathers transmit the characteristic to sons  \n• \nAll sons of an affected father inherit the Y chromosome carrying the relevant allele\n\n40. X Chromosome Inactivation \nFemales typically possess two X chromosomes, while males typically possess one. \nTo balance X-linked gene expression, one X chromosome in many female cells becomes largely \ninactive early in embryonic development. \nThis process is called X chromosome inactivation. \n \n41. Barr Body \nThe largely inactive X chromosome forms a condensed structure called a Barr body. \nIn a typical cell with two X chromosomes, one X chromosome is largely inactivated. \n \n42. X-Inactivation and Mosaicism \nX chromosome inactivation occurs independently in many cells. \nAs a result, females heterozygous for certain X-linked genes can have populations of cells \nexpressing different X-linked alleles. \nThis produces a form of cellular mosaicism. \n \n43. Example of X-Inactivation \nSuppose a female has \nXᴬXᵃ \nSome cells may primarily express \nXᴬ \nwhile other cells may primarily express \nXᵃ \nThe overall phenotype depends on the specific gene and the proportion and distribution of cells \nexpressing each allele. \n \n44. Dosage Compensation\n\nDosage compensation refers to mechanisms that balance gene expression between individuals \nwith different numbers of sex chromosomes. \nX chromosome inactivation is a major dosage compensation mechanism in mammals. \n \n45. Other Sex Determination Systems \nNot all organisms use the human XX-XY system. \nExamples include \n• \nXX-XO system  \n• \nZZ-ZW system  \n• \nHaplodiploidy  \n• \nEnvironmental sex determination  \n \n46. XX-XO System \nIn some insects, females have \nXX \nwhile males have \nXO \nThe O indicates the absence of a second sex chromosome. \n \n47. ZZ-ZW System \nIn birds and some other organisms \n• \nMales are ZZ  \n• \nFemales are ZW  \nHere, the female is the heterogametic sex. \nThis differs from the human system. \n \n48. Haplodiploid Sex Determination\n\nIn some insects such as bees and ants \n• \nFemales develop from fertilised eggs  \n• \nMales develop from unfertilised eggs  \nFemales are diploid while males are haploid. \nThis is called haplodiploid sex determination. \n \n49. Environmental Sex Determination \nIn some reptiles and other organisms, environmental factors can influence sex determination. \nTemperature during embryonic development is an important example in certain reptile species. \nThis demonstrates that sex determination is not universally controlled by sex chromosomes. \n \n50. Sex Determination in Humans \nHuman sex determination involves a complex developmental pathway. \nThe typical sequence can be summarised as \nXX or XY chromosome complement \n↓ \nSRY activity in typical XY development \n↓ \nGonadal differentiation \n↓ \nHormone production \n↓ \nDevelopment of reproductive structures \nThe actual biology involves many genes and signalling pathways. \n \n51. Genetic Sex and Phenotypic Sex\n\nChromosomal sex, gonadal development, hormonal pathways and anatomical development are \nrelated but distinct biological concepts. \nVariations can occur at different stages of sexual development. \nTherefore, biological sex development cannot always be described by chromosomes alone. \n \n52. Sex-Linked Disorders \nExamples of X-linked conditions include certain forms of \n• \nHaemophilia  \n• \nRed-green colour vision deficiency  \n• \nDuchenne muscular dystrophy  \nThe inheritance pattern depends on the specific gene and condition. \n \n53. Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy \nDuchenne muscular dystrophy is an inherited disorder involving progressive muscle weakness. \nIt is commonly inherited in an X-linked recessive manner and is associated with variants in the \nDMD gene. \nThe gene encodes dystrophin, an important protein involved in muscle fibre stability. \n \n54. Why X-Linked Recessive Disorders Are More Common in Males \nMales generally have only one X chromosome. \nTherefore, a recessive disease-associated allele on that X chromosome can be expressed because \nthere is no second X chromosome carrying another allele. \nFemales generally have two X chromosomes, so an X-linked recessive condition typically \nrequires disease-associated variants on both copies for full expression. \n \n55. Carrier Mothers \nA carrier mother can transmit an X-linked recessive allele to both sons and daughters. \nHowever\n\n• \nA son who receives the allele may be affected  \n• \nA daughter who receives the allele is often a carrier if she receives a normal allele from \nher father  \n \n56. Affected Father and Daughters \nAn affected male has the disease-associated allele on his X chromosome. \nHe gives his X chromosome to all daughters. \nTherefore, in an X-linked recessive condition, all daughters of an affected male receive his \ndisease-associated X chromosome. \nWhether they are affected or carriers depends on the allele inherited from their mother. \n \n57. Affected Father and Sons \nAn affected father gives his Y chromosome to his sons. \nTherefore, his X-linked disease-associated allele is not directly transmitted to his sons. \nThis is one of the most important features of X-linked inheritance. \n \n58. Pedigree Example \nConsider a carrier mother \nXᴴXʰ \nand affected father \nXʰY \nPossible offspring include \n• \nXᴴXʰ  \n• \nXʰXʰ  \n• \nXᴴY  \n• \nXʰY  \nTherefore, under a simple X-linked recessive model \n• \nSome daughters may be carriers\n\n• \nSome daughters may be affected  \n• \nSome sons may be unaffected  \n• \nSome sons may be affected  \n \n59. Genetic Counselling and Sex-Linked Disorders \nPedigree information and genetic testing can help determine the probability of inheriting an X-\nlinked condition. \nThis information can be used in genetic counselling to explain possible inheritance patterns to \nfamilies. \n \n60. Important Comparisons \nFeature \nAutosomal X-linked \nY-linked \nGene location \nAutosome X chromosome \nY chromosome \nAffects males \nYes \nYes \nYes \nAffects females \nYes \nYes \nNo \nFather-to-son transmission \nPossible \nNo direct transmission \nYes \nFather-to-daughter transmission Possible \nX-linked alleles transmitted No \n \n61. Key Terms \nSex determination \nThe biological mechanism establishing sexual development. \nSex chromosome \nA chromosome involved in sex determination or sex-associated inheritance. \nHomogametic \nProducing one type of sex chromosome in gametes. \nHeterogametic \nProducing two types of sex chromosomes in gametes.\n\nX-linked \nA gene located on the X chromosome. \nY-linked \nA gene located on the Y chromosome. \nHemizygous \nHaving only one copy of a particular gene, as occurs for many X-linked genes in typical males. \nCarrier \nAn individual carrying a recessive allele without expressing the associated phenotype under a \nsimple inheritance model. \nSex-influenced \nA trait whose expression differs between sexes despite generally being controlled by autosomal \ngenes. \nSex-limited \nA trait whose expression is restricted to one sex. \nX chromosome inactivation \nThe largely inactive state established for one X chromosome in many cells of typical female \nmammals. \nBarr body \nThe condensed, largely inactive X chromosome. \n \n62. Final Summary \nHuman sex determination is associated with the X and Y sex chromosomes. Typical females \nhave an XX chromosome complement and typical males have an XY complement. Females are \ntherefore homogametic, producing eggs carrying X chromosomes, while males are \nheterogametic, producing sperm carrying either X or Y. \nThe Y chromosome contains the SRY gene, which plays an important role in initiating testis \ndevelopment in typical XY development. Sexual development subsequently involves complex \ninteractions among genes, hormones and developmental pathways. \nGenes located on sex chromosomes can show distinctive inheritance patterns. X-linked genes are \nparticularly important because males generally possess only one X chromosome and are\n\ntherefore hemizygous for many X-linked genes. X-linked recessive conditions are consequently \nmore frequently expressed in males. \nImportant examples of X-linked conditions include certain forms of haemophilia, red-green \ncolour vision deficiency and Duchenne muscular dystrophy. X-linked inheritance is characterised \nby the absence of direct father-to-son transmission because fathers pass their Y chromosome to \nsons and their X chromosome to daughters. \nY-linked characteristics can pass from affected fathers to their sons, while sex-influenced and \nsex-limited characteristics involve genes whose expression differs according to sex but are not \nnecessarily located on sex chromosomes. \nPedigree analysis helps identify inheritance patterns by examining the distribution of \ncharacteristics across generations. X chromosome inactivation provides a major mechanism of \ndosage compensation in typical female mammals and can produce cellular mosaicism. \nDifferent organisms use different sex determination systems, including XX-XY, XX-XO, ZZ-\nZW, haplodiploidy and environmental sex determination.	COMPLETED	106	2026-08-19 18:11:44.394671	2026-08-19 18:12:51.882901	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
987	Lesson 5: Gene linkage and chromosome mapping - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 5: Gene linkage and chromosome mapping	PDF	uploads/pdf/4e6364a1aa8c4623a39e3ba80049032d.pdf	\N	Lesson 5 — Gene Linkage and Chromosome Mapping \nIntroduction \nMendel's law of independent assortment applies most directly to genes located on different \nchromosomes or genes sufficiently far apart on the same chromosome. However, genes located \nclose together on the same chromosome tend to be inherited together. \nThis phenomenon is called gene linkage. \nThe study of linkage allows geneticists to determine the relative positions of genes on \nchromosomes. The distance between genes can be estimated by measuring the frequency of \nrecombination produced by crossing over. \n \n1. Gene Linkage \nGene linkage is the tendency of genes located on the same chromosome to be inherited together \nduring meiosis. \nGenes located on the same chromosome form a linkage group. \nThe number of linkage groups in an organism generally corresponds to its haploid chromosome \nnumber. \n \n2. Linked Genes \nGenes located on the same chromosome are called linked genes. \nFor example, if genes A and B are located on the same chromosome, they may tend to be \ninherited together. \nThe closer the genes are to each other, the stronger their linkage tends to be. \n \n3. Independent Assortment Versus Linkage \nIndependent assortment \nLinkage \nGenes assort independently \nGenes tend to be inherited together \nUsually applies to genes on different \nchromosomes \nApplies to genes on the same chromosome \nProduces expected independent combinations \nProduces more parental combinations\n\nIndependent assortment \nLinkage \nRecombinant combinations may occur through \nindependent assortment \nRecombinant combinations mainly arise \nthrough crossing over \n \n4. Chromosomal Arrangement of Alleles \nTwo linked genes can be arranged in different ways on homologous chromosomes. \nConsider a double heterozygote with alleles \nA B / a b \nThe dominant alleles are located on one homologous chromosome and the recessive alleles on \nthe other. \nThis arrangement is called coupling or cis arrangement. \n \n5. Repulsion Arrangement \nAnother possible arrangement is \nA b / a B \nThe dominant allele of one gene is located with the recessive allele of the other gene. \nThis is called repulsion or trans arrangement. \n \n6. Parental Combinations \nThe allele combinations originally present on the homologous chromosomes are called parental \ncombinations. \nFor \nAB / ab \nthe parental combinations are \nAB \nand \nab\n\n7. Recombinant Combinations \nNew combinations of alleles produced by crossing over are called recombinant combinations. \nFor \nAB / ab \nthe recombinant combinations are \nAb \nand \naB \n \n8. Crossing Over \nCrossing over is the exchange of corresponding DNA segments between non-sister chromatids \nof homologous chromosomes during meiosis. \nIt occurs during prophase I of meiosis. \nCrossing over can separate linked genes and generate recombinant chromosomes. \n \n9. Chiasmata \nThe visible points where homologous chromosomes appear to exchange genetic material are \ncalled chiasmata. \nA chiasma represents a physical manifestation of crossing over between homologous \nchromosomes. \n \n10. Non-Sister Chromatids \nCrossing over occurs between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes. \nEach homologous chromosome consists of two sister chromatids. \nTherefore, crossing over involves chromatids from different homologues. \n \n11. Effect of Gene Distance \nThe probability of crossing over between two genes depends partly on their distance apart.\n\nGenes that are \nclose together \nhave a lower probability of crossing over between them. \nGenes that are \nfarther apart \nhave a greater probability of recombination. \n \n12. Complete Linkage \nComplete linkage refers to a theoretical situation in which linked genes are always inherited \ntogether and no crossing over occurs between them. \nThe offspring would contain only parental combinations. \nComplete linkage is uncommon because crossing over can occur. \n \n13. Incomplete Linkage \nIncomplete linkage occurs when linked genes are inherited together most of the time but \ncrossing over occasionally separates them. \nThis produces both \n• \nParental offspring  \n• \nRecombinant offspring  \nIncomplete linkage is the usual situation for linked genes. \n \n14. Linkage and Meiosis \nDuring meiosis, homologous chromosomes pair during prophase I. \nIf crossing over occurs between two linked genes, the alleles can be rearranged. \nThe resulting gametes may therefore contain combinations that were not present on the original \nparental chromosomes. \n \n15. Recombinant Gametes\n\nConsider \nAB / ab \nWithout crossing over between A and B, the major gamete types are \nAB \nand \nab \nIf crossing over occurs between the genes, recombinant gametes can be produced \nAb \nand \naB \n \n16. Recombinant Frequency \nThe proportion of recombinant offspring in a genetic cross is called the recombination \nfrequency. \nIt can be calculated using \nRecombination frequency = Number of recombinant offspring / Total number of offspring \n× 100 \n \n17. Example of Recombination Frequency \nSuppose a cross produces \n800 total offspring \nand \n80 recombinant offspring \nThen \nRecombination frequency = 80 / 800 × 100 \n= 10% \nTherefore, the recombination frequency is \n10%\n\n18. Genetic Map Units \nA recombination frequency of 1% corresponds approximately to 1 map unit. \nOne map unit is also called a \ncentimorgan \nand is abbreviated \ncM \nTherefore \n1% recombination ≈ 1 cM \n \n19. Genetic Distance \nGenetic distance represents the relative distance between genes on a chromosome. \nFor example \n10% recombination ≈ 10 cM \nThe value provides an estimate of the distance between two loci. \n \n20. Genetic Maps \nA genetic map represents the relative positions of genes along a chromosome. \nIt does not necessarily represent the physical distance in base pairs. \nFor example \nA — 10 cM — B — 5 cM — C \nindicates that B is 10 map units from A and C is 5 map units from B. \n \n21. Locus \nA locus is the specific position occupied by a gene or DNA sequence on a chromosome. \nThe plural form is \nloci\n\nGenes can therefore be described as occupying particular loci on chromosomes. \n \n22. Chromosome Mapping \nChromosome mapping involves determining the relative positions of genes or genetic markers \non chromosomes. \nRecombination frequencies can be used to construct genetic maps. \n \n23. Two-Point Test Cross \nA two-point test cross is commonly used to determine linkage between two genes. \nA double heterozygote is crossed with an individual homozygous recessive for both genes. \nFor example \nAB / ab × ab / ab \nThe offspring phenotypes reveal the gametes produced by the heterozygous parent. \n \n24. Why a Test Cross Is Useful \nThe recessive parent produces only one type of gamete \nab \nTherefore, the phenotype of each offspring directly reflects the gamete contributed by the \nheterozygous parent. \nThis makes recombinant and parental offspring easier to identify. \n \n25. Two-Point Test Cross Example \nConsider \nAB / ab × ab / ab \nSuppose the offspring are \nOffspring type Number \nAB \n420\n\nOffspring type Number \nab \n430 \nAb \n75 \naB \n75 \nTotal \n1000 \nParental offspring \n420 + 430 = 850 \nRecombinant offspring \n75 + 75 = 150 \n \n26. Recombination Frequency Calculation \nUsing the previous example \nRecombination frequency = 150 / 1000 × 100 \n= 15% \nTherefore, the estimated genetic distance is \n15 cM \n \n27. Identifying Parental Classes \nIn a test cross involving linked genes, the most frequent offspring classes are generally the \nparental classes. \nThe less frequent classes are generally the recombinant classes. \nThis is because crossing over usually occurs less frequently than the absence of crossing over for \nclosely linked genes. \n \n28. Recombination Frequency and Linkage Strength \nA low recombination frequency indicates strong linkage.\n\nA high recombination frequency indicates weaker linkage. \nTherefore \nCloser genes → lower recombination frequency \nMore distant genes → higher recombination frequency \n \n29. Maximum Observable Recombination Frequency \nFor two genes, the observed recombination frequency cannot meaningfully exceed 50%. \nWhen genes are very far apart on the same chromosome, multiple crossing-over events can \noccur. \nThe resulting gametes can approach a 50:50 parental-to-recombinant distribution, making the \ngenes appear to assort independently. \n \n30. Why Recombination Frequency Does Not Equal Physical Distance Exactly \nA genetic distance based on recombination frequency is not a direct measurement of DNA \nlength. \nMultiple crossing-over events can occur between two genes. \nSome of these events can restore the original allele arrangement. \nTherefore, recombination frequency can underestimate the actual physical distance between \ngenes, especially when genes are far apart. \n \n31. Double Crossing Over \nA double crossover involves two crossing-over events occurring within the same chromosome \nregion. \nDepending on the chromatids involved, double crossing over can produce combinations that \nwould not be predicted from a single crossover. \n \n32. Multiple Crossing Over \nMore than two crossover events can occur between genes.\n\nAs the distance between genes increases, multiple crossover events become increasingly \nimportant. \nThis is one reason why very distant genes may show an observed recombination frequency close \nto 50%. \n \n33. Three-Point Test Cross \nA three-point test cross examines the linkage relationships among three genes simultaneously. \nIt allows researchers to determine \n• \nGene order  \n• \nRelative distances between genes  \n• \nRecombination frequencies  \n• \nDouble crossover events  \n \n34. Three Linked Genes \nSuppose three genes are located on the same chromosome \nA — B — C \nA three-point cross can determine whether this arrangement is correct and estimate the distances \nbetween the genes. \n \n35. Gene Order \nDetermining gene order means identifying which gene lies in the middle. \nFor example, three genes may be arranged as \nA — B — C \nor \nB — A — C \nor \nA — C — B \nGenetic crosses can distinguish among these possibilities.\n\n36. Identifying Gene Order \nIn a three-point test cross \n1. Identify the parental classes.  \n2. Identify the double recombinant classes.  \n3. Compare the parental and double recombinant arrangements.  \n4. Determine which gene has changed position relative to the other two.  \n5. That gene is the middle gene.  \n \n37. Double Recombinants \nThe least frequent offspring classes in a typical three-point test cross are often the double \nrecombinant classes. \nThey result from two crossover events. \n \n38. Why Double Recombinants Are Important \nComparing the double recombinant classes with the parental classes reveals which allele has \nchanged its relationship with the other two. \nThis identifies the middle gene. \n \n39. Genetic Map Example \nSuppose the calculated distances are \nA to B = 8 cM \nB to C = 12 cM \nThe genetic map can be represented as \nA — 8 cM — B — 12 cM — C \nThe estimated distance between A and C is approximately \n20 cM \nwhen there is no adjustment for multiple crossover events.\n\n40. Coefficient of Coincidence \nThe coefficient of coincidence compares the observed number of double crossovers with the \nexpected number. \nIt is calculated as \nCoefficient of coincidence = Observed double crossovers / Expected double crossovers \n \n41. Expected Double Crossovers \nIf two chromosome intervals have recombination frequencies \nr₁ \nand \nr₂ \nthe expected frequency of double crossovers can be estimated as \nr₁ × r₂ \nwhen the two crossover events are assumed to occur independently. \n \n42. Example of Expected Double Crossovers \nSuppose \nr₁ = 0.10 \nand \nr₂ = 0.20 \nExpected double crossover frequency \n0.10 × 0.20 = 0.02 \nTherefore, the expected double crossover frequency is \n2% \n \n43. Interference\n\nInterference describes the effect of one crossover on the probability of another crossover \noccurring nearby. \nIt is calculated using \nInterference = 1 − coefficient of coincidence \n \n44. Positive Interference \nPositive interference occurs when fewer double crossovers are observed than expected. \nOne crossover reduces the likelihood of another nearby crossover. \nThe coefficient of coincidence is less than 1. \n \n45. Negative Interference \nNegative interference occurs when more double crossovers are observed than expected. \nThe coefficient of coincidence is greater than 1. \n \n46. Importance of Linkage Mapping \nGenetic maps are useful for \n• \nLocating genes  \n• \nStudying inheritance  \n• \nIdentifying disease-associated genes  \n• \nUnderstanding chromosome structure  \n• \nStudying genetic variation  \n• \nSupporting genetic research  \n \n47. Linkage and Evolution \nRecombination changes combinations of alleles within populations. \nLinked genes tend to remain associated, but crossing over can generate new combinations. \nThis contributes to genetic variation, which is important for evolutionary processes.\n\n48. Linkage Groups \nAll genes located on the same chromosome form a linkage group. \nFor an organism with a haploid chromosome number of n, the number of linkage groups is \ngenerally approximately n, although chromosome structure and genetic peculiarities can \ncomplicate this relationship. \n \n49. Genetic Markers \nA genetic marker is a DNA sequence or identifiable genetic variation that can be used to track \nthe inheritance of a chromosome region. \nMarkers can be useful when studying the location of genes. \n \n50. Molecular Markers \nModern genetic mapping can use molecular markers such as \n• \nSTRs  \n• \nSNPs  \n• \nMicrosatellites  \n• \nSpecific DNA variants  \nThese markers can be followed through families or populations. \n \n51. Linkage Analysis \nLinkage analysis examines whether two genetic markers or genes are inherited together more \nfrequently than would be expected from independent assortment. \nIf they are frequently inherited together, they may be located close to each other on the same \nchromosome. \n \n52. Recombination and Genetic Distance \nRecombination frequency provides an estimate of genetic distance. \nFor example\n\n5% recombination ≈ 5 cM \n25% recombination ≈ 25 cM \n50% recombination ≈ approximately independent assortment \nThe approximation becomes less reliable as genes become farther apart. \n \n53. Physical Maps Versus Genetic Maps \nA genetic map describes distances using recombination frequencies. \nA physical map describes actual physical positions along DNA, often using base pairs. \nTherefore \nGenetic distance ≠ necessarily physical distance \n \n54. Comparison of Mapping Methods \nFeature \nGenetic map \nPhysical map \nBasis \nRecombination \nDNA structure \nUnit \ncM \nBase pairs \nMeasures \nRelative genetic distance Physical DNA distance \nInfluenced by crossing-over frequency Yes \nNo \n \n55. Linkage Disequilibrium \nLinkage disequilibrium describes a non-random association between alleles at different loci \nwithin a population. \nIt can occur when loci are physically linked, although population history, selection, mutation and \nother evolutionary processes can also influence linkage disequilibrium. \n \n56. Important Factors Affecting Recombination \nRecombination frequency can be influenced by \n• \nDistance between genes\n\n• \nPosition along chromosomes  \n• \nCrossover frequency  \n• \nMultiple crossovers  \n• \nSex and species-specific recombination patterns  \nTherefore, genetic maps represent estimates rather than exact physical measurements. \n \n57. Parental and Recombinant Offspring \nType \nDescription \nParental \nContains original allele combinations \nRecombinant Contains new allele combinations produced by recombination \nIn a linked test cross, parental classes are usually more frequent than recombinant classes. \n \n58. Example of Linked Genes \nSuppose \nA B / a b \nrepresents two linked genes. \nWithout crossing over, gametes are mainly \nAB \nand \nab \nWith crossing over, additional gametes can be produced \nAb \nand \naB \nThe proportion of these recombinant gametes provides information about the distance between \nthe genes.\n\n59. Linkage Does Not Prevent Crossing Over \nLinked genes are not permanently attached. \nCrossing over can separate them. \nTherefore, linkage describes a tendency for genes to be inherited together rather than an absolute \nrule. \n \n60. Linkage and Mendel's Law \nGene linkage does not invalidate Mendel's laws. \nRather, it explains why some genes do not appear to follow independent assortment. \nMendel's law of independent assortment applies when genes behave independently, whereas \nlinked genes can deviate from the expected independent assortment ratio. \n \n61. Key Terms \nGene linkage \nTendency of genes on the same chromosome to be inherited together. \nLinked genes \nGenes located on the same chromosome. \nLinkage group \nAll genes located on one chromosome. \nCrossing over \nExchange of genetic material between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes. \nChiasma \nA visible point associated with crossing over. \nRecombinant \nAn allele combination different from the original parental combinations. \nRecombination frequency \nPercentage of offspring that are recombinant. \nCentimorgan\n\nA unit of genetic distance approximately corresponding to 1% recombination. \nGenetic map \nA representation of the relative positions of genes based on recombination frequencies. \nLocus \nSpecific position of a gene or DNA sequence on a chromosome. \nTest cross \nA cross involving an individual of unknown genotype and a homozygous recessive individual. \nDouble crossover \nTwo crossover events occurring within the same chromosome region. \nGene order \nThe sequence in which genes occur along a chromosome. \nInterference \nThe influence of one crossover on another nearby crossover. \n \n62. Final Summary \nGene linkage occurs when genes are located on the same chromosome and tend to be inherited \ntogether. The closer two genes are to each other, the less likely crossing over is to occur between \nthem. \nDuring prophase I of meiosis, crossing over occurs between non-sister chromatids of \nhomologous chromosomes. This can produce recombinant chromosomes containing new \ncombinations of alleles. \nThe frequency of recombinant offspring is used to calculate recombination frequency. \nApproximately 1% recombination corresponds to 1 map unit or 1 centimorgan. \nTwo-point test crosses can estimate the distance between two genes, while three-point test \ncrosses can determine gene order and distances among three genes. Double crossover classes are \nparticularly useful for identifying the middle gene. \nGenetic maps describe relative genetic distances rather than exact physical distances. Multiple \ncrossover events can cause recombination frequency to underestimate the actual physical \ndistance between genes.\n\nGene linkage, crossing over and recombination are important mechanisms contributing to genetic \nvariation and provide powerful tools for studying chromosome organisation and inheritance.	COMPLETED	107	2026-08-19 18:11:45.354024	2026-08-19 18:12:51.882902	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
988	Lesson 6: Chromosomal aberrations and gene mutations - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 6: Chromosomal aberrations and gene mutations	PDF	uploads/pdf/2e00086d4fab498d97ca57c403f5d418.pdf	\N	Lesson 6 — Chromosomal Aberrations and Gene Mutations \nIntroduction \nThe normal structure and number of chromosomes are essential for the proper development and \nfunctioning of an organism. Changes in chromosome number or structure can alter genetic \ninformation and may lead to genetic disorders. \nChanges can occur at different levels. Chromosomal aberrations involve changes in \nchromosome number or structure, while gene mutations involve changes in the DNA sequence \nof individual genes or small DNA regions. \nUnderstanding these changes is important for explaining genetic disorders, variation, evolution \nand the biological consequences of errors during DNA replication and cell division. \n \n1. Chromosomal Aberrations \nA chromosomal aberration is a change in the normal number or structure of chromosomes. \nChromosomal abnormalities can be broadly divided into \n• \nNumerical abnormalities  \n• \nStructural abnormalities  \n \n2. Numerical Chromosomal Abnormalities \nNumerical abnormalities occur when cells contain an abnormal number of chromosomes. \nThey can result from errors during chromosome segregation. \nMajor types include \n• \nAneuploidy  \n• \nPolyploidy  \n \n3. Euploidy \nEuploidy refers to changes involving complete sets of chromosomes. \nFor example, an organism may possess \nn \n2n\n\n3n \n4n \nor another complete chromosome-set number. \n \n4. Polyploidy \nPolyploidy occurs when an organism possesses more than two complete sets of chromosomes. \nExamples include \n3n — triploid \n4n — tetraploid \n6n — hexaploid \nPolyploidy is particularly common and important in plants. \n \n5. Aneuploidy \nAneuploidy occurs when there is a gain or loss of individual chromosomes rather than an entire \nchromosome set. \nMajor forms include \nMonosomy \nLoss of one chromosome \nTrisomy \nGain of one chromosome \n \n6. Monosomy \nMonosomy occurs when an individual has only one copy of a particular chromosome. \nThe chromosome number can be represented as \n2n − 1 \nA well-known human example is Turner syndrome.\n\n7. Trisomy \nTrisomy occurs when an individual possesses three copies of a particular chromosome. \nThe chromosome number can be represented as \n2n + 1 \nA well-known example is Down syndrome. \n \n8. Nondisjunction \nNondisjunction is the failure of chromosomes to separate properly during cell division. \nIt can occur during \n• \nMeiosis I  \n• \nMeiosis II  \n• \nMitosis  \nNondisjunction can result in cells containing abnormal chromosome numbers. \n \n9. Nondisjunction During Meiosis I \nDuring meiosis I, homologous chromosomes normally separate. \nIf they fail to separate, both homologues may move to the same daughter cell. \nThis produces gametes with abnormal chromosome numbers. \n \n10. Nondisjunction During Meiosis II \nDuring meiosis II, sister chromatids normally separate. \nIf they fail to separate, abnormal gametes can be produced. \nThe resulting gametes may contain \n• \nAn extra chromosome  \n• \nOne chromosome fewer  \n• \nA normal chromosome number\n\n11. Consequences of Nondisjunction \nIf an abnormal gamete participates in fertilisation, the resulting zygote may have an abnormal \nchromosome number. \nFor example \nn + 1 + n = 2n + 1 \nproduces a trisomic zygote. \n \n12. Down Syndrome \nDown syndrome is commonly caused by trisomy 21. \nIndividuals typically have three copies of chromosome 21 rather than two. \nThe condition is associated with characteristic developmental and physical features and variable \ndegrees of intellectual disability. \n \n13. Cause of Down Syndrome \nMost cases result from nondisjunction during meiosis. \nThe extra chromosome 21 can originate from either parent. \nThe likelihood of Down syndrome increases with maternal age, although it can occur at any \nreproductive age. \n \n14. Karyotype of Down Syndrome \nA karyotype showing trisomy 21 contains \n47 chromosomes \ninstead of the typical 46. \nThe additional chromosome is chromosome 21. \n \n15. Turner Syndrome \nTurner syndrome is associated with monosomy X. \nThe typical chromosomal constitution is\n\n45,X \nrather than 46 chromosomes. \nIt generally occurs in individuals who develop along a female pathway. \n \n16. Features of Turner Syndrome \nCommon features may include \n• \nShort stature  \n• \nOvarian insufficiency  \n• \nInfertility  \n• \nWebbed neck  \n• \nCertain cardiovascular abnormalities  \nThe presentation varies among individuals. \n \n17. Klinefelter Syndrome \nKlinefelter syndrome is commonly associated with \n47,XXY \nThe individual has an additional X chromosome and typically develops along a male pathway. \nPossible features include \n• \nTall stature  \n• \nSmall testes  \n• \nReduced testosterone production  \n• \nInfertility  \n• \nVariable breast development  \n \n18. Comparison of Major Aneuploidies\n\nCondition \nChromosomal abnormality Common notation \nDown syndrome \nTrisomy 21 \n47,+21 \nTurner syndrome \nMonosomy X \n45,X \nKlinefelter syndrome Additional X chromosome \n47,XXY \n \n19. Mosaicism \nMosaicism occurs when an individual has two or more genetically distinct cell populations \nderived from the same zygote. \nMosaicism can arise from mitotic errors after fertilisation. \n \n20. Mosaic Down Syndrome \nIn mosaic Down syndrome, some cells have \n47,+21 \nwhile other cells have the typical chromosome number. \nThe clinical presentation can differ from that of full trisomy 21. \n \n21. Structural Chromosomal Abnormalities \nStructural abnormalities occur when chromosome structure is altered. \nMajor types include \n• \nDeletion  \n• \nDuplication  \n• \nInversion  \n• \nTranslocation  \n \n22. Deletion \nA deletion occurs when a chromosome segment is lost. \nThis results in the loss of genetic material.\n\nThe effects depend on which genes are lost and the size of the deleted region. \n \n23. Duplication \nA duplication occurs when a chromosome segment is present in an additional copy. \nThis increases the dosage of genes within that region. \n \n24. Inversion \nAn inversion occurs when a chromosome segment breaks, rotates 180 degrees and reinserts into \nthe chromosome. \nThe genetic material remains present but its orientation has changed. \n \n25. Types of Inversion \nTwo major types are \nParacentric inversion \nThe inverted segment does not include the centromere. \nPericentric inversion \nThe inverted segment includes the centromere. \n \n26. Translocation \nA translocation occurs when a chromosome segment moves to another chromosome. \nIt can involve \n• \nNon-homologous chromosomes  \n• \nExchange between chromosomes  \n \n27. Reciprocal Translocation \nA reciprocal translocation occurs when two non-homologous chromosomes exchange \nsegments. \nThe total amount of genetic material may remain approximately unchanged.\n\nSuch a translocation can therefore be balanced. \n \n28. Balanced Translocation \nA balanced chromosomal rearrangement does not involve an obvious net gain or loss of genetic \nmaterial. \nThe individual may have no major clinical symptoms. \nHowever, abnormal chromosome segregation during meiosis can increase the risk of producing \ngametes with unbalanced chromosome arrangements. \n \n29. Unbalanced Translocation \nAn unbalanced translocation results in a gain or loss of chromosomal material. \nThis can alter gene dosage and may lead to developmental or health problems. \n \n30. Robertsonian Translocation \nA Robertsonian translocation is a specific type of chromosomal rearrangement involving the \nfusion of long arms of certain acrocentric chromosomes. \nIn humans, chromosomes \n13, 14, 15, 21 and 22 \nare acrocentric. \nRobertsonian translocations can have important reproductive consequences. \n \n31. Chromosomal Abnormalities and Fertility \nChromosomal abnormalities can affect fertility by disrupting \n• \nGamete formation  \n• \nMeiosis  \n• \nEmbryonic development  \n• \nGene dosage\n\nIndividuals carrying balanced rearrangements may have normal development but increased \nreproductive risks. \n \n32. Chromosomal Abnormalities and Cancer \nChromosomal rearrangements can contribute to cancer by altering gene regulation. \nSome translocations create abnormal gene combinations that promote uncontrolled cell \nproliferation. \nA well-known example occurs in certain forms of leukaemia. \n \n33. Philadelphia Chromosome \nThe Philadelphia chromosome is associated with many cases of chronic myeloid leukaemia. \nIt results from a reciprocal translocation involving chromosomes \n9 and 22 \nThis creates the \nBCR-ABL1 \nfusion gene. \n \n34. Gene Mutations \nA gene mutation is a change in the DNA nucleotide sequence of a gene. \nMutations can occur naturally through errors in DNA replication or DNA repair. \nThey can also be caused by certain environmental factors. \n \n35. Sources of Mutations \nMutations can arise from \n• \nDNA replication errors  \n• \nDNA repair errors  \n• \nSpontaneous chemical changes  \n• \nRadiation\n\n• \nCertain chemicals  \n• \nSome biological agents  \nAgents that increase mutation rates are called mutagens. \n \n36. Mutagens \nA mutagen is an agent that increases the frequency of mutations. \nExamples include certain forms of \n• \nUltraviolet radiation  \n• \nIonising radiation  \n• \nChemical substances  \nNot every mutation is caused by an external mutagen. \n \n37. Somatic Mutations \nA somatic mutation occurs in a body cell other than a germ cell or germline precursor. \nSomatic mutations are generally not transmitted to offspring. \nHowever, they can be passed to daughter cells during mitotic division. \n \n38. Germline Mutations \nA germline mutation occurs in cells contributing to the production of gametes or is present in \nthe germline. \nSuch mutations can be transmitted to offspring. \nThey are an important source of inherited genetic variation. \n \n39. Point Mutations \nA point mutation involves a change affecting a single nucleotide position or a very small DNA \nregion. \nCommon types include \n• \nSubstitution\n\n• \nInsertion  \n• \nDeletion  \n \n40. Base Substitution \nA substitution occurs when one nucleotide base is replaced by another. \nFor example \nA → G \nThis changes one nucleotide in the DNA sequence. \n \n41. Transition \nA transition is a substitution between bases of the same chemical category. \nPurines \nA and G \nPyrimidines \nC and T \nTherefore \nA ↔ G \nand \nC ↔ T \nare transitions. \n \n42. Transversion \nA transversion is a substitution between a purine and a pyrimidine. \nExamples include \nA ↔ C \nA ↔ T \nG ↔ C\n\nG ↔ T \n \n43. Effects of Substitution Mutations \nA substitution can produce different outcomes. \nIt may be \n• \nSilent  \n• \nMissense  \n• \nNonsense  \nThe effect depends on how the altered codon affects the encoded protein. \n \n44. Silent Mutation \nA silent mutation changes a codon but does not change the amino acid encoded. \nThis is possible because the genetic code is degenerate. \nA silent mutation does not necessarily have no biological effect, but it often does not alter the \nprotein's amino acid sequence. \n \n45. Missense Mutation \nA missense mutation changes a codon so that a different amino acid is incorporated into the \nprotein. \nThe effect depends on \n• \nThe position of the amino acid  \n• \nThe chemical properties of the substituted amino acid  \n• \nThe importance of that region of the protein  \n \n46. Nonsense Mutation \nA nonsense mutation changes a codon into a premature stop codon. \nTranslation therefore terminates earlier than normal. \nThis can produce a shortened protein that may be non-functional.\n\n47. Insertion Mutation \nAn insertion occurs when one or more nucleotides are added to a DNA sequence. \nThe effect depends on the number of nucleotides inserted. \n \n48. Deletion Mutation \nA deletion occurs when one or more nucleotides are removed from a DNA sequence. \nAs with insertions, the consequences depend on the location and size of the deletion. \n \n49. Frameshift Mutation \nIf nucleotides are inserted or deleted in numbers that are not multiples of three, the reading frame \nof the genetic sequence can change. \nThis is called a frameshift mutation. \n \n50. Consequences of Frameshift Mutations \nA frameshift changes the grouping of nucleotides into codons from the mutation onward. \nAs a result \n• \nMany subsequent amino acids may change  \n• \nA premature stop codon may appear  \n• \nThe resulting protein may lose its normal function  \nFrameshift mutations can therefore have substantial effects. \n \n51. In-Frame Mutation \nIf three nucleotides or a multiple of three are inserted or deleted, the reading frame remains \nunchanged. \nThis is called an in-frame insertion or deletion. \nOne or more amino acids may be added or removed without changing the downstream reading \nframe.\n\n52. Mutation in Coding and Non-Coding Regions \nMutations can occur in \n• \nProtein-coding sequences  \n• \nPromoters  \n• \nEnhancers  \n• \nIntrons  \n• \nRegulatory regions  \n• \nOther non-coding DNA  \nA mutation does not need to occur within a protein-coding region to have a biological effect. \n \n53. Regulatory Mutations \nA mutation in a regulatory region can alter the amount, timing or location of gene expression. \nTherefore, a normal protein sequence can sometimes be produced in abnormal quantities or at \nthe wrong time. \n \n54. Loss-of-Function Mutation \nA loss-of-function mutation reduces or eliminates the normal activity of a gene product. \nThe severity depends on the gene and the nature of the mutation. \n \n55. Gain-of-Function Mutation \nA gain-of-function mutation produces a new or increased activity of a gene product. \nSome gain-of-function mutations can contribute to disease. \n \n56. Neutral Mutations \nSome mutations have little or no detectable effect on phenotype. \nThey may occur in regions with little functional consequence or produce changes that do not \nsignificantly alter biological function.\n\n57. Beneficial Mutations \nSome mutations can provide an advantage under particular environmental conditions. \nFor example, a mutation may increase resistance to a particular environmental stress. \nThe effect of a mutation therefore depends on the environment and biological context. \n \n58. Harmful Mutations \nSome mutations interfere with normal biological functions and can cause disease. \nThe effect depends on \n• \nGene affected  \n• \nType of mutation  \n• \nLocation of mutation  \n• \nAllelic state  \n• \nEnvironmental conditions  \n \n59. Mutation and Genetic Variation \nMutations are an original source of new genetic variants. \nThey provide the raw genetic variation on which natural selection and other evolutionary \nprocesses can act. \n \n60. Mutation and Evolution \nA mutation may \nalter DNA \n↓ \nalter RNA or gene regulation \n↓ \nalter protein or gene expression\n\n↓ \nalter phenotype \nIf the mutation is heritable, it can contribute to genetic variation within a population. \nNatural selection may increase or decrease its frequency depending on its effects. \n \n61. Mutation Rates \nDifferent genes and organisms have different mutation rates. \nDNA repair mechanisms help maintain genomic stability. \nHowever, DNA replication is not completely error-free. \n \n62. DNA Repair \nCells possess several DNA repair mechanisms. \nThese mechanisms can correct many types of DNA damage or replication errors. \nExamples include \n• \nMismatch repair  \n• \nBase excision repair  \n• \nNucleotide excision repair  \n• \nDouble-strand break repair  \n \n63. DNA Damage Versus Mutation \nDNA damage and mutation are related but not identical. \nDNA damage \nA chemical or structural alteration of DNA. \nMutation \nA permanent change in the DNA sequence that remains after replication or repair. \nDNA damage can therefore lead to mutation if it is not correctly repaired.\n\n64. Mutations and Cancer \nMutations affecting genes that regulate cell division can contribute to cancer. \nImportant categories include genes involved in \n• \nCell proliferation  \n• \nDNA repair  \n• \nApoptosis  \n• \nCell-cycle control  \nCancer generally results from the accumulation of multiple genetic and epigenetic changes rather \nthan one mutation alone. \n \n65. Oncogenes \nAn oncogene is a gene that can promote cancer development when abnormally activated. \nMany oncogenes originate from normal genes called proto-oncogenes. \nMutations or other changes can cause excessive or inappropriate activity. \n \n66. Tumour Suppressor Genes \nTumour suppressor genes normally help prevent uncontrolled cell growth. \nLoss or inactivation of tumour suppressor function can contribute to cancer development. \n \n67. Germline and Somatic Mutations \nFeature \nGermline mutation Somatic mutation \nOccurs in \nGermline \nBody cells \nCan be inherited \nYes \nGenerally no \nPassed to offspring \nPossible \nGenerally not \nCan contribute to cancer Yes \nYes \n \n68. Chromosomal Aberration Versus Gene Mutation\n\nFeature Chromosomal aberration \nGene mutation \nScale \nLarge chromosome region or whole \nchromosome \nDNA sequence within a gene or small \nregion \nExamples Trisomy, deletion, inversion \nSubstitution, insertion, deletion \nCan \naffect \nMany genes \nOne gene or nearby regulatory region \nCause \nChromosome segregation or structural \nbreakage \nDNA sequence alteration \n \n69. Numerical Versus Structural Abnormalities \nNumerical abnormality \nChanges the number of chromosomes. \nExamples \nTrisomy 21 \nMonosomy X \nStructural abnormality \nChanges chromosome structure. \nExamples \nDeletion \nDuplication \nInversion \nTranslocation \n \n70. Karyotyping \nA karyotype is an organised display of an individual's chromosomes. \nIt can be used to identify certain chromosomal abnormalities such as \n• \nTrisomy\n\n• \nMonosomy  \n• \nLarge deletions  \n• \nLarge duplications  \n• \nSome translocations  \n \n71. Cytogenetic Analysis \nCytogenetics is the study of chromosomes and their structure, number and behaviour. \nTechniques can be used to detect chromosome abnormalities. \nModern approaches may include \n• \nKaryotyping  \n• \nFluorescence in situ hybridisation  \n• \nChromosomal microarray analysis  \n• \nDNA sequencing  \n \n72. Chromosomal Microarray \nChromosomal microarray analysis can detect many relatively small DNA copy-number changes \nthat may not be visible through conventional karyotyping. \nIt can identify \n• \nDeletions  \n• \nDuplications  \n• \nCopy-number variations  \n \n73. DNA Sequencing \nDNA sequencing determines the nucleotide sequence of DNA. \nIt can identify small-scale variants such as \n• \nSingle-nucleotide substitutions  \n• \nSmall insertions\n\n• \nSmall deletions  \nIt therefore complements chromosome-level analysis. \n \n74. Mutation Detection \nDifferent types of genetic abnormalities require different analytical methods. \nAbnormality \nPossible detection method \nLarge chromosome-number change Karyotyping \nLarge structural change \nKaryotyping or molecular cytogenetics \nCopy-number change \nChromosomal microarray \nSmall sequence mutation \nDNA sequencing \nSpecific known mutation \nPCR-based testing or sequencing \n \n75. Mutations in Mitochondrial DNA \nMutations can also occur in mitochondrial DNA. \nMitochondrial DNA is generally inherited maternally in humans. \nTherefore, mitochondrial genetic disorders can display distinctive inheritance patterns. \n \n76. Mutation and Genetic Disease \nGenetic diseases can result from \n• \nSingle-gene mutations  \n• \nChromosomal abnormalities  \n• \nMultiple genetic variants  \n• \nInteractions between genes and environment  \nTherefore, not all genetic disorders follow simple Mendelian inheritance. \n \n77. Chromosome Deletion Example\n\nA deletion removes genetic material. \nIf the deleted region contains important genes, their absence can cause developmental \nabnormalities. \nThe effect depends on \n• \nSize of deletion  \n• \nLocation  \n• \nGenes involved  \n• \nWhether the deletion is inherited or newly formed  \n \n78. Duplication and Gene Dosage \nA duplication increases the copy number of genes in a particular region. \nThe resulting increase in gene dosage can disturb normal biological development. \n \n79. Inversion and Gene Function \nAn inversion may not change the amount of DNA. \nHowever, it can \n• \nDisrupt a gene  \n• \nAlter gene regulation  \n• \nAffect chromosome pairing during meiosis  \n• \nIncrease the production of abnormal gametes  \n \n80. Translocation and Gene Function \nA translocation can affect gene function by \n• \nDisrupting a gene  \n• \nMoving a gene near a different regulatory region  \n• \nCreating a fusion gene  \n• \nAltering gene dosage\n\n81. Chromosomal Abnormalities During Gametogenesis \nErrors during meiosis can produce gametes with abnormal chromosome numbers. \nIf such a gamete participates in fertilisation, the resulting embryo may have a chromosomal \nabnormality. \nThis is one major mechanism underlying aneuploidy. \n \n82. Factors Affecting Chromosomal Nondisjunction \nThe probability of nondisjunction can be influenced by biological and cellular factors. \nIn humans, maternal age is associated with increased risk of certain meiotic aneuploidies. \nHowever, chromosomal abnormalities can occur at many ages. \n \n83. Genetic Consequences of Chromosomal Abnormalities \nChromosomal abnormalities can alter \n• \nGene dosage  \n• \nGene expression  \n• \nProtein production  \n• \nDevelopment  \n• \nFertility  \n• \nEmbryonic survival  \nThe severity depends on the specific chromosome and region involved. \n \n84. Mutation and Natural Selection \nMutations occur independently of whether they are useful to an organism. \nNatural selection acts on the phenotypic consequences of heritable genetic variation. \nTherefore, mutations do not arise because an organism needs a particular adaptation.\n\n85. Mutation as a Source of Variation \nThe relationship can be summarised as \nMutation \n↓ \nNew genetic variation \n↓ \nDifferences in phenotype or gene function \n↓ \nNatural selection and other evolutionary processes \n↓ \nChanges in allele frequencies \n \n86. Key Genetic Conditions \nCondition \nMain genetic basis \nDown syndrome \nTrisomy 21 \nTurner syndrome \nMonosomy X \nKlinefelter syndrome \n47,XXY \nChronic myeloid leukaemia Often associated with BCR-ABL1 fusion \nHaemophilia \nCertain forms involve X-linked mutations \n \n87. Key Terms \nChromosomal aberration \nA change in chromosome number or structure. \nAneuploidy \nGain or loss of individual chromosomes. \nMonosomy\n\nLoss of one chromosome. \nTrisomy \nGain of one chromosome. \nNondisjunction \nFailure of chromosomes or chromatids to separate properly. \nDeletion \nLoss of a chromosome segment or DNA sequence. \nDuplication \nExtra copy of a chromosome segment or DNA sequence. \nInversion \nReversal of the orientation of a chromosome segment. \nTranslocation \nMovement of a chromosome segment to another chromosome. \nMutation \nA permanent change in DNA sequence. \nMutagen \nAn agent that increases the frequency of mutations. \nSubstitution \nReplacement of one nucleotide with another. \nInsertion \nAddition of nucleotide(s). \nDeletion \nRemoval of nucleotide(s). \nFrameshift \nChange in the reading frame caused by an insertion or deletion that is not a multiple of three \nnucleotides. \nMissense mutation\n\nA mutation that changes one amino acid to another. \nNonsense mutation \nA mutation that creates a premature stop codon. \nSilent mutation \nA nucleotide change that does not alter the encoded amino acid. \n \n88. Final Summary \nChromosomal aberrations involve changes in chromosome number or structure. Numerical \nabnormalities include aneuploidy, such as monosomy and trisomy, while structural \nabnormalities include deletions, duplications, inversions and translocations. \nNondisjunction during meiosis is an important cause of aneuploidy. Down syndrome commonly \nresults from trisomy 21, Turner syndrome from monosomy X and Klinefelter syndrome is \ncommonly associated with 47,XXY. \nStructural chromosome abnormalities can alter gene dosage, disrupt genes or change gene \nregulation. Some balanced rearrangements may have little effect on the carrier but can create \nreproductive risks. \nGene mutations involve changes in DNA sequence. Substitutions can produce silent, missense or \nnonsense mutations. Insertions and deletions can cause frameshift mutations when they do not \noccur in multiples of three nucleotides. \nMutations may occur in somatic or germline cells. Germline mutations can be inherited, while \nsomatic mutations generally remain within the individual's cell lineage. Mutations can be \nharmful, neutral or beneficial depending on their effects and environmental context. \nMutations are an important source of genetic variation. Heritable mutations provide genetic \nvariation on which natural selection and other evolutionary processes can act. \nModern genetic analysis uses techniques such as karyotyping, chromosomal microarray analysis, \nPCR and DNA sequencing to detect different types of chromosomal and genetic abnormalities.	COMPLETED	108	2026-08-19 18:11:46.296405	2026-08-19 18:12:51.883005	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
989	Lesson 7: DNA replication and gene expression - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 7: DNA replication and gene expression	PDF	uploads/pdf/576f4524898449cc9db0b0c992461b1c.pdf	\N	Lesson 7 — DNA Replication and Gene Expression \nIntroduction \nDNA contains the hereditary information required for the growth, development, maintenance and \nreproduction of living organisms. For this information to be passed to daughter cells and used to \nproduce functional molecules, DNA must be accurately replicated and its genetic information \nmust be expressed. \nDNA replication produces copies of DNA before cell division. Gene expression is the process \nby which information encoded in DNA is used to produce functional RNA molecules and \nproteins. \nThe major processes involved are \nDNA replication → Transcription → RNA processing → Translation \n \n1. DNA as Genetic Material \nDNA, or deoxyribonucleic acid, is the primary genetic material in cellular organisms. \nDNA stores hereditary information in the sequence of its nucleotides. \nThe information is encoded by four nitrogenous bases \n• \nAdenine  \n• \nThymine  \n• \nGuanine  \n• \nCytosine  \n \n2. Structure of DNA \nDNA consists of two polynucleotide strands arranged as a double helix. \nEach nucleotide contains \n• \nDeoxyribose sugar  \n• \nPhosphate group  \n• \nNitrogenous base  \nThe two strands are held together by hydrogen bonds between complementary bases.\n\n3. Complementary Base Pairing \nDNA follows complementary base pairing. \nAdenine pairs with Thymine \nGuanine pairs with Cytosine \nTherefore \nA = T \nG ≡ C \nAdenine and thymine form two hydrogen bonds. \nGuanine and cytosine form three hydrogen bonds. \n \n4. Antiparallel DNA Strands \nThe two DNA strands run in opposite directions. \nOne strand runs \n5′ → 3′ \nwhile the other runs \n3′ → 5′ \nThis arrangement is called antiparallel orientation. \nThe direction is determined by the structure of the sugar-phosphate backbone. \n \n5. DNA Replication \nDNA replication is the process by which a DNA molecule produces two DNA molecules \ncontaining the same genetic information. \nReplication occurs before cell division. \nIt ensures that daughter cells receive genetic information. \n \n6. Semiconservative Replication \nDNA replication is described as semiconservative.\n\nEach newly produced DNA molecule contains \n• \nOne original parental strand  \n• \nOne newly synthesised strand  \nThis mechanism preserves one strand of the original DNA molecule in each daughter DNA \nmolecule. \n \n7. Experimental Evidence \nThe semiconservative model of DNA replication was demonstrated experimentally by Meselson \nand Stahl. \nThey used nitrogen isotopes to distinguish old DNA from newly synthesised DNA. \nTheir results supported the semiconservative model. \n \n8. Replication Origin \nDNA replication begins at specific DNA sequences called origins of replication. \nAt these locations, the DNA double helix is opened and replication machinery is assembled. \n \n9. Replication Fork \nA replication fork is the Y-shaped region where the DNA double helix is separated and new \nDNA strands are synthesised. \nReplication proceeds from the replication fork. \n \n10. Helicase \nDNA helicase is an enzyme that separates the two DNA strands. \nIt breaks the hydrogen bonds between complementary bases. \nThis allows each original DNA strand to serve as a template. \n \n11. Single-Strand Binding Proteins \nOnce DNA strands separate, single-strand binding proteins attach to the exposed strands.\n\nThey help prevent the separated strands from reannealing. \n \n12. Topoisomerase \nDNA unwinding creates torsional stress ahead of the replication fork. \nTopoisomerases relieve this stress by temporarily breaking and rejoining DNA strands. \n \n13. Primase \nDNA polymerases cannot begin synthesising a new DNA strand from nothing. \nPrimase produces a short RNA primer. \nThe primer provides a free \n3′-OH group \nfrom which DNA synthesis can begin. \n \n14. DNA Polymerase \nDNA polymerase adds DNA nucleotides to the growing strand. \nNew DNA is synthesised in the \n5′ → 3′ \ndirection. \nThe incoming nucleotide is selected according to complementary base pairing with the template \nstrand. \n \n15. Direction of DNA Synthesis \nDNA polymerase can add nucleotides only to the free 3′ end of the growing DNA strand. \nTherefore, DNA synthesis always proceeds \n5′ → 3′ \nThis property explains why the two newly synthesised strands are produced differently.\n\n16. Leading Strand \nThe leading strand is synthesised continuously toward the replication fork. \nOnly one RNA primer is generally required for continuous synthesis of the leading strand within \na replication region. \n \n17. Lagging Strand \nThe lagging strand is synthesised discontinuously away from the replication fork. \nIt is produced as a series of short DNA segments. \nThese segments are called Okazaki fragments. \n \n18. Okazaki Fragments \nOkazaki fragments are short DNA segments synthesised on the lagging strand. \nEach fragment begins from an RNA primer. \nThe fragments are later joined to form a continuous DNA strand. \n \n19. DNA Ligase \nDNA ligase joins adjacent DNA fragments. \nIt forms phosphodiester bonds between neighbouring DNA segments. \nLigase is particularly important for joining Okazaki fragments on the lagging strand. \n \n20. Removal of RNA Primers \nRNA primers must be removed and replaced with DNA. \nDifferent organisms use different enzymes and mechanisms for primer removal. \nIn bacteria, DNA polymerase I has an important role in removing RNA primers and replacing \nthem with DNA. \n \n21. Proofreading \nDNA replication must be highly accurate.\n\nMany DNA polymerases possess proofreading activity that detects incorrectly incorporated \nnucleotides and removes them. \nThis reduces the mutation rate during DNA replication. \n \n22. Replication Accuracy \nDNA replication involves several mechanisms that improve accuracy. \nThese include \n• \nComplementary base pairing  \n• \nDNA polymerase proofreading  \n• \nDNA repair mechanisms  \nTogether, these mechanisms maintain genomic stability. \n \n23. Replication in Prokaryotes \nMost bacterial chromosomes are circular. \nReplication generally begins at a single origin and proceeds in two directions around the \nchromosome. \nThis is called bidirectional replication. \n \n24. Replication in Eukaryotes \nEukaryotic chromosomes are much larger and linear. \nThey therefore contain many origins of replication. \nMultiple replication forks operate simultaneously to replicate the chromosome efficiently. \n \n25. Telomeres \nLinear eukaryotic chromosomes have specialised DNA sequences at their ends called telomeres. \nTelomeres help protect chromosome ends from degradation and inappropriate fusion. \n \n26. Telomerase\n\nTelomerase is an enzyme that extends telomeres using an RNA template. \nIt is active in certain cell types, including many germline and stem cells. \nMany cancer cells also exhibit telomerase activity. \n \n27. DNA Replication Summary \nThe basic sequence is \nDNA unwinding \n↓ \nPrimer formation \n↓ \nDNA nucleotide addition \n↓ \nPrimer removal \n↓ \nJoining of DNA fragments \n↓ \nProofreading and repair \nThe result is two DNA molecules, each containing one parental strand and one newly synthesised \nstrand. \n \n28. Gene Expression \nGene expression is the process by which information encoded in a gene is used to produce a \nfunctional product. \nFor protein-coding genes, the major stages are \nTranscription \n↓ \nRNA processing \n↓\n\nTranslation \n \n29. Central Dogma \nThe classical flow of genetic information is represented as \nDNA → RNA → Protein \nDNA stores information. \nRNA carries or processes information. \nProteins perform many structural, catalytic and regulatory functions. \nThe central dogma describes major information-flow relationships but does not mean that every \nRNA molecule is translated into protein. \n \n30. Transcription \nTranscription is the synthesis of RNA using DNA as a template. \nThe enzyme responsible is RNA polymerase. \nUnlike DNA replication, transcription generally involves only one DNA strand for a particular \ngene. \n \n31. Template Strand \nThe DNA strand used by RNA polymerase to synthesise RNA is called the \ntemplate strand \nor \nantisense strand. \nRNA is synthesised complementary to this strand. \n \n32. Coding Strand \nThe other DNA strand is called the \ncoding strand \nor\n\nsense strand. \nIts sequence is similar to the RNA transcript except that DNA contains thymine while RNA \ncontains uracil. \n \n33. RNA Polymerase \nRNA polymerase synthesises RNA by adding ribonucleotides to the growing RNA strand. \nRNA synthesis occurs in the \n5′ → 3′ \ndirection. \nUnlike DNA polymerase, RNA polymerase can initiate RNA synthesis without requiring a pre-\nexisting primer. \n \n34. Stages of Transcription \nTranscription can be divided into \n• \nInitiation  \n• \nElongation  \n• \nTermination  \n \n35. Transcription Initiation \nRNA polymerase binds to a specific DNA region associated with the beginning of a gene. \nThis region is called the \npromoter. \nThe DNA locally unwinds and RNA synthesis begins. \n \n36. Promoter \nA promoter is a DNA sequence involved in initiating transcription. \nIt provides a binding site for transcription machinery. \nPromoters help determine where transcription begins and influence gene expression.\n\n37. Transcription Elongation \nDuring elongation, RNA polymerase moves along the DNA template. \nIt adds complementary RNA nucleotides to the growing RNA molecule. \nRNA is synthesised \n5′ → 3′ \n \n38. RNA Base Pairing \nRNA contains \n• \nAdenine  \n• \nUracil  \n• \nGuanine  \n• \nCytosine  \nDuring transcription \nA in DNA pairs with U in RNA \nT in DNA pairs with A in RNA \nG pairs with C \nC pairs with G \n \n39. Transcription Termination \nTranscription ends when RNA polymerase reaches a termination signal. \nThe newly synthesised RNA molecule is released. \nThe exact termination mechanism differs between prokaryotes and eukaryotes. \n \n40. Types of RNA \nImportant RNA types include \nMessenger RNA\n\nCarries information used for protein synthesis. \nTransfer RNA \nCarries amino acids to the ribosome. \nRibosomal RNA \nForms a major structural and catalytic component of ribosomes. \n \n41. Messenger RNA \nmRNA carries the genetic information from DNA to the ribosome. \nIts nucleotide sequence is interpreted in groups of three called codons. \n \n42. Transfer RNA \ntRNA carries specific amino acids during translation. \nEach tRNA contains an anticodon that can pair with a complementary codon on mRNA. \n \n43. Ribosomal RNA \nrRNA combines with proteins to form ribosomes. \nIt also contributes directly to the catalytic activity involved in peptide bond formation. \n \n44. Eukaryotic RNA Processing \nIn eukaryotic cells, the initial RNA transcript is commonly modified before becoming mature \nmRNA. \nMajor processes include \n• \n5′ capping  \n• \nPoly-A tail addition  \n• \nRNA splicing  \n \n45. 5′ Cap\n\nA modified nucleotide structure is added to the 5′ end of eukaryotic mRNA. \nThe 5′ cap helps with \n• \nmRNA stability  \n• \nNuclear export  \n• \nRibosome recognition  \n \n46. Poly-A Tail \nA sequence of adenine nucleotides is added to the 3′ end of many eukaryotic mRNAs. \nThis is called the \npoly-A tail. \nIt contributes to mRNA stability and assists with processing and translation. \n \n47. Introns \nIntrons are sequences present in an initial eukaryotic RNA transcript that are removed during \nRNA processing. \nThey generally do not remain in the mature mRNA coding sequence. \n \n48. Exons \nExons are sequences retained in the mature RNA after splicing. \nIn protein-coding genes, exons commonly contain sequences that contribute to the final protein-\ncoding message, although exon regions can also contain untranslated sequences. \n \n49. RNA Splicing \nRNA splicing removes introns and joins exons. \nThe resulting mature mRNA can then be transported to the cytoplasm for translation. \n \n50. Spliceosome\n\nThe spliceosome is a molecular complex responsible for much of eukaryotic pre-mRNA \nsplicing. \nIt consists primarily of small nuclear RNAs and associated proteins. \n \n51. Alternative Splicing \nAlternative splicing allows different combinations of exons to be included in mature mRNAs \nproduced from the same gene. \nThis can allow one gene to produce multiple protein forms. \n \n52. Genetic Code \nThe genetic code specifies the relationship between mRNA codons and amino acids. \nThe code is based on groups of three nucleotides. \nEach three-nucleotide sequence is called a \ncodon. \n \n53. Codons \nA codon consists of three RNA nucleotides. \nThere are \n64 possible codons \nThese specify amino acids or termination signals. \n \n54. Start Codon \nThe usual start codon is \nAUG \nIt codes for methionine and usually signals the beginning of translation. \n \n55. Stop Codons \nThree codons function as stop signals\n\nUAA \nUAG \nUGA \nThey do not encode amino acids. \nInstead, they signal termination of translation. \n \n56. Degeneracy of the Genetic Code \nThe genetic code is degenerate, meaning that most amino acids are specified by more than one \ncodon. \nFor example, several different codons can encode the same amino acid. \nThis property helps explain why some nucleotide substitutions are silent. \n \n57. Universality of the Genetic Code \nThe genetic code is highly conserved across organisms. \nMost organisms use essentially the same codon assignments, although exceptions exist, \nparticularly in some organelles and organisms. \n \n58. Translation \nTranslation is the process by which the nucleotide sequence of mRNA is used to assemble an \namino acid sequence. \nTranslation occurs on \nribosomes. \n \n59. Ribosomes \nRibosomes consist of \n• \nRibosomal RNA  \n• \nRibosomal proteins  \nThey contain sites where mRNA and tRNAs interact during protein synthesis.\n\n60. Ribosomal Sites \nRibosomes contain three major tRNA-binding sites \nA site \nAminoacyl site \nP site \nPeptidyl site \nE site \nExit site \n \n61. Translation Initiation \nTranslation begins when the ribosome assembles around the mRNA near the start codon. \nAn initiator tRNA carrying methionine pairs with the start codon. \nThe ribosomal subunits then form the functional translation complex. \n \n62. Translation Elongation \nDuring elongation \n1. A charged tRNA enters the A site.  \n2. Its anticodon pairs with the mRNA codon.  \n3. A peptide bond forms.  \n4. The ribosome moves along the mRNA.  \n5. The empty tRNA exits.  \n6. The process repeats.  \n \n63. Peptide Bond \nA peptide bond links amino acids together.\n\nDuring translation, ribosomal catalytic activity promotes peptide bond formation between amino \nacids. \nThe growing chain therefore becomes a \npolypeptide. \n \n64. Translation Direction \nThe ribosome reads mRNA in the \n5′ → 3′ \ndirection. \nThe polypeptide is synthesised from its \nN-terminus → C-terminus \n \n65. Translation Termination \nWhen a stop codon enters the ribosome's decoding site, no corresponding tRNA binds. \nInstead, protein release factors participate in terminating translation. \nThe completed polypeptide is released. \n \n66. Polysomes \nMultiple ribosomes can simultaneously translate the same mRNA molecule. \nSuch a structure is called a \npolysome \nor \npolyribosome. \nThis allows many copies of a protein to be produced from one mRNA molecule. \n \n67. Protein Folding \nA newly synthesised polypeptide must fold into an appropriate three-dimensional structure to \nbecome functional.\n\nProtein folding can occur spontaneously or with assistance from molecular chaperones. \n \n68. Post-Translational Modification \nProteins can undergo modifications after translation. \nExamples include \n• \nPhosphorylation  \n• \nGlycosylation  \n• \nAcetylation  \n• \nProteolytic cleavage  \nThese modifications can influence protein activity, localisation and stability. \n \n69. Gene Expression Regulation \nNot every gene is expressed in every cell. \nCells regulate gene expression according to their \n• \nCell type  \n• \nDevelopmental stage  \n• \nEnvironmental conditions  \n• \nPhysiological requirements  \nThis allows cells with the same genome to perform different functions. \n \n70. Levels of Gene Expression \nGene expression can be regulated at multiple stages. \nThese include \n• \nChromatin structure  \n• \nTranscription  \n• \nRNA processing  \n• \nmRNA transport\n\n• \nmRNA stability  \n• \nTranslation  \n• \nProtein modification  \n• \nProtein degradation  \n \n71. Gene Expression in Prokaryotes \nProkaryotic cells do not have a membrane-bound nucleus. \nTherefore, transcription and translation can occur in close association within the cytoplasm. \nIn many bacteria, translation can begin while mRNA is still being transcribed. \n \n72. Gene Expression in Eukaryotes \nEukaryotic cells separate transcription and translation. \nTranscription \nOccurs primarily in the nucleus. \nTranslation \nOccurs primarily in the cytoplasm, on free or membrane-associated ribosomes. \n \n73. DNA Replication Versus Transcription \nDNA replication \nTranscription \nCopies DNA \nProduces RNA \nUsually involves the entire genome Usually involves selected genes \nUses DNA polymerase \nUses RNA polymerase \nRequires a primer \nDoes not require a conventional primer \nProduces DNA \nProduces RNA \nOccurs before cell division \nOccurs according to cellular requirements\n\n74. Transcription Versus Translation \nTranscription \nTranslation \nDNA → RNA \nmRNA → protein \nUses RNA polymerase \nUses ribosome \nOccurs in nucleus in eukaryotes Occurs in cytoplasm \nProduces RNA \nProduces polypeptide \nUses RNA nucleotides \nUses amino acids \n \n75. DNA Replication and the Cell Cycle \nDNA replication occurs during the S phase of interphase. \nThe DNA content of the cell is doubled before chromosomes are separated during cell division. \n \n76. DNA Replication and Mutation \nErrors during DNA replication can introduce changes into the DNA sequence. \nIf such errors escape proofreading and repair, they can become permanent mutations after \nsubsequent replication. \n \n77. DNA Polymerase and Proofreading \nDNA polymerase not only synthesises DNA but, in many organisms and polymerases, also \ncontributes to proofreading. \nIncorrectly paired nucleotides can be removed before replication continues. \n \n78. Template Concept \nBoth replication and transcription use a DNA strand as a template. \nHowever \nReplication \nCopies DNA information into DNA.\n\nTranscription \nUses DNA information to produce RNA. \n \n79. Genetic Information Flow \nThe overall process can be represented as \nDNA \n↓ \nTranscription \n↓ \nRNA \n↓ \nTranslation \n↓ \nProtein \nDNA replication occurs separately to copy the genetic material before cell division. \n \n80. Gene Expression and Phenotype \nGene expression contributes to phenotype through the production and regulation of functional \nmolecules. \nA simplified relationship is \nDNA sequence \n↓ \nGene expression \n↓ \nRNA and proteins \n↓ \nCellular structure and function\n\n↓ \nPhenotype \nEnvironmental factors can also influence phenotype and gene expression. \n \n81. Mutations and Gene Expression \nA mutation can affect gene expression by changing \n• \nProtein-coding sequence  \n• \nPromoter sequence  \n• \nEnhancer sequence  \n• \nRNA processing signals  \n• \nmRNA stability  \n• \nProtein structure  \nTherefore, mutations can affect phenotype through several molecular mechanisms. \n \n82. Importance of DNA Replication \nDNA replication is essential because it \n• \nPreserves genetic information  \n• \nAllows cell division  \n• \nSupports growth  \n• \nSupports tissue repair  \n• \nEnables reproduction  \n• \nMaintains genetic continuity  \n \n83. Importance of Gene Expression \nGene expression allows cells to \n• \nProduce enzymes  \n• \nProduce structural proteins\n\n• \nRespond to signals  \n• \nMaintain metabolism  \n• \nDifferentiate into specialised cell types  \n• \nAdapt to environmental conditions  \n \n84. Key Terms \nDNA replication \nProduction of a new DNA molecule using an existing DNA molecule as a template. \nSemiconservative replication \nReplication in which each daughter DNA molecule contains one parental strand and one newly \nsynthesised strand. \nHelicase \nEnzyme that separates DNA strands. \nPrimase \nEnzyme that synthesises RNA primers. \nDNA polymerase \nEnzyme that synthesises DNA. \nOkazaki fragments \nShort DNA fragments produced on the lagging strand. \nDNA ligase \nEnzyme that joins DNA fragments. \nTranscription \nSynthesis of RNA using DNA as a template. \nRNA polymerase \nEnzyme responsible for RNA synthesis. \nPromoter \nDNA region involved in initiating transcription.\n\nmRNA \nRNA that carries protein-coding information. \ntRNA \nRNA that carries amino acids during translation. \nrRNA \nRNA that forms a major component of ribosomes. \nIntron \nRNA sequence removed during splicing. \nExon \nSequence retained in mature RNA after splicing. \nCodon \nThree-nucleotide sequence on mRNA specifying an amino acid or stop signal. \nAnticodon \nThree-nucleotide sequence on tRNA that pairs with an mRNA codon. \nTranslation \nProduction of a polypeptide using information in mRNA. \nMutation \nA permanent alteration in DNA sequence. \n \n85. Final Summary \nDNA stores hereditary information in its nucleotide sequence. Before cell division, DNA \nundergoes semiconservative replication, producing two DNA molecules, each containing one \nparental strand and one newly synthesised strand. \nReplication begins at origins and involves enzymes including helicase, primase, DNA \npolymerase and DNA ligase. DNA polymerase synthesises DNA in the 5′ to 3′ direction. The \nleading strand is synthesised continuously, whereas the lagging strand is synthesised as Okazaki \nfragments. \nGene expression allows DNA information to be converted into functional products. In protein-\ncoding genes, the process involves transcription, RNA processing and translation.\n\nDuring transcription, RNA polymerase uses a DNA template to produce RNA. In eukaryotes, \npre-mRNA undergoes processing including 5′ capping, poly-A tail addition and splicing. \nDuring translation, ribosomes read mRNA codons and use tRNAs to assemble amino acids into a \npolypeptide. The genetic code contains 64 codons, including one common start codon, AUG, and \nthree stop codons. \nGene expression is carefully regulated, allowing different cells to express different sets of genes \ndespite generally containing the same genome. \nTogether, DNA replication and gene expression ensure that genetic information can be accurately \nmaintained, transmitted and used to control cellular structure and function.	COMPLETED	109	2026-08-19 18:11:48.592742	2026-08-19 18:12:51.883007	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
990	Lesson 8: Regulation of gene expression and biotechnology - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 8: Regulation of gene expression and biotechnology	PDF	uploads/pdf/5341e06dcfd94e898d98d58551d68020.pdf	\N	Lesson 8 — Regulation of Gene Expression and Biotechnology \nIntroduction \nCells contain large amounts of genetic information, but not every gene is active at the same time. \nCells regulate gene expression so that the appropriate genes are expressed in the appropriate cell, \nat the appropriate time and at the appropriate level. \nGene regulation is essential for cell differentiation, development, metabolism and responses to \nenvironmental changes. \nModern biotechnology uses our understanding of DNA, genes and gene expression to manipulate \nand analyse genetic material. Important techniques include recombinant DNA technology, \npolymerase chain reaction and gel electrophoresis. \n \n1. Regulation of Gene Expression \nGene regulation is the control of when, where and how strongly a gene is expressed. \nRegulation allows cells to conserve resources and perform specialised functions. \nFor example, a pancreatic cell and a nerve cell generally contain the same genome but express \ndifferent sets of genes. \n \n2. Importance of Gene Regulation \nGene regulation is important for \n• \nCell differentiation  \n• \nDevelopment  \n• \nMetabolism  \n• \nGrowth  \n• \nReproduction  \n• \nResponse to environmental changes  \n• \nMaintenance of cellular function  \n \n3. Levels of Gene Regulation \nGene expression can be regulated at several stages.\n\nThese include \n• \nChromatin modification  \n• \nTranscription  \n• \nRNA processing  \n• \nRNA transport  \n• \nmRNA degradation  \n• \nTranslation  \n• \nProtein modification  \n• \nProtein degradation  \n \n4. Prokaryotic Gene Regulation \nBacteria must respond rapidly to changes in their environment. \nTheir gene expression is therefore strongly regulated according to the availability of nutrients \nand other environmental conditions. \nA major mechanism of bacterial gene regulation involves operons. \n \n5. Operon \nAn operon is a group of genes under coordinated regulation and controlled by regulatory DNA \nsequences. \nOperons are particularly characteristic of prokaryotes. \nThey allow several genes involved in the same biological pathway to be regulated together. \n \n6. Components of an Operon \nA typical operon may contain \n• \nRegulatory gene  \n• \nPromoter  \n• \nOperator  \n• \nStructural genes\n\nThe exact organisation differs among operons. \n \n7. Regulatory Gene \nA regulatory gene produces a regulatory molecule, often a protein that influences transcription. \nThe regulatory gene may be located adjacent to the operon or elsewhere in the genome. \n \n8. Promoter \nThe promoter is the DNA region where RNA polymerase binds to begin transcription. \nIts sequence influences the efficiency and regulation of transcription. \n \n9. Operator \nThe operator is a regulatory DNA sequence that can bind regulatory proteins. \nBinding of a repressor to the operator can prevent or reduce transcription of the structural genes. \n \n10. Structural Genes \nStructural genes encode proteins or functional RNAs required for particular cellular activities. \nIn a metabolic operon, the structural genes may encode several enzymes involved in the same \nbiochemical pathway. \n \n11. The Lac Operon \nThe lac operon of Escherichia coli is a classic example of bacterial gene regulation. \nIt controls genes involved in the utilisation of lactose. \nThe major structural genes are \nlacZ \nlacY \nlacA\n\n12. lacZ \nThe lacZ gene encodes β-galactosidase. \nThis enzyme breaks lactose into glucose and galactose. \nIt also produces the lactose-derived molecule allolactose, which functions as the natural inducer \nof the lac operon. \n \n13. lacY \nThe lacY gene encodes lactose permease. \nPermease facilitates the transport of lactose into the bacterial cell. \n \n14. lacA \nThe lacA gene encodes thiogalactoside transacetylase. \nIts exact physiological role in lactose utilisation is less central than those of lacZ and lacY. \n \n15. Lac Repressor \nThe lac operon is regulated by the lac repressor protein. \nWhen lactose is unavailable, the repressor binds the operator. \nThis reduces transcription of the structural genes. \n \n16. Lac Operon When Lactose Is Absent \nWhen lactose is absent \nLac repressor active \n↓ \nRepressor binds operator \n↓ \nRNA polymerase is hindered \n↓\n\nStructural gene transcription is strongly reduced \nThis prevents unnecessary production of lactose-utilising enzymes. \n \n17. Lac Operon When Lactose Is Present \nWhen lactose is present, some lactose is converted into allolactose. \nAllolactose binds the lac repressor and reduces its ability to bind the operator. \nThe structural genes can therefore be transcribed. \n \n18. Inducer \nA molecule that causes increased expression of an inducible operon by reducing repressor \nactivity is called an inducer. \nIn the lac system, the natural inducer is allolactose. \n \n19. Role of Glucose \nThe lac operon is also regulated according to the availability of glucose. \nWhen glucose is low, bacterial cells increase intracellular cyclic AMP. \ncAMP binds to CAP, also called the catabolite activator protein. \nThe cAMP-CAP complex enhances transcription of the lac operon when lactose is available. \n \n20. Positive Regulation of the Lac Operon \nWhen \nGlucose low + lactose present \ncAMP levels increase. \nThe cAMP-CAP complex binds near the promoter and helps RNA polymerase initiate \ntranscription efficiently. \nThis results in strong expression of the lac genes. \n \n21. Glucose Repression\n\nWhen glucose is abundant \ncAMP levels are low \nTherefore, the cAMP-CAP complex is less active. \nEven if lactose is present, lac operon transcription is relatively weak. \nThis allows bacteria to preferentially use glucose when it is readily available. \n \n22. Four Basic Lac Operon Conditions \nGlucose Lactose Lac operon expression \nHigh \nAbsent Very low \nHigh \nPresent Low \nLow \nAbsent Very low \nLow \nPresent High \nThe strongest expression occurs when glucose is low and lactose is present. \n \n23. The trp Operon \nThe trp operon of E. coli controls genes involved in the synthesis of the amino acid tryptophan. \nUnlike the lac operon, the trp operon is generally a repressible operon. \n \n24. Repressible Operon \nA repressible operon is normally active but can be turned off when the end product of the \npathway is abundant. \nThis prevents unnecessary synthesis of a substance that is already available. \n \n25. trp Operon When Tryptophan Is Low \nWhen tryptophan is scarce \nRepressor remains inactive \n↓\n\nOperator remains available \n↓ \nStructural genes are transcribed \n↓ \nEnzymes for tryptophan synthesis are produced \n \n26. trp Operon When Tryptophan Is High \nWhen tryptophan is abundant \nTryptophan binds the repressor \n↓ \nRepressor becomes active \n↓ \nRepressor binds the operator \n↓ \nTranscription is inhibited \nThis prevents unnecessary production of tryptophan-synthesis enzymes. \n \n27. Corepressor \nA molecule that binds to and activates a repressor is called a corepressor. \nIn the trp operon, tryptophan functions as a corepressor. \n \n28. Lac Operon Versus trp Operon \nFeature \nlac operon \ntrp operon \nMain function \nLactose utilisation \nTryptophan synthesis \nGeneral type \nInducible \nRepressible \nDefault state \nUsually off \nUsually on\n\nFeature \nlac operon \ntrp operon \nRegulatory \nmolecule \nAllolactose \nTryptophan \nPurpose \nProduce enzymes when substrate is \navailable \nStop enzyme production when product \nis abundant \n \n29. Eukaryotic Gene Regulation \nEukaryotic gene regulation is more complex than bacterial operon regulation. \nIt can involve \n• \nChromatin structure  \n• \nTranscription factors  \n• \nEnhancers  \n• \nSilencers  \n• \nDNA methylation  \n• \nHistone modification  \n• \nRNA processing  \n• \nRegulatory RNAs  \n \n30. Transcription Factors \nTranscription factors are proteins that regulate gene transcription. \nThey can bind specific DNA sequences and influence RNA polymerase activity. \n \n31. Enhancers \nAn enhancer is a regulatory DNA sequence that can increase transcription when bound by \nappropriate regulatory proteins. \nEnhancers may be located far from the promoter. \n \n32. Silencers\n\nA silencer is a regulatory DNA sequence that can reduce gene expression when bound by \nappropriate regulatory proteins. \n \n33. Epigenetic Regulation \nEpigenetic regulation involves changes in gene activity that do not require changes to the \nunderlying DNA sequence. \nImportant mechanisms include \n• \nDNA methylation  \n• \nHistone modification  \n• \nChromatin remodelling  \n \n34. DNA Methylation \nDNA methylation commonly involves the addition of methyl groups to cytosine bases in DNA. \nIn many contexts, increased methylation near gene regulatory regions is associated with reduced \ntranscription. \n \n35. Histone Modification \nDNA is associated with proteins called histones. \nChemical modifications of histones can alter chromatin structure and influence gene \naccessibility. \n \n36. Chromatin Structure \nEuchromatin \nGenerally less condensed and more accessible for transcription. \nHeterochromatin \nGenerally more condensed and less accessible for transcription. \nChanges in chromatin structure can therefore influence gene expression.\n\n37. Recombinant DNA Technology \nRecombinant DNA technology involves combining DNA molecules from different sources. \nIt allows researchers to isolate, modify, copy and transfer specific DNA sequences. \n \n38. Applications of Recombinant DNA Technology \nApplications include \n• \nProduction of recombinant proteins  \n• \nGenetic engineering  \n• \nMedical biotechnology  \n• \nAgricultural biotechnology  \n• \nResearch  \n• \nProduction of vaccines  \n• \nProduction of therapeutic molecules  \n \n39. Restriction Enzymes \nRestriction enzymes are enzymes that recognise specific DNA sequences and cleave DNA at or \nnear those sequences. \nThey are important tools in recombinant DNA technology. \n \n40. Restriction Sites \nRestriction enzymes recognise specific nucleotide sequences called restriction sites. \nMany restriction sites are palindromic sequences. \nFor example, a restriction enzyme may recognise \n5′-GAATTC-3′ \nand its complementary strand \n3′-CTTAAG-5′\n\n41. Sticky Ends \nSome restriction enzymes make staggered cuts that produce single-stranded DNA overhangs. \nThese are called \nsticky ends. \nComplementary sticky ends can base-pair with one another. \n \n42. Blunt Ends \nSome restriction enzymes cut both DNA strands at the same position. \nThis produces \nblunt ends \nwithout single-stranded overhangs. \n \n43. DNA Ligase in Recombinant DNA \nAfter DNA fragments with compatible ends are brought together, DNA ligase can join them. \nLigase forms phosphodiester bonds between adjacent nucleotides. \n \n44. Cloning Vector \nA cloning vector is a DNA molecule used to carry a foreign DNA fragment into a host cell. \nCommon vectors include \n• \nPlasmids  \n• \nBacteriophages  \n• \nArtificial chromosomes  \n \n45. Plasmids \nA plasmid is a small, usually circular DNA molecule found naturally in many bacteria. \nPlasmids can replicate independently of the bacterial chromosome. \nThey are widely used as vectors in genetic engineering.\n\n46. Important Features of a Plasmid Vector \nA useful plasmid vector may contain \n• \nOrigin of replication  \n• \nSelectable marker  \n• \nMultiple cloning site  \n• \nPromoter or regulatory elements  \n \n47. Origin of Replication \nThe origin of replication allows a plasmid to be replicated inside its host cell. \nThis helps maintain copies of the recombinant DNA. \n \n48. Selectable Marker \nA selectable marker allows researchers to identify or select cells that have received the vector. \nAntibiotic resistance genes have historically been used as selectable markers, although modern \nbiotechnology increasingly uses alternative selection systems depending on the application. \n \n49. Gene Cloning \nGene cloning is the production of many copies of a particular DNA sequence. \nA common approach involves inserting the DNA into a vector and introducing the recombinant \nvector into a host cell. \n \n50. Basic Recombinant DNA Process \nA simplified process is \nTarget DNA identified \n↓ \nDNA and vector cut \n↓\n\nCompatible DNA fragments combined \n↓ \nDNA ligase joins fragments \n↓ \nRecombinant DNA produced \n↓ \nRecombinant vector introduced into host \n↓ \nHost cells selected \n↓ \nDNA amplified or expressed \n \n51. Transformation \nTransformation is the uptake of foreign DNA by a cell. \nIn bacterial biotechnology, transformation can introduce recombinant plasmids into bacterial \ncells. \n \n52. Recombinant Protein Production \nA gene encoding a desired protein can be inserted into an expression vector. \nThe host cell then produces the protein. \nThe protein can subsequently be harvested and purified. \n \n53. Recombinant Human Insulin \nRecombinant DNA technology is used to produce human insulin. \nThe human insulin gene or appropriate DNA sequences are introduced into suitable production \nsystems. \nMicroorganisms can then produce insulin or insulin-related molecules that are purified for \nmedical use.\n\n54. Polymerase Chain Reaction \nPolymerase chain reaction, commonly called PCR, is a technique used to amplify a specific \nDNA sequence. \nPCR can produce millions of copies of a target DNA region from a small starting sample. \n \n55. Requirements of PCR \nA PCR reaction requires \n• \nTemplate DNA  \n• \nPrimers  \n• \nDNA polymerase  \n• \nDeoxyribonucleotides  \n• \nAppropriate buffer and ions  \nA thermostable DNA polymerase is used. \n \n56. Primers \nPCR uses two short DNA sequences called primers. \nThey bind to complementary sequences on opposite strands and define the region that will be \namplified. \n \n57. Thermostable DNA Polymerase \nPCR requires repeated heating and cooling. \nTherefore, a heat-stable DNA polymerase is used. \nTaq polymerase, originally isolated from Thermus aquaticus, is a classic example. \n \n58. PCR Cycle \nEach PCR cycle consists of three major stages \nDenaturation\n\nAnnealing \nExtension \nThese steps are repeated many times. \n \n59. Denaturation \nDuring denaturation, the DNA double helix is heated. \nHydrogen bonds between complementary strands are disrupted. \nThe two DNA strands separate. \n \n60. Annealing \nDuring annealing, the temperature is lowered. \nPCR primers bind to their complementary sequences on the template DNA. \n \n61. Extension \nDuring extension, the DNA polymerase adds nucleotides to the primers. \nNew DNA strands are synthesised in the \n5′ → 3′ \ndirection. \n \n62. PCR Amplification \nIdeally, the amount of target DNA approximately doubles during each cycle. \nAfter n cycles, the theoretical amplification is approximately \n2ⁿ \ntimes the initial target amount. \nIn real reactions, amplification eventually slows because reagents become limiting and products \naccumulate.\n\n63. PCR Applications \nPCR is used in \n• \nMedical diagnostics  \n• \nForensic analysis  \n• \nGenetic testing  \n• \nResearch  \n• \nPathogen detection  \n• \nDNA cloning  \n• \nPaternity testing  \n• \nMutation analysis  \n \n64. Reverse Transcription PCR \nRT-PCR can refer to reverse transcription PCR. \nRNA is first converted into complementary DNA using reverse transcriptase. \nThe resulting cDNA can then be amplified by PCR. \nThis is particularly useful for studying RNA expression. \n \n65. Quantitative PCR \nqPCR monitors DNA amplification during the PCR reaction. \nIt can be used to estimate the amount of target nucleic acid in a sample. \n \n66. Gel Electrophoresis \nGel electrophoresis is a technique used to separate DNA fragments according to their size and \nother properties. \nDNA molecules are negatively charged because of their phosphate backbone. \n \n67. Principle of DNA Electrophoresis\n\nWhen an electric field is applied \nDNA moves toward the positive electrode \nbecause DNA carries a net negative charge. \nSmaller DNA fragments generally move through the gel more easily and therefore travel farther \nthan larger fragments. \n \n68. Agarose Gel \nDNA fragments are commonly separated using an agarose gel. \nThe gel contains a network of pores through which DNA fragments migrate. \n \n69. DNA Ladder \nA DNA ladder contains fragments of known sizes. \nIt is loaded alongside samples to estimate the sizes of unknown DNA fragments. \n \n70. Visualisation of DNA \nDNA fragments can be visualised using fluorescent dyes or DNA-binding stains. \nA fluorescent DNA-binding dye can allow DNA bands to be observed under suitable \nillumination. \n \n71. Gel Electrophoresis Procedure \nA simplified process is \nPrepare gel \n↓ \nLoad DNA samples and ladder \n↓ \nApply electric field \n↓ \nDNA fragments migrate\n\n↓ \nSeparate according to size \n↓ \nVisualise DNA bands \n \n72. DNA Band Pattern \nAfter electrophoresis, DNA fragments appear as bands. \nEach band represents DNA fragments of approximately similar size. \nThe pattern can be compared between samples. \n \n73. PCR and Gel Electrophoresis \nPCR and gel electrophoresis are often used together. \nPCR \nAmplifies a target DNA region \nGel electrophoresis \nSeparates and visualises the amplified DNA fragments \n \n74. DNA Profiling \nDNA profiling uses genetic variation to distinguish individuals. \nModern forensic DNA profiling commonly examines highly variable regions such as short \ntandem repeats, or STRs. \n \n75. Biotechnology and Medicine \nMolecular biotechnology supports \n• \nGenetic diagnosis  \n• \nProduction of therapeutic proteins  \n• \nVaccine development\n\n• \nDetection of pathogens  \n• \nCancer genetics  \n• \nPersonalised medicine  \n \n76. Biotechnology in Agriculture \nGenetic technologies can be used for \n• \nCrop improvement  \n• \nDisease resistance  \n• \nPest resistance  \n• \nImproved nutritional characteristics  \n• \nMolecular breeding  \n• \nGenetic modification  \n \n77. Biotechnology in Research \nBiotechnology enables scientists to \n• \nClone genes  \n• \nSequence DNA  \n• \nAnalyse gene expression  \n• \nProduce recombinant proteins  \n• \nIntroduce specific genetic changes  \n• \nStudy gene function  \n \n78. Genetic Engineering \nGenetic engineering involves deliberately modifying an organism's genetic material using \nbiotechnology. \nThe modification may involve \n• \nInserting DNA\n\n• \nRemoving DNA  \n• \nAltering DNA  \n• \nControlling gene expression  \n \n79. Genetically Modified Organisms \nA genetically modified organism, or GMO, has had its genetic material altered using genetic \nengineering or other modern biotechnology techniques. \nGMOs are used in research, agriculture and biotechnology. \n \n80. Recombinant DNA Versus PCR \nRecombinant DNA technology \nPCR \nCombines or modifies DNA molecules \nAmplifies a specific DNA region \nOften uses vectors \nUses primers and DNA polymerase \nCan introduce genes into host cells \nUsually performed in vitro \nUseful for gene cloning and protein production Useful for rapid DNA amplification \n \n81. PCR Versus DNA Replication \nPCR is based on principles similar to DNA replication but is performed artificially in a \nlaboratory. \nDNA replication \nPCR \nOccurs inside cells \nPerformed in vitro \nUses cellular replication machinery \nUses selected laboratory reagents \nReplicates cellular genomes \nAmplifies selected DNA regions \nUses helicase to separate DNA \nHeat separates DNA strands \nPrimers are produced by cellular machinery Synthetic primers are supplied\n\n82. Importance of Restriction Enzymes \nRestriction enzymes allow researchers to cut DNA at specific locations. \nThis makes it possible to \n• \nIsolate genes  \n• \nCreate recombinant DNA  \n• \nConstruct vectors  \n• \nAnalyse DNA fragments  \n \n83. Importance of DNA Ligase \nDNA ligase allows DNA fragments to be joined. \nIt is therefore essential for constructing recombinant DNA molecules. \n \n84. Importance of Plasmids \nPlasmids provide convenient vectors for \n• \nGene cloning  \n• \nGene expression  \n• \nRecombinant protein production  \n• \nGenetic analysis  \n \n85. Importance of PCR \nPCR is valuable because it can amplify DNA rapidly from very small samples. \nThis makes it useful when only a limited amount of DNA is available. \n \n86. Importance of Gel Electrophoresis \nGel electrophoresis allows researchers to \n• \nSeparate DNA fragments  \n• \nEstimate DNA fragment size\n\n• \nCompare DNA samples  \n• \nAnalyse PCR products  \n• \nAssist in DNA profiling  \n \n87. Key Terms \nGene regulation \nControl of gene expression. \nOperon \nA coordinated genetic regulatory system commonly found in prokaryotes. \nPromoter \nDNA sequence where transcription machinery assembles. \nOperator \nRegulatory DNA sequence that can bind regulatory proteins. \nInducer \nMolecule that promotes expression of an inducible operon. \nCorepressor \nMolecule that activates a repressor in a repressible system. \nlac operon \nBacterial operon involved in lactose utilisation. \ntrp operon \nBacterial operon involved in tryptophan synthesis. \nRecombinant DNA \nDNA molecule constructed from DNA sequences originating from different sources. \nRestriction enzyme \nEnzyme that cuts DNA at specific recognition sequences. \nDNA ligase \nEnzyme that joins DNA fragments.\n\nVector \nDNA molecule used to carry DNA into a host cell. \nPlasmid \nSmall, usually circular DNA molecule commonly used as a cloning vector. \nPCR \nTechnique used to amplify a specific DNA sequence. \nPrimer \nShort nucleic acid sequence that provides a starting point for DNA synthesis. \nGel electrophoresis \nTechnique used to separate DNA fragments according to size. \nTransformation \nIntroduction of foreign DNA into a cell. \nGenetic engineering \nDeliberate modification of genetic material using biotechnology. \n \n88. Final Summary \nGene regulation allows cells to control when and how strongly genes are expressed. In bacteria, \noperons provide an efficient mechanism for coordinating genes involved in related metabolic \npathways. \nThe lac operon regulates lactose utilisation and is strongly expressed when lactose is available \nand glucose is scarce. The trp operon regulates tryptophan synthesis and is repressed when \ntryptophan is abundant. \nModern biotechnology uses molecular mechanisms of DNA replication and gene expression to \nmanipulate and analyse genetic material. Restriction enzymes cut DNA at specific sequences, \nDNA ligase joins DNA fragments and plasmids can act as vectors for carrying recombinant \nDNA. \nPCR amplifies specific DNA sequences through repeated cycles of denaturation, primer \nannealing and extension. Gel electrophoresis separates DNA fragments according to size and \ncan be used to analyse PCR products.\n\nTogether, recombinant DNA technology, PCR and electrophoresis form important foundations of \nmodern molecular biology, with applications in medicine, agriculture, research, forensic science \nand biotechnology.	COMPLETED	110	2026-08-19 18:11:49.81619	2026-08-19 18:12:51.883009	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
991	Lesson 1: Introduction to microbiology and cultural methods - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 1: Introduction to microbiology and cultural methods	PDF	uploads/pdf/daea1dc11e964747ba0205ad5a66a24a.pdf	\N	Lesson 1 — Introduction to Microbiology and Cultural Methods \nIntroduction \nMicrobiology is the branch of biology concerned with organisms and biological agents that are \ngenerally too small to be observed clearly with the unaided eye. \nMicroorganisms include bacteria, archaea, microscopic fungi, protozoa, microscopic algae and \nsome other microscopic biological entities. Viruses are also studied extensively in microbiology, \nalthough they are acellular and are not considered living cells. \nMicroorganisms occur in almost every environment and have major roles in ecosystems, \nagriculture, medicine, food production and industry. \n \n1. Microorganisms \nMicroorganisms are organisms or biological entities that are generally microscopic. \nMajor groups studied in microbiology include \n• \nBacteria  \n• \nArchaea  \n• \nFungi  \n• \nProtozoa  \n• \nMicroscopic algae  \n• \nViruses  \n \n2. Importance of Microorganisms \nMicroorganisms have both beneficial and harmful effects. \nThey are involved in \n• \nDecomposition  \n• \nNutrient cycling  \n• \nNitrogen fixation  \n• \nFood production  \n• \nFermentation\n\n• \nMedicine  \n• \nIndustrial production  \n• \nSewage treatment  \n• \nAgriculture  \n• \nHuman and animal diseases  \n \n3. Historical Development of Microbiology \nThe development of microbiology was closely associated with improvements in microscopy. \nImportant scientists contributed to the discovery of microorganisms and the development of \nmicrobiological techniques. \n \n4. Robert Hooke \nRobert Hooke used a compound microscope to examine biological materials. \nIn 1665, he described the small compartments present in cork and introduced the term cell. \nHis observations contributed to the development of cell biology. \n \n5. Antonie van Leeuwenhoek \nAntonie van Leeuwenhoek developed powerful single-lens microscopes and was among the \nfirst scientists to observe living microorganisms. \nHe described microscopic organisms such as bacteria and protozoa. \nHis observations provided important early evidence of the microscopic world. \n \n6. Louis Pasteur \nLouis Pasteur made major contributions to microbiology. \nHis work demonstrated the role of microorganisms in fermentation and helped disprove \nspontaneous generation. \nHe also developed methods for reducing microbial contamination in food and beverages.\n\n7. Pasteurisation \nPasteurisation is a controlled heat treatment used to reduce harmful microorganisms and \nspoilage organisms in food or beverages. \nIt does not normally sterilise the product completely. \nPasteurisation is widely used for products such as milk. \n \n8. Spontaneous Generation \nThe theory of spontaneous generation proposed that living organisms could arise spontaneously \nfrom non-living matter. \nExperiments by scientists including Pasteur provided strong evidence against this theory for \nmicroorganisms. \n \n9. Germ Theory of Disease \nThe germ theory of disease proposes that specific microorganisms can cause specific infectious \ndiseases. \nThe development of germ theory transformed medical microbiology and infection control. \n \n10. Robert Koch \nRobert Koch made major contributions to medical microbiology. \nHe established experimental methods for linking particular microorganisms with particular \ndiseases. \nHe also developed important laboratory techniques for growing and identifying microorganisms. \n \n11. Koch's Postulates \nKoch's postulates are a classical framework used to establish a causal relationship between a \nmicroorganism and a disease. \nThe traditional postulates involve \n1. The microorganism is found in diseased individuals and not normally in healthy \nindividuals.\n\n2. The microorganism can be isolated and grown in pure culture.  \n3. The cultured microorganism causes the disease when introduced into a suitable \nsusceptible host.  \n4. The same microorganism can be re-isolated from the experimentally infected host.  \n \n12. Limitations of Koch's Postulates \nKoch's postulates are historically important but do not apply universally. \nLimitations include \n• \nSome pathogens cannot be grown easily in artificial culture.  \n• \nSome diseases have multiple contributing causes.  \n• \nCertain pathogens infect only humans or particular hosts.  \n• \nEthical considerations prevent deliberate infection experiments in humans.  \n• \nSome microorganisms do not cause disease in every individual.  \n• \nViruses require living host cells for replication.  \nModern molecular methods have expanded the approaches used to establish microbial causation. \n \n13. Microbial Nutrition \nMicroorganisms require nutrients for \n• \nEnergy production  \n• \nCell growth  \n• \nReproduction  \n• \nBiosynthesis  \n• \nMaintenance  \nNutritional requirements vary greatly among microorganisms. \n \n14. Macronutrients \nMicroorganisms require relatively large quantities of elements such as\n\n• \nCarbon  \n• \nHydrogen  \n• \nOxygen  \n• \nNitrogen  \n• \nPhosphorus  \n• \nSulfur  \nThese elements are incorporated into cellular molecules. \n \n15. Micronutrients \nMicroorganisms also require smaller quantities of certain elements. \nExamples include \n• \nIron  \n• \nZinc  \n• \nCopper  \n• \nManganese  \n• \nMolybdenum  \nThese elements often function as enzyme cofactors or components of cellular structures. \n \n16. Carbon Sources \nCarbon is required to build organic molecules. \nMicroorganisms can obtain carbon from different sources. \nAutotrophs \nObtain carbon mainly from carbon dioxide. \nHeterotrophs \nObtain carbon from organic compounds. \n \n17. Energy Sources\n\nMicroorganisms obtain energy from different sources. \nPhototrophs \nObtain energy from light. \nChemotrophs \nObtain energy from chemical compounds. \n \n18. Electron Sources \nMicroorganisms can also be classified according to the source of electrons used in metabolism. \nLithotrophs \nUse inorganic substances as electron donors. \nOrganotrophs \nUse organic compounds as electron donors. \n \n19. Microbial Growth \nMicrobial growth generally refers to an increase in the number of cells in a population. \nIn bacteria, growth commonly occurs through binary fission. \n \n20. Binary Fission \nDuring binary fission \n1. DNA is replicated.  \n2. The cell elongates.  \n3. The replicated chromosomes move apart.  \n4. A division septum forms.  \n5. The cell separates into two daughter cells.  \nUnder favourable conditions, bacterial populations can increase rapidly. \n \n21. Culture\n\nA microbial culture is a population of microorganisms growing under controlled laboratory \nconditions. \nCultures can be grown in liquid media or on solid media. \n \n22. Culture Medium \nA culture medium provides the nutrients required for microbial growth. \nA medium may contain \n• \nCarbon sources  \n• \nNitrogen sources  \n• \nMineral salts  \n• \nWater  \n• \nGrowth factors  \n• \nOther required nutrients  \n \n23. Liquid Culture Medium \nA liquid culture medium is called a broth. \nMicroorganisms grow throughout the liquid. \nBroth cultures are useful for producing large quantities of microbial cells or metabolic products. \n \n24. Solid Culture Medium \nSolid media are commonly prepared using agar as a solidifying agent. \nMicroorganisms grow on the surface or within the solid medium. \n \n25. Agar \nAgar is a polysaccharide obtained primarily from certain red algae. \nIt is widely used as a solidifying agent because most microorganisms do not degrade it and it \nremains solid at typical incubation temperatures.\n\n26. Nutrient Agar \nNutrient agar is a general-purpose medium commonly used for cultivating many non-fastidious \nbacteria. \nIts exact formulation can vary depending on the laboratory and intended use. \n \n27. Selective Medium \nA selective medium contains conditions or substances that favour the growth of certain \nmicroorganisms while inhibiting others. \nThis allows particular microbial groups to be isolated from mixed populations. \n \n28. Differential Medium \nA differential medium allows microorganisms to be distinguished based on visible or \nmeasurable differences in their metabolic activities. \nChanges may appear as \n• \nColour changes  \n• \nPrecipitation  \n• \nGas production  \n• \nDifferences in colony appearance  \n \n29. Enrichment Medium \nAn enrichment medium is designed to increase the relative abundance of a desired \nmicroorganism in a mixed sample. \nIt provides conditions that favour the target organism. \n \n30. Defined Medium \nA defined medium contains precisely known chemical components and concentrations. \nIt is useful when the nutritional requirements of microorganisms need to be studied precisely.\n\n31. Complex Medium \nA complex medium contains ingredients whose exact chemical composition may not be \ncompletely known. \nExamples include media containing \n• \nPeptones  \n• \nYeast extract  \n• \nMeat extract  \n \n32. Pure Culture \nA pure culture contains only one microbial species or strain. \nPure cultures are essential for accurately studying microbial characteristics. \n \n33. Mixed Culture \nA mixed culture contains two or more different microorganisms. \nSamples collected from natural environments commonly contain mixed microbial populations. \n \n34. Contamination \nContamination occurs when unwanted microorganisms enter a culture. \nContamination can compromise experimental results and make identification of the intended \nmicroorganism difficult. \n \n35. Aseptic Technique \nAseptic technique refers to procedures used to prevent unwanted microorganisms from \ncontaminating cultures, equipment and samples. \nImportant practices include \n• \nSterilising equipment  \n• \nDisinfecting work surfaces  \n• \nMinimising exposure of cultures\n\n• \nUsing sterile instruments  \n• \nProperly closing culture containers  \n \n36. Isolation of Microorganisms \nIsolation is the process of obtaining a microorganism separately from other microorganisms in a \nmixed population. \nA common method is the streak plate technique. \n \n37. Streak Plate Technique \nThe streak plate technique uses a sterile inoculating loop to progressively dilute microorganisms \nacross the surface of an agar plate. \nThe aim is to separate individual cells sufficiently that they produce separate colonies. \n \n38. Colony \nA colony is a visible mass of microorganisms that develops from one cell or a small group of \ncells on a solid medium. \nA colony may be examined for characteristics such as \n• \nSize  \n• \nShape  \n• \nColour  \n• \nMargin  \n• \nElevation  \n• \nTexture  \n \n39. Streaking Principle \nThe basic principle is progressive dilution. \nMicroorganisms are spread across successive regions of the agar surface. \nThe number of cells deposited decreases as the streaking process continues.\n\nEventually, individual cells become sufficiently separated to form isolated colonies. \n \n40. Inoculation \nInoculation is the introduction of microorganisms into a culture medium. \nThe material used to introduce microorganisms is called an inoculum. \n \n41. Inoculating Loop \nAn inoculating loop is a laboratory instrument used to transfer microorganisms between cultures \nor media. \nIt must be appropriately sterilised before use. \n \n42. Spread Plate Technique \nIn the spread plate technique, a measured volume of a microbial suspension is placed on the \nsurface of solid agar and distributed evenly using a sterile spreader. \nIndividual cells can form separate colonies. \n \n43. Pour Plate Technique \nIn the pour plate technique, a measured microbial sample is mixed with molten agar and \nallowed to solidify. \nColonies may develop within the agar as well as on its surface. \n \n44. Comparison of Isolation Methods \nMethod \nMain principle \nStreak plate Progressive dilution across agar surface \nSpread plate Even distribution of measured sample on agar surface \nPour plate \nSample mixed with molten agar\n\n45. Incubation \nAfter inoculation, cultures are incubated under appropriate conditions. \nImportant factors include \n• \nTemperature  \n• \nOxygen availability  \n• \npH  \n• \nMoisture  \n• \nNutrient availability  \n• \nIncubation time  \n \n46. Temperature and Microbial Growth \nMicroorganisms have different temperature requirements. \nPsychrophiles \nPrefer low temperatures. \nMesophiles \nPrefer moderate temperatures. \nThermophiles \nPrefer high temperatures. \nHyperthermophiles \nGrow optimally at very high temperatures. \n \n47. Oxygen Requirements \nMicroorganisms differ in their relationship with oxygen. \nObligate aerobes \nRequire oxygen. \nObligate anaerobes \nAre harmed by oxygen or cannot use it for growth.\n\nFacultative anaerobes \nCan grow with or without oxygen. \nAerotolerant anaerobes \nDo not use oxygen but can tolerate its presence. \nMicroaerophiles \nRequire oxygen at concentrations lower than atmospheric levels. \n \n48. pH Requirements \nMicroorganisms also differ in their preferred pH. \nAcidophiles \nPrefer acidic environments. \nNeutrophiles \nPrefer conditions around neutral pH. \nAlkaliphiles \nPrefer alkaline environments. \n \n49. Growth Curve \nWhen microorganisms are grown in a closed batch culture, the population commonly passes \nthrough several growth phases. \nThese are \nLag phase \nLog phase \nStationary phase \nDeath phase \n \n50. Lag Phase \nDuring the lag phase, microorganisms adapt to the new environment.\n\nCells synthesise enzymes and other cellular components required for active growth. \nThere may be little increase in cell number. \n \n51. Log Phase \nDuring the log phase, cells divide rapidly. \nThe population increases exponentially. \nMicroorganisms are often metabolically active during this phase. \n \n52. Stationary Phase \nDuring the stationary phase, the rate of cell production approximately balances the rate of cell \ndeath. \nThis can result from \n• \nNutrient depletion  \n• \nAccumulation of waste products  \n• \nChanges in pH  \n• \nLimited space or other environmental factors  \n \n53. Death Phase \nDuring the death phase, viable cell numbers decrease because cell death exceeds cell division. \nThe rate and pattern of decline depend on the organism and environmental conditions. \n \n54. Generation Time \nGeneration time is the time required for a microbial population to double under specified \nconditions. \nDifferent microorganisms have different generation times. \n \n55. Measuring Microbial Growth \nMicrobial growth can be estimated using\n\n• \nDirect microscopic counting  \n• \nViable plate counts  \n• \nTurbidity measurements  \n• \nDry mass measurements  \n• \nElectronic cell counting  \n \n56. Colony-Forming Units \nA viable plate count is commonly expressed as colony-forming units, or CFU. \nOne CFU represents a viable unit capable of producing a colony under the given culture \nconditions. \nA CFU does not necessarily correspond to exactly one original cell because cells can occur in \nclusters. \n \n57. Serial Dilution \nHighly concentrated microbial samples may be diluted systematically before plating. \nThis is called serial dilution. \nIt allows a suitable number of colonies to develop on a plate for counting. \n \n58. Importance of Pure Culture \nPure cultures are important because they allow scientists to \n• \nIdentify microorganisms  \n• \nStudy microbial metabolism  \n• \nDetermine pathogenic properties  \n• \nTest antimicrobial sensitivity  \n• \nStudy microbial genetics  \n• \nProduce microbial products  \n \n59. Identification of Microorganisms\n\nMicroorganisms can be identified using \n• \nMicroscopic characteristics  \n• \nColony morphology  \n• \nStaining reactions  \n• \nBiochemical tests  \n• \nSerological methods  \n• \nMolecular techniques  \n• \nDNA sequencing  \n \n60. Gram Staining \nGram staining is an important differential staining method used primarily to distinguish bacteria \nbased on differences in their cell envelopes. \nIt separates bacteria into \nGram-positive \nand \nGram-negative \ngroups. \n \n61. Gram-Positive Bacteria \nGram-positive bacteria generally possess \n• \nA thick peptidoglycan cell wall  \n• \nTeichoic acids  \n• \nNo outer membrane  \nThey typically retain the primary crystal violet stain during Gram staining and appear purple. \n \n62. Gram-Negative Bacteria \nGram-negative bacteria generally possess\n\n• \nA thinner peptidoglycan layer  \n• \nAn outer membrane  \n• \nLipopolysaccharide  \nThey do not retain the crystal violet-iodine complex after decolourisation and typically appear \npink or red after counterstaining. \n \n63. Importance of Gram Staining \nGram staining provides rapid preliminary information about bacterial identity and can guide \nfurther identification and antimicrobial treatment decisions. \n \n64. Microbiology and Disease \nSome microorganisms are pathogenic. \nPathogen refers to a biological agent capable of causing disease. \nMicrobiology helps scientists understand \n• \nPathogen structure  \n• \nTransmission  \n• \nVirulence  \n• \nDisease mechanisms  \n• \nDiagnosis  \n• \nPrevention  \n• \nTreatment  \n \n65. Beneficial Microorganisms \nMany microorganisms are beneficial. \nExamples include microorganisms involved in \n• \nYogurt production  \n• \nBread production  \n• \nCheese production\n\n• \nFermentation  \n• \nNitrogen fixation  \n• \nDecomposition  \n• \nWastewater treatment  \n \n66. Microorganisms in Agriculture \nMicroorganisms contribute to agriculture through \n• \nNutrient cycling  \n• \nOrganic matter decomposition  \n• \nNitrogen fixation  \n• \nSoil formation  \n• \nPlant growth promotion  \n• \nBiological control  \n \n67. Microorganisms in Industry \nMicroorganisms can produce \n• \nAntibiotics  \n• \nEnzymes  \n• \nOrganic acids  \n• \nAlcohols  \n• \nVitamins  \n• \nBiofuels  \n• \nTherapeutic proteins  \n \n68. Microbial Culture and Industrial Production \nIndustrial microbiology often requires microorganisms to be grown under controlled conditions. \nImportant parameters include\n\n• \nTemperature  \n• \npH  \n• \nOxygen  \n• \nNutrient concentration  \n• \nAgitation  \n• \nSterility  \n \n69. Aseptic Culture and Pure Culture \nA successful pure culture requires both \nIsolation \nto separate the desired microorganism \nand \nAseptic technique \nto prevent contamination. \n \n70. Key Terms \nMicrobiology \nThe study of microorganisms and microscopic biological agents. \nMicroorganism \nA microscopic organism. \nCulture \nA population of microorganisms grown under controlled conditions. \nCulture medium \nMaterial containing nutrients required for microbial growth. \nPure culture \nA culture containing one microbial species or strain. \nMixed culture\n\nA culture containing multiple microbial types. \nInoculation \nIntroduction of microorganisms into a culture medium. \nAseptic technique \nMethods used to prevent microbial contamination. \nColony \nVisible growth of microorganisms on a solid medium. \nStreak plate \nIsolation technique based on progressive dilution across an agar surface. \nSelective medium \nMedium that favours some microorganisms while inhibiting others. \nDifferential medium \nMedium that allows microorganisms to be distinguished based on observable metabolic \ndifferences. \nKoch's postulates \nClassical criteria used to establish a relationship between a microorganism and a disease. \nBinary fission \nA common form of bacterial cell division. \nGeneration time \nTime required for a microbial population to double. \nCFU \nColony-forming unit used to estimate viable microorganisms. \nGram staining \nDifferential staining method that distinguishes major bacterial cell-envelope types. \n \n71. Final Summary\n\nMicrobiology is the study of microorganisms and microscopic biological agents. Microorganisms \nare found throughout the environment and play essential roles in ecosystems, agriculture, \nmedicine, food production and industry. \nThe development of microbiology was strongly influenced by microscopy and the work of \nscientists such as Robert Hooke, Antonie van Leeuwenhoek, Louis Pasteur and Robert Koch. \nKoch's postulates provided a classical framework for establishing relationships between \nmicroorganisms and infectious diseases, although modern molecular biology has expanded and \nmodified these approaches. \nMicroorganisms require appropriate nutrients and environmental conditions for growth. In the \nlaboratory, they can be cultivated using liquid or solid culture media. Selective, differential and \nenrichment media allow specific microorganisms to be isolated or distinguished. \nPure cultures can be obtained using methods such as streak plating, spread plating and pour \nplating. Aseptic techniques are essential to prevent contamination. \nMicrobial populations commonly pass through lag, log, stationary and death phases in closed \ncultures. Microbial growth can be estimated using methods such as viable plate counts and \nturbidity measurements. \nUnderstanding microbial nutrition, cultivation and identification provides the foundation for \nstudying microbial diseases, industrial microbiology, food microbiology, biotechnology and \nenvironmental microbiology.	COMPLETED	111	2026-08-19 18:11:51.20937	2026-08-19 18:12:51.88301	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
992	Lesson 2: Control of microorganisms and host defense - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 2: Control of microorganisms and host defense	PDF	uploads/pdf/aa68ba25aca645f5b004d71fdd9c60e8.pdf	\N	Lesson 2 — Control of Microorganisms and Host Defense \nIntroduction \nMicroorganisms are essential components of ecosystems and are widely used in food production, \nagriculture, medicine and industry. However, some microorganisms cause infectious diseases, \ncontaminate food and water, and damage materials. \nThe control of microorganisms involves methods used to inhibit their growth, destroy them or \nprevent their transmission. These methods include physical and chemical approaches. \nThe human body also possesses several defence mechanisms that prevent microorganisms from \ncausing disease. These include physical barriers, innate immune responses and adaptive immune \nresponses. \n \n1. Microbial Control \nMicrobial control is the process of reducing, inhibiting or eliminating microorganisms. \nThe method selected depends on \n• \nType of microorganism  \n• \nNumber of microorganisms  \n• \nEnvironmental conditions  \n• \nIntended application  \n• \nSensitivity of the material  \n• \nRequired level of microbial reduction  \n \n2. Sterilisation \nSterilisation is the complete elimination of all forms of microbial life from an object or \nenvironment, including bacterial endospores. \nSterilisation is more extensive than disinfection. \n \n3. Disinfection \nDisinfection is the process of eliminating or reducing many or most pathogenic microorganisms \non inanimate objects.\n\nDisinfection does not necessarily destroy bacterial endospores. \n \n4. Antisepsis \nAntisepsis is the application of antimicrobial chemicals to living tissues to reduce or inhibit \nmicroorganisms. \nAntiseptics are formulated to be suitable for use on tissues. \n \n5. Degerming \nDegerming is the mechanical removal of microorganisms from a limited area. \nHandwashing is an important example. \n \n6. Sanitisation \nSanitisation reduces microbial populations to levels considered acceptable according to public-\nhealth standards. \nSanitisation is commonly used in food-service and food-processing environments. \n \n7. Physical Methods of Microbial Control \nImportant physical methods include \n• \nHeat  \n• \nFiltration  \n• \nRadiation  \n• \nLow temperature  \n• \nDesiccation  \n• \nOsmotic pressure  \n \n8. Moist Heat \nMoist heat destroys microorganisms mainly by denaturing proteins. \nIt is generally more effective than dry heat at the same temperature.\n\nExamples include \n• \nBoiling  \n• \nSteam under pressure  \n• \nPasteurisation  \n \n9. Boiling \nBoiling water can destroy many vegetative bacterial cells, viruses and fungi. \nHowever, boiling does not reliably destroy all bacterial endospores. \nTherefore, boiling should not automatically be considered sterilisation. \n \n10. Autoclaving \nAn autoclave uses saturated steam under pressure to achieve temperatures above the normal \nboiling point of water. \nThe combination of heat, moisture and pressure allows effective sterilisation. \nAutoclaving is commonly used for \n• \nLaboratory equipment  \n• \nCulture media  \n• \nCertain medical materials  \n• \nLaboratory waste  \n \n11. Dry Heat \nDry heat destroys microorganisms primarily through oxidation and other damaging effects on \ncellular components. \nExamples include \n• \nHot-air ovens  \n• \nFlaming  \n• \nIncineration\n\n12. Hot-Air Oven \nA hot-air oven uses dry heated air to sterilise suitable materials. \nIt is particularly useful for materials that may be damaged by moisture. \n \n13. Flaming \nFlaming can be used to sterilise small metal laboratory instruments such as inoculating loops. \nThe instrument is exposed to a sufficiently high temperature to destroy microorganisms. \n \n14. Incineration \nIncineration uses very high temperatures to destroy microorganisms and organic material. \nIt is commonly used for appropriate biological waste. \n \n15. Pasteurisation \nPasteurisation uses controlled heating to reduce populations of pathogenic and spoilage \nmicroorganisms. \nIt is commonly applied to milk and other food products. \nPasteurisation does not normally produce a sterile product. \n \n16. Low Temperature \nLow temperatures generally slow microbial metabolism and reproduction. \nRefrigeration can therefore delay microbial growth in food. \nFreezing can preserve food for longer periods but does not necessarily kill all microorganisms. \n \n17. Desiccation \nDesiccation is the removal of water. \nMany microorganisms require available water for active growth, so drying can inhibit microbial \nmultiplication. \nSome microorganisms and structures are more resistant to drying than others.\n\n18. Filtration \nFiltration removes microorganisms from liquids or gases by passing them through a material \ncontaining pores small enough to retain the microorganisms. \nFiltration is particularly useful for heat-sensitive liquids. \n \n19. Membrane Filtration \nMembrane filters contain microscopic pores that retain microorganisms while allowing liquid to \npass through. \nMembrane filtration is widely used in laboratory and industrial applications. \n \n20. Air Filtration \nAir can be filtered using highly efficient filtration systems. \nHEPA filters can remove very small airborne particles and are widely used in laboratories, \nhospitals and controlled environments. \n \n21. Radiation \nRadiation can damage microbial DNA and other cellular components. \nTwo major categories are \n• \nNon-ionising radiation  \n• \nIonising radiation  \n \n22. Ultraviolet Radiation \nUltraviolet radiation can damage DNA by causing abnormal bonds between adjacent \npyrimidine bases. \nUV radiation is useful for reducing microorganisms on exposed surfaces and in some air and \nwater treatment applications. \nIts effectiveness is limited because it does not penetrate deeply into materials.\n\n23. Ionising Radiation \nIonising radiation has sufficient energy to produce ions and reactive molecules. \nIt can cause extensive DNA damage. \nExamples include \n• \nGamma radiation  \n• \nX-rays  \n• \nElectron beams  \nIonising radiation is used for certain medical products, foods and laboratory materials. \n \n24. Chemical Methods of Microbial Control \nChemical agents used for microbial control include \n• \nAlcohols  \n• \nHalogens  \n• \nPhenolic compounds  \n• \nAldehydes  \n• \nOxidising agents  \n• \nQuaternary ammonium compounds  \n• \nHeavy metals  \n \n25. Alcohols \nCommon antimicrobial alcohols include \n• \nEthanol  \n• \nIsopropanol  \nAlcohols disrupt membranes and denature proteins. \nThey are widely used as skin antiseptics and surface disinfectants. \nAlcohols are not reliable sterilising agents because they do not effectively destroy bacterial \nendospores.\n\n26. Halogens \nImportant halogens include \n• \nChlorine  \n• \nIodine  \nThey interfere with essential cellular components. \n \n27. Chlorine \nChlorine compounds are widely used to disinfect \n• \nDrinking water  \n• \nSwimming pools  \n• \nSurfaces  \n• \nCertain industrial systems  \nChlorine can react with cellular molecules and damage microorganisms. \n \n28. Iodine \nIodine is used as an antiseptic and disinfectant. \nIodophors are iodine-containing preparations designed to improve handling and reduce irritation. \n \n29. Phenolic Compounds \nPhenolic compounds damage cell membranes and proteins. \nSome phenolic derivatives are used as disinfectants. \n \n30. Aldehydes \nAldehydes such as formaldehyde and glutaraldehyde can react with proteins and nucleic acids. \nCertain aldehydes are used for high-level disinfection or sterilisation of suitable materials.\n\n31. Oxidising Agents \nOxidising agents damage cellular components through oxidation. \nExamples include \n• \nHydrogen peroxide  \n• \nPeracetic acid  \n• \nOzone  \n \n32. Hydrogen Peroxide \nHydrogen peroxide produces reactive oxygen species that damage microbial cellular \ncomponents. \nIt is used in disinfection and, under suitable conditions, high-level decontamination and \nsterilisation. \n \n33. Quaternary Ammonium Compounds \nQuaternary ammonium compounds are surface-active chemicals that disrupt cell membranes. \nThey are commonly used for environmental and surface disinfection. \n \n34. Factors Affecting Chemical Control \nThe effectiveness of a chemical antimicrobial agent depends on \n• \nConcentration  \n• \nContact time  \n• \nTemperature  \n• \npH  \n• \nPresence of organic matter  \n• \nType of microorganism  \n• \nNumber of microorganisms  \n \n35. Microbial Resistance\n\nDifferent microorganisms have different levels of resistance to physical and chemical control \nmethods. \nGenerally, resistance can be influenced by \n• \nCell structure  \n• \nEndospore formation  \n• \nProtective biofilms  \n• \nMetabolic state  \n• \nGenetic factors  \n \n36. Bacterial Endospores \nEndospores are highly resistant structures produced by certain bacteria. \nThey can survive \n• \nHeat  \n• \nDesiccation  \n• \nRadiation  \n• \nChemicals  \nFor this reason, sterilisation procedures must be sufficiently rigorous to destroy them. \n \n37. Biofilms \nA biofilm is a structured microbial community attached to a surface and embedded in a self-\nproduced extracellular matrix. \nBiofilms can be more resistant to antimicrobial treatments than free-living cells. \nThey are important in \n• \nMedical infections  \n• \nWater systems  \n• \nIndustrial systems  \n• \nFood-processing environments\n\n38. Antibiotics \nAntibiotics are antimicrobial substances that inhibit or kill susceptible bacteria. \nThey are primarily used to treat bacterial infections. \nAntibiotics do not generally work against viruses. \n \n39. Antimicrobial Agents \nThe broader term antimicrobial agent includes substances that act against different types of \nmicroorganisms. \nThese may target \n• \nBacteria  \n• \nFungi  \n• \nProtozoa  \n• \nCertain viruses  \n \n40. Selective Toxicity \nAn ideal antimicrobial drug should damage the pathogen while causing minimal harm to the \nhost. \nThis property is called selective toxicity. \n \n41. Antibiotic Targets \nAntibiotics can act on several bacterial structures or processes. \nThese include \n• \nCell wall synthesis  \n• \nProtein synthesis  \n• \nDNA replication  \n• \nRNA synthesis  \n• \nMetabolic pathways  \n• \nCell membrane integrity\n\n42. Inhibition of Cell Wall Synthesis \nSome antibiotics interfere with bacterial cell wall synthesis. \nBecause human cells do not possess peptidoglycan cell walls, this can provide relatively high \nselective toxicity. \n \n43. Inhibition of Protein Synthesis \nSome antibiotics bind bacterial ribosomes and interfere with translation. \nBacterial ribosomes differ structurally from eukaryotic cytoplasmic ribosomes, providing \nopportunities for selective targeting. \n \n44. Inhibition of Nucleic Acid Synthesis \nSome antimicrobial agents interfere with bacterial DNA replication or RNA synthesis. \nThis prevents normal growth and reproduction. \n \n45. Antibiotic Resistance \nAntibiotic resistance occurs when bacteria acquire or develop characteristics that allow them to \nsurvive exposure to antibiotics that would normally inhibit or kill them. \nResistance is a major global public-health concern. \n \n46. Origin of Antibiotic Resistance \nResistance can arise through \n• \nMutation  \n• \nAcquisition of resistance genes  \n• \nHorizontal gene transfer  \n• \nNatural selection  \nAntibiotics do not normally create resistance specifically because bacteria need it. Instead, \nexisting resistant variants can be selected when susceptible bacteria are inhibited.\n\n47. Horizontal Gene Transfer \nBacteria can acquire genetic information from other bacteria through several mechanisms. \nThese include \nTransformation \nUptake of free DNA. \nTransduction \nTransfer of DNA through bacteriophages. \nConjugation \nDirect transfer of DNA between bacterial cells through cell-to-cell contact. \n \n48. Transformation \nDuring transformation, bacteria take up DNA fragments from their surroundings. \nIf the DNA contains useful genes and becomes established in the recipient cell, the new \ncharacteristics may be inherited. \n \n49. Transduction \nTransduction involves the transfer of bacterial DNA from one bacterial cell to another through a \nbacteriophage. \nA bacteriophage is a virus that infects bacteria. \n \n50. Conjugation \nConjugation involves direct transfer of genetic material between bacterial cells. \nPlasmids are commonly transferred through this mechanism. \n \n51. Mechanisms of Antibiotic Resistance \nBacteria can resist antibiotics through mechanisms such as \n• \nEnzymatic destruction or modification of the drug\n\n• \nAlteration of the drug target  \n• \nReduced drug uptake  \n• \nIncreased drug efflux  \n• \nAlternative metabolic pathways  \n \n52. Antibiotic-Modifying Enzymes \nSome resistant bacteria produce enzymes that chemically modify or destroy antibiotics. \nFor example, certain bacteria produce β-lactamases, which can inactivate many β-lactam \nantibiotics. \n \n53. Alteration of Drug Targets \nBacteria can acquire mutations or genes that modify the structure of an antibiotic's target. \nThe antibiotic may then bind less effectively. \n \n54. Reduced Permeability \nChanges in bacterial membranes or porins can reduce the entry of certain antimicrobial \ncompounds. \nThis decreases the concentration of the drug inside the bacterial cell. \n \n55. Efflux Pumps \nSome bacteria possess membrane proteins called efflux pumps. \nThese actively transport antimicrobial substances out of the cell. \n \n56. Misuse of Antibiotics \nFactors that contribute to antibiotic resistance include \n• \nUnnecessary antibiotic use  \n• \nIncorrect antibiotic selection  \n• \nInappropriate dosing\n\n• \nPoor adherence to prescribed treatment  \n• \nExcessive agricultural use  \n• \nPoor infection control  \n \n57. Antimicrobial Stewardship \nAntimicrobial stewardship refers to responsible use of antimicrobial drugs. \nThe objectives include \n• \nUsing antimicrobials only when appropriate  \n• \nSelecting suitable drugs  \n• \nUsing appropriate doses  \n• \nLimiting unnecessary exposure  \n• \nReducing development of resistance  \n \n58. Viruses \nViruses are acellular infectious agents consisting of genetic material enclosed within a protein \ncoat and, in some cases, a lipid envelope. \nThey cannot reproduce independently. \nThey require living host cells for replication. \n \n59. Viral Genetic Material \nA virus may contain \n• \nDNA  \nor \n• \nRNA  \nas its genetic material. \nViral genomes may be single-stranded or double-stranded depending on the virus.\n\n60. Viral Capsid \nThe capsid is the protein coat surrounding the viral genome. \nIt protects the viral nucleic acid and contributes to interactions with host cells. \n \n61. Viral Envelope \nSome viruses possess a lipid membrane called an envelope. \nThe envelope is generally derived from host-cell membrane material and contains viral proteins. \n \n62. Viral Replication \nViruses do not reproduce through binary fission or ordinary cell division. \nInstead, they replicate by entering host cells and using host cellular machinery together with viral \ncomponents. \n \n63. General Viral Replication Cycle \nA simplified viral replication cycle involves \nAttachment \n↓ \nEntry \n↓ \nUncoating \n↓ \nGenome replication and gene expression \n↓ \nAssembly \n↓ \nRelease\n\n64. Viral Attachment \nDuring attachment, viral surface structures bind to specific receptors on the host cell. \nThis receptor specificity influences which cells a virus can infect. \n \n65. Viral Entry \nAfter attachment, the virus or its genome enters the host cell. \nEntry mechanisms vary among viruses. \n \n66. Uncoating \nUncoating involves removal or disruption of the viral capsid so that the viral genome becomes \navailable inside the host cell. \n \n67. Viral Genome Replication \nThe viral genome is replicated using viral enzymes, host enzymes or a combination of both, \ndepending on the virus. \n \n68. Viral Gene Expression \nViral genes are expressed to produce proteins required for \n• \nGenome replication  \n• \nHost-cell manipulation  \n• \nCapsid formation  \n• \nViral assembly  \n• \nRelease  \n \n69. Viral Assembly \nNew viral genomes and structural proteins are assembled into new virus particles. \nThe resulting particles are called \nvirions.\n\n70. Viral Release \nViruses can leave host cells through different mechanisms. \nThese include \n• \nCell lysis  \n• \nBudding  \n• \nExocytosis  \nThe mechanism depends on the virus. \n \n71. Lytic Cycle \nIn a lytic cycle, viral replication results in the production of many new virions and eventual \ndestruction of the host cell. \n \n72. Lysogenic Cycle \nSome bacteriophages can enter a lysogenic cycle. \nThe viral DNA becomes integrated into the bacterial chromosome as a prophage. \nThe viral genetic material can then be replicated along with the host DNA. \n \n73. Lytic and Lysogenic Cycles \nLytic cycle \nLysogenic cycle \nRapid viral replication \nViral genome persists with host \nNew virions produced \nViral DNA may integrate into host chromosome \nHost cell commonly lyses \nHost cell may survive initially \nViral genes strongly expressed Viral genes can remain largely repressed \n \n74. Innate Host Defence \nThe human body has defence mechanisms that act before a pathogen establishes an infection.\n\nThese are part of the innate immune system. \nInnate defences are generally rapid and non-specific. \n \n75. Physical Barriers \nImportant physical barriers include \n• \nSkin  \n• \nMucous membranes  \n• \nCilia  \n• \nFlushing actions of body fluids  \nThese prevent microorganisms from entering or remaining in tissues. \n \n76. Skin \nThe skin forms an important physical barrier. \nIts protective properties include \n• \nContinuous epithelial surface  \n• \nLow surface pH  \n• \nRelatively low water availability  \n• \nNormal microbial communities  \n• \nAntimicrobial substances  \n \n77. Mucous Membranes \nMucous membranes line many body passages. \nMucus can trap microorganisms and particles. \n \n78. Mucociliary Clearance \nIn the respiratory tract, mucus traps particles and microorganisms. \nCilia move the mucus toward the throat, where it can be swallowed or expelled.\n\nThis process is called mucociliary clearance. \n \n79. Chemical Defences \nThe body contains antimicrobial substances such as \n• \nLysozyme  \n• \nStomach acid  \n• \nAntimicrobial peptides  \n• \nComplement proteins  \n \n80. Lysozyme \nLysozyme is an enzyme found in substances such as tears, saliva and mucus. \nIt damages bacterial cell walls by breaking bonds in peptidoglycan. \n \n81. Stomach Acid \nThe highly acidic environment of the stomach destroys many microorganisms that enter with \nfood and water. \nHowever, some microorganisms have adaptations that allow them to survive acidic conditions. \n \n82. Normal Microbiota \nThe body contains communities of microorganisms known as the normal microbiota. \nThese microorganisms can help prevent pathogen colonisation by \n• \nCompeting for nutrients  \n• \nOccupying attachment sites  \n• \nProducing inhibitory substances  \n• \nInfluencing host immune responses  \n \n83. Inflammation\n\nInflammation is an important innate immune response to tissue damage or infection. \nIt can result in \n• \nIncreased blood flow  \n• \nIncreased vascular permeability  \n• \nRecruitment of immune cells  \n• \nLocal heat  \n• \nRedness  \n• \nSwelling  \n• \nPain  \n \n84. Phagocytosis \nPhagocytosis is the process by which specialised cells engulf and digest microorganisms or \ncellular debris. \nImportant phagocytic cells include \n• \nNeutrophils  \n• \nMacrophages  \n• \nDendritic cells  \n \n85. Neutrophils \nNeutrophils are abundant white blood cells that rapidly migrate to sites of infection or tissue \ninjury. \nThey are important phagocytes in acute inflammatory responses. \n \n86. Macrophages \nMacrophages are large phagocytic cells found in tissues. \nThey \n• \nEngulf microorganisms  \n• \nRemove dead cells\n\n• \nProduce signalling molecules  \n• \nParticipate in antigen presentation  \n \n87. Natural Killer Cells \nNatural killer cells are lymphocytes involved in innate immune defence. \nThey can destroy certain infected or abnormal cells without requiring the same antigen-specific \nrecognition mechanism used by conventional T cells. \n \n88. Complement System \nThe complement system consists of proteins that circulate in blood and tissue fluids. \nComplement can \n• \nPromote inflammation  \n• \nEnhance phagocytosis  \n• \nDamage microbial membranes  \n• \nHelp eliminate pathogens  \n \n89. Interferons \nInterferons are signalling proteins involved particularly in antiviral defence. \nVirus-infected cells can release interferons that stimulate antiviral responses in nearby cells. \n \n90. Adaptive Immunity \nAdaptive immunity is a specific immune response that develops against particular antigens. \nIts major components include \n• \nB lymphocytes  \n• \nT lymphocytes  \n• \nAntibodies  \n• \nMemory cells\n\n91. Antigens \nAn antigen is a substance or molecular structure that can be specifically recognised by \ncomponents of the adaptive immune system. \nAntigens can originate from pathogens or other foreign substances. \n \n92. B Lymphocytes \nB lymphocytes, or B cells, are responsible for humoral immune responses. \nAfter appropriate activation, B cells can differentiate into plasma cells that produce antibodies. \n \n93. Antibodies \nAntibodies are immunoglobulin proteins produced by plasma cells. \nThey bind specific antigens. \nTheir functions include \n• \nNeutralisation  \n• \nOpsonisation  \n• \nAgglutination  \n• \nActivation of complement  \n \n94. T Lymphocytes \nT lymphocytes participate in cell-mediated immunity. \nImportant types include \n• \nHelper T cells  \n• \nCytotoxic T cells  \n• \nRegulatory T cells  \n• \nMemory T cells\n\n95. Helper T Cells \nHelper T cells coordinate immune responses by releasing signalling molecules called cytokines. \nThey can assist \n• \nB-cell activation  \n• \nCytotoxic T-cell responses  \n• \nMacrophage activation  \n \n96. Cytotoxic T Cells \nCytotoxic T cells can recognise and destroy infected or abnormal host cells. \nThey are particularly important in defence against intracellular pathogens such as viruses. \n \n97. Memory Cells \nSome activated B and T cells develop into memory cells. \nThese cells can persist for long periods. \nIf the same antigen is encountered again, the immune response can be faster and stronger. \n \n98. Primary Immune Response \nThe primary immune response occurs when the immune system encounters an antigen for the \nfirst time. \nIt generally takes time to develop because antigen-specific lymphocytes must become activated \nand expand. \n \n99. Secondary Immune Response \nThe secondary immune response occurs when the same antigen is encountered again. \nMemory cells allow a faster and often stronger response. \nThis principle is important in vaccination. \n \n100. Vaccination\n\nA vaccine exposes the immune system to an antigen or antigenic material in a form designed to \ngenerate protective immunity without causing the intended disease. \nVaccination can generate immune memory. \n \n101. Active Immunity \nActive immunity develops when the individual's own immune system produces a response \nfollowing exposure to an antigen. \nIt can result from \n• \nNatural infection  \n• \nVaccination  \n \n102. Passive Immunity \nPassive immunity occurs when antibodies are received from another source. \nExamples include \n• \nMaternal antibodies transferred to an infant  \n• \nAdministration of antibody-containing preparations  \nPassive immunity provides immediate protection but generally does not produce long-lasting \nimmune memory. \n \n103. Innate Versus Adaptive Immunity \nInnate immunity \nAdaptive immunity \nRapid \nDevelops more slowly during first exposure \nBroad recognition \nHighly antigen-specific \nPresent from birth \nDevelops through exposure \nNo classical antigen-specific memory Strong immunological memory \nIncludes barriers and phagocytes \nIncludes B and T lymphocytes\n\n104. Host Defence Against Infection \nProtection against microorganisms involves multiple levels \nPhysical barriers \n↓ \nChemical barriers \n↓ \nInnate immune responses \n↓ \nAdaptive immune responses \nThese mechanisms work together rather than functioning independently. \n \n105. Key Terms \nSterilisation \nComplete elimination of microbial life, including bacterial endospores. \nDisinfection \nReduction or elimination of many pathogenic microorganisms on non-living surfaces. \nAntisepsis \nUse of antimicrobial substances on living tissue. \nPasteurisation \nControlled heating used to reduce harmful and spoilage microorganisms. \nAutoclave \nDevice that uses pressurised steam for sterilisation. \nAntibiotic \nAntimicrobial substance used primarily against bacteria. \nAntibiotic resistance \nAbility of bacteria to survive exposure to antibiotics that would normally inhibit or kill them. \nBiofilm\n\nMicrobial community attached to a surface and embedded in an extracellular matrix. \nVirus \nAcellular infectious agent that requires host cells for replication. \nVirion \nComplete infectious virus particle. \nPathogen \nBiological agent capable of causing disease. \nInnate immunity \nRapid, broadly acting host defence. \nAdaptive immunity \nSpecific immune response involving lymphocytes and immunological memory. \nAntigen \nMolecule or structure specifically recognised by the immune system. \nAntibody \nAntigen-specific immunoglobulin produced by plasma cells. \nPhagocytosis \nEngulfment and digestion of particles or microorganisms by specialised cells. \nVaccine \nPreparation designed to stimulate protective adaptive immunity. \n \n106. Final Summary \nMicrobial control involves methods used to prevent the growth, transmission or survival of \nmicroorganisms. Sterilisation eliminates all microbial life, while disinfection reduces \nmicroorganisms on non-living surfaces and antisepsis applies antimicrobial control to living \ntissues. \nPhysical control methods include heat, filtration and radiation. Chemical methods include \nalcohols, halogens, aldehydes, oxidising agents and quaternary ammonium compounds. \nAntibiotics target bacterial structures or processes such as cell wall synthesis, protein synthesis \nand nucleic acid metabolism. The misuse and overuse of antibiotics can promote the selection\n\nand spread of antibiotic-resistant bacteria. Resistance can occur through mutation and horizontal \ngene transfer. \nViruses are acellular infectious agents that require host cells for replication. Their replication \ngenerally involves attachment, entry, uncoating, genome replication, gene expression, assembly \nand release. \nThe human body protects itself through multiple levels of host defence. Physical and chemical \nbarriers provide the first line of protection, while innate immune mechanisms such as \ninflammation, phagocytosis, complement and natural killer cells provide rapid responses. \nAdaptive immunity involves B and T lymphocytes, antibodies and memory cells. \nTogether, microbial control and host defence are fundamental to preventing infectious disease, \nmaintaining public health and developing effective medical treatments.	COMPLETED	112	2026-08-19 18:11:55.08325	2026-08-19 18:12:51.883012	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
993	Lesson 3: Microorganisms in food, agriculture, and industry - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 3: Microorganisms in food, agriculture, and industry	PDF	uploads/pdf/513a5ead4fe6441ead44e5498d61e08d.pdf	\N	Lesson 3 — Microorganisms in Food, Agriculture, and Industry \nIntroduction \nMicroorganisms have enormous importance in food production, agriculture, environmental \nmanagement and industrial biotechnology. Although some microorganisms cause food spoilage \nand disease, many others are deliberately used to produce valuable foods, chemicals, enzymes, \nmedicines and other products. \nThe useful application of microorganisms is based on their ability to carry out metabolic \nprocesses efficiently under controlled conditions. \n \n1. Microorganisms in Food Production \nMicroorganisms are involved in the production and preservation of many foods. \nImportant microorganisms used in food production include \n• \nBacteria  \n• \nYeasts  \n• \nMoulds  \nTheir activities can change the chemical composition, flavour, texture and nutritional properties \nof food. \n \n2. Fermentation \nFermentation is a metabolic process in which microorganisms convert organic substances into \nother products, often under anaerobic or oxygen-limited conditions. \nImportant fermentation products include \n• \nEthanol  \n• \nLactic acid  \n• \nAcetic acid  \n• \nCarbon dioxide  \nFermentation is widely used in food and beverage production. \n \n3. Alcoholic Fermentation\n\nAlcoholic fermentation is mainly carried out by yeasts such as Saccharomyces cerevisiae. \nGlucose is converted into ethanol and carbon dioxide. \nThe simplified reaction is \nGlucose → Ethanol + Carbon dioxide + Energy \nThis process is important in \n• \nBread production  \n• \nProduction of fermented beverages  \n• \nIndustrial ethanol production  \n \n4. Yeast in Bread Production \nYeast is used in bread making because it carries out fermentation. \nDuring fermentation \n• \nSugars are metabolised  \n• \nCarbon dioxide is produced  \n• \nCarbon dioxide becomes trapped within the dough  \n• \nThe dough expands  \n• \nEthanol is also produced  \nMost of the ethanol evaporates during baking. \n \n5. Lactic Acid Fermentation \nLactic acid bacteria convert carbohydrates into lactic acid. \nThis process is important in the production of \n• \nYogurt  \n• \nCertain cheeses  \n• \nFermented vegetables  \n• \nOther fermented foods\n\nLactic acid lowers the pH and contributes to the characteristic taste and preservation of \nfermented foods. \n \n6. Yogurt Production \nYogurt production involves lactic acid bacteria. \nCommonly used bacteria include species of \n• \nLactobacillus  \n• \nStreptococcus  \nThese bacteria ferment lactose in milk and produce lactic acid. \nThe decrease in pH causes milk proteins to coagulate, producing the characteristic texture of \nyogurt. \n \n7. Cheese Production \nMicroorganisms contribute to cheese production by \n• \nProducing acids  \n• \nContributing to flavour  \n• \nModifying proteins and fats  \n• \nSupporting maturation  \nDifferent microbial species are responsible for the characteristics of different types of cheese. \n \n8. Vinegar Production \nVinegar production involves microorganisms that oxidise ethanol to acetic acid. \nAcetic acid bacteria such as Acetobacter are important in this process. \nThe simplified process is \nEthanol → Acetic acid \nThis process generally requires oxygen. \n \n9. Food Spoilage\n\nFood spoilage is the deterioration of food that makes it unacceptable or unsuitable for \nconsumption. \nMicroorganisms can cause changes in \n• \nTaste  \n• \nSmell  \n• \nColour  \n• \nTexture  \n• \nNutritional quality  \n \n10. Microorganisms Responsible for Food Spoilage \nFood spoilage can be caused by \n• \nBacteria  \n• \nYeasts  \n• \nMoulds  \nThe type of microorganism that grows depends on factors such as moisture, temperature, pH and \noxygen availability. \n \n11. Factors Affecting Food Spoilage \nImportant factors include \n• \nTemperature  \n• \nWater availability  \n• \npH  \n• \nOxygen  \n• \nNutrient composition  \n• \nStorage conditions  \n• \nMicrobial contamination  \nControlling these factors can slow microbial growth.\n\n12. Water Activity \nMicroorganisms require available water for growth. \nWater activity refers to the availability of water for microbial and chemical processes. \nReducing water activity can inhibit microbial growth. \nMethods include \n• \nDrying  \n• \nSalting  \n• \nSugaring  \n \n13. Food Preservation \nFood preservation involves methods used to prevent or delay food spoilage and extend shelf \nlife. \nMethods include \n• \nRefrigeration  \n• \nFreezing  \n• \nDrying  \n• \nSalting  \n• \nSugaring  \n• \nPasteurisation  \n• \nCanning  \n• \nFermentation  \n• \nChemical preservation  \n \n14. Refrigeration \nRefrigeration reduces microbial growth by lowering temperature. \nIt does not normally eliminate microorganisms. \nSome microorganisms can continue growing slowly at refrigeration temperatures.\n\n15. Freezing \nFreezing greatly reduces the availability of liquid water and slows microbial activity. \nIt can preserve food for extended periods. \nHowever, freezing does not necessarily destroy all microorganisms. \n \n16. Drying \nDrying removes water from food. \nThe reduction in water availability inhibits the growth of many microorganisms. \nExamples include \n• \nDried fruits  \n• \nDried fish  \n• \nDried grains  \n• \nPowdered foods  \n \n17. Salting \nHigh concentrations of salt reduce water availability to microorganisms through osmotic effects. \nSalting can therefore inhibit the growth of many microorganisms. \n \n18. Sugaring \nHigh concentrations of sugar also reduce water availability. \nThis principle is used in products such as \n• \nJams  \n• \nJellies  \n• \nPreserves  \n \n19. Canning\n\nCanning involves placing food in sealed containers and applying heat to destroy microorganisms \nand inactivate enzymes. \nThe sealed container prevents subsequent contamination when properly processed. \nCommercially sterile canned foods can have long shelf lives. \n \n20. Fermentation as Food Preservation \nFermentation can preserve food by producing substances that inhibit undesirable \nmicroorganisms. \nThese may include \n• \nOrganic acids  \n• \nAlcohol  \n• \nCarbon dioxide  \n• \nOther antimicrobial compounds  \nFermentation can therefore improve both shelf life and sensory characteristics. \n \n21. Food Poisoning \nFood poisoning can result from consuming food containing harmful microorganisms or \nmicrobial toxins. \nFoodborne illness can result from \n• \nInfection by pathogens  \n• \nToxin production in food  \n• \nToxin production after microorganisms enter the body  \n \n22. Food Hygiene \nFood hygiene reduces the risk of contamination. \nImportant practices include \n• \nProper handwashing  \n• \nCleaning food-contact surfaces\n\n• \nPreventing cross-contamination  \n• \nAppropriate cooking  \n• \nCorrect storage  \n• \nMaintaining suitable temperatures  \n \n23. Microorganisms in Agriculture \nMicroorganisms are essential components of agricultural ecosystems. \nThey contribute to \n• \nNutrient cycling  \n• \nSoil fertility  \n• \nDecomposition  \n• \nNitrogen fixation  \n• \nPlant growth  \n• \nBiological pest control  \n• \nOrganic matter breakdown  \n \n24. Soil Microorganisms \nSoil contains diverse microbial communities. \nThese include \n• \nBacteria  \n• \nArchaea  \n• \nFungi  \n• \nProtozoa  \nThey interact with plants, animals and organic matter. \n \n25. Decomposition \nMicroorganisms break down dead organisms and organic waste.\n\nDuring decomposition, complex organic substances are converted into simpler compounds. \nThis returns nutrients to the soil and supports ecosystem productivity. \n \n26. Mineralisation \nMineralisation is the conversion of organic forms of nutrients into inorganic forms that can \nbecome available to plants. \nMicroorganisms play major roles in mineralisation. \n \n27. Nitrogen Fixation \nAtmospheric nitrogen exists mainly as nitrogen gas, which most plants cannot use directly. \nCertain microorganisms can convert atmospheric nitrogen into biologically useful nitrogen \ncompounds. \nThis process is called nitrogen fixation. \n \n28. Biological Nitrogen Fixation \nBiological nitrogen fixation is mainly carried out by certain bacteria and archaea. \nThe process converts atmospheric nitrogen into ammonia or related nitrogen compounds. \nNitrogen-fixing microorganisms are therefore important in maintaining soil fertility. \n \n29. Rhizobium \nSpecies of Rhizobium form symbiotic associations with legumes. \nThey live within specialised structures called root nodules. \nThe bacteria fix atmospheric nitrogen while receiving organic nutrients from the plant. \n \n30. Root Nodules \nRoot nodules are specialised structures that develop on the roots of many leguminous plants \nfollowing association with nitrogen-fixing bacteria. \nThey provide an environment suitable for biological nitrogen fixation.\n\n31. Mycorrhizae \nMycorrhizae are mutualistic associations between fungi and plant roots. \nThe fungal partner can increase the effective absorptive surface of the root system. \nBenefits can include improved uptake of \n• \nPhosphorus  \n• \nWater  \n• \nMineral nutrients  \nThe plant provides organic compounds to the fungus. \n \n32. Plant Growth-Promoting Microorganisms \nSome microorganisms promote plant growth by \n• \nImproving nutrient availability  \n• \nProducing plant growth regulators  \n• \nSuppressing pathogens  \n• \nImproving root development  \nThese microorganisms can contribute to sustainable agriculture. \n \n33. Biofertilisers \nBiofertilisers are preparations containing living microorganisms that improve nutrient \navailability or plant growth when applied appropriately. \nExamples include microorganisms involved in \n• \nNitrogen fixation  \n• \nPhosphate solubilisation  \n• \nPlant growth promotion  \n \n34. Biological Pest Control\n\nMicroorganisms can be used to control agricultural pests. \nThis approach is known as microbial biological control. \nIt can reduce dependence on certain chemical pesticides. \n \n35. Bacillus thuringiensis \nBacillus thuringiensis, commonly called Bt, produces insecticidal proteins. \nThese proteins can affect susceptible insect larvae after ingestion. \nBt-based biological control products are used against certain agricultural insect pests. \n \n36. Microbial Plant Diseases \nNot all agricultural microorganisms are beneficial. \nSome microorganisms cause diseases in plants. \nImportant plant pathogens include \n• \nBacteria  \n• \nFungi  \n• \nOomycetes  \n• \nViruses  \n• \nCertain nematode-associated microorganisms  \nPlant diseases can reduce crop yield and quality. \n \n37. Industrial Microbiology \nIndustrial microbiology involves the use of microorganisms or their products for commercial \npurposes. \nMicroorganisms can be grown in controlled environments to produce valuable substances. \n \n38. Industrial Fermentation \nIndustrial fermentation uses microorganisms to produce useful products on a large scale.\n\nThe process can be carried out in specially designed vessels called bioreactors or fermenters. \n \n39. Bioreactor \nA bioreactor is a controlled vessel in which microorganisms or other biological systems are \ncultivated to produce a desired product. \nImportant conditions include \n• \nTemperature  \n• \npH  \n• \nOxygen concentration  \n• \nNutrient availability  \n• \nAgitation  \n• \nSterility  \n \n40. Batch Culture \nIn a batch culture, nutrients are introduced at the beginning of the process and the culture is \nallowed to grow for a defined period. \nThe culture is then harvested. \nBatch systems are widely used in industrial microbiology. \n \n41. Continuous Culture \nIn a continuous culture, fresh nutrient medium is continuously supplied while an equivalent \nvolume of culture is removed. \nThis allows microorganisms to remain in a controlled growth state for extended periods. \n \n42. Industrial Products from Microorganisms \nMicroorganisms can produce \n• \nAntibiotics  \n• \nEnzymes\n\n• \nOrganic acids  \n• \nAlcohols  \n• \nVitamins  \n• \nAmino acids  \n• \nBiofuels  \n• \nHormones  \n• \nVaccines  \n• \nOther pharmaceutical products  \n \n43. Microbial Enzymes \nMicroorganisms are important sources of industrial enzymes. \nExamples include \n• \nAmylases  \n• \nProteases  \n• \nLipases  \n• \nCellulases  \n• \nPectinases  \nThese enzymes are used in food processing, detergents, textiles and other industries. \n \n44. Amylase \nAmylase breaks down starch into smaller carbohydrates. \nIt has applications in \n• \nFood processing  \n• \nBaking  \n• \nBrewing  \n• \nStarch industries\n\n45. Protease \nProteases break down proteins into smaller peptides and amino acids. \nThey are used in \n• \nDetergents  \n• \nFood processing  \n• \nLeather processing  \n• \nBiotechnology  \n \n46. Lipase \nLipases catalyse the breakdown of fats into fatty acids and glycerol. \nThey have applications in \n• \nFood industries  \n• \nDetergents  \n• \nBiotechnology  \n \n47. Cellulase \nCellulases break down cellulose. \nThey have applications in \n• \nTextile processing  \n• \nPaper industries  \n• \nAnimal feed  \n• \nBiofuel production  \n \n48. Pectinase \nPectinases break down pectin in plant tissues. \nThey are used in industries such as fruit juice processing to improve juice extraction and \nclarification.\n\n49. Antibiotic Production \nCertain microorganisms naturally produce substances that inhibit other microorganisms. \nThese compounds can be developed into antibiotics. \nAn important example is penicillin, originally obtained from fungi belonging to the genus \nPenicillium. \n \n50. Organic Acid Production \nMicroorganisms can produce organic acids on an industrial scale. \nExamples include \n• \nLactic acid  \n• \nAcetic acid  \n• \nCitric acid  \nThese substances have applications in food and chemical industries. \n \n51. Citric Acid Production \nThe fungus Aspergillus niger is widely used for industrial production of citric acid. \nCitric acid is commonly used in \n• \nFood and beverages  \n• \nPharmaceuticals  \n• \nCosmetics  \n• \nIndustrial processes  \n \n52. Biofuel Production \nMicroorganisms can contribute to the production of biofuels. \nExamples include \n• \nBioethanol\n\n• \nBiogas  \n• \nCertain microbial-derived oils  \nMicrobial fermentation plays an important role in several biofuel production processes. \n \n53. Biogas \nBiogas is a mixture of gases produced through anaerobic microbial decomposition of organic \nmatter. \nIts major combustible component is usually methane. \nOther components include carbon dioxide and smaller quantities of other gases. \n \n54. Anaerobic Digestion \nAnaerobic digestion is the breakdown of organic material by microorganisms in the absence of \noxygen. \nIt occurs through several microbial stages and ultimately produces biogas and a nutrient-rich \nresidue called digestate. \n \n55. Methanogens \nMethanogens are archaea that produce methane during anaerobic metabolism. \nThey play an important role in the final stages of biogas production. \n \n56. Sewage \nSewage is wastewater containing domestic, industrial or other waste materials. \nIt can contain \n• \nOrganic matter  \n• \nNutrients  \n• \nMicroorganisms  \n• \nSuspended solids  \n• \nChemical contaminants\n\nUntreated sewage can cause serious environmental and public-health problems. \n \n57. Sewage Treatment \nSewage treatment uses physical, chemical and biological processes to remove contaminants. \nMicroorganisms are particularly important in the biological treatment stages. \n \n58. Primary Treatment \nPrimary treatment mainly involves physical removal of large and suspended materials. \nProcesses can include \n• \nScreening  \n• \nSedimentation  \nThis reduces the amount of solid material entering subsequent treatment stages. \n \n59. Secondary Treatment \nSecondary treatment uses microorganisms to break down biodegradable organic matter. \nAerobic microorganisms can metabolise organic compounds and reduce the biological oxygen \ndemand of wastewater. \n \n60. Activated Sludge Process \nThe activated sludge process is a biological wastewater treatment method. \nWastewater is aerated in the presence of microbial communities. \nMicroorganisms consume organic matter and form microbial aggregates called flocs. \nThe flocs can then be separated from treated water. \n \n61. Biological Oxygen Demand \nBiological oxygen demand, or BOD, is an indicator of the amount of dissolved oxygen required \nby microorganisms to decompose biodegradable organic matter in water. \nHigh BOD can reduce oxygen availability in natural aquatic ecosystems.\n\n62. Eutrophication and Wastewater \nUntreated wastewater can contain high concentrations of nutrients such as nitrogen and \nphosphorus. \nExcessive nutrient input can contribute to eutrophication, which can cause excessive algal \ngrowth and oxygen depletion in aquatic environments. \n \n63. Microorganisms in Waste Management \nMicroorganisms can convert organic waste into useful products. \nExamples include \n• \nCompost  \n• \nBiogas  \n• \nBiofertiliser  \n• \nRecovered nutrients  \nMicrobial decomposition is therefore important in sustainable waste management. \n \n64. Composting \nComposting is the controlled biological decomposition of organic material under mainly aerobic \nconditions. \nMicroorganisms break down organic waste and produce a stable organic-rich material called \ncompost. \n \n65. Stages of Composting \nComposting involves microbial communities that change as environmental conditions change. \nImportant factors include \n• \nTemperature  \n• \nMoisture  \n• \nOxygen\n\n• \nCarbon-to-nitrogen ratio  \n• \nParticle size  \n \n66. Importance of Composting \nComposting can \n• \nReduce organic waste  \n• \nProduce soil amendments  \n• \nRecycle nutrients  \n• \nImprove soil structure  \n• \nReduce the amount of waste sent to landfills  \n \n67. Microorganisms in Pharmaceutical Production \nMicroorganisms are used to produce several pharmaceutical products. \nThese include \n• \nAntibiotics  \n• \nVaccines  \n• \nVitamins  \n• \nEnzymes  \n• \nTherapeutic proteins  \nModern biotechnology has greatly expanded the range of products that can be produced using \nmicrobial systems. \n \n68. Recombinant Microorganisms \nMicroorganisms can be genetically modified to produce specific proteins or other useful \nsubstances. \nFor example, genetically modified bacteria can be used to produce human proteins for medical \napplications.\n\n69. Advantages of Microbial Production \nMicrobial production has several advantages. \n• \nRapid growth  \n• \nRelatively simple cultivation  \n• \nAbility to use inexpensive substrates  \n• \nControlled production conditions  \n• \nHigh productivity  \n• \nAbility to produce specific metabolites  \n \n70. Factors Affecting Industrial Microbial Production \nSuccessful microbial production requires control of \n• \nTemperature  \n• \npH  \n• \nOxygen  \n• \nNutrient concentration  \n• \nMoisture  \n• \nAgitation  \n• \nContamination  \nSmall changes in these conditions can affect product yield. \n \n71. Contamination in Industrial Fermentation \nContamination occurs when unwanted microorganisms enter an industrial culture. \nContamination can \n• \nCompete for nutrients  \n• \nAlter environmental conditions  \n• \nProduce unwanted substances  \n• \nReduce product yield\n\n• \nCompromise product quality  \nTherefore, industrial fermentation requires appropriate sterile or controlled conditions. \n \n72. Food, Agriculture and Industry \nThe applications of microorganisms can be summarised into three major areas. \nArea \nImportant applications \nFood \nFermentation, preservation, yogurt, bread and cheese \nAgriculture Nitrogen fixation, mycorrhizae, biofertilisers and biological control \nIndustry \nEnzymes, antibiotics, organic acids, biofuels and pharmaceuticals \n \n73. Key Terms \nFermentation \nMicrobial metabolic process used to convert organic substrates into useful products. \nFood spoilage \nDeterioration of food caused by microbial and other processes. \nFood preservation \nMethods used to delay or prevent food spoilage. \nBiofertiliser \nPreparation containing beneficial microorganisms that improve nutrient availability or plant \ngrowth. \nBiological nitrogen fixation \nConversion of atmospheric nitrogen into biologically useful nitrogen compounds by \nmicroorganisms. \nMycorrhiza \nMutualistic association between fungi and plant roots. \nBiological control \nUse of biological agents to control pests or pathogens.\n\nBioreactor \nControlled vessel used for cultivating microorganisms or other biological systems. \nIndustrial fermentation \nLarge-scale cultivation of microorganisms for production of useful substances. \nAnaerobic digestion \nMicrobial decomposition of organic matter in the absence of oxygen. \nBiogas \nGas mixture produced through anaerobic microbial decomposition, containing methane as its \nmajor combustible component. \nSewage treatment \nProcesses used to remove contaminants from wastewater. \nComposting \nControlled biological decomposition of organic material into stable organic matter. \nBOD \nAmount of dissolved oxygen required by microorganisms to decompose biodegradable organic \nmatter in water. \n \n74. Final Summary \nMicroorganisms have major beneficial applications in food production, agriculture, \nenvironmental management and industry. \nIn food production, microorganisms carry out fermentation processes that produce products such \nas yogurt, bread and fermented foods. Fermentation can also contribute to food preservation. \nHowever, microorganisms can cause food spoilage and foodborne disease, making proper food \nhygiene and preservation essential. \nIn agriculture, microorganisms contribute to soil fertility through decomposition, nutrient cycling \nand nitrogen fixation. Symbiotic organisms such as Rhizobium and mycorrhizal fungi can \nimprove plant nutrition. Certain microorganisms can also be used as biofertilisers and biological \ncontrol agents. \nIndustrial microbiology uses microorganisms to produce valuable substances such as antibiotics, \nenzymes, organic acids, vitamins, biofuels and pharmaceutical products. These processes are \ncommonly carried out in controlled bioreactors.\n\nMicroorganisms also play an important role in environmental biotechnology. Anaerobic digestion \nproduces biogas from organic waste, while microbial communities are essential in biological \nsewage treatment and composting. \nTherefore, microorganisms are not only agents of disease and food spoilage. They are \nfundamental biological resources that can be harnessed for food production, sustainable \nagriculture, waste management, industrial manufacturing and modern biotechnology.	COMPLETED	113	2026-08-19 18:11:58.908592	2026-08-19 18:12:51.883014	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
994	Lesson 4: Microbial diseases and immunology applications - Official Study Notes (PDF)	Complete comprehensive lesson study material for Lesson 4: Microbial diseases and immunology applications	PDF	uploads/pdf/b94c6b94bf614584a9e7b83b11c06afc.pdf	\N	Lesson 4 — Microbial Diseases and Immunology Applications \nIntroduction \nMicroorganisms are responsible for many infectious diseases affecting humans, animals and \nplants. Important human pathogens include bacteria, viruses, fungi and protozoa. Understanding \ntheir transmission, pathogenic mechanisms, prevention and treatment is essential for maintaining \npublic health. \nModern immunology has also developed technologies that use the principles of antigen-antibody \ninteractions. Vaccines, monoclonal antibodies, hybridoma technology and ELISA are important \napplications of immunology in disease prevention, diagnosis and treatment. \n \n1. Microbial Diseases \nA microbial disease is a disease caused by a pathogenic microorganism. \nMajor groups of human microbial pathogens include \n• \nBacteria  \n• \nViruses  \n• \nFungi  \n• \nProtozoa  \nDifferent pathogens cause disease through different mechanisms. \n \n2. Pathogen \nA pathogen is a microorganism or infectious agent capable of causing disease. \nPathogens can enter the body through different routes and may colonise tissues, multiply and \ndamage host cells. \n \n3. Infection \nAn infection occurs when a microorganism enters a host, establishes itself and multiplies. \nAn infection does not always result in noticeable disease. \n \n4. Disease\n\nA disease occurs when normal body functions are disrupted and characteristic signs or symptoms \ndevelop. \nDisease may result from \n• \nDirect microbial damage  \n• \nToxins  \n• \nHost immune responses  \n• \nDestruction of host tissues  \n \n5. Pathogenicity and Virulence \nPathogenicity is the ability of an infectious agent to cause disease. \nVirulence refers to the degree of disease-causing ability of a particular pathogen or strain. \n \n6. Transmission of Infectious Diseases \nPathogens can be transmitted through several routes. \nThese include \n• \nDirect contact  \n• \nIndirect contact  \n• \nDroplet transmission  \n• \nAirborne transmission  \n• \nFood and water  \n• \nBlood  \n• \nSexual transmission  \n• \nVectors  \n• \nContaminated objects  \n \n7. Direct Transmission \nDirect transmission occurs when a pathogen passes directly from an infected individual to \nanother person without an intermediate object or vector.\n\nExamples include \n• \nDirect physical contact  \n• \nDroplet exposure at close range  \n• \nSexual contact  \n \n8. Indirect Transmission \nIndirect transmission occurs when pathogens reach a new host through an intermediate source. \nExamples include \n• \nContaminated food  \n• \nContaminated water  \n• \nContaminated surfaces  \n• \nMedical equipment  \n \n9. Vector Transmission \nA vector is an organism that carries and transmits a pathogen between hosts. \nImportant vectors include \n• \nMosquitoes  \n• \nTicks  \n• \nFleas  \n• \nCertain flies  \n \n10. Reservoir \nA reservoir is the natural habitat in which an infectious agent normally lives, grows or persists. \nReservoirs can include \n• \nHumans  \n• \nAnimals  \n• \nSoil\n\n• \nWater  \n• \nEnvironmental surfaces  \n \n11. Zoonotic Diseases \nA zoonotic disease is an infectious disease that can naturally be transmitted between animals and \nhumans. \nAnimals can therefore act as reservoirs for certain human pathogens. \n \n12. Bacterial Diseases \nBacteria can cause a wide range of human diseases. \nImportant examples include \n• \nTuberculosis  \n• \nCholera  \n• \nTyphoid  \n• \nTetanus  \n• \nDiphtheria  \n• \nGonorrhoea  \n \n13. Tuberculosis \nTuberculosis is caused mainly by Mycobacterium tuberculosis. \nIt primarily affects the lungs but can affect other organs. \nTransmission commonly occurs through inhalation of airborne particles released by an infected \nperson. \nImportant symptoms can include \n• \nPersistent cough  \n• \nFever  \n• \nNight sweats  \n• \nWeight loss\n\n• \nFatigue  \n \n14. Cholera \nCholera is caused by Vibrio cholerae. \nIt is primarily transmitted through contaminated water or food. \nThe bacterium produces cholera toxin, which causes severe intestinal fluid loss. \nThe major danger is rapid dehydration. \n \n15. Typhoid \nTyphoid fever is caused by Salmonella enterica serovar Typhi. \nIt is commonly transmitted through contaminated food and water. \nPoor sanitation and inadequate hygiene increase the risk of transmission. \n \n16. Tetanus \nTetanus is caused by Clostridium tetani. \nThe bacterium produces a neurotoxin that interferes with nervous-system function. \nA characteristic feature is severe and sustained muscle contraction. \nTetanus can be prevented through vaccination and appropriate wound management. \n \n17. Diphtheria \nDiphtheria is caused by toxigenic strains of Corynebacterium diphtheriae. \nThe bacterium can produce a toxin that damages tissues and may interfere with protein synthesis. \nVaccination provides effective protection. \n \n18. Gonorrhoea \nGonorrhoea is caused by Neisseria gonorrhoeae. \nIt is primarily transmitted through sexual contact.\n\nIt can affect the reproductive tract and may cause serious complications if untreated. \n \n19. Viral Diseases \nViruses cause many human diseases. \nExamples include \n• \nInfluenza  \n• \nDengue  \n• \nMeasles  \n• \nHepatitis  \n• \nRabies  \n• \nHIV infection  \n• \nCOVID-19  \n \n20. Influenza \nInfluenza is caused by influenza viruses. \nTransmission commonly occurs through respiratory droplets and aerosols. \nSymptoms can include \n• \nFever  \n• \nCough  \n• \nMuscle aches  \n• \nHeadache  \n• \nFatigue  \nVaccination can reduce the risk of severe disease and transmission. \n \n21. Dengue \nDengue is caused by dengue viruses and is primarily transmitted by Aedes mosquitoes. \nCommon manifestations include\n\n• \nFever  \n• \nHeadache  \n• \nMuscle and joint pain  \n• \nRash  \nSevere dengue can cause serious bleeding, plasma leakage and circulatory complications. \n \n22. Measles \nMeasles is caused by measles virus. \nIt is highly contagious and spreads mainly through respiratory transmission. \nTypical features include \n• \nFever  \n• \nCough  \n• \nRunny nose  \n• \nConjunctivitis  \n• \nCharacteristic rash  \nVaccination is the principal method of prevention. \n \n23. Hepatitis B \nHepatitis B is caused by hepatitis B virus. \nIt primarily affects the liver. \nTransmission can occur through \n• \nBlood  \n• \nSexual contact  \n• \nContaminated needles  \n• \nMother-to-child transmission  \nPersistent infection can lead to cirrhosis and liver cancer.\n\n24. Rabies \nRabies is caused by rabies virus. \nIt is commonly transmitted through the bite or saliva of an infected animal. \nThe virus affects the nervous system. \nOnce clinical symptoms develop, rabies is almost invariably fatal, making prompt post-exposure \ntreatment extremely important. \n \n25. HIV Infection \nHuman immunodeficiency virus, or HIV, infects cells of the immune system, particularly CD4-\npositive T lymphocytes. \nProgressive loss of immune function can lead to AIDS, the acquired immunodeficiency \nsyndrome. \nTransmission can occur through \n• \nBlood  \n• \nSexual contact  \n• \nContaminated needles  \n• \nMother-to-child transmission  \nAntiretroviral therapy can suppress viral replication and greatly improve health outcomes. \n \n26. COVID-19 \nCOVID-19 is caused by SARS-CoV-2, a coronavirus. \nTransmission primarily occurs through respiratory particles. \nClinical severity varies considerably among individuals. \nVaccination, ventilation, respiratory hygiene and appropriate public-health measures can reduce \ndisease burden. \n \n27. Fungal Diseases \nFungi can cause diseases called mycoses.\n\nExamples include \n• \nRingworm  \n• \nAthlete's foot  \n• \nCandidiasis  \nFungal infections may affect the skin, mucous membranes or internal organs depending on the \npathogen and host condition. \n \n28. Ringworm \nRingworm is a superficial fungal infection of the skin. \nIt is caused by dermatophyte fungi rather than a worm. \nTypical lesions can be circular and scaly. \n \n29. Athlete's Foot \nAthlete's foot is a dermatophyte infection affecting the feet. \nIt commonly occurs in warm and moist environments. \nGood hygiene and keeping the feet dry can help prevent transmission. \n \n30. Candidiasis \nCandidiasis is commonly caused by Candida species, particularly Candida albicans. \nIt can affect \n• \nMouth  \n• \nSkin  \n• \nGenital tract  \n• \nBlood and internal organs in severe cases  \n \n31. Protozoan Diseases \nProtozoa can also cause important human diseases.\n\nExamples include \n• \nMalaria  \n• \nAmoebiasis  \n• \nGiardiasis  \n \n32. Malaria \nMalaria is caused by Plasmodium species and transmitted mainly by infected female Anopheles \nmosquitoes. \nThe parasite undergoes part of its life cycle in humans and part in mosquitoes. \nCommon symptoms include \n• \nFever  \n• \nChills  \n• \nSweating  \n• \nHeadache  \n• \nFatigue  \nSevere malaria can cause life-threatening complications. \n \n33. Amoebiasis \nAmoebiasis is commonly caused by Entamoeba histolytica. \nTransmission occurs mainly through ingestion of food or water contaminated with infective \ncysts. \nThe disease can cause intestinal symptoms including diarrhoea and abdominal discomfort. \n \n34. Giardiasis \nGiardiasis is caused by Giardia duodenalis. \nTransmission occurs through ingestion of infective cysts, often through contaminated water or \nfood. \nIt can cause\n\n• \nDiarrhoea  \n• \nAbdominal cramps  \n• \nBloating  \n• \nMalabsorption  \n \n35. Prevention of Microbial Diseases \nPrevention strategies include \n• \nVaccination  \n• \nHand hygiene  \n• \nSafe drinking water  \n• \nProper sanitation  \n• \nFood hygiene  \n• \nVector control  \n• \nSafe sexual practices  \n• \nSterilisation of medical equipment  \n• \nAppropriate antimicrobial use  \n \n36. Vaccines \nA vaccine stimulates an adaptive immune response against a specific pathogen or its \ncomponents. \nVaccination can produce immunological memory without requiring the individual to experience \nthe full disease. \n \n37. Major Types of Vaccines \nImportant vaccine approaches include \n• \nLive attenuated vaccines  \n• \nInactivated vaccines  \n• \nSubunit vaccines\n\n• \nToxoid vaccines  \n• \nConjugate vaccines  \n• \nViral-vector vaccines  \n• \nNucleic-acid vaccines  \n \n38. Live Attenuated Vaccines \nThese vaccines contain weakened forms of pathogens. \nThey can produce strong and long-lasting immune responses. \nHowever, they are not suitable for everyone because the weakened organism may pose risks in \ncertain individuals. \n \n39. Inactivated Vaccines \nInactivated vaccines contain pathogens that have been killed or rendered unable to replicate. \nThey cannot cause infection through replication but can stimulate an immune response. \n \n40. Subunit Vaccines \nSubunit vaccines contain selected components of a pathogen rather than the whole organism. \nExamples can include specific proteins or polysaccharides. \n \n41. Toxoid Vaccines \nA toxoid vaccine uses an inactivated bacterial toxin. \nThe immune system develops antibodies against the toxin. \nExamples include vaccines against \n• \nTetanus  \n• \nDiphtheria  \n \n42. Conjugate Vaccines\n\nSome bacterial polysaccharide antigens produce weak immune responses, particularly in young \nchildren. \nIn conjugate vaccines, a polysaccharide is linked to a protein carrier to improve the immune \nresponse. \n \n43. Viral-Vector Vaccines \nViral-vector vaccines use a modified virus to deliver genetic instructions for an antigen into host \ncells. \nThe cells then produce the antigen and stimulate an immune response. \n \n44. Nucleic-Acid Vaccines \nNucleic-acid vaccines deliver genetic instructions that allow host cells to produce an antigen. \nExamples include \n• \nmRNA vaccines  \n• \nDNA-based vaccines  \n \n45. Antigen-Antibody Reactions \nThe adaptive immune system recognises specific antigens. \nAntibodies bind to particular regions called epitopes. \nThis specificity forms the basis of many diagnostic and therapeutic technologies. \n \n46. Monoclonal Antibodies \nMonoclonal antibodies are identical antibodies produced from a single clone of antibody-\nproducing cells. \nThey recognise the same specific epitope. \nThey have applications in \n• \nDisease diagnosis  \n• \nMedical imaging\n\n• \nCancer treatment  \n• \nAutoimmune disease treatment  \n• \nLaboratory research  \n \n47. Polyclonal and Monoclonal Antibodies \nPolyclonal antibodies \nMonoclonal antibodies \nProduced by multiple B-cell clones Derived from one B-cell clone \nRecognise multiple epitopes \nRecognise one specific epitope \nMixture of antibody molecules \nHighly uniform antibody population \nBroader recognition \nHighly specific recognition \n \n48. Hybridoma Technology \nHybridoma technology is used to produce monoclonal antibodies. \nIt combines an antibody-producing B lymphocyte with an immortal tumour cell. \nThe resulting hybrid cell is called a hybridoma. \n \n49. Production of Hybridomas \nThe general process involves \n1. Immunising an animal with the target antigen  \n2. Isolating antibody-producing B cells  \n3. Fusing B cells with myeloma cells  \n4. Selecting successful hybrid cells  \n5. Screening hybridomas for the desired antibody  \n6. Cloning the selected hybridoma  \n7. Producing and purifying the monoclonal antibody\n\n50. Why Hybridomas Are Useful \nNormal antibody-producing B cells have a limited lifespan. \nMyeloma cells can divide continuously. \nA hybridoma combines \n• \nAntibody production from the B cell  \n• \nLong-term proliferation from the myeloma cell  \nThis allows continuous production of identical antibodies. \n \n51. ELISA \nELISA stands for enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay. \nIt is a laboratory technique based on specific antigen-antibody interactions. \nAn enzyme-linked reagent produces a detectable signal when the target reaction occurs. \n \n52. Principle of ELISA \nThe basic principle involves \nAntigen-antibody binding \n↓ \nEnzyme-linked detection \n↓ \nSubstrate reaction \n↓ \nDetectable signal \nThe intensity of the signal can be related to the amount of target present, depending on the assay \ndesign. \n \n53. Direct ELISA \nIn direct ELISA, an enzyme-labelled antibody binds directly to the target antigen.\n\nThe method is relatively simple but may provide lower amplification than some indirect \napproaches. \n \n54. Indirect ELISA \nIn indirect ELISA, an unlabelled primary antibody binds to the target antigen. \nAn enzyme-linked secondary antibody then binds to the primary antibody. \nThis can increase detection sensitivity. \n \n55. Sandwich ELISA \nIn sandwich ELISA, the target antigen is captured between two antibodies. \nGeneral arrangement \nCapture antibody \n↓ \nAntigen \n↓ \nDetection antibody \nThis method is useful for detecting and quantifying specific antigens. \n \n56. Competitive ELISA \nIn competitive ELISA, the target substance competes with a labelled or otherwise detectable \ncounterpart for antibody binding. \nThe signal is generally inversely related to the concentration of the target. \n \n57. Applications of ELISA \nELISA can be used to detect or measure \n• \nPathogen antigens  \n• \nAntibodies  \n• \nHormones\n\n• \nAllergens  \n• \nCertain drugs  \n• \nOther biological molecules  \n \n58. ELISA in Disease Diagnosis \nELISA can be used to determine whether a patient has antibodies against a particular pathogen. \nA positive antibody result can indicate exposure or immune response, although interpretation \ndepends on the specific disease, timing and assay. \n \n59. Antigen Detection \nELISA can also detect pathogen-derived antigens. \nThis can provide evidence of current infection depending on the pathogen and test design. \n \n60. Diagnostic Immunology \nImmunological diagnostic techniques are based on the specificity of immune recognition. \nThey can detect \n• \nAntigens  \n• \nAntibodies  \n• \nHormones  \n• \nProteins  \n• \nOther biological molecules  \n \n61. Sensitivity and Specificity \nSensitivity refers to the ability of a test to correctly identify individuals who have the condition \nbeing tested for. \nSpecificity refers to the ability of a test to correctly identify individuals who do not have the \ncondition. \nBoth characteristics are important when evaluating diagnostic tests.\n\n62. False Positive \nA false positive occurs when a test indicates that the target condition or substance is present \nwhen it is actually absent. \n \n63. False Negative \nA false negative occurs when a test fails to detect a target that is actually present. \nThe possibility of false results means that diagnostic tests must be interpreted appropriately. \n \n64. Immunology in Cancer Treatment \nMonoclonal antibodies can be designed to recognise specific molecules associated with cancer \ncells. \nSome therapeutic antibodies can \n• \nBlock growth signals  \n• \nMark cancer cells for immune destruction  \n• \nDeliver therapeutic substances  \n• \nModulate immune responses  \n \n65. Immunotherapy \nImmunotherapy uses components of the immune system or substances that modify immune \nresponses to prevent or treat disease. \nExamples include \n• \nMonoclonal antibodies  \n• \nImmune checkpoint inhibitors  \n• \nCertain cellular therapies  \n \n66. Passive Immunisation\n\nPassive immunisation provides ready-made antibodies rather than stimulating the body to \nproduce them. \nIt can provide rapid protection but generally produces shorter-lasting immunity than active \nimmunisation. \n \n67. Antiserum \nAntiserum is blood serum containing antibodies against a particular antigen. \nAntisera can be used for passive immunisation or diagnostic purposes. \n \n68. Herd Immunity \nWhen a sufficiently large proportion of a population becomes immune to an infectious disease, \ntransmission can be reduced. \nThis population-level protection is commonly called herd immunity. \nIt can provide indirect protection to individuals who cannot develop adequate immunity. \n \n69. Importance of Immunisation \nVaccination can \n• \nReduce disease incidence  \n• \nReduce severe disease  \n• \nPrevent complications  \n• \nReduce transmission  \n• \nProtect vulnerable populations  \n• \nContribute to disease elimination or eradication programmes  \n \n70. Microbial Diseases and Their Prevention\n\nDisease \nMain pathogen \nMajor transmission \nroute \nImportant prevention \nTuberculosis Mycobacterium \ntuberculosis \nAirborne \nVaccination, early detection, \ntreatment and ventilation \nCholera \nVibrio cholerae \nContaminated food and \nwater \nSafe water and sanitation \nTyphoid \nSalmonella Typhi \nContaminated food and \nwater \nHygiene, sanitation and \nvaccination \nTetanus \nClostridium tetani \nContaminated wounds \nVaccination and wound care \nDengue \nDengue virus \nMosquito vector \nVector control \nMeasles \nMeasles virus \nRespiratory \nVaccination \nMalaria \nPlasmodium species Mosquito vector \nVector control and preventive \nmeasures \nRabies \nRabies virus \nInfected animal saliva \nAnimal vaccination and post-\nexposure treatment \nHepatitis B Hepatitis B virus \nBlood and sexual \ntransmission \nVaccination and prevention of \nexposure \nCandidiasis Candida species \nUsually endogenous or \nopportunistic \nAppropriate hygiene and \nmanagement of risk factors \n \n71. Key Terms \nPathogen \nDisease-causing microorganism or infectious agent. \nInfection \nEntry, establishment and multiplication of an infectious agent in a host. \nVirulence \nDegree of pathogenicity of a pathogen or strain. \nVector\n\nOrganism that transmits a pathogen between hosts. \nReservoir \nNatural habitat in which a pathogen normally persists. \nVaccine \nPreparation designed to stimulate protective adaptive immunity. \nMonoclonal antibody \nIdentical antibody molecules derived from a single clone of antibody-producing cells. \nHybridoma \nCell produced by fusion of an antibody-producing B cell with an immortal myeloma cell. \nELISA \nEnzyme-linked immunosorbent assay used to detect or quantify specific biological molecules. \nAntigen \nMolecule or structure specifically recognised by the immune system. \nAntibody \nImmunoglobulin produced by plasma cells that specifically binds an antigen. \nSensitivity \nAbility of a diagnostic test to correctly identify true positive cases. \nSpecificity \nAbility of a diagnostic test to correctly identify true negative cases. \nImmunotherapy \nTreatment that uses or modifies immune responses to combat disease. \n \n72. Final Summary \nMicrobial diseases are caused by pathogenic bacteria, viruses, fungi and protozoa. Their \ntransmission can occur through direct contact, contaminated food and water, respiratory \nparticles, blood, sexual contact or biological vectors.\n\nDisease prevention depends on interrupting transmission and strengthening host protection. \nImportant measures include vaccination, sanitation, food hygiene, vector control, appropriate \nantimicrobial treatment and infection-control practices. \nVaccines stimulate adaptive immunity and can generate immunological memory. Different \nvaccine technologies include live attenuated, inactivated, subunit, toxoid, conjugate, viral-vector \nand nucleic-acid vaccines. \nImmunology has also produced important diagnostic and therapeutic technologies. Monoclonal \nantibodies are highly specific antibodies produced using hybridoma technology. They are used \nin diagnosis, research and treatment. \nELISA uses antigen-antibody specificity together with enzyme-linked detection to identify or \nmeasure biological molecules. Different forms include direct, indirect, sandwich and competitive \nELISA. \nThe application of microbiology and immunology has therefore transformed modern medicine \nby improving disease prevention, diagnosis, treatment and public-health management.	COMPLETED	114	2026-08-19 18:12:01.413328	2026-08-19 18:12:51.883015	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	general	36
