592	591	During the light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis, what is the direct source of electrons that replace those lost by Photosystem II (P680)?	MCQ	["A. Carbon dioxide", "B. NADPH", "C. Water", "D. Oxygen", "E. Photosystem I"]	C	Photolysis of water molecules provides electrons to replace those lost by Photosystem II, releasing oxygen gas and protons as byproducts.	1	EASY	UNDERSTAND	\N	APPROVED	al_exam	210	G.C.E. A/L Biology Paper I (First)	2026-08-18 06:09:31.533867	["G.C.E. A/L Biology Paper I (First)", "generic_mcq"]	\N	60	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	\N	\N	\N	\N
618	617	Which of the following ecosystems or biomes in Sri Lanka is characterized by high endemism, high annual rainfall, multi-layered canopy structure, and is situated primarily in the southwestern region?	MCQ	["A. Dry mixed evergreen forest", "B. Thorn scrub land", "C. Montane cloud forest", "D. Mangrove ecosystem", "E. Tropical lowland rain forest (Sinharaja type)"]	E	The tropical lowland rain forest (such as Sinharaja) in Sri Lanka's wet zone is characterized by high rainfall (>2500 mm), multi-layered canopy structure, and extremely high biodiversity and endemism.	1	EASY	UNDERSTAND	\N	APPROVED	al_exam	210	G.C.E. A/L Biology Paper I (First)	2026-08-18 06:09:31.731328	["G.C.E. A/L Biology Paper I (First)", "generic_mcq"]	\N	60	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	\N	\N	\N	\N
593	592	Select the correct statement regarding plant transport mechanisms and translocation.	MCQ	["A. Transpiration pull is the primary force for water movement in the xylem and requires metabolic energy expended directly by tracheary elements.", "B. The pressure-flow hypothesis explains that organic solutes move through phloem from a source with lower hydrostatic pressure to a sink with higher hydrostatic pressure.", "C. Guttation occurs through stomata during periods of high transpiration and high soil moisture.", "D. Loading of sucrose into companion cells and sieve tube elements at the source involves active transport utilizing proton pumps.", "E. Cavitation in xylem vessels enhances the upward movement of water columns under high negative pressure."]	D	Sucrose loading into companion cells/sieve tubes at source regions requires active transport mediated by ATP-driven proton pumps and sucrose-H+ symporters. Tracheary elements are dead at maturity (no metabolic energy). Pressure-flow moves sap from higher to lower hydrostatic pressure. Guttation occurs through hydathodes, not stomata. Cavitation disrupts water columns.	1	MEDIUM	APPLY	\N	APPROVED	al_exam	210	G.C.E. A/L Biology Paper I (First)	2026-08-18 06:09:31.537872	["G.C.E. A/L Biology Paper I (First)", "five_statement_truth"]	\N	60	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	\N	\N	\N	\N
594	593	Which of the following structures is primarily responsible for the lateral transport of water and mineral ions in a woody dicotyledonous stem?	MCQ	["A. Sieve tube elements", "B. Vascular cambium", "C. Companion cells", "D. Cortical fibers", "E. Ray parenchyma cells"]	E	Ray parenchyma cells (medullary rays) are living parenchymatous cells that extend radially across the secondary xylem and secondary phloem, facilitating the lateral transport of water, mineral ions, and organic nutrients in woody stems.	1	EASY	UNDERSTAND	\N	APPROVED	al_exam	210	G.C.E. A/L Biology Paper I (First)	2026-08-18 06:09:31.543431	["G.C.E. A/L Biology Paper I (First)", "generic_mcq"]	\N	60	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	\N	\N	\N	\N
595	594	During the secondary growth of a dicotyledonous stem, what is formed immediately to the outside of the vascular cambium?	MCQ	["A. Secondary phloem", "B. Primary xylem", "C. Secondary xylem", "D. Periderm", "E. Cork cambium"]	A	The vascular cambium is a lateral meristem that divides to produce secondary xylem towards the inside of the stem and secondary phloem towards the outside.	1	EASY	UNDERSTAND	\N	APPROVED	al_exam	210	G.C.E. A/L Biology Paper I (First)	2026-08-18 06:09:31.54888	["G.C.E. A/L Biology Paper I (First)", "generic_mcq"]	\N	60	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	\N	\N	\N	\N
583	582	Select the correct statement regarding human kidney function and osmoregulation.	MCQ	["A. Antidiuretic hormone (ADH) increases the water permeability of the distal tubules and collecting ducts by inserting aquaporins.", "B. Aldosterone decreases sodium reabsorption in the distal convoluted tubules and collecting ducts.", "C. Glomerular filtration is primarily driven by active transport mechanisms of podocytes.", "D. The loop of Henle establishes an osmotic gradient in the renal medulla where osmolarity decreases from the cortex to the inner medulla.", "E. High blood pressure in the afferent arteriole decreases net filtration pressure in the glomerulus."]	A	ADH stimulates the insertion of aquaporins into the membranes of collecting duct cells, increasing water reabsorption. Aldosterone increases sodium reabsorption (not decreases). Glomerular filtration is driven by blood hydrostatic pressure. Medullary osmolarity increases (not decreases) towards the inner medulla.	1	MEDIUM	APPLY	\N	APPROVED	al_exam	210	G.C.E. A/L Biology Paper I (First)	2026-08-18 06:09:31.435998	["G.C.E. A/L Biology Paper I (First)", "five_statement_truth"]	\N	60	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	\N	\N	\N	\N
584	583	Match the plant growth substances in Column I with their primary physiological roles in plant development in Column II.	MCQ	["A. P-(iii), Q-(iv), R-(i), S-(ii)", "B. P-(iv), Q-(iii), R-(ii), S-(i)", "C. P-(i), Q-(ii), R-(iii), S-(iv)", "D. P-(ii), Q-(i), R-(iv), S-(iii)", "E. P-(ii), Q-(iv), R-(iii), S-(i)"]	D	P. Auxin (IAA) promotes apical dominance (ii). Q. Gibberellin stimulates stem elongation and seed germination (i). R. Cytokinin delays leaf senescence and promotes cell division (iv). S. Abscisic acid (ABA) promotes stomatal closure during water stress (iii).	1	MEDIUM	APPLY	\N	APPROVED	al_exam	210	G.C.E. A/L Biology Paper I (First)	2026-08-18 06:09:31.489731	["G.C.E. A/L Biology Paper I (First)", "matching_column"]	\N	60	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	\N	\N	\N	\N
585	584	Which of the following cellular organelles is bound by a single membrane?	MCQ	["A. Lysosome", "B. Nucleus", "C. Mitochondrion", "D. Chloroplast", "E. Ribosome"]	A	Lysosomes, vacuoles, peroxisomes, and the components of the endomembrane system are bounded by a single membrane. Nuclei, mitochondria, and chloroplasts are double-membrane bound, while ribosomes are non-membranous.	1	EASY	REMEMBER	\N	APPROVED	al_exam	210	G.C.E. A/L Biology Paper I (First)	2026-08-18 06:09:31.496539	["G.C.E. A/L Biology Paper I (First)", "generic_mcq"]	\N	60	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	\N	\N	\N	\N
