591	590	Match the cellular organelle or structure in Column I with its primary cellular function in Column II.	MCQ	["A. (1)-(b), (2)-(a), (3)-(d), (4)-(c)", "B. (1)-(a), (2)-(b), (3)-(c), (4)-(d)", "C. (1)-(a), (2)-(c), (3)-(b), (4)-(d)", "D. (1)-(d), (2)-(b), (3)-(a), (4)-(c)", "E. (1)-(c), (2)-(d), (3)-(a), (4)-(b)"]	B	Rough ER synthesizes proteins; Golgi modifies and sorts them; Peroxisomes handle fatty acid oxidation and catalase-mediated H2O2 breakdown; Mitochondria perform oxidative phosphorylation.	1	MEDIUM	UNDERSTAND	\N	APPROVED	al_exam	210	G.C.E. A/L Biology Paper I (First)	2026-08-18 06:09:31.52933	["G.C.E. A/L Biology Paper I (First)", "matching_column"]	\N	60	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	\N	\N	\N	\N
596	595	Identify the correct statement concerning plant hormone functions.	MCQ	["A. Abscisic acid (ABA) promotes seed germination and inhibits stomatal closure under water stress.", "B. Gibberellins stimulate stem elongation, cell division, and breaking of seed dormancy.", "C. Auxins (IAA) inhibit apical dominance and stimulate lateral bud development.", "D. Cytokinins inhibit cell division and accelerate leaf senescence.", "E. Ethylene inhibits fruit ripening and induces drought tolerance."]	B	Gibberellins stimulate stem elongation, cell division, and seed germination by breaking dormancy. ABA inhibits germination and promotes stomatal closure; auxins promote apical dominance; cytokinins delay leaf senescence; ethylene promotes fruit ripening.	1	MEDIUM	APPLY	\N	APPROVED	al_exam	210	G.C.E. A/L Biology Paper I (First)	2026-08-18 06:09:31.552874	["G.C.E. A/L Biology Paper I (First)", "five_statement_truth"]	\N	60	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	\N	\N	\N	\N
597	596	Match the plant tissue or cell type in Column I with its primary functional characteristic in Column II.	MCQ	["A. 1-B, 2-A, 3-C, 4-D", "B. 1-C, 2-B, 3-D, 4-A", "C. 1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-A", "D. 1-A, 2-C, 3-B, 4-D", "E. 1-D, 2-B, 3-A, 4-C"]	C	Collenchyma provides flexible mechanical support (1-B); Sclereids provide rigid support/protection in hard structures (2-C); Endodermis regulates selective entry via Casparian strips (3-D); Companion cells metabolically support sieve tubes (4-A).	1	MEDIUM	UNDERSTAND	\N	APPROVED	al_exam	210	G.C.E. A/L Biology Paper I (First)	2026-08-18 06:09:31.556885	["G.C.E. A/L Biology Paper I (First)", "matching_column"]	\N	60	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	\N	\N	\N	\N
598	597	Which of the following adaptations typically characterize xerophytic plants?\nA. Thick cuticles covering epidermal surfaces\nB. Presence of sunken stomata\nC. Increased number of chloroplasts in spongy mesophyll\nD. Reduction of leaves into spines or scales	MCQ	["A. A, C and D only", "B. B, C and D only", "C. A, B, C and D", "D. A, B and D only", "E. A and C only"]	D	Xerophytes adapt to arid conditions by minimizing water loss through thick cuticles (A), sunken stomata (B), and leaf reduction/spines (D). An increased number of chloroplasts in spongy mesophyll is not a standard xerophytic adaptation for water conservation.	1	MEDIUM	APPLY	\N	APPROVED	al_exam	210	G.C.E. A/L Biology Paper I (First)	2026-08-18 06:09:31.56409	["G.C.E. A/L Biology Paper I (First)", "combination_grid"]	\N	60	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	\N	\N	\N	\N
599	598	Which of the following structural features are adaptations of the mammalian small intestine for efficient nutrient absorption?\nA. Presence of microvilli forming a brush border\nB. Extensive folding of the inner mucosa into circular folds (plicae circulares)\nC. Presence of lacteals within each villus for lipid transport\nD. Dense network of blood capillaries in each villus maintaining a concentration gradient	MCQ	["A. A, B and D only", "B. A, C and D only", "C. B, C and D only", "D. A and C only", "E. A, B, C and D"]	E	All listed features (microvilli, circular folds, lacteals, and blood capillaries) maximize surface area and facilitate rapid transport of absorbed water-soluble and lipid-soluble nutrients.	1	MEDIUM	APPLY	\N	APPROVED	al_exam	210	G.C.E. A/L Biology Paper I (First)	2026-08-18 06:09:31.568297	["G.C.E. A/L Biology Paper I (First)", "combination_grid"]	\N	60	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	\N	\N	\N	\N
